/* Subscriber content. */
Object.assign(window.TPR_SIM=window.TPR_SIM||{},{"c-m-01":{"setup":"Thursday 13:05, A&E relatives' room. Jamie Walsh, 24, your community patient, died at 12:10 after a suspected overdose. His mother has been told only that he is 'very unwell'. With the ED nurse, tell her, answer her questions and plan next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Lorraine Walsh, 52, Jamie's mother","open":"Yes. Sit. Where is he? Nobody's telling me anything. Can I see him?","manner":"Frightened and pressured; numb, then sobbing after the news; angry at the team for a moment; quieter and practical once she has had time and a plan.","free":["Jamie texted her at 23:40 last night: 'Night Mum, love you'","Her sister is parking the car","Jamie saw the community team nine days ago","She thought he was doing better; he had cut down his drinking"],"ifasked":["Jamie's brother Connor, 20, is at university in Leeds and does not know yet","She will stay at her sister's tonight","She has fleeting thoughts of not wanting to wake up, with no plan","Her GP is Dr Ahmed","Jamie's father died when Jamie was 12","She wants to see Jamie today"],"worries":["How did the team not see this coming?","Can she have the note?","Will there be a post-mortem, and when can she plan the funeral?","Who will tell Connor?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses euphemisms or delays, she says, 'Is he dead? Just say it.'","Becomes angrier if the doctor defends the team's care; settles if her question is acknowledged and a named contact promised","Takes in practical information only after she has had time to cry","Mentions her own wish not to wake up only if asked directly and kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Walsh? I'm Dr Patel, one of the doctors from Jamie's community mental health team. This is Grace, the nurse who's been looking after him. May we sit down with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. Sit. Where is he? Nobody's telling me anything. Can I see him?"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'm going to tell you everything I know. Is anyone with you, or on their way?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"My sister's parking the car. I couldn't wait."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"Would you like us to wait for her, or shall I go on now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"No. Just tell me. Please."},{"t":"0:38","band":"What she knows","w":"D","s":"What have you been told so far?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"That the crisis team found him at his flat and brought him here. That he's very unwell. Is he in intensive care?"},{"t":"0:50","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid I have some very bad news.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Oh God."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"When the team found Jamie this morning, he wasn't breathing. The paramedics and the doctors here tried for a long time to restart his heart. I'm so sorry, Mrs Walsh. Jamie died at ten past twelve.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No. No. He texted me last night. He can't be dead."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"He said, 'Night Mum, love you.' That's all. I thought he was getting better."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"That message must feel so precious right now. Take all the time you need. We're not going anywhere.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"What happened? Did he do something to himself?"},{"t":"1:35","band":"How he died","w":"D","s":"I'll tell you what we know. He was found in bed. There were empty medicine packets beside him, and a note addressed to you. It looks as though he may have taken his own life. The coroner will confirm the cause.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"A note. Where is it? I want it."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. The police are keeping it for the coroner for now, because his death was sudden. It will come back to you, and the coroner's officer can tell you when.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"He saw you lot nine days ago. How did nobody see this coming?"},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"You have every right to ask that. I saw him nine days ago, and I don't have the answer for you today. The trust will look carefully at his care, and you'll be involved and told what we find.","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Don't fob me off."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"I won't. You'll have a named person from the trust, and you can put every question to them, including that one. I'll make sure it happens.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Can I see him?"},{"t":"2:43","band":"Seeing him","w":"D","s":"Yes. Grace will take you whenever you're ready. Because the coroner is involved, the tubes the doctors used are still in place. He's covered up to his shoulders. There may be a tube in his mouth. You can hold his hand, talk to him, step out and go back in as often as you want.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"I want to see him. In a minute."},{"t":"3:07","band":"What happens next","w":"D","s":"When you feel able, can I explain what happens over the next few days? Or is that too much right now?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Tell me. I need to know what to do."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Because his death was sudden, it's been reported to the coroner. That's the normal process for any sudden death. A coroner's officer will phone you, usually in the next day or two.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Will they cut him open?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"The coroner will probably ask for a post-mortem to confirm the cause. The coroner's officer will explain it, and when he can go to a funeral director. You can choose a funeral director now, but not fix a date yet.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"And then a court case?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"There'll probably be an inquest. It isn't a trial. It's a hearing to record who he was, and when, where and how he died. You can attend and ask questions. It's usually some months away.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"I can't take all this in."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable. You don't need to remember any of it today. I'll write the key points down, and the coroner's officer will go through it again.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"4:20","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"Grace has a booklet called Help is at Hand. It's written for families after a suicide, and it covers all of this.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"I don't want a leaflet. I want my son."},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. Nothing makes this better. Keep it for later, for when questions come at three in the morning.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"I've also written down two organisations. Support After Suicide Partnership can link you to people bereaved in the same way. Cruse Bereavement Support has a free helpline.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:53","band":"Her safety","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about you? Who'll be with you tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"My sister. I'll stay at hers. Oh God, Connor. His brother's at university in Leeds. Who tells Connor?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"We can help you plan it, so he isn't alone when he hears. Would you and your sister like to ring him together, with a friend of his close by?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. He shouldn't be on his own."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Mrs Walsh, sometimes after a loss like this, people have thoughts of not wanting to go on themselves. Have you had any thoughts like that?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Not like that. I just don't want to wake up tomorrow."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If those thoughts get stronger, ring Samaritans on 116 123, any time, or NHS 111 and choose option 2. Can I ask your GP to see you this week?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Yes. Dr Ahmed."},{"t":"5:51","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. What will you tell your sister happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"The coroner's person rings tomorrow. Probably a post-mortem. Someone from the trust contacts me. And the note comes back later."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Our family liaison lead will call you within two working days. I'll cancel all Jamie's appointments today, so no letters come to you.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"I hadn't even thought of that. Thank you."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"My consultant, Dr Ross, will be here soon and would like to meet you, today or whenever you choose. Grace will take you to Jamie now, and I'll wait with your sister.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Later. I want to see my boy."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Grace, thank you. I've booked half past four for everyone who looked after Jamie, to talk about how we're all doing. Will you come?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll tell the resus team."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and nurse, sits at her level, checks who is with her and whether to wait","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she has been told before giving any news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then says 'died' clearly within the first minute","d":"Content"},{"c":"Pauses after the news and responds to grief and anger with silence and validation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains what is known about how he died honestly, without speculating or defending care","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers to see him, warning that tubes stay in place for the coroner","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains coroner's officer, post-mortem and inquest in plain words, when she is ready","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Promises a named trust family liaison contact and her involvement in the review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives Help is at Hand, Support After Suicide Partnership and Cruse details in writing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks who will be with her, helps plan telling her son, and asks about her own suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say back the next steps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with next contacts, cancels his appointments and books a team debrief","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Says 'died' early and plainly, stays with her grief, answers honestly, explains the coroner and support, checks her safety and arranges the debrief.","borderline":"Delivers the news clearly but rushes into process, or forgets her own risk, the appointments or the team debrief.","fail":"Uses euphemisms or delays the news, defends the team's care, speculates about cause, or leaves her without support or a safety check."}},"c-m-02":{"setup":"Wednesday 10:30, male acute ward. Last night Mr Hollis, 58, was given his 300 mg clozapine night dose twice, became drowsy and hypotensive, and was monitored overnight at the acute hospital. With the ward manager, tell him what happened.","role":{"who":"Mr Derek Hollis, 58, informal inpatient with treatment-resistant schizophrenia","open":"Groggy. Like I've been hit by a bus. Why was I in the other hospital? Nobody's said.","manner":"Drowsy and wary at first; flares into suspicion ('poisoned') and anger; frightened about losing clozapine; calms and becomes grateful when spoken to plainly.","free":["He remembers his tablets at 20:00, being woken, then an ambulance","He feels groggy and a bit dizzy","Clozapine is the only drug that has helped him in 30 years","His sister Carol visits on Sundays"],"ifasked":["He felt dizzy getting up to the toilet at 07:00","His bowels last opened two days ago","He has no chest pain or cough","He wants Carol told and to see the letter","He used to believe staff were putting things in his food, years ago","He is thinking of complaining or suing"],"worries":["Was he poisoned on purpose?","Has his heart been damaged?","Will they stop his clozapine?","Who did it, and will it happen again tonight?"],"rules":["If the doctor is vague ('a medication issue'), he asks, 'What exactly did you give me?'","Becomes more suspicious if the doctor blames a named nurse or avoids the apology","Settles when his fear about clozapine is acknowledged and answered","Agrees to Carol being told only if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Hollis, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the ward doctors. This is Jo, the ward manager. How are you feeling this morning?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Groggy. Like I've been hit by a bus. Why was I in the other hospital? Nobody's said."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's what we've come to talk about. Is now OK, and would you like anyone with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"No. Just tell me."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"Before I start, what do you remember about last night?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"My tablets at eight. Then a nurse shaking me. Then an ambulance. That's it."},{"t":"0:44","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid we made a mistake with your medicine last night.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"What mistake?"},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"You were given your night dose of clozapine twice. You had 300 milligrams at eight o'clock, and another 300 at half past nine. So 600 instead of 300.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Twice? You poisoned me?"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like that. It's frightening to hear. I'm truly sorry. This should not have happened, and on behalf of the ward and the trust, I apologise.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sorry. Everyone's always sorry."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"You're right to be angry. Sorry isn't enough on its own, so I want to tell you exactly what happened and what we're doing about it.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Go on, then."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"What we know so far is this. The first dose wasn't signed on your chart. A second nurse saw no signature and gave it again.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Who was it? I want their name."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that. I'm not going to name staff today. What matters is how the checks failed, so it can't happen to anyone again. The review will look at exactly that.","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Hmm."},{"t":"2:16","band":"What was done","w":"D","s":"By eleven you were very sleepy and your blood pressure was low. That's why the night doctor sent you to the general hospital, so you could be watched closely.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Am I damaged? My heart?"},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Your heart tracing and blood tests there were normal, and your blood pressure came back up overnight. We don't expect any lasting harm.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"You don't expect. That's not a no."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"That's a fair challenge. The risk was highest last night, and it's falling. That's why we're keeping a close eye on you today.","m":[10,7]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"The nurses will check your blood pressure lying down and then standing every four hours, and we'll do another heart tracing this afternoon and a blood test for your clozapine level.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"What am I looking out for?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Tell a nurse straight away if you feel dizzy when you stand, your heart races, you have any jerks or a fit, a cough or a temperature, or your bowels stop. Sit on the edge of the bed before you stand.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Dizzy, heart, fits, cough, bowels. Right."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"We held this morning's dose, on advice from the doctors there and our pharmacist.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Are you stopping it? It's the only thing that's ever worked. I'm not going back to the way I was."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much it matters to you. We're not stopping it. If your checks are fine today, you'll have your usual 300 milligrams tonight. Missing one dose doesn't mean starting again from scratch.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Right. Good."},{"t":"4:16","band":"Review and follow-up","w":"D","s":"Jo reported this on our incident system last night. Because it harmed you, there'll be a review of how it happened.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"Will I find out what they find?"},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Yes. We'll tell you what the review finds and what changes because of it. You can ask questions at any point.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"And what stops it happening again tonight?"},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"From last night, every clozapine dose on this ward is signed straight after it's given, and a second nurse checks the chart before any night dose.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Jo will also send you a letter within ten working days, with what I've told you, the apology and how the review will run.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I'll want that. My sister will want to read it. Carol."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Would you like us to phone Carol today and tell her what happened?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Yes. She'll go mad, mind."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. We'll be honest with her too. If either of you want to complain or get independent help, PALS or an advocate can support you, and it won't affect your care.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"I might."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've told you a lot. Can you tell me back what happened, and what you'll look out for?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Got my night tablets twice. Blood pressure dropped, went to the other hospital, tests fine. Watch for dizzy, racing heart, fits, cough, bowels. Tablets tonight if I'm OK."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. How are you feeling about it all now?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"Shaken. I trusted you lot with my tablets. How do I trust it now?"},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"That's a completely fair thing to feel. Trust has to be earned back. Would it help to watch the nurse check and sign your dose tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"I will."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"I'll see you again after your heart tracing this afternoon, and Dr Varma will see you tomorrow. Jo and I are here all day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks for being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and ward manager, checks he is ready and offers someone to be with him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how he feels and what he remembers before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning, then states plainly he was given his night dose twice, with doses and times","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a clear, sincere apology on behalf of the ward and the trust","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the facts known so far without speculating, naming or blaming staff","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains what was done: acute hospital monitoring, normal tests, morning dose held","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains today's monitoring and the warning signs he should report","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms clozapine continues tonight at his usual dose if checks are stable","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the incident report, the review, immediate changes, and that findings will be shared","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates anger and fear before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Agrees the written follow-up, offers to tell his sister with consent, offers PALS and advocacy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and closes with named contacts and review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the error plainly, apologises sincerely, explains what was done and what happens next, keeps clozapine on track, and promises review findings and a letter.","borderline":"Apologises and explains, but is vague on doses, monitoring or the written follow-up, or does not check understanding.","fail":"Hides or minimises the error, blames a nurse, speculates, or leaves him fearing his clozapine will stop."}},"c-m-03":{"setup":"Monday 15:10, male acute ward. Mr Brian Doyle, 64, waited 9 hours in A&E overnight for a bed. His wife stops you in the corridor wanting to complain. He has agreed she can be told about his care. Respond to her complaint.","role":{"who":"Mrs Pat Doyle, 61, Mr Doyle's wife","open":"Are you a doctor? I want to make a complaint, and I want it done properly.","manner":"Angry, clipped and determined; tearful once her fear is named; softens and becomes cooperative when she is heard and sees concrete action.","free":["Decision to admit at 21:30; he waited until 06:30 on a corridor chair","No food until 03:00","She was sent home at midnight and not told he had moved","A nurse on arrival said 'You're lucky, some people wait two days'"],"ifasked":["He missed his evening amlodipine and sertraline","A security guard sat with him most of the night","His glasses were left in A&E","They have been married 41 years","She feared he would leave and kill himself","She wants it on record and does not want it to happen to anyone else"],"worries":["Why did it take nine hours?","Will complaining affect his care?","Does Brian have to agree to the complaint?","What will happen about the nurse's remark?"],"rules":["If interrupted or contradicted early, she gets louder and says, 'You're not listening'","Becomes tearful when asked about her night at home; settles if validated","Rejects a leaflet offered before she has been heard","Calms noticeably once concrete actions with times are offered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Osei, one of the doctors on this ward. Can I help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Are you a doctor? I want to make a complaint, and I want it done properly."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear it properly. Let's not do it in the corridor. There's a quiet room here. Can I get you a drink?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Water. Fine."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"Can I check who you are?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Pat Doyle. Brian's wife. Brian Doyle. He came in last night."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Mrs Doyle. Brian told the team this morning he's happy for us to talk to you, so I can be open with you. Please tell me what happened, from the start.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"We got to A&E at seven. The mental health nurse saw him and said at half nine he needed a bed. Then nothing. Nine hours. On a plastic chair in a corridor, next to a drunk man shouting. Brian. Who's never been in trouble in his life."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Go on.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Nobody gave him anything to eat till three in the morning. He missed his evening tablets. And they sent me home at midnight. Nobody rang me. I didn't know he'd moved here till he texted at seven."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Was there anything else?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"When he got here, a nurse said, 'You're lucky, some people wait two days.' Lucky! And he left his glasses down there. He can't read a thing."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Which tablets did he miss, do you know?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"His blood pressure one and his antidepressant."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"And while he waited, was someone with him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"A security man, mostly. That's what upset him. Like he was a criminal."},{"t":"2:03","band":"Summarise","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it all. He waited nine hours on a corridor chair after the decision to admit him. No food till three. Missed evening tablets. You were sent home and not told when he moved. A hurtful remark when he arrived. And his glasses are still in A&E. Have I missed anything?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No. That's it. I sat at home all night thinking he'd walk out of there and... I thought I'd get a call to say he'd done something."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. He's very unwell, and you were at home all night with no news. Anyone would be frightened and angry.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Forty-one years we've been married. Sorry. I'm not a crier."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. Take your time.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:56","band":"Apologise and explain","w":"D","s":"I am sorry. Brian should not have waited that long, in those conditions, and you should have been kept informed. I'm also sorry for what was said when he arrived. That wasn't acceptable.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Why did it take nine hours?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you. There were no free beds in the trust last night until someone was discharged this morning. That's an explanation, not an excuse. It shouldn't fall on patients like Brian.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"So it'll happen to someone else tonight."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"It might. That's exactly why your complaint matters. Long waits are reported and reviewed, and complaints like yours show the trust what they mean for families.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:44","band":"Put things right","w":"D","s":"Let me tell you what I'll do today. As soon as we finish, I'll check his chart to make sure he's had this morning's tablets and that he carries on as normal tonight.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"He said he'd had them."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"Good, I'll confirm it. Next, I'll ring A&E and ask them to send his glasses up today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"His consultant, Dr Hughes, will see you both at the ward round on Wednesday. And I can tell you his plan now, if you'd like.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Yes. Nobody's told me anything."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"He's settled and slept a few hours this morning. Today is a full assessment and blood tests. His named nurse, Mo, will ring you every evening at six.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"And the complaint? I want it on record. Not a pat on the head."},{"t":"4:46","band":"The process","w":"D","s":"It will be on record. There are two routes. PALS, our patient advice and Liaison Service (PALS) (PALS) (PALS), can take your concerns and get quick answers, often within days. Or you can make a formal complaint to the trust, by letter, email or phone.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"What happens with a formal one?"},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"They acknowledge it within three working days, agree with you what they'll look into, including A&E, and send you a written response. If you're not satisfied, you can go to the Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Does Brian have to agree?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"For anything about his care, yes, they'll ask him. Your own experience, being sent home and not told, you can complain about yourself.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Will it go against him? Will they treat him differently?"},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"No. Complaining won't affect his care in any way. I'd rather know when we get things wrong.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"You're also his carer. You're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council, for support for you. Our carers' lead can arrange it.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"I've never thought of myself as a carer."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"Many people don't. Last night shows how much you carry.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So, I'll check his tablets and get his glasses up. Mo rings you at six. Dr Hughes sees you on Wednesday. Here are the PALS and complaints details in writing. Does that cover everything?","m":[12,5]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"And the nurse who said that?"},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"I'll tell the ward manager today. I'm also reporting the long wait and the missed tablets on our incident system, and I'll write what you've told me in his notes.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Right. Thank you for listening, at least."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. If anything else worries you, ring the ward, day or night.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Moves her to a private room, introduces self and offers a seat and drink","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks her identity and the patient's consent before discussing his care","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lets her tell the whole story without interrupting or defending","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks specific questions to clarify each concern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises all her concerns back and checks nothing is missed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Apologises for the specific failures without blaming colleagues","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and validates her fear and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a short, honest explanation for the wait without excuses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Takes concrete actions today: medicines, glasses, consultant meeting, daily update","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains PALS, formal complaint, 3-working-day acknowledgement and the Ombudsman","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Reassures care is unaffected and offers a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Records, reports incidents, informs ward manager and confirms next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Listens fully, summarises, apologises specifically, fixes what can be fixed today, explains both complaint routes and records and escalates.","borderline":"Apologises and explains PALS, but interrupts, misses a concern or offers no same-day action.","fail":"Defends or blames A&E, argues with her, discourages a complaint, or hands over a leaflet without listening."}},"c-m-04":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:00, CMHT clinic. Mrs Rania Khalil, 45, from Syria, is referred with six months of low mood. A professional Arabic interpreter is booked; her family want her 16-year-old son to interpret. Brief the interpreter, then assess her mood and risk.","role":{"who":"Mrs Rania Khalil, 45, Syrian, speaking through Ms Huda Saleh, a professional Arabic interpreter","open":"Thank you, doctor. My son is outside. His English is very good. He can help.","manner":"Polite, quiet and guarded; describes sadness as a squeezed heart; tears when her mother is mentioned; opens up when spoken to directly and kindly; worried about stigma.","free":["Her heart feels squeezed and she does not sleep","Her mother died in Syria six months ago; she could not go to the funeral","She has three children; her daughters now cook","She feels guilty for leaving her family behind"],"ifasked":["She wakes at 4 or 5 a.m. and cannot get back to sleep","Her clothes are loose; about 5 kg weight loss","She enjoys nothing, even time with her children","She sometimes asks God to take her in her sleep; no plan or intent","She has occasional nightmares about bombing","She feels safe at home; her husband worries about money"],"worries":["People will say she is mad","Will she have to take tablets forever?","What will her son be told?"],"rules":["Agrees to see the doctor without her son only if given a kind reason","Gives short answers if the doctor speaks to the interpreter in the third person ('ask her...')","Discloses passive death wishes only if asked directly","The interpreter adds one cultural explanation and responds if asked how she is afterwards"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Brief the interpreter","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ms Saleh, thank you for coming. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can we take two minutes before we go in?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Of course."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil is 45, from Syria, referred by her GP with low mood. Will Syrian Arabic be comfortable for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Yes, I'm from Damascus. No problem."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"Good. Do you know her or her family? It's a small community.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: I don't think so. I'll tell you if I recognise her."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Please interpret everything, as closely as you can, in the first person: 'I feel', not 'she says she feels'. Even things that seem off-topic.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Yes. Sometimes a phrase needs explaining."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Please do, and just tell us both you're adding an explanation. We may talk about painful things, including suicide. Is that OK for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: That's fine. Thank you for asking."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"I'll speak in short chunks and pause. Let's sit in a triangle so she and I face each other. Everything stays confidential.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil, my name is Dr Rahman. I'm a psychiatry doctor. This is Ms Saleh, a professional interpreter. She'll repeat everything we both say, and it's all confidential.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. My son is outside. His English is very good. He can help."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Your son sounds very caring. Today it's better for you to talk through Ms Saleh. You can say things privately, and he doesn't have to carry your worries. He can come in at the end, if you'd like.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Yes. Maybe that's better. He's only sixteen."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Doctor, my heart is squeezed. All the time. Here. And I don't sleep."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"When you say your heart is squeezed, what is it like for you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Like a heavy stone. The sadness sits here."},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: Doctor, I'm adding something. This phrase is commonly used for deep sadness or worry, not only for pain."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's helpful. Mrs Khalil, how long have you felt this heaviness?","m":[7,6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"About six months. Since my mother died in Syria. I couldn't go to her funeral."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Not being able to say goodbye to your mother must be very painful.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Every day I think, I left her there. I left everyone."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Take your time. There's no rush.","m":[10]},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"How is your sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"I wake at four or five and can't sleep again. I think and think."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And your appetite? Your energy?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"I'm not hungry. My clothes are loose. I'm tired all the time. My daughters cook now."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you still get pleasure from?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Even with my children, I'm there but not there. I feel guilty."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Do you have bad dreams or memories of the war?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Some nights I dream of the bombs. Less than before."},{"t":"3:36","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people feel this low, they feel life isn't worth living. Have you had thoughts like that?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I ask God to take me. In my sleep."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you ever thought of ending your life yourself, or made any plans?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"No. Never. My children need me. And in my faith it is forbidden. I would not do it."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Your children and your faith matter a lot. If those thoughts ever got stronger, I'd want you to tell us straight away.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Do you feel safe at home? Has anyone hurt you, or made you afraid?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No, no. My husband is a good man. He is tired too. He worries about money."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If that ever changed, you could tell us or your GP privately, always with an interpreter, never family.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain the plan","w":"D","s":"Mrs Khalil, I think you have depression. It's a common illness, and it can be treated. It's not weakness, and it's not your fault.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Depression. In my family we don't talk of this. People will say I'm mad."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. Depression isn't madness, and many people have it. What we discuss stays private.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"What do I do?"},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"Two things help. First, a tablet called sertraline. It's not addictive. One each morning. It takes two to four weeks to help. At first you may feel sick or more nervous; that usually settles.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Will I take it forever?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"No. Usually for at least six months after you feel better, then we reduce it slowly together. Please don't stop it suddenly.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Second, talking therapy, with an Arabic interpreter every time. I'll also ask your GP for blood tests, to check things like your thyroid and iron.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"To make sure I explained it well, can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I have depression. It's an illness. One tablet in the morning; it helps after some weeks; maybe I feel sick at first. Talking therapy with an interpreter. Blood tests with my GP."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. If those thoughts get stronger, call NHS 111 and press 2, and say 'Arabic' for an interpreter. I'll give you this in writing in Arabic. I'll see you in two weeks, with an interpreter.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Would you like your son to come in now? What would you like us to tell him?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Only that I have depression and will take medicine. Not about my mother. He's sad too."},{"t":"6:38","w":"D","s":"Of course. Ms Saleh, afterwards, thank you. Some of that was heavy. How are you doing?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Interpreter: I'm fine. It reminded me of home, but I'm OK. Thank you for asking."}],"marks":[{"c":"Briefs the interpreter: purpose, first-person interpreting, everything interpreted, distressing topics","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks dialect, that the interpreter does not know the family, seating and confidentiality","d":"Content"},{"c":"Declines the son as interpreter kindly with a reason, and offers him a part at the end","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Introduces self and interpreter and explains how the interpreting and confidentiality work","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Speaks directly to the patient in short, single questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about core depressive symptoms, duration, sleep, appetite, enjoyment and trauma","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the meaning of her bodily description of distress without assuming","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about safety at home and abuse while she is without family","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her grief, guilt and fear of stigma","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains depression, sertraline and interpreted talking therapy simply","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back, safety-nets, books follow-up and debriefs the interpreter","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Briefs the interpreter, keeps family out of the assessment, speaks directly to her, covers mood and risk including home safety, and confirms understanding.","borderline":"Uses the interpreter well but misses the briefing, the home safety question or the teach-back.","fail":"Lets the son interpret, talks about her in the third person, or does not ask directly about suicide."}},"c-m-05":{"setup":"Saturday 14:30, family room off the adult ward. Mrs Sarah Bennett, 38, was admitted on Thursday under Section 2 with mania. Her daughter Lily, 10, thinks it is her fault. Mum has agreed Lily should be told. With her father present, explain.","role":{"who":"Lily Bennett, 10, Mrs Bennett's daughter (with her father, Mr Tom Bennett, 40)","open":"OK.","manner":"Quiet and drawing at first; blurts out her guilt; cries when told it is not her fault; asks lots of direct questions once she feels safe; brightens at the idea of a card.","free":["She saw the police and paramedics take Mum","Mum was talking very fast and said the TV was talking to her","She thinks it is her fault because of a fight about bedtime","Her cat is called Biscuit"],"ifasked":["She told Mum she hated her that night","She has been making her own breakfast and helping Mum since the summer","She has not slept well since Thursday","Her teacher is Mrs Shah; she does not want the whole class to know","She wants to see Mum but is scared Mum will be 'weird'","Dad: he is exhausted, juggling work and hospital visits"],"worries":["Is Mum going to die?","Is Mum in prison or in trouble with the police?","Can she catch it?","When is Mum coming home?"],"rules":["Goes quiet and keeps drawing if spoken to with long or medical words","Cries when told clearly that it is not her fault; settles when reassured that Mum asked about her","Gets upset if promised a date and later told it might change","Dad speaks only if asked or to confirm what Mum said"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Lily, I'm Dr Ade. I'm one of the doctors looking after your mum. Hello, Mr Bennett. Is it OK if I sit down here with you both?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I like your drawing. Is that your cat?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"It's Biscuit. He's ginger."},{"t":"0:23","band":"What she knows","w":"D","s":"Lily, Dad told me you've got some questions about Mum. Before I explain, what do you think has happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Mum went mad and the police took her. Because I shouted at her."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That sounds like a really scary night.","m":[7]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"She was talking really fast and she wouldn't stop. And she said the TV was talking to her."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That must have been very confusing and frightening to see.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:01","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I'm going to explain what's happening. Mum has an illness in her brain. You can't see it, like a cold or a cut. It changes how she thinks and feels.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Like what?"},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Right now her thoughts are going super fast, like a car without brakes. She hasn't been able to sleep. And sometimes the illness makes her believe things that aren't true, like the TV talking to her.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Is she going to die?"},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"No. This illness doesn't make people die. Doctors know a lot about it, and most people get better with medicine and rest.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"But it's my fault. We had a fight about bedtime and I said I hated her."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Not your fault","w":"D","s":"Lily, I want you to hear this really clearly. It is not your fault. Nothing you said or did made Mum ill. Not the shouting, not the fight about bedtime. This illness happens by itself.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"But I said I hated her."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Lots of children say that when they're cross. Mum knows you love her. She asked about you this morning.","m":[4,7]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Dad: She did, Lily. She asked if you'd fed Biscuit."},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Really?"},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Really. And it's OK to feel sad, or scared, or even a bit cross with Mum. All those feelings are allowed.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","band":"The police","w":"P","s":"Why did the police come? Is she in trouble?"},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"No, she's not in trouble. She hasn't done anything wrong. When someone is very poorly and doesn't realise it, the police sometimes help get them to hospital safely. That's all they were doing.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Is she in prison here?"},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"No, this is a hospital. She has her own bedroom. There are nurses here all day and all night. She's eating, and she's starting to sleep better.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Can I catch it?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"You can't catch it, like you can't catch a broken leg. Hugging Mum or being near her is completely safe.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"When's she coming home?"},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"I don't know yet, and I don't want to make a promise I can't keep. Usually it's a few weeks. When she's sleeping and her thoughts slow down, we'll start talking about home.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Weeks?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"I know that feels like forever. It's OK to be sad about that.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","band":"Seeing Mum","w":"D","s":"But you don't have to wait weeks to talk to her. If Mum's calm tomorrow, you could speak to her on the phone, with a nurse helping.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Can I see her?"},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Yes. There's a family room just outside the ward, with sofas and games. The nurses will plan a visit with Dad, maybe early next week, when she's a bit calmer. It might be short, about half an hour.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Will she be weird?"},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"She might still talk fast, or seem sleepy from her medicine. That's the illness, not Mum changing. If it feels too much, you can stop the visit, and that's fine.","m":[9,8]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"Can I bring her a card?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"She'd love that. You could draw Biscuit.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"Mr Bennett, there's a young carers service locally. They support children whose mum or dad is unwell, with clubs and someone to talk to. Would you like me to refer Lily?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Dad: She does a lot for her mum. Yes, please."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"It might also help to tell Lily's teacher on Monday, so school can look out for her. Lily, how would you feel about that?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"Only Mrs Shah. Not the whole class."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Dad can tell just Mrs Shah.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:41","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Lily, can you tell me what you'd say to Biscuit about Mum?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Mum's brain is poorly and her thoughts are too fast. It's not my fault. I can't catch it. She's in hospital, not prison. I can phone her tomorrow and make her a card."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"That's brilliant. You've got it all. If more questions pop up later, Dad can ask the nurses, or you can ask me when you visit.","m":[11,8]},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Mr Bennett, how are you doing with all this?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Dad: Honestly, I'm shattered. Work, Lily, the hospital."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"That's a huge amount. Our carers' lead can arrange a carer's assessment for you too. I'll ask the nurses to ring you tonight about tomorrow's call. Any questions for me?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Dad: No. Thank you. That was more than I could explain."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you both. Bye, Lily. Say hi to Biscuit.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Greets Lily by name at her level, connects with her interest, and includes Dad","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Lily what she thinks has happened before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the illness in simple, concrete words with no jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Says clearly it is not her fault, linked to the bedtime argument","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains Mum is safe and cared for, and that she cannot catch it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the police role honestly without frightening detail","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear, sadness and anger, and says all feelings are allowed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Answers questions honestly, including uncertainty about when Mum comes home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees a phone call, a planned visit in the family room and a card","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers Young Carers support and school involvement with Lily's say","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks Lily's understanding by asking her to explain it in her own words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks on Dad, offers a carer's assessment and closes with the next contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses simple words, corrects the guilt clearly, answers honestly, keeps Lily connected to Mum, and arranges Young Carers and support for Dad.","borderline":"Explains kindly but uses some jargon, or forgets to check understanding or to offer support beyond the visit.","fail":"Uses vague euphemisms, does not address her guilt, promises a date, or talks to Dad over Lily's head."}},"c-m-06":{"setup":"EIP clinic, Thursday afternoon. Daniel Mensah, 22, three weeks home after a first episode of psychosis, comes with his mother. She wants him to stop aripiprazole and see a faith healer. Agree a safe plan with them.","role":{"who":"Mrs Grace Mensah, 51, Daniel's mother (Daniel, 22, is with her and answers when asked directly)","open":"Doctor, I'll be honest. We don't think this is a sickness of the mind. We want him to stop the tablets and see a healer next Saturday.","manner":"Polite but guarded, expecting to be judged. Tearful about feeling judged at church. Warms and becomes flexible once her faith is respected and a middle way is offered.","free":["The family believe the illness is a spiritual attack that began after a party","A visiting healer will hold a deliverance service next Saturday","She wants Daniel to stop his tablets","She feels judged at church for using medicine"],"ifasked":["The service requires three days of fasting with no food or water beforehand","There is prayer, anointing with oil and laying on of hands; nobody will hold him down; no plans to travel abroad","Daniel paces every evening since starting the tablets","No voices for a month; he sleeps well","Her own mother took blood pressure tablets and prayed every day","The pastor may allow water for those who are unwell","Daniel's father agrees with her but will follow the pastor's advice"],"worries":["Do you think we are backward for believing this?","If God heals him, why does he need tablets?","What if the pastor says no water?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about judgement at church; settles if validated","Becomes defensive and closed if the belief is called a delusion or nonsense","Agrees to continued tablets only after her faith is respected and a middle way is offered","Daniel speaks only when asked directly; he wants to keep the tablets but avoids contradicting his mother"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Ahmed, one of the doctors in the team. Daniel, good to see you. Are you happy for your mum to join us today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Yeah, it's fine. She wants to talk to you anyway."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Mrs Mensah, thanks for coming in. Daniel's happy for us to talk openly. What would you like to talk about?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Doctor, I'll be honest. We don't think this is a sickness of the mind. We want him to stop the tablets and see a healer next Saturday."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Their beliefs","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest with me. That really helps. Can you tell me what you believe has been happening to him?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"It started after he went to that party. Something followed him home. The voices, the fear. Our pastor says it is a spiritual attack on our family."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"So you see it as something spiritual that came into his life. What has your church told you would help?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Prayer. Deliverance. A healer is visiting from abroad and we have a place for Daniel."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"It sounds as if your faith has carried your family through this. I'm not going to argue with your beliefs. What worries you about the tablets?","m":[3,2]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"He walks up and down all evening, he can't sit still. And people at church say we are weak to give him medicine. (voice breaks) I just want my son back."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That's so hard: watching him struggle, and feeling judged at church as well. You've done everything you can for him. I think we want the same thing: Daniel well and back to himself.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. That is all I want."},{"t":"2:03","band":"Daniel's view","w":"D","s":"Daniel, what do you think has been going on, and what would you like to do?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Daniel: I don't know. The voices were real. The tablets helped, honestly. But the pacing does my head in. And I don't want to upset Mum."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Daniel. That's really useful. The pacing sounds like a side effect called akathisia, and it can usually be fixed. When is it worst?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Evenings. I can't sit through a film."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"We could lower the dose from 15 to 10 milligrams and see if that settles it, rather than stopping. Would you try that?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Yeah, I'd try that."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Since you came home, any voices, or feeling people are against you? How's your sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Daniel: No voices for a month. I sleep okay."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Daniel: No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:14","band":"The service","w":"D","s":"Mrs Mensah, tell me about next Saturday. What will happen at the service?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Prayer, anointing with oil. They lay hands on him. And he must fast for three days before, no food or water, to prepare."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Will anyone hold him down, or is there any plan for him to travel for more healing?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"No, no. Nobody will hurt him. Just prayer."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"I'm glad to hear it. Prayer and anointing sound peaceful. My worry is three days without water. That could make him seriously ill. Could he fast from food but keep drinking?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"I would have to ask the pastor. Maybe water is allowed for the sick."},{"t":"4:07","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain my worry about stopping the tablets?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"After a first episode, if someone stops their tablets in the first year or two, the illness is much more likely to come back. And each time it comes back, it can take longer to recover.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"But if God heals him, he will not need them."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I understand. Many families we work with see medicine as one of the ways God helps. You don't have to choose. He goes to the service, you all pray, and he takes his tablet each morning. Would that sit right with your faith?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"(pause) My own mother took tablets for her blood pressure, and she prayed every day. Maybe."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Daniel, how does that sound to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Daniel: That's what I want. Go with Mum, keep the tablets."},{"t":"5:12","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"We have a chaplaincy team here who work with all faiths. Would you like them to meet you, and, if you agree, speak with your pastor about the fasting?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"A chaplain from the hospital? Yes. The pastor might listen to a man of God."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Let's also agree what to watch for. If Daniel isn't sleeping, gets suspicious, or mentions voices, call us that day. Out of hours, ring 111 and choose option 2 for mental health.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping, suspicious, voices. I'll write it down."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"His care coordinator, Sam, will visit on the Monday after the service, and I'll see you both in four weeks after the dose change.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"And if the pastor says no water?"},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"Then please ring me before Saturday, and the chaplain and I will talk with him. Daniel's safety comes first, and I think the pastor will want that too.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"6:21","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it clearly? What will you tell Daniel's dad tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"That the doctor respects our faith. Daniel prays, drinks water, takes his tablet. The chaplain will call us."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Thank you both. It took courage to tell me what you really thought.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Daniel: Thanks, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, greets Daniel first and confirms he agrees to his mother joining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks the mother's understanding of the illness with open questions before giving a view","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges her faith and distress without arguing, and names a shared goal","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks Daniel directly what he thinks and wants about tablets and the service","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what the service involves: fasting, fluids, restraint, travel","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks for relapse signs and thoughts of harm to self or others since discharge","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains relapse risk of stopping in the first 1 to 2 years in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about side effects and offers a dose change rather than stopping","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees prayer and medication together, with fluids and daily doses continued","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a chaplaincy referral and a link with the family's own pastor","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives relapse signs, crisis line and a dated review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, summarises and thanks them","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respects the family's faith, hears Daniel, checks the ritual's risks, keeps medication alongside prayer, links chaplaincy and leaves a relapse plan.","borderline":"Polite and safe but lectures about medication, or misses Daniel's own view or the fasting risk.","fail":"Dismisses the beliefs as delusional, talks only to the mother, or agrees to stop the antipsychotic."}},"c-m-07":{"setup":"Wednesday 12:00, acute ward. Dr Fraser, 58, the consultant, has agreed discharge today for Mr Hughes, 47, admitted after a planned overdose. Since the ward round you have new worrying information. Raise your concern with him.","role":{"who":"Dr Martin Fraser, 58, male consultant psychiatrist and Mr Hughes's responsible clinician","open":"Make it quick, I've a meeting at quarter past. What's up?","manner":"Busy, kind but brusque; brushes off the first hint. Briefly defensive when challenged, then admits he is stretched and engages fully once given facts, a clear concern and a plan.","free":["Mr Hughes looked brighter on the ward round and asked to go home","The bed manager needs two beds by 4pm","The GP will follow him up","Mr Hughes denied suicidal thoughts this morning"],"ifasked":["He did not ask about weekend plans in detail on the ward round","He did not know about the dog or the 'sorted everything' remark","Home treatment team have not been asked to see him","He is free at two o'clock","The nurse in charge today is Ade","He would rather be told than read about it in an incident report"],"worries":["Are you saying I've got it wrong?","We've three people waiting; what do you suggest?","What if at two I still think he's fit to go?"],"rules":["Brushes off a vague hint ('he's fine, don't worry'); responds only when the concern is stated clearly","Becomes defensive if accused of being unsafe or bed-driven; softens if the bed pressure is acknowledged","Admits he is stretched after four overnight admissions; warms if this is acknowledged","Agrees a joint review if given specific facts and a specific request"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Dr Fraser, have you got two minutes, somewhere private? It's about Mr Hughes's discharge.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Make it quick, I've a meeting at quarter past. What's up?"},{"t":"0:17","band":"The facts","w":"D","s":"After the ward round, Jo, the healthcare assistant, told me he gave his dog to his brother yesterday. And he said to her, 'I've sorted everything now.'","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"People rehome pets when they're unwell. He was bright as a button this morning. He's fine, don't worry."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"He did look brighter, I agree, and I know we're under real pressure for beds. But I'm concerned. I went and saw him myself just now.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"And?"},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"He denied any plans, but he was guarded about the weekend. His wife told the nurses there are painkillers at home. He lives alone, he's lost his job and his marriage, and he took forty co-codamol a week ago.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"We've been through all that. Three people are waiting for beds. The GP will see him."},{"t":"1:32","band":"Challenge","w":"D","s":"I understand. But I'm not comfortable with him going home today. I think the calm, the dog and 'sorted everything' could mean he's made a decision, not that he's better.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"(pause) Are you saying I've got it wrong?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"No. You saw him this morning, and you've far more experience than me. This came up after the ward round, so you didn't have it. I'd just feel much safer if we looked again.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Sorry. It's been a hell of a week. Four admissions overnight and the bed manager on my back."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"That sounds relentless. Thank you for hearing me out when you're this stretched.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"So what do you want to do?"},{"t":"2:40","band":"The request","w":"D","s":"Could we see him together before he goes? I'll fit around you.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"I'm in the meeting till half one. Two o'clock?"},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Two works. Meanwhile, can I ask the nurse in charge to pause the discharge paperwork, and put him on fifteen-minute checks until we see him?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Yes, fine. Tell Ade."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"I'll also ring his wife and brother before two, with his agreement, to hear how he's been and what's at home.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"Good idea. What if he wants to go before then?"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"He's informal. If he tries to leave and we're worried, the nurse can use Section 5(4) for up to six hours, and you or I, as your nominated deputy, can use 5(2) while an assessment is arranged.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"Hopefully it won't come to that."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"And if we do decide he can go, I'd like home treatment to see him first, a written safety plan, his brother to clear the tablets, and follow-up within 72 hours.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"That's sensible. You've thought about it."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Recording it","w":"D","s":"I'll write a note now: what Jo told me, my review, our discussion and the plan for two o'clock. Facts only.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Fine. Do you need me to sign anything?"},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"No. I'll record that we discussed it and agreed the plan together. You can add your own review at two.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"And if at two I still think he's fit to go?"},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Then I'd respect that, as long as we've both seen him with the new information and a safety plan is in place. If I was still very worried, I'd want to talk it through with Dr Shah as clinical director, and I'd tell you first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"(pause) Fair enough. That's what you should do. I'd rather be told than read it in a report."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Honestly, I wasn't sure how to bring it up.","m":[3]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"You did it well. Next time, grab me straight away, even mid ward round."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"I will. I'll speak up as soon as something comes up.","m":[1]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"Right. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:02","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just to recap. I'll ask Ade to pause discharge and start fifteen-minute checks now. I'll call his wife and brother by half one. We see him together at two. I'll document it all.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"And home treatment?"},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"I'll give them a heads-up now, so they can see him today if we do discharge him. Is that everything?","m":[9,12]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"That's everything. Go."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Dr Fraser. See you at two.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"See you at two."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks for a private moment and states the topic clearly at the start","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the new facts concisely: what was said, by whom, when","d":"Content"},{"c":"Acknowledges the consultant's view, experience and bed pressure without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names the concern explicitly, for example 'I'm concerned'","d":"Content"},{"c":"Steps up to a clear challenge ('I'm not comfortable') after the first brush-off","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the facts to suicide risk: recent attempt, losses, lives alone, means at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Makes one specific request: a joint review before discharge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Proposes interim safety steps: pause discharge, observations, collateral, legal holding powers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Proposes a safer discharge if it goes ahead: HTT, safety plan, means, 72-hour follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States they will document the facts, discussion and outcome factually","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names the next escalation step respectfully, telling the consultant first","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises actions with owners and times, and thanks the consultant","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the new facts and a clear concern, steps up calmly after a brush-off, secures a joint review with interim safety steps, documents it and knows the escalation route.","borderline":"Raises the concern but hints, accepts the first reassurance, or agrees a review without interim safety steps.","fail":"Says nothing, accuses the consultant, involves the patient in the disagreement, or does not document."}},"c-m-08":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Mr Lee Barker, 38, with generalised anxiety, has been buying 'blues' online and wants a month of diazepam. The clinic nurse is next door. Respond to his request and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Lee Barker, 38, male patient with generalised anxiety disorder","open":"Look, I'll save us both time. Diazepam is the only thing that works. I need a month's worth.","manner":"Tense and restless; stands and shouts at the refusal and threatens a complaint. Sits and calms when his distress is named and a limit is set calmly; tearful about his child; then engages with the plan.","free":["Anxiety for years, much worse since redundancy in the spring","An out-of-hours GP gave him diazepam in August and it worked","He has been buying 'blues' online","His heart pounds and some days he can't leave the house"],"ifasked":["Takes 3 or 4 blues a day for about 8 weeks; last one this morning","Gets shaky and sweaty if he misses a day; no fits","Drinks 4 cans of lager most evenings; no other drugs","Never had talking therapy; tried citalopram 2 years ago for a week and stopped because of nausea","Fleeting thoughts of 'what's the point'; no plans or intent; his child keeps him going","Wants to be able to take his child to school"],"worries":["Why should I suffer when there's a tablet that works?","Antidepressants are for depressed people","Am I an addict now?"],"rules":["Stands and raises his voice at the first refusal; sits if the doctor stays calm, names his anger and asks him to sit","If the doctor argues or lectures, heads for the door threatening a complaint","Becomes tearful about not taking his child to school; settles if validated","Agrees to sertraline only after dependence is explained without judgement"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Barker. I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors here. Your GP asked us to see you about anxiety. Where would you like to start?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Look, I'll save us both time. Diazepam is the only thing that works. I need a month's worth."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Okay. Before we get to that, tell me what the anxiety is like at the moment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"My heart pounds all day. Some days I can't get to the shop. The diazepam the out-of-hours doctor gave me in August was the first time I felt normal in years."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Living with that every day, then finding something that made you feel normal: I can see why you want it back.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"So you'll give it me?"},{"t":"1:05","band":"The no","w":"D","s":"I won't prescribe diazepam today. I know that's not what you want to hear. In the long run it makes anxiety worse and it's hard to come off. But I do want to help, and there are things I can do.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"(stands, louder) Unbelievable. You lot never listen! I'll put in a complaint. I'll go to the papers!"},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"(calm, seated) I can hear how angry and let down you feel. I do want to listen. Would you sit back down with me so we can sort out a plan?","m":[4,2]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"(pause, sits) Go on then."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And you have every right to complain. I'll give you the PALS details before you go. It won't change how I look after you.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Right. Fine."},{"t":"2:11","band":"The blues","w":"D","s":"Your GP mentioned you've been getting tablets online. How many a day, and since when?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Three, four a day. Blues. About two months. Last one this morning."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"What happens if you miss a day? Any shakes, sweats, or ever a fit?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Shaky and sweaty. Never had a fit."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"And alcohol, or anything else, like painkillers or pregabalin?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Four cans most nights. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being straight with me. Many tablets sold online as diazepam are actually stronger drugs, and you can't tell the dose. With alcohol on top, people have stopped breathing.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"They're from the same seller every time."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Even so, the batches change. And the shakes when you miss a day suggest your body is already used to them. That's dependence, and more diazepam from me would deepen it.","m":[8,3]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"(quieter) So I'm an addict now?"},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"No. It's what happens to anyone's body after weeks of daily use. You haven't done anything wrong; you were trying to cope. It just means we help you come off safely.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I just want to be able to take my kid to school."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"That really matters, and we'll work towards it. Can I ask: when things are this bad, do you ever feel it's not worth going on?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think, what's the point. I'd never do anything. My kid."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made any plans, or come close to acting on it?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"4:27","band":"What I can offer","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'd suggest. First, a tablet called sertraline. It's one of the main treatments for anxiety, and it doesn't cause dependence.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Antidepressants? I'm not depressed. And citalopram made me sick."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that way. It's used for anxiety too. We'd start low, 25 milligrams each morning, then 50 after a week. You might feel jittery or sick for the first week or two. It takes about four weeks to really help.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Four weeks. And in the meantime?"},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Two things. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies today for CBT. And I'll refer you to the drug and alcohol service this week, so they can help you cut down the blues safely.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Will they just tell me to stop?"},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"No. Stopping suddenly could be risky. They'll assess you and agree a safe plan, sometimes a prescribed reducing dose that they supervise.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's more than I thought."},{"t":"5:49","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Until then: don't take more than you do now, don't mix them with drink, and don't use alone. If you have a fit, call 999. If things feel desperate, ring 111 and choose option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Got it."},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've been clear. What's the plan?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Start sertraline, sign up for therapy, you refer me to the drug people. No diazepam."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly. No diazepam, but a real plan. I'll see you in two weeks to see how the sertraline's going, and I'll write to your GP. Here's the PALS leaflet too.","m":[12,3,11]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"Sorry for shouting."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying that. You've been under a lot of strain. See you in two weeks.","m":[2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, opens with an open question and hears his request without interrupting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and validates his distress before and after refusing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives one clear no to diazepam with a simple reason, and holds it calmly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Stays calm and seated when he stands, does not argue, and invites him to sit","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about street benzodiazepine use: amount, duration, last dose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about withdrawal symptoms, fits, alcohol and other drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains street 'blues' and dependence risk in plain, non-judgemental words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers sertraline with start dose, early side effects and time to work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers NHS Talking Therapies and a drug and alcohol service referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to the complaint threat with PALS details, without defensiveness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives harm reduction and crisis advice, checks understanding and sets a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Validates, says one clear no with a reason, explores street use and risk, offers sertraline, therapy and drug service, and handles the complaint calmly.","borderline":"Safe refusal but little validation or exploration, or a vague plan without doses or referrals.","fail":"Prescribes to end the conflict, argues or matches his anger, misses street use or suicide risk, or discourages a complaint."}},"c-m-09":{"setup":"Friday 16:10, acute ward. Mr Jordan Ellis, 34, detained under Section 3, has waited since Wednesday for his tribunal decision. It arrived today: he stays detained. His solicitor calls on Monday. Tell him, explain his options and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Ellis, 34, male patient detained under Section 3 (his named nurse, Sam, is present)","open":"Is it the tribunal? Just tell me. Am I going home?","manner":"Hopeful and tense, then shouts and stands at the news, then flat and tearful. Calms when his feelings are named; engages when he hears concrete options.","free":["He has waited since Wednesday","His solicitor told him he had a good chance","He feels well and wants to go home to his flat","He thinks the team 'told the tribunal what to say'"],"ifasked":["Last night he thought about tying something round his neck if he lost; did nothing and got nothing ready","No current plan or intent; his mother and his cat stop him","He has thought about walking off next time he is on ground leave","No thoughts of harming anyone, including the neighbour","His mother is feeding his cat and is his nearest relative","He takes olanzapine but hates the weight gain"],"worries":["When can I appeal again?","Is this going to go on forever?","So I'm locked in now as well?"],"rules":["Shouts and stands at the news; sits if the doctor stays calm and names his anger","If given legal detail before his feelings are acknowledged, says 'You're not listening'","Discloses last night's ligature thought only if asked directly about suicide","Agrees to stay on the ward and spend time with a nurse if offered concrete options and some control"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Jordan, it's Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. Sam's here with us. Can we sit in the quiet room for a few minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Is it the tribunal? Just tell me. Am I going home?"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"It's about the tribunal. Can I check first what your solicitor told you to expect?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"She said I had a good chance. I've done everything they asked."},{"t":"0:35","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"Jordan, I'm afraid it isn't the news you were hoping for.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"The tribunal has decided you should stay on Section 3 for now. I'm sorry.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"(stands, shouting) That's a joke! You lot told them what to say! I've done everything!"},{"t":"1:02","band":"His reaction","w":"D","s":"(pause) I can see how angry you are. You've waited since Wednesday, and you've worked hard. This feels really unfair.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"It is unfair. Ten weeks. I'm not ill."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"I hear you. Would you sit with us? I want to hear how you're feeling, and then tell you what you can do next.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"(sits, quieter) What's the point."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"The tribunal is independent of the ward. They read the reports, heard from you, and made their own decision. I know that doesn't make it easier.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"My solicitor didn't even ring."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"She's in court today. She'll call you on Monday to go through their written reasons with you.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"And then what? Another six months stuck here?"},{"t":"2:07","band":"Your options","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. There are things you can do. First, you can ask the Hospital Managers to review your section. That's a separate panel, and you can ask today. They can discharge you.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Today?"},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Yes. I can help you write the request now, and Sam will pass it to the Mental Health Act office. An advocate, called an IMHA, can help you prepare. I'll ask them to see you on Monday.","m":[6,8]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"And the tribunal? Can I go again?"},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"You can apply again once your section is renewed, in about four months. And if your solicitor thinks the tribunal made a legal mistake, she can ask to appeal. She'll advise you on that.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Four months. Great."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Your consultant can also give you more leave, or discharge you, at any time if things keep going well. I'll ask Dr Clarke to set out on Monday exactly what needs to happen.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"(quietly, tearful) I just want to go home. My mum's feeding my cat. I miss my own bed."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. Ten weeks away from home is hard. It's okay to feel gutted about this.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"I told the nurses if I lost there'd be no point any more."},{"t":"3:54","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've said that. When you say no point, are you having thoughts about ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Last night I thought about tying something round my neck. If I lost."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that wasn't easy to say. Did you do anything, or get anything ready?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"No. Just thought about it."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"And now, after the news? Any plan or intention to act on it?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"No. My mum would be in bits. And who'd have the cat."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Your mum and your cat matter a lot. Any thoughts of hurting anyone else, or of leaving the ward without permission?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"I did think about just walking off next time I'm on ground leave. But no. I'm not hurting anyone."},{"t":"5:03","band":"The weekend","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Here's what I suggest for this weekend. Sam and the night nurses will check on you every fifteen minutes and spend time with you. We'll pause ground leave and review it with you tomorrow. If the thoughts come back, tell a nurse straight away.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"So I'm locked in now as well."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I understand it feels like that. It's for now, while things are raw, and we'll look at it again tomorrow. Would you like to ring your mum tonight?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She'll want to know."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Sam can help with that. Your mum is your nearest relative and she has rights of her own too. The advocate can explain those on Monday.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check what you'll take from this? What happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"I can ask the managers for a hearing, there's an advocate Monday, my solicitor rings Monday, and I can go to the tribunal again after the renewal."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll write it down for you with your rights leaflet. And I'm handing over to the weekend doctor so they know how you're feeling.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Fine. Thanks for telling me straight."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you for talking with me, Jordan. I know this is hard. Sam will stay with you for a while.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and the nurse, and moves to a private room","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks what he knows and expects before giving the news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then the decision clearly in one sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Allows silence and names his anger and disappointment without defending the team","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the tribunal is independent, without blaming it or him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains a Hospital Managers' hearing can be requested at any time, and offers help","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains reapplication on renewal and appeal on a point of law via his solicitor","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers IMHA support and contact with solicitor and mother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, acts, plans and intent, including last night","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to others and about leaving the ward","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a weekend plan: observations, leave review, nurse support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives realistic hope: the RC can grant leave or discharge, with clear steps on Monday","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for tonight and closes kindly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gives the news early and clearly, sits with his anger, explains Managers' hearing, next tribunal and IMHA accurately, and assesses and manages suicide and absconding risk.","borderline":"Accurate news and rights but rushes past his feelings or does not ask directly about suicide.","fail":"Delays or hides the news, gives wrong appeal information, offers false hope, or misses the 'no point' risk."}},"c-m-10":{"setup":"Tuesday 13:30, CMHT office. Mrs Margaret Doyle, 72, on lithium and housebound, has vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse tremor and unsteadiness, and refuses A&E. Ring her GP, Dr Moss, for an urgent home visit and blood test.","role":{"who":"Dr Rachel Moss, 45, female GP partner at Mrs Doyle's practice","open":"I've got five minutes between patients. What is it?","manner":"Rushed, tired and defensive at first; pushes the task back to psychiatry and suggests A&E. Softens when her workload is acknowledged and the request is specific and shared; apologises for being short at the end.","free":["She has a full surgery and two visits already booked","She thinks psychiatry should manage lithium","She suggests A&E as the simplest option","She has not seen Mrs Doyle for six months"],"ifasked":["The practice has a paramedic practitioner who does visits","She could go herself after surgery, around 4pm","The duty GP receives critical results until 18:30; after that they go to out of hours","Mrs Doyle's eGFR was 58 last time","Mrs Doyle's daughter is listed as next of kin","The practice prefers plans confirmed by email to the urgent inbox"],"worries":["Why can't your team take the bloods?","Can it wait until tomorrow?","Who's checking the result if it comes back late?"],"rules":["Says no to a vague request; agrees to a clear, specific request with a time","Becomes curt if lectured about duty; softens if her workload is acknowledged","Agrees to visit only once roles for results and follow-up are clear","Asks for the plan in writing before ending the call"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Dr Moss, it's Dr Iqbal, CT2 with the North community mental health team. Is now okay for a quick, urgent call?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I've got five minutes between patients. What is it?"},{"t":"0:19","band":"Situation","w":"D","s":"I'm calling about Margaret Doyle, 72. I'm worried she may have lithium toxicity, and I need your help today.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. Go on."},{"t":"0:33","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"She has bipolar disorder and takes lithium 600 milligrams at night. Her level three months ago was 0.7. She's housebound since her hip fracture.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"0:49","band":"Assessment","w":"D","s":"Her CPN is with her now. She's had diarrhoea and vomiting for three days, a coarse tremor, and she's unsteady. She's been taking ibuprofen from the chemist for her hip.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Is she confused?"},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"No, she's alert and oriented, and drinking a little. But she's unsteady on her frame, and I'm worried her level is high and her kidneys are struggling.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Sounds like she needs A&E."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"I agree that's the safest place, and the CPN offered. She's refused, and the CPN thinks she has capacity for that decision. She will accept a blood test at home.","m":[7]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Then I don't know what you want from me. Can't your team deal with it? Lithium's yours."},{"t":"1:54","band":"Recommendation","w":"D","s":"I hear you, and I know you're flat out. Here's exactly what I'm asking: could someone visit her today, by four, to assess her and take an urgent lithium level and U&E?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"We've no visits left. I've forty patients and two visits already."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"That's a huge day. I'm sorry to add to it.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Can't it wait till the morning? We could send the phlebotomist."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"My worry is that she's still being sick, so her level could keep rising overnight, and at 72 she can become toxic quickly. If she gets confused tonight, it's an emergency.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"And why can't your team take the bloods?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"She needs a medical assessment of her fluids and kidneys too, which we can't do at home. Here's what we'll do: the CPN stays till a visit's confirmed, she holds tonight's lithium and stops the ibuprofen, and I'll ring her tomorrow morning.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"(pause) We do have a paramedic practitioner. Or I could go after surgery, about four."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"That would be brilliant, thank you. Four o'clock, you, at her home. Could the bloods be lithium, U&E with eGFR and calcium, marked urgent, with her last dose time on the form? That was nine last night.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Fine. Her eGFR was 58 last time, by the way."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's useful. Any drop from that would worry me too.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Who's chasing the result? It won't be back before six."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Who does what","w":"D","s":"Can we agree that now? Who gets the result this evening?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"The duty GP gets critical results till half six. After that, it goes to out of hours."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Then could you ask the lab to phone the duty GP and flag her to out of hours? If the level is 1.5 or more, or her kidneys are worse, she needs admission. She may agree once she knows the result.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Agreed. And if it's normal?"},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Then lithium stays stopped until our consultant reviews her tomorrow and advises on the dose. Could you send the result to our team inbox?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. What should she do in the meantime?"},{"t":"5:10","band":"Red flags","w":"D","s":"The CPN will leave fluids within reach and tell her and her daughter: if she becomes confused, can't stand, has slurred speech, keeps vomiting or has a fit, call 999.","m":[10,8]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"Fine. Remind them about the ibuprofen."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"Will do. Paracetamol for the hip instead.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Okay. Anything else?"},{"t":"5:40","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me read it back. You visit at four and take urgent lithium, U&E and calcium. Duty GP gets results till half six, then out of hours. Level 1.5 or more, or worse kidneys, means admission. We stop lithium and ibuprofen now, the CPN stays till you're confirmed, and I ring her at nine tomorrow.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"I'll put that in our notes and email it to your urgent inbox within the hour, with her medication list.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Please do. Mark it urgent so reception flags it."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"I will. Thanks so much, Dr Moss. I know it's a busy day.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"No problem. Sorry I was short. It's been one of those days."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Completely understand. Thanks for fitting her in.","m":[6]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, role and team, and checks the GP can talk now","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States the situation and the worry of lithium toxicity in the first sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives background: diagnosis, lithium dose, last level, housebound, kidney function","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives assessment: vomiting, diarrhoea, coarse tremor, unsteadiness, ibuprofen, alertness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Makes one specific request: a visit today by a set time with urgent lithium level and U&E","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Acknowledges the GP's workload and frustration without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why A&E is not happening and why it cannot wait until tomorrow","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers what the CMHT will do: CPN stays, lithium held, ibuprofen stopped, follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees who receives results, when, and the action thresholds","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees red flags and the route to 999 or admission","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reads back the plan with owners and times","d":"Close"},{"c":"Commits to document and email a summary, and thanks the GP","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States the concern first, uses SBAR, makes one specific timed request, handles reluctance with empathy, and agrees owners, results and red flags with a read-back.","borderline":"Secures a visit but leaves results or red flags vague, or takes too long to say what is wanted.","fail":"No clear request, argues about whose job it is, accepts tomorrow without stating the risk, or no documentation."}},"c-s-02":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, CMHT clinic. Ella Morgan, 21, did not attend her first appointment; her mother, Mrs Sarah Morgan, has come instead. Ella has agreed by phone that you can talk to her mother. Take a collateral history and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Mrs Sarah Morgan, 52, female, Ella's mother","open":"I'm so sorry. She just couldn't get out of bed this morning. I hope it's all right that I came instead.","manner":"Apologetic and anxious, eager to help, speaks quickly. Becomes tearful when her husband's death comes up; steadies when acknowledged. Relieved when given a clear plan.","free":["Ella was fine until her boyfriend Josh ended the relationship about ten months ago","Low nearly all day, every day; has dropped netball, friends and music","In bed until 2 p.m. most days with no energy","Eats a slice of toast in the morning and half a dinner; about 6 kg lost since the summer"],"ifasked":["Lies awake until after midnight, then wakes at 5 a.m. and cannot get back to sleep","Talks and moves more slowly than before","Can't concentrate to read; deferred her university course","Blames herself for the break-up, calls herself useless, sees no future","Last week said she wished she wouldn't wake up; no known plan or self-harm","Paracetamol in the kitchen; GP prescribed sertraline, not started","Father had depression and took his own life when Ella was 14","Showers about weekly; lost her café job; not left the house for two weeks"],"worries":["Is she anorexic?","Should she make Ella take the tablets?","Will Ella be told what she said?","What should she do if things get worse tonight?"],"rules":["Gives eating details only if asked specifically about weight, shape and purging","Becomes tearful about her husband's suicide; settles if validated and given time","Has never noticed anything odd, such as voices or strange beliefs, or any high spells","Calms down once she hears there will be contact with Ella today"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Ella's consent and explains limits of what can be shared","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Thanks the mother and acknowledges her worry before and during questioning","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, trigger, duration and worsening course","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits core symptoms: low mood, loss of interest and low energy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits sleep with early waking, appetite, weight loss and slowing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits concentration, guilt, worthlessness and hopelessness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about intake, weight and shape concerns and purging to exclude an eating disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic and manic features and substance use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about death wishes, plans, self-harm, access to means and risk to others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks family history and responds with empathy to the father's suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about previous episodes, GP tests and treatment so far","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes impact on self-care, work and study","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises likely depression and agrees urgent contact, safety advice and crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks the mother's understanding, offers carer support and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Morgan? I'm Dr Chen, one of the psychiatry doctors. Thank you for coming in. I hear Ella couldn't face it today.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"I'm so sorry. She just couldn't get out of bed this morning. I hope it's all right that I came instead."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Of course, and I'm glad you did. Ella phoned yesterday and said we can talk to you. So I can listen to everything and share general information; anything she tells us privately, I'd check with her first. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine. I just want her to get help."},{"t":"0:39","band":"Her story","w":"D","s":"Tell me what's been happening, in your words.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"She was fine until about ten months ago. Josh, her boyfriend, ended it. I thought she'd bounce back, but she's got worse and worse."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"So getting steadily worse over ten months. Has she ever been like this before?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Never. She was always the sociable one."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"How is her mood most days now? And does she still enjoy anything?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Flat. Low nearly all day, every day. Netball, friends, music, she's dropped the lot."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"And her energy?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"None. She's in bed till two in the afternoon."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"How is she sleeping?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"She goes up at ten but lies awake till past midnight. Then I hear her up at five and she can't get back off."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"And her eating?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"That's what frightens me. She says food tastes of nothing. A slice of toast in the morning, then half a dinner. She's lost about six kilos since the summer."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Eating or depression?","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening to watch. Can I ask a few specific questions? Does she talk about her weight or shape, or seem to want to be thinner?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"No, not at all. She doesn't care. She's just not hungry."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Any sign of making herself sick, using laxatives, exercising hard, or eating large amounts in secret?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"No. She barely moves, let alone exercises."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Has she seemed slowed down, in how she moves or talks?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Now you say it, yes. She talks slowly. Takes ages to answer."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Her thinking","w":"D","s":"How is her concentration? And what does she say about herself, or the future?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"She can't read a page; that's why she deferred uni. She says the break-up was her fault, she's useless, there's no point planning anything."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Has she ever heard things others can't, said anything that seemed strange, or had spells of being unusually high? Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Nothing like that. She drank a lot at uni, hardly anything now. Drugs, I honestly don't know."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"These next questions are hard, but important. Has she ever said she wishes she wasn't here, or talked about dying?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Last week she said she wished she wouldn't wake up."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Has she talked about ending her life, made any plans, or hurt herself in any way?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of. I haven't seen any marks."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"What medicines are in the house? And has she ever been angry towards Josh, or anyone else?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Paracetamol in the kitchen, and the sertraline the GP gave her. She hasn't started it. She's never angry at Josh. She blames herself."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in the family had depression or other mental health problems?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"Her dad. He had depression. He took his own life when Ella was fourteen."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a huge loss for you both, and I can see why this is so frightening now. Take your time.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking, what if it's happening again?"},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense. It's also exactly why I'm glad you came; it helps us act quickly.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Has the GP done any tests?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Bloods three weeks ago, thyroid and everything. All normal."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"How is she managing day to day: washing, getting out, work?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"She showers maybe once a week if I push. Lost her café job. Hasn't left the house in a fortnight."},{"t":"5:06","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really clear. This sounds like depression, and a fairly severe one: low mood, no interest or energy, early waking, weight loss, slowing, feeling worthless and hopeless. It doesn't sound like an eating disorder.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"So what happens now?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"Because of the weight loss, her wish not to wake up and her dad's history, I don't want to wait. We'll ring Ella today, and with her agreement two of us will see her at home within two days.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Is there anything I should do?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Please lock away the paracetamol and the sertraline tonight. If she talks about ending her life, makes plans, or you're worried she isn't safe, ring the crisis line, or NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or 999 in an emergency. I'll write the numbers down.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me back what you'll do tonight, so I know I've been clear?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"Lock the tablets away. If she says anything about ending things, ring the crisis line, or 999 if it's urgent."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. And you're carrying a lot. You can ask the council for a carer's assessment, and I'll give you details of the local carers' service. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for listening."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. We'll ring Ella this afternoon.","m":[14]}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms consent, builds a full depressive picture including slowing, rules out an eating disorder, elicits the father's suicide, and leaves the mother with an urgent plan and safety advice.","borderline":"Good symptom history but thin on eating attitudes or risk to others, or a vague plan without timing.","fail":"Refuses to engage over confidentiality, misses risk or the family history of suicide, or rebooks routinely with no safety advice."}},"c-s-03":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, acute inpatient ward. Grace Mensah, 31, an informal patient with schizophrenia, has refused her last two doses of haloperidol and says it is 'driving her mad'. Explore her concern, work out what is happening, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Grace Mensah, 31, female, patient","open":"Everyone keeps telling me to take it. Nobody's listening. This tablet is driving me mad.","manner":"Tense and irritable, cannot stay seated; stands, walks a few steps, sits again. Softens quickly if taken seriously. Withdraws and asks for the consultant if she feels pressured to take the tablet.","free":["Can't keep still; feels wound up inside","Changed to haloperidol from aripiprazole three weeks ago","Refuses to take any more haloperidol at the current dose","Wants a medicine she can stay on long term"],"ifasked":["Dose increased to 10 mg daily last week; restlessness began a few days later","Feels like ants in her legs plus a tension she can't describe","Worst when queuing for medication; can't sleep because she can't settle","No stiffness, tremor or spasms; not worse at night lying down","Not worried about anything else; mood fine apart from this; voices have gone","Two cups of tea a day, no drugs; no other medicines; no asthma or heart problems","If this never stopped she would rather be dead; no plan or intent"],"worries":["Is the illness coming back?","Will the voices return if the dose is lowered?","Doesn't want to go back to aripiprazole, and worries olanzapine causes weight gain"],"rules":["Stands and rocks foot to foot if asked to stand still","Becomes tearful when talking about not wanting to live like this; settles if validated","Becomes irritable and withdrawn if told she must take the tablet","Accepts a lower dose if offered something for the restlessness"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, lets her move, and asks for her concern in her own words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes the complaint seriously and validates her distress without pushing the tablet","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the switch, dose increase, timing of onset and other medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits inner restlessness and where and when it is worst","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observes visible restlessness by asking her to stand still briefly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about stiffness, tremor, spasms and night-time restless legs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Distinguishes from anxiety, low mood and relapse; asks about caffeine and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, and their link to the restlessness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names akathisia and explains it plainly as a real, treatable side effect","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers dose reduction, switch and propranolol, checking for asthma and heart problems","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a plan she accepts, with monitoring and consultant and nurse involvement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets and closes with a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Grace, I'm Dr Patel, one of the ward doctors. Please don't feel you have to sit still; walk about if it helps. The nurses said the new tablet is bothering you. What's happening?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Everyone keeps telling me to take it. Nobody's listening. This tablet is driving me mad."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm listening now, and I'm not here to push you into anything. It sounds awful. Tell me what it's doing to you.","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I can't keep still. Inside I'm wound up like a spring. I've got to keep moving."},{"t":"0:39","band":"The medicine","w":"D","s":"When did this start, and what's changed with your tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"They swapped my aripiprazole for haloperidol three weeks ago. Then upped it last week. This started a few days after."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"What dose are you on now, and when did you last take it? Any other medicines?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Ten milligrams. I haven't had it since Sunday morning. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:10","band":"What it feels like","w":"D","s":"Where do you feel it most?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"My legs. Like ants crawling inside. And this horrible tension all over. I can't describe it."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"When is it worst?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"In the queue for meds. I can't stand there. I have to walk off. And I can't settle to sleep."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Would you try something for me? Stand still for a minute while I watch, and tell me how it feels.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"See? I can't. I'm going from foot to foot already."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps. I can see you rocking and shifting your weight. Sit or walk, whatever's easier.","m":[5,2]},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Any stiffness, shaking, or muscle spasms, say in your neck or your eyes?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"No. It's not stiff. It's the restlessness."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Is it worse at night lying in bed, with an urge to move your legs that eases when you walk?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No, it's all day. Night's bad because I can't settle."},{"t":"2:26","band":"What it isn't","w":"D","s":"Some people mistake this for worry, or the illness coming back. Are you anxious about things, or is it more in your body?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"It's my body. I'm not worried about anything, except this."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? And the voices you had before?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine apart from this. The voices have gone. The haloperidol did work for that. That's the annoying bit."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Any coffee, energy drinks, cannabis or other drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Two teas a day. No drugs."},{"t":"3:05","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"When something feels this unbearable, people sometimes think about not wanting to live. Has that crossed your mind?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"If this never stopped, yes. I'd rather be dead than feel like this for years."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; it shows how bad this is. Do you have any plans, or thoughts of acting on it?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"No. I want to get better. I just need it to stop."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"So those thoughts come from the restlessness, not from feeling low?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Exactly. I was fine before this."},{"t":"3:48","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think you have akathisia. It's a known side effect of some antipsychotics, especially haloperidol, and especially after a dose goes up. It causes that inner tension and the need to move. It's not you relapsing, and it's not in your head.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad?"},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"No. It's real and physical, and it's treatable. It usually eases within days of the right change.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are three main options. We can lower the haloperidol dose, which often helps, though we'd watch for the voices. We can switch to a different medicine less likely to do this. Or we can add propranolol, a low dose of a heart tablet that calms akathisia. We can combine them.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"I don't want the voices back. And I don't want to go back to aripiprazole."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"That's fair. Do you have asthma, or any heart problems?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Then one plan is: haloperidol down to 5 milligrams, propranolol 10 milligrams three times a day, the nurses check your pulse and blood pressure before doses, and we see how you are in three days. If it's not much better, we talk about switching, for example to olanzapine.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"Olanzapine, doesn't that make you put on weight?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"It can, so we'd weigh you and check your bloods, and it would be your choice after we talk it through. Does the first step sound acceptable?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll take the 5 milligrams if I get the propranolol."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Good. I'll go through it with Dr Okoro, your consultant, this afternoon. I'll also let the nurse in charge know about those thoughts, so the team can check in with you.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"OK. That's fine."},{"t":"6:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"It's akathisia, not the illness. Lower dose, propranolol, pulse checks, and a switch if it doesn't work."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If the restlessness gets worse, you feel dizzy or faint, or those thoughts get stronger, tell a nurse straight away, day or night. I'll see you again on Thursday.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. You're the first one who's believed me."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told us. I'll get the leaflet on akathisia for you now.","m":[12]}],"global":{"pass":"Listens without pushing the tablet, nails the timeline and both parts of akathisia, rules out look-alikes, asks about suicidal thoughts, and agrees a plan she accepts.","borderline":"Recognises akathisia but thin on look-alikes or risk, or imposes a plan rather than agreeing it.","fail":"Calls it anxiety or relapse, pushes adherence, offers an anticholinergic or sedation only, or never asks about suicidal thoughts."}},"c-s-04":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, psychiatric outpatient clinic. Corporal Ryan Doyle, 29, was diagnosed with OCD last week (Y-BOCS 26/40). He has come back to learn about the diagnosis and the treatment options. Explain both and agree next steps.","role":{"who":"Corporal Ryan Doyle, 29, male, patient","open":"Thanks for seeing me again. I've read a bit since last week. I suppose I just want to know: is this just who I am now?","manner":"Calm, polite and engaged, a little embarrassed. Brightens with practical explanations. Becomes sceptical if spoken to in jargon.","free":["Agrees with the diagnosis; has read about OCD online","Can't stop thinking he carries germs that will make his partner and baby daughter ill","Washes his hands and uniform for hours; relief never lasts","Keen for help but unsure if anyone really gets better"],"ifasked":["Thinks OCD is 'just who he is' because he was an anxious child","Knows the fear is excessive but can't resist washing","Hands are cracked and sore","Worried therapy means talking about his childhood, which was happy","Not depressed; worried antidepressants are addictive","No asthma, epilepsy, bleeding problems or other medicines"],"worries":["Could he wash 'properly' once and be rid of the thoughts?","Could he have antibiotics 'just in case'?","Are antidepressants addictive, and why take one if he isn't depressed?","Will he get back to his unit?"],"rules":["Becomes embarrassed when describing his fears about his baby; settles if this is normalised","Accepts clear reasons why antibiotics and 'proper washing' would not help","Asks 'what does that mean?' if jargon is used","Wants to read about sertraline before starting, and appreciates that being respected"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sets the agenda and asks what he knows and wants from today","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his ideas about the cause and separates OCD from his identity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains obsessions in plain words and normalises intrusive thoughts","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains compulsions and the relief-then-return cycle that maintains OCD","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the severity score and gives honest hope about recovery","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ERP: what it involves, how often, who delivers it, and homework","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the SSRI: name, starting dose, time to work and common side effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses addiction worry: not addictive, but taper to avoid discontinuation symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends combined treatment for severe OCD and gives the reason","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains why 'proper washing' and antibiotics would feed the cycle","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates embarrassment and responds to his worries about therapy and work","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and invites questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees next steps, gives written information and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Corporal Doyle, I'm Dr Hughes, one of the psychiatrists working with Dr Rahman. Thanks for coming back. Today I'd like to explain the diagnosis and the treatments. What would you like to get out of it?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me again. I've read a bit since last week. I suppose I just want to know: is this just who I am now?"},{"t":"0:26","band":"His understanding","w":"D","s":"That's a really important question. What do you already know about OCD, and what do you think caused it?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Thoughts that won't go away, and you do things to stop them. I was an anxious kid, so I figured this is just me."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Being an anxious child can make OCD more likely, but it isn't your personality and it isn't your fault. It's a condition, and it's very treatable.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"That's good to hear."},{"t":"1:02","band":"The cycle","w":"D","s":"Can I draw something? Almost everyone gets unwanted thoughts that pop into their head. In OCD a thought like 'I'm carrying germs' sticks, and it brings a wave of anxiety.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"That's exactly it. And I know it's daft, but I can't shake it."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"It's not daft; it's what OCD does. How do you feel when you think about the baby?","m":[11]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Ashamed. What sort of dad thinks he'll make his daughter ill?"},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"A dad who loves her. OCD latches on to what matters most to you. That's why the thoughts are so upsetting.","m":[11,3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"I hadn't thought of it like that."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"So the thought brings anxiety. Washing brings quick relief, and your brain learns that washing kept everyone safe. So next time the thought comes back stronger, and the washing gets longer.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"The relief never lasts. Ten minutes, maybe."},{"t":"2:11","band":"The score","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Treatment breaks that loop. Your score last week was 26 out of 40, which is in the severe range. That sounds alarming, but it tells us which treatment works best, and most people get a lot better.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"So it's bad, but it can be fixed?"},{"t":"2:33","band":"Therapy","w":"D","s":"Yes. There are two main treatments. The first is a talking therapy called CBT with exposure and response prevention, or ERP.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"I'm not lying on a couch talking about my childhood. My childhood was fine."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"It's nothing like that. ERP is practical and about now. With a therapist you build a ladder of things you fear, like touching a door handle without washing, and climb it step by step.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Without washing? That sounds horrible."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"It's hard at first. The anxiety rises, peaks, then falls on its own, and each time it falls faster. Sessions are weekly, about an hour, with practice at home, and you set the pace with the therapist.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"OK. I could try that."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"The second is a medicine called an SSRI, for example sertraline. It turns the anxiety down and treats OCD directly, whether or not you're depressed.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"I'm not depressed though. And aren't antidepressants addictive?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Fair question. They're not addictive: no craving and no needing more to get the same effect. If you stop suddenly, you can get dizziness or feel flu-ish, so we always reduce slowly.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"How quickly does it work?"},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"We'd start 50 milligrams each morning and often go higher for OCD. It takes a few weeks to start and up to twelve weeks to work fully. Early on, some people feel sick or a bit jittery for a week or two. If your mood ever drops or you get dark thoughts, tell us straight away.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Which one should I do?"},{"t":"4:34","band":"Combined","w":"D","s":"For OCD at your level, the guidelines and the evidence say both together work best. Therapy breaks the habit; the medicine makes the anxiety easier to face.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Can I ask something? If I washed properly, just once, really properly, would the thoughts finally stop?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"I can see why that feels like the answer. But every wash, however thorough, teaches your brain the danger was real. The thought would come back.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"What about antibiotics, just in case I have picked something up?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"I understand the pull of 'just in case'. That's the OCD asking for certainty. Antibiotics wouldn't help, they have side effects, and they'd feed the loop. Treatment teaches your brain to live with not being certain.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I suppose that makes sense."},{"t":"5:34","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words how the cycle works, and what the two treatments do?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Thought, panic, wash, relief, and it comes back worse. ERP stops the washing bit. The tablet turns the anxiety down. Both is best."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"That's spot on. Any questions?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Will I get back to my unit?"},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Many people return to full duties once the OCD is under control. If you're happy for me to, I'll write to your medical officer so they can plan a phased return with you.","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine. I'd like to read about the tablet before I start."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"That's sensible. I'll refer you for ERP today and give you a leaflet on sertraline. If you decide to start, we'll see you two weeks later to check how you're getting on.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I feel a lot more hopeful."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. This is very treatable. See you soon.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Explains the OCD cycle clearly, separates OCD from his identity, explains ERP and the SSRI accurately, recommends combined treatment, and handles the antibiotic and addiction worries.","borderline":"Accurate but lecture-style, with little exploration of his beliefs or no teach-back.","fail":"Jargon-heavy or inaccurate (SSRIs addictive, work in days), omits ERP, or agrees to antibiotics or reassurance."}},"c-s-05":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, psychiatric outpatient clinic. Mr Daniel Whitfield, 34, an accountant, was referred by his GP with 'sudden, unbearable terrors'. His heart tests were normal. Take a history, reach a diagnosis and tell him what you think.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Whitfield, 34, male, patient","open":"I'm not sure I should be here, to be honest. I thought it was my heart. I just don't want to be fobbed off.","manner":"Calm and articulate but wary of being dismissed. Embarrassed describing the 'going mad' feeling. Relaxes once the doctor explains what is happening in his body.","free":["Sudden 'bouts of terror' for about two years, roughly once a week","Feels like he is going to die; thought it was his heart","GP did an ECG, a 24-hour heart monitor and blood tests, all normal","The attacks are making his life miserable"],"ifasked":["Starts with a dry mouth, then pounding heart, breathlessness, dizziness, sweating, tingling lips and fingers","Peaks within a few minutes; lasts 15 to 30 minutes","At the peak the world feels unreal and he fears going mad; desperate to get out","Comes out of the blue: at his desk, on the sofa, once woke him up","Worries constantly about the next one; stopped going to the gym; turned down a promotion","No problem with crowds, queues, trains or going out alone; fine socially; not a worrier generally","No obsessions, rituals or trauma; mood good; never suicidal; no voices","No medicines; four coffees and an energy drink a day; a few beers at weekends; no drugs"],"worries":["Have the tests missed a heart problem?","Is he going mad?","Can he have something to take when an attack starts?"],"rules":["Becomes guarded if told it is 'just anxiety' without explanation","Gets embarrassed describing the unreal feeling; settles if it is normalised","Gives caffeine amounts only if asked for numbers","Visibly relieved when the overbreathing explanation is given"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, validates his fear of being dismissed, and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits a typical attack: first symptom, speed to peak, duration and frequency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the physical symptoms of an attack","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits catastrophic thoughts and derealisation, and normalises them","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes attacks are unexpected and asks about worry and avoidance between attacks","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for agoraphobia: crowds, queues, transport, being out alone","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for generalised anxiety, social anxiety, OCD and trauma","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, enjoyment, sleep and suicidal thoughts, and psychotic symptoms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks GP tests, medicines and inhalers to exclude physical causes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about caffeine, alcohol and drugs in amounts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the impact on work and daily life","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names panic disorder and explains it plainly via the body's alarm and overbreathing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding, outlines next step and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Good morning Mr Whitfield, I'm Dr Adebayo, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming. Your GP says you've been having some frightening attacks.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I'm not sure I should be here, to be honest. I thought it was my heart. I just don't want to be fobbed off."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable, and I won't fob you off. These sound really frightening. I'd like to go through them in detail. Tell me about them in your words.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Out of nowhere I'm terrified. Like I'm about to die. It's been going on for two years."},{"t":"0:43","band":"The attack","w":"D","s":"Take me through the last one, from the very first thing you noticed.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"I was at my desk. My mouth went dry, then my heart started pounding. I couldn't get a breath."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"What else happened in your body?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Dizzy. Sweating. And weirdly my lips and fingers tingled."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"How quickly does it build, how long does it last, and how often do they come?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"It peaks in a few minutes. Fifteen, maybe thirty minutes in all. About once a week."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"At the worst point, what goes through your mind?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"That I'm having a heart attack. That I'm going to die. And sometimes, this sounds stupid, the world doesn't feel real. Like I'm going mad."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"It doesn't sound stupid at all. That unreal feeling is very common at the peak of intense fear, and it isn't madness. It passes as the attack settles.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Really? I've never told anyone that."},{"t":"2:04","band":"Pattern and avoidance","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Where are you when they happen? Is there a pattern?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"No pattern. At work, on the sofa watching TV. Once it woke me up."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Between attacks, do you worry about the next one? Is there anything you've stopped doing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"I worry constantly. I stopped going to the gym because my heart races. And I turned down a promotion. I couldn't risk an attack in front of my team."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"That's a big loss, and I'm sorry they've cost you that.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Do you avoid crowds, queues, public transport, open spaces, or being far from home on your own?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"No, none of that. I'm fine on the train, in shops."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Other anxiety","w":"D","s":"Are you a worrier in general, about everyday things?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Not really. Before all this, I was pretty laid-back."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"How are you meeting new people or speaking in front of others?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Fine. I used to present to clients without a problem."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Do you get thoughts you have to cancel out, or things you have to check or repeat? And has anything very frightening or traumatic ever happened to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"No to both."},{"t":"3:35","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Are you still enjoying things, and how's your sleep?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"Mood's good. I enjoy life, apart from this. Sleep's OK."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had thoughts about ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"And outside the attacks, have you heard voices when nobody's there, or felt something strange was going on around you?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"4:06","band":"Physical and substances","w":"D","s":"What tests did your GP do?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"An ECG, a heart monitor for 24 hours, and bloods, thyroid and everything. All normal."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Any medicines, inhalers or supplements?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"None."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"How much coffee, tea or energy drinks a day? And alcohol, or any other drugs?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Four coffees. An energy drink in the afternoon in busy season. A few beers at the weekend. No drugs."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"How are things at work and at home overall?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"I'm coping, but it's ruining things. I'm on edge all the time."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really clear. Can I share what I think is going on? This is panic disorder. Your body's alarm system, the one that gets you ready to fight or run, is going off when there's no danger.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"So it's not my heart?"},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Your tests say your heart is healthy. When the alarm goes off, adrenaline makes your heart pound and you breathe fast. Overbreathing causes the tingling and dizziness. Then the thought 'heart attack' pushes the alarm even higher. It's horrible, but it isn't dangerous.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"That actually makes sense. Can I have something to take when it starts?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting that. Tablets like diazepam make panic worse in the long run and can be habit-forming. The best treatment is a short course of CBT for panic. And the caffeine may be adding fuel, so cut down gradually.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly, can you tell me in your words what's happening?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"My alarm goes off for no reason, the breathing makes it worse, it's not my heart, and therapy helps."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll refer you for CBT for panic, write to your GP, and see you in eight weeks. Any other questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I feel taken seriously."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You were right to come. See you soon.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Describes the attacks in detail, confirms they are unexpected with fear of recurrence, screens agoraphobia, other anxiety disorders, mood and psychosis, excludes physical and substance causes, and explains panic disorder plainly.","borderline":"Describes the attacks well but misses agoraphobia, caffeine or mood screening, or gives a thin explanation.","fail":"Calls it 'just anxiety', misreads derealisation as psychosis, omits physical and substance causes, or offers a benzodiazepine."}},"c-s-06":{"setup":"Acute ward, Tuesday afternoon. Rebecca Doyle, 34, has her third depressive episode; amitriptyline 150 mg has helped a little. The team want to add lithium. Explain it and answer her questions. No history needed.","role":{"who":"Ms Rebecca Doyle, 34, woman, informal inpatient with recurrent depression","open":"Still really low, to be honest. And confused. The nurse said they want to give me lithium.","manner":"Quiet, polite and low, asks a question at each pause. Becomes tearful about her father; settles and engages more once her worries are taken seriously.","free":["Heard lithium is for 'manic depression'","Third episode of depression in three years","Amitriptyline has helped only a little","Dislikes needles"],"ifasked":["Takes ibuprofen for period pain a few days most months","Father died of kidney failure after years of poorly controlled diabetes","Plans a baby with her partner in about two years","Uses condoms only","Had thoughts of ending her life before admission, none now","No other regular medicines"],"worries":["Does this mean I'm bipolar?","Will it damage my kidneys like my dad's?","Can I take it when I'm pregnant, and can it harm the baby?","I read it can poison you."],"rules":["Raises her father's kidney failure only once long-term side effects are mentioned","Becomes tearful about her father; settles if validated","Mentions the baby plan only if asked about pregnancy or when toxicity is discussed","Wants to decide after seeing her blood results"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Rebecca, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the ward doctors. I'm sorry you missed the end of the ward round. How are you feeling now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Still really low, to be honest. And confused. The nurse said they want to give me lithium."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to take in when you're feeling this low. What have you heard about lithium?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Only that it's for manic depression. Do they think I'm bipolar now?"},{"t":"0:33","band":"Why lithium","w":"D","s":"That's a very common worry, and no, your diagnosis hasn't changed. Lithium is used in bipolar disorder, but it's also a well-tested add-on for depression that keeps coming back.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"You've now had three episodes in three years, while amitriptyline has helped only a little. Adding lithium often gives it the extra push it needs. You'd keep taking the amitriptyline.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"So it's like a booster. How long before I'd notice anything?"},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Usually within a few weeks of reaching the right level. Does that make sense so far?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. Okay."},{"t":"1:14","band":"Tests before starting","w":"D","s":"Before the first dose we'd do a blood test to check your kidneys, thyroid and calcium, and a blood count. We'd check your weight and do a heart tracing, an ECG.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"We'd also do a pregnancy test and go through every tablet you take, including anything from the chemist. Do you take anything else?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Just ibuprofen for period pains. Most months, for a few days."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really useful. Ibuprofen can push the lithium level up, so we'd swap it for paracetamol, which is safe.","m":[8]},{"t":"1:53","band":"Levels","w":"P","s":"Will I need blood tests all the time?"},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"More at the start. We'd begin with one tablet at night, and check the level after a week. The test is taken 12 hours after your dose, so mid-morning if you take it at ten at night.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"We check weekly after any change until it's steady, and three-monthly after that. Kidneys, thyroid and calcium are checked every six months.","m":[4,6]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"That's a lot of needles. I hate needles."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"I know it's a lot, and I'm sorry it's unpleasant. Those tests are what keep lithium safe, so I'd rather be honest with you about them now.","m":[9,4]},{"t":"2:40","band":"Side effects","w":"P","s":"What about side effects?"},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Early on, some people feel a bit sick, get loose stools, a metallic taste, feel thirsty and pass more urine, or have a fine shake of the hands. These usually ease after a few weeks.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Over the longer term, it can slow the thyroid, cause some weight gain and, in some people over years, reduce how well the kidneys work. That's why we keep checking.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"The kidneys. My dad died of kidney failure. I watched him on dialysis for two years."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry, Rebecca. That must have been really hard to watch, and I can see why this worries you. Do you know what caused his kidney failure?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"He had diabetes for a long time. He didn't look after it."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"That helps. Diabetes is a common cause, and it isn't something you inherit from lithium. We'd check your kidneys before starting, then every six months. If they show any change, we'd review the lithium straight away.","m":[9,6]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's better than I thought."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Toxicity","w":"D","s":"The other important thing is the level getting too high. Signs are being sick or having diarrhoea, a coarse shake, feeling unsteady, slurred speech, or being drowsy or confused.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"If that happens, stop taking the lithium and get a blood level that day. If you're very unwell, go to the emergency department.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"I read online that it can poison you."},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"You're right that too much is dangerous, which is exactly why we check. Dehydration, hot weather, stomach bugs, ibuprofen, and some blood pressure and water tablets all push the level up.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"And please don't stop it suddenly unless you have those signs. If we ever stop it, we reduce slowly over at least four weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:47","band":"Suicide and safety","w":"D","s":"Lithium also lowers suicide risk in people with mood problems. Can I ask, are you having any thoughts about ending your life at the moment?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Not now. I did before I came in."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Both tablets are dangerous in large amounts, so after discharge we'd give a week's supply at a time to start with.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:15","band":"Pregnancy","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about pregnancy? Is that something you're planning, now or later?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"We want a baby in about two years. Can I take it while I'm pregnant? Will it harm the baby?"},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"It's a really important question. Lithium carries a small extra risk of heart problems in the baby. Most babies are fine, but we'd plan any pregnancy together first.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Until then, reliable contraception matters. What are you using now?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Condoms, mostly."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"It's worth talking to your GP about something more reliable. If you did get pregnant unexpectedly, don't stop lithium; ring us that day. And breastfeeding isn't advised while you're taking it.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Right. That's a lot to think about."},{"t":"6:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"It is a lot. To check I've explained it well, can you tell me which signs mean you should stop it and have a blood test?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Being sick or diarrhoea, a bad shake, feeling wobbly or confused."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Here's the lithium booklet, a record book for your levels and an alert card. Would you like to start, or would you rather think it over?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"Can I have the blood tests first and decide when I see the results?"},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible plan. We'll do the tests today, and the consultant and I will go through the results with you on Thursday. Ask any of the nurses if questions come up before then.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks how she is and asks what she has heard about lithium","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains augmentation for recurrent depression and that the diagnosis is unchanged","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Lists baseline tests: kidneys, thyroid, calcium, blood count, weight, ECG, pregnancy test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains levels 12 hours after a dose, weekly until stable, then every three months","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes common early side effects in plain language","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes long-term effects on thyroid, weight and kidneys with monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names toxicity warning signs and what to do the same day","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about other medicines and warns about NSAIDs, dehydration and interacting drugs","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress about her father and her needle fear, and gives a specific plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Mentions lithium's protective effect, asks about suicidal thoughts and limits supplies","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers pregnancy risk, planning, contraception and breastfeeding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains that lithium is reduced gradually and not stopped suddenly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks understanding, including teach-back of toxicity signs","d":"Close"},{"c":"Leaves the decision with her and agrees the information pack and next review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains why lithium and how it is monitored, covers toxicity and interactions, responds to her kidney and pregnancy worries, and agrees her decision after teach-back.","borderline":"Accurate on the drug but rushed or lecture-like, missing the ibuprofen, contraception or breastfeeding points.","fail":"Implies she has bipolar disorder, omits toxicity signs or levels, dismisses the kidney worry, or gives wrong pregnancy advice."}},"c-s-07":{"setup":"Acute ward family room. Peter Lowe, 66, detained under Section 3 with severe depression, is barely eating or drinking. The consultant has recommended ECT; he lacks capacity. His daughter is opposed. Explain and hear her concerns.","role":{"who":"Ms Joanne Lowe, 39, woman, Peter's daughter","open":"Not great. I've just come from his room. He didn't even look at me. And now I hear they want to give him shock treatment.","manner":"Anxious, protective and sceptical at first; picture of ECT drawn from films. Softens and asks practical questions once she feels heard and given honest answers.","free":["Thinks ECT was banned and used in asylums","Fears it is painful","Worries he will never forgive her","Saw him today and he did not look at her"],"ifasked":["Did not know his kidneys were affected","He never discussed ECT or wrote anything down","No lasting power of attorney","He values his independence highly","Her late mother said he sometimes went 'quiet'","She lives 20 minutes away and visits daily"],"worries":["Will he lose his memory or forget who I am?","Is it safe for his heart at 66?","Why would it work when tablets didn't?","Can't you just wait and try another tablet?"],"rules":["Raises memory only after the procedure is explained","Becomes tearful about not knowing he was low; settles if validated","Becomes more hostile if told she has no say","Accepts the plan if she is offered a visit and a meeting with the consultant"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors looking after your dad. Thank you for coming in. How are you doing?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Not great. I've just come from his room. He didn't even look at me. And now I hear they want to give him shock treatment."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting, seeing him like that. What have you heard about ECT, and what worries you most?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"That it's what they did in asylums. Strapping people down. I thought it was banned. And he'll never forgive me if I let this happen."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening picture, and a heavy thing to carry. Can I explain why the team suggested it and what it actually involves today? Then I'll answer anything you want.","m":[11,1]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"1:01","band":"Why ECT","w":"D","s":"Your dad's depression has become very severe. For four days he's eaten nothing and had only sips to drink. His kidney blood test is getting worse.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"His kidneys? Nobody told me that."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that must be a shock. Without enough fluid, the kidneys start to struggle, and lying still for days also risks clots and infections.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Over five months, two antidepressants and then lithium haven't helped. ECT is the most effective treatment we have for depression this severe, and it works faster than tablets.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"But why would it work when nothing else has?"},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"It's fair to be sceptical. ECT works differently from tablets. Most people as unwell as your dad improve with it, often within two or three weeks.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:01","band":"What happens","w":"P","s":"What actually happens? Does it hurt?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"He wouldn't feel it. He'd have nothing to eat from midnight. In the ECT suite, an anaesthetist puts him fully to sleep and gives a muscle relaxant, so his body barely moves.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"A brief current is passed across his head, causing a seizure in the brain lasting under a minute. He wakes up in recovery within minutes and comes back to the ward.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"How many times?"},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Usually twice a week, often between six and twelve treatments. We review him after each one and stop when he's well.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","band":"Side effects and memory","w":"P","s":"What are the side effects?"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Afterwards, a headache, aching muscles, feeling sick or confused for an hour or so are common. Those settle.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"And his memory? I've read people lose years."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you. Memory for the weeks around the treatment is often patchy. Most of it improves over the following weeks, but some people say a few older memories don't come back.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Will he still know who I am?"},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Yes. He won't forget who you are. To reduce memory effects, we usually treat one side of the head first and check his memory regularly, adjusting if needed.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"What about his heart? He's 66."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"His blood pressure and pulse rise briefly during treatment. He'll have an ECG, blood tests and a check by the anaesthetist first. Serious problems are very rare, about the same as any short anaesthetic.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","band":"Alternatives","w":"P","s":"Can't you just wait and try another tablet?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"We could change his medicine again, but that usually takes weeks to work. With him barely drinking, waiting risks kidney failure, clots and infection.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"So waiting isn't really safe either."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"That's right. That's why the team feel ECT is the best option now. Does that make sense so far?","m":[9,13]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"I think so."},{"t":"4:34","band":"Safeguards and her role","w":"D","s":"At the moment your dad can't take in and weigh up this decision, so he can't consent himself. Because he's detained, an independent doctor from the Care Quality Commission must agree ECT is appropriate.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"If he'd ever written a refusal of ECT, it would be respected. Did he ever say what he'd want if he became this ill, or give anyone lasting power of attorney?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"No. He never talked about it. He just always wanted to stand on his own feet. He'd hate being like this."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful to know, and I'll make sure it's recorded. You're not making this decision or doing this to him. Telling us what he'd want is the most helpful thing you can do.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I didn't even know he'd been so low. Mum used to say he got 'quiet' sometimes."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"That must be so hard to hear now. Depression often hides, especially in men of his generation. You're here now, and that matters to him.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"5:54","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Would it help to visit the ECT suite tomorrow with the nurse who runs it, and take this leaflet home?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'd like to see it."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Just so I know I've been clear, could you tell me the main points you'll take away?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"He's very ill and his kidneys are suffering. He'd be asleep. Twice a week. Memory might be patchy, mostly comes back. And an independent doctor has to agree."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. I'll pass your views to the consultant, and we'll meet her tomorrow at four. If you think of questions tonight, ring the ward and ask for me.","m":[14,12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you for being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and explores her understanding and fears before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why ECT is recommended now, including dehydration, kidney injury and failed medicines","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a realistic account of likely benefit and time to improve","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes the procedure: fasting, anaesthetic, muscle relaxant, brief seizure, recovery","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States the usual frequency and number of treatments","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes common short-term side effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Is honest about memory effects and explains how they are reduced","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses physical safety, including heart and anaesthetic checks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains alternatives and the risks of not treating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains capacity, the independent doctor safeguard and asks about advance decisions or LPA","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates her fear, guilt and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers a visit to the ECT suite, written information and time","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, including teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees a follow-up meeting and how to contact the team","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears her fears, explains reasons, procedure, side effects and memory honestly, covers alternatives and safeguards, asks about his wishes, and agrees a visit and follow-up.","borderline":"Accurate but rushed, with little exploration of her fears, or vague on the safeguards or alternatives.","fail":"Promises no lasting memory loss, tells her she must consent or has no say, or never explains why ECT is urgent."}},"c-s-08":{"setup":"CMHT clinic, Monday morning. Graham Fielding, 56, has recurrent depression and has tried four antidepressants; ECT worked but he hated the anaesthetic. On trazodone 300 mg, mood worse again. He wants to discuss rTMS. Explain the options and rTMS.","role":{"who":"Mr Graham Fielding, 56, man, self-employed electrician","open":"Yes. My wife saw the trust has started it. I want to know if it'll work for me.","manner":"Keen, practical and well-read; frustrated by failed tablets. Brings the talk back to rTMS if the doctor lingers on other options. Brightens when driving is addressed.","free":["Wants to know about rTMS","Mood has dropped over two months; every day is a slog","Tablets have not helped","ECT worked before"],"ifasked":["No suicidal thoughts; wife and work protective","Felt sick and groggy after each ECT anaesthetic","Could not drive for two days after each ECT; needs his van for work","No metal in head, no implants, no seizures","Drinks about six pints at weekends","Takes trazodone 300 mg at night"],"worries":["How effective is it, and how quickly?","Can I drive to the sessions?","Will it affect my memory or give me a fit?","Would you recommend it to me?"],"rules":["Steers back to rTMS if the doctor talks at length about tablets","Gets irritated if asked many history questions","Becomes warmer once driving is addressed","Asks for a recommendation at the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Fielding, I'm Dr Banerjee, one of the psychiatrists. I understand you'd like to talk about rTMS.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. My wife saw the trust has started it. I want to know if it'll work for me."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Let's go through it properly. Can I spend a couple of minutes first on how you've been and what's helped before?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Fine, but I really want to hear about the magnetic treatment."},{"t":"0:37","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"Of course. How's your mood been over the last couple of months?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Every day's a slog. I get up, I go to work, I come home and sit. Nothing feels good."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. When things are that hard, some people think about ending their life. Have you had any thoughts like that?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"No. My wife and my work keep me going."},{"t":"1:11","band":"Past treatments","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you have them. Which treatments have helped in the past?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"The tablets did next to nothing. Mirtazapine, sertraline, the lot. ECT worked, though."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"What was difficult about the ECT?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"I felt rough after every anaesthetic, sick and groggy all day. And I couldn't drive for two days each time. I'm an electrician. No van, no work."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"That's a real problem when your living depends on it. It's frustrating when the thing that works comes at that cost.","m":[12,3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Exactly. I'm sick of trying things."},{"t":"1:59","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are a few routes. We could add lithium or a small dose of aripiprazole to your tablet, try venlafaxine, offer ECT again, talking therapy, or rTMS.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"As rTMS is what you're most interested in, let me go through it properly.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","band":"How it works","w":"P","s":"How does it actually work?"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"A magnetic coil is held against your head. It sends quick pulses into the front part of the brain, which tends to be underactive in depression. You're awake the whole time, with no anaesthetic.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"So it's like ECT without the anaesthetic?"},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"In a way. Both stimulate the brain, but rTMS doesn't cause a seizure. It's also used for OCD and some types of migraine.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Does it work?"},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Honestly, it's not as powerful as ECT. About four in ten people improve a lot, and about three in ten get fully well. It builds gradually, usually over two to four weeks.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:16","band":"Before starting and sessions","w":"P","s":"Do I need to do anything before?"},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"We'd check some safety questions. Do you have any metal in your head, a pacemaker or any implants, or have you ever had a fit?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"No metal. Never had a fit."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"And how much alcohol do you drink over a typical week?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Maybe six pints at the weekend."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"That's fine; we'd just ask you not to drink heavily during the course. At the first session they find the right strength by seeing what makes your thumb twitch.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"How long does each session take? How many?"},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"You sit in a chair; it makes clicking noises and taps on your scalp. Depending on the type, sessions last a few minutes up to about forty. It's five days a week for four to six weeks.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:27","band":"Side effects","w":"P","s":"Side effects?"},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"Headache and scalp discomfort are common at first, and some people's face twitches during the session. Paracetamol helps. A fit is very rare, which is why we screen. It doesn't affect memory.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:45","band":"Medicines, driving, top-ups","w":"P","s":"Do I stop the trazodone?"},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"No, keep taking it as usual. Just tell the team if anything changes.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"And can I drive?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Most people drive themselves to and from the sessions. There's no anaesthetic, so no two-day gap.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"Right. That changes everything. And if it works, will I need it forever?"},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Most people don't. If your mood dips later, top-up sessions are possible.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:23","band":"Availability and recommendation","w":"P","s":"Would you recommend it for me?"},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"I would. The tablets haven't helped much, ECT worked but cost you your work, and rTMS works in a similar way without the anaesthetic. It's a sensible next step.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Our trust clinic takes referrals from us, but there's usually a waiting list of several weeks. I'll refer you today and ask the consultant about adding lithium if it doesn't work.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Okay. What do I do while I wait?"},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Keep your trazodone going, and I'll refer you for talking therapy as well.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the main points, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"Every weekday for about a month, I stay awake, I can drive, keep the tablet, maybe a headache."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll see you in four weeks. If your mood drops or you have any thoughts of hurting yourself, ring the crisis line on this card, or NHS 111 and choose option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. That's the first good news in months."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and agrees an agenda that focuses on rTMS","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks briefly about current mood and confirms no suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Elicits past treatments, ECT's benefit and its practical downsides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Outlines the main options briefly: augmentation, switch, ECT, therapy, rTMS","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains how rTMS works in plain language and its other uses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives honest efficacy figures and time course, compared with ECT","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Completes a safety screen: metal, implants, seizures, alcohol","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains session length, frequency and course length","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes common side effects and the rare risk of seizure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers medication, driving and top-up questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains availability, referral route and a fallback plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his frustration with failed treatments","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives a reasoned recommendation and checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up and gives crisis safety-netting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Brief, focused history including safety, a quick overview of options, a full and honest account of rTMS including driving, and a reasoned recommendation with referral.","borderline":"Covers rTMS but misses the safety screen or driving, or overstates efficacy.","fail":"Long history or medication talk that never reaches rTMS, no suicide check, or inaccurate information."}},"c-s-09":{"setup":"EIP clinic, Thursday morning. Callum Reid, 25, left university a year ago and has been 'saying odd things' at home. Risk and full history come later. Elicit his first-rank symptoms precisely.","role":{"who":"Mr Callum Reid, 25, man, former engineering student living with his mother","open":"My mind's being taken over. By the network. They know everything about me.","manner":"Perplexed, tense and a little guarded; describes things vaguely unless asked precisely. Becomes more open and relieved when the doctor is curious rather than dismissive.","free":["Believes 'the network' is taking over his mind","Hears voices from the vent in his room","Feels the neighbours know his business","Cannot think straight"],"ifasked":["Voices are two men who discuss him as 'he'","They describe his actions as he does them","Sometimes repeat his thoughts a second later","Thoughts are inserted and pulled out by the network","Thoughts leak through walls; neighbours know them","Right hand taps by itself, moved by them; feelings are his own","Buzzing in stomach from their machines","Saw a red van in March and suddenly knew he had been chosen for an experiment"],"worries":["Do you think I'm mad?","Will you lock me up?","Why are you asking such strange questions?"],"rules":["Answers 'yes' to vague questions but only gives the true detail to precise ones","Denies made feelings: his emotions are his own","Gets more guarded if his beliefs are challenged","Reveals the red van only if asked when things became clear"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Callum, I'm Dr Adeyemi from the early intervention team. Thanks for coming in. Your GP said things have felt a bit odd lately. Can you tell me what's been happening?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"My mind's being taken over. By the network. They know everything about me."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Tell me more about how the network does that.","m":[12,2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"They talk. They get into my head. I can't explain it properly."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Take your time. Some of my questions may sound unusual, but I ask everyone them. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:53","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"You said they talk. Do you ever hear voices when nobody's there?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Yeah. From the vent in my room. Two men."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Do they talk to you directly, or about you to each other?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"About me. 'He's useless.' 'He'll never finish anything.' They argue about me."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Do they call you he or him, or do they say you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"He. Always he."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Do they ever describe what you're doing, while you're doing it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"All the time. 'He's getting up.' 'He's going to the kitchen now.'"},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"And do you ever hear your own thoughts spoken aloud, just after you think them?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. I think something and a second later they say it. Like an echo."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"That must be exhausting to live with.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"It is. I can't think straight any more."},{"t":"2:14","band":"Thought alienation","w":"D","s":"Do thoughts come into your head that don't feel like yours, as if someone else put them there?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah. The network's thoughts."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"How do you know they aren't your own thoughts, ones you just don't like?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Because they're not how I think. They're put there. I can feel them arrive."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Do your thoughts ever stop suddenly, as if something took them out of your head?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Mid-sentence. They pull them out. Then there's nothing."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Is that like losing your train of thought, or does something take them?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Something takes them. The network."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Do you feel your thoughts leave your head, so others know them without you saying anything?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"They leak through the walls. The neighbours know what I'm thinking."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Could they be guessing from your face or what you've said?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"No. They know the thoughts themselves. I don't say anything."},{"t":"3:42","band":"Passivity","w":"D","s":"What about your feelings? Do you ever feel your emotions are put there by someone else?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"No. My feelings are mine. I'm just scared."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"And urges, or movements? Does anything outside you control what your body does?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"My right hand. It taps by itself. They're working it. Like I'm a puppet."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Are there any sensations in your body that you feel are being done to you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"A buzzing in my stomach. It's their machines."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds really distressing, Callum. Thank you for telling me all this.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"You think I'm mad, don't you?"},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"I don't think that. These experiences are real and frightening for you, and I want to understand them so we can help.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:53","band":"Delusional perception","w":"D","s":"When did it first become clear to you what was happening? Was there a particular moment?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Back in March. I saw a red van parked outside Mum's."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"What happened when you saw it?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"I just knew. Straight away. I'd been chosen for their experiment."},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Was there anything about the van itself that told you that, like writing on it?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"No. It was just a van. But I knew. Completely."},{"t":"5:37","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've got it right? You hear two men discussing you, describing what you do and echoing your thoughts. Thoughts feel put in, taken out, and known by the neighbours.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Your right hand is moved by them, there's a buzzing put in your stomach, your feelings are your own, and seeing the red van made it all clear. Have I missed anything?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"No. That's it. It's a relief someone gets it."},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you felt able to tell me. My colleague, your care coordinator, will see you next to understand more about your life and how you're coping. Then we'll plan help together.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"If things get worse before then, ring our duty number on this card, or NHS 111 and choose option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose and puts him at ease","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses open questions to let him describe experiences first","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hearing his own thoughts spoken aloud (thought echo)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about voices discussing or arguing about him in the third person","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about running commentary on his actions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits thought insertion and checks the thoughts are felt as not his own","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits thought withdrawal, distinguished from losing his train of thought","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits thought broadcast, distinguished from others reading his face","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about made feelings, impulses and acts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about somatic passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits delusional perception with its two steps","d":"Content"},{"c":"Stays non-judgemental and validates his distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises symptoms present and absent and checks accuracy","d":"Close"},{"c":"Explains next steps and gives a crisis contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Moves from open narrative to precise questions, elicits every first-rank symptom with outside origin checked, stays warm and summarises accurately.","borderline":"Covers most symptoms but accepts vague 'yes' answers, or misses delusional perception or passivity.","fail":"Closed checklist with no clarification, argues with beliefs, or wanders into risk and history and misses several symptoms."}},"c-s-10":{"setup":"CMHT clinic, Friday afternoon. Hannah Rahman, 27, has schizophrenia and is 12 weeks pregnant. She fears passing it on and is thinking of ending the pregnancy. Explain the causes and risks, and plan her care.","role":{"who":"Mrs Hannah Rahman, 27, woman, 12 weeks pregnant, schizophrenia for six years","open":"Terrible. I've been crying all morning. I'm pregnant. Twelve weeks.","manner":"Very anxious and tearful; wants clear numbers. Relaxes visibly when given the real figures and told the decision is hers.","free":["Read online that schizophrenia is genetic","Thinking of ending the pregnancy to avoid passing it on","Is 12 weeks pregnant with her first baby","Came alone; husband at work"],"ifasked":["Believed the risk was about half","No mental illness in her husband's family","Converted to Islam on marriage; termination is against her faith","Fears her family would disown her","Stopped aripiprazole six months ago to conceive, without telling the team","No current psychotic symptoms; sleeping fine; no self-harm thoughts","Really wanted this baby"],"worries":["Is there a test to check the baby?","What else causes it?","Is it selfish of me to have a baby?","What should I do?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about her faith and family; settles if validated","Asks the doctor what she should do; becomes distressed if steered","Discloses stopping medication only if asked","Wants to involve her husband once she feels calmer"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Hannah, I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatrists. Everything we talk about today stays between us and your care team. How are you feeling?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Terrible. I've been crying all morning. I'm pregnant. Twelve weeks."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've had such a hard morning. What's been worrying you?","m":[1,6]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"I read online that schizophrenia is genetic. I can't give this to my baby. I'm thinking of ending it."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, and I can hear how much you care about this baby already. What exactly did you read?","m":[6,1]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"That it runs in families. That if I have it, my child will probably get it."},{"t":"0:59","band":"Inheritance","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can I explain what we actually know? Schizophrenia isn't caused by one gene. Lots of genes each add a tiny bit of vulnerability.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"What's passed on is a higher chance, not the illness itself. Whether it ever develops depends on much more than genes.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"So it's not definitely going to happen?"},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"No, definitely not. Can I give you the actual numbers?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","band":"Numbers","w":"P","s":"Yes. Please."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"In the general population, about 1 in 100 people develop schizophrenia. When one parent has it, the chance is about 13 in 100.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That means about 87 in every 100 children with a parent like you won't develop it. Does anyone in Imran's family have a mental illness?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No, no one. I thought it was more like half."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"A lot of people think that. It's about 4 or 5 in 10 only when both parents have it.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"That's so different from what I read."},{"t":"2:27","band":"Testing and causes","w":"P","s":"Is there a test? Can you check the baby?"},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"I wish I could give you that certainty. There's no test, before or after birth, that can tell whether a child will develop schizophrenia.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"So what else causes it, if it's not just genes?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Other things add to the risk: heavy cannabis use as a teenager, serious stress or trauma in childhood, and some problems around birth. Some of those you can influence.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"So I could protect them a bit?"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Yes. Good antenatal care, a loving, stable home and steering them away from cannabis all help.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:22","band":"Her decision","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about the pregnancy itself, apart from this worry?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(crying) We wanted this baby so much. And abortion is against my faith. If my family knew I'd even thought about it, they'd disown me."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"That's a really painful place to be, Hannah. You've been carrying this alone.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"This decision is yours, and only yours. Whatever you choose, we'll support you, and nothing will be shared with your family without your agreement.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"If you'd like to talk it through with someone independent, I can give you NHS pregnancy counselling details. You don't have to decide today.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I think... I think I want to keep it. But I need to talk to Imran."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a good next step. Would it help if he came to the next appointment?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Yes."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Her health","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about you now? Are you taking any medication at the moment?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"I stopped my tablets six months ago. I wanted to get pregnant. I didn't tell anyone."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. I'm not cross. It's important we know. How have your sleep and your thoughts been? Any of the old experiences coming back?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"I'm sleeping okay. No voices. Just worrying a lot."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"With all this worry, have thoughts of hurting yourself come into your mind?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. Pregnancy and especially the weeks after birth bring a higher risk of becoming unwell again, and more so off medication.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:42","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"I'd like to refer you to the perinatal mental health team. They specialise in pregnancy and will talk with you about whether to restart a tablet, weighing it up carefully.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"With your agreement, I'll let your GP and midwife know, so they can support you too.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's fine."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what the chance is for your baby, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"About 13 in 100. So most won't get it. And there's no test."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll see you next week, with Imran if you'd like. If you feel worse before then, ring our duty number on this card, or NHS 111 option 2.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like I can breathe again."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, assures confidentiality and explores what she has read and fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains that many genes add vulnerability and the illness itself is not inherited","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives risk numbers both ways and asks about the other parent's family history","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States that no test can predict schizophrenia, before or after birth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains other causes, including factors she can influence","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear, guilt and religious and family conflict","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Leaves the decision with her, without steering, and offers counselling and time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about her medication and current symptoms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains raised relapse risk in pregnancy and after birth","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans perinatal referral, medication review and GP and midwife liaison","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back of the risk figure","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up and gives crisis contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears her fear, gives accurate numbers both ways, says there is no test, supports her decision without steering, finds she is off medication and refers to the perinatal team.","borderline":"Accurate on genetics but misses her medication and relapse risk, or is slightly directive.","fail":"False reassurance or wrong figures, steers her decision, or ignores her own mental health."}},"c-s-13":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, adult ADHD clinic. Tom Reid, 19, takes methylphenidate and has been picked for national judo championships. His manager and coach wants it stopped. Tom has consented to you discussing his ADHD, treatment and the competition.","role":{"who":"Mr Steve Morgan, 45, male, Tom's manager at the judo academy and his coach","open":"Good, because we've got a problem. He needs to come off the Ritalin. Today.","manner":"Proud of Tom, frustrated and pushy at first. Doubts the diagnosis. Softens when the doctor offers a real route for Tom to compete.","free":["Tom picked for the nationals in eight weeks","Told methylphenidate is banned","Has coached Tom since he was fourteen","Wants the tablets stopped now"],"ifasked":["Tom makes careless mistakes with bookings","Doesn't follow instructions","Can't sit still in team talks; interrupts","Has a temper and run-ins with other players","Has found a herbal 'calming' remedy online","Would be willing to make changes at work"],"worries":["Does he even have ADHD?","Isn't it a performance drug?","Can you give a certificate today?","Did you know he takes ecstasy?"],"rules":["Pushes to stop the medicine until offered the exemption route","Raises the ecstasy question late; accepts the doctor not commenting if done kindly","Becomes cooperative once workplace help is discussed"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Morgan, I'm Dr Bello, one of the doctors in the ADHD clinic. Thanks for coming in. Tom has given written consent for me to talk about his ADHD, his treatment and the competition.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Good, because we've got a problem. He needs to come off the Ritalin. Today."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Before we get to that: anything else about Tom's life I can't discuss, though I'm happy to listen. What have you been told, and what would help most?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"British Judo says his tablets are banned. He's been picked for the nationals in eight weeks. Eight weeks! I've coached him since he was fourteen."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"That's a huge achievement for him, and for you. I can hear how gutting the timing is. Let's find a way through that keeps him well and keeps him competing.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Is there one? And honestly, does he even have ADHD? Loads of lads are restless."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Why it's ADHD","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Tell me what you notice about him at work and on the mat.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Careless mistakes with the bookings. Doesn't follow my instructions. Can't sit still in a team talk. Interrupts. And the temper. He's had a few run-ins with other lads."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really useful. Those are the three parts of ADHD: trouble with attention, restlessness, and acting on impulse. Tom's had them since he was little.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"I thought kids grew out of it."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Some do. Many still have symptoms as adults, as Tom does. That's why he still takes the medicine.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Well, he can't now. So he stops."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Stopping and alternatives","w":"D","s":"I'd strongly advise against that. Stopping isn't dangerous in itself, but the medicine works day by day. Within a day his focus and self-control would drop: at work, on the mat, even driving.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"So swap him to something that isn't banned."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"I wish it were that easy. He tried the main non-stimulant, atomoxetine, at sixteen. His symptoms got worse and it affected his liver, so it isn't safe to retry.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"What about the others?"},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"The other first-choice medicines are also stimulants, so they're banned too. Guanfacine isn't licensed for adults and would be a specialist last resort. Methylphenidate is the right medicine for Tom.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I've found a herbal thing online. Calms you down. Natural."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"I can see you're trying hard to find a way. There's no good evidence those work for ADHD. And supplements sometimes contain banned substances. If he tested positive, he'd be held responsible.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Really? Even if it's natural?"},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Even then. It's called strict liability. Whatever's in his body is his responsibility.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Hang on. If it's banned, isn't it a performance drug? Maybe he's had an unfair advantage."},{"t":"3:35","band":"The ban","w":"D","s":"I understand why it looks like that. In ADHD, the medicine brings his focus up to an ordinary level; it doesn't give him an edge. It's banned in competition because other people could misuse it.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"So what do we do?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"There's a proper route. It's called a Therapeutic Use Exemption, from UK Anti-Doping. It lets an athlete compete on a prescribed medicine they genuinely need.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"Brilliant. So you can write him a certificate today?"},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"I can't grant it myself; only UK Anti-Doping can. What they need is a specialist report: how he was diagnosed, what's been tried, and his dose. Our consultant can write that this week.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"How long does it take?"},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"It can take several weeks, so he should apply now, with our report. Plenty of people with ADHD compete at a high level. We want Tom to be one of them.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Right. Can I ask you something else? Did you know he takes ecstasy at parties?"},{"t":"4:53","band":"A worry raised","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I can see you're worried about him. I can't discuss anything Tom hasn't agreed to share, so I won't comment on that.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"I'm not trying to get him in trouble."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"I know. In general, any drug like that can mean a positive test and a ban, and it's risky alongside his medicine. I'd encourage you to talk to him, and we'll make sure we cover drug use with him too.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"Okay. Fair enough."},{"t":"5:29","band":"Work","w":"D","s":"Can we talk about work? You mentioned the mistakes and missed instructions. A few changes make a real difference.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Short written task lists. One job at a time. A quick check-in at the start of each shift. Breaks where he can move. And a quiet spot for the bookings.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"I could do the lists. And the bookings in the back office."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"That's great. There's also Access to Work, a government scheme that can pay for an ADHD coach. And if it helps, with Tom's agreement, I can write to you with these suggestions.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"That'd help, yeah."},{"t":"6:19","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So I can check I've explained it clearly, what will you take back from today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Keep him on the tablets. Apply for the exemption now, with your report. No herbal stuff. Lists at work."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Tom's in the waiting room. Shall we bring him in so we can agree the plan with him? I'll see him again in four weeks.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thanks, doctor. I came in ready for a fight."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and states what Tom has consented to share","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what the manager knows and wants from the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and his pride in Tom","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains why Tom has adult ADHD using inattention, overactivity and impulsivity","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains what stopping would do and advises firmly against it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why atomoxetine and other medicines are not suitable for Tom","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises against the herbal remedy: no evidence and contamination risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Acknowledges the ban but explains the medicine restores focus rather than giving an edge","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the TUE route, the specialist evidence needed, and that the doctor cannot certify it","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Keeps confidentiality when drug use is raised, while advising on the risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Suggests specific workplace adjustments and Access to Work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a supportive message that people with ADHD can thrive in sport","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the manager's understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes by involving Tom and agreeing actions and follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"States consent limits, explains adult ADHD from his observations, firmly advises against stopping, rules out alternatives with reasons, explains the TUE route, holds confidentiality and agrees adjustments.","borderline":"Correct advice but no clear consent boundary, or the exemption and workplace adjustments left vague.","fail":"Agrees to stop or switch the medicine, discusses information beyond consent, or promises a certificate."}},"c-s-15":{"setup":"Tuesday late morning, neuropsychiatry clinic. Chloe Turner, 26, has video-EEG-confirmed functional seizures and refused to attend. Her father has come. She has consented to you discussing her diagnosis and treatment with him. Explain and address his concerns.","role":{"who":"Mr Graham Turner, 58, male, Chloe's father","open":"She wouldn't come. She says the neurologists think she's making it all up. She's devastated.","manner":"Worried, protective, a little defensive of his daughter. Becomes tearful about his wife's death; relieved once he hears it is real and treatable.","free":["Chloe thinks doctors believe she is faking","Attacks for a year, getting more frequent","He has watched them and knows she is not faking"],"ifasked":["First attack was before an important job interview","Now they happen at any time","Chloe's mother died 18 months ago of breast cancer","Chloe is anxious, withdrawn and not sleeping","They do not talk about her mother","No self-harm or suicidal talk that he knows of","He calls an ambulance every time"],"worries":["Do they think she's making it up?","Will the epilepsy tablets work?","Will she get better?","What do I do during an attack?"],"rules":["Bristles if the doctor says 'psychological' or 'psychogenic' without explanation","Shares the bereavement only if asked about recent stresses","Says 'I should have done something'; settles if reassured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Turner? I'm Dr Huang from the neuropsychiatry team. Thank you for coming. Chloe rang this morning and agreed I can talk to you about her diagnosis and treatment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"She wouldn't come. She says the neurologists think she's making it all up. She's devastated."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That must be awful for both of you. What did she tell you they said?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"'Non-epileptic.' 'Psychogenic.' She looked it up and it basically says it's in her head. She's been having these fits for a year. I've watched them. She's not faking."},{"t":"0:44","band":"The fear","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hard it's been watching her go through this, and then feeling she isn't believed. So let me be really clear: Chloe isn't making this up. The attacks are real, and she isn't doing them on purpose.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Then why send her to a psychiatrist?"},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Before I explain, what worries you most?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"What it actually is. Whether those tablets will work. And whether she'll ever get better."},{"t":"1:18","band":"What it is","w":"D","s":"Let's take those in turn. The attacks are called functional, or dissociative, seizures. The brain itself is healthy, but the way it works goes wrong for a while.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"But all the tests were normal. How can there be anything wrong?"},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"Think of a computer. The hardware's fine, which is why the scans were normal. But the software crashes. That's what happens during an attack.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"How do they know it's not epilepsy, though?"},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"In hospital, three of her usual attacks were filmed while her brain waves were recorded. With epilepsy the brain waves change during an attack. Hers stayed normal throughout. That's how we can be sure.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that. Nobody explained it like that."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"And she's not alone. About one in five people sent to specialist seizure clinics have this. It's common, and it's real.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"So what causes it?"},{"t":"2:29","band":"Why","w":"D","s":"The body often reacts to feelings without us choosing. Embarrassment makes us blush; nerves make the heart race. Functional seizures are an automatic shutdown like that, often linked with stress, though not always.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"The first one was before a big job interview. Now they happen any time."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"That fits. They often start around stress and then take on a life of their own. Has anything else been hard for Chloe in the last few years?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Her mum died eighteen months ago. Breast cancer. It was quick."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a huge loss for you both. How has Chloe been since?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Quieter. Anxious. Not sleeping. We don't really talk about her mum. I find it too hard, to be honest."},{"t":"3:27","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable; grief is hard to talk about. Has she seemed low, or said anything about wishing you weren't here, or harming herself?","m":[3,9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. Never anything like that. Just withdrawn."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Thank you. We'll ask her about that gently ourselves. Losing her mum may be part of this picture. But nobody caused it.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking I should have done something."},{"t":"3:59","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"You're here today, fighting her corner. That is doing something. Now, the tablets. The epilepsy medicine works on electrical seizures, so it won't help these.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"So she can just stop it?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Not suddenly, please. Her neurologist will reduce it slowly and safely.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"Then what does help?"},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"A talking therapy designed for these seizures, usually about twelve sessions with our psychologist. She learns to spot warning signs and use grounding skills to ride them out.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Like what?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Slow breathing, feeling her feet on the floor, naming things she can see. We'd also treat any anxiety or low mood, and offer bereavement support, for you both if you'd like.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Will she get better?"},{"t":"5:00","band":"Outlook","w":"D","s":"Many people improve a lot, and some stop having attacks altogether. Understanding the diagnosis and starting therapy early make the biggest difference. She's been diagnosed within a year, which is good.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"What do I do when she has one? I usually panic and call an ambulance."},{"t":"5:24","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"That's a natural reaction; it's frightening to watch. Stay calm, move things she could hit, don't hold her down or put anything in her mouth, and talk to her quietly. Time it.","m":[3,13]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"And the ambulance?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Call 999 if she's injured, struggling to breathe, or it's very different from her usual attacks. Otherwise stay with her until she's back.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's actually a relief."},{"t":"5:56","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"What do you think would help Chloe come and talk to us? She could come with you, or we could do a video call.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Video, I think. She'd feel safer at home."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"I'll write to her today offering that, and include a website called neurosymptoms.org that explains it well. So I know I've been clear, how will you explain it to her tonight?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"It's real, she's not faking, it's the brain's software crashing. The tablets won't help, but therapy will. And she's not the only one."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"That's perfect. I'll copy her GP in, and we'll speak again in four weeks. Any other questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I think she'll talk to you now."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and confirms Chloe's consent and its scope","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he understands and what worries him before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his distress and Chloe's feeling of being disbelieved","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States early that the attacks are real and not deliberate","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains functional seizures and how video-EEG made the diagnosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Normalises the diagnosis as common","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives an everyday example of the mind-body link without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explores possible stressors sensitively, including the bereavement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about Chloe's mood, anxiety and any thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains anti-epileptic drugs do not help and must be tapered by neurology","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines treatment: specialist therapy, grounding skills and treating anxiety or depression","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives an honest, hopeful outlook and what improves it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives first-aid advice and when to call 999","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding and agrees how to invite Chloe back, with written information","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Answers the 'faking' fear early, explains functional seizures and how they were diagnosed, explores mood and stressors, gives treatment, outlook and first aid, and leaves a route back for Chloe.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but stigmatising words, no mood or risk check, or no plan to engage Chloe.","fail":"Implies she can control the attacks, advises stopping anti-epileptics suddenly, or offers no treatment path."}},"c-s-16":{"setup":"Tuesday 14:20, CMHT clinic. Rachel Dunmore, 37, well on risperidone 6 mg, has stopped having periods. Her prolactin is 1,100 mIU/L. Take a focused history, exclude other causes, explain the result and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Rachel Dunmore, 37, woman with schizophrenia, outpatient","open":"Rachel's fine. Is this about the blood test? Nobody told me what it was for.","manner":"Friendly but anxious; embarrassed about sex; becomes frightened at the word 'tumour' and protective of risperidone; relaxed once she has a choice.","free":["Periods lighter and shorter since the summer, none last month","Well for 14 months; voices gone","Wants a baby with her partner Tom soon"],"ifasked":["Breasts sore for a few days, no milk","Has been avoiding sex because of low libido","No sex for two months; not pregnant","No other medicines or herbal remedies","No headaches or visual problems","No acne or extra hair","Periods were regular before risperidone"],"worries":["Is it a tumour? Her aunt had a brain tumour","She will not go back to olanzapine after gaining weight","Will this stop her having a baby?"],"rules":["Gives sexual history only if asked sensitively","Refuses to stop risperidone unless her fear of relapse is acknowledged","Agrees to adding aripiprazole if options are explained clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Ms Dunmore? I'm Dr Haddad, one of the doctors in the team. Thanks for coming back. Is Rachel all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Rachel's fine. Is this about the blood test? Nobody told me what it was for."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"It is. Before I go through it, what did you understand the test was looking at?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Something to do with my periods? I just want to know if something's wrong with me."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"That's fair. It was a hormone test, and I'll explain the result properly. First, can I ask a few personal questions so I get the full picture?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Her symptoms","w":"D","s":"Tell me what's been happening with your periods.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"They got lighter and shorter from about the summer. Then last month nothing at all. They were always regular before."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed any changes in your breasts? Tenderness, swelling, or any milky fluid from the nipples?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Sore for the last few days. No milk or anything. I'd have noticed."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Some people find their interest in sex drops with this. Has that happened to you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Yes. I've been avoiding it with Tom, to be honest. It's embarrassing talking about it."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. It's a really common side effect, and it's nothing to be embarrassed about. It's exactly what I need to know.","m":[9]},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Any headaches, or any change in your eyesight, like losing the edges of what you can see?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"No headaches. My eyes are fine."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"And any spots or extra hair growth on your face or body?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"I have to ask everyone this. Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"No. We've not slept together for two months. But we do want a baby, soon. That's why I'm worried."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"That helps me a lot. We'll still do a quick urine pregnancy test today, just to be certain. Is that okay?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Do you take anything apart from risperidone? Contraception, anything for sickness or indigestion, antidepressants, or herbal remedies?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Just the risperidone, 6 milligrams at night."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Any tiredness, feeling the cold, weight change, or problems with your kidneys?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. I feel well, honestly. Best I've been in years."},{"t":"3:00","band":"The result","w":"D","s":"Here's what the test showed. Prolactin is a hormone from a small gland under the brain. Its main job is making breast milk, and when it's high it switches periods off and lowers sex drive.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"So mine's high?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Yes. Before risperidone it was normal. Now it's 1,100, about twice the top of normal. That fits the periods, the soreness and the low sex drive.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Why would it go up?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Risperidone blocks a brain chemical called dopamine. That calms the voices, but dopamine is also the brake on prolactin. So the most likely cause is the risperidone.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Is it a tumour? My aunt had a brain tumour."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"I can hear that's frightening, especially after your aunt. A level like yours on risperidone, with no headaches or sight changes, is very unlikely to be a tumour. If it stays high after we change things, we'd arrange a scan.","m":[9,6]},{"t":"4:09","band":"Why act","w":"D","s":"It's worth treating rather than ignoring. Over years, high prolactin can thin the bones, it makes getting pregnant harder, and some studies link it to a small rise in breast cancer risk.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"But I don't want to come off risperidone. It's the first thing that's worked. I'm not going back to olanzapine."},{"t":"4:32","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense, and keeping you well comes first. There are four options. One: swap gradually to aripiprazole, which hardly raises prolactin and doesn't usually cause weight gain.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Two: stay on risperidone and add a low dose of aripiprazole, 5 milligrams in the morning. That often brings prolactin down within a few months.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Three: lower the risperidone, though that risks the voices returning. Four: change nothing and keep checking, but your symptoms would likely continue.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Adding the small one feels safer. Does it have side effects?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Some people feel restless or a bit sick for the first week or two. Tell us if you can't sit still. We'd see you in a fortnight.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Pregnancy plans","w":"D","s":"Because you want a baby, two important things. As prolactin falls, you can become fertile again quickly, so use condoms until you've decided you're ready.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Oh. I didn't know that."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"And before trying, I'll refer you to our perinatal mental health team for advice on the safest plan, ideally one medicine. Start folic acid, 400 micrograms a day, from when you start trying.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:54","band":"Tests and checks","w":"D","s":"Today: the pregnancy test, a repeat prolactin taken after you've rested, and thyroid and kidney tests. We'll recheck prolactin three months after starting aripiprazole.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's a lot of blood."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"It is. Can I check I've explained it clearly? What will you tell Tom when you get home?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"My hormone's high from the risperidone. Probably not a tumour. Add a small tablet, more bloods, condoms for now, and see the baby team."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. If you get headaches or changes in your sight, or the voices start coming back, ring the duty line the same day. I'll agree the plan with Dr Moss, my consultant, today, and see you in two weeks.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I feel better knowing what it is."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity and asks what she understands about the test","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about periods, breast changes, nipple discharge and libido","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about headache, visual field change, acne and excess hair","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about chance of pregnancy and arranges a pregnancy test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks other prolactin-raising medicines and thyroid or kidney symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains prolactin, her level and the risperidone link in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains long-term effects: bone thinning, fertility and possible breast cancer link","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers switch, add aripiprazole, dose reduction or monitoring, with pros and cons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates embarrassment, fear of a tumour and fear of relapse","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Covers fertility return, contraception, folic acid and perinatal preconception referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees repeat prolactin, thyroid and kidney tests, and a review date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking her to say the plan back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets for headache, sight change and relapse, and closes with consultant input","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a focused prolactin history, excludes pregnancy and other causes, explains clearly, offers real options and plans for her fertility.","borderline":"Explains well but misses pregnancy, other drugs or pituitary red flags, or forgets that fertility returns.","fail":"Blames the drug without excluding pregnancy, stops risperidone abruptly, or imposes a switch without her choice."}},"c-s-17":{"setup":"Wednesday 15:00, ward family room. Daniel Price, 25, on Section 3, refuses all tablets after his third relapse this year. His father asks about alternatives. Daniel has agreed you can talk to him. Explain depot injections and answer his questions.","role":{"who":"Mr Martin Price, 58, Daniel's father","open":"Has he? He won't even look at me. He thinks I'm working for the police.","manner":"Tired and protective; wary of anything that sounds forceful; calmer and keen once the explanation is clear and his guilt is acknowledged.","free":["Third relapse this year, each after stopping tablets","Found unopened boxes of risperidone","Daniel is well and back at work when he takes it"],"ifasked":["No side effects that he knows of","Bloods were normal","Daniel stops because he doesn't believe he's ill","Daniel lives with him","Daniel has never been violent"],"worries":["How often would the injection be?","What are the side effects?","What happens if he refuses after he leaves hospital?","Does a test dose come first?"],"rules":["Is put off by jargon; asks 'what does that mean?' if it is used","Feels guilty about going behind Daniel's back; settles if validated","Agrees with the idea if explained clearly and honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Price? I'm Dr Okafor, one of the doctors on the ward looking after Daniel. Thanks for waiting. Daniel's said we can talk to you about his treatment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Has he? He won't even look at me. He thinks I'm working for the police."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"That must be really painful, when you're the one who's been looking after him.","m":[10]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"It is. I just want my son back."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"You asked the nurses about something other than tablets. What have you heard about injections so far?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Not much. Someone said 'depot'. It sounds like something you'd do to a horse."},{"t":"0:49","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"Before I explain, help me understand the pattern. What happens with his tablets at home?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"He takes them for a few weeks, feels better, then stops. I found boxes of risperidone in his room, not even opened. Third time this year."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"When he's taking them, how is he?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Brilliant. Back at work, laughing. No side effects that I know of. His bloods were fine."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"And why do you think he stops?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"He doesn't think he's ill. Once he feels better he says he doesn't need them."},{"t":"1:34","band":"What a depot is","w":"D","s":"That's a very common pattern, and it's exactly the situation injections are designed for. A depot is the same kind of medicine as a tablet, given as an injection into a muscle.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"The medicine sits in the muscle and releases slowly into the blood over weeks. So he gets a steady level, without needing to remember a tablet every day.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"So it's not a stronger drug?"},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"No. It works the same way as the tablet. Because risperidone helped him, the obvious choice is paliperidone. It's what risperidone turns into in the body, so we'd expect the same benefit.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Are there other kinds?"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Yes. There's a risperidone injection every two weeks, an aripiprazole one monthly, and older ones like flupentixol, every one to four weeks. We'd agree the choice with Daniel and his consultant.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:38","band":"How often","w":"P","s":"How often would he have to have it?"},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"With paliperidone, two starting injections in the upper arm, a week apart. Then one a month, in the arm or buttock. If he stays well for four months, it can go to once every three months.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"On the ward, the nurses give it. At home, it's the depot clinic or his care coordinator, who can come to the house.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"What about a test first? I don't want him reacting badly to something that stays in him for weeks."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"That's a really sensible question. With the older injections, we give a small test dose first and wait. With paliperidone, the months Daniel did well on risperidone tablets already show he tolerates it.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","band":"Side effects","w":"P","s":"And side effects? I don't want to hurt him."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"Of course you don't. You're trying to protect him. The side effects are the same as the tablets: possible weight gain, sleepiness, stiffness or restlessness, and hormone changes.","m":[10,6]},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"The injection itself can be sore for a day or two, and rarely the area swells. The nurses rotate the site. Because it lasts weeks, any side effect lasts too, so we check carefully.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"We'd check his weight and blood pressure regularly, and his blood sugar, cholesterol and a hormone called prolactin, at three months and then every year.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"He'll say no. He's refusing everything here."},{"t":"4:23","band":"Consent and the law","w":"D","s":"We'll try to get his agreement first, and many people come round when they hear it means no daily tablets. Daniel is detained under Section 3 of the Mental Health Act.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"For the first three months of treatment under the section, his consultant can authorise medicine even if he refuses. After that, he must agree with capacity, or an independent second-opinion doctor must approve it.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"So they'd hold him down?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Only as a last resort, and planned carefully to keep him safe. Most people accept it once they're given time and a choice of injection site.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"5:08","band":"After discharge","w":"P","s":"But what happens when he leaves? He won't go to a clinic."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Good question. Nobody can force an injection at home. One option is a Community Treatment Order. It lets him live at home with conditions, such as having the injection.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"If he refuses and becomes unwell, his consultant can recall him to hospital for up to 72 hours to give it. Usually just knowing that helps people keep to the plan.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"I feel like I'm going behind his back."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels that way. You're not. You're trying to stop a fourth relapse. And the decision is the team's, not yours.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:59","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Can I check I've explained it properly? How would you describe it to his mum?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Same medicine, but an injection that lasts a month. Two to start. Same side effects, and a sore arm. They'll try to get him to agree. At home, maybe this treatment order thing."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. I'll give you a leaflet on depot injections, and the ward will offer you a carer's assessment.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"The consultant will discuss it with Daniel at Thursday's ward round. Would you like to come to the next family meeting and ask anything else then?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you. That's the first time anyone's made sense of it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent to share, and asks what father already knows","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores adherence pattern, past response, side effects and reason for stopping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains a depot as the same medicine released slowly from muscle","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the choice of injections, linking paliperidone to his risperidone response","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the schedule: starting doses, monthly, three-monthly option, who gives it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains side effects, injection-site problems, long duration and monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains test doses for older depots and why past tolerance matters","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains Section 3, the three-month rule and second-opinion doctor accurately","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains what happens if he refuses after discharge, including a CTO and recall","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates the father's distress, guilt and fear of hurting his son","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by asking him to explain it back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers a leaflet, carer's assessment and a follow-up meeting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains depots clearly and accurately, ties the choice to his past response, is honest about the law and checks understanding.","borderline":"Explains the injection well but is vague or wrong on the three-month rule or a CTO, or never checks understanding.","fail":"Uses jargon, implies forced treatment at home, ignores the father's distress, or gives inaccurate dosing."}},"c-s-18":{"setup":"Monday 11:00, home visit. Kofi Mensah, 57, an IT consultant, has barely left home for two months, says his daughter. Take a history, reach a diagnosis and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Kofi Mensah, 57, IT consultant, at home","open":"Come in, come in. Sit. I don't know why everyone's fussing. I'm fine. Tea?","manner":"Warm, hospitable, minimising; opens up when normalised; ashamed about relying on his daughter; relieved after naming it.","free":["Works from home","Daughter worries too much","Enjoying the football"],"ifasked":["Intimidated by youths outside the supermarket two months ago","Heart racing, breathless, thought he was dying","Relief on getting home","Avoids buses, supermarket, church, crowds","No attacks at home","No flashbacks or nightmares","Clothes unwashed; daughter shops online","Small whisky twice a week"],"worries":["Is he going mad?","Can it be fixed so he can get back to church?"],"rules":["Minimises until the doctor normalises embarrassment","Becomes quiet and ashamed about his daughter shopping; settles if validated","Denies suicidal thoughts firmly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Mensah? I'm Dr Brennan, one of the doctors from the mental health team. Your GP asked us to visit. Thank you for letting me into your home.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Come in, come in. Sit. I don't know why everyone's fussing. I'm fine. Tea?"},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"I'd love one, thank you. Your daughter's been worried you're not going out much. How have things been for you lately?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Ah, Abena worries. I work from home anyway. It's easier. Nothing's wrong."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Lots of people work from home now. I'm wondering, though, has something changed in the last couple of months?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Well... I'd rather not make a big thing of it."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"Sometimes the things people feel embarrassed about are exactly what help us understand. Whatever it is, I won't judge.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:06","band":"What happened","w":"P","s":"About two months ago, outside the supermarket. A group of lads. Shouting, crowding me. They didn't touch me. But I felt like a fool."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Anyone would be shaken by that. What happened in your body at the time?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"My heart was banging. I couldn't breathe. I honestly thought I was dying, or losing my mind. I ran for the bus home."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"And when you got home?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"It just melted away. Like walking into a cold shower after a hot day."},{"t":"1:52","band":"Where the fear is","w":"D","s":"Since then, which places bring that feeling back?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Thinking about the supermarket. The bus. Even the door sometimes. I tried to go to church on the bus and had to get off and come straight back."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"What's the worst thing you fear might happen in those places?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"That I'll be stuck somewhere I can't get out of. That I'll collapse in front of everyone and no one will help. Maybe die."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"And what about crowds, queues, or being out on your own anywhere else?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"All of it. If I can't get home quickly, I panic."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had one of those attacks out of the blue, here at home, for no reason?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. Never at home. Home is safe."},{"t":"3:00","band":"Other conditions","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, when you're at home?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Good, actually. I'm enjoying the football. Sleeping fine, eating fine. Work's going well."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Do you ever have nightmares or flashbacks of that day, or jump at noises more than before?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. I just don't want to go back there."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Some people after something like that start to feel those lads are watching them or following them on purpose. Has anything like that happened?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"No. They were just idiots. They don't know me. It's being out there that frightens me."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"Are you worried about what other people think of you, like being judged? Or do you have to check or wash things repeatedly?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"No. I like people. And no, nothing like that."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink? Any coffee, energy drinks or other drugs? And any shakiness, sweating or weight loss?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"A small whisky twice a week. Two coffees. No drugs. No, nothing like that. I'm not on any medicines."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Looking after himself","w":"D","s":"How are you managing from day to day? What about food?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Abena orders my shopping online. That's the embarrassing part. A grown man, a father, can't buy his own bread."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"I can hear how hard that is for you. It doesn't make you less of a father. Is there enough food in the house now?","m":[12,10]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Yes, the fridge is full. She's very good."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"And washing, clothes, keeping the house going?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I wash every day. But I haven't been to the launderette. The clothes are starting to smell a bit."},{"t":"5:19","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel stuck like this, they sometimes have thoughts of not wanting to live, or of harming themselves. Have you had any thoughts like that?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I've got grandchildren. I just want to go to church again."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And any thoughts of harming anyone else, including those lads?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"No. I just want to forget them."},{"t":"5:52","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think is happening. That day you had a panic attack: a huge rush of adrenaline. Your mind then learned that going out is dangerous, so you stay where you feel safe.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"That's called agoraphobia, with panic attacks. It's common, it's not madness, and it's very treatable with the right kind of talking therapy.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"So it can be fixed? I can go back to church?"},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Most people improve a great deal. I'll ask your GP to do some routine blood tests, including your thyroid, and we'll talk about therapy that helps you go out step by step. Does that sound okay?","m":[13,9]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you, doctor. I feel lighter for saying it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the visit and builds rapport in his home","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses open questions and normalising to uncover the precipitating event","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits panic symptoms: autonomic features and catastrophic thoughts, relief at home","d":"Content"},{"c":"Maps feared situations and the fear of being trapped or without help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about unexpected panic attacks to separate agoraphobia from panic disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression: mood, enjoyment, sleep, appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis by testing whether the fear is persecutory","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for PTSD, social anxiety and OCD","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, caffeine, drugs and physical causes such as thyroid","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses self-neglect: food, washing, laundry and home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds to embarrassment and shame with validation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains the likely diagnosis in plain words and agrees next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets past the minimising, maps panic and avoidance, rules out the look-alikes, checks self-neglect and suicide risk, and names agoraphobia.","borderline":"Finds the avoidance but skips panic disorder, psychosis or self-neglect screening.","fail":"Accepts 'I'm fine', labels it depression or paranoia, or never asks about risk."}},"c-s-19":{"setup":"Wednesday 10:30, CMHT base. Samuel Owusu, 43, referred with suspected first-episode psychosis, has a frontal meningioma on his routine CT. He has asked that his sister be told everything. Break the news and answer her questions.","role":{"who":"Miss Esi Owusu, 39, Samuel's younger sister","open":"Yes, he can't face coming. He said you've got his scan results. Is he starting the tablets now?","manner":"Hopeful and businesslike; devastated and tearful at the news; needs a pause; angry if rushed; calmer and practical once questions are answered honestly.","free":["Samuel has been suspicious of the neighbours, hearing noises","Expected the scan to be normal","Samuel asked her to come"],"ifasked":["He has had headaches for weeks","He seems weaker; she thought he wasn't eating","He drives a delivery van for work","Their mother doesn't know she's here","She cannot face telling him herself"],"worries":["How long has he got, and can anything be done to save him?","Can he still drive, and will he lose his job?","Will she have to make his decisions?","How should Samuel be told?"],"rules":["Cries and stops listening for a while after the news","Becomes angry if the doctor talks over her or gives false reassurance","Settles if given time and honest answers"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Prepare and open","w":"D","s":"Miss Owusu? I'm Dr Kaur, one of the doctors looking after Samuel. Thank you for coming in. We won't be disturbed here. Samuel rang us this morning and asked us to tell you everything.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Yes, he can't face coming. He said you've got his scan results. Is he starting the tablets now?"},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"Would you like anyone else with you, or on the phone, while we talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"No. Mum doesn't know I'm here. Just tell me."},{"t":"0:42","band":"What she knows","w":"D","s":"Before I go through it, what's your understanding of why we did the scan?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"To rule things out. The doctor said it would almost certainly be normal. Then he'd start the medicine for the psychosis."},{"t":"1:02","band":"The news","w":"D","s":"I'm afraid the scan has shown something we weren't expecting.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"What do you mean?"},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"It shows a growth in the front part of his head. It's called a meningioma. That's a lump growing from the lining around the brain, not from the brain itself.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"A brain tumour? Oh my God. Oh, Sammy."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a huge shock.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(crying) He's my best friend. Brain tumours kill people."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Take your time. I'm right here. There's no rush.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"(after a pause) Sorry. Go on."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"There's nothing to be sorry for. What you're feeling makes complete sense. Shall I tell you a bit more about what it means?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Is it cancer? How long has he got?"},{"t":"2:16","band":"What it means","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you about what we know and don't know yet. Most meningiomas are not cancer, and they usually grow slowly. Many can be removed or controlled.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"But I can't tell you exactly what this one is until he's had a more detailed scan and been seen by the brain specialists. I won't guess, because you deserve accurate answers.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"So it's not a death sentence?"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"For most people with a meningioma it isn't. I can't promise anything for Samuel yet, but there's real reason for hope.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Is this why he's been so strange? The neighbours, the noises?"},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Very possibly. The front of the brain helps with judgement and behaviour. A growth pressing there can cause headaches, changes in personality and suspiciousness.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"He's had headaches for weeks. And he's been so weak. I thought he wasn't eating."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"That fits too. You noticed a lot, and you were right to be worried.","m":[5,4]},{"t":"3:41","band":"Next steps","w":"P","s":"Can they save him? What happens now?"},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"The scan team has booked an MRI for this Friday. My consultant has sent an urgent referral to the brain surgery team today. They'll see him quickly and explain the options.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Treatment might be an operation, radiotherapy, or sometimes just close watching. They may also give a steroid medicine to reduce any swelling. Samuel will decide with them.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"He drives a van for work. Can he still drive?"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"He must stop driving now, including for work, and he must tell the DVLA. I know that's hard. The specialists will advise when it might be safe again.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"He'll lose his job. They won't give him paid leave."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"That's a real worry. He can have a fit note, so he's entitled to at least statutory sick pay. Our team's support worker can help with his employer and benefits.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Will I have to make all his decisions now?"},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"No. Samuel makes his own decisions unless he's shown to lack capacity for a specific one. You can support him, come to appointments, and he can name you to be involved.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:23","band":"Keeping him safe","w":"D","s":"Until he's seen, if his headache gets much worse, he vomits, becomes drowsy or confused, has a fit, or gets new weakness, call 999 or take him to A&E.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"Okay. Worse headache, sick, drowsy, fit, weakness."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Telling Samuel and close","w":"D","s":"How would you like Samuel to hear this? I could see him at home tomorrow with you, so he hears it from a doctor and you don't carry it alone.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. I couldn't tell him myself."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"We'll keep seeing him too. We'll hold off the antipsychotic until the specialists advise, and help with his distress meanwhile. Can I check what you'll take away today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"It's a growth on the lining of his brain. Probably not cancer. MRI Friday, brain surgeons urgently. No driving. You'll come and tell him tomorrow."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Here's a written summary, my direct number, and details for The Brain Tumour Charity. You're not on your own with this.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you for not rushing me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, ensures privacy, confirms Samuel's consent, offers support person","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she knows and expects before giving news","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a warning shot, then names the finding and explains 'meningioma' plainly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Allows silence and validates shock and grief before continuing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Links the finding to his headaches, weakness and behaviour change","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers prognosis honestly: usually benign and slow, without false promises","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains MRI, urgent neurosurgical referral and possible treatments","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises he must stop driving now and inform the DVLA","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses work: fit note, sick pay and practical support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains Samuel keeps his own decisions; capacity is presumed and decision-specific","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives red-flag symptoms needing 999 or A&E","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers to tell Samuel together, written information, ongoing psychiatric support; checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Warns, names the finding plainly, stays with her shock, answers honestly, covers next steps, driving and decisions, and plans to tell Samuel together.","borderline":"Delivers the news clearly but rushes past her emotion or misses driving, red flags or how Samuel will be told.","fail":"Uses jargon, gives false reassurance or a prognosis, ignores her distress, or overloads her with technical detail."}},"c-s-20":{"setup":"Thursday 11:00, ward clinic room. Harjit Sandhu, 57, with schizoaffective disorder, had haloperidol raised to 5 mg twice daily two weeks ago. His mother says he is slow, blank and shuffling. Take a brief history, examine for EPSEs and explain.","role":{"who":"Mr Harjit Sandhu, 57, inpatient with schizoaffective disorder","open":"Yes. Mum's right. I feel like I'm made of wood.","manner":"Slow, soft-voiced, cooperative; worried about Parkinson's disease and falling; reassured by clear explanation.","free":["Feels stiff, especially arms and legs","Started two weeks ago after the dose increase","His mother noticed he seems slow and blank"],"ifasked":["Hard to get out of bed, out of a chair, or start walking","Shuffles; fell in the corridor last week, bruised hip","Hands shake when watching TV","Face less expressive; voice flat; thinking slower","No spasms, no inner restlessness, no mouth movements","No fever, sweating or confusion"],"worries":["Is it Parkinson's disease?","Will he be stuck like this?","He is scared of falling again"],"rules":["Follows clear instructions; moves slowly","Asks 'Is it Parkinson's?' after hearing the findings","Settles when told it is a reversible side effect"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Sandhu? I'm Dr Mensah, one of the ward doctors. Your mum mentioned you've seemed slower and stiffer lately. Can I ask you about that, and then examine you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. Mum's right. I feel like I'm made of wood."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"That sounds really uncomfortable. Let's find out what's going on. Can you check your date of birth for me?","m":[10,1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Fourth of March, 1969."},{"t":"0:36","band":"His symptoms","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice the stiffness?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"About two weeks ago. Since they put the haloperidol up to twice a day."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"What's it like day to day? Getting out of bed, out of a chair, starting to walk?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"All hard. I shuffle now. I fell over last week, in the corridor."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. Did you hurt yourself?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Just a bruise on my hip."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"Any shaking in your hands?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"People say my hands shake when I'm watching telly. And my face doesn't move like it did. My voice is flat."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Have you had any sudden muscle spasms, like your neck twisting, your eyes rolling up, or your tongue feeling thick?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Do you feel restless inside, like you can't sit still or have to keep pacing?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"No. If anything, I can't get moving."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Any movements of your mouth or tongue you can't control, like chewing or lip smacking?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Any fever, sweating, or feeling muddled? And any other new tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"No. Just the haloperidol."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Prepare to examine","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd like to examine how your muscles and movements are working. I'll look at your face, hands, arms and walking. Nothing painful. Is that okay?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Yes, fine."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Nurse Alice will be with us as a chaperone. I've cleaned my hands. Any pain anywhere, apart from your hip?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"No. Just the hip's a bit sore."},{"t":"3:02","band":"Examine: at rest and mouth","w":"D","s":"Please sit back with your hands on your knees and feet flat. I'm just going to watch for a few seconds.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"I can see a gentle tremor in your right hand, and your face doesn't move much. Now please open your mouth and leave your tongue resting. Then stick it out.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"(opens mouth, then protrudes tongue)"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Your tongue is still, with no unusual movements. Good.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:39","band":"Examine: arms","w":"D","s":"Hold both arms out in front of you, palms down. Now relax your arm and let me move it. I'll bend your wrist and elbow.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"I can feel a catch, catch stiffness at your wrist. Now tap your thumb and finger together quickly. That's getting slower and smaller.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"It feels like it's sticking."},{"t":"4:10","band":"Examine: standing and walking","w":"D","s":"Can you stand up for me, if you can without pushing on the chair? I'll stay right beside you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"(slowly, with effort) There."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"You're standing still, with no restlessness. Lift your arms out to the side, and now let them drop.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Now walk to the door, turn, and walk back. Alice is beside you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"(shuffles to the door, turns in several steps, returns)"},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"Short steps, and your arms don't swing much. Thank you. Please sit down.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Here's what I found. Your muscles are stiff, your hands shake at rest, and your movements and walking have slowed. This is a side effect called parkinsonism, caused by the higher haloperidol dose.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Is it Parkinson's disease? Will I be stuck like this?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thought. It isn't Parkinson's disease. It's from the medicine, and it usually gets better once we change it.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:32","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are three options. Go back to the lower dose, if your mood and thinking stay settled. Switch to a medicine less likely to cause stiffness. Or add a tablet called procyclidine for a short while.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Procyclidine can cause a dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision and muddled thinking, so it's only short term. I'll discuss this with your consultant today.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Because of the fall, the physio will check your walking and we'll check your blood pressure lying down and then standing. Use the handrails meanwhile.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you understood?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"The stiffness is from the haloperidol. It should get better if you change it. Careful walking for now."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Tell the nurses at once if you get a fever, sweating, muddled thinking, or sudden spasms. We'll review you together tomorrow. Can I update your mum?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. She'll be relieved."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, explains purpose and acknowledges the mother's concern","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about stiffness, slowness, tremor, gait and falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about acute dystonia and inner restlessness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about involuntary mouth, tongue or facial movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links onset to the dose increase, checks other drugs and screens for NMS","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gains consent, cleans hands, offers a chaperone and asks about pain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observes at rest with hands on knees, and examines face, mouth and tongue","d":"Content"},{"c":"Examines outstretched hands, wrist and elbow tone, and finger tapping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observes standing for restlessness, arm drop, and gait with arm swing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his discomfort, fall and fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains findings in plain words and links them to haloperidol","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers options: dose reduction, switch, short-term procyclidine; plans falls safety","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for NMS or dystonia, and arranges review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks about all four EPSE groups and NMS, examines in a clear order with consent and chaperone, explains parkinsonism and offers sensible options.","borderline":"Finds the stiffness but skips mouth, standing or gait, or never asks about akathisia or dystonia.","fail":"Unstructured examination without consent, misses the dose link, or prescribes long-term procyclidine without reviewing the drug."}},"c-s-21":{"setup":"Willow Ward, Tuesday afternoon. Gary Pendleton, 54, on day 8 of an alcohol detox after admission with suicidal thoughts, has asked for a liver check. Examine his abdomen and look for signs of liver disease and encephalopathy, telling him what you find.","role":{"who":"Mr Gary Pendleton, 54, inpatient with alcohol dependence","open":"Gary's fine. Yeah. Years ago they said my liver was 'a bit fatty'. Now I'm off the drink, it's all I think about.","manner":"Friendly, grateful and a little anxious. Does whatever is asked once it is explained. Goes quiet and frightened when a sign is found; settles when the doctor is honest and kind.","free":["Told years ago his liver was fatty","Eight days without a drink, the longest in three years","Wants to know if he has done lasting damage"],"ifasked":["No pain anywhere","No yellow eyes, itching, swelling or black stools noticed","Happy for HCA Steve to chaperone","Knows the date and the ward name","His father died of liver disease at 60","Morning shakes have stopped since the detox"],"worries":["How am I looking?","So I could have cirrhosis?","What happens if it's bad?"],"rules":["Asks 'What are you doing?' if touched without explanation","Asks 'How am I looking?' if the doctor stays silent about findings","Becomes quiet and tearful when spider naevi are mentioned; settles if validated","Signs to find: palmar erythema, four spider naevi, liver edge 2 cm below the ribs; everything else normal"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Pendleton? I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. I've just gelled my hands. The nurses tell me you'd like your liver checked.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Gary's fine. Yeah. Years ago they said my liver was 'a bit fatty'. Now I'm off the drink, it's all I think about."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"That's a very natural worry once your head clears. Before I start, any pain anywhere, or anything you've noticed, like yellow eyes, a swollen tummy or itching?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"No pain. Nothing I've spotted. It's just the worry, really."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"I'll look at your hands, face, chest, tummy and legs, and test your coordination. A complete check also covers the back passage and genitals, which we don't need to do today. Is that all right?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Go for it."},{"t":"0:53","band":"Position","w":"D","s":"Would you like a nurse with us as a chaperone? Then if you could take your top off and lie back with one pillow. I'll keep a sheet over your legs.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Steve can stay, he's been looking after me. Like this?"},{"t":"1:11","band":"From the end of the bed","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll talk you through each bit. First I'm just standing back to look at your colour, whether you're shaky, and how alert you are.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Do I look all right?"},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Good colour, no yellow tinge, you're wide awake and not trembling, and your breath smells normal, with none of the sweet musty smell a struggling liver can cause.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"1:36","band":"Hands and pulse","w":"D","s":"Hold your hands out flat, then turn them palms up.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"They used to shake like mad first thing in the morning."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That was withdrawal, and it's settled. Your nails look healthy, but your palms are red around the edges. That redness can go with liver strain, so I'm noting it.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Is that bad?"},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"On its own it's a clue, not a verdict. Let me build the whole picture. I'm feeling your pulse now: 76, steady and regular.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"2:10","band":"The flap and coordination","w":"D","s":"Now arms straight out, bend your wrists right back as if stopping traffic, fingers apart, and hold it while I count.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Like a lollipop man?"},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Exactly. That's fifteen seconds, and no flapping. A jerky flap there would mean toxins from the liver were reaching the brain. Now touch your nose, then my finger, and keep going back and forth. Then the other hand.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Am I doing it right?"},{"t":"2:38","band":"Thinking","w":"D","s":"Spot on, no wobble. Two quick checks of your thinking: what's the date, and where are we? Then could you copy this five-pointed star?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Tuesday the 10th. Willow Ward. There's your star. Wonky, but it's there."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"All five points. Muddled thinking or trouble copying shapes can be the earliest sign of liver toxins, so that's reassuring.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"3:02","band":"Eyes, mouth and chest","w":"D","s":"Look up for me, and I'll gently pull your lower lid down. No yellow, not pale. Now keep your head still and watch my finger.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Fine, no double vision."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"No flickering of the eyes. Open your mouth... healthy. And on your chest I can see four little red spots with spidery vessels. They're called spider naevi.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"(goes quiet) So I've done damage. I knew it. My dad died of his liver at sixty."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry about your dad. It's no wonder this frightens you. These spots tell me the liver has been under strain, not how much. We'll find that out, and stopping drinking is the biggest thing you can do. You've done it.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Eight days. Longest in three years."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"That's a real achievement. No breast swelling either, which liver disease can cause. Now your tummy: any sore spots before I press? I'll be watching your face.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:05","band":"Tummy","w":"P","s":"No, go ahead."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"Gently all over, then deeper. Now a big breath in, and out. I can feel your liver edge about two finger-breadths below the ribs. It's smooth and not tender.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Is it meant to be there?"},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Normally you can't feel it, so it's a little enlarged, which fits with fat or inflammation from alcohol. Now your spleen on the left, then each kidney with a hand behind your back. Neither felt.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"I'm tapping down your ribs to size the liver, then across your tummy. Could you roll onto your side towards me? Thanks. No fluid moving. And I'll listen: normal bowel sounds.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:53","band":"Legs and walking","w":"P","s":"Rolling back now."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Last bit: your legs. I'm pressing on your shins and ankles for swelling, then tapping your knee, ankle and elbow reflexes.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Ticklish! Nothing swollen?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"No swelling, and normal reflexes. If you feel steady, walk to the door and back, then stand feet together and close your eyes. I'm right beside you.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"Straight line, see? No wobble."},{"t":"5:19","band":"What it means","w":"D","s":"Steady as a rock. That's everything; do you need a hand with your top? So: no yellowing, no fluid, no flap, no confusion, normal eyes and balance. No sign your liver is failing or affecting your brain.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"But?"},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"But three things show strain: red palms, a few spider naevi and a slightly large liver. A hands-on check can't tell how scarred a liver is.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"So I could have cirrhosis?"},{"t":"5:50","band":"Next steps and close","w":"D","s":"It's possible, and I won't pretend otherwise. So today we'll take blood for liver tests, clotting, blood count and kidneys, and I'll ask for an ultrasound and a FibroScan, a painless scan that measures liver stiffness.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"And if it's bad?"},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"Then you'd see the liver team, and earlier is better. Livers can recover a lot once drinking stops. Could you sum up for me what happens next, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Bloods today, then a scan to see how stiff it is. And stay off the booze."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Tell the nurses straight away if you get yellow eyes, a swelling tummy, feel muddled or very sleepy, or see blood in vomit or black stools. I'll speak to Dr Hughes and bring your results tomorrow.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"Will do. Better to know, isn't it? Thanks, doc."}],"marks":[{"c":"Gels hands, introduces, explains the check, gains consent and says rectal and genital checks are omitted","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks briefly about pain and symptoms before starting, without a drinking history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers a chaperone and positions him flat on one pillow with dignity","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Inspects from the end of the bed for jaundice, alertness, tremor and fetor","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks nails, palms and the radial pulse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests for asterixis for at least 15 seconds and checks finger-nose coordination","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation and constructional ability with a star or clock","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks eyes for jaundice, pallor and nystagmus, and the chest for spider naevi and gynaecomastia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Palpates the abdomen lightly and deeply, for liver, spleen and kidneys, watching his face","d":"Content"},{"c":"Percusses the liver, tests shifting dullness and listens for bowel sounds","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks legs for oedema, reflexes, gait and Romberg","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tells him findings as he goes and responds to his fear with empathy and honesty","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains next tests (liver bloods, INR, FBC, ultrasound, FibroScan) and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets four warning signs and closes with who will follow up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A complete, ordered liver examination with a proper flap test and confusion screen, findings shared as he goes, and honest next steps with a safety net.","borderline":"Covers the abdomen well but rushes the flap or skips orientation, or examines quietly and explains only at the end.","fail":"Starts at the abdomen and misses the encephalopathy signs, touches without explaining, or falsely tells him his liver is fine."}},"c-s-35":{"setup":"Monday 10:00, CAMHS clinic. Mrs Kerry Walsh, 41, is worried about her son Callum, 12, who is at school, underachieving and in frequent detention. Take a collateral history to explore possible ADHD and address her concerns.","role":{"who":"Mrs Kerry Walsh, 41, woman, Callum's mother","open":"School, mainly. He's in detention three times a week and they've moved him down to the bottom sets. I'm terrified he'll be excluded.","manner":"Anxious and talkative, protective of Callum; tearful about his self-esteem; a little defensive about shouting at home. Relieved by a clear, honest plan.","free":["Detentions three times a week; moved to bottom sets in Year 8","Teachers say he doesn't listen, shouts out and walks out of lessons","She wonders if it is ADHD"],"ifasked":["Can do homework for about ten minutes but games for three hours","Loses PE kit and belongings weekly","Born five weeks early by emergency Caesarean; milestones normal","Problems since nursery; worse since Year 7","Same at football and at his grandmother's","Two A&E visits: broken wrist and stitches","Father was similar and left school at 15","No reading problems, good friends, not defiant; on the Xbox until 11 pm","Parents shout but have never hit him","He says he's stupid and dreads Mondays; no talk of self-harm"],"worries":["Will he be excluded?","Is it ADHD, and is it from his dad?","Will tablets turn him into a zombie?"],"rules":["Gives everyday examples readily when asked for them; vague if asked 'Can he concentrate?'","Becomes tearful when talking about his self-esteem; settles if validated","Mentions the A&E visits only if asked about risky behaviour","Admits shouting only if asked kindly how they handle it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Walsh. I'm Dr Iqbal, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Thanks for coming in while Callum's at school. What's been worrying you most?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"School, mainly. He's in detention three times a week and they've moved him down to the bottom sets. I'm terrified he'll be excluded."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and worrying, especially with the school ringing so often. Let's go through it, and I'll ask about his whole life, not just school. Is that alright?","m":[11,1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Yes. I just want someone to understand him."},{"t":"0:39","band":"Inattention","w":"D","s":"What do the teachers say is happening in class?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Not listening, staring out of the window, never finishing work. He forgets his homework every single day."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"At home, how long can he stick at something that's a bit boring, like homework or reading?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Ten minutes, maybe. Then he's off."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Does he lose things, or start jobs and leave them?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"PE kit, coat, his phone charger. Every week. But he can play the Xbox for three hours."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"That's really common. Games give constant rewards, so they don't tell us much about attention. What about getting ready in the morning?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"I have to tell him five times to put his shoes on."},{"t":"1:33","band":"Hyperactivity","w":"D","s":"Does he find it hard to stay sitting, at meals or in class?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"He's up and down at dinner. Teachers say he walks out of lessons. He's always fidgeting, tapping."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Is he loud or always on the go, more than other boys his age?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Much more. His cousins are calm next to him. He's like a motor."},{"t":"2:00","band":"Impulsivity","w":"D","s":"Does he blurt out, interrupt, or find it hard to wait his turn?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Shouts out answers. Pushes in. Then he says sorry two minutes later and means it."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Has he done risky things without thinking, like running into the road or jumping off things?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"He's been to A&E twice: a broken wrist off his bike and stitches from jumping off a wall."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Onset and settings","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice all this? Was it the same at primary school?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Since nursery, really. The reports always said 'needs to focus'. It got much worse in Year 7."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Is it the same at football, at his gran's, at his dad's?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Football coach says he never listens to instructions. My mum says she can't take him to the shops."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Development and family","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about the pregnancy and his early years?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Pregnancy was fine, but he came five weeks early, an emergency Caesarean. He walked and talked on time."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Does anyone in the family have similar difficulties?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"His dad. Never sat still, left school at fifteen. I've wondered if he's got ADHD himself."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Other explanations","w":"D","s":"Some other things can look similar, so I'll check. Does Callum struggle with reading, spelling or maths?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"No, he reads fine when he sits still long enough."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"How does he get on with friends? Any very fixed interests or routines, or trouble with noise or textures?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"He's got loads of mates. No routines, he's the opposite."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Does he argue and defy you on purpose, or is it more that he forgets and acts first?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"He's not nasty. He does what he's told, he just forgets."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"How is he sleeping, and has his hearing ever been checked? Any staring spells where he switches off?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"He's on the Xbox till eleven. Hearing was fine last year. No staring spells."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"At 12, I ask every parent: any alcohol, vaping or drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"No. Not that I know of."},{"t":"4:30","band":"Impact and risk","w":"D","s":"How is all this affecting him in himself?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"He says he's stupid. He's started saying he hates school and feels sick on Monday mornings. (Her voice breaks.) He's a lovely boy, and everyone just sees the bad bits."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you love him, and how hard it is to watch him lose confidence. Has he ever talked about hurting himself, or wishing he wasn't here?","m":[11,8]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"No, never that. Just sad and fed up."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Has he lashed out, or been picked on by other children?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"He pushed a boy once. And some lads wind him up to get him in trouble."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"When he won't listen, it can be really hard at home. How do you and his dad handle it?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"We shout. I'm not proud of it. We've never hit him."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest; most parents shout sometimes. What are you hoping for from us?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Is it ADHD? And I don't want him drugged up like a zombie."},{"t":"5:46","band":"Explain and close","w":"D","s":"From what you've described, ADHD is a real possibility: problems since nursery, at home, school and football, and they're affecting him. I can't diagnose from one conversation. I'll ask school for a report and questionnaires, and see Callum himself.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"And the tablets?"},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Medicine is never automatic. If it is ADHD, we'd start with information and a parent programme, and talk about medicine together. Used properly, it shouldn't make him a zombie.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's a relief, actually."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me back what happens next? And if he ever talks about hurting himself, call us or 111 straight away.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"You get the school forms, then see Callum. And I ring if he says anything like that."},{"t":"6:42","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll also write to the school's SENCo about support now, without waiting for a diagnosis.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduced self, opened with her main concern and set the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explored inattention with everyday examples at home and school","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explored hyperactivity with examples and comparison with peers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explored impulsivity, including blurting, waiting turns and risky acts","d":"Content"},{"c":"Established onset in early childhood and presence across settings","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about pregnancy, prematurity, milestones and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screened for learning difficulty, autism, defiance, sleep, hearing, absences and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assessed impact on self-esteem and mood, including self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked about injuries, peer conflict and how discipline is handled at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explored her ideas and concerns about ADHD and medicine","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responded with empathy to her worry and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explained that ADHD is possible but needs school information and seeing Callum","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked her understanding, gave a safety net and closed with clear next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers all three domains with examples, onset and settings, development, the main look-alikes and risk, and leaves her with an honest plan.","borderline":"Covers the symptoms but misses differentials, home discipline or her concerns about medicine.","fail":"Asks only about school behaviour, diagnoses or promises medicine on her account alone, or misses risk and impact."}},"c-s-36":{"setup":"CAMHS clinic, Tuesday afternoon. Jake Bradley, 9, was diagnosed with ADHD two weeks ago. His father has come back alone with questions about ADHD and medication. Explain the condition and the plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Bradley, 42, Jake's father, an electrician","open":"Look, I'll be straight. Half the family reckon ADHD's just a label for a naughty kid.","manner":"Blunt, slightly defensive, arms folded. Softens and becomes curious once he feels he isn't being blamed; quietly emotional when he recognises himself in Jake.","free":["Jake has run into the road twice this year","The family say ADHD is a label for bad behaviour","He wants to know about 'these tablets'","Jake's mum couldn't come; he will report back"],"ifasked":["He was 'exactly the same' at school and left at 16","The school moved Jake to the front a year ago and uses a sticker chart; it hasn't helped much","No fainting, chest pain or palpitations; no sudden deaths in the family","Jake sleeps by about 9 pm and eats well","He works long hours and feels guilty","Jake says he's 'the bad one' in class"],"worries":["Will it turn him into a zombie?","Isn't a stimulant addictive?","Will he grow out of it?","Is it my fault?"],"rules":["Interrupts if given more than three facts without a pause: 'Hang on, slow down'","Becomes quiet and a bit tearful when he admits he was the same; settles if validated","Agrees to the plan only after the medicine worries are answered honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mr Bradley, I'm Dr Khan, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Thanks for coming back. I've got about half an hour for you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Look, I'll be straight. Half the family reckon ADHD's just a label for a naughty kid."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you said that straight out; a lot of parents hear it. What have you made of it all yourself since we met?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I don't know. He's a handful, but he's a good lad. And the school keep ringing. I want to know about these tablets too."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Let's cover both, then. What's the one thing you most want answered today?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Whether he actually needs medicine. And whether this is something we did."},{"t":"0:50","band":"What ADHD is","w":"D","s":"I'll come to both. First, ADHD affects three things: keeping attention, controlling how active you are, and stopping to think before acting.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"All 9-year-olds are lively. With Jake it's far more than his age, at home and at school, and it's causing harm, like running into the road.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"That's true. He's off before you can grab him."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"And a naughty child chooses it. Jake gets told off and still can't stop, even when he's trying. Does that fit?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Yeah. He told me last week he's 'the bad one' in class. That got to me."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"That must have been hard to hear. It's one reason getting this right matters: so he stops seeing himself that way.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","band":"Why it happens","w":"D","s":"You asked if it's something you did. ADHD comes from how the brain develops. The parts that work like brakes develop differently.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Genes play the biggest part. Being born early, or smoking or alcohol in pregnancy, add a little. Parenting, sugar and screens don't cause it.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"I work all hours, though. I'm hardly there."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear you feel guilty about that. Your hours didn't cause this. Your time with him helps him cope, but it isn't the cause. Was anyone in the family like Jake as a boy?","m":[5,4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"...Me, honestly. Teachers said I'd never sit still. I left school at sixteen."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That fits: it runs strongly in families. It also means you know how he feels. If it still affects you, your GP can refer you for an adult assessment.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"Hang on, slow down. So it's in the genes, and it's not a label."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. Shall I go on to what helps?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:02","band":"Home and school","w":"D","s":"There are three parts: home, school and medicine. For home, we'll offer you and Jake's mum an ADHD parent group, about 10 to 12 weekly sessions.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"A parenting class? I'm not a bad dad."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"You're clearly not. It isn't a test. It teaches what works for ADHD brains: short instructions, instant praise, firm routines. Parents often say it's the most useful part.","m":[6,5]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. What about school? They moved him to the front last year."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"With your permission, I'll write to the SENCO, who leads on special needs, asking for a written support plan: short tasks, movement breaks, and a rating form so we can track progress.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Yes, do that."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Medicine","w":"D","s":"Because he's still struggling despite those changes, and he's at risk near roads, medicine makes sense. The first choice is methylphenidate.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"It helps those brakes work for the school day. It starts within an hour. It doesn't cure ADHD, but most children focus better and feel more in control.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"I don't want him turned into a zombie."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. At the right dose children are still themselves. If he seems flat or tearful, the dose is too high and we lower it.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Before he starts, a few heart questions. Has Jake ever fainted while running, had chest pain or a racing heart? Any sudden deaths in the family under 40?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Good. We'll also check his height, weight, pulse and blood pressure first. A heart tracing is only needed if there's a concern, and there isn't.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Isn't it addictive, though? It's a stimulant."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Taken by mouth as prescribed, children don't get addicted. It is a controlled drug, so keep it locked away and never share it.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:20","band":"Side effects and monitoring","w":"D","s":"The common side effects are a smaller appetite at lunch, trouble getting to sleep, headaches or tummy aches. Usually mild, and often they settle.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"We start low, at 18 milligrams each morning with breakfast, and adjust weekly. We check pulse and blood pressure at each change, weight every three months and height every six.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"And will he grow out of it?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"The restlessness often eases with age; concentration problems more often carry on, but people learn to manage. We'll review every year whether he still needs it.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"6:02","w":"D","s":"One safety point: keep holding his hand near roads until we see a change.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's a lot. What will you tell Jake's mum tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"That it's how his brain's wired, not us. There's a parent group, a letter to school, and the tablets with heart and growth checks."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Spot on. Here's a leaflet. If he faints, gets chest pain or seems very low, stop it and call us or 111.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"Once his mum agrees, we'll start. I'll phone weekly while we adjust the dose and see you both, with Jake, in six weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:44","w":"P","s":"Right. Thanks, doc. That's clearer than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, and sets the purpose of the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he knows and what he most wants answered before explaining","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains ADHD's three core features and why it differs from ordinary liveliness or naughtiness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains causes: brain development, strong genetic link, pregnancy factors; not parenting","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to his guilt and to Jake calling himself 'the bad one'","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers an ADHD parent-training group and explains it is not a judgement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a letter to the SENCO, with consent, for a written school support plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains how methylphenidate helps, how fast, and that it is not a cure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Takes a cardiac and family sudden-death history and names baseline checks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains common side effects, starting dose, titration and the monitoring schedule","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding in chunks and with a teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives safety advice: road safety, locked storage, when to stop and call","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Closes with a leaflet, titration contact and a dated follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"He leaves knowing ADHD is not his fault, what the home, school and medicine plan is, and what checks keep Jake safe.","borderline":"Accurate facts but delivered as a lecture, with the cardiac screen or school plan missed.","fail":"Medicine-only talk, blaming or dismissing the father, no cardiac questions, or no checking of understanding."}},"c-s-37":{"setup":"CAMHS clinic, Thursday morning. Mrs Laura Bennett, 33, is worried about her son Noah, 5, who talked late and seems unlike his older brother. Build his developmental history in time order.","role":{"who":"Mrs Laura Bennett, 33, Noah's mother, a dental receptionist","open":"His brother Alfie was chatting at two. Noah just isn't the same, and nobody's told me why.","manner":"Anxious, talks quickly, compares Noah with Alfie. Becomes tearful about head-banging and her guilt about working; steadier and more detailed once validated.","free":["Noah was late to talk","He is 'in his own world'","The teacher says he doesn't join in","A paediatrician once said he was 'just a late talker'"],"ifasked":["Normal pregnancy, born at 39 weeks, no special care","Sat at 7 months, walked at 13 months","First words at about two and a half; now short phrases to ask for things; repeats cartoon lines","Doesn't turn to his name; leads her by the hand; doesn't point to share","Lines up cars, spins wheels, knows every train on his line; must take the same route to school","Hates hand dryers and sock seams; eats only plain pasta, toast and crisps","Bangs his head on the floor in meltdowns; no injuries needing treatment","Hearing test normal last spring; no fits; not overactive; takes two hours to fall asleep; his father's cousin is autistic"],"worries":["Did going back to work cause this?","Is it autism?","Will he have friends?"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers to closed questions; gives rich detail when asked for an example","Becomes tearful describing head-banging; settles if validated","Mentions going back to work only if feeling listened to"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Bennett, I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Thanks for coming. Tell me what you've noticed about Noah.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"His brother Alfie was chatting at two. Noah just isn't the same, and nobody's told me why."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That sounds worrying, and you know him best. I'll go through his story from pregnancy to now, then how he talks, plays and copes with change. Is that OK?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Yes. The paediatrician said he was just a late talker, but it's more than that."},{"t":"0:35","band":"Early development","w":"D","s":"Let's start at the beginning. How was the pregnancy? Was he born on time, and did he need any special care after birth?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Fine. Born at 39 weeks, normal delivery, no special care."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How old was he when he sat up, and when he started walking?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Sat at about seven months, walked at thirteen. That was all normal."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"And his talking: as a baby, did he babble? When did his first words come, and when did he put two words together?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Not much babbling. First real words were about two and a half. Two words only this last year, like 'want juice'. Alfie had sentences at two."},{"t":"1:31","band":"Communication and social","w":"D","s":"Show me what he does when he wants a drink and the words won't come.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"He grabs my hand and pulls me to the fridge. He puts my hand on the door."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Does he ever repeat things, like lines from TV?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"All the time. Whole bits from his cartoons, in the same voice."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"If you call his name from across the room, what happens?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"Usually nothing. I used to think he was deaf."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Has his hearing been tested?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Last spring. It was normal."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Does he point to show you something interesting, like a plane, just to share it? Does he look back to see if you've seen?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"No. He only points if he wants something."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"How does he play? Any pretend play, like feeding a teddy? And with other children?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"He lines his cars up and spins the wheels. At school he plays on his own. He doesn't hug me much either."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That must be hard, as his mum. Does he share a smile or bring things to show you?","m":[11,5]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, when he's happy. Not often."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Routines and sensory","w":"D","s":"What does he love most, and what happens if a routine changes?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Trains. He knows every train on our line. We have to take the same route to school or he melts down. He flaps his hands when he's excited."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"Is he bothered by noises, clothes or food textures?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Hand dryers terrify him. Socks have to be inside out. He eats plain pasta, toast and crisps, that's it."},{"t":"3:38","band":"Course and safety","w":"D","s":"When did you first feel he was different? And has he ever lost skills or words he once had?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Around two. He's never lost anything, he's just been slow."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"When he melts down, what does he do? Does he ever hurt himself?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"(tearful) He bangs his head on the floor. I hold a cushion under him. I can't stop him."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening to watch, and you're doing the right thing protecting his head. Has he ever needed checking for an injury?","m":[11,10]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"No, just red marks. Sorry. I went back to work when he was one. I keep thinking I did this."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. These differences come from how a child's brain develops. Going back to work didn't cause them.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:34","band":"Other conditions","w":"D","s":"A few more areas. How are his sleep and eating? Is he very active, or clumsy?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"Takes two hours to fall asleep. He's not hyper, not clumsy."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"Any fits, or spells where he stares blankly and you can't reach him? How does he learn compared with others in his class?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"No fits. His teacher says he knows letters and numbers well. It's joining in that's hard."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Anyone in the family with autism, late speech or learning difficulties?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"His dad's cousin is autistic."},{"t":"5:12","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"How is all this affecting Noah, you and Alfie day to day?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Everything's planned around him. Alfie gets left out. I'm exhausted. My mum helps a bit."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry. Does anyone from school or social care support you at the moment?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"No. School are kind, but nothing formal."},{"t":"5:37","band":"Summary and next steps","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it: walked on time, talked late, repeats TV lines, doesn't turn to his name or point to share, plays alone, loves trains and routine, and finds noise and textures hard. Is that right?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's Noah. Is it autism? Just tell me."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"I understand you want an answer. Some of this fits autism, but we need a full assessment to be sure and to rule out other things.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"Our team will see Noah, get a report from school with your consent, and arrange speech and language therapy. We'll also give you a sleep plan and ideas for meltdowns now.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"What will you tell his dad about what happens next?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"That you're going to assess him properly, ask school, and get him speech therapy."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If he ever hurts his head and is sick or drowsy, take him to A&E. Otherwise, call us with any worries.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"Thank you. It's a relief someone's listening."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, invites her concerns openly and explains the structure","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes pregnancy, birth and neonatal history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes motor and language milestones with ages, in time order","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about requesting, echolalia and response to name, with examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pointing to share, shared enjoyment, pretend play and peer play","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about narrow interests, routines and repetitive movements","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sensory sensitivities, including food","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about loss of skills and a hearing test","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens sleep, activity, coordination, seizures, learning and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-injury during meltdowns and safety","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her distress and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about impact on Noah, family and current support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises back, explains next steps without a premature diagnosis, and checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A dated timeline with examples in every autism area, regression and hearing asked, self-injury explored, and a clear next step.","borderline":"Covers autism features but out of order or with closed questions, missing regression or co-occurring conditions.","fail":"No milestones, a symptom checklist without examples, a diagnosis given on the spot, or her distress ignored."}},"c-s-38":{"setup":"CAMHS clinic. Isla Doyle, 14, has completed her autism assessment and meets criteria. She has agreed her mother hears the outcome first. Explain the diagnosis and next steps to Mrs Doyle.","role":{"who":"Mrs Claire Doyle, 45, Isla's mother, a primary school teaching assistant","open":"So, is it autism? And if it is, how did everyone miss it for ten years?","manner":"Organised and direct, has a list of questions. Becomes upset when blaming herself; relieved and engaged once reassured. Wants practical answers.","free":["She suspected autism since Year 7","A friend's son was diagnosed at four","She has a list of questions"],"ifasked":["Isla refuses school about two Mondays a month and says she's exhausted","Isla lies awake for one to two hours most nights","She eats a narrow range of foods; weight is stable","No self-harm or talk of suicide that she knows of","Isla's father is 'very like her'","Isla wants to read her report herself before talking"],"worries":["Is it Asperger's?","Did vaccines or something I did cause it?","Is there a tablet?","Will she live independently?"],"rules":["Gets tearful when asking if she caused it; settles with clear reassurance","Interrupts with 'what does that mean?' if jargon is used","Asks 'what about school?' if school isn't raised by 5 minutes"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Doyle, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Isla's told us she's happy for me to share everything with you today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"So, is it autism? And if it is, how did everyone miss it for ten years?"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'll answer both. Before I do, what do you already know about autism, and what else is on your list?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Bits from the internet. Asperger's, what causes it, whether there's a tablet, and what school will do."},{"t":"0:36","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Thank you. The assessment shows Isla is autistic.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"(pause) Right. I thought so."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"It means her brain works differently in how she reads people, makes conversation and copes with change. She'll often have strengths too, like deep focus and honesty.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"It's lifelong and part of who she is, not an illness she caught. How does that sit with you?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Relief, honestly. And a bit sad. I feel I should have pushed sooner."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Both feelings make sense. You did notice, and that's why she's here.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:20","band":"Why now","w":"D","s":"You asked why it was missed. Many girls watch and copy others, and hide their difficulties.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"That can work in primary school. In secondary, friendships get more complicated and it becomes exhausting to keep up.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"That's exactly Year 7. She came home and just shut down."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"That fits. You mentioned Asperger's: that was the old name for autism without language delay. Now doctors use one term, autism, for the whole range.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"So she hasn't got 'mild autism'?"},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"We'd say she's autistic without a learning disability or language difficulty. Her needs are real even though she copes well on the surface. Does that make sense so far?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Yes. Go on."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Causes","w":"D","s":"Autism is mostly genetic, from many genes working together. It isn't caused by parenting, diet or vaccines.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I did wonder about the vaccines. And I worked full time when she was small."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"I can see you've been carrying that. Please hear me clearly: nothing you did caused this. Is anyone in the family a bit like Isla?","m":[6,5]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Her dad, actually. Routines, hates parties. Now I think of it."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"That's common, and it fits with the genetic link.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:56","band":"What else to watch for","w":"D","s":"Autistic young people more often have other difficulties too: anxiety, low mood, sleep problems, eating difficulties, and sometimes ADHD. How is Isla with those?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Anxious, definitely. She refuses school some Mondays. Says she's exhausted."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. How are her sleep and eating?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Awake an hour or two most nights. She eats the same few foods, but her weight's fine."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Has she ever talked about hurting herself, or about wishing you weren't here?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"No. She says she hates being 'weird', but nothing like that."},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've asked her. We'll keep checking her mood, eating and any self-harm at each visit. The anxiety and Monday exhaustion are things we can help.","m":[8,7]},{"t":"3:55","band":"What helps","w":"P","s":"Is there a tablet for autism?"},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"There's no medicine for autism itself. Medicine can help things alongside it if they become severe, like anxiety or sleep.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"First, we'll offer CBT adapted for autistic young people for her anxiety, and a sleep plan. If sleep stays poor, melatonin is an option later.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"What about school?"},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"With your and Isla's consent, I'll write to the SENCO, who leads special needs support. We'd suggest a time-out card, a quiet space at lunch, and warning before timetable changes.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Autism can count as a disability, so school must make reasonable adjustments. If she needs more, the council can do an EHC needs assessment.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"That would really help. They'd listen if it came from you."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"And for you: there's a parent group after diagnosis, and you can ask the council for a carer's assessment. The National Autistic Society has good information too.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Will she manage as an adult? Live on her own, have a job?"},{"t":"5:17","w":"D","s":"That's a natural worry. Many autistic adults live independently, work and have relationships. Support now gives her the best chance.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Shall we pause there? What questions do you still have?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Just how we tell Isla. She's been asking."},{"t":"5:40","band":"Telling Isla and close","w":"D","s":"Good question. How would Isla like to hear it, and who should be there?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"She'd want to read the report herself first, then talk. Maybe with me."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"Then we'll send it to you both and see her next week, at her pace. She can decide who else she tells.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"To check I've been clear, what will you tell Isla's dad tonight?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"That she's autistic, it's genetic, not our fault. No tablet for it, but help for her anxiety and sleep, and a letter to school."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. You'll get the written report, and so will your GP.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"If she starts self-harming, loses weight, or stops going to school, ring us. In an emergency, 111 or A&E.","m":[7,13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I came in dreading this, and I feel better."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Isla's consent, and asks what she knows and wants to know","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States the diagnosis plainly and explains autism in everyday words, including strengths","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains late recognition in girls: masking and rising demands at secondary school","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains that Asperger's is now included within autism","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains causes: mainly genetic; not parenting, diet or vaccines","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to relief, sadness and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about anxiety, mood, self-harm, sleep and eating","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains co-occurring conditions and how each will be followed up","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains that no medicine treats autism itself","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees consented SENCO letter with specific adjustments and the EHC route","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers family support: parent group, carer's assessment, information","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Chunks information, pauses for questions and uses teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans how Isla hears it, gives a safety net and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear diagnosis, honest answers to every question, co-occurring problems screened, a school and family plan, and Isla kept in charge.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but no screening for mood, self-harm or eating, or no plan for telling Isla.","fail":"Jargon or deficit-only framing, a promise of medicine or cure, or the mother's guilt left unanswered."}},"c-s-39":{"setup":"Paediatric ED, Saturday 01:30. Chloe Hughes, 15, is medically cleared after an ibuprofen overdose. The charge nurse wants to know whether she can go home when her mother arrives. Agree the plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Sam Patel, 38, male, paediatric ED charge nurse","open":"She's medically fit, mum's twenty minutes away. Can she go home when mum gets here? I've got six kids waiting.","manner":"Friendly but harried; repeats the bed pressure twice. Becomes cooperative and offers an observation bed once his pressure is acknowledged and the reasons are clear.","free":["ED have medically cleared her","Six children are waiting for cubicles","Her mother is about 20 minutes away","Chloe says she wants to go home"],"ifasked":["Weight 55 kg; sixteen 200 mg tablets at 22:00 (about 58 mg/kg)","Paracetamol and salicylate levels negative at 4 hours","She has not tried to leave or harm herself in ED","CP-IS shows she is not known to children's social care","An observation bed on the paediatric ward is free","Chloe told a nurse there is 'stuff at home' she won't talk about"],"worries":["Can't she go to the psych ward?","What if she tries to walk out?","How do you decide tomorrow?","What follow-up is there?"],"rules":["Pushes for discharge twice before accepting the plan","Offers the paediatric observation bed only after his pressure is acknowledged","Gets irritated by guideline numbers without reasons; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and align","w":"D","s":"Hi, I'm Dr Ali, the psychiatry doctor on call. Thanks for waiting. You're Sam, the charge nurse?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes. She's medically fit, mum's twenty minutes away. Can she go home when mum gets here? I've got six kids waiting."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Six waiting at this hour sounds really tough. I want to help you free a cubicle. Can I check the medical side first?","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"What was the dose per kilo, and were paracetamol and salicylate levels done at four hours?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Sixteen 200s, she's 55 kilos, so about 58 per kilo. Paracetamol and salicylate both negative."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that's reassuring. Has she tried to leave or harm herself here?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"No. Quiet. Just keeps saying she wants to go home."},{"t":"1:02","band":"Why not home","w":"D","s":"Medically she's fine. My worry is different. She told the doctor she'd be better off dead, she's been low for six weeks, and this is her first time with us.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"We've no information yet from home, school or social care, and our senior CAMHS team aren't in until morning. Sending her home tonight wouldn't be safe.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"But mum's coming. Mum could watch her at home."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Mum may well be brilliant. But I haven't met her, and we don't know what home is like yet. One night here lets us do it properly.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Can't she go to your psych ward then? We need the space."},{"t":"1:54","band":"Where she stays","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd ask. An adult ward isn't safe or lawful for a 15-year-old, and she doesn't need a mental health bed tonight.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Young people's units are for severe ongoing risk or illness that can't be managed at home. We'd only consider that after a full assessment.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"The likely plan is a paediatric bed tonight, with mum, and our liaison team sees them both in the morning.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Every time it's the same. You lot leave them with us all night."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"You're right that it lands on your team, and I'm sorry. I'll write a clear plan, give you my bleep, and make sure liaison come first thing.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"OK. There's a bed on the observation unit. I can move her there."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"That's perfect, thank you. It frees your cubicle and keeps her safe.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Keeping her safe","w":"D","s":"Can we agree observations? Within eyesight while she's alone; at least every 15 minutes once she's settled with mum.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"We can do that. Mum can stay on the chair-bed."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Great. Could the room be cleared of cords and medicines, and her bag checked with her agreement?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Yes. What if she tries to walk out at four in the morning?"},{"t":"3:32","band":"Consent","w":"D","s":"Please keep her safe and bleep me straight away. I'll check whether she understands the decision; at 15 we use Gillick competence.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"If she's competent and still refuses, I'll get the on-call CAMHS consultant. We don't just override her with mum's consent; a Mental Health Act assessment is the route if it's needed.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"And what do we tell mum? Chloe asked us not to say much."},{"t":"4:06","band":"Confidentiality and safeguarding","w":"D","s":"Mum needs to know about the overdose and the safety plan, because there's a risk to Chloe's life. I'll tell Chloe what's shared and why, and keep other details private where we can.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"She did tell one of my nurses there's 'stuff at home' she won't talk about."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That's important, thank you. Please ask your nurse to write down her exact words. Has CP-IS been checked?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Not known to social care."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Good. I'll ask her gently tonight, and tell the hospital safeguarding team in the morning. If she discloses harm, we refer to children's social care.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"She was also alone at home late at night, so we'll ask mum about supervision without assuming anything.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:07","band":"Tomorrow","w":"P","s":"So how do you decide tomorrow whether she goes home or gets admitted?"},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Our liaison team sees Chloe alone and with mum, and with consent speaks to school and her GP.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"If her risk settles and home is safe, she goes home with a written safety plan. If intent stays high, she's very unwell, or home isn't safe, we look at a young people's inpatient bed.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"And if she goes home, what does she get? We see them back in a month."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"A community CAMHS appointment within seven days, often sooner, and the crisis line number. We'll ask mum to lock away all medicines, and school to deal with the online bullying.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"That's more than I thought."},{"t":"6:07","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So, to recap: observation bed with mum, eyesight then 15-minute checks, safe room, bleep me if she tries to leave or anything changes, liaison in the morning. Does that work for you?","m":[13,6]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Works. I'll move her when mum arrives."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Sam. I'll see Chloe now, then meet mum. I'll write the plan in the notes within half an hour. My bleep's 3361.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Cheers. Appreciate you being straight with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and acknowledges the nurse's bed pressure before disagreeing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks the medical clearance: dose per kilo and paracetamol and salicylate levels","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why she should not go home tonight, naming specific risk factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States the likely outcome: overnight paediatric bed and morning CAMHS review","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why a psychiatric ward is not right tonight and when CAMHS inpatient care is considered","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a specific observation level and safe room measures","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a plan if she tries to leave: Gillick competence, consultant, no override via mother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains what her mother will be told and why","d":"Content"},{"c":"Raises safeguarding: disclosure recorded, CP-IS, safeguarding team, supervision at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains how the morning assessment decides between discharge and admission","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains follow-up: CAMHS within 7 days, safety plan, means restriction, school","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates the nurse's frustration and negotiates collaboratively","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises, checks agreement, and gives documentation and escalation route","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A safe, agreed overnight plan with observations, a leaving plan, safeguarding steps and a clear morning pathway, and the nurse on side.","borderline":"Right decision but argued rigidly, or missing the plan if she leaves or the safeguarding check.","fail":"Agrees discharge tonight, suggests an adult ward, or ignores consent, confidentiality or safeguarding."}},"c-s-40":{"setup":"A&E, Monday evening. Jayden Clarke, 16, came in with a headache; physical checks are normal. The ED doctor is worried about his beliefs. Take a history to establish what is going on.","role":{"who":"Jayden Clarke, 16, male, sixth-form college student","open":"Not here. They can hear what I think.","manner":"Guarded, glances behind him, mutters under his breath. Frightened rather than hostile. Opens up when treated with respect; frustrated if his new word isn't understood.","free":["His headache started yesterday","The government implanted a device in his brain while he slept this week","They are tracking him","He uses the word 'zintered' for the signal entering his head"],"ifasked":["Suspicion began six months ago after reading an article; threw his phone and laptop in the canal last week","Hears voices for a week, talking about him, saying he's watched and 'don't trust the doctor'","The device sends his thoughts out to others; no thought insertion or passivity","Headache dull, no vomiting, visual change, weakness, head injury, fits or fever; no medicines","Weekend cannabis until about four months ago; no spice, cocaine or alcohol","Stopped college three weeks ago after friends called him paranoid","Normal childhood development; father was in a psychiatric hospital once and was 'paranoid'","No thoughts of self-harm; no plan to harm anyone; carries nothing to protect himself"],"worries":["You don't believe me, do you?","Am I going mad?","Don't tell my mum."],"rules":["Mutters and glances behind when the voice speaks; admits it if gently asked","Repeats 'zintered' slowly and becomes irritated if the doctor pretends to understand","Shuts down if told the device isn't real; engages if his fear is validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"P","s":"(glancing behind) Not here. They can hear what I think."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. I'm Dr Okoro, one of the doctors. This is a private room, and I'm here to help, not to judge. Tell me about the headache. When did it start, and what do you think is causing it?","m":[1,12,2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Yesterday. It's the device. They put it in my head while I was asleep."},{"t":"0:32","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"Who put it there?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"The government. They've been tracking me for months."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"How did you first realise?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"I read an article six months ago about them watching people. Then I noticed it was me. My phone, my laptop. I threw them in the canal last week. So they put the device in instead."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"How sure are you about the device, out of 100?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"A hundred. You don't believe me, do you?"},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"I can see how real this is for you, and I won't pretend otherwise. Could there be any other explanation for the headache?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"No. It's zintered. The signal. Why don't you get it?"},{"t":"1:32","band":"Form of thought","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, I don't know that word, and I want to get it right. What does 'zintered' mean?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"(slowly, irritated) Zin-tered. When the signal goes into you. Obviously."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I noticed you looked behind you just then. Are you hearing something right now?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:55","band":"Voices and thoughts","w":"P","s":"(quietly) There's a voice. It says don't trust the doctor."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, I know that's hard with it there. Does it say anything else, or tell you to do things?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"There are two of them. They talk about me, like 'he's going out now, he's being watched'. They don't tell me to do things."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"When did the voices start? Do you hear them when you're alone, in a quiet room?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"About a week. Mostly when it's quiet."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Can other people know what you're thinking?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"The device sends my thoughts out. Everyone in it can hear them."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Does anyone put thoughts in your head, or take them out? Control your body? Do you hear your own thoughts out loud, or see signs meant just for you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody controls me. They just watch. It's just the voices."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Organic screen","w":"D","s":"Back to the headache. Is it worse lying down or first thing in the morning? Any vomiting, blurred vision, weakness, numbness or trouble speaking?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"No. It's just a dull ache at the front."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Have you hit your head, had a fit, a fever, or started any new medicines?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:41","band":"Substances","w":"D","s":"Lots of people your age try drugs, so I ask everyone. What have you used: cannabis, skunk, spice, vapes, cocaine, ketamine, alcohol?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Weed, at weekends. But I stopped about four months ago when I stopped seeing my mates."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Anything else, like spice or anything in a vape? Any alcohol?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"No. I don't drink."},{"t":"4:11","band":"Mood and background","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? And your sleep?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Scared. I'm not sleeping much. Not sad though."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Any times you've felt very high, full of energy, or needing hardly any sleep?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"When did things start changing at college?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"I stopped going three weeks ago. My mates said I was paranoid."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"As a little boy, did you learn to talk and walk at the usual time? Anyone in the family had problems like this?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"Mum says I was normal. My dad... he was in hospital once. Mum says he wasn't well. Paranoid, like."},{"t":"4:59","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. When you feel tracked like this, have you thought of doing anything about the people behind it?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"No. I just want it out of my head."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Do you carry anything to protect yourself? Have you had any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"No, nothing. I'm not going to hurt myself."},{"t":"5:25","band":"Insight and next steps","w":"P","s":"Am I going mad?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"No, you're not mad. When the brain is under a lot of strain, it can create experiences that feel completely real. Could that be part of this?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"I don't think so. It's real."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I hear that. Either way, you're frightened and not sleeping, and I'd like to help with that. Can we do some blood tests and a urine test today to check your body?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:56","w":"P","s":"If it's not more devices."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"No devices, just a blood sample. I'd also like a team for young people with these experiences to meet you soon. And can I talk to your mum with you there?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"Don't tell her about the voices. She'll freak."},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"I understand. She's here because she's worried about you. Can we decide together what I tell her, with you in the room?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"...OK. If I'm there."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So we'll do the tests, talk to your mum together, and I'll arrange that team. Does that sound OK?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his fear of being overheard, and offers privacy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the headache and the belief: content, onset and evidence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction and alternative explanations without arguing or colluding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hallucinations: onset, number, person, content and commands","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about thought insertion, withdrawal, broadcast, echo, passivity and delusional perception","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies the neologism and checks responding to voices during the interview","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks headache red flags, focal symptoms, head injury, seizures, fever and medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis, synthetic cannabinoids, other drugs and alcohol, with timing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about mood, sleep, manic symptoms and the prodrome","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about early development and family history of mental illness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to others and to self, including acting on beliefs and weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear and frustration throughout","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores insight sensitively and agrees tests, family involvement and referral","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Psychotic symptoms established with conviction tested, organic and drug causes screened, risk assessed, and a plan he agrees to.","borderline":"Delusion and voices found but first-rank symptoms, headache red flags or risk to others thin.","fail":"Argues with or colludes with the belief, misses the organic screen, or no risk assessment."}},"c-s-41":{"setup":"A&E relatives' room, 18:40. Josh Whitfield, 16, has first-episode psychosis, lacks capacity and refuses admission; Section 2 is being considered. Explain the diagnosis, treatment, early intervention and the law to his father.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Whitfield, 48, Josh's father, a delivery driver","open":"Where is he? Nobody's told me anything. Why am I seeing a psychiatrist and not a proper doctor?","manner":"Anxious, a bit defensive; his wife usually talks to doctors. Shocked and frightened by 'psychosis'. Settles and asks good questions once his fears are answered directly.","free":["Josh has stayed in his room for about three months","He has heard Josh muttering but thought it was normal","His wife is a midwife on nights and asked him to come","He does not want Josh 'locked up' or 'drugged'"],"ifasked":["Josh's grades dropped this term and he stopped football","Josh smoked cannabis at weekends last year; says he stopped in the summer","His aunt (Paul's mother's sister) had 'a breakdown' and was in hospital","Josh unplugged the router last week, saying someone was watching","No threats or violence towards anyone at home","Josh has no physical illnesses and takes no medicines"],"worries":["Are you saying he's mad?","Is he dangerous?","Will he be locked up for ever?","I don't want him zombified on medication."],"rules":["Becomes tearful when he realises he missed the signs; settles if told it was not his fault","Pushes to take Josh home once; accepts admission if the risk and time limit are explained clearly","Says 'I don't understand' if jargon is used"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Whitfield. I'm Dr Hassan, one of the child and adolescent psychiatrists. Josh is safe; he's with a nurse just down the corridor. He's agreed I can talk with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Where is he? Nobody's told me anything. Why am I seeing a psychiatrist and not a proper doctor?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you've been left waiting; that must have been awful. I'll explain everything. First, can you tell me what you've noticed in Josh recently?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"He's been in his room for months. Muttering. I thought it was just teenage stuff. His mum's the one who talks to doctors, she's on nights."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Has anything else changed, at school or with friends?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"His grades dropped. He quit football. Last week he unplugged the router, said someone was watching."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What's worrying you most right now?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"That he's hit his head or taken something. Why a psychiatrist?"},{"t":"1:10","band":"Tests and the diagnosis","w":"D","s":"The medical team checked him carefully: blood tests, a drug screen, a heart tracing and a brain scan. All of them are normal. So we don't think this is an injury or drugs today.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"So what is it, then?"},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"We think Josh has an illness called psychosis. Can I explain what that means?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Psychosis? Are you saying he's mad?"},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"No. He's unwell, the way someone with pneumonia is unwell, and it's treatable. Psychosis means that, for now, his mind is struggling to tell what's real from what isn't.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"For Josh, that means hearing voices that nobody else hears, and believing his classmates are filming him through his phone. That's why he was so frightened in the street.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"God. And I thought he was just being moody. I should have seen it."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"That's what almost every parent thinks; it came on slowly and looked like normal teenage stuff. It isn't your fault, and the things you've noticed are helping us now.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Okay. Thanks."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Just so I know I've been clear, how would you describe psychosis in your words?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"His brain's playing tricks on him, and he believes it."},{"t":"2:34","band":"Cause and risk","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. There's rarely one cause. Genes, stress and sometimes cannabis add up. Has Josh used cannabis, or is there any family history of mental health problems?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"He smoked weed last year, says he's stopped. My mum's sister had a breakdown, years ago. Is he dangerous?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"That's an important question. Most young people with psychosis are frightened rather than violent. The main risk tonight is to himself: he stepped into traffic to get away from the voices.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"So what happens now? Can I take him home?"},{"t":"3:14","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"I can see how much you want him home. Tonight he isn't safe there, so we're recommending a young people's psychiatric ward. Can I explain the treatment first, then the legal part?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"Go on. But I don't want him turned into a zombie with drugs."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Nor do we. On the ward we'd start a medicine called an antipsychotic at a low dose, raise it slowly and check for side effects every week, like restlessness or weight gain.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Alongside that, talking therapy for Josh, and family sessions for all of you. And an early intervention team will stay with him for up to three years after he leaves hospital.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Three years? So it's for life?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. Many young people recover well from a first episode, and some never have another. The support is long because starting early and staying well gives the best outlook.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"Right. And he'll agree to go to this ward?"},{"t":"4:20","band":"The law","w":"D","s":"That's the hard part. Josh doesn't feel ill, and he's refusing. Because he's 16, your consent can't lawfully keep him in hospital, so we use the Mental Health Act.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Sectioned? Locked up for ever?"},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"No. It's called Section 2. It's for up to 28 days, to assess and start treatment. A specialist social worker and two doctors assess him, and the social worker will speak with you before anything is decided.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Josh can appeal to an independent tribunal within the first 14 days, and he'll have a free advocate. As his nearest relative, you can ask in writing for his discharge.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"So I do get a say."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"You do. If doctors thought discharge would put him in danger, they can block it, but they'd explain why. Does that make sense?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Yeah. It's a lot."},{"t":"5:23","band":"Plan and support","w":"D","s":"It is a lot. Here are leaflets on psychosis and on Section 2, with the ward's number. A nurse stays with Josh until his bed is ready, and you can see him before he goes.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"Can his mum come tomorrow?"},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"Yes. The consultant will ring you both at nine tomorrow, and we'll arrange a family meeting this week. If you're worried tonight, ring the CAMHS crisis line on the leaflet.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before you go in to see him, what will you tell Josh's mum?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"That he's got psychosis, it's treatable, no drugs or head injury. Section 2, 28 days, not for ever. He can appeal, and I can ask for him out."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Is there anything we haven't covered?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"No. Just look after him."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"We will. Thank you for coming so quickly, and for everything you've told me. It's helped Josh tonight.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, reassures that Josh is safe and confirms his consent to share","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges the father's distress and guilt and validates before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains that bloods, drug screen, ECG and brain scan are normal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains psychosis in plain words using Josh's own symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers 'Is he mad?' directly without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis and family history and explains cause as several factors","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains low-dose antipsychotic with side-effect monitoring and addresses the 'zombie' fear","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains talking therapy, family work, early intervention and a hopeful but honest outlook","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses risk honestly and explains why parental consent is not enough at 16","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains Section 2: 28 days, AMHP and two doctors, appeal, advocate, nearest relative rights","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives leaflets, contacts, consultant call and family meeting","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back at least twice and summarises","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains psychosis in Josh's own symptoms, says tests are normal, answers each fear, covers treatment and early intervention and explains Section 2 with rights, then checks by teach-back.","borderline":"Accurate but jargon-heavy, or covers the law without appeal and nearest relative rights.","fail":"Lectures without asking what he knows, suggests parental consent instead of the Act, or leaves 'dangerous' and 'for ever' unanswered."}},"c-s-42":{"setup":"A&E side room, Saturday 21:15. Maya Rossi, 15, has been 'buzzing' for a week. Her parents are waiting outside. Carry out a full mental state assessment with risk, then present it to your consultant by phone.","role":{"who":"Maya Rossi, 15, female, Year 11 pupil (the on-call consultant, Dr Adeyemi, takes the phone at the end)","open":"Hey, hey, the doctor's here! Doctor, doctor, gimme the news, I've got a bad case of the blues. Kidding! I feel amazing.","manner":"Smiley, fast, rhyming, restless and overfamiliar. Turns irritable and patronising if pushed on the theory; calms when the doctor moves on.","free":["She feels amazing and has done for about a week","She is working on a revolutionary physics theory","School is a waste of time; she's outgrown the teachers","She doesn't need much sleep"],"ifasked":["Sleeps about three hours a night for the last few days; barely eats","Spent about £400 on her mum's card on clothes and make-up","A 19-year-old she met online wants to meet her tomorrow","Threw her phone at her sister tonight","No voices, no visions, no one controlling her thoughts","Never felt really low; denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts","No alcohol or drugs","Her grandmother was in a psychiatric hospital once and had big mood swings"],"worries":["Are you married, doc?","Am I going to be locked up?","You wouldn't understand my theory."],"rules":["Becomes irritable and says 'Stop asking!' if pressed twice on the theory; settles if the doctor acknowledges it and moves on","Answers orientation correctly but with irritation","Discloses the online contact only if asked about meeting people or online contacts"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Maya, I'm Dr Okoro, one of the doctors for young people. Is it okay if we chat on our own, with your mum and dad just outside?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hey, hey, the doctor's here! Doctor, doctor, gimme the news, I've got a bad case of the blues. Kidding! I feel amazing."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"What we talk about stays between us, unless I'm worried someone could get hurt. Then I'd tell you first. Okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Deal, seal, real! You look tired, doc. Late night? Got a girlfriend?"},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"I'm going to keep things about you, not me. That's how I work with everyone. So, what's been happening this week?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Everything! I've been writing, creating, I quit school, I bought new clothes, new face. Do you like my lipstick?"},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"I notice you're talking quickly and finding it hard to sit still. Is that how it's been feeling inside?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Like rockets. My thoughts are rockets, pockets, sockets."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Mood and energy","w":"D","s":"If 0 is the worst you've ever felt and 10 the best, where's your mood today?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Ten. Eleven. Best ever."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"How much sleep have you been getting? And food?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Three hours. Don't need more. Food's boring."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"And how long has it been like this?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"About a week. Since the theory came."},{"t":"1:33","band":"Thought content","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the theory. What does it mean about you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"It's going to change particle physics for ever. Honestly, it's above your level."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that it's right, from 0 to 100?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"A hundred. Why do you keep asking? You're just like my parents!"},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"I can hear I've annoyed you, and that's not my aim. Let's leave the theory there.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"(sighs) Fine."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Perception and passivity","w":"D","s":"Some people, when they're this full of energy, hear voices or see things others don't. Has that happened to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel someone is controlling your thoughts, or putting ideas into your head?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No! I'm in control. Totally."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Cognition","w":"D","s":"Quick check, Maya: where are we, and what's today's date?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"A&E. Saturday the 7th of November. Obviously."},{"t":"2:34","band":"Low mood and risk","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Have there been times, even before this week, when you felt really low?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Not really. Never like proper sad."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or of not wanting to be alive?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"God, no. Life's incredible."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Have you been spending money, or doing things you wouldn't normally do?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"I spent like £400 on Mum's card. I deserve it. I look amazing in everything."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"And have you been talking to people online you don't know in real life?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"There's this guy, he's 19. He gets me. He wants to meet tomorrow."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that took trust. Because you're 15 and he wants to meet, I'm worried. I'll need to share that to keep you safe, and I'll tell you what happens.","m":[8,1]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"(goes quiet) He's nice though. You'll ruin it."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"I can see he matters to you, and that's hard to hear. My job is to make sure you're safe. Has anyone got hurt when you've felt irritable?","m":[11,9]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"I threw my phone at my sister tonight. She was being annoying."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs, even once?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"No. I don't need them. I'm naturally high!"},{"t":"4:00","band":"Insight","w":"D","s":"Do you think anything might be wrong at the moment?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I'm the best I've ever been. They're overreacting."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open, Maya. I'm going to talk to my consultant and your parents now, and we'll come back and explain the plan before anything happens.","m":[1]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Okay, okay. Make it quick, I've got work to do."},{"t":"4:46","band":"Present","w":"D","s":"Dr Adeyemi, it's Dr Okoro. Maya Rossi, 15, brought by parents with a week of elated mood, three hours' sleep and poor intake. No previous episodes; grandmother possibly bipolar.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"On mental state: heavily made up, restless, overfamiliar. Pressured speech with rhyming. Mood 'ten out of ten', elated and labile, irritable when challenged.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Flight of ideas. A grandiose belief that her physics theory will change the field, held with full conviction, possibly delusional. No hallucinations or passivity. Oriented. No insight.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Risk?"},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"To self: little sleep or food, £400 spent; no suicidal thoughts or past low mood. To others: threw a phone at her sister. From others: a 19-year-old online wants to meet her tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Consultant: That worries me. Diagnosis?"},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"First manic episode. Differentials: drug-induced, though the screen is negative; thyroid, bloods pending; and schizoaffective disorder. I'd suggest admission and a safeguarding referral tonight.","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Agreed. Adolescent unit, 1:1 till then, and talk to the parents about the card and her phone."},{"t":"6:30","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Will do. I'll tell the parents, raise safeguarding and document the full mental state. I'll ring you back once the bed's confirmed.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Consultant: Good, thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Sees Maya alone and explains confidentiality with its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Comments on appearance, behaviour and pressured speech as observed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores mood rating, sleep, appetite and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the grandiose belief and tests conviction without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about hallucinations and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation briefly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past low mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about spending and online contact, and explains the need to share","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about aggression to others and substance use","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Stays calm and professional with overfamiliarity and irritability, validating feelings","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Presents the MSE in domain order with observed examples","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises three-way risk and gives a working diagnosis with differentials","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a clear next step and closes the call","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers every MSE domain with observed signs, asks about low mood, suicide and online contact, stays calm with overfamiliarity, then presents a clear MSE, risk and impression.","borderline":"Good mania picture but misses perception, insight or risk from others, or presents in a jumble.","fail":"Argues about the theory, answers personal questions, or misses suicide and exploitation risk."}},"c-s-43":{"setup":"Learning disability clinic, Wednesday morning. Bethany Lane, 24, moderate learning disability, newly diagnosed with ADHD. Explain starting methylphenidate, the ECG, pulse, blood pressure and weight checks and follow-up, in words she can follow.","role":{"who":"Ms Bethany Lane, 24, female, attends an all-day Saturday catering course","open":"Hiya. Why have I come back so soon? I only came last month.","manner":"Friendly, a bit anxious, keen to do well at her course. Says 'I don't get it' if jargon is used. Grimaces and gets upset at the ECG; calms with a kind, concrete explanation.","free":["She was told she has ADHD but isn't sure what it means","She can't sit still all day at her cooking class and forgets instructions","She really wants to finish the course"],"ifasked":["Her uncle has a heart rhythm problem and has a device in his chest","She has never fainted, had chest pain or a racing heart","She eats breakfast most days; sleeps well","She takes no regular medicines","She would like Dawn to come to appointments","She'd prefer a female nurse"],"worries":["What's stimulant? Is it drugs?","How long do I have to take it?","What happens if it doesn't work?","Will the wires hurt my heart?"],"rules":["Says 'I don't get it' to any medical word that is not explained","Grimaces and goes quiet at the first mention of the ECG; agrees if it is explained kindly and she can bring Dawn","Repeats things back correctly if asked in small chunks"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Bethany, I'm Dr Patel. Thanks for coming. Is it okay if we talk on our own, with Dawn in the waiting room?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hiya. Yeah. Why have I come back so soon? I only came last month."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Good question. Last time we found out you have ADHD. Today is about something that might help. What do you remember about ADHD?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"That I've got it. I don't really know what it means."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"That's okay. ADHD means your brain finds it hard to concentrate and keep still. What's hardest for you at your cooking class?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Sitting still all day. And I forget what the teacher said."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, especially when you want to do well. Have you got any ideas about what might help?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Maybe breaks? I don't know."},{"t":"1:10","band":"The medicine","w":"D","s":"Breaks are a good idea, and we can ask your tutor. There's also a medicine that helps your brain focus and helps you sit still for longer.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"The man said stimulant. What's stimulant? Is it drugs?"},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"It's a medicine, made for ADHD and given by doctors. It's called methylphenidate. Lots of people with ADHD take it.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what the medicine is for?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"To help me focus at cooking."},{"t":"1:50","band":"How we start","w":"D","s":"Exactly. We start with a small tablet at breakfast and another at lunch. Each week we might make it a bit bigger, if you're okay.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Two tablets every day?"},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"At first. When we find the right amount, you can have one tablet in the morning that lasts all day, right through your class.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"That's better. One in the morning."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Body checks","w":"D","s":"Before you start, we check three things: your heartbeat, your blood pressure and your weight.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Why do you need to weigh me?"},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"This medicine can make you less hungry. Weighing you shows us you're eating enough. It can also make your heart go a bit faster, so we check that too.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"And if it does?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"If your heart or blood pressure goes too high, or you lose weight, we change the medicine or stop it. We check every six months. Can you tell me why we check your heart?","m":[6,4]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"To see it's not going too fast."},{"t":"3:11","band":"The ECG","w":"D","s":"Spot on. One more test: an ECG. It's a heart picture. Can I show you a picture of one?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"(grimaces) Wires? I don't want wires on me. Will it hurt my heart?"},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"I can see that worries you, and that's okay. Lots of people feel like that. Can I explain exactly what happens?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Okay."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"You lie on a bed. A nurse puts small stickers on your chest, arms and legs. The stickers just listen to your heart, like a stethoscope. Nothing goes into you, and it doesn't hurt.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"How long?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"About five minutes. Then the stickers peel off like plasters.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Why me, though? My heart's fine."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"You told us your uncle has a heart problem. Sometimes that runs in families. We check your heart is safe before you start, because this medicine makes the heart work a bit faster.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"And if it's not safe?"},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"Then we ask a heart doctor, and we'd choose a different medicine. Would it help if Dawn came with you?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Yeah. And a lady nurse."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"We can do both. Shall I book it for this week?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Okay. If Dawn's there."},{"t":"4:49","band":"Side effects and what-ifs","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Bethany. Some people feel less hungry, find it hard to sleep, or get a headache. Eat breakfast before the tablet, and tell Dawn or ring us if you feel funny.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"How long do I have to take it?"},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"As long as it helps you. We'll check with you every few months, and you can always talk to us about stopping.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"What happens if it doesn't work?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Then we try a different amount, or a different medicine. There are other choices.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"I'm a bit stupid with all this. I can't remember everything."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"You're not stupid at all; it's a lot of new things. I'll give you a leaflet with pictures, and we'll go over it again next time.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:51","band":"Teach-back and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the three things we do before you start?","m":[4]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Heart picture. Blood pressure. Weigh me."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Perfect. And what do you do if your heart races or you feel faint?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Tell Dawn and ring you."},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Are you happy to have the ECG and then try the medicine?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I want to finish my course."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Brilliant. ECG this week, I'll ring you a week after you start, and I'll see you in four weeks. Shall we bring Dawn in so she hears the plan too?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Thanks, Dr Patel."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and checks she is happy to talk alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what she remembers about ADHD and what she finds hard, using her words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains what the medicine does in plain words without jargon","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back in small chunks, at least three times","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains start low, weekly increases and the once-daily long-acting tablet","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why pulse, blood pressure and weight are checked and how often","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the ECG concretely and why it is needed (family history)","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to her fear and offers practical choices","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Warns about common side effects and what to do","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Answers how long, and what if it doesn't work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers an easy-read leaflet and confirms her agreement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear next steps with dates and involves her support worker at her choice","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses simple words in small chunks, explains the medicine, start-low plan, checks and ECG with reasons, eases her fear with practical choices, and confirms by teach-back.","borderline":"Accurate but uses some jargon, checks understanding only once, or rushes the ECG fear.","fail":"Talks over her or to the support worker, uses medical language, or skips the ECG because she is upset."}},"c-s-44":{"setup":"A&E liaison room, Friday 17:30. Callum Reid, 32, has morbid jealousy, a past assault on an ex-partner and a new flick knife, and refuses admission. Explain the diagnosis and plan to his partner, Hannah, and discuss her safety.","role":{"who":"Ms Hannah Brooks, 29, Callum's partner, an office manager","open":"I don't understand. He fell off his bike. Why is a psychiatrist involved?","manner":"Loving, loyal and bewildered, then defensive about him. Tearful when the past assault is explained. Comes round to admission and a safety plan if the doctor is kind but persistent.","free":["They have been together two years and living together six months","She has never worried about his mental health","She likes that he gets a bit jealous; it makes her feel special","She wants to take him home tonight"],"ifasked":["He asked about a work-party photo months ago","He reads her messages and writes down when she gets home","There has been no affair; she barely knows the colleague","She saw a knife in his jacket last week; he said it was for protection","He has never threatened or hit her","No children; she could stay with her sister","She did not know about his previous conviction or hospital stay"],"worries":["What causes it?","Can't you just give him tablets and send him home?","Will he get better?","Should I leave him?"],"rules":["Insists 'he's just protective' twice; accepts the risk if it is linked clearly to his past","Becomes tearful on hearing about the past assault; settles if validated","Opposes compulsory admission until the reasons are explained calmly and clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, are you Hannah? I'm Dr Mensah, one of the psychiatrists. Callum is safe, and he's agreed I can talk with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"I don't understand. He fell off his bike. Why is a psychiatrist involved?"},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"It must be confusing to arrive and find us involved. Can I start by asking what you've noticed about Callum over the last few months?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Nothing really. He's fine. He's a bit jealous sometimes, but I like that. It makes me feel special."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Has he checked your phone, or kept track of when you come home?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"He reads my messages. He writes down when I get in. He's just being protective."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Did he ever mention a photo from your work party?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Yeah, months ago. Some guy from accounts was next to me. I told him to stop being paranoid."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"I have to ask this, and I'm not judging at all: has anything happened between you and that colleague?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"No! I hardly know him."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Has Callum ever threatened you or hurt you? And did you know he had a knife?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Never touched me. I saw a knife last week. He said it's for protection."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Do any children live with you, or come to visit?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"No, just us."},{"t":"1:28","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Let me explain what I think is happening. Callum has an illness called morbid jealousy. His brain has fixed on a false belief that you're being unfaithful.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Jealousy's an illness?"},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Normal jealousy comes and goes. This is different: from one photo, he's become certain, and nothing you say changes it. That certainty, with the checking, is the illness. It's a type of psychosis.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"Psychosis? He's not mad. What causes it?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"We don't know exactly. Genes, brain chemistry and stress all play a part. It isn't caused by anything you've done.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"If you were explaining this to your sister, what would you say?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"He really believes I'm cheating, even though I'm not, and it's an illness."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Callum's agreed I can share something difficult. Six years ago, he had this same belief about a previous partner, and he assaulted her. He was convicted and spent two years in a secure hospital.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"(stunned) What? He never told me. That was years ago. He must have been stressed."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"I can see that's a huge shock, and I'm sorry you're hearing it this way. Take a moment.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I just... I thought I knew him."},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"You do know the Callum who loves you. The illness is the part that's hidden. What worries me is that then, like now, it was the same belief, and it ended in violence.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"He's also followed your colleague home and bought a flick knife. So I'm worried for you and for your colleague. We have a duty to protect him, so we've told the police.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"The police? He hasn't done anything!"},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"I know it feels drastic. It's to prevent harm before it happens, not to punish him.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:38","band":"Admission","w":"D","s":"That's why we think he needs to be in hospital now. Here we can keep everyone safe, restart the medicine that helped before, and assess him properly.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give him his tablets back and let me take him home? I'll watch him."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"I wish it were that simple. The tablets take weeks to work, he says he won't take them, and he's carrying a knife. Home is where the belief is strongest. It isn't safe yet.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"He won't go into hospital. He'll hate it."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"He's refused, so a specialist social worker and two doctors are assessing him under the Mental Health Act. If they agree, he'll be admitted, and he has the right to appeal.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"So you're sectioning him. That'll destroy him."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I can hear how upsetting that is. It's to keep him and others safe and get him treated. Last time hospital treatment worked well for him.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:47","band":"Treatment and outlook","w":"P","s":"Will he get better?"},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"Many people improve with medicine and later talking therapy. But it can come back, sometimes with a new partner, so he'll need follow-up for a long time.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Should I leave him? I love him."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Separation and safety","w":"D","s":"That's your decision, and I can hear how much you love him. Some couples do well once treatment works. If it doesn't, living apart is sometimes the only safe option.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"For now, while he's this unwell, please don't be alone with him, and don't argue with the belief.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"Okay. I hadn't seen it like that."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Is there somewhere safe you can stay tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"My sister's. She's ten minutes away."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Good. The reading of your messages and tracking you is part of the illness, but it's also controlling, and you deserve support. Can I refer you to an adviser who supports partners, called an IDVA?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I suppose so. Yes."},{"t":"5:56","w":"D","s":"Here's the National Domestic Abuse Helpline, 0808 2000 247, open 24 hours. If you're ever in danger, ring 999. The ward will tell you before any leave or discharge.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:12","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"What will you take away from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"It's an illness, he believes it completely. He hurt someone before. Hospital's safest. I stay at my sister's and call that number if I need to."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. The ward will ring you within two days and invite you to a meeting. Any questions?","m":[12,11]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for being honest."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for listening. It took a lot to hear all that tonight.","m":[2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent to share and asks what she has noticed","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her shock and distress before continuing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about checking, the photo, any affair, threats, the knife and children","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains morbid jealousy as a fixed false belief, its causes, in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discloses the past assault sensitively and links it to the current belief","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States the risk to the colleague and the police disclosure","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why admission is needed now and why tablets at home are not enough","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the Mental Health Act assessment and his right to appeal","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains treatment, outlook and recurrence honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses separation honestly while respecting her choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan: safe place, helpline, IDVA, 999, informed of leave","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and gives follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Names morbid jealousy plainly, links the past assault and knife to risk for her and the colleague, holds firm on admission, discusses separation honestly and leaves a safety plan.","borderline":"Explains the diagnosis well but softens the risk, or gives no concrete safety plan.","fail":"Agrees to send him home on tablets, hides the past violence, or orders her to leave him."}},"c-s-45":{"setup":"Liaison office, Sunday 00:45. You have assessed Rachel Doyle, 38, who believes her married employer loves her and was found in his garden with a knife. Present her to your consultant by phone and propose a diagnosis and plan.","role":{"who":"Dr Ruth Mensah, 55, female, consultant liaison psychiatrist, on call from home","open":"Hi, it's Ruth. What have you got?","manner":"Brisk but supportive; tired at night. Interrupts if the summary drags past 2 minutes. Probes reasoning; warms when the trainee is clear and gives their own plan.","free":["Wants a short summary first","Will ask about extra information, diagnosis and differentials","Expects an immediate and a long-term plan"],"ifasked":["Agrees to a forensic opinion in the morning","Happy with a general adult ward if Rachel stays settled","Will act as one of the doctors for the MHA assessment if needed","Wants the police told and a note of the reasons","Knows the sister has rung the ward twice"],"worries":["What type of delusional disorder?","Why not schizophrenia?","She's calm. Why not discharge to the CMHT?","Her sister keeps ringing; what do we tell her?"],"rules":["Cuts in at about 2 minutes: 'What's your impression?'","If told 'delusional disorder', asks 'Can you be more specific?'","Prompts for short-term and long-term plans if not offered","Sounds concerned about the sister's distress; settles when the trainee has a plan for her"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Dr Mensah, it's Dr Ali in A&E. Sorry to wake you. Can I present a new patient with a significant risk to others?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Hi, it's Ruth. What have you got?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Rachel Doyle, 38, a dental nurse, brought by police after her employer's wife found her in their garden at 11 pm with a kitchen knife in her bag.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","band":"History and MSE","w":"D","s":"For seven months she's been certain her employer, Mr Hale, 52 and married, secretly loves her. It began when he praised her at the Christmas party.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"She's sent over 200 texts and cards and been to his house four times. He's asked her to stop. She's suspended and got a police warning last month.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"She believes his wife intercepts his replies. She says the knife was 'in case his wife attacks me'.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"Mental state: well kempt, calm, euthymic, reactive affect. No thought disorder. The belief is held with full conviction. No hallucinations. Cognition grossly intact. No insight.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Background: hypothyroid on levothyroxine. A bottle of wine at weekends, no drugs. No psychiatric or forensic history before this. Lives alone, divorced three years.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. What other information would you want?"},{"t":"1:33","band":"Extra sources","w":"D","s":"Police records, including the warning and tonight's report. Her GP summary. Her sister, with consent. And the workplace account through the police.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"I've sent thyroid function, routine bloods, a glucose and a urine drug screen. She isn't intoxicated.","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Good. So what's your impression?"},{"t":"1:59","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"I think this is a delusional disorder.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Can you be more specific? What type?"},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Erotomanic type, de Clérambault's syndrome. She's certain a higher-status man secretly loves her.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"What makes you say that?"},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"The belief has lasted over three months. He's out of reach: senior, married, her employer. She reinterprets his rejection as his wife blocking him. She's pursuing him, now with a weapon. And she has no insight.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Why not schizophrenia?"},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"The belief is circumscribed. Apart from it, she's functioned normally, with no hallucinations, thought disorder or negative symptoms. But schizophrenia is a differential, and we'd watch for it.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Other differentials?"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Bipolar disorder, though her mood is normal. Substance-induced psychosis, with the alcohol. And organic causes, especially thyroid disease, given her levothyroxine.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:06","band":"Immediate plan","w":"P","s":"Fine. What do you want to do tonight?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"She's medically fit pending thyroid tests. She's on a 1:1 with security, and the police have the knife.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"I'd offer informal admission. She lacks insight and I'm not sure she'll accept. If she refuses, I'd request an MHA assessment for Section 2.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"And if she tries to leave before the AMHP comes?"},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Section 5(2) doesn't apply in A&E. If she lacks capacity to decide, staff can hold her briefly in her best interests using the MCA, and police would be called if needed.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Which ward?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"A general adult ward if she stays settled; PICU if she tries to abscond or becomes agitated. I'd like forensic advice in the morning.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"She's polite and calm, though. Why not discharge her to the community team?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"I understand she seems settled. But she took a knife to their home, she blames his wife, and she has no insight. Calm doesn't mean safe here.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Fair. And risk?"},{"t":"4:25","band":"Risk and long-term plan","w":"D","s":"High risk to Mr Hale and especially his wife, as the perceived obstacle. Low current risk to herself, but I'll ask again after the assessment. I'd tell the police tonight to protect them, and record why.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Agreed. Longer term?"},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"Biologically, an antipsychotic such as risperidone, starting low, with prolactin, weight and metabolic checks. Advise cutting out alcohol.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Psychologically, a structured stalking risk assessment and HCR-20 with forensic psychology, and CBT once she'll engage.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Socially, no contact with Mr Hale or his family, with police considering a Stalking Protection Order. Occupational health, her union and the social worker for work and money. Then CMHT follow-up with police liaison.","m":[11,10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Would you refer to the Fixated Threat Assessment Centre?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"No. They handle threats to public figures. For a private victim, it's local police and the forensic team.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"Good. Her sister's rung the ward twice, in tears, asking if Rachel will lose her job. It's a lot for them."},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard for the family. I'll ask Rachel's consent to speak to her sister, and once she's settled, the ward can involve occupational health. I'll ring the sister after we finish.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Anything else?"},{"t":"6:10","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Just to confirm: offer admission, MHA assessment if she refuses, 1:1 till then, police informed of the risk, bloods chased, forensic advice in the morning. Is that right?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"That's right. I'll come in for the assessment if needed."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll document the plan and the disclosure, and ring you after the AMHP has seen her or if anything changes.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Good presentation. Speak later."}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens with a one-line summary including age, setting and headline risk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Presents history and MSE in a clear order within about 2 minutes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Lists additional sources: police, GP, family, workplace","d":"Content"},{"c":"Confirms medical clearance and bloods including thyroid and drug screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names delusional disorder, erotomanic type, with supporting features","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives differentials with reasons: schizophrenia, bipolar, substance, organic","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Identifies the wife as the perceived rival and at risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Proposes a safe immediate setting and level of observation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the legal route: informal offer, MHA assessment, MCA if she leaves","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans police information sharing to protect both people at risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a long-term biological, psychological and social plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to the family's distress and plans to contact them","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises and confirms the plan and safety-netting with the consultant","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Presents concisely, names erotomanic delusional disorder with its features and differentials, protects both people at risk and proposes a clear immediate and long-term plan.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but rambling, or a plan missing the legal route, police liaison or the wife's risk.","fail":"Reads the clerking aloud, misses the diagnosis, or proposes discharge despite the knife and stalking."}},"c-s-46":{"setup":"Thursday 15:10 on the acute ward. Ryan Kelly, 28, on Section 2, has just lit a fire in the activity room. Nobody is hurt. Explore the psychotic experiences behind it, assess the risk, and agree immediate safety actions.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Kelly, 28, male, patient on Section 2 (Joanne, the nurse in charge, joins at the end)","open":"Is the alarm off now? I didn't want anyone to get hurt.","manner":"Calm, polite and earnest, matter of fact about the fire. Becomes guarded if challenged early about it; opens up when the doctor is curious about his experiences.","free":["His parents brought him in because he was out all night","He found his late grandfather's compass and knew he was chosen","He has a mission to switch off phone masts so people can think freely","He feels there is nothing wrong with him"],"ifasked":["Radio adverts tell him which masts to target","A device in his tooth carries voices","One man says 'go now'; two others discuss him and call him slow","He does what the voice tells him","The device put in the thought 'light the way out' and took his fear away","He kept a lighter from the smoking shelter yesterday","He chose the room for the paper, paint and fire door","He would set another fire; only discharge would stop him"],"worries":["Will the police get involved?","Will staff watch him all the time?","Will he be made to take tablets?"],"rules":["If asked 'why did you start the fire?' in the first minute, says 'I had to' and goes quiet","Never volunteers the commands or the repeat intent unless asked directly","Becomes briefly upset about his grandfather; settles if acknowledged","Accepts observations grudgingly if the reason is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and check safety","w":"D","s":"Hello Ryan, I'm Dr Okafor, one of the ward doctors. Are you hurt anywhere? Any burns, or trouble breathing?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Is the alarm off now? I didn't want anyone to get hurt. I'm fine."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"It's off, and no one's hurt. I'd like to hear how things have been for you these past weeks. Is that OK?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:23","band":"How it began","w":"D","s":"What was going on before your parents brought you in?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I was out all night, looking for where to start. They panicked."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"Where to start what? When did things first feel different?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"When I found Grandad's compass in his drawer, after the funeral. He died in June."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry about your grandad. It sounds like he mattered a lot to you.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"He did. (pauses) He was the only one who got me."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Take your time. What happened when you found the compass, what did you realise, and what did you do next?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"The needle swung round and pointed at me. I just knew I'd been chosen. I spent weeks working out what for."},{"t":"1:28","band":"The mission","w":"D","s":"What is it you've been chosen to do? And how would you do it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Switch off the phone masts so people can think for themselves again. I'll know how when I get there."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"How do you know which masts? Do you get messages or signs meant just for you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Radio adverts. They read out numbers. Those are the masts."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Is anyone working against you, or trying to stop you?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody's after me. They just don't understand."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Voices and the implant","w":"D","s":"When you need to know what to do next, how do you find out?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"The device in my tooth. It was put in for the mission."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Do you hear anyone through it, when nobody else is there? Speaking to you, or about you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A man talks to me. And two others talk about me. They say I'm too slow."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Does the man tell you to do things, and do you do what he says? Do you see, smell or feel anything others don't?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"He says 'go now'. I always do. Nothing else, just the voices."},{"t":"2:54","band":"Thoughts","w":"D","s":"Does the device affect your thinking? Do thoughts get put in, or taken out?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Both. It took my thoughts about Mum away. Today it put one in."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"What was the thought it put in today? Did it make the fire easier?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"'Light the way out.' And it took the fear away, so I could do it."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Can other people hear or read your thoughts? Does anything control your body or your feelings?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"No. My head's clear. I'm in control."},{"t":"3:34","band":"Mood and insight","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Unusually high, or low? And your sleep and energy?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Normal. Focused. I sleep fine."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Could any of this be an illness, the way your parents and the team think?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"No. It's all real. I need to get out and get on with it."},{"t":"3:57","band":"The fire","w":"D","s":"Thank you for explaining. Tell me about the fire. What did you want to happen?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Alarm goes, doors open, I walk out the fire exit."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"How did you plan it? Where did the lighter come from?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Kept one from the smoking shelter yesterday. Waited till it was quiet after lunch."},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Why the activity room? And did you want anyone to get hurt?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Paper and paint, right by the fire door. No. I don't want anyone hurt."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Might you light another one? What, if anything, would stop you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"Yes, if that's what it takes. The mission comes first. Letting me go would stop me."},{"t":"4:48","band":"Other risks","w":"D","s":"Would you try to leave the ward any other way? Would you hurt anyone who stood in the way?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Any way I can. But I wouldn't hurt anyone."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Any urge to harm yourself? And would you take medicine to help?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"No, never. And no tablets. They'd block the signal. Are the police going to get involved?"},{"t":"5:14","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"I won't pretend I know yet; the team will decide with your consultant. Let me check I've understood: since the compass, you've had a mission, the voice told you to go, the thought 'light the way out' came in, and you'd do it again. Is that right?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"I'm worried a fire could hurt you, other patients or staff. So staff will stay within sight of you for now, and we'll check you and your room for lighters. I know that's hard to hear.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"If you watch me all the time I'll never finish it."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"It's to keep you safe, not to punish you, and we'll review it every day. I'll talk to your consultant today about treatment. You can see an advocate and appeal your section; the nurses will show you how.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"Fine. I don't like it."},{"t":"6:06","band":"Hand over and close","w":"D","s":"Joanne, he set it on purpose to escape, on the orders of voices and an inserted thought, and says he'd do it again. Eyesight obs now, search him and his room with his knowledge, escorted smoking only. I'll ring the RC, do the incident report and raise PICU.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Agreed. I'll start the search with him now."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Ryan, if the voice tells you to do anything, please tell a nurse straight away. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"Someone stays with me, you check for lighters, and you talk to my consultant."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Thank you for talking so openly with me.","m":[2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checked for burns and smoke inhalation before starting the interview","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Built rapport with open, curious questions and validated his grief and frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicited the delusional perception, grandiose mission and referential delusions; screened for persecutory ideas","d":"Content"},{"c":"Characterised voices: person, content, commands and obedience; screened other modalities","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicited thought insertion and withdrawal, its content and link to the fire; screened broadcast and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screened mood for elation, sleep and energy, and assessed insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Established motive, planning, ignition source, location choice, harm intent and intent to repeat","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screened absconding, harm to others, self-harm and willingness to take medication","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarised the findings back to him and checked accuracy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained the safety measures honestly, with the reason","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-netted and checked understanding, including his right to an advocate and appeal","d":"Close"},{"c":"Handed over clearly to the nurse in charge: obs, search, smoking, RC, incident report, PICU","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits each psychotic experience, links them to a planned fire with intent to repeat, and starts concrete safety measures today.","borderline":"Covers the symptoms but leaves the fire risk vague or misses the commands or repeat intent.","fail":"Interrogates about the fire early, lists symptoms without linking them, or leaves without a safety plan."}},"c-s-47":{"setup":"Saturday 10:20 on call. Dean Holloway, 45, admitted overnight on Section 2, has just assaulted another patient and been restrained. Assess his mental state and violence risk, and agree immediate measures with the nurse in charge.","role":{"who":"Mr Dean Holloway, 45, male, patient on Section 2 (Amira, the nurse in charge, briefly present)","open":"Who are you? Another one of them?","manner":"Suspicious, tense and scanning the room at first. Relaxes and talks freely once the doctor explains their role and doesn't argue about his beliefs.","free":["Police and 'fake doctors' took him from home last night","He is here because MI5 want him locked away","The man in the corridor was an assassin","He feels scared, not ill"],"ifasked":["He meant to kill the man; 'kill or be killed'","He heard whispering outside his room all night","He thinks there are more assassins dressed as patients and staff","He stopped collecting his medication months ago","Many admissions including PICU; he says he was injected with 'memory serum'","Two prison sentences for violence","Smokes cannabis daily at home; none since admission","He would attack again if approached"],"worries":["Will he be injected again?","Is he being sent somewhere worse?","Who will protect him from the others?"],"rules":["Becomes offended and shuts down if told the whispering is 'in his head'","Answers fully once the doctor says they are not police","Does not volunteer prison or intent to repeat unless asked","Accepts a tablet if offered calmly with a choice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Safety and opening","w":"D","s":"Amira, before I go in: is the other man being looked after, and is Mr Holloway OK after the restraint?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Cut eyebrow, he's being checked. Three minutes, seated hold. His obs are normal. He refused lorazepam."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Mr Holloway, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors. I'm not police. Can I sit here, near the door?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Who are you? Another one of them?"},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"I'm just a doctor. I want to hear what happened this morning, from your side.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"You lot dragged me here last night. Now you want to talk?"},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Being taken from your home at night would shake anyone. I can see you're angry. What happened in the corridor?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:45","band":"The incident","w":"P","s":"He came up to me. Friendly voice. But I knew. He was an assassin."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. How did you know?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"The tone. They train them to sound nice."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"What did you want to happen when you hit him?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I wanted him dead. It's kill or be killed."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Did you have anything in your hands, anything you could use as a weapon?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Just my fists and feet."},{"t":"1:17","band":"The beliefs","w":"D","s":"Who do you think sent him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"MI5. I know too much about them. This place is part of it."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"How sure are you of that, out of a hundred?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"A hundred. The rooms are bugged. They track where I go."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Is there anyone else here you're worried about?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"There's more of them. Dressed as patients. Some as staff."},{"t":"1:44","band":"Perceptions, mood, insight","w":"D","s":"Last night, did you hear anything when nobody was around?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Whispering outside my door all night. About me."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"What were they saying? Did you see anything unusual?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Where I was, what I was doing. No, didn't see anything. And don't tell me it's in my head."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"I won't. It's clearly very real to you. How's your mood been: low, or on top of the world?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Scared. Not depressed. Not high. I haven't slept."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Has anyone said this could be part of an illness? What do you think?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"They say schizophrenia. They're wrong."},{"t":"2:25","band":"The past","w":"D","s":"Have you been in hospital before? Ever on an intensive care ward?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Loads of times. PICU too. Held down and jabbed with memory serum."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. What medicine were you on, and when did you stop?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Some tablets. Stopped picking them up months ago. I don't need them."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Have the police ever been involved because of fights? Ever been to prison?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"Prison twice. Both times for fights."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Any cannabis, alcohol or other drugs? Who's at home with you?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Cannabis every day. Not since I got here. No drink. I live alone. No job, no mates."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Now and next","w":"D","s":"How do you feel now about the man you hit?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"No regrets. He'd have done me."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"If someone came up to you today, what would you do?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"If another one comes near me, I'll finish the job."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest; that helps me keep everyone safe, you included. Any thoughts of self-harm or of getting out of here?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"I'm not suicidal. I'd leave if I could, but they lock the doors."},{"t":"3:39","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got this right. You believe MI5 have people on the ward to harm you, you heard whispering all night, and you'd act again if approached. Is that right?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"That's right."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"To keep you and everyone safe, a nurse will stay close to you, and you'll have the quieter area away from the main corridor.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"So what now, am I getting locked up somewhere worse?"},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Fair question. I'll talk to your consultant today about the safest place for you, and I'll tell you what we decide and why.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Not the serum though."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"I'd like to offer you a tablet, olanzapine, to ease the fear and help you sleep. You can ask me anything about it first.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"A tablet. Not a needle. I'll think about it."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"That's fair. If you feel unsafe, tell the nurse with you straight away rather than acting on it. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Nurse stays with me. You ring the consultant. I think about the tablet."},{"t":"4:41","band":"Hand over and close","w":"D","s":"Amira, he still believes patients and staff are assassins and would attack again. Enhanced obs within eyesight, nursed away from the corridor, olanzapine offered, RT plan written, I'll ring the RC about PICU and do the incident report.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Nurse in charge: Got it. And the other man wants to speak to the police."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"That's his right. Please support him with that and log it. Mr Holloway, thank you for talking with me.","m":[12,11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Checked the victim's care and post-restraint safety before the interview","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explained role, sat safely and validated fear and anger without arguing about the beliefs","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explored the assault: trigger, intent, weapon","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Elicited persecutory delusions: content, source, conviction, others involved","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about hallucinations, mood and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Took historical factors: admissions, PICU, adherence, prison, substances, social situation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Established remorse and intent to repeat","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assessed risk to self and absconding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarised the findings and checked accuracy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explained immediate measures and offered oral medication honestly","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-netted, checked understanding and closed respectfully","d":"Close"},{"c":"Handed over: obs, separation, RT plan, RC, PICU, incident report, victim support","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits the persecutory beliefs and intent to repeat, covers past, present and future factors, and starts proportionate measures now.","borderline":"Good MSE but thin history of past violence or vague measures.","fail":"Argues with the delusions, ignores historical factors, or treats a calm patient as low risk."}},"c-s-48":{"setup":"Wednesday 14:00, low secure unit family room. Callum Price, 24, recovered on clozapine, is discharged in two weeks. He assaulted his mother when psychotic. With his consent, go through relapse signs, risk factors and a crisis plan with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Price, 50, female, Callum's mother","open":"I want him home eventually, but I'm frightened, if I'm honest.","manner":"Warm, tense and quick to blame herself. Becomes tearful talking about that night; steadies when acknowledged and given practical steps.","free":["She knows the diagnosis is schizophrenia","She wants to keep seeing him","She's scared he will stop his tablets or smoke cannabis again","She wants to know what to do if he ever frightens her"],"ifasked":["She thought schizophrenia might go away","She knew he smoked cannabis but didn't think it mattered","First he slept all day and ignored her texts","Then he kept checking the windows and talked about the neighbours","In the last days he refused food she cooked","She still startles and sleeps badly; her sister supports her","No weapons at home except kitchen knives","She has never wanted to hurt him"],"worries":["Will it go away?","What if he thinks she's poisoning him again?","Can he come home for Sunday dinner?","Will the team actually answer the phone?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about the assault; settles if the doctor names how hard it was","Gives the early signs only if asked what she noticed first","Pushes for home visits; accepts a staged plan if reasons are given","Repeats the plan back accurately if it is given in three clear steps"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Price, I'm Dr Bose, one of Callum's doctors. Thank you for coming. Callum has asked us to talk openly with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"I want him home eventually, but I'm frightened, if I'm honest."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense. You're his mum, and you were the one who got hurt. Today is about early signs and keeping you safe when he leaves.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Nobody's really said that."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Her understanding","w":"D","s":"What have you been told about Callum's illness?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"That it's schizophrenia. But will it ever go away?"},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"For most people it's long term, a bit like diabetes, but it can be kept well controlled. The key is staying on clozapine even when he feels completely well.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"So he'll be on it for life?"},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"For a long time, yes. What do you think the cannabis had to do with it?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"I knew about the weed. I should have stopped him."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"It's natural to look back and wish you had. You couldn't have controlled it. Strong cannabis can trigger psychosis and makes relapse much more likely, so staying off it really matters.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"He says he's stopped."},{"t":"1:13","band":"Her and that night","w":"D","s":"He has, and he's putting real effort in. Only as much as you want to share: how have you been since that night?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I still jump when a door slams. I don't sleep. He broke my cheekbone. My own son."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That sounds frightening beyond words, and it's still with you. Who's been supporting you?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"My sister. Nothing official."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"Would you be open to your GP referring you for talking therapy? And you're able to ask for a carer's assessment from the council. I'll give you the details.","m":[4,9]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Maybe. Yes. What happens when he leaves?"},{"t":"1:51","band":"Discharge plan","w":"D","s":"He'll live in supported accommodation, with staff there day and night. He stays on clozapine, with regular blood tests through the clozapine clinic.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"A care coordinator from the community team visits him weekly at first, a psychiatrist reviews him, and he keeps seeing the drug service. Is there anything you'd add?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"That's a lot. But what if he quits the clozapine, or goes back to the weed?"},{"t":"2:16","band":"Relapse signature and risk factors","w":"D","s":"Those are exactly the right worries. You know him best. Last time, what changed first, before anything else?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"He slept all day. Stopped answering my texts."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"And after that?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"He kept checking the windows. Said the neighbours were watching him."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"And just before that night?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"He wouldn't eat anything I cooked. What if he thinks I'm poisoning him again?"},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"That's a very real fear after what happened. So his signature is: sleeping late and going quiet, then watching windows, then refusing your food. That last one means act at once.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"The things that make relapse more likely are stopping clozapine, cannabis, a change in his smoking, because it alters clozapine levels, and stress. The clinic and staff will notice missed doses quickly.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"I didn't know the smoking mattered."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Visits and home","w":"D","s":"If he stops or starts smoking, tell us so we can check his level. How are you hoping to see him?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Can he come home for Sunday dinner? Just once?"},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you want normal life back. To start with, visits where he lives are safest, with staff knowing you're coming. We'd review home visits at his first CPA meeting.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"That's fair, I suppose."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Is there anything at home that could be used as a weapon? And when he was unwell, did his fears ever involve your house or neighbours?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Just kitchen knives. The neighbours, a bit, but he's not said anything since."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"Some parents carry a lot of anger after an assault like this. Has any part of you wanted to hurt him back?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Never. I love him. I just want to feel safe."},{"t":"4:01","band":"Crisis plan","w":"D","s":"Then let's make a plan in three steps. If you notice early signs, like sleeping late and not answering, ring his care coordinator.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"If things get worse, or it's evening or weekend, use the crisis line or 111 and choose the mental health option. If you ever feel in danger, leave, get somewhere safe and call 999. Then tell us.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"How do I know your team will actually answer the phone?"},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"Fair question. I'll write down the care coordinator's direct line, the duty number and the crisis line, so there's always a second number.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"OK. That helps."},{"t":"4:36","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Talk me through what you'd do if he refused food you'd made?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"Leave, ring 999 if I was scared, then ring you. Before that, if he's just sleeping all day, ring his coordinator."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Is there anything that might stop this plan working for you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Only if my phone's dead. I'll keep it charged."},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"Good idea, and tell your sister when you visit. I'll give you all this in writing, and we'll meet again at his first CPA review.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel a bit less scared."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so honest. You've been through a lot, and you're a big part of keeping him well.","m":[2,11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirmed Callum's consent and stated the purpose of the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Showed empathy for her as both his mother and the victim of the assault","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checked her understanding of schizophrenia and cannabis, and filled the gaps simply","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asked about her health and support, and offered GP referral and carer's assessment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained the discharge plan: accommodation, clozapine monitoring, care coordinator, drug service","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Built his relapse signature with her: early, middle and late signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Named his risk factors, including the effect of smoking on clozapine","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agreed safe visits and asked about weapons, home-related fears and her own feelings","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agreed a written three-step crisis plan with named numbers and 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked understanding with teach-back and asked where the plan might fail","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closed with written plan, follow-up date and thanks","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds the relapse signature from her own observations, covers risk factors, home risks and her feelings, and leaves her with a three-step plan she can repeat.","borderline":"Explains warning signs well but misses her trauma, weapons at home or a usable crisis plan.","fail":"Lectures without listening, promises it won't happen again, or gives no emergency route."}},"c-s-49":{"setup":"Tuesday 11:30, acute medical unit. You have diagnosed delirium tremens in Mr Anil Mehta, 64, after a two-day long lie. The newly qualified ward nurse asks you to explain how you made the diagnosis and the management plan.","role":{"who":"Staff Nurse Chloe Barnes, 23, female, newly qualified ward nurse","open":"Thanks for coming back. I've never heard of DTs before. What is it, exactly?","manner":"Keen, a little anxious about her shift and slightly embarrassed not to know. Asks for repeats politely if jargon is used; grows confident as the plan becomes concrete.","free":["She hasn't nursed alcohol withdrawal before","He keeps grabbing at the air and shouting","The neighbour offered to translate","She is worried about managing him alone tonight"],"ifasked":["He has had no glucose yet","His IV cannula is working; urine output is low","No fits so far","The side room by the nurses' desk is free","The neighbour says he drinks a bottle of whisky a day","She knows the CIWA score from her training"],"worries":["What causes it, and who's at risk?","Why not just give haloperidol?","What if he fits on her shift?","Can she use the CIWA score?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses jargon, politely asks them to explain it more simply","Asks 'could you repeat the timing bit?' once","Becomes anxious about fits; settles when given clear steps","Repeats the plan back if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Chloe, I'm Dr Chaudhry from liaison psychiatry. The medical registrar is on the way. Is someone with Mr Mehta now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes, the HCA. Thanks for coming back. I've never heard of DTs before. What is it, exactly?"},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Good question, and lots of people haven't. Have you looked after anyone in alcohol withdrawal before?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Not really. I'm a bit embarrassed."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"No need at all. It's not common on every ward. What would be most useful for me to cover?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"What causes it, how you know he's got it, and what I do."},{"t":"0:31","band":"What it is","w":"D","s":"DTs is the most severe kind of alcohol withdrawal: withdrawal plus delirium, so sudden confusion. It's a medical emergency, but very treatable.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"Alcohol slows the brain down. After years of heavy drinking, the brain revs up to balance it. Stop the alcohol suddenly and it goes into overdrive.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"So his brain's lost its brakes?"},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Exactly. It's most likely in long-term heavy drinkers, people who've had withdrawal fits or DTs before, older people, and anyone physically unwell.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","band":"How we know","w":"P","s":"And how do you know that's what he's got?"},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"Three things together. First, his neighbour says he drinks a bottle of whisky a day, and he's had nothing for about two days. DTs usually starts two to four days after the last drink.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Sorry, could you say the timing bit again?"},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"Of course. Ordinary withdrawal starts within hours. DTs comes later, usually day two to four. He was on the floor about two days, so it fits.","m":[4,2]},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Second, delirium: he can't keep his attention on me, he doesn't know where he is, and he's seeing things, which is why he grabs at the air.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"Third, the withdrawal signs: tremor, sweating, pulse 112, blood pressure up, and a slight temperature.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Could it be a head injury from the fall, though?"},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Very sensible thought. The head scan is normal. We're also checking his sugar, infection, salts and liver, because those can cause confusion too, or happen alongside.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"And his raised CK tells us muscle has broken down from lying on the floor, which can strain the kidneys.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"What happens if it's not picked up?"},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Untreated, DTs can cause fits, dangerous heart rhythms, choking, falls and death. That's why we act now rather than wait for every result.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:17","band":"Treatment","w":"P","s":"So what do we actually give him?"},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Lorazepam is the main treatment. It calms the overdrive. It's charted by mouth, repeated as the protocol says, aiming for calm but rousable.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Pabrinex is a high-dose vitamin B drip that protects his brain from a condition called Wernicke's. Has he had any glucose yet?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"No, nothing yet."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Good. Pabrinex goes in before or with any glucose. Then drip fluids for the dehydration and muscle damage, and we'll replace any low salts.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Shouldn't we just give him haloperidol? He's seeing things."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Fair thought, because it works in other delirium. Here the hallucinations come from withdrawal, so lorazepam treats the cause. Haloperidol can make fits more likely, so we'd only add it if lorazepam isn't enough.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:04","band":"Monitoring and care","w":"P","s":"Can I use the CIWA score to decide doses?"},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Good thinking, but CIWA needs him to answer questions, which he can't while delirious. Use obs and NEWS2 at least hourly, a sedation score, fluid balance and blood sugar instead.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Can we move him to the side room by the nurses' desk, lights on, a clock he can see, the same faces, and someone with him all the time?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"The side room's free. His neighbour speaks Gujarati. Can she translate?"},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"She can help reassure him, which is great. For medical questions and decisions we need a professional interpreter. Could you book one by phone for the registrar's review?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Yes. What if he fits on my shift? I'm on till nine."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible worry. Recovery position, protect his head, time it, oxygen, and put out the emergency call. A fit over five minutes needs IV lorazepam straight away.","m":[7,2]},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"Call the medical team at once if he fits, becomes very drowsy or hard to rouse, or his NEWS2 reaches 5. They may move him to HDU.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Fit, very drowsy, NEWS2 five. Got it."},{"t":"4:06","band":"Longer term and close","w":"D","s":"Once he's clear, the alcohol care team will see him about the drinking and support at home. I'll refer him today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Can you talk me through what you'll do over the next few hours, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"Side room, someone with him. Lorazepam as charted. Pabrinex before any glucose, then fluids. Hourly obs, NEWS2, sedation and sugar. Interpreter. Call if he fits, gets drowsy or NEWS2 is five."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"That's spot on. You'll be great. I'll write it all in the notes, and you can bleep me any time today.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel much better about tonight."}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirmed patient safety and medical team involvement before teaching","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Found out what the nurse knew and wanted, and was supportive, not patronising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explained what DTs is, the mechanism, who is at risk, and why it is an emergency","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Justified the diagnosis: heavy drinking, timing since last drink, delirium and withdrawal signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explained differentials considered and what excluded them, including the long lie and CK","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gave the treatment plan: lorazepam first line, Pabrinex before glucose, IV fluids, electrolytes, antipsychotic only as add-on","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Specified monitoring (not CIWA), seizure first aid and escalation triggers","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covered nursing environment, one-to-one care and a professional interpreter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Outlined longer-term follow-up with the alcohol care team","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checked understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closed with documentation and how to reach the team","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clearly justifies DTs from history, timing and signs, gives a NICE-consistent plan with monitoring and triggers, and checks the nurse can carry it out.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and drugs but vague monitoring or escalation, or no check of understanding.","fail":"Jargon-heavy or patronising, misses thiamine or benzodiazepines, or recommends an antipsychotic alone."}},"c-s-50":{"setup":"Sunday 13:30, acute medical unit. Tom Ashworth, 29, a solicitor, woke from a GBL coma this morning. Take a substance use history focused on GBL, assess risk including chemsex and suicidality, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Tom Ashworth, 29, male, patient","open":"I'm really sorry. I'm taking up a bed someone actually needs.","manner":"Polite, anxious and apologetic; deflects questions about the party at first. Opens up when reassured about confidentiality and not judged; becomes upset about the memory gap.","free":["He was at a party and doesn't remember leaving","He took G in a drink and had some beers","He is worried about his job","He didn't mean to overdose"],"ifasked":["It was a chemsex party; sex with several men, no condoms","He has used G for five years; now every 2 hours at weekends","For four months he has taken a small dose most nights to sleep","He gets shaky, sweaty and can't sleep without it; feels like that now; last dose about midnight","He has collapsed on G twice before","He injects methamphetamine monthly at parties; shared a spoon once","Never tested for HIV or hepatitis; not on PrEP","He can't remember the end of the night and isn't sure what happened"],"worries":["Will his firm find out?","Is he an addict?","Is there help for sex without G?"],"rules":["Redirects the conversation when first asked about the party; opens up if confidentiality is explained","Only discloses night-time dosing if asked about sleep or mornings","Becomes tearful about the memory gap; settles if validated and given choices","Denies suicidal thoughts clearly if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Tom, I'm Dr Adeyemi from the mental health team. We see everyone after a serious overdose; it's routine. Is now OK to talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"I'm really sorry. I'm taking up a bed someone actually needs."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"You don't need to apologise. You were seriously unwell, and this is what the bed is for.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"OK. It was just a stupid night."},{"t":"0:21","band":"Last night","w":"D","s":"What do you remember about last night?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"A party at a friend's. I don't really want to go into it."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. What you tell me stays within the health team unless someone's at serious risk. I see a lot of people after G, and nothing will shock me.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Will this get back to my firm?"},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Not from us. I'd only share more widely if someone was at serious risk, and I'd talk to you first. What did you take last night?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"G in my drink. Four or five beers. Then I felt sick and left. Then nothing."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"Did you mean to take that much, or to hurt yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"No. God, no. I just misjudged it."},{"t":"1:04","band":"The pattern","w":"D","s":"When did you first use G, and how has your use changed since?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Five years ago. Now most weekends. I need more than I used to."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"How often across a weekend, and how much each time?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"About a millilitre and a half. Every two hours or so."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"And during the week? Do you ever use it to sleep, or first thing in the morning?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"A bit most nights, for the last four months. To sleep."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Have you ever passed out on it before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Twice. Friends put me on my side."},{"t":"1:40","band":"Withdrawal","w":"D","s":"What happens if you miss your night dose?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Shaky, sweaty, heart racing, can't sleep. Actually, a bit like now."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"When was your last dose?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Around midnight. So I'm not addicted, am I? It's just to sleep."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Needing it to sleep and feeling shaky without it are signs your body now relies on it. That matters, because withdrawal from G can get serious quickly. I'll ask the medical team to start treatment straight after we talk.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Oh. OK. That's scary."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Other drugs","w":"D","s":"It is, and it's very treatable. What else do you use, at parties or other times?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Crystal, once a month, at parties."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke it or inject it? Do you always use new equipment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Inject. New needles. I shared a spoon once."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"And alcohol in a usual week?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Not much. A few beers at weekends."},{"t":"2:35","band":"Mood and intent","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, especially after a weekend?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Pretty low on Tuesdays. Then I'm fine."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Have you ever wished you wouldn't wake up, or thought about killing yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Never. I love my job. My sister would kill me."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Sexual health","w":"D","s":"Was the party about sex as well as drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Yes. It was a chemsex party. Several guys."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Did you use condoms? When were you last tested for HIV and hepatitis? Are you on PrEP?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No condoms. I've never been tested. I'm too scared. Not on PrEP."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Has anything ever happened at a party that you didn't agree to?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I can't remember the end of last night. I don't know what happened."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"That must be really frightening. You don't have to decide anything now. If something happened you didn't agree to, a specialist centre can help, and you choose whether police are involved.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I'll think about it."},{"t":"3:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Because there was sex without condoms last night, I'd like sexual health to see you today for PEP, medicine that lowers the chance of HIV. It works best as soon as possible, within 72 hours.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that existed."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"They'll also offer HIV and hepatitis tests, a hepatitis B jab and talk about PrEP. Until you're ready to stop G: never with alcohol, measure it with a syringe, leave two hours between doses, never use alone.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"I can't really enjoy sex without G. Is there actually help for that?"},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Yes, and asking is a big step. Chemsex support services help people rebuild sex without drugs. I can refer you today, and write to your GP if you're happy.","m":[9,2]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. Both."},{"t":"4:14","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the main things we've agreed?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Treatment for the shakes now. Sexual health today for PEP and tests. The chemsex service. Never G with drink."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you feel more shaky, confused or start seeing things, tell the nurse straight away. I'll see you tomorrow before you go home.","m":[10,11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Explained role, routine nature of the review and confidentiality with its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Was non-judgemental, validated his guilt and distress, and normalised","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Took a GBL history: last night, onset, dose, frequency, tolerance, night-time use, previous collapses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked about withdrawal symptoms and timing of last dose, and recognised early withdrawal","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked about other drugs, alcohol and injecting practice","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asked directly about intent, mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asked about chemsex, condom use, BBV testing, PrEP and consent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranged same-day sexual health review for PEP and testing","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gave harm reduction advice and offered chemsex support and GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Escalated withdrawal to the medical team and safety-netted symptoms","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checked understanding and set a follow-up review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Establishes dependence and current withdrawal, asks directly about intent and chemsex risks, and leaves with withdrawal treatment, same-day PEP and a support referral.","borderline":"Good drug history but misses the withdrawal signs, consent or the PEP window.","fail":"Judgemental or vague about sex and drugs, accepts 'accidental' without asking, or discharges without a plan."}},"c-s-51":{"setup":"General adult clinic, Tuesday afternoon. Mr Gareth Hollis, 46, referred by his GP with six weeks of low mood; he has had pancreatitis and drinks daily. Take a history to reach a likely diagnosis and assess his risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Gareth Hollis, 46, man, council office administrator","open":"The GP said it's complicated. I just thought I'd get some antidepressants.","manner":"Flat, slow and hopeless at first; little eye contact. Becomes tearful talking about his wife. Opens up about drinking once he feels he is not being judged.","free":["Six weeks of feeling low and not enjoying anything","Off work for two weeks and expects to be sacked","His wife moved to her sister's three weeks ago, saying he drinks too much","He expected antidepressants"],"ifasked":["Drinks 4-6 pints of 4% lager a day, more on a binge; at home in the daytime and at the pub alone in the evening","Only started drinking three years ago; it became daily and heavy over the last few months, before the low mood","Craves a drink every couple of hours and cannot stop once started; needs more than he used to","Shakes and diarrhoea within hours of stopping; once managed two days and became confused and saw frightening things on the walls","Has thought of getting very drunk and jumping in the river near home; hope of reconciling with his wife keeps him going","Started a small pan fire while drunk and put it out himself","Father and grandmother had alcohol problems; no other mental illness in him or the family","Has never been violent; shouts at his wife and feels guilty; does not drive"],"worries":["Why can't I just have antidepressants?","Am I an alcoholic?","Will I lose my job?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when his wife is mentioned; settles if validated","Gives vague answers about drink ('a few') unless asked for a typical day","Discloses the river plan only if asked directly about suicide","Becomes defensive if lectured about drinking"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Hollis. I'm Dr Patel, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP asked me to see you about your mood. What we talk about stays within the team looking after you. How have things been?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"The GP said it's complicated. I just thought I'd get some antidepressants."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds frustrating, coming in expecting one thing. Your low mood matters, and I want to understand it properly so we get the right help. Tell me about it.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"I'm just flat. Nothing's worth doing. I haven't been to work in two weeks."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"When did you last feel like your usual self?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"About six weeks ago, I suppose. It's been going downhill since."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you still get pleasure from? And how are your sleep, appetite and energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I wake at all hours, I barely eat, and I'm shattered all day."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Do you find yourself blaming yourself for things? How's your concentration?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Can't concentrate on anything. And yes. My wife's gone to her sister's. That's on me."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry, with your wife away as well. I'm sorry. What did she say when she left?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"(tearful) That I drink too much. That I'm horrible to be around. I shout at her over nothing."},{"t":"1:50","band":"Drinking","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. I'm not here to judge. Can you talk me through yesterday, from when you woke up, including every drink?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"A few cans at home in the day. Then the pub in the evening."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"What do you drink, what strength, and roughly how many in a day?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Lager, ordinary stuff. Four to six pints a day, more some days. Mostly on my own."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"So about 11 to 14 units a day, roughly 80 a week. How strong is the urge to drink? Once you start, can you stop?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"I'm thinking about it every couple of hours. Once I start, that's it."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Do you need more now to feel the same effect? And what have you given up because of drinking?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"Yeah, more than I used to. I don't do anything else. Just the pub."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"You were in hospital with pancreatitis. Did anyone link it to alcohol, and did you carry on afterwards?","m":[5,10]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"I'm pretty sure it was the drink. It scared me. But I kept going."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Withdrawal","w":"D","s":"What happens if you go a morning without a drink?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Shakes, and my stomach goes. I can't sleep unless I've had a drink."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Have you ever tried to stop? Any fits, confusion, or seeing or hearing things that weren't there?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Once I managed two days. I was all over the place. Saw things on the walls. Horrible nightmares."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. It tells me your body reacts strongly to stopping, so stopping must be done safely, with medical help.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:54","band":"Which came first","w":"D","s":"Can I check the order of things? When did the drinking become daily and heavy, and when did your mood drop?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"I only started drinking three years ago. It's been heavy for a few months now. The low mood came after that."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Since it got heavy, have you had a sober week or two? How was your mood then?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"No. Not a single week."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"Before the drinking, any depression, panic or anxiety? Have you heard or seen things when you weren't stopping drink? And anyone in the family with mental illness or alcohol problems?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"No, never. Only when I stopped. My dad and my nan both drank."},{"t":"4:46","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Feeling this low, some people start to think about dying. Has that happened for you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I think about getting really drunk and going in the river near ours."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That tells me how much pain you're in. How close have you come, and how likely is it you'd act on it?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"I've walked down there. I reckon I'll be gone within a month, especially if I get sacked or she says it's over."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going right now?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Just hoping she'll come back if I get better."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"That hope matters. Has anything risky happened when drunk: cooking, driving, falls, or anyone getting hurt?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"I set a pan on fire once. Put it out. I don't drive. Never hit her, ever."},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"Any problems with memory, balance, numb feet, or yellow eyes?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"Not that I've noticed."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"From what you've said, the drink has a real hold on you. We call that alcohol dependence. Heavy drinking can cause low mood very like this, and it started after the drinking got heavy.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"So I'm not depressed?"},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"Your low mood is real. Once you've been safely off alcohol for 3 to 4 weeks, we look again. If it's still there, we treat depression in its own right.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:28","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Because of the river thoughts, I want the crisis team to see you today, and I'll speak to my consultant now. Please don't stop drinking suddenly on your own. Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"The drink might be making me low. I see the crisis team today, and I don't stop on my own."},{"t":"6:48","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. I'm grateful you were so honest today.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and confidentiality, opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to his distress and expectations with empathy, without judging his drinking","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes core depressive symptoms and six-week duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies drinking: drink, amount, units, typical day, setting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits dependence: craving, loss of control, tolerance, neglect, persistence despite harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits withdrawal symptoms and past confusion and hallucinations on stopping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes timeline: heavy drinking before low mood; mood in any sober period","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis and anxiety outside withdrawal, and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plan, intent and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about fire, driving, accidents, harm to others and physical complications","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains likely alcohol dependence and reassessment of mood after abstinence, in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees same-day safety action, warns against sudden stopping, checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Counts units, confirms dependence and complicated withdrawal, dates which came first, finds the river plan and names a likely diagnosis kindly.","borderline":"Covers mood and drinking but blurs the timeline or assesses suicide risk superficially.","fail":"Diagnoses depression without quantifying drink or ordering the timeline, or misses the suicide plan."}},"c-s-52":{"setup":"Alcohol service clinic, Thursday morning. Mr Desmond Clarke, 60, long-standing alcohol dependence, has been falling and confabulating; Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is suspected. Clinic nurse Sam is present. Examine his cerebellar function and act on your findings.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond Clarke, 60, man, retired bus driver with alcohol dependence (Sam, clinic nurse, chaperones)","open":"Morning. I've just come to drop this letter off for the manager.","manner":"Cheerful, cooperative and unaware of his memory problems. Fills gaps with confident stories. Anxious about falling when asked to close his eyes; settles with reassurance.","free":["Says he is here to deliver a letter","Happy to be examined","Says he feels fine"],"ifasked":["Walks with a stick most of the time and has fallen twice this month","Mild ache in his lower back, nothing severe","Last drink last night; drinks most days","Feet feel numb 'like walking on cotton wool'","Sometimes sees double when looking sideways","Cannot say today's date; says it is 'early in the year'"],"worries":["What's all this for?","Am I going to fall?","I'm not staying in hospital, am I?"],"rules":["Forgets multi-step instructions; succeeds if each step is demonstrated and given singly","Walks wide-based with short steps; cannot heel-toe; sways with feet together and steps out when eyes close","Shows horizontal nystagmus on looking to each side; heel-shin clumsy both sides; arms only mildly affected","Reluctant to go to hospital; agrees if the reason is explained simply and kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Introduce","w":"D","s":"Good morning. I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors here. Can I check your full name and date of birth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Desmond Clarke. Fourth of May, nineteen sixty-six. I've come to drop this letter off for the manager."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you for bringing it, Desmond. While you're here, I'd like to check your balance and coordination. It won't hurt, and I'll show you each step. Is that alright?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Course. I'm fine, mind."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"This is Sam, one of our nurses. Would you like Sam to stay with us? And do you have any pain anywhere?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Sam can stay. Bit of a sore back, nothing much."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll be gentle with your back. I'm just washing my hands first.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:54","band":"Inspect","w":"D","s":"I see you've brought a stick. Do you use it all the time?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Mostly. I've had a couple of tumbles lately."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Sorry to hear that. Could you sit forward, away from the back of the chair, with your arms folded? Like this.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"(sways to the left, unfolds an arm to steady himself) Bit wobbly."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Gait and Romberg","w":"D","s":"That's helpful, thank you. Now could you stand up? I'm right beside you. Walk to the door and back at your normal pace.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"(walks with feet wide apart, short steps, veering) There you go."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Well done. Next, walk heel to toe, like on a tightrope. Watch me first. Sam and I are either side of you.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"(manages one step, then lurches) Can't do that. Never could."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That's fine; that tells me a lot. Now put your feet side by side, touching, hands down by your sides, eyes open.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"(sways a little) Like this?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Now I'd like you to close your eyes for a few seconds. I'm right here and I'll catch you.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"I don't like this. I'll go over."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"That's a sensible worry. Sam's on one side, I'm on the other. Just three seconds.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"(closes eyes, sways more and steps out; doctor steadies him) Whoa."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Got you. Eyes open now. Let's sit you down. You did really well.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:40","band":"Speech and eyes","w":"D","s":"Can you say after me: British constitution.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"British consti... constitution. (slightly slurred)"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"And: baby hippopotamus.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"Baby hippopotamus."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now keep your head still; just your eyes follow my finger. Say if you see two of it.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"(eyes flick rapidly on looking left and right) Goes double out to the side a bit."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. Up and down too... good.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:26","band":"Arms","w":"D","s":"Let your arm go floppy; I'll move it at the elbow and wrist. Now the other one.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Floppy as you like."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Now watch me: pat one palm with the other hand, flipping front, back, front, back, quickly. You try with this hand.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"(slightly irregular) What was I doing again?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"No problem, let me show you again. Front, back, front, back. Lovely. Now the other hand.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"(slightly clumsy on both sides) Like that?"},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Now finger to your nose, then out to my finger, and keep going. Then swap hands.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"(slightly overshoots the finger on both sides) Moving target, that."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Legs","w":"D","s":"Now let your legs dangle. I'll tap your knees and ankles with this hammer.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"(knee jerks present; no ankle jerks) Feet are numb anyway. Like cotton wool."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's useful. Lie back on the couch. Put this heel on the other knee and slide it down your shin, like this. Then the other side.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"(heel wobbles and slips off the shin on both sides) Not very good, am I?"},{"t":"5:00","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"You did everything I asked, and that helps me a lot. That's all the tests. Thank you, Desmond. I'm washing my hands again.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Your balance, your legs and your eyes tell me your body is short of a vitamin called thiamine. Drink uses it up. A drip in hospital today can stop things getting worse.","m":[8,9,10]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Hospital? I've got things to do. I'm not stopping in."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I hear you; nobody wants a hospital day. Without it, your memory and walking could get worse for good. Sam can go with you. Would you be willing?","m":[7,9,10]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"If Sam comes. And it's for my legs."},{"t":"5:48","band":"Interpret and act","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Sam, he has a wide-based ataxic gait, leg ataxia worse than arms, unsteady even with eyes open, and absent ankle jerks with numb feet.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:02","w":"D","s":"With new nystagmus and confusion, I'm treating this as possible Wernicke's on top of Korsakoff's. He needs IV Pabrinex today. I'll phone the medical registrar and my consultant now.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Sam: I'll take him over to medical admissions and stay till he's seen."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Desmond, can you tell me what's happening next?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"Sam's taking me for a drip. A vitamin."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"That's right. Thank you for being so patient with me today.","m":[8]}],"marks":[{"c":"Confirms identity, washes hands, explains and gains consent, offers chaperone, asks about pain","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Inspects for walking aids and tests sitting balance for truncal ataxia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses gait, heel-to-toe walking and Romberg's test, standing close to prevent falls","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses speech with test phrases and eye movements for nystagmus","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses tone, rapid alternating movements and finger-nose on both sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses reflexes and heel-shin test on both sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks his understanding of what happens next and responds to his reluctance with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Thanks him, repeats hand hygiene, and summarises the findings accurately","d":"Close"},{"c":"Recognises possible Wernicke's and arranges same-day IV thiamine with senior input","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the findings and the reason for treatment to him in plain, jargon-free words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Demonstrates each test and repeats instructions patiently because of his memory loss","d":"Explanation"}],"global":{"pass":"Fluent, safe, complete bilateral cerebellar examination with demonstrations, correct interpretation and same-day IV thiamine.","borderline":"Most components done but one side missed, Romberg unsafe, or interpretation vague.","fail":"Omits gait or Romberg, misses nystagmus, rushes a confused man, or does not act on possible Wernicke's."}},"c-s-53":{"setup":"Urgent clinic, Monday afternoon. Mr Ryan Okafor, 25, with paranoid schizophrenia, stopped olanzapine six weeks ago and is hearing a voice again; he is smoking cannabis to cope. Take a brief history and agree a short-term plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Ryan Okafor, 25, man, graphic designer","open":"I just want this voice to stop.","manner":"Tense, tired and a little embarrassed. Relieved once he feels understood. Becomes guarded if lectured about cannabis; firm about not going to hospital.","free":["The voice is back and he wants it gone","He stopped olanzapine six weeks ago","Smoking cannabis helps him calm down"],"ifasked":["Stopped because he could not get an erection","Felt fine for two weeks after stopping, then thought colleagues were sabotaging his work and influencing his manager","The way cars are parked opposite seems to send him messages; he avoids the window","For the past fortnight, an intermittent single voice that seems to come from above him","3-4 joints most evenings for three weeks; heavy cannabis at both past episodes; no alcohol or other drugs","No thoughts of harming himself or anyone else","His mother lives nearby; his manager asked if he was OK","Does not drive"],"worries":["Can I keep smoking a bit?","I'm not going to hospital, am I?","Do I have to take olanzapine forever?"],"rules":["Reveals the erection problem only if asked why he stopped, and more readily if reassured that side effects are common","Will restart olanzapine for now but not long term; accepts aripiprazole if side effects are explained","Wants the voice treated before seeing the drug service","Becomes upset if admission is insisted on; settles if it is offered as an option"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Shah, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming in straight from work. First I'll hear what's going on, then we'll work out a plan together. OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"I just want this voice to stop."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Let's start there. When did you first notice something was off?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"A month back, maybe. I got the feeling my team was turning on me."},{"t":"0:30","band":"What came back","w":"D","s":"What did you think they were doing?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Trying to wreck my project. Getting at my manager somehow."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"Have ordinary things started to feel like they're meant for you, like signs?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"The cars opposite. How they're parked feels like a message. I keep the blinds shut now."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voice. How long has it been there, and where do you hear it?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"A fortnight. Sort of over my head, comes and goes. I know it's the illness."},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening, especially at work. It took courage to come in.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:12","band":"Why he stopped","w":"D","s":"Your GP says you came off olanzapine six weeks ago. Side effects are a very common reason. What was it for you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"It's a bit embarrassing."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"Take your time. Nothing you say will shock me, and it really helps me plan.","m":[1,3]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"I couldn't get an erection. It was wrecking things with my girlfriend."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's common and we can work around it. How were you after stopping?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Good for a fortnight or so. Then the worrying crept in."},{"t":"1:54","band":"Cannabis","w":"D","s":"Your GP mentioned cannabis. How many joints in a typical day right now?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Three or four joints most nights. Since the worry started."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"What does it do for you? And was cannabis around the last two times you were unwell?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Takes the edge off. I was smoking loads at uni, and three years ago too."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or other drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No. Just weed."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Risk and support","w":"D","s":"With your team feeling against you, have you thought of hurting anyone, or felt you need to defend yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"No. I just keep my head down."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"And any thoughts of self-harm or of being better off dead?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Who's around you outside work? And do you drive?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Mum's ten minutes away. I get the bus. My manager pulled me aside last week and asked if I was OK. That's why I went to the GP."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"So two weeks after stopping olanzapine, the worry about work, the cars and the voice came back, and you've been smoking to cope. I think the illness is returning, and both stopping the tablets and the cannabis have played a part. How does that sound?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that fits. I just want to feel normal again."},{"t":"3:34","band":"Medicine","w":"D","s":"Olanzapine has worked for you twice, so going back on it is the fastest way to settle this. Once you're settled, we switch to one that's kinder on sex, like aripiprazole. Or we start aripiprazole now; it may take a bit longer. What do you think?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"I'll take the olanzapine for now. But not for good."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"That's fair; we'll write the switch into your plan today. Problems with erections aren't always the tablet, so I'll also do a blood test for prolactin, a hormone that can cause them.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"The fear often eases within days and the voice over a few weeks. Olanzapine makes you sleepy and hungry, so take it at night. We'll check weight, sugar and cholesterol.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"And the other one?"},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Aripiprazole may make you restless, queasy or wakeful in the first weeks. Rarely it triggers urges, like gambling or spending; tell us straight away if so.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:38","band":"Cannabis plan","w":"P","s":"Could I still have a smoke in the evenings? It settles me."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like it helps. Honestly, I'd advise stopping completely. It tends to feed the voice and the fear, and blunts what the medicine can do.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Our drug service can help you stop, including with sleep and irritability. Shall I refer you now, so it's ready when you are?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"OK. But I want the voice sorted first."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"That's reasonable. I'll make the referral today.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:14","band":"Support","w":"D","s":"I'd like the crisis team to visit you at home this week, and a care coordinator to see you weekly. A hospital stay is an option, but from what you've told me, home treatment should work.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"Good. I don't want hospital."},{"t":"5:34","w":"D","s":"How about a week off work, with your mum popping round in the evenings? If the fear is bad, a few days of a calming tablet may help too.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I'll ask Mum. A week off would help."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"I'll talk to my consultant, write to your GP, and give you the plan in writing. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up today's plan?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Olanzapine tonight, then switch later. Stop the weed. Crisis team visits. Drug service when I'm ready."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Your next appointment is within seven days. Should things worsen, or the voice tells you to do anything, ring the crisis line or 111, option 2, or go to A&E.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I feel a bit better already."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, agrees the purpose, and responds empathically to his fear and embarrassment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits persecutory ideas, ideas of reference and hallucination, with onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks why he stopped olanzapine and times relapse to stopping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies cannabis, its purpose, past episodes, and other substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about harm to others and self, support and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises and shares a view: relapse with two causes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers olanzapine with planned switch or aripiprazole now; plans prolactin check","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains expected benefit and side effects of the options","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises stopping cannabis with reasons and offers drug service at his pace","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges crisis team and care coordinator; mentions admission without insisting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and gives review within a week","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives crisis safety net: crisis line, 111 option 2, A&E","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds the erection problem and cannabis pattern, offers a medicine choice with side effects, and agrees a home-based plan with review and safety net.","borderline":"Good history but plan imposed, side effects thin, or cannabis advice without help offered.","fail":"Never asks why he stopped, ignores cannabis, or insists on admission."}},"c-s-54":{"setup":"Outpatient clinic, Wednesday morning. Mr Josh Pennington, 23, a semi-professional cricketer, has been low since a costly dismissal in a deciding match. Identify his cognitive distortions and explain them to him in words he understands.","role":{"who":"Mr Josh Pennington, 23, man, semi-professional batsman","open":"(long pause) I don't really know why I'm here. I just messed up.","manner":"Slow to answer, poor eye contact, bleak. Becomes tearful about his children. Engages and brightens slightly when his evidence is gently reflected back.","free":["He was caught for 22 in a deciding match and they lost","He has refused to play since and thinks he should retire","He feels he lets everyone down"],"ifasked":["Nobody else on the team scored more than 10","Season batting average of 74, top of the league","His coach says professional clubs have asked about him; he thinks the coach is being kind","Married with two young children; nobody at home has criticised him","Believes he is failing as a husband and father","No voices or unusual experiences","No thoughts of suicide; his children keep him going","If shown inconsistencies, accepts he might be wrong though it still feels true"],"worries":["So you think I'm making it up?","Will thinking positive fix it?","What if I go back and fail again?"],"rules":["Long pauses at first; speeds up if the doctor is patient","Tearful when talking about his kids; settles if validated","Conviction drops from 95 to about 60 if evidence is gently reflected, not argued","Disengages if bombarded with jargon"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, Josh. I'm Dr Brennan, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP says things have been hard since a match. Can you tell me about it, in your own time?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"(long pause) I don't really know why I'm here. I just messed up."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"That's alright. Take your time. What happened?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Big match. Deciding game. I played a stupid shot and got caught. We lost."},{"t":"0:36","band":"The match","w":"D","s":"What went through your mind as you walked off?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"That I'd lost it for everyone. It was all on me."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That sounds a crushing thing to carry. How did the rest of the team score that day?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Badly. Nobody else got to 10. I got 22."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"So you top-scored with more than double anyone else, yet the loss feels all yours.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I was supposed to win it."},{"t":"1:14","band":"The season","w":"D","s":"How's your season been overall?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"Average of 74. But that doesn't count. Most of those were against weak sides."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"What does your coach think of you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"He says pro clubs have asked about me. He's just being nice."},{"t":"1:36","band":"The future","w":"D","s":"Where does this leave your cricket?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I'll never be good again. You're either great or you're nothing. I should retire."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Home","w":"D","s":"How are things at home?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I'm a useless husband. A useless dad. They deserve better."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you love them, and that makes this so painful. I'm sorry you're going through it. Has your wife or anyone said you're failing them?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody's said it. I just know."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Test","w":"D","s":"Can I ask: out of 100, how sure are you that you lost the match for the team?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Ninety-five."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"You scored 22 when nobody else reached 10. Is it possible the loss wasn't down to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"(pause) I suppose... if the others had got 20 each we'd have won."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"And your coach, your teammates and your family all rate you highly. Is it possible you're underselling yourself? How sure are you now?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"Maybe sixty. It still feels true, though."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"That's really honest. Feelings often lag behind the facts. Have you heard voices, or had experiences others might find strange?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"When you said your family deserve better, do you ever think about ending your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"No. I couldn't do that to my kids."},{"t":"3:24","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can I share what I notice? When people feel low, the mind bends thinking in typical ways. Would that be helpful?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"First: you took the blame for a loss the whole team shared. Doctors call that personalisation: making something about you when it isn't. Does that fit?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I do that."},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"Second: your average of 74 doesn't count, but one shot means everything. That's shrinking the good and blowing up the bad: minimisation and maximisation. Focusing on one moment and ignoring the rest is called selective abstraction. Make sense so far?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Like I only see the bad bit."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. Third: 'I'll never be good again' jumps to the worst possible outcome. That's catastrophising.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"And 'great or nothing' is black-and-white thinking: no middle ground, like a 74 average being worthless after one innings. How does that sound?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Put like that, it sounds daft."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"Not daft at all. It's what low mood does to everyone. Last one: deciding you're failing your family when nobody has said so is jumping to a conclusion without evidence. We call that arbitrary inference.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"So it's the depression talking?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Largely, yes. How would you put what we've covered, in your words?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"I blame myself, I shrink the good stuff, and I think the worst. And it's because I'm low."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Perfectly put. What questions do you have so far?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Will thinking positive fix it?"},{"t":"5:20","band":"Hope and plan","w":"D","s":"Not positive: balanced. Like a coach reviewing the whole innings, not one shot. I see lots of people with this, and it's very treatable. A talking therapy called CBT teaches you to catch and test these thoughts.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"What would I have to do?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Before we meet again, write down one bad moment each day: the thought, how sure you are, and the evidence for and against. I'll refer you for CBT and see you in four weeks.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"I can try that."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Good. If things get worse, or thoughts of hurting yourself appear, ring your GP, the crisis line or 111, option 2. Thanks for being so open, Josh.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Thanks. It's helped, actually."}],"marks":[{"c":"Opens gently, gives him time, and responds empathically to his distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits his thoughts about the match and his season in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits his thoughts about his future and his family in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction against his own evidence and rates belief before and after","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for hallucinations and unusual experiences","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains each distortion in plain words with his own examples before giving the label","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding in small chunks and invites questions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Normalises, offers hope and explains what CBT would do","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a concrete next step, review date and safety net","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Identifies five or more distortions from his words, tests them gently, explains each plainly with checking, screens risk and offers hope.","borderline":"Names distortions correctly but argues or lectures, or skips testing conviction.","fail":"Lists jargon without explanation, misses the delusion check, or ignores suicide risk."}},"c-s-55":{"setup":"Home visit, Friday morning. Mrs Helen Marsh, 42, has agoraphobia with panic and has not been seen for a year. Self-help and sertraline have helped little. Review her treatment, explain systematic desensitisation and graded exposure, and agree a new plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Helen Marsh, 42, woman, former shop assistant","open":"Thank you for coming. I'm sorry I kept missing appointments. I just couldn't get out.","manner":"Low but calm at home and relieved someone has come. Becomes visibly anxious talking about leaving the house; tearful about losing her job. Warms with patience and plain explanations.","free":["She cannot leave the house unless her husband Mark is with her","She lost her job two months ago through lateness","She is relieved someone came"],"ifasked":["At the door she shakes, sweats, feels dizzy and thinks she might die","Online self-help two years ago: tasks confusing; she panicked even thinking about them","Sertraline 50 mg every day for 18 months; helped a little at first","On some mornings she thinks her family would be better off without her; no plans; would never hurt Mark; no past attempts","Two glasses of red wine a week; no drugs; no family history of mental illness","Easiest step: imagining going out; hardest: the supermarket alone","Would prefer therapy at home; Mark could drive her to clinic later"],"worries":["Will you make me go out today?","Does desensitisation mean throwing me in at the deep end?","Who does the therapy, and where?"],"rules":["Becomes anxious if the plan sounds like big jumps; settles with reassurance about small steps","Tearful about her job; settles if validated","Agrees to clinic only as a later step; agrees readily to home-based start","Discloses morning thoughts of death only if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Marsh. I'm Dr Adeyemi; I've taken over your care from the clinic. Thank you for letting me visit.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Thank you for coming. I'm sorry I kept missing appointments. I just couldn't get out."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. It sounds like the last year has been really hard. That's exactly why I've come to you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","band":"What she has tried","w":"D","s":"Can we start with what you've tried? What did you make of the online course?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"I went every week. But the tasks confused me, and I'd panic just thinking about them."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"So the steps felt too big, and you were on your own with them. How's the sertraline been? Do you take it every day?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Religiously. 50 milligrams. It helped a bit at first, but my life's the same."},{"t":"0:56","band":"Current picture","w":"D","s":"What happens when you try to go out?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"(anxious) I stand at the door shaking. My heart races, I feel sick and dizzy. Like I'll die. So I go back in."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. Thank you for describing it. What can you manage with your husband?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"With Mark I can get to the car. That's how I lost my job, being late every day. (tearful) I loved that job."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. That's a real loss, and it shows how strong this anxiety is, not that you've failed.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:36","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"With things this bleak, some people start to wish they weren't alive. Has that crossed your mind?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Some mornings I think they'd all be better off without me."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you made any plans, or ever tried to harm yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I couldn't do that to Mark."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink? Anything else to calm your nerves? And any mental illness in the family?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Two glasses of wine a week. Nothing else. No family history."},{"t":"2:14","band":"The anxiety curve","w":"D","s":"Can I explain how anxiety works, because it's the key to the treatment? Anxiety is like a wave. If you stay where you are, it rises, peaks, and then falls by itself, usually within the hour.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"It never feels like it'll fall."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"That's because you've never been able to stay long enough. Going back in brings relief quickly, but it teaches your brain the door is dangerous. Each time you ride a wave out, the next one is smaller. Does that make sense?","m":[4,9]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"So going back in keeps it going."},{"t":"2:52","band":"The treatments","w":"D","s":"Exactly. The first treatment is graded exposure. We make a list of situations from easiest to hardest, and you face them in order, staying each time until the anxiety drops.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Does desensitisation mean throwing me in at the deep end?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Good question. No, that's a different approach. Systematic desensitisation uses the same ladder, but first you learn a relaxation skill, slow breathing and relaxing your muscles, and use it at every step, often starting by just imagining it. It's hard to be relaxed and panicky at once.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"I like the sound of the relaxation. I'd want that."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Then we'll build it in. Can you tell me the difference between the two in your words?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Both are small steps. The second one, I learn to relax first and use it each time."},{"t":"3:48","band":"Hierarchy","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Let's make your ladder. What's the smallest step you can imagine? Rate it from 0, no anxiety, to 100, the worst ever.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"Just imagining going out. Maybe 20. Opening the front door, 30."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"And standing on the doorstep for two minutes? Walking to the gate?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Doorstep, 40. Gate, 55. The post box with Mark, 65."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"And the top of the ladder?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"The corner shop with Mark, 80. The supermarket on my own, 100."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That's a really clear ladder. We start at the bottom and only move up when a step feels about half as scary as it did.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:38","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"Who does the therapy? Do I have to come to clinic?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"A therapist from our psychological therapies team, weekly, about an hour each time, usually 7 to 14 hours over a few months. The first sessions here at home, then outside, and clinic later as a step on your ladder.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Here? Then yes, I can do that."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Between sessions you'd practise every day and keep a diary. Mark can come with you for the early steps, then gradually step back. Would he be up for that?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"He'd do anything. He's been brilliant."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"I'd also like to increase your sertraline to 100 mg. You might feel a bit more jittery or sick for a week or two; that usually settles. I won't add anything like diazepam, because it makes this kind of learning harder.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"What if it all gets too much when I'm practising?"},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Good to plan for. Use your slow breathing, try to stay put, ring Mark, and note it for the therapist. If you feel unsafe or the dark thoughts get stronger, call the crisis line or 111, option 2. I'll write it all down for you.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:56","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me how you'd explain the plan to Mark tonight?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Therapist comes here weekly. Small steps, with relaxation, staying until it drops. Practise every day. Sertraline goes up."},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll refer you today and phone you in four weeks. Thank you, Mrs Marsh; you've been really brave today.","m":[9,1]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like there's a way out now."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges missed appointments without blame, and validates her distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reviews self-help, sertraline dose and adherence, and current symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, past attempts, alcohol and family history","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the anxiety curve, habituation and how escape maintains fear","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains graded exposure and systematic desensitisation accurately, including the relaxation pairing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Builds a hierarchy with her, rated 0 to 100, and explains when to move up","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees who delivers therapy, where, how often and daily practice, with husband's role","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reviews medication and plans for overwhelming anxiety with crisis numbers","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and agrees a review","d":"Close"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, other calming substances and family history","d":"Risk"}],"global":{"pass":"Reviews past treatment and risk, explains both methods clearly and distinctly, builds a rated hierarchy, and agrees a home-based plan with a safety net.","borderline":"Explains exposure but blurs desensitisation, or builds no hierarchy, or thin plan.","fail":"Skips suicide question, proposes clinic-only therapy or benzodiazepines, or explanations are confusing."}},"c-s-56":{"setup":"A&E, 23:10. Erin Walsh, 29, is three months into weekly psychodynamic therapy. She came in on her therapist's advice and wants you to tell the therapist she is quitting. Her session is tomorrow at 10:00. Explore and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Erin Walsh, 29, female, trainee solicitor, patient","open":"It's mortifying, honestly. I just want someone to tell her I'm done.","manner":"Bright, articulate and embarrassed. Brisk at first. Tearful when describing the silences. Thoughtful and relieved once links are offered gently; prickly if told it is 'all in her head'.","free":["Three months into weekly therapy; feels worse and cries after sessions","Wants the doctor to tell the therapist she is stopping","Therapist is often silent for minutes","No practical barrier to attending"],"ifasked":["Thinks the therapist is bored and waiting to get rid of her","Father moved to Spain with a new family when she was 7; rings on birthdays","Has always believed she 'wasn't worth staying for'","Boyfriend Jack working in Leeds; she fears he will get bored and thinks of ending it first","Feels her work supervisor prefers others","Therapist is punctual, professional and not leaving","No thoughts of self-harm"],"worries":["So it's all in my head?","Why does she go silent?","What if she agrees I should go?"],"rules":["Mentions her father only if asked whether the feeling reminds her of anyone","Becomes prickly if feelings are dismissed as 'just transference'; settles if they are called real","Agrees to go back only if her choice is respected"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Erin? I'm Dr Siddiqui, one of the psychiatrists working in A&E tonight. I've read the notes from your therapy service.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. Sorry. I feel ridiculous being here."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"You don't need to apologise. You look a bit embarrassed, and that's understandable. Talking about therapy to a stranger at this hour isn't easy.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"It's mortifying, honestly. I just want someone to tell her I'm done."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand that first. I won't go over your low mood again tonight; I want to hear what's been happening in therapy. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:46","band":"Her reasons","w":"D","s":"What has made you want to stop now, three months in?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"I feel worse than when I started. I cry after every session. It's not working."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"What did you want therapy to help with?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Make me feel normal again. Get my head straight for work."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Is anything practical getting in the way: the times, travel, work, cost?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"No. It's ten minutes from the office. Work have been fine about it."},{"t":"1:22","band":"The therapist","w":"D","s":"Has your therapist done anything that worried you, like being late, cancelling or overstepping?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"No. She's always on time. Very professional."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Has she mentioned leaving, or seemed inexperienced? And do you trust her with what you share?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"She's not leaving. I suppose I trust her. She just doesn't care."},{"t":"1:50","band":"The feeling in the room","w":"D","s":"Tell me about that. What is it like sitting with her, especially when she is quiet?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"She says nothing for minutes. I look at her and think she's bored. She'd rather see someone more interesting."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"That sounds a really lonely place to be, every week. What goes through your mind in those silences?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"That she's waiting for the hour to end. That she's going to get rid of me. (tearful) Sorry."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Take your time. It's clearly painful. Thank you for telling me.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"It's fine. It's just how it is."},{"t":"2:34","band":"Who else","w":"D","s":"Can I ask: does that feeling, of being about to be dropped, remind you of anyone, now or when you were younger?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Maybe my dad. He moved to Spain when I was seven. New family. He rings on my birthday."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"That must have been very hard at seven. How did you make sense of it then?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"That I wasn't worth staying for. I've never said that out loud."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"And now? Does that feeling come up with anyone else in your life?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Jack, my boyfriend. He's working in Leeds for three months. I keep waiting for him to say he's bored of me. Sometimes I think I should just end it first. Even my supervisor at work gives the good cases to the others."},{"t":"3:32","band":"The link","w":"D","s":"I'm wondering about something, and tell me if it doesn't fit. The feeling with your dad, with Jack, at work, and with your therapist sound similar: that you'll be left, so you think about leaving first. What do you make of that?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Oh. That's... a bit uncomfortable. But yes. That's exactly what I do."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"And I wonder if a bit of it is here tonight: you came wanting me to end it for you, so you don't have to face her.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Maybe. That's quite true actually."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"There's a name for this: transference. Strong feelings from an important earlier relationship get carried into a new one, like the one with your therapist.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"So it's all in my head?"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"No, the feelings are completely real. They just may have older roots. And I checked that she hasn't done anything wrong, because sometimes a therapist will have. Here it sounds as if she hasn't.","m":[8,4]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Why does she go silent though? It feels deliberate."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"It often is. Her kind of therapy leaves space so your own thoughts come up. In therapy like yours, these feelings are expected; they're often where the useful work begins. Feeling worse for a while in the middle is common.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"So feeling like this might mean it's working?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"It might. You've reached something that matters. Does that make sense so far?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"It does. Weirdly."},{"t":"5:08","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"It's your decision whether to carry on, and no one can make you. Before you decide, could you tell her tomorrow exactly what you've told me, including that you want to stop?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"What if she agrees I should go?"},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense after what happened with your dad. Therapists expect to hear this. You'd be testing the old fear with someone trained to stay with it.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll try."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"With your permission, I'll email her tonight to say you came in and why, so she isn't caught off guard. Is that alright?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. That would help."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"And if, after talking it through, it still feels wrong, she and her supervisor can look at other options with you, including another therapist.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Good to know there's a way out."},{"t":"6:03","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Before you go, any thoughts of hurting yourself tonight? And who is around for you?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Jack's back tomorrow night. I'll ring him."},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Go at ten, tell her the truth, including wanting to quit. You'll email her tonight."},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll also send a short letter to your GP. If things get worse, call the therapy service in the day, your GP, or 111 option 2 at night. Any questions?","m":[10,11,12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I feel lighter, actually."},{"t":"6:44","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so honest tonight. Good luck tomorrow.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her embarrassment and sets a non-judging frame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fears with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores why she wants to stop now and rules out practical barriers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks the therapist's conduct, experience, plans to leave and trust","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores what she feels and thinks in the room, including the silences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links the feeling to her father, partner and work with open questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers the pattern tentatively and notices it may be happening tonight","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains transference and the silences in plain words as real, expected and useful","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Respects her choice; agrees she raises stopping with the therapist; names other options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Seeks consent to email the therapist and letters the GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a brief safety check and a clear safety net","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears her out, checks the therapist's conduct, gently links the feeling to her father and partner, explains transference simply and sends it back to tomorrow's session.","borderline":"Kind and safe but explains transference as jargon, or skips the check on the therapist.","fail":"Agrees to pass on her resignation, dismisses her feelings as imaginary, or takes a full depression history instead."}},"c-s-57":{"setup":"CAMHS clinic. Maya Kowalski, 13, is still moderately depressed after CBT and fluoxetine. Her parents clash over her care and may be separating. Explain family therapy to her father and explore whether it suits them.","role":{"who":"Mr Tomasz Kowalski, 46, male, Maya's father","open":"Honestly? She's thirteen. I think she's being a teenager. And the CBT's done nothing. Fourteen sessions.","manner":"Tired, sceptical, a little defensive. Flares if blamed, calms once told nobody caused it. Becomes open and quietly sad when the separation is discussed kindly.","free":["Thinks Maya is 'just a teenager'","Sceptical about CBT and therapy generally","Maya has dropped hockey and stays in her room","Works long shifts"],"ifasked":["Told Maya she had nothing to be sad about during the thought diary task; row with her mother followed","He and Maya's mother are separating; they have not told Maya","Maya asked if they were splitting up and he denied it","Mother is depressed and on GP antidepressants; stopped bringing Maya","Arguments are shouting only; never physical","Not sure the mother would sit in a room with him"],"worries":["What does it involve: how often, how many?","Do we both have to come every time?","Why would this work when CBT didn't?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if he hears blame; calms only when told nobody caused it","Reveals the separation only if asked how the parents are getting on","Agrees to try once his three questions are answered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mr Kowalski? I'm Dr Mensah, one of the doctors in the CAMHS team. Thanks for coming in. Maya's with her therapist and she's happy for us to talk.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Fine. Her therapist said you wanted to talk about something else now."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That's right, family therapy. Before I explain, how do you see Maya at the moment, and how have you found the CBT?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Honestly? She's thirteen. I think she's being a teenager. And the CBT's done nothing. Fourteen sessions."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds frustrating, after all that time and effort. Has she improved at all?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Bit less tearful, maybe. Still in her room all evening. Dropped hockey."},{"t":"0:50","band":"The home pattern","w":"D","s":"What happens at home when she's low? How do you and her mum each respond?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"I tell her to get on with it. Her mum fusses. We don't agree."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Maya mentioned the evening with the thought diary. How was that from your side?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"She wanted me to say what bad thoughts she has. I said she's got a good life, nothing to be sad about. Her mum went for me. Big row."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you were trying to reassure her, and it went badly. Lots of parents disagree about how to help. That's very common.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"I suppose."},{"t":"1:35","band":"Parents and safety","w":"D","s":"Can I ask how things are between you and Maya's mum at the moment?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"(pause) We're separating. We haven't told Maya. She asked me last week and I said no."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. It sounds a really hard time for all of you.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"It is. I'm at work most of the time. Her mum's not been well either."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"How is her mum herself?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Depressed. The GP's got her on tablets. That's why she's stopped bringing Maya."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry to hear that. When you two argue, does it ever get physical, or does anyone at home feel frightened?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"No. Shouting. Never more than that."},{"t":"2:26","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Let me explain family therapy. It sees a family a bit like a team: what each person does affects everyone else.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"The therapist asks each of you how you notice things and how you react, so no one person is the problem.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Hang on. So you think we're the problem? That we've done this to her?"},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"No, and I'm sorry if it sounded that way. Nobody caused Maya's depression; it's rarely down to one thing. You clearly love her. Families are a big part of recovery, so involving you is a strength.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"(calmer) Right. Okay."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Some teams also have colleagues watching on video who share ideas with you, but only if you agree. You can meet them first.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"That sounds a bit odd. But fine, if we can say no."},{"t":"3:21","band":"Why not CBT","w":"P","s":"But why would this work when CBT didn't?"},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. CBT has given Maya some skills and she's a little better. What she says is it gets undone at home. Family therapy works on that part, which CBT on her own can't reach.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"The diary thing, you mean."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Yes. And children usually sense tension even when nothing is said. Do you think she might be picking something up?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"She asked if we're splitting up. So... yes. I suppose she knows."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"That's a really honest thing to say. Family therapy could also help you both work out how to tell her together.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"That would help. Neither of us knows how."},{"t":"4:12","band":"How it runs","w":"P","s":"So what does it involve? How often? How many?"},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"It varies, but often sessions are every two to three weeks, about an hour, for a few months. The family therapist agrees the details with you at the first meeting.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Do we both have to come every time? I do twelve-hour shifts. And I don't know if her mum would sit with me."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"No. Not everyone needs to come every time. Separated parents can come together or separately, and we can ask for evening slots.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"That's better than I thought."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Maya will be asked who she wants there too. She told her therapist she wants you both involved.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Did she? (pause) Okay."},{"t":"5:04","band":"Fit and decision","w":"D","s":"Would you be willing to give it a try?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"I'm still not sure about all this therapy stuff. But if it helps her, yes."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Could you talk to Maya's mum about it? We can also offer her a separate chat, and she should keep seeing her GP.","m":[9,5]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"I'll tell her tonight."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"Maya stays on her fluoxetine, and her CBT therapist keeps seeing her for now. If you ever notice talk of self-harm, or things getting much worse, call our duty line or 111 option 2.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"Okay. Will do."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what family therapy would involve?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Sessions every few weeks, not always all of us, working on how we deal with her at home. Not blaming us."},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll make the referral today, and give you a leaflet. Any other questions?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks. Better than I expected."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open. I'll see you at Maya's review in four weeks.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Maya's consent and asks the father's view first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and distress with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores how each parent responds when Maya is low, without criticism","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about the parents' relationship and the hidden separation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for domestic abuse and asks about the mother's own health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains family therapy as a whole-family, no-blame approach, including observing teams with consent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers why family therapy may help when CBT has not","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains frequency, duration and flexible attendance, deferring details to the therapist","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a referral and that both parents and Maya will be asked who attends","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Continues current treatment and gives a safety net","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Invites questions, offers written information and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains family therapy clearly, names that nobody is to blame, explores the separation and safety, answers all three questions and agrees a flexible referral.","borderline":"Accurate explanation but misses the separation or the abuse screen, or sounds faintly critical of the parents.","fail":"Blames the parents, lectures on therapy models without answering his questions, or ignores the family conflict."}},"c-s-58":{"setup":"EIP clinic. Callum Reid, 19, is well on risperidone seven months after a first episode of psychosis, but still smokes cannabis. Explore his vulnerability, share a formulation and assess whether CBT for psychosis suits him.","role":{"who":"Mr Callum Reid, 19, male, engineering student, patient","open":"Sure. I got a bit paranoid at uni, I suppose.","manner":"Friendly, a bit guarded. Embarrassed about side effects. Quiet and hurt about the school betrayal. Engaged and curious once the formulation links up.","free":["Believed housemates had cameras in the smoke alarm; heard whispering","Three weeks in hospital, then home treatment; well for months","Takes risperidone every night","Wants to finish his degree"],"ifasked":["Born at 33 weeks; two weeks in special care; normal milestones","Maternal uncle has schizophrenia","Parents split when he was 11; father drinks","At 15 a friend shared his private messages with the year group; mistrust since","Was smoking skunk most days before becoming unwell; now weekends","Erectile difficulty on risperidone; thinks about stopping","Not sure it was psychosis","Notices that feeling judged makes him anxious and stay in"],"worries":["How does talking stop me getting ill again?","Why would I trust a therapist?","How long does it take?"],"rules":["Mentions side effects only if asked about sexual side effects specifically","Becomes defensive if lectured about cannabis; open if not judged","Accepts the formulation and agrees to CBT if it is explained in his terms"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hi Callum, I'm Dr Afolabi, one of the doctors in the EIP team. Thanks for coming in. The team has recommended CBT for psychosis, a talking therapy. I'd like to understand how things came about for you, then see together if it suits you. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Sure. I got a bit paranoid at uni, I suppose."},{"t":"0:22","band":"The episode","w":"D","s":"Can you take me back to when things started to feel wrong?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"October last year. New shared house. I got convinced the lads had put cameras in the smoke alarm to film me and post it online. And I heard whispering through the wall."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What happened then, and how long before you felt better?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Mum came and got me. Three weeks in hospital, then the home team. About two months to feel normal."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"And now? Any whispering, feeling watched, low mood, or thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"None of that. Bit stressed about coursework. That's it."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Predisposing","w":"D","s":"Good. Can I go right back? Were there any problems in your mum's pregnancy, or when you were born?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"I was born early, thirty-three weeks. Two weeks in special care. Walked and talked normally, Mum says."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"Is there any family history of mental health problems?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Mum's brother has schizophrenia. He's in a care home."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What was growing up like?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Okay. Mum and Dad split when I was eleven. Dad drinks. I stayed with Mum and my sister."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Was there a time someone really let you down, or you were bullied?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"(quiet) At fifteen a mate screenshotted my private messages and sent them round the whole year. I didn't go out for weeks."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"That sounds really humiliating, and painful. How has it affected you since?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"I don't trust people. I keep stuff to myself."},{"t":"2:04","band":"Precipitating","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. What changed in the months before you got ill?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Moved into the house. Deadlines. And I was smoking skunk most days."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Perpetuating","w":"D","s":"How about cannabis now?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Weekends. Everyone at uni does. Go on, tell me to stop."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"I'm not going to lecture you; it's your choice. We'll come back to it. How do you feel about the risperidone?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I take it every night. But I think about stopping."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Is anything about it bothering you? Some men notice sexual side effects.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"(pause) Yeah. Problems in bed. It's embarrassing."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's common, and fixable. I'll make sure we look at it. How did it feel being told it was psychosis?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Not sure. Maybe it was just stress and weed."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Protective","w":"D","s":"So what stops you from stopping the tablets?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"I'm scared of it happening again. I can't lose another year."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"What matters most to you right now, and who's in your corner?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Finishing my degree. Mum and my sister. My best mate from home."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"When you're stressed, do you notice how your thoughts and feelings affect what you do?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Yeah. If I think people are judging me, I get anxious and stay in. I like knowing how stuff works."},{"t":"3:42","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you how I'm making sense of it? Then tell me if it doesn't fit?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Your uncle's illness and being born early may have made you more sensitive. The messages at fifteen left you wary of people. Then a new house, deadlines and strong cannabis may have turned that wariness into feeling filmed.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Right now, cannabis, the side effects and doubts about the diagnosis could raise the risk again. Your degree, your family and the tablets protect you. Does that fit?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"I've never linked the school thing before. Yeah. That fits."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So how does CBT actually help? It's just talking."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Good question. CBT looks at how thoughts, feelings, what we do and body sensations all feed each other.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Say you think 'they're filming me'. You feel scared, your heart pounds, and you stay in your room, and so you never find out otherwise. With the psychologist you'd map that, test thoughts gently, and spot warning signs early.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Like debugging my own thinking."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. It can also help with trust, the cannabis and your doubts about the tablets. You don't have to agree it was psychosis to do it. It's one-to-one with our psychologist, at least sixteen sessions, usually weekly, alongside the tablets.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:15","band":"Suitability","w":"D","s":"From what you've said, you're curious, you can see links, and you really want to stay well. Those are what help CBT work. How do you feel about trying it?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"After the school thing I don't really trust people. Why would I open up to a therapist?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"That makes complete sense. She'll go at your pace, and building trust can be part of the work.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. I'll give it a go."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Great. I'll refer you to the psychologist today. I'll also book a prolactin blood test and talk to the consultant about the side effects. And if you want help cutting down cannabis, our substance worker can see you.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:58","w":"P","s":"Yeah, the blood test. Maybe the weed worker too."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me in your words what CBT would do for you?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"Help me understand why it happened and catch it early if it starts again."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If you notice poor sleep or feeling watched again, call your care coordinator, or 111 option 2 out of hours. Any questions?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"No. That actually made sense."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Callum. You've been really open today.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and explains the aim of the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets a timeline of the episode and checks current symptoms and thoughts of harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about pregnancy, birth, family history and childhood adversity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies precipitants: the move, deadline stress and cannabis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies perpetuating influences: cannabis now, medication doubts, sexual side effects, doubt about diagnosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies protective influences and his goals","d":"Content"},{"c":"Shares a tentative formulation linking past mistrust to paranoia, and checks it fits","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains CBT for psychosis using his own experience, with its format","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Assesses psychological mindedness, motivation and readiness to engage","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees referral, side-effect review and cannabis support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets and closes","d":"Close"},{"c":"Responds empathically and without judgement to distress, embarrassment and cannabis","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers all 5Ps including birth and cannabis, shares a formulation he accepts, explains CBT for psychosis in his terms and agrees a referral.","borderline":"Good history but a thin or absent formulation, or misses side effects or obstetric history.","fail":"Takes a symptom history only, lectures about cannabis, or leaves CBT unexplained."}},"c-s-59":{"setup":"Eating disorders clinic. You diagnosed Chloe Bennett, 18, with anorexia nervosa last week (BMI 15). With Chloe's consent, explain the illness, its risks, treatment and outlook to her mother, and how she can help.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Bennett, 49, female, Chloe's mother","open":"Only what I've read online. People die from it. I'm terrified she's going to die.","manner":"Anxious and frightened, firing questions. Tearful when she discloses her own teenage eating problems. Calmer and hopeful once given clear information and a role.","free":["Shocked by Chloe's weight loss when she came home","Chloe runs every day, even in the dark","Meals end in tears","Chloe is her only child"],"ifasked":["She had problems with food at 16 and fears she passed it on","She has never talked about weight at home","She has been taking Chloe's arguments about food personally","She would like to join a carers' group"],"worries":["Is she going to die?","Why can't she just eat?","Is there a tablet? Will she go into hospital?","Can people fully recover, and what can I do?"],"rules":["Mentions her own past only if the doctor is warm and non-judging","Becomes more frightened if given a list of statistics with no context","Settles when told she is not to blame and given a clear job"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Bennett? I'm Dr Rahman, one of the doctors in the eating disorders service. I saw Chloe last week, and she's fine with me talking to you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I've been desperate to talk to someone."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I can imagine. What do you already know about anorexia, and what's worrying you most?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Only what I've read online. People die from it. I'm terrified she's going to die."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thing to read about your own daughter. It is a serious illness, and that's why we'll watch her closely. Can I explain it step by step, and you stop me any time?","m":[2,1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. Please."},{"t":"0:42","band":"What it is","w":"D","s":"Anorexia is an illness where someone eats far too little, often exercises a lot, and is very frightened of putting on weight.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Chloe's weight is well below the healthy range. She counts calories, runs to stop weight gain, and her periods have stopped. Together, that's why we've made the diagnosis.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"She runs even when it's dark and icy. I couldn't understand it."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"That fits. Does that make sense so far?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"Yes. But why? Why would she do this?"},{"t":"1:13","band":"Why","w":"D","s":"There's no single cause. Genes, personality such as perfectionism, life stresses and pressure about looks all play a part. Starting university, new routines and worry about putting on weight may have tipped it.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"(quietly) I had problems with food at sixteen. Did I pass this on to her?"},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that can't have been easy to say. Genes play a small part, but many things together cause anorexia. You haven't caused this, and your support now really matters.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've been so careful never to talk about weight at home."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"That shows how much you've tried to protect her. It's natural to look for a reason, but this isn't your fault.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Risks","w":"D","s":"Let me explain the physical side, because that's what we watch first. Very low weight strains the heart. Chloe's pulse is slow, 54, and her blood pressure low.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"So her heart could stop?"},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"That's the risk we guard against. Her heart tracing is normal now, which is reassuring. Low weight can also thin the bones, stop periods, and lower the infection-fighting white cells; hers are slightly low.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"So each week we'll check her weight, pulse, blood pressure and blood tests, with her GP. Does that make sense?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Yes. Weekly. Good."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Treatment","w":"P","s":"What treatment is there? Is there a tablet?"},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"I wish there were, but no medicine treats anorexia itself. Most people are treated as outpatients. A dietitian gives a clear meal plan, building up gradually.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"And a therapist offers a talking therapy designed for eating disorders, often weekly for many months.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Will she have to go into hospital?"},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Not at the moment. If her weight or heart checks get worse, a hospital stay may be needed to keep her safe. We'd talk that through with you both.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Why can't she just eat? She knows she's too thin."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"It's so hard to watch. The illness makes eating feel frightening, almost like a phobia. That's why she needs a plan and therapy, not just willpower.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"That makes sense of it."},{"t":"3:33","band":"Outlook","w":"P","s":"Can people fully recover?"},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Many do. About half recover fully, and many more improve a lot, though it takes time, often a year or more.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Starting treatment early, as Chloe has, and having family support both make recovery more likely.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"That's more hopeful than I thought."},{"t":"3:55","band":"How to help","w":"P","s":"What can I actually do? Every meal ends in tears."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Many parents feel just this. Keep mealtimes regular and calm, eat together, and talk about other things rather than food or weight.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Try to see the illness as separate from Chloe. When she argues about food, that's often the anorexia talking.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"That helps. I've been taking it personally."},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"Please look after yourself too. Beat, the eating disorder charity, has a helpline and advice for parents. We run a carers' group, and you can ask the council for a Carer's assessment.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"I'd like the group."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"I'll add you. And here's a leaflet that covers everything we've said today.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:43","band":"Safety net","w":"D","s":"If she faints, has chest pain or palpitations, gets breathless, or struggles to stand up or climb stairs, ring us the same day, or 111 or 999 out of hours.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Fainting, chest pain, struggling to stand. Okay."},{"t":"5:00","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the main points in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"It's serious but treatable. Weekly checks. Meal plan and therapy, no tablet. About half fully recover. I keep meals calm and join the group."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Anything else you'd like to ask me?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:19","w":"P","s":"No. I feel I can breathe again."},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Mrs Bennett. You're already helping her. We'll see you both at the next review.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Chloe's consent and asks what the mother knows and fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges fear and guilt with empathic statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains anorexia in plain words and why Chloe meets the diagnosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains causes as a mix of causes and lifts the mother's guilt","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains physical risks and weekly monitoring, linking to Chloe's results","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains treatment: dietitian, therapy, no medicine for anorexia, hospital only if needed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a balanced, hopeful outlook","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives practical advice on supporting Chloe at mealtimes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers carer support and written information","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives clear red flags that need urgent help","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding after explanations and with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Invites final questions and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains anorexia, causes, risks, treatment and outlook in plain chunks, addresses guilt, gives a clear role at home with carer support and red flags.","borderline":"Accurate but jargon-heavy or rushed, or misses the guilt cue or carer support.","fail":"False reassurance, blames the family, suggests medication cures it, or overwhelms her with frightening facts."}},"c-s-60":{"setup":"CMHT clinic. Leah Morgan, 25, has anorexia nervosa, depression and EUPD, and is being considered for discharge. Take a brief history of the favourable and poor prognostic signs in her anorexia, and explore her personality traits.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Morgan, 25, female, patient","open":"Okay. I'm not sure where to start, really.","manner":"Quiet, short answers, arms folded at first. Opens up with open questions, reflections and patience. Briefly guarded about her parents. Hopeful and warm by the end.","free":["Eating became difficult at about 15","Finished a day programme","Healthy weight for four years","Works full time"],"ifasked":["Bullied about her weight at school","Restriction, slow eating, daily running, mirror checking; never binged, vomited or used laxatives or pills","About three years before help; collapsed at college at 18; lowest BMI 14","Two depressions at 17 and 21, each with a paracetamol overdose and admission; cut herself at times of intense emotion or fear of being left; stopped six years ago","Quiet, perfectionist, intense feelings, panics about people leaving","Parents emotionally distant; no abuse","Two close friends, monthly psychologist, supervisor at work, good A-levels; no current low mood, self-harm or suicidal thoughts; minimal alcohol, no drugs"],"worries":["Why do you need to know about my parents?","Are you going to discharge me? What if it comes back?"],"rules":["Gives one-line answers to closed questions; more to open questions and reflections","Mentions bullying and the three-year delay only if asked","Becomes guarded if pushed about parents; relaxes if told she can share what feels okay"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Leah? I'm Dr Novak, one of the doctors in the team. I'd like to understand your journey with food, and what has helped you. Some of this may be difficult to talk about. Take your time, and we can pause whenever you like.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'm not sure where to start, really."},{"t":"0:21","band":"How it began","w":"D","s":"That's fine; there's no right place. Could you take me back to the time eating first became hard?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"In my teens. About fifteen."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"What was happening in your life at that point?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"School. I was bigger then."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"It sounds as if your weight got noticed. How did other people treat you?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Kids called me names. Every day, pretty much."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful at that age. What did you start doing about food?","m":[12,2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Ate less and less."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Behaviours","w":"D","s":"Tell me more about what that looked like.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Tiny portions. Cutting things up small, eating really slowly. Running every day. Checking the mirror all the time."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Some people also binge, make themselves sick, or use laxatives or diet pills. Did any of that happen for you?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"No. Never any of that."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"How long did it go on before anyone knew, or you got help?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"About three years. I collapsed at college at eighteen. Lowest my BMI got was fourteen."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Three years is a long time to carry that alone. It's very common to hide it; the illness pushes people to. You got help in the end. What treatment did you have?","m":[12,4]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"A day programme. Six months. I finished it."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"And how have food, weight and exercise been since?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Normal weight for four years now. I eat properly. I walk, run a bit, but not like before."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"That's a real achievement. Any alcohol or drugs, now or in the past?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"A couple of drinks with friends. Never drugs."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Mood and self-harm","w":"D","s":"Your notes mention two overdoses. What was happening for you each time?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"I got really depressed. At seventeen and at twenty-one. Took paracetamol both times. Ended up on a ward."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you ever hurt yourself in other ways?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"(pause) I used to cut. When feelings got too big, or when I thought people would leave me."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"It sounds as if the feelings were overwhelming, and cutting was how you got through them. When did it stop?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"When I started the day programme. Not for six years."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"How's your mood now? Any thoughts of self-harm, or of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Mood's okay. No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:01","band":"Personality","w":"D","s":"I'm glad. How would you describe yourself as a person?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Quiet. Always the quiet one. I like things done properly. A perfectionist, my friends say."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"And how do your feelings work? Do they come on strongly?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Very. Something small can feel huge."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"You mentioned worrying people would leave. How do you react when that fear comes?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I panic. I used to cut, or push them away first. Now I talk to my psychologist."},{"t":"3:32","band":"Family and early life","w":"D","s":"That's a big change. Can I ask about growing up? How are things with your parents?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Why do you need to know about them?"},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Family relationships can affect how people recover. Only tell me what feels okay.","m":[12,6]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"They were never really there emotionally. Dad worked away. Mum was busy. We don't talk much now."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds lonely. Has anyone ever hurt you, physically, sexually or emotionally?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. Just the bullying."},{"t":"4:04","band":"Strengths and support","w":"D","s":"Who is there for you now?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"My two best friends; I live with one. My psychologist, monthly. She's really good."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"What about work, and things you're proud of?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"Full time at a garden centre, three years. I'm a supervisor now. I got good A-levels too."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Other screens","w":"D","s":"Briefly, have you ever had times of feeling unusually high or full of energy, or hearing things others can't?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"4:43","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it. You had a hard road: bullying, three years of restricting before help, two depressions with overdoses, and cutting. But you never binged or purged, you finished treatment, and you've stayed at a healthy weight for four years, with work, friends and a therapist you trust. Is that fair?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Are you going to discharge me? What if it comes back?"},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"That's an understandable worry. Nothing will be decided without you. I'll talk to my consultant, and any plan would include early warning signs and how to get help back quickly.","m":[12,11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Okay. That would help."},{"t":"5:24","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If things change before then, if you start cutting back on food or feel very low, contact your psychologist, your GP or 111 option 2. Is there anything you'd like to ask?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That was easier than I expected."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open, Leah. You've come a long way.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose and invites her to go at her pace","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gets onset, early behaviours and the trigger in time order","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies poor signs: long untreated illness, low lowest BMI, depression, overdoses, self-harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies favourable signs: no binge or purge, completed treatment, sustained recovery, no substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores personality: perfectionism, introversion, fear of abandonment, emotional intensity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores family relationships and bullying, and screens for abuse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current mood, anxiety, self-harm and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies current supports and strengths: friends, therapist, work, achievements, outlook","d":"Content"},{"c":"Briefly screens for other conditions such as mania or psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises the balance of poor and favourable signs back to her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses her worry about discharge, gives a safety net and closes","d":"Close"},{"c":"Uses open questions, reflections and empathy to engage a quiet patient","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Engages her gently, maps the illness course, sorts poor and favourable signs including absent behaviours, explores personality and family, checks current risk and summarises.","borderline":"Covers the history but misses absent behaviours, personality traits or current risk, or uses mostly closed questions.","fail":"Treats her as acutely unwell, takes a generic eating disorder history, or ignores her strengths and current risk."}},"c-s-61":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, community mental health clinic. Ms Chloe Marsh, 29, has bipolar disorder and has been well on lithium for five years. She wants to try for a baby. Discuss the risks, her options and a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Chloe Marsh, 29, female, patient","open":"Thanks for seeing me. I've read lithium damages babies' hearts, and I'm panicking. Do I have to come off it?","manner":"Organised and anxious; has done a lot of reading. Becomes tearful about past admissions. Calms when given honest numbers and a real say in the choice.","free":["Well on lithium 800 mg at night for five years","She and her partner Tom want to start trying soon","Read online that lithium causes a heart defect","Wants to stay well above all"],"ifasked":["Three manic relapses aged 19 to 24, on antipsychotics she took properly","Two admissions, one under section","Short depressive spell last year, managed by the team","Never stopped lithium since starting","On the progestogen-only pill; period last week","Not taking folic acid; takes ibuprofen for period pains","Mother 'had a breakdown' after her brother was born","Hoped to breastfeed"],"worries":["Can lithium harm the baby?","Will it affect getting pregnant?","Can she breastfeed?","What are the alternatives if she stops?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about being sectioned; settles if validated","If told lithium is 'completely safe', challenges the doctor with what she read","Wants to stay on lithium once she hears her own relapse risk","Asks for a leaflet to show Tom"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explores what she has read and her main fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes relapse history, past medicines and response to lithium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks pregnancy status, contraception, other medicines, folic acid and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the small rise in heart defects honestly, including Ebstein's rarity and a heart scan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the high risk of relapse and postpartum psychosis after birth","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Sets out continuing, switching and slow tapering, each with its main risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers fertility and breastfeeding accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains level monitoring in pregnancy and after birth, and toxicity signs","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises folic acid and never stopping lithium suddenly if pregnant","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and confirms the decision is hers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with perinatal referral, leaflet, crisis contacts and review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Marsh, I'm Dr Mensah, one of the psychiatry doctors. I hear you're thinking about a baby. What's on your mind?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me. I've read lithium damages babies' hearts, and I'm panicking. Do I have to come off it?"},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening to read, and I'm glad you came before changing anything. What exactly did you find?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Something called Ebstein's. It said lithium makes it far more likely."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"I'll come back to that with real numbers. First I need to understand your illness, because your own risk matters just as much.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","band":"Her relapse risk","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the times you were unwell before lithium.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Three times between 19 and 24. I was high, not sleeping, spending everything. Two admissions. One time I was sectioned."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Were you taking your medicines then?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Every day. They tried three different ones. It still happened."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"And since lithium?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Five years well. A short low patch last year, that's all. I can't go back to hospital. Not with a baby."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Those admissions sound really hard, and it makes complete sense you'd want to protect yourself and a baby from that. That's why planning now, while you're well, is so worthwhile.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:39","band":"Health now","w":"D","s":"Could you be pregnant at the moment, and what contraception are you using?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"No, period last week. I'm on the mini pill."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Any other medicines, including painkillers, and are you taking folic acid?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Ibuprofen for period pains. No folic acid."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Mum had some sort of breakdown after my brother. Nobody talks about it."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"That's useful to know, and we'll find out more if you're happy to ask her.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","band":"Risk to the baby","w":"D","s":"Now the heart. Lithium early in pregnancy does slightly raise the chance of a heart problem, more so at higher levels. That's real and I won't pretend otherwise.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"So the website was right."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"Partly. Ebstein's anomaly is rare, and newer large studies show most babies exposed have perfectly normal hearts. A detailed heart scan in mid-pregnancy would check.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Will it stop me getting pregnant?"},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"No, lithium isn't known to affect fertility.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:56","band":"Her own risk","w":"D","s":"The biggest risk for you is the first weeks after birth. With bipolar disorder, the chance of becoming seriously unwell then is high, including postpartum psychosis, and stopping medicine pushes that up a lot.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Postpartum psychosis. Is that what Mum had?"},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"It may have been, which would raise your risk too. It can come on within days of birth, and it's very treatable when spotted early.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:27","band":"The options","w":"D","s":"So there are three routes. One: stay on lithium with close monitoring. Two: switch to an antipsychotic before trying, but those didn't keep you well before.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"And three?"},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Taper lithium slowly over at least four weeks, try for the baby off it, and restart straight after birth. The cost is a high chance of relapse. Stopping suddenly is the one thing never to do.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Hearing that, staying on it feels safest. But can I breastfeed?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"I know that matters. Lithium passes into breast milk, and levels can build up in a baby, so we usually advise against breastfeeding on it. Bottle-feeding also lets Tom do a night feed so you sleep.","m":[8,2]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"That's sad, but I'd rather be well."},{"t":"4:22","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"If you stay on lithium, your body clears it faster in pregnancy, so levels fall. We'd check blood levels every four weeks, weekly from 36 weeks, and within a day of birth, because levels jump back up after delivery.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"What happens if it goes too high?"},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Watch for vomiting, diarrhoea, a coarse shake, unsteadiness or confusion. If that happens, skip the dose and go to A&E for a level. And swap ibuprofen for paracetamol: ibuprofen pushes lithium up.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","band":"The plan","w":"D","s":"Start folic acid 400 micrograms today. And if your test is positive, keep taking lithium and ring us that day.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"Not stop it. Got it."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"To check I've explained it well, can you tell me what you're taking away?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Small heart risk, scan to check. High risk of getting ill after the birth. Stay on lithium, blood tests, no breastfeeding, folic acid now, ring you if I'm pregnant."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Spot on. It's your decision, and you can change your mind after talking with Tom.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Can I have something to show him?"},{"t":"5:40","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Yes, here's the bumps leaflet on lithium. I'll refer you to the perinatal team for advice before you conceive, and they'll write a full plan for the birth.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"And if I start getting high again?"},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"Ring the team in hours; out of hours, NHS 111 and choose the mental health option, or 999 if you feel unsafe. Let's book you back in six weeks with Tom.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I came in thinking I had to choose. I feel much calmer."},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. You've done exactly the right thing planning ahead. I'll copy my letter to you and your GP.","m":[2,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her fear, establishes her high relapse risk, gives honest heart and postpartum risks, offers three options and agrees a written plan with monitoring.","borderline":"Accurate facts but no options offered, or monitoring and breastfeeding left vague.","fail":"Tells her to stop lithium, gives false reassurance, or omits the postpartum relapse risk."}},"c-s-62":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, perinatal clinic. Mrs Natalie Brooks, 33, has bipolar I disorder and is well on sodium valproate and sertraline. Her history is in the notes. She wants to start a family. Explain the risks and plan her medicines.","role":{"who":"Mrs Natalie Brooks, 33, female, patient","open":"Hi. I know valproate's a problem if I get pregnant. I need to know if I can actually have a baby.","manner":"Direct and slightly defensive; a hairdresser who speaks plainly. Becomes upset about social services and olanzapine weight. Warms when answered honestly.","free":["Married last summer; she and her husband Sam want a baby","Knows valproate is risky but not the detail","Well for three years","Has the implant and signs a form every year"],"ifasked":["Not pregnant; implant still in","Has not used cannabis or cocaine for three years","Olanzapine worked but she gained two stone","Lithium caused incontinence and aripiprazole did nothing","Not taking folic acid","Sam's family live nearby and help"],"worries":["Can she start a family with her illness?","Would social services take the baby?","What are the risks of valproate and sertraline?","What can she take instead, and when is she most at risk?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asking about social services; settles if reassured honestly","Initially refuses olanzapine because of weight; agrees if weight support is offered","Wants to remove the implant soon; accepts the reason to wait if explained","Asks what happens to the baby after birth"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, says the history is known, and asks her views and worries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fears before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reassures that her diagnosis does not bar a family and explains social services' supportive role","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives valproate's birth defect and developmental risks as numbers","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the Pregnancy Prevention Programme and keeping contraception until the switch is complete","d":"Content"},{"c":"Advises never stopping valproate suddenly and ringing the team if pregnant","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains postpartum relapse and psychosis risk, and timing of highest risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains sertraline can continue and what the baby needs after birth","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Proposes olanzapine with its downsides, and explains why other options suit her less","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes a gradual cross-taper with folic acid, weight monitoring and review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and seeks her agreement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with summary, leaflets, crisis contacts and next review","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Brooks, I'm Dr Nwosu, one of the perinatal psychiatrists. I've read your history, so I won't make you repeat it. What are your thoughts about a pregnancy?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Hi. I know valproate's a problem if I get pregnant. I need to know if I can actually have a baby."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's a really important question. What worries you most?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"That with my history, people will say I shouldn't. And that social services would take the baby off me."},{"t":"0:32","band":"Her fears","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a heavy thing to carry, and I'm glad you said it out loud. Wanting a family is completely reasonable. Your diagnosis doesn't stop you being a mum.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"But I've been sectioned three times."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"You have, and you've also been well for three years and drug-free, which is a real achievement. Social services wouldn't be involved just because you have bipolar disorder. If you ever became unwell, their role would be to support you, not to remove your baby.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. I've been dreading asking that."},{"t":"1:08","band":"Valproate","w":"D","s":"Now valproate. Of all the mood stabilisers, it's the one we worry about most in pregnancy. Can I give you the numbers?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:18","w":"D","s":"About 11 in every 100 babies exposed have a major birth defect, like spina bifida or a heart problem. Normally it's 2 or 3 in 100. And 30 to 40 in 100 children have learning, behaviour or developmental problems, such as autism.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"That's so much worse than I thought."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"It's a lot to hear. That's exactly why you have the implant and the yearly form: so a pregnancy never happens on valproate by accident.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"So should I stop it now and get the implant out?"},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd want to get going. But stopping suddenly could bring on mania, and you'd be unprotected. We switch step by step, and the implant comes out last, once you've been settled on the new medicine for a few months.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"And if I got pregnant by accident before then?"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Keep taking valproate, don't stop it suddenly, and ring us that day. We'd see you urgently and plan the safest change.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:21","band":"Her risk","w":"D","s":"Can I talk about your own risk? Pregnancy itself isn't usually the riskiest time, though stress can play a part. The danger is the first weeks after birth.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Why then?"},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Hormones, broken sleep and the upheaval. With bipolar I and past psychosis, there's a high chance of becoming unwell, including postpartum psychosis, which can start within days. Staying on an effective medicine cuts that risk a lot.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"And my sertraline?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"That can carry on. It's one of the best-studied antidepressants in pregnancy and fine for breastfeeding. Some babies are jittery or sleepy for a few days, so the midwives keep an eye on the baby after birth.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Would the baby need to stay in hospital?"},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Usually just the normal postnatal stay with extra checks on feeding and settling. Once off valproate, the main worries go.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","band":"Alternatives","w":"D","s":"So what instead? Lithium and aripiprazole didn't suit you, and lamotrigine on its own won't prevent mania. Olanzapine kept you well before and is often used in pregnancy.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not going back on olanzapine. I put on two stone."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"That weight gain was really upsetting, and it's fair to be wary. This time we'd get the dietitian in before you start, check your weight weekly, and use the lowest dose that keeps you well. Could we look at it on those terms?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"If you actually help with the weight, maybe."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"We will. Risperidone helped in your first admission and is a back-up, though it can raise prolactin, which may affect periods and fertility.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:09","band":"The switch","w":"D","s":"Here's how it would go. Olanzapine 5 milligrams at night, up to 10 after a week. Once you're settled, valproate comes down by 250 milligrams every two weeks, so about two months.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"That's slow."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"Slow is what keeps you well. While you're still on valproate, take folic acid 5 milligrams a day. We'll check weight, blood sugar and cholesterol, and see you every two weeks.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"And when can the implant come out?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Once you've been well on olanzapine alone for three to six months. Then you can start trying, and the perinatal team plans the birth with you.","m":[5,10]},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"That's a lot. To make sure I've been clear, can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"Valproate's dangerous for babies. Start olanzapine, come off valproate slowly, take folic acid, implant stays till I'm settled, never stop anything suddenly."},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. How do you feel about it?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"Nervous. I've been well three years. What if changing breaks it?"},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"That's a completely understandable fear. We go slowly and watch closely. If early signs appear, less sleep, spending, racing thoughts, we act fast. Is it okay if Sam knows those signs too?","m":[2,7,11]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Yes. He'd want to know."},{"t":"5:39","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: you can have a family; valproate gets replaced before you try; olanzapine first, valproate down slowly; folic acid now; implant stays for now.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Here are leaflets on valproate, olanzapine and sertraline in pregnancy. If you feel high or very low, ring us, or NHS 111 and the mental health option out of hours. I'll see you in two weeks, with Sam if he can come.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I came in expecting to be told no."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"I'm glad we could plan it together. I'll write to your GP and copy you in.","m":[12]}],"global":{"pass":"Answers parenthood and social services fears, gives valproate risks in numbers, explains the safety rules, and agrees a stepped switch with contraception kept until she is stable.","borderline":"Correct risks but a vague switch plan, or no weight support for olanzapine.","fail":"Stops valproate or the implant at once, suggests drugs that failed before, or leaves her social services fear unanswered."}},"c-s-63":{"setup":"Monday lunchtime, perinatal team office. Ms Ruth Adebayo, health visitor, rings about Mrs Laura Bennett, 32, five months postnatal, not improving on sertraline 200 mg after CBT. Explore what is keeping her unwell, then recommend next steps.","role":{"who":"Ms Ruth Adebayo, 45, female, health visitor","open":"Hi, it's Ruth, Laura Bennett's health visitor. She's on the top dose, she's done her CBT, and she's still really low. What's stopping her getting better?","manner":"Experienced, caring and a little frustrated on Laura's behalf; worries she has missed something. Becomes engaged and practical once given specific actions.","free":["Laura is 32; baby Isla is five months old","Sertraline 200 mg for eight weeks; CBT finished last month","Still low, tearful and flat; EPDS 19 last week","Laura has consented to Ruth talking to the team"],"ifasked":["Takes sertraline daily; husband Mark sees her take it","No alcohol or drugs","Mark is supportive; her mother lives nearby; house and money are fine","Bonding is good; Isla is thriving and on her growth line","Laura calls herself a rubbish mum and a useless wife","Barely leaves the house; has stopped baby groups and seeing friends","Stopped breastfeeding at two months when overwhelmed and still cries about it","Last week Laura denied thoughts of self-harm or harming Isla; nothing odd in her thinking"],"worries":["Should we just give the sertraline more time?","What can be done with medicine and talking therapy?","What if the next step doesn't work either?","What can she do herself on her visits?"],"rules":["Becomes upset that she may have missed something; settles if reassured","Only mentions the breastfeeding regret if asked about feeding","Asks for specifics if the doctor gives vague advice","Wants to know exactly when to ring back"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, thanks her, and confirms the patient consented to sharing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges the health visitor's concern and validates her role","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises the course, treatments, doses and duration so far","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks adherence, alcohol and drugs, relationships, support, housing and finances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores self-beliefs, daily activity and feelings about stopping breastfeeding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about risk to self and baby, the baby's wellbeing and psychotic features","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names the perpetuating factors and explains why they maintain depression","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends switching antidepressant with named options, cross-taper and physical checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Recommends named psychological interventions linked to her beliefs","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Recommends social interventions inside and outside the team, including carers' support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a plan B including augmentation, diagnostic review, ECT or rTMS and mother and baby unit","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees escalation triggers, checks understanding and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ruth, I'm Dr Cohen, one of the perinatal psychiatrists. Thanks for ringing about Laura. Has she agreed to us talking?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yes, she's happy for me to call. She's on the top dose, she's done her CBT, and she's still really low. What's stopping her getting better?"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"It's a really good question, and you're right to push it. Can you give me the picture now compared with when she was referred?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Honestly, not much different. Tearful, flat, her EPDS was 19 last week."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"So sertraline started by the GP, up to 200 milligrams, eight weeks at that dose, and a full course of CBT with our team. Is that right?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"0:47","band":"Rule things out","w":"D","s":"Is she taking it every day, as far as you know?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Yes, Mark sees her take it."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"No, nothing."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"And how are things at home: Mark, family, money, housing?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"All solid. Mark's brilliant, her mum's round the corner, no money worries."},{"t":"1:11","band":"What keeps it going","w":"D","s":"That's a lot in her favour. How does she talk about herself as a mother and a partner?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"She says she's a rubbish mum and a useless wife. But she's lovely with Isla. It breaks my heart."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"That sounds hard to watch, especially when you can see how well she's doing. What does a normal day look like for her now?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"She barely leaves the house. She's stopped the baby groups and doesn't see friends."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"And feeding: how did that go?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"She stopped breastfeeding at two months, she was exhausted. She still cries about it, actually."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. It can leave a lot of guilt, so it's worth exploring with her.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"1:58","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How is Isla doing, and how is Laura with her?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Isla's thriving, right on her line. Laura's gentle and attentive with her."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Has Laura mentioned thoughts of harming herself or Isla, or anything unusual in her thinking?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"I asked last week. She said no to all of it. Have I missed something, though? I've known her since the birth."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"You haven't missed anything, you've spotted it. Ringing us now is exactly right, and what you've told me is shaping the plan.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:32","band":"Making sense of it","w":"D","s":"Here's what I think. The things that often keep depression going, money, relationships, substances, aren't there. What stands out is how harshly she judges herself and how little she's doing.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"So the thoughts are part of it?"},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Yes. If you believe you're failing, you pull back, then you have fewer good moments to prove yourself wrong, so the belief grows. The feeding guilt may feed it too.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Shouldn't we just give the sertraline longer?"},{"t":"3:03","band":"Biological","w":"D","s":"I understand that instinct. Eight weeks at the top dose with little change is a fair trial, so I'd switch. As she's had only one antidepressant, changing is better than adding a second drug.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"To what?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"I'll see her this week to decide together. Options include mirtazapine, which helps sleep, or venlafaxine. We'd bring sertraline down while the new one goes up, so she doesn't dip.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"I'd also ask the GP for her thyroid, blood count and iron. Thyroid problems after birth can keep mood low.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"And therapy? The CBT didn't do much."},{"t":"3:43","band":"Psychological","w":"D","s":"I'll ask our psychologists to see her for interpersonal therapy, which looks at the big change in her role, or parent-infant work, where seeing herself with Isla on video can challenge that 'rubbish mum' belief.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"She'd like that, I think."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Social","w":"D","s":"Our occupational therapist and nursery nurse can help her get out with Isla, one small plan a day. There are perinatal peer groups through charities like PANDAS, and Home-Start volunteers.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"Mark could have a carers' assessment, and if the family want extra help, Early Help is an option. We'd also offer Mark and her mum a session on postnatal depression.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"What if the new tablet doesn't work either?"},{"t":"4:29","band":"Plan B","w":"D","s":"Then we'd add a second medicine, like quetiapine or lithium, and rethink the diagnosis, including whether this could be bipolar depression. ECT or magnetic stimulation are options for severe cases, and a mother and baby unit if she needs admission.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"That's reassuring. What can I do on my visits?"},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"A lot. Tell her specifically what she does well with Isla, encourage one outing a day, and keep asking about risk.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"4:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Please ring our duty worker the same day if she mentions harming herself or Isla, stops caring for herself or the baby, or says anything unusual.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Will do."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Just so we're aligned, could you tell me what you'll pass on to Laura?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"You'll see her this week about a new tablet, bloods via the GP, psychology for a different therapy, OT and a group. And I ring the same day if I'm worried."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write to the GP and copy you in. Thank you, Ruth; your observations really helped.","m":[12,2]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel much better knowing there's a plan."}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms consent, summarises the course, rules out the usual causes, names her beliefs and inactivity as drivers, and gives a specific switch, therapy, social plan, plan B and escalation route.","borderline":"Right medication advice but vague psychological and social options, or no plan B.","fail":"Advises waiting longer on sertraline, gives generic advice, or never asks about risk to mother and baby."}},"c-s-64":{"setup":"Friday morning, perinatal clinic. Ms Jade Harper, 35, has bipolar I disorder and is 15 weeks pregnant, well on quetiapine XL 400 mg. She wants her medicine reviewed because of past gestational diabetes. Discuss the risks and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Jade Harper, 35, female, patient","open":"Hi. I need to know if the quetiapine is going to give me diabetes again. Should I be changing it?","manner":"Worried, practical and a little tearful; wants a straight answer. Upset about the caesarean and about being alone. Settles once she has a clear recommendation and a plan.","free":["15 weeks pregnant; daughter Lily is 3","Well on quetiapine XL 400 mg for four years, including the last pregnancy","Had gestational diabetes last time","Wants to avoid diabetes this time"],"ifasked":["Needed metformin last time; sugars were often high","Had a caesarean at 38 weeks, which she found upsetting","Nausea again now; eating little and often; taking quetiapine every night","No blood sugar test yet this pregnancy","Recently separated; parents live two hours away","Mood fine, sleeping well, no early warning signs","Wants to breastfeed"],"worries":["What are quetiapine's risks in pregnancy?","Could it cause diabetes again?","What are the alternatives and their risks?","How likely is she to become unwell after the birth, and what would the doctor recommend?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about the caesarean; settles if validated","Asks directly 'What would you do?' and is unhappy with a non-answer","Mentions being alone only if asked about support","Asks whether she could come off everything until the birth"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and explores her first pregnancy and current worries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress about the birth and her situation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current mood, adherence with nausea, glucose testing so far and support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains quetiapine is not linked with clear birth defect rise, and newborn adaptation","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains breastfeeding on quetiapine with infant monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the diabetes evidence is mixed and previous diabetes is the main risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Compares aripiprazole and olanzapine, and excludes lamotrigine and valproate, with reasons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains high postpartum relapse and psychosis risk and the value of prevention","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a clear recommendation and seeks her view","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees early OGTT, repeat at 24 to 28 weeks, dietitian and activity advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans to share with obstetrics and addresses support after the birth","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Ms Harper, I'm Dr Siddiqui, one of the perinatal psychiatry doctors. Congratulations on the pregnancy. What would you like to get from today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hi. I need to know if the quetiapine is going to give me diabetes again. Should I be changing it?"},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"That's a really fair question. Can you tell me how your first pregnancy went?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"My mood was fine. But I got diabetes, my sugars were all over the place, I needed metformin, and then I had a caesarean at 38 weeks. I'd wanted a normal birth."},{"t":"0:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a really hard experience, and it's clearly still with you. I'm sorry it went that way.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"I just don't want it all again."},{"t":"0:46","band":"Now","w":"D","s":"Understandably. How are you now: mood, sleep, any early warning signs?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Mood's good. Sleeping fine. Just the sickness again."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"Are you managing to keep the quetiapine down every night?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Yes. I eat little bits and take it before bed."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Good. Has anyone checked your blood sugar yet this pregnancy?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"No, nothing yet."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Okay, we'll sort that. Who's around to support you and Lily?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Honestly, no one nearby. My partner left two months ago. Mum and Dad are two hours away."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to manage while pregnant, with a toddler too. Thank you for telling me; it changes how we plan.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:31","band":"Quetiapine","w":"D","s":"Let me go through quetiapine. It isn't linked with a clear rise in birth defects. Late in pregnancy, some babies are a bit unsettled or sleepy for a few days, so the team watches the baby after birth.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Lily was fine, actually."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"That's reassuring. And if you want to breastfeed, only small amounts get into milk. We'd check the baby isn't too sleepy, and feeding before your night dose helps.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"I'd like to try this time. But the diabetes?"},{"t":"2:05","band":"Diabetes","w":"D","s":"Honestly, the evidence is mixed. Quetiapine can raise appetite and weight, and that may add a little risk. But the biggest factor is that you had it before: it comes back in about 1 in 3 women or more, whatever they take.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"So changing tablets wouldn't stop it."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"It might shift the risk slightly, not remove it. What really helps is testing early and acting quickly. Normally that means a glucose tolerance test soon after booking, then again at 24 to 28 weeks if the first is clear.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"What are the other options, though?"},{"t":"2:43","band":"Alternatives","w":"D","s":"Aripiprazole affects weight less, which helps with diabetes. But it's untested for you, and it can reduce milk supply. Olanzapine is widely used in pregnancy but has similar weight effects.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"What about lamotrigine? My friend's on that."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"It's fine in pregnancy, but it mainly prevents low moods, not highs, so it wouldn't protect you from mania or illness after birth. And valproate isn't used in pregnancy at all.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Could I come off everything until the baby's born?"},{"t":"3:17","band":"Her risk","w":"D","s":"I can see why that's tempting. But with bipolar I, the weeks after birth carry a high chance of becoming unwell, including postpartum psychosis, which can start within days.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"How high?"},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"Without preventive medicine, more than half of women in your position relapse. Staying on an effective medicine roughly halves that, and illness tends to be milder and caught earlier.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"I can't be ill with a newborn and Lily on my own."},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"No, and you shouldn't have to face that. Keeping you well protects you, the baby and Lily.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"So what would you do if you were me?"},{"t":"3:58","band":"Recommendation","w":"D","s":"I'll give you a straight answer. Quetiapine has kept you well for four years and through a pregnancy. I'd stay on it and tackle the diabetes risk head-on. How does that sit with you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"That's what I hoped you'd say, actually. What do I do about the sugar?"},{"t":"4:15","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'll write to the obstetric team today for an early glucose test in the next fortnight, and the antenatal diabetes clinic and dietitian. Regular meals, fewer sugary foods, and about 150 minutes of gentle activity a week, like walking.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"And if I get it anyway?"},{"t":"4:34","w":"D","s":"Then it's caught early and treated with diet, metformin or insulin, like before. And I'll ask about a birth choices appointment so your wishes are part of the plan this time.","m":[10,11,2]},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"For after the birth, could your parents come and stay for the first few weeks, so you can sleep and have help with Lily?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"Mum's offered. I'll take her up on it."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Great. We'll write a full care plan by 32 weeks with the midwife, obstetrician, GP and health visitor, including a review soon after birth.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:07","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan in your words, so I know I've been clear?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Stay on quetiapine. Early sugar test and again later. Dietitian, walking. Mum stays after the birth. Plan by 32 weeks."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you notice poor sleep, racing thoughts or feeling unlike yourself, ring us, or NHS 111 and the mental health option out of hours. I'll see you in four weeks, and here's a leaflet on quetiapine in pregnancy.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel like I've got a proper plan now."}],"global":{"pass":"Hears the first pregnancy story, explains quetiapine and the uncertain diabetes link, compares alternatives, explains postpartum risk, recommends continuing and books early testing through obstetrics.","borderline":"Accurate risks but no clear recommendation, or the early glucose test and obstetric letter are missed.","fail":"Switches or stops quetiapine without reason, suggests lamotrigine or valproate, or ignores the diabetes screening."}},"c-s-65":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, community mental health clinic. Mrs Rachel Evans, 35, has a six-week-old son. Her GP says her long-standing obsessions have worsened since the birth. Ask about her obsessions and compulsions and their impact.","role":{"who":"Mrs Rachel Evans, 35, female, patient","open":"Hi. I know it sounds mad. I wash his bottles over and over and I can't stop.","manner":"Embarrassed and insightful; keen to be understood. Becomes tearful and frightened when disclosing the harm image. Relaxes when normalised.","free":["Worse since her baby was born","Washes and re-sterilises the bottles repeatedly","Knows it is irrational but cannot stop","Has had OCD before"],"ifasked":["Son Leo, six weeks old","OCD since 29; CBT helped; clomipramine for some years, switched to sertraline 3 years ago for sedation","Washes each bottle exactly six times; panics if she tries to stop","For three weeks, checks locks, switches and the cooker six times","Will not let her husband Dan prepare bottles; avoids visitors","Image of dropping Leo on the stairs; horrified; never wants to act","Rituals take about three hours a day; one feed delayed about 20 minutes","Not breastfeeding because she thought sertraline made it unsafe"],"worries":["Am I going mad?","Am I a danger to Leo?","Will social services take him?","Is it my fault for having a baby with OCD?"],"rules":["Only discloses the harm image if asked gently after normalising","Becomes tearful and asks about social services after disclosure; settles if reassured","Answers mood and psychosis questions honestly: anxious but not low; no psychotic symptoms","Shuts down if the doctor uses jargon or looks alarmed"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, normalises, and invites her story with open questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and shame without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, progression and link to the birth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores past OCD, treatment history and current medicine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores obsession content and form: own thoughts, senseless, resisted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about intrusive harm thoughts and checks intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Elicits washing, checking and counting rituals, and the anxiety relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores avoidance and time spent on rituals","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses impact on the baby's care, self-care, sleep and family life","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, suicidal thoughts, psychosis, anxiety and alcohol","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises the findings back in plain language and checks accuracy","d":"Close"},{"c":"Explains next steps and safety net without jargon","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello Mrs Evans, I'm Dr Nowak, one of the psychiatry doctors. Your GP asked me to see you about some worries. In your words, what's been happening?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Hi. I know it sounds mad. I wash his bottles over and over and I can't stop."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"It doesn't sound mad at all; it sounds exhausting. Lots of new mums get worries like these. Who's 'he'?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"My son, Leo. He's six weeks."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Onset and history","w":"D","s":"Congratulations. When did the worries start, and how have they gone since?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Right after he was born. Every week it gets a bit worse."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Have you had worries like this before Leo?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I've had OCD since I was 29. CBT helped a lot. I was on clomipramine, but it made me so sleepy, so I've been on sertraline 100 for three years."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"Thank you. And what do you think has set it off now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Having him. He's so tiny. If he got ill, it'd be my fault."},{"t":"1:01","band":"The obsessions","w":"D","s":"That's a huge sense of responsibility. Talk me through what goes through your mind before you wash a bottle again.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"That there's still germs, and they'll give him an infection. It just pops in and won't go."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Do those thoughts feel like your own, or like they come from somewhere else?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"They're mine. I know they're silly. That's the worst bit."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"What happens if you try to push the thought away and not wash it again?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"I panic. My heart races until I give in and wash it. Then I feel better, for a bit."},{"t":"1:36","band":"Harm thoughts","w":"D","s":"Can I ask something I ask all new mums? Some get sudden, horrible thoughts or pictures of the baby coming to harm. Has anything like that happened to you?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Sometimes I see myself dropping him down the stairs. Am I a danger to him?"},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that; it takes real courage. Thoughts like that are common, and the fact they horrify you is typical. Have you ever wanted to act on it?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Never. I only carry him downstairs when Dan's home now. Are you going to tell social services?"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightening that is. No. Unwanted thoughts like these aren't a reason to involve them. My job is to help you feel better.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I've been so scared to say it."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you did. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:30","band":"Compulsions and avoidance","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the washing. How many times, and what happens if it doesn't feel right?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Six times each. If I lose count, I start again. Then I sterilise them again."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Is there anything else you have to check, count or repeat?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"For the last three weeks, the locks, light switches and cooker. Six times each, before bed."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Do you wash Leo or change him more than you think you need to, or wash yourself a lot?","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"My hands, loads. They're raw. Not him, he just has his normal bath."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid because of the worries?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"I don't let Dan make bottles. I put off visitors. I haven't been to baby group."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Roughly how much of your day does it all take?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"About three hours, I think."},{"t":"3:22","band":"Impact and risk","w":"D","s":"That's a lot on top of a newborn. Has it ever delayed a feed or got in the way of looking after Leo?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Once. He was screaming and I was still washing. Maybe twenty minutes late. I felt awful."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting. How is Leo doing, his weight and feeding?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"The health visitor says he's gaining well. I'm bottle-feeding because I was told I couldn't breastfeed on sertraline."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"You made the best choice with what you were told. In fact sertraline is one we prefer in breastfeeding, worth knowing for the future. How are you sleeping and eating?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Four or five hours with feeds and checking. I forget to eat."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"And how is it affecting you and Dan, and seeing family or friends?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"Dan's patient but frustrated. I've not seen my friends since he was born."},{"t":"4:17","band":"Other illness","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Are you still able to enjoy things, and enjoy Leo?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"I'm anxious, not low. I love him to bits. Cuddles are the best bit."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts lately of hurting yourself, or of ending your life?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Any odd experiences, like hearing voices or feeling something odd was going on? Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. I don't drink."},{"t":"4:45","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. Your OCD has come back since Leo's birth, with worries about germs, washing bottles six times and now checking. You also get a frightening image of dropping him, which you'd never act on. It takes about three hours a day. Leo is thriving and your mood is okay. Is that right?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's exactly it."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"This sounds like your OCD flaring after the birth, which is common and very treatable. I'd like to offer the talking therapy that helped before, focusing on gradually facing the worries without washing, through our perinatal team, and review your sertraline with you.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Okay. That sounds hopeful."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"With your agreement I'll let your health visitor and GP know. If you ever feel close to acting on a thought, or your mood drops, ring us the same day, or NHS 111 out of hours. I'll see you in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel lighter already."}],"global":{"pass":"Normalises early, covers onset, obsessions with form, all rituals, avoidance, harm thoughts with intent, impact on the baby, comorbidity, and summarises clearly.","borderline":"Good on washing but misses the checking, avoidance or time cost, or screens comorbidity briefly.","fail":"Never asks about harm thoughts or the baby's care, reacts with alarm, or misses mood and psychosis screening."}},"c-s-66":{"setup":"You are the psychiatrist in a general adult outpatient clinic. Daniel Hughes, 32, fears people think he is a paedophile and checks his messages repeatedly. Take a history, decide what this is, and tell him.","role":{"who":"Mr Daniel Hughes, 32, man, the patient","open":"Daniel's fine. I don't even know how to say it. I keep thinking people believe I'm a paedophile.","manner":"Ashamed and anxious, avoids eye contact. Opens up if the doctor stays calm and unshocked. Becomes tearful when asked directly about sexual interest; settles if his horror is acknowledged.","free":["Thoughts that others see him as a paedophile, and fear it might be true","He rereads every text before sending","It started after his girlfriend of five years left, eight months ago","He hates the thoughts and wants them gone"],"ifasked":["At first the thoughts came once a day and he knew they were absurd","Now most of the day; he believes them '50:50'","Checks sockets, cooker, doors and windows nightly for nearly an hour","Checks his body for arousal near children and avoids his nephews","Off work for six weeks and seeing no one","A similar episode of checking the gas at 19 that settled on its own","Mother is 'a worrier'; drinks a few beers at weekends, no drugs","Mood low because of the thoughts, still enjoys football; no suicidal thoughts"],"worries":["Will you have to report me?","Am I going mad?","Does having the thought mean I want it?","Can this be treated?"],"rules":["Only reveals body checking and avoiding children if asked about other checking or after the direct question","Becomes upset at the direct question; calms if the doctor names his distress","If told he is fine without explanation, asks again for reassurance","Denies all psychotic symptoms clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mr Hughes. I'm Dr Mensah, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP has told me a little. People bring all sorts of unwanted thoughts here, and nothing you tell me will shock me. Where would you like to start?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Daniel's fine. I don't even know how to say it. I keep thinking people believe I'm a paedophile."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening to carry around, and it took courage to say out loud. Thank you. What exactly goes through your mind?","m":[11]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"That people at work, or in the shop, look at me and think it. Then I start wondering if they're right. If I am one."},{"t":"0:43","band":"The thoughts now","w":"D","s":"How do these thoughts arrive? Do you choose to think them, or do they push in?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"They just jump in. Ten, twenty times an hour some days. I hate them."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"Whose thoughts are they? Your own mind's, or put there from outside?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Mine. That's what scares me. They're coming from my own head."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"When one comes, what do you do with it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Try to shove it away. Then I go over and over it, testing myself, trying to prove I'm not."},{"t":"1:23","band":"Onset and course","w":"D","s":"When did this start, and what was happening in your life then?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"About eight months ago. Rachel left me. We'd been together five years."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"That's a big loss. At the start, how many times a day did they come, and how many now?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"At first maybe once a day, and I could laugh it off. Now it's most of the day."},{"t":"1:51","band":"Insight then and now","w":"D","s":"Back at the start, what did you make of them?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"I knew they were stupid. Over the top. Nobody thought that about me."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"And today, how likely does it feel that people really think this?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"I don't know any more. Maybe fifty-fifty. Part of me still says it's rubbish. It's just so loud."},{"t":"2:18","band":"Checking and impact","w":"D","s":"So part of you can still stand back from it. That matters, and I'll come back to it. You told your GP you check your messages. Talk me through that.","m":[4,5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"I read every text five, six times before I send it, in case something sounds wrong. Sometimes I delete it and don't send anything."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Is there anything else you must check or repeat, over and over?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Plug sockets, the cooker, doors and windows. Every night. It takes nearly an hour."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"And what has all this stopped you doing?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"I've been signed off work six weeks. I don't see my mates. I don't go to my sister's because of my nephews."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Is it a wish?","w":"D","s":"I need to ask something directly. I ask everyone with these thoughts, and I ask it without judgement. Have you ever felt sexually drawn to children, or had any urge to act on these thoughts?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"No! God, no. The idea makes me feel sick. Do you think I'm dangerous?"},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much that question hurts, and I'm sorry I had to ask it. Your horror at the idea tells me a lot. Have you ever looked at sexual images of children, or done anything around a child you regret?","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Never. I keep away from kids now. When I do see one, I check my body to make sure I'm not reacting."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"That kind of checking is very common with this sort of thought, and it keeps the doubt alive. Thank you for telling me.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Am I going mad?"},{"t":"3:58","band":"Psychosis screen","w":"D","s":"No. These are questions I ask everyone with strong thoughts. Do you feel people are plotting against you, or that you get signs or messages meant only for you, on TV, online or from strangers?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"No. It's not like they're sending me signals. It's just in my head."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Any voices or visions other people don't hear or see? Any sense your thoughts are put in, taken out or broadcast?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:27","band":"Mood, risk and past","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, and is there anything you still get pleasure from?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Low, because of all this. But I still watch the football and play games online with my brother."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"What about sleep, eating, and your view of yourself and what lies ahead?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Sleep's late because of the checking, but I sleep. Eating's fine. I just want my life back."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"When things are this distressing, some people start to wish they weren't alive. Has that happened to you?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never. My mum would be destroyed."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"Have you ever been through a spell like this before? And any mental health problems in the family, or drink and drugs?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"At nineteen I kept checking the gas, scared I'd kill someone. It went after a few months. Mum's a worrier. A few beers at weekends, no drugs."},{"t":"5:21","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Let me tell you what I think. This is obsessive-compulsive disorder, OCD, and it's a severe episode.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"OCD? Isn't that people who like things tidy?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"That's a common idea. Really, OCD is unwanted thoughts that cause dread, and things you do to ease the dread. Yours push in, they're your own, you fight them, and at first you knew they were over the top.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"But now I half believe them."},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"When OCD gets this severe, the doubt grows until the thought feels true. Feeling true isn't the same as being true. The checking eases the dread for a minute, then feeds it. Nothing you've told me suggests psychosis, and nothing suggests a sexual interest in children. Your horror is the opposite of that.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"I really thought I was a monster."},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"You're not. The thoughts are the illness, not your character. And OCD responds well to treatment.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"What treatment?"},{"t":"6:20","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"A talking therapy called exposure and response prevention, where a therapist helps you face the thoughts without checking, and a tablet called sertraline. When it's this severe, the two together work best. I'll go through it with my consultant and see you in two weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Okay. I think I can do that."},{"t":"6:38","w":"D","s":"If you ever feel hopeless or think about harming yourself, call your GP or NHS 111 option 2, or go to A&E. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what we've found today?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"6:48","w":"P","s":"It's OCD, a bad bout. It feels real because it's severe, not because it is. And the checking keeps it going."},{"t":"6:56","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll write to you and your GP today.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, normalises unwanted thoughts and sets a non-judgemental tone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the thought content, that it intrudes, is his own and is resisted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset after the break-up and worsening frequency over time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks how he saw the thoughts at first and how true they feel now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies checking of texts, sockets, doors and body, plus sick leave and isolation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks plainly about sexual interest in children, urges, images and contact","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for persecution, reference, hallucinations and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, enjoyment, sleep, appetite and outlook","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Finds the past similar episode and asks family history, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his shame and fear before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names severe OCD and explains why from his history, not psychosis or a wish","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines ERP and an SSRI, follow-up, and gives a safety net","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Shows the thoughts began as resisted obsessions, excludes the three mimics calmly, and names severe OCD with reasons he understands.","borderline":"Reaches OCD but skips how insight changed over time, or asks about sexual interest awkwardly.","fail":"Calls it a delusion, avoids the direct question, reports him for thoughts alone, or defers the diagnosis to a senior."}},"c-s-67":{"setup":"You are the psychiatrist in a general adult outpatient clinic. Chloe Bennett, 22, has been referred by her GP with mood changes and possible bipolar disorder. Take a history, exclude the differentials and tell her the diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Bennett, 22, woman, the patient","open":"Hi. I'm a bit nervous. My GP thinks I might have bipolar.","manner":"Polite and anxious, worried about the word bipolar. Relieved and more open once the doctor asks about the pattern. Tearful when talking about the dark thoughts; settles when validated.","free":["Spells of low mood and snapping at people","About six years, much worse in the last year","She has looked up bipolar and is scared","Wants to know what is causing this"],"ifasked":["Spells start about a week before her period and lift within a day or two of bleeding, most months","Tired, sleeps and eats more, no motivation, self-critical in the spells","Completely well between spells; friends call her bubbly","Better on the combined pill at 18 to 19; stopped it due to headaches","Bloating, breast tenderness and aches that week","No highs, no reduced need for sleep, no unusual experiences","Passive thoughts that people would be better off without her in that week for six months; no plans or acts","A few drinks at weekends, no drugs, regular periods, no known illness"],"worries":["Is it bipolar?","Will I need lithium?","Am I just weak?","Can anything help?"],"rules":["Never links the spells to her period unless asked how they start and end or about her cycle","Becomes tearful when the dark thoughts are discussed; settles if validated","Asks 'so is it bipolar?' near the end if not already answered","Answers screening questions briefly and clearly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Iqbal, one of the psychiatrists. Thanks for coming in. How are you finding today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Hi. I'm a bit nervous. My GP thinks I might have bipolar."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"That's a big word to hear, and it's natural to feel nervous. Let's work out together what's really going on. Can you tell me about these mood changes?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I get really low and snappy. Then I'm fine again. I don't understand it."},{"t":"0:31","band":"The spells","w":"D","s":"Talk me through one of these spells. How do you feel, and what changes?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Flat. I cry at nothing. I can't be bothered with anything, even the gym."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"How about sleep, appetite and energy, and how you see yourself at those times?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"I sleep loads and still feel shattered. I eat everything. And I think I'm useless."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"How does it affect work, and the people around you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"I make mistakes at work. And I'm horrible to my boyfriend. I don't recognise myself."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, especially when it's not who you are. Between spells, how are you? How would friends describe you then?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Back to normal. They'd say I'm bubbly."},{"t":"1:28","band":"Course and why now","w":"D","s":"When did the spells start, and how have they changed?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Since about sixteen. But this last year they've been much worse."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"What made you go to your GP now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"I've started having dark thoughts. It scared me."},{"t":"1:50","band":"The cycle","w":"D","s":"Thank you for saying that. I'll come back to those thoughts carefully. First, how does a spell start, and how does it end? Have you noticed it links with anything?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"It just comes on. Then it goes."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"Some people notice a link with their periods. Where in your cycle do the spells come?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Actually... it's about a week before my period. Then a day or two after it starts, I'm fine."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Is that most months, or only sometimes?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Most months. Nearly every month, now I think about it."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Were you ever on the pill or any other hormonal contraception? Did that change the spells?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"I was on the pill at eighteen. They were better then. I stopped because of headaches."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"And in that week, any physical changes: bloating, sore breasts, aches?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Bloated, sore, achy. All of it."},{"t":"2:56","band":"Clearing the mimics","w":"D","s":"I'd like to check for other things too. Have you ever had the opposite of low: lots of energy, needing little sleep, racing thoughts, spending?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"No. Never. When I'm not low I'm just normal."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Have you ever heard or seen things others couldn't, or believed things other people found strange?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Outside that week, does your mood swing within a day? Any temper outbursts, impulsive decisions, worries about how you see yourself or your relationships, or times you've hurt yourself on purpose?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I'm pretty steady. Same boyfriend three years, same friends since school. Never hurt myself."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Any health problems, such as thyroid trouble, irregular periods, acne or extra hair growth? Any regular medicines? And how much do you drink, or use any drugs?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"No. Regular as clockwork. No medicines. A few drinks at weekends, no drugs."},{"t":"4:06","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can we go back to the dark thoughts? What goes through your mind?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"That everyone would be better off without me. It's horrible."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"It sounds frightening to have those thoughts in your own head. How often do they come, and have you ever made a plan or done anything?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Only that week. For six months or so. No plans. I wouldn't. It just scares me."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going through that week?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"My family. My boyfriend. Knowing it'll pass."},{"t":"4:43","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Those matter a lot. Let me summarise. For six years, worse this year, you've had low, tearful, irritable spells with tiredness and eating more. They come in the week before your period and lift soon after it starts. Between them you're well. No highs, no unusual experiences. Is that right?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. So is it bipolar?"},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"No, I don't think so. Bipolar needs times of feeling high or energised, and you've never had that. What you describe fits premenstrual dysphoric disorder, PMDD.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"I've never heard of it. Isn't that just PMS?"},{"t":"5:22","w":"D","s":"It's related, but much more severe. Many women notice some change before a period. In PMDD the brain is extra sensitive to normal hormone changes, so mood drops sharply and life is disrupted. It's real and recognised, and it's treatable.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"I honestly thought I was going mad. Or weak."},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"You're neither. You've been coping with something difficult without a name for it.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"What happens now?"},{"t":"5:50","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"I'd like you to rate your symptoms every day for two cycles, on a sheet or an app. That confirms the pattern. I'll ask your GP to check your thyroid and blood count. Treatments include a talking therapy, an antidepressant even just for the second half of the cycle, or a certain type of pill.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"And the dark thoughts?"},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Let's make a plan for that week now: your warning signs, what helps, two people you can call, and Samaritans on 116 123 or NHS 111 option 2. If the thoughts get stronger or you start planning, go to A&E.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Okay. That helps."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what we've found today?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"It's PMDD, not bipolar. It's linked to my cycle. Diary for two months, bloods, and there's treatment."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see you in eight weeks with the diaries, and I'll write to you and your GP today.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her fear of 'bipolar' and sets the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the character of spells: mood, interest, sleep, appetite, energy, self-view","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks impact on daily life and how she is between spells","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset, worsening over the last year, and why she sought help now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links spells to the premenstrual week, lifting after bleeding starts, in most cycles","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hormonal contraception and bodily premenstrual symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for elevated mood, reduced need for sleep and psychotic experiences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for unstable mood, impulsivity, self-image, relationships and self-harm outside that week","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens thyroid, PCOS features, medicines, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores suicidal thoughts: content, timing, plans, acts and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her distress before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises, names PMDD, explains it and states it is not bipolar disorder","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees two-cycle diary, bloods, treatment options and a safety plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds the premenstrual pattern, clears bipolar and the other mimics, handles the suicidal thoughts and names PMDD clearly.","borderline":"Reaches PMDD but misses the personality or physical screen, or treats the suicidal thoughts briefly.","fail":"Runs a mania checklist and never finds the cycle, accepts 'bipolar', or dismisses it as PMS without a safety plan."}},"c-s-68":{"setup":"You are the psychiatrist in outpatient clinic. Karen Doyle, 49, well on lithium and citalopram since 2020, has had a few months of mood difficulties. Take a history of the cause and tell her what you think.","role":{"who":"Mrs Karen Doyle, 49, woman, the patient","open":"Thanks for seeing me so quickly. I'm worried it's all starting again.","manner":"Warm, a little fed up, anxious about relapse. Embarrassed about sexual symptoms; brief tears when describing snapping at her daughter, then relieved and practical once given a likely cause.","free":["Four months of irritability, tearfulness and worry","Poor concentration at work and feeling exhausted","She fears her depression is returning","Daughter left for university in September"],"ifasked":["Mood not low, still enjoys gardening, no negative thoughts; unlike past episodes","Woken by night sweats, not panicky","Hot flushes at work during the day","Vaginal dryness and painful sex, causing strain with her husband","Periods lighter and irregular, then none for three months","Takes ibuprofen for headaches a few times a month","Last bloods four months ago, normal; no tremor, diarrhoea or thirst","Has stopped motorway driving because of poor focus"],"worries":["Is my depression coming back?","Isn't 49 too young for the menopause?","Can I take HRT with lithium?","Should I stop the lithium?"],"rules":["Mentions painful sex only when asked about stresses at home or vaginal symptoms","Mentions night sweats only when asked what disturbs her sleep","Becomes tearful about snapping at her daughter; settles when validated","If pushed repeatedly about depression, becomes a little irritated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, Mrs Doyle. I'm Dr Adeyemi, one of the psychiatrists. Your GP asked us to see you sooner than planned.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Thanks for seeing me so quickly. I'm worried it's all starting again."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"You've been well for six years, so that worry makes complete sense. Let's work out together what's causing this. What's been happening?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I've just not been myself. Snappy, crying at nothing, and my head's all over the place."},{"t":"0:34","band":"What's new","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice it, and has it changed since?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"About four months. It's crept up. Worse the last few weeks."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"When do you get snappy or tearful? Does anything set it off?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Silly things. I snapped at my daughter on the phone and then cried for an hour."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting, especially when you're close. How are your concentration and energy?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Concentration's shot. I read an email three times. And I'm exhausted all the time."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Stresses and medicines","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed at home or work, or been stressful?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Our daughter left for university. And my husband and I are a bit tense with each other."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Can I ask what's been straining things?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"It's embarrassing. Sex has started to hurt, so I avoid it."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's common and very treatable, and I'll come back to it. Any new illness or new tablets, including anything from the chemist?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Just ibuprofen for headaches, a few times a month."},{"t":"1:56","band":"Is it depression?","w":"D","s":"How is your mood underneath all this? Do you still enjoy things?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Honestly, not low. I still love my garden. I'm just on edge."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"What do you think of yourself these days, and of what lies ahead? And is this like your depression before?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"I'm fine about the future. Before, I couldn't get out of bed and I thought I was evil. This is nothing like that."},{"t":"2:27","band":"Sleep and the body","w":"D","s":"That's a clear difference. How are you sleeping?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Badly. I wake up two or three times a night."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"What wakes you? What's happening when you come round?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"I'm drenched. I have to change my nightie."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Do you feel panicky or frightened then, heart racing?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"No, just boiling hot and wet."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Any hot flushes during the day?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Yes, at work my face goes bright red. I thought it was stress."},{"t":"3:04","band":"Periods","w":"D","s":"You mentioned sex hurting. Have you noticed dryness or soreness?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, dry. That's why."},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"What have your periods been doing over the last year? When was the last one?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Lighter at first, then all over the place. Then nothing for about three months."},{"t":"3:29","band":"Lithium and thyroid","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd also like to check your lithium. Any shaky hands, diarrhoea, being sick, unsteadiness or extra thirst? Any feeling cold, gaining weight, dry skin or constipation?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"No, none of that. If anything I'm too hot."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"When was your last lithium blood test?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"About four months ago. All normal, they said."},{"t":"3:59","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some questions I ask everyone. Any thoughts lately of harming yourself, or anyone else? Any sense people are against you, or strange experiences like ten years ago?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"No. Never. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Has your concentration affected work or driving?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"I've stopped using the motorway. I don't trust my focus."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"That's sensible. Let me summarise. Four months of irritability, tears, worry, poor focus and tiredness. Your mood isn't low and this feels different from before. You have night sweats, flushes, dryness, and your periods stopped three months ago. Is that right?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Yes. So is it my depression?"},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"I don't think so. These changes point to the perimenopause, the years when periods wind down. Hormone changes can cause irritability, tearfulness, poor focus and broken sleep, as well as sweats and flushes.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Isn't forty-nine too young?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"The average age in the UK is 51, and changes often start a few years earlier. So 49 is very typical.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"Oh. I'd never have connected it."},{"t":"5:27","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Many people don't. There are a few things I'd like to do. Heavy sweating and ibuprofen can push lithium levels up, so I'll check your level, kidneys, thyroid and calcium today. Please use paracetamol instead of ibuprofen.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"Should I stop the lithium?"},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Please don't. Stopping it suddenly raises the risk of relapse. If you get shaky, sick, diarrhoea or unsteady, stop that night's dose and get a same-day blood test.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Okay. And the sweats?"},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"Your GP can talk to you about HRT, which helps sweats, sleep and mood, and a vaginal cream for dryness. HRT is fine with lithium and citalopram. You'll still need contraception for a while.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:17","w":"P","s":"That's a relief, honestly."},{"t":"6:21","w":"D","s":"If you notice early waking, guilt, withdrawing or unusual thoughts like last time, call us straight away. So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:33","w":"P","s":"Probably the menopause, not my depression. Bloods today, paracetamol not ibuprofen, see my GP about HRT, and call you if the old signs come back."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. I'll write to you and your GP, and see you in six weeks.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her fear of relapse and sets the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes time course of symptoms and how they have changed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits irritability, tearfulness, triggers, poor concentration and fatigue","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about life stresses, new illness and medicines including over-the-counter drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, enjoyment and outlook, and compares once with past episodes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Finds night sweats without panic and daytime hot flushes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about vaginal dryness and the change and stop in periods","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for lithium toxicity, hypothyroid symptoms and date of last level","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self and others, psychotic symptoms, work and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises and explains likely perimenopause with reasons, not relapse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her distress and embarrassment before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Plans lithium level, renal, thyroid and calcium bloods, NSAID advice, HRT via GP, toxicity safety net","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees relapse warning signs, checks understanding with teach-back and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Shows this is not a relapse, finds the hidden perimenopause, checks lithium and thyroid, and gives a clear likely cause and plan.","borderline":"Reaches perimenopause but skips the lithium checks or the periods, or labours the depression screen.","fail":"Treats it as relapse, never asks about sweats or periods, ignores lithium, or defers the diagnosis to a senior."}},"c-s-69":{"setup":"You are the liaison psychiatrist on the acute medical unit. Sophie Ward, 18, has been unresponsive since collapsing yesterday; all tests are normal. Talk briefly with her mother to get the background, then explain what you think is wrong.","role":{"who":"Mrs Helen Ward, 45, woman, Sophie's mother","open":"Is she going to be all right? Nobody can tell me anything.","manner":"Exhausted and frightened after a night at the bedside. Bristles if she thinks Sophie is being called a fake. Breaks down when asking about her brother-in-law; steadies with an honest, kind answer.","free":["Sophie collapsed walking home from college with friends yesterday","She hasn't moved, spoken, eaten or drunk since","All the tests are normal and nobody can explain it","Sophie has been unsettled lately"],"ifasked":["She went down mid-sentence onto grass; no jerking, wetting, tongue biting or injury","Tests: CT and MRI head, EEG, bloods, drug screen, all normal","Her sister's husband, Paul, was arrested eight days ago for indecent images of children; the police searched the house","Paul has moved out; he has daughters of 12 and 15","Sophie took it hardest: withdrawn, missing college","Sophie was close to Paul as a child; often stayed over","No past mental health problems, self-harm, alcohol or drugs; no family history"],"worries":["What is wrong with her?","If the tests are normal, is she putting it on? Can she snap out of it?","Does this mean Paul did something to her?","What happens now and how can I help?"],"rules":["Becomes angry if the word 'psychological' is used without explanation","Asks the abuse question with tears; settles only with a direct, honest answer","Volunteers that Sophie seemed unsettled, but gives details of the arrest only if asked","Wants to know what she can do to help"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Mrs Ward? I'm Dr Patel from the psychiatry team that works in this hospital. You've been here all night. How are you holding up?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Is she going to be all right? Nobody can tell me anything."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That uncertainty must be awful. I'll explain what I think is happening. Sophie can't tell us her wishes just now, so I'll tell you what you need to know to support her. Can I ask a few questions first?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Yes. Anything."},{"t":"0:34","band":"The collapse","w":"D","s":"Who was with Sophie when she collapsed, and what did they see?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Her friends. They were walking home from college, chatting. She just went down mid-sentence onto the grass."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Did they see any shaking or jerking? Did she wet herself, bite her tongue or hurt herself?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"No shaking. Nothing like that. She landed on the grass. She just lay there."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Since then, has she moved, spoken, opened her eyes, or eaten or drunk anything?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Not a sip. I held a cup to her lips all night."},{"t":"1:22","band":"The tests","w":"D","s":"That must have been so hard to watch. What tests have the doctors done, and what did they tell you?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"A CT and an MRI of her brain, the brainwave one, bloods, a drug test. All normal. That's what scares me."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"Has she had any illness before, or taken regular medicines? Any alcohol or drugs, as far as you know?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Never ill. No medicines. She hardly drinks. No drugs."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Recent stress","w":"D","s":"You said she'd been unsettled lately. What's been happening?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"My sister's husband, Paul, was arrested last week. For indecent images of children. The police searched their house."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"That's a dreadful shock for the whole family. How did Sophie take it?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Worse than any of us. She went quiet. Stopped going to college. Stayed in her room."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"How close has she been to Paul over the years?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Close. When she was little she'd stay over with her cousins. He's always been the fun uncle."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"And before this week, has Sophie ever had problems with her mood or nerves, or harmed herself?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Never. She's always been a happy girl."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before? Anyone in the family with mental health problems?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing. What's wrong with her?"},{"t":"3:02","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've understood. Sophie was well, was badly shaken by the news about Paul, withdrew all week, then collapsed mid-conversation yesterday. Since then she hasn't responded, and every test is normal. Is that right?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"I think Sophie has what we call a dissociative stupor. After a big shock, the mind can shut down movement, speech and response for a while, as a kind of protective switch-off.","m":[7,9]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"But the tests are normal. Are you saying she's putting it on?"},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"No, and I'm sorry if it sounded that way. The normal tests are good news: her brain and body are healthy. This is still a real condition. It's about how the brain is working, not damage. She isn't choosing it.","m":[7,8,11]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Then can't she just snap out of it?"},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"She can't switch it off, and pushing her can make it last longer. She may well be hearing us, even though she can't respond.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"So it's because of Paul?"},{"t":"4:18","band":"The hardest question","w":"D","s":"The timing fits. She was shaken by the news and withdrawn all week. Sometimes a shock like that is more than the mind can hold for a while.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"(crying) Does this mean he did something to her?"},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"That's a terrible thing to be wondering, and I'll be honest with you. I don't know. It's possible, and I won't pretend otherwise. It's just as possible that the shock of the news alone has done this.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"I couldn't bear it if he had."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Please don't question her about it. Once she's recovered, we'll give her the chance to talk, in her own time. Should she ever tell us something did happen, she'll have our support, and we'll bring in the right services, and the police already have an investigation open.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Okay. Okay. What happens now?"},{"t":"5:18","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"She stays here on the medical unit, because she's not drinking and needs the drip. If she still isn't eating in a couple of days, the team may use a feeding tube for a while. We'll see her every day.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"Could it be something else?"},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"We'll check for a related condition called catatonia, which can look similar and responds to a medicine called lorazepam. I'll discuss that with my consultant today.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Will she get better?"},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Most people come out of this over days to weeks, though I can't say exactly when. Then talking therapy can help her with what's behind it.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"What can I do?"},{"t":"6:08","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Sit with her, talk to her normally about ordinary things, keep it calm. Try not to fuss over the symptoms or push her to respond. And please get some rest yourself.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"I can do that."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"So I know I've been clear, how would you sum up what's happening to Sophie?","m":[14]},{"t":"6:30","w":"P","s":"Her mind has shut down because of the shock. It's real, she's not faking, and she'll probably come out of it. I keep things calm and don't push her or ask about Paul."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. I'll come back this afternoon, and the nurses will call me if anything changes.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her night, explains what can be shared and why","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies how she collapsed, witnesses, seizure features and injury","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes her state since and the tests done, all normal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about physical health, medicines, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the uncle's arrest, Sophie's reaction and how close they were","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks past mental health, self-harm, previous episodes and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises, names dissociative stupor and explains what normal tests mean","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains she is not choosing this and cannot simply snap out of it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links the presentation to the shock of the recent news","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers the abuse question directly and honestly, without assuming either way","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear, anger and guilt before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains plan: medical ward, fluids or feeding, daily review, catatonia check, prognosis","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises calm, normal support without pushing or questioning, and self-care","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and gives a time for the next update","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gathers the key history quickly, explains dissociative stupor without blame, answers the abuse question honestly, and leaves her with a safe plan and a role.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but vague on the plan, or a hesitant answer to the abuse question.","fail":"Implies Sophie is faking, dodges the abuse question, or plans transfer to a psychiatric ward while she cannot drink."}},"c-t-01":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, cardiology ward. You have just assessed Graham Whitfield, 63, three weeks after an NSTEMI. Priya, a final-year medical student, is shadowing. Present the case, explain the likely diagnosis and plan, and answer her questions.","role":{"who":"Priya Shah, 23, female, final-year medical student","open":"Yes, hi. I'm with the team this week.","manner":"Bright, keen and polite; asks focused questions and gently pushes for specifics. Visibly moved by the patient's story; grows more confident as the doctor checks her understanding.","free":["Has done cardiology but not liaison psychiatry","Wants to know what the diagnosis is","Thinks some sadness after a heart attack is normal"],"ifasked":["Can name the core symptoms of depression","Saw citalopram used a lot on her GP placement","Does not know about SSRIs and bleeding risk","Has not heard of the Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway","Would like some reading for tonight"],"worries":["Is it just normal sadness after a heart attack?","Who else would you speak to?","Which antidepressant, and why that one?","What happens in the longer term?"],"rules":["If the doctor talks for a long time on one topic, asks to move on to the plan","If the plan misses psychological or social help, asks 'Is there anything non-drug that would help?'","If a drug is named, asks 'Why that one?'","Says the case is sad; settles when the doctor acknowledges it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Is it Priya?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi. I'm with the team this week."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Great. I've just seen Mr Whitfield on cardiology. I'll present him, then talk through diagnosis and plan. Stop me with questions at any point.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Perfect. Could you start by summarising him for me?"},{"t":"0:19","band":"Present the case","w":"D","s":"Mr Whitfield is 63, an estate agent, three weeks after an NSTEMI. His heart is recovering well, but the ward is worried because he isn't engaging with cardiac rehab.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"OK. And mood-wise?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Since the infarct he's been persistently low and has lost all enjoyment: he misses playing his guitar. He wakes at 4 a.m., eats only when the nurses insist, and thinks he's lost weight.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"He blames himself for the heart attack, feels guilty about his wife's long journeys to visit, and fears losing his job.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Could the 4 a.m. waking just be the monitors beeping?"},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Good thinking, it might partly be. That's why I'll ask the night nurses whether he wakes on quiet nights too.","m":[4,13]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"What about his background?"},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Hypertension and type 2 diabetes. He's on amlodipine, metformin, aspirin, clopidogrel, atorvastatin and bisoprolol. Before admission he drank 3 to 5 pints a night and he smokes 30 a day.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Importantly, he denies any thoughts of ending his life. But he's an older man with a new serious illness, heavy drinking and job worries, so I'd keep asking at each review.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Is there anything else you'd want to know?"},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"Yes. With his permission I'd speak to his wife about how he was before and since. I'd ask the nurses about sleep and eating, and check his GP records for any past depression.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Right. So what do you think it is?"},{"t":"2:03","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"What do you think, from what I've said?","m":[13]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Depression? But isn't it normal to feel low after a heart attack?"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"Good point, some sadness is normal. What tips it over is the duration and the number of symptoms: three weeks of low mood and no enjoyment, early waking, weight loss and guilt. I'd call it a moderate depressive episode.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"What else could it be?"},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"An adjustment disorder, though the symptoms are too many and too marked. Alcohol-related low mood. Hypothyroidism, so I'd check thyroid function, B12 and folate. And hypoactive delirium, but he's fully oriented.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Could the bisoprolol be doing it?"},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Sensible question. The link is weak, and stopping it would risk his heart, so I wouldn't change it without cardiology.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"It's quite sad, isn't it? He sounds like a lovely man."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"It is, and it's fine to feel that. The good news is he's very treatable.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:05","band":"Why it matters","w":"D","s":"And it matters: depression after a heart attack is common and is linked with poorer rehab uptake and worse heart outcomes. Treating his mood is part of treating his heart.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"So how would you treat him while he's still here?"},{"t":"3:21","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Bloods first: TFTs, B12, folate, liver tests and sodium, plus an ECG for the QTc. Then sertraline 50 mg in the morning.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Why sertraline? On my GP placement it was always citalopram."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Sertraline has the best safety evidence after a heart attack. Citalopram can lengthen the QT interval. Tricyclics I'd avoid altogether here.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"One catch: SSRIs increase bleeding risk, and he's on aspirin and clopidogrel. So I'd add lansoprazole, not omeprazole, because omeprazole weakens clopidogrel. And recheck his sodium in two to four weeks.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Oh, I didn't know that. Is there anything non-drug that would help?"},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Lots. Now: behavioural activation, small daily goals tied to rehab and to things he values, like music. Later: CBT through the Talking Therapies long-term conditions pathway, which can work on his guilt and self-blame.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Socially: the rehab nurses restart a paced programme, his wife brings in his guitar, a side room for sleep if we can, and a fit note and occupational health for a phased return to work.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"And the drinking and smoking?"},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Brief alcohol advice now, the alcohol liaison nurse before discharge, and nicotine replacement agreed with cardiology, plus the stop smoking service.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"What about the longer term?"},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"GP follow-up, review sertraline at two weeks and again at four to six, and continue it for at least six months once he's well. Plus rehab classes and therapy as an outpatient.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Makes sense."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the three things you'd check before starting sertraline in him?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"The QTc on his ECG, his sodium, and the bleeding risk with the antiplatelets."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So, a moderate depressive episode after NSTEMI: treat it actively, choose the drug for his heart, and link it to rehab.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Is there anything I could read tonight?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"Have a look at NICE guidance on depression in people with a chronic physical health problem. Come and see him with me on Friday, and you can present the review.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"I'd love that. Thank you, that was really useful."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, agrees a clear structure and invites questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Presents a concise summary: age, event, mood symptoms, background and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"States the absence of suicidal thoughts and notes his risk factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names collateral sources: wife with consent, ward nurses, GP records","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names a moderate depressive episode and justifies it from the symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives reasoned differentials, including adjustment disorder, alcohol and physical causes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why depression after a heart attack matters for recovery","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Chooses sertraline with a reason, and names baseline tests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the bleeding interaction with antiplatelets and the PPI choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers psychological and social help, including therapy type, alcohol and smoking","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers the longer-term plan after discharge","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds warmly to the student's reaction to the case","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Teaches interactively and checks her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises, suggests reading and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A crisp summary, a justified diagnosis with differentials, a specific heart-safe drug plan with the antiplatelet interaction, and a full biopsychosocial plan, taught interactively.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and an antidepressant, but vague on drug choice and interactions or missing psychological, social or long-term parts.","fail":"Reads the notes out, calls it normal sadness, or suggests a tricyclic or high-dose citalopram without cardiac caution."}},"c-t-02":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, acute male ward. Daniel Mensah, 25, has treatment-resistant schizophrenia; the team will start clozapine. His mother has asked to meet you, and Daniel has agreed you may share details. Explain the plan and answer her questions.","role":{"who":"Mrs Abena Mensah, 52, female, Daniel's mother","open":"Abena. Good, because nobody tells me anything.","manner":"Frustrated and tired at first; tearful when describing Daniel shouting at her. Settles once listened to, then asks many detailed questions. Agrees to support clozapine if the answers sound honest.","free":["Daniel shouted at her on her last visit","He still believes ministers read his texts","She knows he has tried three medicines, but not their names","She has read that clozapine is 'the last resort'"],"ifasked":["He has been cutting down cigarettes because the ward is smoke-free","He was better on aripiprazole the first time, about three years ago","She worries he is already very sleepy on his current tablets","She lives alone and visits three times a week","She has no support for herself"],"worries":["If it's so good, why wasn't it tried before?","Can it kill your blood? Will he die young?","Could it be an injection instead?","Can his other medicines be reduced?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about Daniel shouting at her; settles if validated","If the doctor talks for over a minute without checking, says 'Sorry, you've lost me'","Asks 'What about his weight?' if weight gain is mentioned","Agrees clozapine is worth trying if her questions are answered honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists on Daniel's ward. Are you Mrs Mensah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Abena."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming in. Daniel has said I can talk to you about his treatment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Good, because nobody tells me anything."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it's felt that way. Can I hear your worries first, then explain the new plan?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:20","band":"Her understanding","w":"P","s":"He shouted at me on Sunday. My own son. He still thinks the government is reading his texts."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, to visit and be shouted at by your son. It's the illness talking, but it still hurts.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"It does. And now they say a new drug, clozapine. I looked it up. It says last resort, and that it can kill your blood."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"I can see why that would frighten you. What do you know about the medicines he's had so far?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Three, I think. One helped the first time. The others did nothing."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Why clozapine","w":"D","s":"That's right. Aripiprazole helped at first, then he relapsed. This time olanzapine and haloperidol haven't worked. When two medicines haven't worked at a proper dose, we call it treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Clozapine is the one medicine shown to work best in that situation. It's not old-fashioned; it's the right tool now.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"If it's so good, why wasn't it tried before?"},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Fair question. Because it needs blood tests and close checks, guidelines say to try two others first. He's now had three.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"How long does it take to work?"},{"t":"1:33","band":"Starting it","w":"D","s":"We start very low, 12.5 mg, and increase slowly over two to three weeks. Some people improve within weeks; for others it takes a few months, so we'll need some patience.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"He's on so many tablets already. Is this just one more?"},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Good point. As the clozapine goes up, the haloperidol comes down and stops. If clozapine works, we'll aim to reduce the clonazepam and promethazine too.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Could he have it as an injection? He forgets things."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"There's no long-acting injection of clozapine, so it's tablets. We'll help him with a routine, and the blood tests show us he's taking it.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Is that making sense so far?","m":[13]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. So, the blood. What are you checking?"},{"t":"2:19","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"In a small number of people, clozapine drops the white cells that protect against infection. So he's registered with a monitoring service, and has a blood test weekly for 18 weeks, every two weeks until a year, then monthly.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"If the count drops, we find out early and stop the drug. He can't get a supply without a safe result.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"So it is dangerous."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"It has rare serious risks, and I'd rather you knew. There's also a rare heart inflammation in the first couple of months, so we check his pulse, blood pressure and temperature daily, and do heart blood tests weekly at first, plus an ECG.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","band":"Side effects","w":"P","s":"What else will it do to him? I don't want a zombie."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"That's a common worry. Common effects are sleepiness, especially early on, drooling at night, dizziness, a fast pulse, constipation and weight gain. Most improve or can be treated.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Weight? He's already put on weight."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"We'll weigh him and check his sugar and cholesterol regularly, with diet and activity support from the start. If he still gains, a medicine like metformin can help.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"One that really matters: constipation. Clozapine slows the gut, and rarely that becomes a blockage. So he'll have a laxative from day one.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"With all this, won't he die young?"},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"That's a very natural fear. Large studies show people on clozapine live longer on average than those on other antipsychotics, partly because it lowers suicide.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Oh. I didn't expect that."},{"t":"3:57","band":"Her role","w":"D","s":"You see him a lot, so you may notice things first. If he has a fever, sore throat or flu-like symptoms, chest pain or breathlessness, or no bowel movement for three days, ring the ward straight away.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Does he smoke?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"He's been cutting down because the ward won't let him."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Smoking speeds up how fast clozapine is cleared, so cutting down raises the level. Tell us if he starts or stops, and we'll check his level and adjust the dose.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"I'll tell you. Who helps me, though? I'm on my own."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"You matter too. I'll refer you for a carer's assessment, and there are carers' groups through Rethink Mental Illness. Family sessions with Daniel can help as well.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me which signs would make you ring the ward?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Fever, sore throat, chest pain, or if he can't go to the toilet for three days. And tell you about smoking."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Perfect. So: clozapine because three medicines haven't worked, a slow start, close blood and heart checks, and you as an extra pair of eyes. Any other questions?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"No. If it's watched that closely, I think he should try it."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Here's a leaflet and the ward number. Shall we meet with Daniel and the pharmacist next week to go through it together?","m":[12,14]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. Thank you for explaining it properly."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and confirms Daniel's consent to share","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her frustration and distress before giving information","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes her understanding and worries early","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains treatment resistance and why clozapine, including why not earlier","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains starting dose, slow rise, cross-titration, time to work and no depot","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the blood test schedule and the reason for it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains heart monitoring: obs, ECG and blood tests early on","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Covers common side effects, weight management and mortality fear honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains constipation risk and laxatives from the start","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about smoking and explains its effect on clozapine levels","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives her warning signs to act on and offers carer support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a leaflet and a joint follow-up meeting","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding during and after the explanation","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises and invites remaining questions before closing","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Validates her, explains why clozapine with honest balance, gives the exact monitoring schedule, covers constipation, infection, heart and smoking, and gives her a clear role.","borderline":"Accurate on blood tests but thin on constipation, heart checks or smoking, or little checking of understanding.","fail":"Dismisses her concerns, calls it completely safe, or omits monitoring and warning signs."}},"c-t-03":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, CMHT clinic. Major Owen Fairbairn, 45, a serving Army officer, was referred by his GP for difficulties after an operational tour of Afghanistan. Take a history to reach a diagnosis, including risk, and explain your conclusions.","role":{"who":"Major Owen Fairbairn, 45, male, patient","open":"Owen's fine. I'm not sure I need to be here.","manner":"Upright, clipped and guarded; scans the room and jumps at a noise in the corridor. Opens up when given control and treated as an equal. Briefly tearful about his soldiers; steadies when validated.","free":["His GP and his wife pushed him to come","He sleeps badly and is 'on edge all the time'","Something happened on a tour of Afghanistan some years ago","He does not want this on his military record"],"ifasked":["An IED destroyed the vehicle in front; two of his soldiers died; he has gaps in memory of the next few hours","Nightmares of the convoy most nights; fireworks or a car backfiring put him back there, heart pounding","Avoids the news, being driven by others, and talking about the tour","Feels 'behind glass' with his wife and two teenage children; stopped running","Sits facing doors, startles badly, cannot sleep before 2 a.m., irritable, cannot concentrate; on sick leave","Drinks about half a bottle of whisky most nights to sleep","Fleeting thoughts the family would be better off, no plans; no weapons at home now","Blames himself for choosing the route"],"worries":["Will his commanding officer find out?","Is he 'a head case'?","Does anything actually work?"],"rules":["Gives only one-line answers until told he controls how much he shares","Becomes tearful if asked about his soldiers; settles if validated and not pressed","Admits the drinking only if asked plainly and without judgement","If asked to describe the explosion in detail, shuts down and says 'I'd rather not'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Major Owen Fairbairn?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Owen's fine. I'm not sure I need to be here."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Owen. Lots of people feel like that at first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"My wife and the GP rather insisted. And I don't want this going to my CO."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. What you tell me stays confidential unless you agree, or there's a serious risk to you or someone else. And you only need to share what you're comfortable with. Could you tell me what's been troubling you?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Sleep, mainly. I'm on edge all the time. It started after a tour of Afghanistan."},{"t":"0:38","band":"The trauma","w":"D","s":"You don't need to go into detail. Could you give me a rough idea of what happened?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Convoy. An IED went off under the vehicle in front. Two of my lads were killed."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. That's a terrible thing to have lived through. Do you remember all of it, or are there gaps?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"The next few hours are a blur. They were my lads. I chose that route."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That's a heavy thing to carry, and it makes sense you feel responsible for them. We don't need to go further into it today.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Sorry."},{"t":"1:14","band":"Re-experiencing","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise. Do memories of it come back when you don't want them to?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"All the time. Pictures of the vehicle. Of them."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"When they come, is it like remembering, or does it feel as if it's happening again, right now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Like I'm back there. Fireworks, a car backfiring, and my heart's pounding, I'm sweating, I can smell it."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"And at night, do you have nightmares?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Most nights. Same one. Riding along, then the bang."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Avoidance and numbing","w":"D","s":"Are there things you avoid because they bring it back: places, the news, talking about it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"I don't watch the news. I won't be driven by anyone else. And I don't talk about it. Ever."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"How do you feel with your wife and children? Close, or cut off?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Like I'm behind glass. I know they love me. I just can't feel it. I've stopped running too."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Hyperarousal","w":"D","s":"How do you react to sudden noises? Do you find yourself on guard, checking exits?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"I hit the deck in Tesco when a pallet dropped. I always sit facing the door. Like now."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, temper and concentration?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Can't sleep before two. Short fuse. I can't read a page. I've been on sick leave four months."},{"t":"2:34","band":"Comorbidity","w":"D","s":"Lots of people find ways to cope with nights like that. Some use a drink. How much do you have on a typical night?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Half a bottle of whisky. It's the only thing that gets me to sleep."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. Any shakes in the morning, or drinking earlier in the day? Any drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"No shakes. Never before evening. No drugs."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Would you call your mood low, or more numb? And did you lose consciousness or hit your head in the blast?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Numb. Not sad exactly. I wasn't hurt. Not a scratch. That's the worst bit."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Risk and function","w":"D","s":"When things are this bad, has it ever crossed your mind to end your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think they'd be better off. But no. I wouldn't do that to the kids."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Any plans, ever? And do you have access to any weapons, at home or at work?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No plans. No guns at home since last year. I'm on leave, so no access at work."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"Has your temper ever spilled over at home, towards your wife or the children?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"I shout. I've never laid a finger on anyone."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Owen. What you've described is called post-traumatic stress disorder: the nightmares, the reliving, avoiding reminders, feeling cut off and always on guard.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"So I'm a head case after all."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"No. It's a normal brain reacting to an abnormal event. Plenty of very capable soldiers get it. The whisky has become a way of coping, and it's keeping things going too.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Is there anything that actually works, or do I just live with it?"},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"It works. A talking therapy called trauma-focused CBT helps most people, usually 8 to 12 weekly sessions. Cutting down the drink alongside helps it work. Tablets aren't the first choice.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Right. Who does it?"},{"t":"4:24","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"With your agreement, I'll refer you through your military GP to the Department of Community Mental Health, and write to your GP. Nothing goes to your CO without your say-so.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"If things get bad, you can ring the Combat Stress helpline any time, or NHS 111 option 2. Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"It's PTSD, there's a therapy for it, I cut the whisky down, and I ring someone if it gets bad."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Thank you for trusting me with this, Owen. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That was easier than I thought."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and addresses confidentiality worries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Normalises reluctance, gives control and validates distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Outlines the trauma briefly, including memory gaps, without forcing detail","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits re-experiencing: intrusions, flashbacks in the present, nightmares","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits avoidance and emotional numbing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits hyperarousal: startle, hypervigilance, sleep, irritability, concentration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol and drugs non-judgementally, including dependence features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression and blast head injury","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts and access to weapons at home and work","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about anger and harm to others at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains PTSD in plain, non-stigmatising words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains trauma-focused therapy, alcohol plan, referral route and crisis contacts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Earns trust, covers every PTSD cluster with examples, asks about drink, weapons and suicide plainly, and explains PTSD and its treatment with hope.","borderline":"Covers most symptoms but misses alcohol or weapons, or explains the diagnosis without a clear route to treatment.","fail":"Forces a detailed account of the explosion, misses risk, or labels him without explanation."}},"c-t-04":{"setup":"Sunday early morning, the emergency department. Lukas Petrov, 30, was brought in by British Transport Police after a rail worker grew worried about him near the platform edge. He is medically fit and sober. Assess his suicide risk and agree a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Lukas Petrov, 30, male, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Flat, tired and embarrassed; short answers at first and keen to leave. Opens up when not judged. Tearful about his partner leaving; settles when validated. Accepts admission if it is offered as help, not punishment.","free":["The police brought him in from the railway last night","He wants to go home","He lost his job recently"],"ifasked":["Planned it for about two weeks; wrote a note on his phone to his sister and ex-partner","Drank half a bottle of vodka, then waited at the platform end for an express train; expected to die","A rail worker spoke to him and he froze; she held his arm and called the police","Not sure he is glad to be alive; no new plan; has paracetamol at the flat","Made redundant from his warehouse team leader job five weeks ago; partner Kasia left two weeks ago","Low every day for about six weeks; poor sleep and appetite; feels hopeless; no voices or paranoia","Drinks about eight cans a night since losing his job; cut his arms once at 24; impulsive","Sister Elena in Leeds is close to him; no friends locally; no children"],"worries":["Is he in trouble with the police?","Is he going to be locked up?","Can he just promise and go home?"],"rules":["Mentions the note only if asked about preparations","Becomes tearful when Kasia is mentioned; settles if validated","Offers to 'promise' to be safe if told he is worrying the doctor","Agrees to informal admission if his sister can be told and it is framed as help"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Lukas Petrov?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for talking with me, Lukas. I hear the police brought you in last night.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I guess so. Can I just go home now? I'm fine."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I can hear you want to get out of here, and it's been a long night. I'm not here to judge you. Could you help me understand how things got to that point? Then we'll plan together.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Last night","w":"D","s":"Take me back to yesterday evening. What was happening, and when did you decide to head for the railway?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"I'd been thinking about it for a couple of weeks. Last night I just thought, tonight."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Did you do anything to prepare before you left, like writing anything or saying goodbye?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"I wrote a note on my phone. To my sister. And to Kasia."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Had you been drinking?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Half a bottle of vodka. Then I went to the far end of the platform, where the fast trains don't stop."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"What did you think would happen?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"That it'd be over. Quick."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"And what stopped it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"A woman in a hi-vis jacket asked if I was alright. I just froze. She held my arm and called the police."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Now","w":"D","s":"How do you feel now about still being here?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Not glad, exactly. Just tired."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Are you still having thoughts about ending your life? Any plan?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Not a plan. The thoughts are still there."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Is there anything at home you could use to hurt yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Paracetamol, I suppose. A couple of boxes."},{"t":"1:36","band":"Lead-up","w":"D","s":"What's been happening over the last few weeks?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"I got made redundant five weeks ago. Then Kasia left two weeks ago. Said I'd changed."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"That's two huge losses close together. I'm sorry. It makes sense you've felt like this.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I keep crying."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"There's nothing to apologise for. How's your mood been, day to day, and your sleep and appetite?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Low, every day, about six weeks. I wake at four. I don't eat. I can't see it getting better."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices when no one's there, or felt people were against you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:13","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt yourself or tried to end your life before?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Never tried before. I cut my arms once, at 24, after a break-up."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink on a normal day? Any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Since the job, about eight cans a night. A bit of weed now and then."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Would you call yourself impulsive? And has anyone in your family died by suicide?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"I do things without thinking. Especially with a drink. Nobody in the family."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Protective","w":"D","s":"Who's important to you? Who knows how you're feeling?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"My sister, Elena. She's in Leeds. No one round here. I moved for the job."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Do you have any children, or anyone you'd want to hurt?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"No kids. I don't want to hurt anyone."},{"t":"2:56","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so honest, Lukas. Last night was very serious: you planned it, wrote a note and chose a method that could easily have killed you.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"You're still not sure you're glad to be alive, you've had two big losses, you're on your own here, and the drink makes things worse. On your side: Elena, and that you let someone help. So I'm very worried about you going home alone.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Are you going to lock me up?"},{"t":"3:23","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"You're not in trouble. My advice is a few days in hospital, as a voluntary patient, so you're safe while we look at your mood and the drinking.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"If I promise not to do anything, can I go?"},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"I appreciate you offering. But you're unsure about being alive, you'd be on your own and there's paracetamol at home. A promise isn't enough tonight. If you wanted to leave while this unsafe, I'd need to ask colleagues to assess you under the Mental Health Act, and I'd much rather we agreed.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"OK. I'll stay. Can someone tell Elena?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Yes, with your permission I'll ring her now. On the ward we'll watch for alcohol withdrawal, give you vitamins, and write a safety plan together.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"And after you leave, NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans on 116 123, or 999 in an emergency. Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"I stay a few days, you ring Elena, and we sort out a plan. And I can ring those numbers."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You did the hard thing tonight by letting people help. A nurse will stay with you until the bed's ready. Anything you want to ask?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks. Just ring Elena."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose, and engages him despite wanting to leave","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to distress and embarrassment with empathy, without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the attempt: planning, note, alcohol, method, expected outcome, intervention","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses current intent, feelings about surviving, plans and means at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores triggers, depressive symptoms, hopelessness and a psychosis screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past self-harm, alcohol and drugs, impulsivity and family history","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies protective factors, dependants and risk to others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Shares a clear risk formulation with him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers informal admission as help and answers his fear of being locked up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the legal option honestly if he refuses while at high risk","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Involves his sister with consent and covers alcohol withdrawal and safety planning","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives crisis numbers and checks understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes warmly with a clear immediate safety arrangement","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers the attempt in sequence including the note and expected outcome, current intent and means, mood, alcohol and protection, formulates risk and agrees admission kindly.","borderline":"Gathers most facts but misses final acts or alcohol, or gives a vague plan without addressing what happens if he leaves.","fail":"Accepts a promise and discharges him alone, or is judgemental or coercive without explanation."}},"c-t-05":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Clarissa Oduya, 45, has had eight months of depression despite three antidepressants and some talking therapy. Take a history to judge whether this is treatment-resistant depression, then agree next steps with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Clarissa Oduya, 45, female, patient","open":"Yes. I'm starting to think nothing works.","manner":"Slow, quiet and flat; tearful when talking about giving up therapy. Brightens a little when given hope and real choices. Wary of the word 'antipsychotic'.","free":["Feels low all the time and has lost hope","Has tried three tablets and none worked","Started talking therapy but did not finish"],"ifasked":["Low every day, worse in the mornings; wakes at 5 a.m.; lost a stone; some days stays in bed","No longer enjoys reading or time with her husband","Fluoxetine 20 mg then citalopram 20 mg, three months each; mirtazapine 30 mg for two months; never missed doses; few side effects","Stopped CBT after four sessions because she was too exhausted","Two past episodes: late teens (got better alone) and mid-30s (fluoxetine and CBT worked)","Never been high; no voices; GP bloods normal; a glass of wine at weekends; off work three months","Some nights hopes she won't wake up; no plans; faith and husband stop her; no tablets saved"],"worries":["Is it her fault for stopping therapy?","Does an antipsychotic mean she is 'mad'?","Will she need lots of blood tests?","What about ECT?"],"rules":["Mentions passive death wishes only if asked about life being worth living","Becomes tearful about stopping therapy; settles if told it isn't her fault","Rejects lithium because of blood tests; chooses aripiprazole if it is explained simply","If not given any hope early, says 'What's the point of this?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Clarissa Oduya?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'm starting to think nothing works."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that sounds really disheartening. Thank you for coming. There are still good options, and I'd like to find the right one with you.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"I'd like to go through how you've been and what you've tried, then talk about next steps. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:23","band":"Current symptoms","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, day to day? Is any time of day worse?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Low every single day. Mornings are the worst. I cry before I've even got up."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. How are your sleep, appetite and energy?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"I'm awake at five. I don't want food; I've lost a stone. Some days I don't get out of bed at all."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"What do you still enjoy?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I used to read every night. I don't even want to sit with my husband."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"Has there ever been a stretch when you felt unusually high or full of energy, barely needing sleep?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And have you heard voices, or had beliefs that others found strange, like being a terrible person or being punished?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"No. I feel guilty for being useless, that's all."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Have you been depressed before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Twice. As a teenager, which passed. And in my thirties. Fluoxetine and CBT sorted it then."},{"t":"1:19","band":"Treatments","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Let's go through this time. What dose of fluoxetine, how long, and did you take it every day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Twenty milligrams, three months. Every day. It did nothing this time."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"And the citalopram and mirtazapine?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Citalopram 20, three months, then mirtazapine 30 at night for two months now. I never miss. Hardly any side effects. Just no change."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"And the talking therapy?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"I went to four sessions of CBT and then stopped. I was too tired. Maybe it's my fault it's not working."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"It isn't your fault. Exhaustion is part of depression, and it makes therapy very hard. We can try again in a way that fits your energy.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I've felt such a failure about that."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Other factors","w":"D","s":"How much alcohol do you drink? Any drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Just the odd glass of wine on Saturdays. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Any pain or health problems? And how are things at home and at work?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"The GP did bloods last month, all normal. No pain. I've been off work three months. My husband has been wonderful."},{"t":"2:19","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things are this hard, some people feel life isn't worth living. Do you ever feel like that?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Some nights I hope I just won't wake up."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I know that's hard to say. Has it gone further, to thinking about ending your life, or any plans?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never. My faith, and what it would do to my husband."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Do you have any tablets saved up at home? And are you managing to eat and wash?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"No tablets saved. My husband makes sure I eat something."},{"t":"2:49","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Clarissa. You've taken three antidepressants properly, at proper doses, and they haven't helped. We call that treatment-resistant depression.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"So nothing works for me."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"No, it means we change the approach. It's common, and many people improve with the next step. You also responded before, which is a good sign.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:10","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"There are a few routes. We could increase the mirtazapine. Another choice is adding a second medicine as a booster: lithium, or a small dose of aripiprazole. Or we could switch to something like venlafaxine.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Aripiprazole. Isn't that for schizophrenia? I'm not mad."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"You're not, and I'm sorry if it sounded that way. At a small dose it boosts antidepressants, and it's used this way a lot for depression that hasn't lifted.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"What about the blood tests?"},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"For aripiprazole: weight, sugar and cholesterol at the start and after three months. The main side effect is restlessness. Lithium works well but needs regular blood level checks.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"And ECT? I read about that."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Fair question. It works well for very severe depression, or if other steps fail. I'd keep it in reserve for now. Alongside any tablet, I'd also refer you for CBT or behavioural activation again, starting gently.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Which of these feels right for you?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"I can't face lots of blood tests. The aripiprazole, I think, and the therapy."},{"t":"4:11","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"That's a good choice. Keep the mirtazapine, add aripiprazole 2.5 mg each morning, going up to 5 mg after a week. We'll check your weight and bloods today.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"If the thoughts about not waking get stronger, or you think about acting on them, tell your husband and ring NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the plan, and what you'd watch for?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Keep mirtazapine, add the new one, watch for restlessness. Therapy again. Ring 111 if I get worse."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll ring you in two weeks and see you in four, and write to your GP today. Anything else?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That's the first time I've felt any hope."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her hopelessness and agrees the plan for the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates distress and guilt and offers hope early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses current mood, diurnal variation, sleep, appetite, weight, energy and enjoyment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for bipolar and psychotic features and asks about past episodes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes each antidepressant's dose, duration, adherence, side effects and response","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the therapy history and why it stopped","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, drugs, physical health and life stresses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses passive and active suicidal thoughts, plans, means and self-neglect","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains treatment-resistant depression plainly, with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers clear options: optimise, augment, switch, therapy and ECT in reserve","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Makes a shared decision and gives a specific drug plan with monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a safety plan with crisis numbers and follow-up dates","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms adequate dose, duration and adherence for each drug, excludes bipolarity, covers risk including passive wishes, and agrees a specific next step with hope.","borderline":"Covers treatments but misses adherence, bipolar screening or passive death wishes, or offers options without helping her choose.","fail":"Labels it resistant without checking the trials, ignores risk, or leaves her with no clear plan or hope."}},"c-t-06":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon on an acute medical ward. Geoffrey Amankwah, 56, with paranoid schizophrenia and type 2 diabetes, has refused all insulin since a hyperosmolar collapse three days ago. Assess his capacity to refuse insulin and explain your conclusion.","role":{"who":"Mr Geoffrey Amankwah, 56, male, patient","open":"Yes. Is this about the injections?","manner":"Defensive and irritated at first, expecting to be called mad. Engages once treated as an equal. Calm and certain about the chips. Briefly angry at the conclusion; settles if reassured he won't be forced.","free":["He collapsed because his sugar went very high","The diabetes doctor says he needs insulin","He has refused every dose and will keep refusing","He is annoyed he cannot go out to smoke"],"ifasked":["He believes nurses put tracking chips in the insulin vials that read and control his thoughts","Belief began after admission; at home he believed the TV talked about him","He is 100% sure; a sealed pen would make no difference","He would take tablets from a sealed pharmacy box","He takes olanzapine on and off","He does not want to die; mood fed up, not depressed","He knows the date and where he is","His sister Abena is the person he trusts"],"worries":["Will they hold him down and inject him?","Is he being called mad?","Can he go out for a smoke?"],"rules":["Becomes hostile if the doctor tries to persuade him or argues about the chips","Only mentions the chips if asked directly why he refuses","Repeats the risks correctly if asked later in the conversation","Settles if told nobody will force him and his sister will be involved"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Geoffrey Amankwah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is this about the injections?"},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"It is. The diabetes team asked me to talk with you about your decision. I'll be honest, they've asked whether you're able to make it. It's still your decision; I'd like to understand your reasons.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"So you think I'm mad. That's why they've sent a psychiatrist. They won't even let me out for a smoke."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"I can hear how insulting that feels, and being kept in without a smoke on top of it. I don't start from the view that you can't decide. Most people I see can.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Fine. Ask your questions."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Understanding","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened when you collapsed, and what caused it?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Sugar went sky-high. I'd felt rotten for days, drinking pop all the time. Then I woke up in here."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And what have they told you about insulin, and why they want it now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Type 2. They say tablets and diet aren't enough any more, so I need injections."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"That's right. Let me add a bit. The sugar got so high it dried your body out badly, which is why you collapsed. Insulin brings it down most reliably right now. Does that make sense?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'm not stupid."},{"t":"1:29","band":"Risks and alternatives","w":"D","s":"You've clearly followed it. The team's worry is that without insulin the sugar climbs again within days: another collapse, a coma, even death. Over years it also harms eyes, kidneys, nerves and heart, and you've some of that already.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"I know about my eyes. They keep telling me."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Did they mention any other way of treating it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Maybe tablets later, they said. Insulin now."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Could you say back to me what might happen without insulin? I'll ask again before I go, just to check it's clear.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Sugar goes up, I collapse again, coma, maybe die. Eyes and kidneys get worse."},{"t":"2:12","band":"Using and weighing","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So help me understand. With all that, what makes you say no?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"It's not the insulin. It's what's in it. The nurses put chips in the bottles."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Tell me more about the chips. What do they do?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Tracking chips. They go in with the needle and they read my thoughts, then control them. I've seen the nurse shaking the vial."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening, believing something is being put inside you. When did you first notice this?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Since I came in. At home it was the TV, talking about me. Now it's here."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"How sure are you, from nought to a hundred? Could there be any other reason the nurse shakes it?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"A hundred. Why else would she do it?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"So on one side there's the chance of another collapse or dying. On the other, the chips. How do you weigh those up?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Sugar's a risk, I know. But the chip's worse. Once it's in, I'm not me. Not that I want to die."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"What if it came as a sealed pen you opened yourself? Or tablets?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"They'd have got to the pen first. They've got keys. Tablets, maybe. Tablets can't carry chips, not from a sealed box."},{"t":"3:38","band":"Decision","w":"D","s":"That's useful to know. So what's your decision about insulin today, and has it been the same each day?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"No insulin. Same as yesterday. Same tomorrow."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Mind and brain","w":"D","s":"Thanks. A few questions I ask everyone. What day is it today, and where are we?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Tuesday. Ward 7, the General."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"And your mood lately? Any thoughts about ending your life, or wishing you weren't here?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Fed up, not depressed. I want to live. I just want to go home."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Have you been taking your olanzapine? Hearing any voices?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"On and off. No voices. Only the TV, before."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Retention and conclusion","w":"D","s":"Before I sum up, can you tell me again what the team said could happen without insulin?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"Collapse, coma, maybe die. Eyes and kidneys worse. I told you."},{"t":"4:36","w":"D","s":"You did, and you've got it all. Here's where I've got to. You understand and remember it all, and your answer is clear. But the worry about chips outweighs everything else, and I think it comes from your illness. So for this one decision, today, I don't think you can weigh it up.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"So that's it. You've decided I'm mad. Now they'll hold me down."},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that's hard to hear and I understand why you're angry. It isn't about you as a person, only about insulin, today. Nobody is going to hold you down.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"5:12","band":"Plan and close","w":"P","s":"So what happens now?"},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the diabetes team today whether tablets could be safe for now, since you'd take those. And I'll come back tomorrow. If your thinking about the chips changes, my answer may too.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"And if they say it has to be insulin?"},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Then the team works out what's best for you, and your own wishes count in that. Anything forceful would need careful legal steps. Is there someone you'd want involved?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"My sister, Abena. She's sensible."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"I'll ring her with your permission. So what do you understand happens next?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"You talk to the sugar doctors about tablets, ring Abena, and come back tomorrow."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll also let your care coordinator know. Anything you want to ask me?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Can I go out for a smoke?"},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the nurse in charge about a smoke with a staff member today. Thanks for talking so openly, Geoffrey.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose honestly and states the decision is his","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and fear without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes his understanding of the collapse, his diabetes and why insulin is advised","d":"Content"},{"c":"Fills gaps in plain words and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains risks of refusing (recurrence, coma, death, complications) and asks about alternatives","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention again later in the conversation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores his reasons and how he weighs risks against them, without persuading","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the belief about the vials: content, onset, conviction, link to illness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for confusion, low mood and suicidal thinking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Confirms a clear, consistent decision is communicated","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a decision- and time-specific conclusion with reasons, without blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers less restrictive options, family involvement, reassessment and closes clearly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Tests all four abilities fairly, links the failure to weigh to the delusion, gives a specific conclusion kindly and offers tablets and reassessment.","borderline":"Covers the four abilities but tests retention only once, or gives the conclusion without the causal link.","fail":"Finds incapacity because of schizophrenia or an unwise choice, argues with him, or proposes the Mental Health Act to give insulin."}},"c-t-07":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon on an adult acute ward. Owen Pritchard, 21, admitted this week with a first episode of psychosis, has agreed you can talk with his mother. She overheard a nurse say 'schizophrenia'. Answer her questions and concerns.","role":{"who":"Mrs Bethan Pritchard, about 50, female, Owen's mother","open":"Yes, Bethan. Nobody's told me anything. And then I hear a nurse at the desk say 'schizophrenia', like it's nothing.","manner":"Angry and talks fast at first, then tearful about the knife. Softens once heard and given clear answers. Asks direct questions and wants honesty.","free":["Owen left university in May after falling behind","He stays in his room, stopped seeing friends and won't wash","She believes he is depressed","She overheard 'schizophrenia' and thinks it means violence and drugs"],"ifasked":["He only eats food he has made himself from sealed packets","She has heard him muttering as if talking to someone","His flatmate said he smoked cannabis most days in first year","Her brother had a 'breakdown' at 22 and was in hospital","She did not know about the microchip or Gabriel","Her husband Rhys is at home; they have knives and paracetamol in the kitchen"],"worries":["Is it schizophrenia, and is he dangerous?","Did we cause this?","Will he be on tablets forever?","Will he get better and go back to university?"],"rules":["Talks over the doctor for the first minute; calms if allowed to finish and validated","Cries when describing the night with the knife; settles if acknowledged","Pushes back if the doctor dismisses depression without explaining","Apologises for shouting at the end if treated respectfully"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and listen","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists looking after Owen. Are you his mum, Mrs Pritchard?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, Bethan. Nobody's told me anything. And then I hear a nurse at the desk say 'schizophrenia', like it's nothing. He's depressed, not schizophrenic. Schizophrenics are violent, on drugs. That's not my son."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it has felt that way, Bethan. Hearing that word in passing must have been a real shock. Owen has said I can talk openly with you, so I can explain everything. Please tell me all of it first.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"I've been ringing for days. Nobody rings back. And now this."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"That's not good enough, and I'll raise it with the ward manager. I've got time now. Could I first hear what you've noticed at home, and what worries you most?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","band":"Her account","w":"P","s":"He came home from uni in May. Stopped seeing his mates, stayed in his room, wouldn't shower. That's depression, isn't it?"},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Those are real changes, and low mood can look like that. What else did you notice: eating, sleeping, how he talks?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"He'd only eat things he'd made himself, out of sealed packets. And he muttered, like he was talking to someone. I thought he was on his phone."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"And the night he hurt his arm?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"He was screaming. Blood everywhere, my kitchen knife in his hand. I thought... (cries) I thought I was going to lose him."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"That must have been terrifying. You did exactly the right thing getting help. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I'm OK."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"As far as you know, has Owen used cannabis or other drugs? And has anyone in the family had serious mental health problems?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"His flatmate said he smoked weed most days in first year. And my brother had a breakdown at twenty-two. We never talked about it."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. What do you understand schizophrenia to mean, and what worries you most about it?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Violence. Drugs. Locked up for life. Is that what Owen's got?"},{"t":"2:12","band":"What psychosis is","w":"D","s":"Let me explain what we've found, then answer that directly. On the ward round Owen told us he cut his arm to dig out a microchip he believes MI5 put there to control his thoughts. And that the archangel Gabriel has been speaking to him for months.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"He told you that? He's never said a word to me."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"People often keep it hidden, especially when they feel watched. Hearing a voice nobody else hears, and firmly believing something that isn't true, is what we call psychosis. The brain starts to misread what's real. It's an illness, not a choice or a weakness.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"But how is that not depression?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Fair question. In the early months, pulling away and not washing look just like depression. But the voice, the microchip belief and the sealed food all point to psychosis as the main problem. His mood doesn't seem low enough to explain them, and we'll keep checking.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"So is it schizophrenia or isn't it?"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Psychosis describes the symptoms. Schizophrenia is one illness that causes it, usually when it lasts or keeps coming back. Right now this is a first episode of psychosis, and it's too early to say more. And most people with schizophrenia are never violent; they're more often victims.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"OK. That's a bit better."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Does that make sense so far? How would you put it?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Psychosis is what's happening. Schizophrenia might be the reason, but you don't know yet."},{"t":"3:44","band":"Causes","w":"P","s":"Why has this happened? Did we do something?"},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"No, you didn't cause this. We think of it as vulnerability plus stress. Genes play a part, which may link with your brother. Heavy cannabis use raises the risk a lot. And stress, like falling behind at uni, can tip someone over.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"I should have seen it sooner."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"Lots of parents say that, and I understand why. Early on it looks exactly like depression or a young man pulling away. You noticed, and you acted when it mattered.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"4:16","band":"Checks and treatment","w":"D","s":"On the ward we're checking for physical causes: blood tests, a urine drug screen and a heart tracing. If anything is unusual we may arrange a brain scan.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"And the tablets? Olanzapine, the nurse said."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Yes. It's an antipsychotic. It turns down the volume of the voice and loosens the grip of the beliefs, over a few weeks. It makes people sleepy and hungry, so we'll check his weight, blood sugar and cholesterol regularly.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"How long will he be on it?"},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"Usually at least one to two years after he's well, because stopping early is the commonest reason for relapse. Any reduction would be planned slowly, with him.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","band":"Safety","w":"P","s":"Will he do it again, with the knife?"},{"t":"5:01","w":"D","s":"That's an important question. He's safe here and staff check on him regularly. Before any leave we'll review the risk with you, and please lock away or remove knives and sharp things for now. If he talks about hurting himself or acting on the voice, ring the ward, the crisis line or 999.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"I'll put them in the car boot. Rhys will help."},{"t":"5:24","band":"Outlook","w":"D","s":"After the ward, the Early Intervention in Psychosis team will support him for up to three years: a care coordinator, a talking therapy called CBT for psychosis, and family sessions with you and Rhys.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"Will he get better? Will he go back to uni?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Many young people recover well from a first episode, especially with early help and staying off cannabis. Some have further episodes, and a smaller number keep some symptoms. The team also helps people back into study.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"So there's hope."},{"t":"5:56","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Real hope. What will you tell Rhys tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"That Owen has psychosis, it's an illness, it's not our fault, the tablets and therapy should help, and we lock the knives away."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. Here's a leaflet on psychosis and the carers' support details; you're able to ask for a carer's assessment. This is the ward number. I'd like to book a meeting with you, Owen and his consultant this week.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:24","w":"P","s":"Yes, please. Thank you. Sorry I shouted."},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise; you were worried about your son. Any other questions before I go?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"Not right now."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Owen's consent to share, and invites her concerns","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Lets her finish and validates her anger, fear and guilt","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes her account: withdrawal, self-care, food fears, muttering, the night with the knife","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what she understands and fears about schizophrenia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains psychosis in plain words using Owen's own experiences","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why depression is not the main problem, and psychosis versus schizophrenia","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains causes without blame: genes, cannabis, stress; asks about drugs and family history","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ward checks and olanzapine: benefits, side effects, monitoring, duration","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses safety: knives and sharps at home, leave review, crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Describes Early Intervention care and a realistic, hopeful outlook","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding during and at the end","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers written information, carers' support, contact number and a family meeting","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Lets her vent, uses Owen's own symptoms to explain psychosis, answers all four of her questions honestly, agrees sharps removal and offers a family meeting.","borderline":"Explains psychosis accurately but talks over her early, or skips the depression question or home safety.","fail":"Confirms schizophrenia or lifelong illness, dismisses her, gets defensive, or leaves out knives and crisis contacts."}},"c-t-08":{"setup":"Wednesday morning in the CMHT clinic. Des Achterberg, 47, on clozapine for 15 years and well, read that people with severe mental illness die years earlier. Explain why this may be true and agree what he can do about it.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond 'Des' Achterberg, 47, male, patient","open":"Yes. Des.","manner":"Polite, anxious and a little hurt that nobody told him. Brings the newspaper cutting. Keen to act once given hope; becomes enthusiastic about stopping smoking.","free":["Read that people with schizophrenia die 10 to 25 years earlier","Takes all his tablets and sees his GP whenever asked","Nobody has ever discussed this with him","Wants to know what he can do"],"ifasked":["Smokes 20 a day since 18; plans to stop cold turkey this Saturday","Gained about three stone on clozapine; always hungry and tired","Rarely goes out except to buy cigarettes; no money for a gym","Eats takeaways and white bread; not confident cooking","GP said his BP is high; he refused tablets because he feels fine","Last HbA1c was 'borderline'; doesn't know his cholesterol","Father died of a stroke at 68","Opens bowels every 3 or 4 days, hard stools"],"worries":["Is the clozapine killing him; should he stop it?","Is he going to die at sixty?","Why did nobody tell him?"],"rules":["Only mentions his cold-turkey plan if asked how he will stop","Gets upset if told the statistic doesn't apply to him","Responds well to 'we' and choices; goes quiet if lectured","Doesn't mention constipation unless asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, a psychiatrist in the outpatient team. Are you Desmond Achterberg?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Des."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in, Des. I hear you read something worrying in the paper. I'm glad you asked us about it. Could you tell me what you read, then we'll go through it together?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Here. It says people like me die twenty years early. I do everything I'm told. I see the GP, I take my tablets. Nobody ever said."},{"t":"0:30","band":"His worry","w":"D","s":"That must have been a real shock, and I'm sorry nobody raised it with you. How have you been since you read it?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping. I keep thinking I'll be dead at sixty. I'm frightened. And a bit angry, to be honest."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"Both of those make complete sense. You've looked after yourself and you deserved to know. Let's use today to put that right.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:55","band":"Is it true","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you: the gap is real. On average, people with illnesses like yours live around 15 to 20 years less. But mostly that's heart disease, stroke, lung disease and diabetes, not the illness itself. And a lot of it can be changed.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"So it's not certain?"},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Not at all. It's an average. The main reasons are smoking, weight gain, not moving much, some effects of the medicines, and checks being missed. Let's look at which apply to you.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","band":"His risks","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke, Des?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Twenty a day. Since I was eighteen."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"And alcohol or anything else?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Couple of cans at the weekend. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"How has your weight changed over the years? And what's a usual day like, for food and getting out?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Three stone heavier since clozapine. I'm always hungry. Takeaways mostly. I only go out for fags. Too tired, and I can't afford a gym."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"That's really common on clozapine, the hunger and tiredness. Has the GP mentioned your blood pressure, sugar or cholesterol?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Blood pressure's high, they said. I said no to tablets; I feel fine. Sugar was borderline. Cholesterol, no idea."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had chest pain, a heart attack or a stroke? And anyone in the family?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Not me. Dad died of a stroke at sixty-eight."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry about your dad. One more that people don't often mention: how are your bowels? How often do you go?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Every three or four days. Hard. Is that a problem?"},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"It is on clozapine. It slows the gut, and if it stops completely it can be dangerous. So we'll start regular laxatives today, and if you don't go for three days, ring us.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:53","band":"Smoking and clozapine","w":"P","s":"OK. Actually, I'm stopping smoking this Saturday. Cold turkey."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"That's brilliant, Des. It's the single best thing you can do. Can I explain one thing first, because it really matters on clozapine?","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"The smoke from cigarettes makes your liver clear clozapine faster. When you stop, the clozapine builds up, sometimes a lot within days. That can make you very drowsy, drool, get muddled, or even have a fit.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Nobody told me that either. So I shouldn't stop?"},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"You should, just with a plan. We set a date in two weeks, check your clozapine level before and a week after, and lower the dose if needed. Patches and lozenges or a tablet called varenicline will help with cravings, and the stop-smoking service can see you weekly.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Patches. I'd try patches."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Lifestyle","w":"D","s":"Great. What else would you like to work on? Some people start with walking, or getting help with cooking.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"I'd walk if someone came with me. And I can't cook healthy stuff."},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"There's a free walking group three times a week; could your key worker come along at first? And the staff at the house can help with shopping and simple meals. We'd usually suggest a dietitian too.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"Leon would come. He's always on at me to get out."},{"t":"4:22","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"Perfect. On our side, every year we'll check your weight, waist, blood pressure, blood sugar, cholesterol and a heart tracing, and your blood count every four weeks as now.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"OK. Nobody's done the heart tracing for years."},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"I'll also write to your GP about your blood pressure and sugar, and ask for a heart risk score. With your dad's stroke and smoking, treating the blood pressure could make a big difference. Would you talk it through with them?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"If it helps me live longer, yes."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Mood and medication","w":"P","s":"Should I come off the clozapine, though? If it's making me fat?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd wonder. But clozapine keeps you well, and people on it actually live longer overall than those whose illness isn't treated. Please don't stop it. We'll manage the side effects instead.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Has this made you feel hopeless at all, or like there's no point?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"No. If anything it's made me want to live longer."},{"t":"5:22","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"That's good to hear. Just to check I've explained it well, what are the main things you'll do first?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"Blood test, then stop smoking with patches, not Saturday. Laxatives. Walk with Leon. See the GP about my pressure."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Spot on. If you feel very drowsy, drool, get muddled or have jerks after stopping, or don't go to the toilet for three days, ring us that day. A fever or sore throat too.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Right. I'll write that down."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll give you a leaflet with it all on, and see you again in four weeks. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. I feel better than when I came in."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, praises him for asking and agrees the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores what he read and how he feels, validates and apologises","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms the gap honestly, explains main causes, gives hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about smoking, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about weight, diet, activity and clozapine hunger and tiredness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about BP, glucose, cholesterol, personal and family cardiovascular history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about bowel habit and explains clozapine constipation risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Supports smoking cessation with NRT or varenicline and the stop-smoking service","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains clozapine level rise on stopping smoking and plans level checks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees practical diet and activity goals with named support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains annual monitoring and liaison with the GP, including BP and risk score","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks mood and hopelessness and advises against stopping clozapine","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets for toxicity and constipation, arranges review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Confirms the gap honestly, maps all his risks including family history and bowels, makes quitting safe with a clozapine level plan, and agrees goals he chose.","borderline":"Good lifestyle advice and risk factors, but vague on the level check or misses constipation.","fail":"Encourages stopping smoking without the clozapine interaction, falsely reassures him, or lectures without engaging."}},"c-t-09":{"setup":"Friday late morning on a trauma ward. Liam Doherty, 27, is four days after a motorbike crash and his behaviour has worried staff. His mother, Maureen, wants to talk. Take a collateral history, consider the causes and address her worries.","role":{"who":"Mrs Maureen Doherty, 55, female, Liam's mother","open":"Yes, Maureen. He's not himself at all.","manner":"Worried and embarrassed, twisting her rings. Becomes tearful about whether he will be the same. Defensive about his drinking at first; opens up when not judged.","free":["Liam came off his motorbike four days ago when a car pulled out","He is rude and crude to nurses, which is not like him","He was always gentle and easy-going","She is scared it is brain damage"],"ifasked":["Paramedics said he was knocked out for several minutes","He remembers nothing of the day of the crash or the first two days here","Scan on arrival was 'clear'; one night in intensive care for his chest","Worse at night; last night pulled his drip out and tried to leave; picking at sheets","Drinks most evenings, six to eight pints, sometimes vodka; more since his girlfriend Jess left four months ago","Last drink the night before the crash; he has been shaky and sweaty","Nurses mentioned a temperature last night; he is on a morphine pump","He keeps asking for Jess and fretting about a job already finished"],"worries":["Is this brain damage? Will she get her son back?","Why don't they just sedate him?","Will he be able to work and ride again?"],"rules":["Downplays the drinking ('a few after work') until asked for amounts without judgement","Cries when asked what worries her most; settles if validated","Does not know the GCS or technical details; becomes flustered if pressed","Says the crash was definitely an accident if asked gently"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Liam's mum, Mrs Doherty?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, Maureen. He's not himself at all."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks for waiting, Maureen. Liam's happy for us to talk. The surgeons asked us to see him because of his behaviour, and you know him best. That sounds really worrying for you.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"It is. And the things he's saying to those young nurses. I'm mortified."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I understand, and so do the staff. It helps us a lot to know it's out of character. Can I start with the crash itself?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:33","band":"The accident","w":"P","s":"A car pulled out on him on the way to work. He had his helmet on."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Was he knocked out, do you know, and for how long?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"The paramedics said several minutes."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"What's the last thing he remembers before the crash, and when did his memory seem to come back?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"He doesn't remember that whole day. Or the first two days in here. Even now he forgets what I told him yesterday."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"Has he had any scans, and was he in intensive care?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"A scan when he came in. They said it was clear. One night in intensive care for his ribs and lung, then the operation on his arm."},{"t":"1:12","band":"The change","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What's different about him now? Can you give me an example?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"He swears at the nurses, makes crude comments. He laughs at nothing. He keeps asking for Jess, his ex. She left four months ago. And he's fretting about a job they finished last month."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"Can he follow a conversation? And is he worse at any particular time of day?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"He drifts off mid-sentence. Nights are worst. Last night he pulled his drip out and headed for the door."},{"t":"1:46","band":"Before the crash","w":"D","s":"What was Liam like before all this: personality, work, friends?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Gentle. Easy-going. Everyone likes him. He's a partner in a building firm with his mate."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Any mental health problems before? How had his mood been lately?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Never. He was low after Jess left. Drowning his sorrows a bit."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Alcohol and drugs","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this, and I'm not judging him. How much does he usually drink?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Just a few after work. Everyone does."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"It is common. Roughly how many, and how many nights a week? It matters because stopping suddenly can cause exactly this kind of confusion.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Most nights. Six or eight pints. His flatmate says vodka some nights too, since Jess."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"When was his last drink before the crash? And since he's been here, has he been shaky, sweaty, or seeing things?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"The night before. His hands are shaking, he's sweaty, and last night he was picking at the sheets like there was something on them."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"That's really important. Any drugs, now or in the past?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"A bit of cannabis as a teenager. Nothing now that I know of."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Has anyone mentioned a temperature or infection? And he's on a morphine pump, isn't he?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"A nurse said he had a temperature last night. Yes, the pump."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"I have to ask this gently. Given how low he was, is there any chance the crash was anything other than an accident?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"No. A car pulled out. The police said so. He'd never."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he hurt anyone, or said anything about hurting himself?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"He pushed a nurse's hand away, and swears. Never anything about himself."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Thank you. One more thing: he mustn't drive or ride until his doctors clear him.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"His bike's a write-off anyway."},{"t":"3:37","band":"Her concerns","w":"D","s":"What worries you most, Maureen?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"(cries) Is this brain damage? Will I get my son back?"},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"That's such a frightening thought, and it's natural to ask. Take your time.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Sorry. Go on."},{"t":"3:53","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"I think there are three things, possibly together. First, confusion from being so unwell, an infection and the strong painkillers. Second, coming off alcohol suddenly; that often peaks around now. Third, the knock to his head. The first two are very treatable.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"And the head?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Being out for minutes and losing two days of memory means a real brain injury. Changes like his often improve over weeks and months. Honestly, it's too early to say how much; we'll know more once the confusion clears.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Why don't they just give him something to calm him?"},{"t":"4:34","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"Good question. If it's alcohol withdrawal, the right treatment is a specific sedative given to a schedule, plus high-dose vitamins into a vein. I'll also ask for blood tests and a chest X-ray, a review of his painkillers, and discuss a repeat brain scan with the surgeons.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"Later, when he's clearer, a psychologist can test his memory and concentration, and there's brain injury rehab if he needs it.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"OK. That's more than anyone's told me."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"What will you tell your husband tonight, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"Could be confusion, the drink, and his head. They'll treat the first two now, and we'll see about the head."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's information from Headway, the brain injury charity, and the ward number. Look after yourself too. I'll see Liam again on Monday, sooner if needed. Any other questions?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you, doctor. I feel a bit better."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Liam's consent and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and embarrassment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes mechanism, loss of consciousness, memory gap, scan, ICU stay and surgery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Describes the change with examples, attention and night-time worsening","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes previous personality, work and mental health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes detailed alcohol and drug history, last drink and withdrawal signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about infection, pain relief and other delirium causes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively whether the crash might have been deliberate","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses aggression, leaving, self-harm and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains delirium, withdrawal and brain injury as possible causes in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains next steps: delirium screen, withdrawal treatment, scan, later neuropsychology","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses her fears about recovery honestly and offers support","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives information and contact, and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Grades the injury, links night-time confusion and heavy drinking to delirium and withdrawal, keeps the brain injury in view, and supports his mother honestly.","borderline":"Good injury and behaviour history but a vague alcohol history, or no clear next steps.","fail":"Labels it brain damage or bad behaviour, misses alcohol withdrawal, or ignores her distress."}},"c-t-10":{"setup":"Monday afternoon on an adult acute ward. Darren Okafor, 35, has just been admitted under Section 3. His CPN, Paul Henshaw, has come to hand over. Take a collateral history about Darren's recent presentation, background and risks, and agree an initial plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Paul Henshaw, 30s, male, Darren's community psychiatric nurse","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Professional and organised but a bit rushed; new to the team. Quietly worried he should have acted sooner. Warms up when thanked and when the plan is clear.","free":["Known Darren two weeks; he has deteriorated quickly","Darren believes his neighbours are filming him","He stopped his olanzapine about three weeks ago","He has been sleeping in his car"],"ifasked":["Diagnosed with schizophrenia in 2018; Section 2 then; Section 3 in 2022 after stopping meds and cannabis","Stopped olanzapine because of two stone weight gain and feeling flat; it worked well","Smokes skunk daily, about £20 a day; four cans of strong lager a day; 20 cigarettes","Threatened to 'smash his neighbour's face in' last week; police attended, no arrest","Punched a stranger he thought was following him in 2022; got a caution","Said once, 'Might as well end it if they keep watching'; no plan known","Daughter aged 7 lives with ex-partner Kelly; contact alternate Saturdays, next one this weekend","Lives alone in a council flat on benefits; mother is supportive; has been driving his car"],"worries":["Should he have pushed for admission sooner?","Will Darren be put on a depot?","What happens about his daughter's visit this Saturday?"],"rules":["Gives a brief overview, then answers only what is asked","Only mentions the suicide comment if asked about self-harm or suicide","Only mentions the daughter if asked about children or family","Becomes more open if the doctor acknowledges his effort"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and set structure","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Paul Henshaw, Darren's CPN?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming over, Paul. Darren arrived an hour ago, so your view is really valuable. Could you give me the main picture first, then I'll ask some specific questions?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Sure. I'm new to the team, so I've only known him two weeks. He's gone downhill fast. Stopped his olanzapine, thinks the neighbours are filming him, sleeping in his car."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Recent change","w":"D","s":"That's a clear start. What was he like when you first met him, and how has it changed?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"First visit he let me in, a bit suspicious but chatty. Then he missed two visits. Last week he wouldn't open the door, talked through the letterbox."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"What does he believe is happening? Has he mentioned voices?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Neighbours filming him through the smoke alarm, sharing it online. He hears them commenting on what he's doing."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"And sleep, eating, self-care? What's he done because of it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Sleeping in his car for about a week so they can't film him. Barely eating, lost weight. Hasn't washed."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"When was he diagnosed, and how many admissions has he had?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"2018. Section 2 that time. Then Section 3 in 2022."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"What led to the 2022 relapse? What tends to set him off?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Same as now. Stopped tablets, back on the weed."},{"t":"1:35","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"What's he prescribed, and when did he stop?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Olanzapine 10 milligrams at night. Stopped about three weeks ago."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Do you know why he stopped? And how well did it work before?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Put on two stone and says it makes him feel flat. But when he took it, he was well. Risperidone before that, stopped for sexual side effects."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Has a depot ever been discussed?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"He refused one in 2022. Are you going to put him on one this time?"},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Fair question given the pattern. We'll restart what worked first, then discuss a long-acting injection with him when he's settled enough to choose. I'd like you involved in that.","m":[5,12]},{"t":"2:20","band":"Substances","w":"D","s":"What's he using? Type, how much, and what it costs him?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Skunk, every day. About twenty quid a day, he said. Four cans of strong lager. Twenty cigarettes."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Anything else: cocaine, other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"2:38","band":"Physical and forensic","w":"D","s":"Any physical health problems? And any contact with police or courts?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"No physical problems. He got a caution in 2022 for punching a stranger he thought was following him."},{"t":"2:51","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he threatened or hurt anyone recently? Any weapons?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Last week he banged on the neighbour's door, said he'd smash his face in. Police came; no arrest. No weapons I've seen."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"Has he named anyone else he feels is involved?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Just the couple next door."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of suicide or self-harm?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"He did say once, 'Might as well end it if they keep watching.' I didn't push it. Maybe I should have."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you; that's important and the ward will know. Did he say anything about how, or do anything to prepare? Any self-harm before?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No plan I know of. Never self-harmed."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Is anyone exploiting him, or is he at risk out in the car?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Not that I know. It's freezing, though. That's grim."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Does he have children, or anyone vulnerable he sees? And has he been driving?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"A daughter, seven. Lives with his ex, Kelly. He has her every other Saturday; this Saturday's due. And yes, he's driving around."},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Thanks for flagging it. That contact needs to pause while he's this unwell; I'll speak to the ward social worker today about her mum and children's services. We'll advise him not to drive.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Social","w":"D","s":"Where does he live, and how are his money and support?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Council flat on his own. Benefits. His mum's in town and very supportive. (pauses) I keep thinking I should have pushed for this a week earlier."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"That's understandable, and it shows you care. You noticed the change, escalated it and got him safe. That's exactly what was needed.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:33","band":"Ward plan","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'm thinking. Observations every 15 minutes to start, a search of his things, and no leave until the RC sees him tomorrow. Restart olanzapine 10 mg tonight, with lorazepam if he's very agitated. Bloods, a drug screen and an ECG, plus nicotine replacement and alcohol withdrawal monitoring.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Sounds right. What about the flat?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"The ward social worker can start talking to housing about a move away from that neighbour. And I'll make sure he gets his rights explained and an advocate.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:08","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it. A known relapse pattern: stopped olanzapine over weight and flatness, daily skunk, neighbours filming him, voices, sleeping in his car. Threatened a neighbour, previous assault, one passive suicidal comment, a daughter with contact due, and he's been driving. Anything I've missed?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"No, that's it. His mum will want to hear from you."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I'll ask Darren if we can call her. Could you join the weekly ward review, so we plan the CPA and discharge together?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Yes, Thursdays work. I'll be there."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"Brilliant. Here's the ward number. Thanks again, Paul.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, thanks him, and sets a clear structure","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes how long and how well Paul knows Darren","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a timeline of the recent change: beliefs, voices, sleep, eating, self-care, behaviour","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers diagnosis, previous admissions and relapse triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers medication, adherence, reasons for stopping, previous response and depot history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies cannabis, alcohol and other substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about physical health and forensic history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to others: threats, violence, weapons, named targets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self: suicidal thoughts, self-harm, neglect, vulnerability","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about children in contact and driving, and acts on both","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers housing, finances and family support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Agrees an initial ward plan: observations, leave, medication, tests, safeguarding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises back, supports the colleague and agrees ongoing contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a structured, complete collateral, asks why he stopped medication, covers every risk including his daughter and driving, and agrees a clear joint plan.","borderline":"Good history of the relapse but misses children, driving or the reason for stopping medication.","fail":"Unstructured chat that misses risk to others or self, or no plan and no agreed contact with the community team."}},"c-t-11":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, the emergency department. Roger Whitfield, 52, was found after trying to hang himself in a hotel 80 miles from home and is medically cleared. He wants to leave. Take a history of the attempt and assess his risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Roger Whitfield, 52, male, patient","open":"Yes. I'm fine now, honestly.","manner":"Polite, calm and keen to leave; plays it down at first. Opens up if the doctor is patient and non-judgemental. Becomes tearful about his sons and the debt; quietly honest about still seeing no way out.","free":["Wants to go home today","Says it was 'a stupid moment' and he is fine","Was at a hotel 'for work'","Has always coped on his own"],"ifasked":["Booked the hotel a week ago, 80 miles from home, so his wife Denise and sons would not find him","Told Denise he was at a supplier event","Left letters in his desk drawer at home; changed his will on Monday","Researched hanging online; bought rope two days before; about half a bottle of whisky first; tied it to the wardrobe rail, which snapped, and staff heard the crash","Sorry it failed; feels 'at peace'; nothing has changed; cannot promise he would not try again","Low for three months, stopped golf, wakes at 4 a.m., lost about a stone, feels a failure; no voices","Building firm went into liquidation a month ago; about £90,000 debt, part secured on the house, hidden from Denise","Father died by suicide when Roger was 19; rope and tools in his garage"],"worries":["Will Denise find the letters?","Is he going to be locked up?","Will everyone find out about the money?"],"rules":["Minimises at first; opens up only after his wish to leave is acknowledged and he is given a reason to talk","Becomes tearful when his sons are mentioned; settles if validated","Agrees to informal admission if told honestly why, and if the Mental Health Act is explained without threat","Agrees Denise can be told he is in hospital but wants to tell her about the money himself"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Roger Whitfield?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'm fine now, honestly."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for waiting, Roger. I know you'd like to head home. I'd like to understand what happened first, and then we'll plan the next step together. Is that alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. But it was just a stupid moment."},{"t":"0:22","band":"Why the hotel","w":"D","s":"Could we start with how you came to be at the hotel? It's a long way from home.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Work thing. Well. That's what I told my wife."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"What was it really for, Roger?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"I booked it last week. I didn't want Denise or the boys to be the ones who found me."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a very lonely decision to carry. So you planned this a week ago, somewhere you wouldn't be found?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:54","band":"The attempt","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through yesterday evening, step by step?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"I checked in, put the sign on the door, had some whisky. About half the bottle. Then I used the rope."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Had you looked up how to do it? And did you leave a note, or sort out money or your will?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"I read up on it for days. Bought the rope on Monday. Changed my will the same day. There are letters in my desk at home for Denise and the boys."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"And what happened when you tried?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"I tied it to the wardrobe rail. It snapped. Made a hell of a noise and the staff came."},{"t":"1:35","band":"Now and the calm","w":"D","s":"How do you feel now about still being here?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Sorry it didn't work."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest with me. The nurse noticed you seem calm this morning. What's changed since last night?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Nothing's changed. I just feel at peace. I know there's a way out now."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Are you still having thoughts about ending your life, today?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"I'm not going to promise you anything. I don't have a plan right now, if that's what you mean."},{"t":"2:06","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Let's look at how you've been. How's your mood been over the last few months? What do you still enjoy?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"Low since the summer. I stopped golf. Stopped seeing the lads. Can't be bothered with anything."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, appetite and energy?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Awake at four every morning. Lost about a stone. Exhausted."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself these days? And have you heard voices or felt you deserve punishment?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"A failure. But no, nothing like voices. I'm not mad."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Not at all; I ask everyone. How much have you been drinking lately?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"A few whiskies at night used to be it. Last three weeks, about half a bottle a day."},{"t":"2:49","band":"Life stresses","w":"D","s":"Something big seems to have happened since the summer. What led to all this?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"My building firm went under a month ago. I owe about ninety grand. Some of it's against the house. Denise has no idea. Twenty-odd years I built that. I can't look the boys in the eye."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"That's an enormous amount to carry alone, and I can hear the shame in it. Losing a business you built is a real loss. There is help with debt, even debt this size.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Doesn't feel like it."},{"t":"3:22","band":"Risk and protective factors","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family ever taken their own life?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"My dad. When I was nineteen."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. If you went home today, is there anything there you could use to harm yourself?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"There's rope and all sorts in the garage. It's a builder's garage."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going, even a little? And would you accept help from us?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"The boys, I suppose. I've never seen anyone like you before. I don't know."},{"t":"3:51","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Roger, I'm really glad you told me all of this, and I'm worried enough that I think you need to stay for now.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"So can I just go home now?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Not today. You planned this carefully, you're sorry it failed, and nothing that led to it has changed. I'd like you to come into hospital voluntarily, to keep you safe and treat the depression, which does respond to treatment.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"And if I say no? You can't keep me here."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"You've done nothing wrong, and this isn't a punishment. If you wanted to leave, I'd ask colleagues to assess you under the Mental Health Act. I'd much rather you chose to stay.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be locked up. But I don't trust myself at home, to be honest."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"That's a brave thing to say. We'll look for a bed near home. Can I tell Denise you're safe in hospital? You can choose when to talk about the money.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Tell her I'm in hospital. I'll tell her about the money myself. What about the letters?"},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Good question. We can ask her not to open anything in your desk until you've spoken. Would that help?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you."},{"t":"5:01","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it right: a planned attempt, a deep low for months, more drinking, and a debt you've carried alone. Is that fair?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"That's about it."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"So what's the plan as you understand it?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"I stay, you find a bed near home, and you tell Denise I'm here."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Exactly. A nurse will stay with you meanwhile, and we'll keep an eye on alcohol withdrawal. My consultant will see you today. If the urge to harm yourself comes back, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Alright."},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for talking so openly, Roger. That took courage.","m":[2,12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his wish to leave and agrees the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his distress, shame and loss without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes planning, distance, cover story, letters, will, research, alcohol, method and discovery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores regret at surviving, the calm and current suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for depressive and biological symptoms, psychotic features and alcohol use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies the business loss, debt and secrecy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history, means at home and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States clearly that he should not go home and why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers informal admission and explains the Mental Health Act route without threat","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Seeks consent to involve his wife and addresses the letters","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and checks his understanding of the plan","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets: observation, withdrawal, consultant review, tell staff if urges return","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Draws out a planned, hidden attempt and its cause, reads the calm as a warning, and agrees admission honestly.","borderline":"Covers the attempt but misses the final acts or the debt, or leaves the plan vague.","fail":"Accepts 'I'm fine', discharges him, or tries to hold him under Section 5(2) in the ED."}},"c-t-12":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, the adult gender identity clinic. Nadia Bright, 33, a trans woman referred by her GP, is at her first appointment. Assess her readiness for gender-affirming care and explain what happens next.","role":{"who":"Ms Nadia Bright, 33, trans woman, patient","open":"Nadia, and she/her.","manner":"Warm but guarded, braced for judgement after years of waiting. Relaxes if her name and pronouns are used and questions are explained. Briefly upset if asked about voices without a reason.","free":["Has felt she is a girl since about age five","Has lived fully as Nadia for two years","Partner Marcus is very supportive","Wants hormones and later surgery"],"ifasked":["Wore her sister's clothes as a child; puberty was unbearable, especially her voice breaking","Changed name by deed poll; works as a dental nurse; colleagues accepting","Expects oestrogen to raise her voice","Would like children with Marcus one day; had not thought about sperm storage","Smokes five cigarettes a day; drinks about six units a week","Punched on a night bus last year; fractured cheekbone; anxious on buses; no nightmares or flashbacks","A friend has offered online oestrogen; she has not taken any","Feels disgust about her genitals; no thoughts of self-surgery; monogamous; no sexual health check for two years"],"worries":["When can she start hormones?","Will she be made to have therapy first?","Will her voice change?"],"rules":["If asked about voices or obsessions without explanation, asks 'Do you think I'm mad?'; settles if the doctor explains","Becomes tearful talking about the assault; settles if validated","If told she cannot have hormones for years, says she may buy them online"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the gender clinic. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Nadia, and she/her."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Nadia. Your GP referred you to talk about hormones and surgery. I'm here to understand your journey and then plan the next steps with you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Okay. I've waited a long time for this."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"I know the wait has been long, and I'm glad you're here. Could you tell me about how you've felt about your gender over time?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:29","band":"Gender history","w":"P","s":"Since I was tiny. Five, maybe. I knew I was a girl. I used to wear my sister's clothes when no one was looking."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"How was puberty for you, Nadia?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Horrible. My voice broke and I wanted to disappear. I hated shaving."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard. Has that sense of being a woman ever wavered?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Never. I just hid it for a long time."},{"t":"0:53","band":"Social transition","w":"D","s":"What does your life look like now: name, clothes, work, who knows?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Two years living as Nadia. Changed my name by deed poll. I'm a dental nurse and work have been fine. Marcus, my partner, has been amazing."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"And how does living as Nadia feel, compared with before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Like I can breathe. I finally feel like me."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Goals","w":"D","s":"What are you hoping hormones and surgery will change for you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Breasts, my shape, softer skin. Surgery later. And my voice going higher, hopefully."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"That's a really common hope. Oestrogen doesn't change the voice, because the vocal cords have already changed. Voice therapy works well, though. Facial hair usually needs laser or electrolysis too.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Oh. I didn't know that about the voice."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about whether you and Marcus might want children one day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Maybe. I hadn't really thought about it with hormones."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Hormones can reduce fertility, sometimes for good, so we can arrange sperm storage first if you'd like.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'd want that option."},{"t":"1:58","band":"Mental and physical health","w":"D","s":"Next I'll ask some questions I ask everyone, about mood and other experiences. It's not because I think being trans is an illness. How have your mood, sleep and appetite been?","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Fine, really. Mood's good. I sleep okay."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Have you ever heard voices when no one's there, or had thoughts that won't leave you alone?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. Honestly, it's a bit insulting."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"I understand, and I'm sorry it feels that way. It's routine for everyone, so nothing gets missed. Is your distress about one particular feature, or your body as a whole?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"My whole body being male. Not one bit."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Any medical problems? Do you smoke, drink or use drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"No medical problems. Five cigarettes a day. A few glasses of wine a week."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever wanted to end your life, or thought of harming parts of your body you're unhappy with?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"I hate my body down there. But no, I'd never cut anything. And I'm not suicidal."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being open. Have you ever bought or been offered hormones online?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"A friend offered me some. I haven't. I've been tempted though."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that pull while they wait. Unmonitored hormones can cause clots and liver problems, so please talk to us first.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Has anyone ever hurt you or treated you badly because you're trans?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Last year someone punched me on a night bus. Broke my cheekbone. I still get scared on buses."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry, Nadia. You didn't deserve that. Do you get nightmares or flashbacks about it?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"No. Just nervous. I reported it."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about sexual health? Is it just you and Marcus, and when were you last checked?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"Just Marcus. A couple of years ago, maybe."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Support and consent","w":"D","s":"Some changes from hormones can't be undone, like fertility. How do you feel about weighing that up? And who supports you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"I've thought about it for years. I understand. Marcus, my sister, and a friend at work."},{"t":"3:54","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"Here's what happens next. Baseline blood tests, then a second appointment. After that, hormones can be recommended, usually prescribed by your GP with our support.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"So can I start hormones soon?"},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"Yes, we can plan that. Before hormones I'll refer you for sperm storage and to a speech and language therapist for voice. Stopping smoking really matters, because smoking plus oestrogen raises clot risk.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"I'll try. Do I have to have therapy first?"},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"No, it's not a hurdle. Therapy is there if you'd find it helpful, say with the anxiety on buses. I'll also offer a sexual health check and peer support details.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:34","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"So: a lifelong sense of being a woman, two years living as Nadia, good support and no other health problems. What happens next, as you understand it?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Bloods, sperm storage, voice therapy, stop smoking, then a second appointment."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you feel low, unsafe, or tempted to buy hormones, call us, your GP or NHS 111 option 2. Is it alright if I write to your GP?","m":[11,8]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you for listening properly."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Nadia. I'll see you at your next appointment.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks her name and pronouns at the start and uses them throughout","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and experiences without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores gender feelings from childhood, through puberty, to now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores social transition, work and support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores goals and corrects expectations about voice, hair and fertility","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why, then screens mood, psychosis, OCD, body dysmorphia and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, self-surgery, online hormones, assault and sexual health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks understanding of irreversible effects and consent to share with GP","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains next steps: bloods, second appointment, fertility, voice therapy, optional therapy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and checks her understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets about online hormones, smoking and distress with contact numbers","d":"Close"},{"c":"Closes warmly with a clear follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Respectful throughout, covers history, transition, goals, health and risk, corrects expectations, and gives clear next steps.","borderline":"Covers most areas but misses fertility, online hormones or assault, or screens without explaining why.","fail":"Misgenders her, pathologises her identity, or over-promises or blocks treatment."}},"c-t-19":{"setup":"Monday morning, community learning disability team. Josh Okonkwo-Banks, 32, with a moderate learning disability, has changed over six weeks. His key worker Declan has come in. Take a collateral history and address his concerns.","role":{"who":"Mr Declan Fairweather, 41, male, Josh's key worker","open":"Yes, Josh's key worker. Thanks for seeing me.","manner":"Caring, tired and keen to help. Talks freely when invited. Becomes anxious about staff safety; relieved when given clear steps.","free":["Josh has become withdrawn and quiet over six weeks","Stays in his room, skips meals, has lost weight","Stopped going to the day centre","Cries at night","Hit a staff member when encouraged out of his room"],"ifasked":["Baseline: cheerful, sings, short sentences, loves the day centre and kitchen","Nothing seems to cheer him up now","GP saw him two days ago: severe constipation; laxatives started; no bowel movement for five days","Head-banging against the wall a few times, no injury","No voices, high mood or odd behaviour","Favourite day centre worker left two months ago","Mother now visits monthly instead of weekly","No bruises, no fear of anyone, no allegations; no epilepsy; no regular medicines"],"worries":["Will he hurt someone again?","What should staff do tonight?","Can't you give him something to calm him?"],"rules":["Mentions constipation only if asked about bowels, pain or recent GP visits","Gets defensive if abuse is asked about bluntly; fine if explained as routine","Visibly relieved if acknowledged as knowing Josh best"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in the learning disability team. Are you Declan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, Josh's key worker. Thanks for seeing me."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming in. Josh has agreed we can talk about him. You know him well, so your view really matters.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I've been really worried about him."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"That comes across. Tell me what you've noticed, and then I'll ask some more detailed questions.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"He's just shut down. Stays in his room, stopped going to the day centre, skips meals. He's lost weight. Last week he hit Sarah when she tried to get him to come down."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds hard for everyone, and I'm sorry Sarah was hurt. We'll come back to that. First, what is Josh like when he's well?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Cheerful. Always singing. He talks in short sentences, but he gets his point across. Loves the day centre and helping in the kitchen."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"What can he do for himself day to day?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Washes and dresses with prompts. Needs help with money and cooking."},{"t":"1:08","band":"What has changed","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice a change?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"About six weeks ago. Gradually, then more and more."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"How is he sleeping?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Badly. Night staff find him crying at two or three in the morning."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"And eating? Do you know how much weight he's lost?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Picks at food. About four kilos."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Is there anything he still enjoys, like his music?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Nothing. He used to put his music on first thing. Now he doesn't bother."},{"t":"1:36","band":"The body first","w":"D","s":"When someone can't easily tell us what's wrong, pain often shows up like this. Any signs of pain: holding his tummy, his jaw, his ears?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Not that I've seen."},{"t":"1:47","w":"D","s":"How are his bowels? Has he seen the GP recently or started anything new?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Oh, actually, the GP saw him two days ago. Said he was very constipated. He's on laxatives but he hasn't been yet."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really important. When did he last open his bowels? Any vomiting or swollen tummy?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Five days, I think. No sick. His tummy's a bit hard."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"That needs looking at again today. Any regular medicines, epilepsy, or problems with his teeth or ears?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"No regular tablets, no epilepsy. Not been to the dentist in a while."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Mind and world","w":"D","s":"Has he seemed to hear voices, been unusually high or giggly, or done anything odd?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Just sad."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Has anything changed around him: staff, visitors, the day centre?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"His favourite worker at the day centre, Kemi, left about two months ago. And his mum's visiting monthly now instead of every week. Work."},{"t":"2:42","w":"D","s":"Those are big losses for him. Has he said anything about them?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"He asked for Kemi a few times. Then stopped asking."},{"t":"2:49","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the head-banging and when he hit Sarah. What happened just before?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"We'd been trying to get him up for lunch. He was crying, Sarah took his arm, and he lashed out. Took two of us to calm him. The head-banging is in his room, maybe four times. No marks."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Has he ever said he doesn't want to live, or tried to hurt himself any other way?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"I ask about everyone in this situation: any bruises, any fear of a particular person or place, anything that worries you about how he's treated?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"(pauses) No. We'd report it. No bruises. He's fine with all the staff."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Thank you; I know it's an uncomfortable question. Is he still washing and drinking enough?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Drinks OK. Washing, only if we really push."},{"t":"3:36","band":"His concerns","w":"D","s":"What worries you most, Declan?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"That he'll do it again. Sarah was shaken. And honestly, can't you just give him something to calm him down?"},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that, after last week. But calming medicine would hide what's wrong. Let's find the cause first; that's what will help him and keep staff safe.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"OK. So what do you think it is?"},{"t":"4:01","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"From what you've described, this sounds most like depression, maybe set off by losing Kemi and seeing less of his mum. But the constipation may be making him feel awful, and it can become dangerous.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"I didn't think constipation could matter that much."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"It really can. I'll ring the GP today. If he vomits, his tummy swells or he's in a lot of pain, take him to A&E. Does that make sense?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Yes. And for tonight?"},{"t":"4:30","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Gentle prompts, not taking his arm. Offer food in his room for now. Keep a simple chart of sleep, meals, bowels and any incidents, with what happened just before.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"We can do that."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"We'll arrange blood tests, a dental check, and our psychologist to look at a behaviour support plan. If the low mood continues, an antidepressant at a small dose may help. Could someone contact his mum about more visits or video calls?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"I'll ring her."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"If he hurts his head, stops eating or drinking, or you can't keep him safe, call us the same day, or 111 out of hours; 999 in an emergency. We'll review in two weeks. Any questions?","m":[11,14]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"No. That's really helpful. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent and invites Declan's account","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges his worry and the assault, and responds to his request for sedation with empathy","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes Josh's baseline personality, communication and abilities","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits six-week change: sleep, appetite, weight, enjoyment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens physical causes: pain, bowels, GP visit, medication, teeth, epilepsy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis and mania","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores changes in people and environment as triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self and to others, including the incident's context and suicidality","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly and sensitively about abuse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains probable depression and why sedation is not the answer","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges urgent constipation review with safety-netting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives staff practical steps and a broader plan","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees review and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets baseline and change, finds the constipation, covers mental illness, triggers, abuse and three-way risk, and leaves staff with a usable plan.","borderline":"Good depression history but misses the constipation or abuse question, or the plan for tonight is vague.","fail":"Puts it down to the learning disability, offers sedation, or never asks about physical health or abuse."}},"c-t-20":{"setup":"Friday morning, community learning disability team. Owen Brightwell, 39, has Down's syndrome and has become low and forgetful over a month. His mother Bernadette asked for an urgent review. Take a collateral history towards a likely diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Ms Bernadette Lyle, 68, female, Owen's mother and carer","open":"Yes. I'm sorry, I'm a bit on edge.","manner":"Anxious and apologetic at first; relaxes when the doctor is warm. Tearful when asked about her own coping. Has read online that Down's means early dementia.","free":["Owen has been low and forgetful for about a month","He muddles the days of the week","He keeps losing his comfort blanket","He got lost once looking for it; a neighbour brought him back"],"ifasked":["Baseline: chatty, cheerful, dresses himself, loves football at the day centre; tests done at 30","Struggling with buttons; still recognises family","Doesn't enjoy football; walks about at night; eating less","Snores loudly and seems to stop breathing sometimes","Hearing and eyes not checked for two years","No fits, jerks, voices or odd behaviour; no new medicines; no changes at home or day centre","She locks the door since he got lost","Arthritis; no regular help; exhausted"],"worries":["Is it dementia?","What happens to him when she can't cope?","Is locking the door wrong?"],"rules":["Apologises and gives short answers until her anxiety is acknowledged","Becomes tearful when asked how she is coping; settles if validated","Pushes for a yes/no on dementia; accepts the explanation if reasons are given"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette, Owen's mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'm sorry, I'm a bit on edge."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable. Owen is happy for us to talk. You know him better than anyone.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"I just want to know what's happening to him."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Let's start with what you've noticed, and I'll ask more as we go.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"He's just not himself. Forgetful, low. It's all happened in a month."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Usual Owen","w":"D","s":"Before this started, what was Owen like at his best?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Chatty, always laughing. Dressed himself, helped me with the washing up. Football at the day centre twice a week; he lived for it."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"Has he ever had a memory or skills assessment, maybe around thirty?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Yes, your team did some tests then."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Memory and function","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful; we can compare. What has he been forgetting?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"The days. He thinks it's Saturday when it's Tuesday. And he keeps losing his blanket. He's had it since he was a boy."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Has he got lost, or struggled with things he used to manage?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"He went out looking for the blanket and got lost. A neighbour brought him back. And he can't do his buttons now."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Does he still recognise family and friends?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Yes, he knows us all."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"Did it come on suddenly, or creep up over the month?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Gradually, but quickly, if that makes sense. Worse each week."},{"t":"1:31","band":"Mood and biology","w":"D","s":"How has his mood been? Does he still enjoy football?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Low. He won't go. Sits in his room."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"How is he sleeping and eating?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Up in the night, walking about. He's eating less too."},{"t":"1:46","band":"Treatable causes","w":"D","s":"Some physical problems can cause exactly this and are treatable. When was his thyroid last checked?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Not for a while. A year or two?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"Any change in his hearing or eyesight? When were they last tested?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"About two years. He does have the telly loud, but he always has."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Does he snore, or seem to stop breathing at night?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Terribly. Sometimes it stops and I hold my breath till he starts again."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's useful. Any recent illness, falls, bumps to the head, new tablets or changes at home or the day centre?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. No tablets. Nothing's changed."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Seizures and psychosis","w":"D","s":"Has he had any fits, sudden jerks, staring spells, or wet himself?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Has he talked to people who aren't there, or seemed frightened of things that aren't real?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Any family history of dementia or depression?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Not that I know."},{"t":"2:42","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Since he got lost, what have you done to keep him safe?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"I lock the front door. I feel awful, like I'm imprisoning him."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"You did it to keep him safe, which is caring. We can look at other ways, like a door alarm. Any worries about the kitchen, roads or his washing?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"He needs more help washing. I don't let him near the cooker now."},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"Any concerns about how anyone treats him, or any thoughts he's mentioned about harming himself?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"No. He's surrounded by people who love him. And never anything like that."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Her coping","w":"D","s":"And how are you managing, Bernadette?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"(tearful) My arthritis is bad. It's just me. I'm sixty-eight. What happens to him when I can't cope?"},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"That's a big worry to carry on your own, and you've done so much for him. You don't have to do it alone. We'll arrange a carer's assessment so you get some help.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"(nods) Thank you. So, is it dementia? I've read people with Down's get it early."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"You're right that the risk is higher. But this has come on fast, his mood is low, and there are treatable causes we haven't checked: his thyroid, hearing, eyes and sleep. Depression alone can look very like memory loss.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"So it might not be dementia?"},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"It might not. We won't know until we've checked those and compared his memory with the tests at thirty, maybe more than once. Does that make sense?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. What happens now?"},{"t":"4:15","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Blood tests including thyroid, a urine test, hearing and eye checks, and a sleep assessment. Our psychologist will repeat his memory tests. If it's depression, there's treatment that helps.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"For safety: a door alarm instead of locking, keep him away from the cooker for now, and fill in a Herbert Protocol form with the police in case he gets lost. If he does go missing, ring 999.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"I didn't know about that."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"I'll see Owen now and then see you both again in four weeks with the results. What questions do you have?","m":[14]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"None. That's a relief. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms consent and invites her account","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her anxiety, guilt and exhaustion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes baseline and asks about a previous cognitive assessment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores memory, function, getting lost, face recognition and speed of onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores mood, enjoyment, sleep and appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens thyroid, hearing, vision, sleep apnoea, illness, falls, medicines and life events","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens seizures, psychosis and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses wandering, home and road safety, abuse and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about her own coping and offers a carer's assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains depression versus early dementia and why tests come first","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines investigations and serial assessment against baseline","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives safety advice: door alarm, cooker, Herbert Protocol, 999","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees follow-up and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Settles her, establishes baseline and a fast decline with low mood, screens treatable causes, seizures and risk, supports her, and explains why dementia is not yet the answer.","borderline":"Good memory history but misses thyroid, senses or sleep, or skips her coping.","fail":"Labels dementia on the day, ignores mood, or misses wandering risk."}},"c-t-21":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, police custody suite. Gareth Mulvaney, 47, was arrested on suspicion of indecent exposure after sunbathing naked in view of his neighbour. The sergeant wants a psychiatric view. Take a history and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Gareth Mulvaney, 47, male, detained person (the custody sergeant joins at the end)","open":"Yes. And I don't see why I need a shrink.","manner":"Angry and defensive, calls the neighbour a prude. Settles and becomes candid, embarrassed, once he is sure the doctor is not police and not judging. Quiet and worried by the end.","free":["Has sunbathed naked in his garden most days for two weeks","Knows the neighbour's kitchen looks on to his garden","Feels he can do what he likes on his own property","Separated from his wife last year and is out of work"],"ifasked":["Knows the neighbour works from home, on her own all day","Being seen by her aroused him; erections several times; started masturbating there recently","Has fantasised about going round to her house but never approached her","Exposed himself to women in the street a few times as a teenager; never caught","Heterosexual, high sex drive, legal online pornography; no interest in children or other paraphilias","Hoped she might like it; accepts she may be frightened","Mood flat but no depressive symptoms; no voices; rarely drinks; no drugs","No previous convictions or mental health history"],"worries":["Will the doctor tell the police everything?","Is he a pervert?","Will he go to prison?"],"rules":["Stays guarded and argumentative until the doctor explains the role and is non-judgemental","Answers intimate questions honestly only if asked plainly and with a reason","Becomes upset if the questions feel like an accusation; settles if validated","Never volunteers the teenage exposures unless asked about the past"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Mulvaney?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. And I don't see why I need a shrink."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"That's fair to ask. I'm not police. My job is to check how you are and whether you need any support.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Fine. Go on."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Anything you tell me may be shared if there's a risk to someone. Is that okay? And have you spoken to a solicitor?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"On the phone, yeah. This is ridiculous. It's my own garden."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"I can hear how angry you are, and being arrested is a real shock. Take me through what's been happening in the garden.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:42","band":"The incident","w":"P","s":"It's been hot. I sunbathe with nothing on. Two weeks, most afternoons. She's a prude, she rang the police."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Where can she see you from? Did you know she was at home?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Her kitchen window looks straight on to it. She works from home, so she's always in."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"So she's on her own there during the day. When you went out, what did you expect would happen if she saw you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I thought she might like what she saw."},{"t":"1:18","band":"Sexual motivation","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight. I know this is embarrassing, but I need to ask everyone in this position some personal questions. Is that all right?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"You're making me sound like some sort of pervert."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that's not my aim. Honest answers are what let me get you the right help. When you were out there, did the thought of her seeing you excite you sexually?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Yes. Knowing she could see me got me going."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Did that lead to an erection, or to touching yourself or masturbating in the garden?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"A few erections. And the last few days, yes, I have."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Do you have sexual thoughts about her? Have you imagined going round, or approaching her?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"I've thought about knocking on her door. I haven't. I've never been near her."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Past pattern","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened before, at any age? Any trouble with the police in the past?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"When I was a teenager I flashed at women in the street a few times. It gave me a buzz. Never caught. Nothing since I met my wife."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Tell me about relationships and your sex life over the years. Any difficulties?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"I was shy with girls. My wife was my first. It was good with her, until it wasn't."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"Do you have any other sexual interests, like watching people, pain, or younger people and children? And do you use pornography?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"God, no, nothing with kids. Normal porn, online, adults. I've got a high sex drive, that's all."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Do you believe she's interested in you? How sure are you? Could she simply be upset by it?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"I hoped she was. But I suppose she could be scared, if she rang the police."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since the separation? Sleep, appetite, enjoying things?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"Flat. Bit lonely. I sleep and eat fine. I get on with it."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Any spells of feeling high, racing, needing little sleep? Hearing voices, or feeling people are against you? Any memory problems?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"No. None of that."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"What's your drinking like, and do you use any drugs? Any past contact with mental health services?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"A can now and then. No drugs. Never seen anyone before."},{"t":"3:46","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever watched her, followed her, or tried to contact her?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"No. I see her over the fence, that's it."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"If you go home today, what will you do about her? And do you spend time around children?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Keep my clothes on, I suppose. No kids in my life."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"This is a lot to deal with. Has it crossed your mind to end your life, now or once you're home?","m":[7,2]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"No. I'm embarrassed, not stupid. My brother's about."},{"t":"4:20","band":"Protective factors","w":"D","s":"How do you think she felt, seeing you? And if I put you in touch with help for these urges, would you go?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Frightened, probably. Yeah, I'd go, if it's private. Are you telling the police all this?"},{"t":"4:35","w":"D","s":"Most of it stays in your health record. The risk to your neighbour I do need to share, and I'll tell you exactly what I pass on.","m":[1,9]},{"t":"4:44","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. Being seen by her excited you, you've masturbated out there, you've thought about going round, and it happened years ago too. Is that fair?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Yes. So am I some kind of pervert?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"No. What you've described has a name, and there's help available alongside whatever the police decide. I don't think a mental illness is causing it.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"I'll ask your GP to refer you to a psychologist, and I'll give you the Stop It Now number for confidential support now. If you feel low, call 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123. What will you do if you feel low?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"Ring my brother. Or that 111 option 2."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Sergeant, no mental illness. He's fit for interview and doesn't need an appropriate adult. It's sexually motivated and aimed at the neighbour, with fantasies of going round, so please factor her safety into bail.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Custody sergeant: Understood. We'll look at a no-contact condition. Report to follow?"},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Yes, within the hour, and a GP letter. Thank you, Gareth, for being so honest with me.","m":[12,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains role as independent of police and the limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Stays non-judgemental and validates anger and embarrassment before intimate questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the incident: duration, visibility, neighbour home alone, his expectation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks plainly about arousal, erections, masturbation and fantasies about the neighbour","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores past exposure, psychosexual history, paraphilias including children, pornography and offences","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction about her interest and screens mood, mania, psychosis, cognition and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk of approach, risk to children, and suicide risk after release","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores victim empathy, supports and willingness to engage","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises findings back and checks accuracy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the opinion honestly and without labels, with hope of help","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives crisis contacts and checks he knows what to do if low","d":"Close"},{"c":"Advises custody on illness, fitness, appropriate adult and risk to neighbour","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets an honest account of arousal and the past pattern, excludes illness and erotomania, and gives custody a clear risk opinion.","borderline":"Covers the incident and mental state but asks about arousal vaguely or misses the past pattern or children.","fail":"Judgemental or police-like, never asks about sexual motivation, or leaves without a risk opinion."}},"c-t-22":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, CMHT clinic. Wesley Thorne, 35, a plasterer, was referred by his GP after asking for sleeping tablets. He lives with his partner, Shona. Take a focused history to reach the likely diagnosis and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Mr Wesley Thorne, 35, male, patient","open":"Yeah. I thought I'd just get some sleeping pills.","manner":"Tense and on edge but willing to talk. Becomes heated if the belief is challenged head-on; calms if listened to. Defensive when confidentiality is raised; grudgingly accepts a plan.","free":["Not sleeping and feels stressed","His partner Shona of three years is having affairs","Smelt men's aftershave on her coat six months ago","This morning she said good morning to the postman 'seductively'"],"ifasked":["Estimates she has had hundreds of lovers","No other possible explanation; completely certain","Checks her phone and follows her; many rows, she denies it","Will get a confession 'one way or another': would use force; has searched online for injectable truth serums","Off sick from work to gather evidence","Five to six cans of beer a day, more since the suspicion began; no drugs","Two-year prison sentence for ABH on an ex-partner; restraining order","No intention to harm the men; no suicidal thoughts; no weapons; no children"],"worries":["Why can't he just have sleeping tablets?","Will the doctor tell Shona?","Will he be locked up?"],"rules":["Raises his voice if told he is imagining it; settles if the doctor says they want to understand","Only explains 'one way or another' if asked directly what he means","Discloses the prison sentence only if asked about past violence or police","Refuses admission but agrees to stay at his brother's if the reason is explained honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Wesley Thorne?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I thought I'd just get some sleeping pills."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"I understand, and weeks of bad sleep is exhausting. Your GP wanted us to look at the stress behind it. Can you tell me about it?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"It's my partner, Shona."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Go on. I'm listening, and I'd like the whole picture.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:24","band":"The stress","w":"P","s":"She's sleeping around. Has been for months."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. When did you first think this, and what happened?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Six months ago she came in from work and her coat stank of men's aftershave."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"What else have you noticed since then?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"She flirts with every man she sees. This morning, the postman. The way she said good morning. Seductive."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How many men do you think she's been involved with?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Hundreds. Easily. Five hundred, maybe."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Belief quality","w":"D","s":"Is there any other way to explain the aftershave, or how she spoke to the postman?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Don't tell me I'm imagining it. I know what I smelt."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"I'm not saying that. I can hear how real this is for you. I just want to understand it. On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"A hundred. There's no other explanation."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Do you have any other worries, like people watching you, plotting against you, or messages meant just for you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"No. It's just her."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Behaviours","w":"D","s":"What have you done to find out the truth? Do you check her phone, or follow her?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"I go through her phone when she's asleep. I follow her after work. I've been off sick so I can watch."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"When you've had it out with her, what happens?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Massive rows. She cries, denies it. Lies to my face. I'll get the truth out of her one way or another."},{"t":"2:04","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"I want to come back to that. First, how's your mood? Low, or ever unusually high?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Stressed. Not down. Not high."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Any voices when nobody's there, or seeing things others can't?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not mad."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"What's your drinking like now, and when did that change? Any drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Five or six cans a night. Since this started. Before that, weekends. No drugs."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Any problems with sex or erections lately?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. That's not it."},{"t":"2:38","band":"Risk to partner","w":"D","s":"You said you'd get the truth one way or another. What exactly do you mean?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"If she won't tell me, I'll make her."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Make her how? Would you use force?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"If I have to. I've been reading about injections. Truth serum. You can get stuff online."},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you got hold of anything? Any weapons at home?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Not yet. No weapons."},{"t":"3:07","band":"Other risks","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt a partner before, or been in trouble with the police?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"I did two years for ABH on my ex. There's a restraining order. That was different."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of harming the men you suspect? Or yourself? Any children living with you or visiting?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Not bothered about them. I'd never hurt myself. No kids."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got this. For six months you've been sure Shona's had hundreds of affairs, you check and follow her, and you'd force a confession. Is that right?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Yeah. So what can you give me?"},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"I can see how tormented you've been. I think an illness is driving this belief. It's called morbid jealousy, and it's treatable, but tablets for sleep won't touch it.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. She's the problem."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"I hear you see it differently. I have to be honest: I'm worried Shona could be badly hurt, and you could end up in prison again. I can't keep that to myself; she needs to know.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"You're going to tell her? Great. That's great."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"I know that's hard to hear, and I'll tell you what we share and why. I'd like my consultant to see you today, and I'd recommend a short hospital stay.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"I'm not staying in hospital."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Then my consultant will see you today to decide the next step, which may include a Mental Health Act assessment. Tonight, could you stay at your brother's rather than at home?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"Fine. My brother's. But I'm not happy."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Just so we're clear, what happens next?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Your boss sees me today, I go to my brother's, you talk to Shona."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"That's right. If things feel out of control, call the crisis line on 111 option 2. Please wait here while I fetch my consultant.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and acknowledges his request for sleep before exploring the stress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates distress and avoids arguing with the belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, evidence and extent of the infidelity belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction and alternative explanations to distinguish delusion from overvalued idea","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens other delusions, hallucinations, mood, alcohol timing, drugs and sexual problems","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits checking, following and confrontations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Clarifies 'one way or another': force, method, truth serum research, weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about past partner violence, police, rivals, self-harm and children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises findings back and checks accuracy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names morbid jealousy plainly and explains why hypnotics alone will not help","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains honestly that partner will be told; arranges same-day senior review and safe accommodation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding of next steps and gives crisis contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Tests the belief, uncovers planned force and past partner violence, and acts the same day to protect Shona.","borderline":"Reaches morbid jealousy but leaves the risk vague or forgets past violence.","fail":"Prescribes for sleep, argues with the belief, or lets him leave with no plan to warn his partner."}},"c-t-23":{"setup":"Friday late afternoon, orthopaedic ward. Margaret Penrose, 64, is three days after lumbar decompression and has become suddenly confused, agitated and shaky. Carry out a mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis. A staff nurse is with you.","role":{"who":"Mrs Margaret Penrose, 64, female, patient (the staff nurse helps; her husband arrives at the end)","open":"Can't you see them? They're everywhere!","manner":"Terrified, restless, sweating and shaking, stamping at the floor. Distractible; settles for short spells when reassured and asked to look at the doctor. Shouts out once in fear.","free":["Can see insects pouring out of the walls and running across the floor","Is terrified and wants them squashed","Does not understand why the doctor cannot see them"],"ifasked":["Can feel insects crawling up her legs under her gown","Feels her life is in danger; no thoughts of self-harm","No voices; nobody controlling her thoughts","Does not know the date, the hospital name, or that the doctor is a doctor","Remembers coming in for back surgery; everything since is 'a blur'","Several bottles of red wine every day since retiring four years ago","Last drink the day before the operation; never stopped before; no drugs; no past mental health problems","Back sore; bowels not opened for days"],"worries":["Why can't anyone see them?","Is she going mad?","Will they go away?"],"rules":["Becomes more agitated if told the insects are not real; settles if the doctor says they believe she sees them","Can answer a question or two if asked to look at the doctor's face first","Minimises drinking at first; gives the real amount if asked kindly and specifically","Shouts out once, around the middle; calms with reassurance"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Margaret Penrose?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Can't you see them? They're everywhere!"},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"I can see you're very frightened, Margaret. You're safe here, and I'm staying with you.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"On the floor. Look. Look!"},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Can you look at me for a moment? Just at my face. I'd like to ask you a few questions.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"(looks up briefly) All right. Quickly."},{"t":"0:28","band":"Appearance and behaviour","w":"D","s":"I notice you're shaking and sweating, and you keep stamping your feet. You seem to find it hard to keep still.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"I have to squash them before they reach me."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"You're speaking very fast. Take your time; there's no rush.","m":[3,2]},{"t":"0:45","band":"Mood and thoughts","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling in yourself right now?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Terrified. Something awful is going to happen. I can feel it."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Do you feel your life is in danger?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Yes. I think I'm going to die here."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"Any urge to hurt yourself, or to get off the ward and away?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"No, I don't want to hurt myself. I just want them gone."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Is anyone trying to harm you, or putting thoughts into your head, or controlling you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody. It's the insects."},{"t":"1:23","band":"Perception","w":"D","s":"Tell me what you're seeing. Where are they coming from?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Beetles. Hundreds. Pouring out of that wall, across the floor towards me."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"I can't see them myself, but I believe you're seeing them, and that must be horrible. Can you feel anything on your skin?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"(shouts) They're on my legs! Get them off! Get them off me!"},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Margaret, I'm here. The nurse is here. I'm holding your hand. Breathe with me. Look at my face.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"(breathing) They crawl up under my gown."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Do you hear anything, voices or sounds, that other people don't?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No. Just them scuttling."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Cognition","w":"D","s":"What's the date today? And what is this place?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Some hospital. I don't know which."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Who do you think I am?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Are you a policeman?"},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"I'm a doctor. Starting from December, can you go back through the months?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"December, November... September? I've lost it. They're moving again."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"That's all right. What do you remember since your operation?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"I remember the operation. Since then it's all a blur."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Alcohol history","w":"D","s":"Margaret, when did you last have a drink of alcohol?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"The night before I came in. I only have a glass or two, like everyone."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Lots of people find this hard to talk about. It really helps me treat you safely: how many bottles would you get through on a usual day?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Two, three. Red. Since I retired. Four years, I suppose."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Have you ever stopped before and had the shakes, a fit, or seen things? Any other drugs or medicines?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"Never stopped. Nothing else. Never seen a psychiatrist."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Physical","w":"D","s":"Any pain in your back or your tummy? Any trouble passing water or opening your bowels?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"My back's sore. I haven't been for days."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Staff nurse, could you get a full set of obs and a blood glucose now, please?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Staff nurse: Pulse 118, BP 168 over 96, temperature 37.6, sats 97, glucose 5.8."},{"t":"3:41","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Margaret, I think your body is reacting to suddenly stopping the wine after drinking a lot for years. It's serious, but it's very treatable.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Will they go away? Am I going mad?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"No, you're not going mad. As the treatment works, the insects should fade. You'll have medicine to calm the shaking and vitamins through a drip, and a nurse will stay close.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Don't leave me."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"The nurse will stay with you. Staff nurse, this looks like delirium tremens. Please bleep the orthopaedic registrar now: lorazepam per protocol, IV Pabrinex before any glucose, bloods including magnesium, fluids, hourly obs, side room near the desk.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Staff nurse: Bleeping now. Her husband's just arrived."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Mr Penrose, I'm Dr Chinonso from psychiatry. Margaret is very confused and frightened because of alcohol withdrawal. The team are starting treatment now.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Husband: Is it the anaesthetic? I should have told them how much she drinks."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"It's not the anaesthetic. You couldn't have known this would happen, and you're helping now. Can you tell me what you understand so far?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Husband: It's withdrawal from the wine, it's serious, and they're treating it now."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write the plan in her notes and come back tomorrow. If she gets worse or has a fit, the team will call us straight away.","m":[11,12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, reassures, lowers stimulation and redirects her attention to engage her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear throughout and stays calm during her outburst","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Comments on appearance, behaviour and speech as observed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores mood, sense of doom, thought content, passivity and risk to self","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits visual and tactile hallucinations and screens auditory, without colluding","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation, attention and memory since surgery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes alcohol history sensitively: amount, duration, last drink, past withdrawal, drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens physical causes and asks for observations and glucose","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the likely cause to her in plain, reassuring words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names delirium tremens and hands over an urgent plan including benzodiazepine and thiamine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Updates husband kindly, addresses guilt and checks his understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with follow-up and escalation if she deteriorates","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Settles her, covers each mental state domain, finds the alcohol history and acts on delirium tremens as a medical emergency.","borderline":"Covers perceptions and cognition but takes a vague alcohol history or a vague medical plan.","fail":"Calls it psychosis, argues with or colludes with the hallucinations, or never asks about alcohol."}},"c-t-24":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Natalia Petrenko, 49, a school cook, was referred by her GP for odd beliefs about her neighbour. The letter mentions heavy drinking and abnormal liver tests. Take a focused psychiatric history to establish the likely diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Mrs Natalia Petrenko, 49, female, patient","open":"Yes. I don't know why the doctor sent me here.","manner":"Friendly, open and relaxed, a little puzzled. Becomes sad and quiet when her husband is mentioned; warms if this is acknowledged. Relieved when the explanation makes sense.","free":["Hears her neighbour singing nursery rhymes through the keyhole at night","Opens the door and nobody is there","Recognises his voice and doesn't know why he'd torment her; they used to get on","Hasn't been sleeping much"],"ifasked":["About four years of two bottles of red daily, since her husband died","Never feels drunk and it has never affected her work","Stopped completely a week ago on GP advice after abnormal liver tests","Shaky and sick for two or three days after stopping; it settled","The singing began a few days after she stopped","No other voices, visions, feelings of being controlled or persecuted; mood fine","Oriented, no memory problems; no medicines, drugs or family history","Has not confronted the neighbour and won't; no thoughts of self-harm"],"worries":["Why has she been sent to a psychiatrist?","Is she going mad?","What should she do about the neighbour?"],"rules":["Volunteers nothing about drinking unless asked specifically how much","Becomes tearful when her husband's death comes up; settles if validated","Says she is not an alcoholic because she never gets drunk","Accepts the explanation readily if it is given in plain words"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Petrenko?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. I don't know why the doctor sent me here."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. Your GP was concerned about how you've been sleeping and some worries about your neighbour. Can you tell me about that?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"He sings to me at night."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd like to hear it all, and then ask about some other parts of your life.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","band":"The experiences","w":"D","s":"What exactly do you hear, and when?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"Nursery rhymes. Every night, once I'm in bed. Through the keyhole in my front door."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"That must be unsettling, night after night. Is it as clear as my voice is now?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Just as clear. Like he's right outside."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"When you open the door, what do you find?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Nobody. The landing is empty. Then I go back to bed and it starts again."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"How do you know it's him? And why do you think he'd do that?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"I know his voice. I don't know why. We always got on. I haven't said anything to him."},{"t":"1:06","band":"Nature of voices","w":"D","s":"Do you hear any other voices, or see or feel things other people don't?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"No. Just him singing."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Do you feel anyone is against you, or that your thoughts are being interfered with or controlled?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"When did the singing start?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Four nights ago, maybe five."},{"t":"1:27","band":"Alcohol history","w":"D","s":"Your GP mentioned your liver tests. What do you usually drink, and how much on a typical day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Red wine. But I'm not an alcoholic; I never get drunk."},{"t":"1:38","w":"D","s":"Lots of people who drink every day don't feel drunk; the body gets used to it. Roughly how many bottles in a day?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Two bottles. Most days."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"How long has it been that much, and what changed?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"About four years. Since my husband died. The evenings are so empty. (tearful)"},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Losing him must have changed everything, and the evenings sound like the hardest part. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Thank you. A glass with him after work, then it was a bottle, then two."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Did you ever drink first thing to steady yourself, or struggle to cut down?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"I never tried to cut down. Not in the morning."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Stopping drinking","w":"D","s":"When did you last have a drink?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"A week ago today. The GP said my liver was bad, so I stopped completely."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Well done for stopping. Afterwards, did you have shakes, sweats, fits, or feel muddled?","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Shaky and sick for two or three days. Then it passed. No fits. Never muddled."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"So you stopped a week ago, the shakes went, and the singing started a few nights later. Is that right?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Yes. I hadn't thought of it like that."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Mental state and causes","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Are you still enjoying things?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Good, apart from the sleep. I love my job with the children."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"What's the date today, and what's this place? Any trouble with memory?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Monday the ninth of November. The mental health clinic. My memory's fine."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Any illnesses, regular medicines, street drugs, or anyone in the family with mental illness?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Nothing. No medicines. Never any drugs. No one in the family."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about having it out with him? Any thoughts of harming him, or yourself?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"No. I hate arguments. Never anything like that."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Putting it together: two bottles a day for years, stopping suddenly a week ago, and the singing starting a few days later.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"So am I going mad?"},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"No, you're not going mad. When someone stops after years of heavy drinking, the brain can produce voices that sound completely real. It's called alcoholic hallucinosis, and it usually fades if you stay off drink.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"So it's the wine. That makes sense, strangely. What do I do about my neighbour?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Please don't confront him; I think the voice comes from your brain, not from him. I'll ask your GP to start vitamin B1 and check bloods, refer you to the alcohol service and bereavement support, and see you in two weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"That sounds sensible."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"If you become shaky, confused or have a fit, go to A&E or call 999. If the voices get worse, call us or 111 option 2. What will you do if things change?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"If I get shaky or muddled, A&E. If the singing gets worse, ring you or 111."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I appreciate you being so open, Natalia.","m":[12,1]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains reason for referral and puts her at ease","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates distress, including grief, and normalises drinking questions without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Characterises the voice: content, timing, source, clarity, her explanation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens other hallucinations, persecutory ideas, thought interference and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies alcohol: type, amount, duration, trigger, dependence features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes last drink, withdrawal symptoms, and orientation to exclude delirium tremens","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, medical illness, medicines, drugs and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to the neighbour and to herself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises the timeline back to her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains alcoholic hallucinosis in plain words with reassurance","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a clear plan: thiamine, bloods, alcohol service, bereavement support, review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets for withdrawal or worsening voices and checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Characterises the voice, builds the drinking-to-voices timeline, excludes delirium tremens and explains hallucinosis plainly.","borderline":"Finds the drinking but misses the timeline or the check for confusion and withdrawal.","fail":"Never asks about alcohol, or labels it schizophrenia without the timeline."}},"c-t-25":{"setup":"Thursday late morning, psychiatry outpatients. Toby Mensah, 24, a bakery chef with contamination OCD and cracked hands, has had fluoxetine for 12 weeks, now 40 mg. Discuss a treatment plan, focusing on ERP and CBT.","role":{"who":"Mr Tobias (Toby) Mensah, 24, male, patient","open":"Yes. Toby.","manner":"Embarrassed, low and preoccupied, little eye contact; uses alcohol gel from his belt after touching the chair. Warms when not judged; anxious at the idea of exposure; hopeful by the end.","free":["Wipes every surface twice with detergent after each batch","Stays late scrubbing colleagues' areas","Washes hands repeatedly; they are cracked and bleed","Finds the clinic dirty and hard to concentrate in"],"ifasked":["Fears customers or he will catch HIV from surfaces","Knows it is somewhat silly; urge about 9 out of 10","About two extra hours a day; job nearly impossible","Cracked skin makes him more scared of HIV getting in","Mood low for about two months; no thoughts of harming himself or ending his life","Taking fluoxetine every day; slight benefit; wants to continue","No voices or unusual experiences; drinks little","His sister knows about the problem"],"worries":["Will the therapy actually stop it?","Can therapy be at home, at work or by phone or video?","Can he stay on fluoxetine?"],"rules":["Becomes more anxious if told to stop cleaning completely; settles if steps are small and his choice","Loses the thread if given long explanations; follows well if understanding is checked","Says 'that sounds mad' about exposure; accepts it if validated and explained","Asks about home or video sessions at some point"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tobias Mensah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Toby."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Toby. Your GP told me a bit about the cleaning and your hands. That must be really hard to talk about.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"It's embarrassing, to be honest. (uses gel from his belt)"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"You're not alone in this. Can we go over how things are now, then talk through treatment?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"OK. Sorry, this place feels dirty. It's hard to think."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; I'll keep it simple and check in as we go.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","band":"Obsessions and compulsions","w":"D","s":"What goes through your mind when you look at a counter after a batch?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"That there's germs. That a customer could get HIV from it."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"And what do you do then? How long does the relief last?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Wipe it twice with detergent. Every batch. Then my hands. A minute's peace, then it starts again."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Part of you thinks it's over the top. Out of ten, how likely is HIV from a counter, really?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"Maybe three? I know it's silly, but I can't risk it. The urge is like a nine."},{"t":"1:07","band":"Impact and risk","w":"D","s":"How many hours a day does it eat up, and how are your hands?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Two extra hours. I stay late. They crack and bleed, and then I worry HIV gets in through the cracks."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting and painful. How's your mood been? Sleep, appetite?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Down. A couple of months. I eat and sleep OK."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Any times you've felt life isn't worth it, or wanted to hurt yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never do that. I just want it gone."},{"t":"1:36","band":"Name it and explain","w":"D","s":"What you're describing is OCD. It's common, and it's very treatable. Can I draw how it works?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"You see the counter, the HIV thought comes, anxiety shoots up, you clean, it drops. So your brain learns cleaning is the only way, and next time the urge is stronger. Does that fit?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"That's exactly it. It never stops."},{"t":"2:01","band":"How ERP works","w":"D","s":"The main treatment is called ERP: exposure and response prevention. You face the worry, but without doing the cleaning.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"You want me to touch dirty things on purpose? That sounds mad."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"It does sound odd, I agree. You'd never start with the hardest thing. We'd build a ladder together, from easiest to hardest. What might be near the bottom?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Touching a counter and waiting a few minutes before washing, maybe."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Perfect. And leaving a colleague's area unscrubbed might be higher up. Each time, you stay with the anxiety. It rises, then falls on its own, and the fear gets weaker.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Will it actually stop it, though?"},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"It's the treatment with the best evidence for exactly this, and most people improve a great deal. I'll be honest: anxiety goes up at first, before it comes down.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:54","band":"CBT around it","w":"D","s":"Alongside ERP, CBT looks at the thoughts: how responsible you feel for everyone's safety, and the facts about HIV.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"What facts?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"HIV isn't passed on through worktops, food or touch. The therapist would help you test your predictions with small experiments, with homework between weekly sessions.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Can it be at home or by phone? I can't focus here."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Practical plan","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. Video sessions and some in your kitchen are possible, and doing ERP at work actually suits it. I'll ask for that. It's usually weekly, more than ten hours in all.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Can I keep taking the fluoxetine?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Yes. It's had only a few weeks at 40 mg. Medicine plus therapy works better than either alone, and we can raise it to 60 mg later if needed. Any side effects?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Bit of nausea at the start. Fine now. I take it every day."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Good. For your hands, your GP can give an emollient and a soap substitute, and a steroid cream if needed. Healing skin will ease the fear too.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:59","band":"Work and support","w":"D","s":"Would it help if occupational health agreed what normal cleaning looks like in your kitchen, and gloves while your hands heal?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. A clear standard would really help."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"OCD-UK has good information and support. Would you like your family to know about the plan?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"My sister knows. She could help."},{"t":"4:19","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"I've said a lot. Just to check I've explained it well, how would you describe ERP back to me?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"I face it in small steps, I don't clean, and the fear drops by itself."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So: a therapy referral for CBT with ERP, some at work or by video, fluoxetine continued, skin cream, and occupational health. I'll see you in six weeks.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"OK. That feels like a plan."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"If your mood drops or you think of harming yourself, call us or 111 option 2. You've done well talking about this today.","m":[12,2]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and agrees the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Normalises embarrassment and validates distress, without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reviews obsessions, compulsions, insight and daily impact","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names OCD and explains the anxiety-ritual cycle, checking fit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains ERP with a personal ladder and honest warning about rising anxiety","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains CBT elements: responsibility, HIV facts, experiments, homework","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds flexibly to request for sessions outside clinic; gives frequency and duration","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Continues fluoxetine with dose plan and addresses skin care","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees work support with occupational health and family involvement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Summarises plan, review date and safety net","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Checks symptoms and risk, explains the OCD cycle, ERP and CBT with his own examples, and agrees a flexible practical plan.","borderline":"Explains ERP accurately but generically, or skips risk or checking understanding.","fail":"Lectures without engaging him, tells him to stop cleaning outright, or misses mood and risk."}},"c-t-26":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, community eating disorder clinic. Hannah Mwangi, 24, has two years of bingeing and vomiting; BMI 22, observations normal. Explain the diagnosis and the treatment options. No risk assessment is needed.","role":{"who":"Ms Hannah Mwangi, 24, female, account manager, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Embarrassed and quiet, avoids eye contact at first. Relaxes when the doctor is matter-of-fact. Anxious about weight gain and work finding out. Brightens when the cycle makes sense.","free":["Skips breakfast and lunch on workdays, goes to the gym at lunchtime","Most evenings eats dinner then cereal, crisps and chocolate quickly, feeling out of control","Feels disgusted and guilty afterwards and makes herself sick with her fingers","Worried about her weight since her teens; tried several diets at university"],"ifasked":["Vomits every weekday, but eats regularly at weekends and does not vomit then","No laxatives, diuretics or diet pills; no diabetes","Turns down dinners with friends; checks herself in the mirror and thinks she is too big","Has severe heartburn, puffy cheeks and hard skin on the knuckles of her right hand","No palpitations, fainting or cramps; has not seen a dentist for two years","Drinks after work twice a week, about four drinks each time"],"worries":["Will treatment make her put on weight?","Will her employer find out?","Is there a tablet to stop the binges?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful and says 'I'm disgusting' if asked directly about vomiting before rapport is built; settles if validated","Resists eating in the day until the cycle has been explained using her own week","Says 'that's literally my week' if the cycle is drawn clearly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, and reduces shame with a non-judgemental tone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her embarrassment and distress before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies binges, loss of control and the weekday-weekend pattern","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about all purging: vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, exercise, fasting","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores weight and shape concerns and their effect on self-worth","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names bulimia nervosa and explains why anorexia and binge eating disorder do not fit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the restrict-binge-purge cycle using her own week","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains physical risks, including potassium and teeth, and arranges a blood test","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines guided self-help, regular eating and CBT-ED as next step","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States medication is not first-line and addresses weight-gain fear honestly","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with referral, Beat, review date and urgent warning signs","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Hannah Mwangi?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Hannah. Your GP shared a bit about the bingeing and being sick. I know that can be hard to talk about.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"I'm so embarrassed."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"There's nothing to be embarrassed about here. Lots of people go through this, and it's very treatable. Can I check a few things, then explain what I think is happening?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:30","band":"The pattern","w":"D","s":"Could you walk me through a normal weekday, food-wise, from when you wake up?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"No breakfast. Gym at lunch instead of eating. Then I get home starving, have dinner, and then I just keep going. Cereal, crisps, chocolate. Whatever's there."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"While that's happening, how much do you feel in control?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Not at all. It's like I'm watching myself. Then I feel disgusting and I make myself sick."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"That sounds really distressing, and thank you for telling me so honestly. How often are you sick, and what about weekends?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"Every weekday now. Weekends are weirdly fine. I eat proper meals with my flatmate and don't need to."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Apart from being sick, do you use anything else to get rid of food or calories: laxatives, water tablets, diet pills, or extra exercise?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Just the gym. And not eating in the day. No pills."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"How much does your weight or shape affect how you feel about yourself as a person?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Massively. I check myself in the mirror all the time. I've stopped going to dinners with friends because I can't eat in front of people."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"That must be lonely, missing out on time with friends. Have you noticed any physical changes: heartburn, swelling, palpitations, dizziness or cramps?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"Awful heartburn. My cheeks look puffy. And hard skin here on my knuckles. No palpitations."},{"t":"1:59","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you're describing fits a condition called bulimia nervosa. That means binges where you lose control, then trying to undo them, here by being sick, with your weight and shape affecting how you feel about yourself.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"So it's an actual thing. I thought I was just weak."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"It's an actual thing, and not weakness at all. It isn't anorexia, because your weight is in the healthy range. And it's more than binge eating disorder, because of the vomiting.","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"2:32","band":"The cycle","w":"D","s":"Can I show you how it keeps going? When you skip food all day, your body is starving by evening, so it pushes you to eat a lot, fast. That's the binge.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"Then the guilt hits, you're sick, and you promise yourself you'll eat less tomorrow. So you skip meals again, and round it goes. Notice your weekends: when you eat regularly, there are no binges.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"Oh my God. That's literally my week."},{"t":"3:04","band":"Physical effects","w":"D","s":"It's very common. Being sick also matters for your body. It can lower your potassium, which your heart needs for a steady rhythm. The acid wears your teeth and throat, which is the heartburn, and the puffy cheeks are swollen salivary glands.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"That's scary. My BMI's normal, though."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"I understand that's reassuring, and your weight being stable is good. But these effects happen at any weight. I'd like to check your potassium with a blood test today. Is that OK?","m":[8,2]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"Yes, fine."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Also, after being sick, rinse with water and wait an hour before brushing, as brushing straight away scrubs the acid in. And please register with a dentist.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"3:49","band":"Treatment options","w":"D","s":"The good news is that treatment works well. The first step is guided self-help: a book-based programme on bulimia with short sessions with a therapist, about four to nine over four months. Some can be by phone around work.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"And what does it actually get me to do?"},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"The most powerful part sounds simple: eating regularly. Three meals and two or three snacks, with no gap over three or four hours. And a food and mood diary to spot what sets binges off.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"But if I eat breakfast and lunch, I'll put on loads of weight."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"That's the worry almost everyone has, and it makes sense. Being sick doesn't get rid of most of what you've eaten, so the binges are where the calories come from. When people eat regularly, binges fall and weight usually stays fairly steady. I won't promise weight loss; the aim is to get you out of this cycle.","m":[10,2]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"OK. That makes sense, actually."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"If guided self-help isn't helping after about four weeks, we move to a fuller therapy called CBT for eating disorders, about 20 weekly sessions.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Is there a tablet that just stops the binges?"},{"t":"5:01","band":"Medication and checks","w":"D","s":"Tablets aren't the first treatment. Therapy works better. If therapy alone isn't enough, a medicine called fluoxetine can be added, at a higher dose than for depression, but never on its own.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"I'd rather not take tablets anyway. Will work find out about any of this?"},{"t":"5:19","w":"D","s":"That's a really understandable worry. No, this stays between you, our team and your GP unless you decide otherwise. If you ever want support at work, we can help you plan that.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Good. Thank you."},{"t":"5:33","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up, in your words, the main points and what you'll try first?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:39","w":"P","s":"It's bulimia. Starving in the day makes me binge, then I'm sick, then I starve again. So I start with breakfast and lunch, do the diary, and get my blood tested."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. I'll refer you for guided self-help today, write to your GP, and give you details for Beat, the eating disorder charity, which has a helpline and online groups.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"If you get palpitations, faint, have bad cramps or vomit blood, call 111 or 999 straight away. We'll see you again in four weeks. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:18","w":"P","s":"No. I actually feel a bit hopeful. Thank you."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"You've done the hardest part by coming in. Take care, Hannah.","m":[2,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Makes it safe to talk, confirms the full pattern including all purging, names bulimia with differentials, explains the cycle and physical risk, and agrees guided self-help with regular eating.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and treatment, but delivered as a lecture, misses other purging, or omits potassium and dental advice.","fail":"Judgemental or shaming tone, offers tablets as the main treatment, or promises weight loss."}},"c-t-27":{"setup":"Sunday, 4:20 in the morning, the section 136 suite. Declan Ferris, 30, was found by police walking in a main road at 3am, smelling of cannabis. He has no psychiatric history. Carry out a mental state assessment.","role":{"who":"Mr Declan Ferris, 30, male, patient","open":"Declan. Why am I here?","manner":"Frightened and watchful, keeps glancing at the door. Guarded if rushed; opens up when believed and not argued with. Becomes tearful when asked how long he's felt like this.","free":["People outside his flat have been shouting at him for three weeks","Ran out of the flat tonight to get away from them","Hasn't slept properly for weeks","Lost his job as a delivery driver two months ago"],"ifasked":["It started when a car flashed its lights outside; he suddenly knew a gang had decided he was a 'grass'","Several men's voices call him a grass and say they'll get him; they talk to him directly; no commands","Can faintly hear them now, in the suite","The TV news ticker sends him warnings","Smokes skunk, about five joints a day, more since losing his job; last joint at 10pm; four cans of lager a week; no other drugs","Keeps a hammer by his front door in case they break in; has never used it on anyone","No suicidal thoughts; ran into the road only to escape"],"worries":["Why is he here, and is he in trouble?","Is he going to be locked up?","Can his sister be told?"],"rules":["Pauses mid-sentence and looks at the door when the voices get louder; answers honestly if asked what he is hearing","Clams up if told the voices are not real","Accepts help to sleep and feel safe, but insists the threat is real"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks for injuries, and explains section 136 simply","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his fear and avoids challenging his beliefs","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Notes behaviour and asks sensitively when he appears to be listening","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and elation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits persecutory beliefs, their content, onset and conviction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies the delusional perception and delusions of reference","d":"Content"},{"c":"Characterises voices: number, person, content, commands, location, present now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens other hallucinations and thought interference","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies cannabis type, amount, last use, and screens alcohol and other drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation and attention, and assesses insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicide, accidental harm and defensive violence, including weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises honestly and explains the MHA assessment and next steps","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Can I check your name?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Declan. Why am I here?"},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"The police brought you here because they were worried about your safety on the road tonight. Are you hurt anywhere?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"No. They were shouting at me."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. You're on something called section 136. It just means a doctor and a social worker check how you are. You haven't done anything wrong. I want to understand what's been going on. Could you tell me about tonight?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"I had to get out. They were outside the flat, screaming. I just ran. I didn't even see the road."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Appearance and behaviour","w":"D","s":"I notice you keep looking at the door. Is something worrying you right now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Just checking. In case they come."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"You're safe in this room, and staff are right outside. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:54","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How have you been feeling in yourself, in your mood?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Scared. All the time. Not sad, just scared."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"How's your sleep been? And have there been times you felt unusually high, or full of energy?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"I barely sleep. I sit up listening. No, nothing like high."},{"t":"1:13","band":"Thoughts","w":"D","s":"Who do you think is outside, and what do they want?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"A gang from the estate. They think I'm a grass. They're going to get me."},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"How long has this been going on?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Three weeks. Maybe a bit more."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Three weeks of feeling hunted. That's exhausting. Do you remember the very first thing that made you realise they were after you?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"A car flashed its lights outside my window. And I just knew. Right then. They'd decided I was a grass."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Do things on the TV, radio or online ever seem to be about you, or meant for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"The news. The bit that scrolls along the bottom. It warns me where they'll be."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"How sure are you about all this, from nought to a hundred?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"A hundred. It's real."},{"t":"2:04","band":"Perception","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the shouting. Whose voices are they, and how many?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Men. Three, four. They call me a grass, a rat. Say they'll cut me."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"Do they talk to you directly, or about you to each other? Do they ever tell you to do things?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"To me. 'You're dead, rat.' They don't tell me to do stuff. (He stops and stares at the door.)"},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"You've gone quiet. Are you hearing them now?","m":[3,7]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Faint. They followed me."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"That must be scary, hearing them even here. Have you noticed anything else odd: smells, tastes, seeing things, feelings on your skin, or thoughts being put into or taken out of your head?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"No. Just the voices and the news."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Cannabis","w":"D","s":"The police mentioned cannabis. How much do you smoke, and what kind?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Skunk. Five joints a day, maybe more. It calms me down."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Has that gone up recently? And when did you last have some?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Since I lost my job. Two months. Last one about ten tonight."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol, cocaine, spice or anything else?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Few cans a week. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Cognition and insight","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what day it is, and where we are?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Sunday. Early hours. The hospital."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Some people find stress, no sleep or strong cannabis can make the mind play tricks. Could that be part of this?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"No. It's real. I'm not making it up."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"I believe you're hearing them, and I'm not saying you're making it up. Would you accept help to sleep and feel safer?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I just want to sleep."},{"t":"3:47","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you ran into the road, were you trying to hurt yourself? Any thoughts of wanting to be dead, or of suicide?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"No. I was getting away. I'd never kill myself."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about protecting yourself from them? Do you have anything at home for that?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"There's a hammer by my door. In case they come in."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you ever used it or taken it out with you? Any thoughts of going after them?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not like that. I just want them to leave me alone."},{"t":"4:23","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Declan. You've been through a lot. I think your mind has been under huge strain, and the strong cannabis may be adding to it. You need more help than you can get at home tonight.","m":[12,2]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Am I getting locked up?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Later this morning a social worker and another doctor will see you together and decide the best plan with you. Hospital is possible. Until then you'll stay here, safe, and we'll help you rest.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Can my sister know where I am?"},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Yes, with your permission I'll ring her. Is there anything you'd like to ask me?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"No. Just make them stop."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"We'll do everything we can to help with that. Try to rest; staff are right outside.","m":[2,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Calms and orients him, elicits delusions including the delusional perception, characterises voices and checks them now, quantifies cannabis, checks cognition and insight, and covers accidental, self and defensive risk.","borderline":"Finds delusions and voices but misses the onset moment, other senses, or the weapon question.","fail":"Argues with his beliefs, labels it a drug reaction without exploring it, or leaves out risk."}},"c-t-28":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, a quiet room in a supported hostel. Marcus Adeyemi, 43, with paranoid schizophrenia, was discharged six weeks ago and has withdrawn to his room for three weeks. Take a history of events since discharge and assess his mental state.","role":{"who":"Mr Marcus Adeyemi, 43, male, patient","open":"Yeah. What's this about?","manner":"Wary and guarded, short answers at first. Relaxes if not blamed. Becomes irritated if pushed about hospital. Accepts help once his weight concern is taken seriously.","free":["Other residents are loud and leave the kitchen filthy","Stays in his room and orders takeaways","Sleep has been poor for about three weeks","Does not want to go back to hospital"],"ifasked":["Stopped olanzapine about a month ago because he gained two stone","Believes staff open and read his post before he gets it; quite sure","For two weeks a man's voice comments on what he does ('He's getting up now'); no commands","Last relapse started with poor sleep and staying in, then thinking neighbours were watching","Not depressed, but cannot be bothered with much","Smokes cigarettes by his window; has thought about leaving the hostel","Two or three cans of beer a week, no cannabis for years"],"worries":["Is he being sent back to hospital?","Will a new medicine make him put on more weight?","Have the staff been talking about him?"],"rules":["Becomes curt if the first question is about stopping his tablets","Says 'I'm not going back to hospital' when admission is hinted at; calms if reassured","Agrees to a new medicine if weight gain is acknowledged and addressed"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the visit, and puts him at ease","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and fears without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores routine, stresses, sleep and eating since discharge","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about adherence and side effects without blame, and screens substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Compares current changes with his previous relapse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits persecutory beliefs about staff with conviction and actions taken","d":"Content"},{"c":"Characterises the voice and screens for commands and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, enjoyment and drive, and asks why he withdraws","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicide, harm to others, self-neglect, fire and leaving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a medication change addressing weight, with monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, arranges follow-up and gives crisis contacts","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Adeyemi?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yeah. What's this about?"},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me, Marcus. The staff here say you've mostly stayed in your room lately, and they wanted to check you're alright.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Have they been talking about me?"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"They're concerned, that's all, and I'd rather hear it from you. Could you tell me how things have been since you left the ward?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Fine at first. Then it got noisy here."},{"t":"0:30","band":"Since discharge","w":"D","s":"Noisy how? What's a normal day like for you now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"The others play music all night. Kitchen's filthy. So I stay in my room, order takeaways, watch stuff on my phone."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"That sounds really wearing. How have you been sleeping and eating?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Sleep's bad. Three, four hours. Food's one takeaway a day, if that."},{"t":"0:54","band":"Medication","w":"D","s":"How are you getting on with your olanzapine? Lots of people stop medicines when the side effects bother them.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"I stopped it. About a month ago. I put on two stone on that stuff. None of my clothes fit."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That's a lot of weight, and it's a really common reason to stop. It matters, and we can do something about it. Any cannabis, alcohol or other drugs lately?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Few cans a week. No weed, not for years."},{"t":"1:29","band":"Early warning signs","w":"D","s":"Thinking back to before your last admission, what were the first signs that things were slipping?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"I stopped sleeping. Stayed in. Then I got the idea the neighbours were watching me."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Have any of those come back now?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"The sleep, and staying in. I suppose."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"What do you make of that yourself?","m":[9]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"I'm stressed. That's all. Anyone would be, living here."},{"t":"1:55","band":"Psychotic symptoms","w":"D","s":"Has anyone here been bothering you, or doing things behind your back?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Staff open my post. Before I get it. The envelopes are all creased."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"That must feel like you've got no privacy at all. How sure are you that it's the staff?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Certain. Who else would it be?"},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"What have you done about it?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I just don't talk to them. I don't eat with them either."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people are stressed they hear voices when no one's around. Has that happened to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"There's a man. Started a couple of weeks ago. He says what I'm doing. 'He's getting up now. He's going to the window.'"},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Does he ever talk to you directly, or tell you to do things? And do you ever feel someone else is controlling your thoughts or movements?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"No. He just talks about me. Nobody controls me."},{"t":"2:48","band":"Mood and drive","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any sense that things are hopeless?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Not depressed. Fed up."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Do you still enjoy anything? And is staying in your room more about feeling safe, or about not having the energy?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Safe, mostly. But I can't be bothered with much. I used to like going to the gym."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"Do you think the man's voice and the post could be linked to stopping the olanzapine, like last time?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Maybe the voice. The post is real."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things feel like this, do you ever think of harming yourself or ending your life?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Have you felt like getting back at the staff for the post?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never hurt anyone. I just keep away."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke in your room? And have you thought about leaving the hostel?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"By the window. I've thought about going, yeah. Somewhere quieter."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Marcus. I think stopping the olanzapine plus the stress here has brought some of the old symptoms back, early. The good news is we've caught it early.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"So what happens now? I'm not going back to hospital."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"I hear you, Marcus, and our aim is to get you feeling settled here, not to rush you back onto the ward.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"There's a medicine called aripiprazole. Most people put on much less weight with it than with olanzapine. One tablet each morning. We'd check your weight and bloods. Would you try it?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"If it won't fatten me up. I'll try it."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Some people feel a bit restless on it at first; if you do, tell staff. Your care coordinator will visit twice a week, and the staff will help with the tablets if that's OK with you.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up for me what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"New tablet in the morning, the coordinator comes twice a week, and I tell someone if it gets worse."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If the voice starts telling you to do things or you feel unsafe, tell staff or ring the crisis line, or 111 and press 2. And please smoke outside, not in your room. Anything you'd like to ask?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for listening about the weight."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"It mattered. Take care, Marcus. I'll see you next week.","m":[2,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Builds trust, establishes stopping olanzapine and why, compares with the last relapse, elicits the persecutory belief and commenting voice, separates fear from low mood, covers risk including fire, and agrees an acceptable plan.","borderline":"Finds non-adherence and symptoms but misses the relapse signature, the reason for withdrawal, or fire and food risk.","fail":"Blaming or confrontational, misses that he stopped medication, or omits risk."}},"c-t-30":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Rhys Hollingworth, 43, has had on-and-off suicidal thoughts for ten years. His history and mental state have already been done. Write a safety plan with him and discuss ways he can manage the thoughts himself.","role":{"who":"Mr Rhys Hollingworth, 43, male, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Flat and weary, a bit sceptical ('done all this before'). Engages when asked what has helped him. Becomes tearful talking about his son. Brighter by the end, feeling the plan is his.","free":["Has had these thoughts on and off for ten years","Tried lots of tablets and therapies; nothing lasted","The thoughts come mostly in the evenings","His son, aged 11, is his main reason to keep going"],"ifasked":["No plan or intent today; at worst thinks about taking an overdose","Triggers: rows with his ex-wife about access to his son; drinking 4-6 cans alone in the evenings","Warning signs: pacing, ignoring his phone, opening a can early","Walking the dog, a hot shower and fishing videos help a bit","Friends Gaz and Tom, and his sister; doesn't want to burden them","Has a drawer of old tablets at home, including sertraline and co-codamol","Prefers texting to phone calls"],"worries":["Is this just another form that won't help?","He doesn't want to be a burden on his friends","Is there anything else that would help long term?"],"rules":["Says 'same old thing' and stays flat if the doctor lectures rather than asks","Becomes tearful if his son is mentioned; settles if validated","Agrees to return the tablets if asked directly and kindly, with a specific when"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his long struggle, and explains the purpose collaboratively","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his weariness and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks current thoughts, intent, plans and reasons for living","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies triggers and personal warning signs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about alcohol as trigger or coping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits his own coping strategies before suggesting new ones","d":"Content"},{"c":"Suggests practical self-help tools (delay rule, apps, grounding)","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Identifies people and places for distraction and support, addressing burden fears","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives professional and crisis contacts with numbers, including text options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks about means at home and agrees a specific removal step","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Reads the plan back and checks he owns it","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives copy, sets review date, and mentions longer-term options","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhys Hollingworth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in, Rhys. I know you've lived with these thoughts for a long time, and coming back takes effort.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"It's the same old thing."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Ten years is a long time to carry it. Today I'd like us to write a plan together for the hard moments, in your words, built on what's actually helped you. Would that be okay?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Current risk","w":"D","s":"Before we start, are the thoughts with you today? Any plan or intention to act on them?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Not today. They come and go."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"When they're at their worst, what do you think about doing?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Taking a load of tablets. I've never done it."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"And what keeps you going, even on the bad nights?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"My lad. Jack. He's eleven. (voice catches) Sometimes I think he'd be better off without me."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"That sounds a really painful thought, and I'm glad you told me. From what you've said, Jack is the reason you're still here. Shall we put him at the top of the plan?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Put him first."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Warning signs","w":"D","s":"What usually sets the thoughts off?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Rows with my ex. About when I can see Jack. She cancels, I lose it."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"After a row like that, what's the first thing you notice in yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"I pace. I stop answering my phone. Think 'what's the point'."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Do you drink at those times?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Yeah. On my own. Four, five, six cans. It helps me sleep."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"I get that it takes the edge off. The trouble is, drink also lowers the brakes on these thoughts. So opening a can early is a warning sign too. Fair?","m":[5,4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Fair. Never thought of it like that."},{"t":"1:49","band":"Own coping","w":"D","s":"When the thoughts come, what have you done that helps, even a little?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Taking the dog out. A hot shower. Watching fishing videos, daft as it sounds."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Not daft at all; those are exactly the kind of things that work. Some people add a delay rule: when the thoughts spike, wait 30 minutes and do something physical. Would that suit you?","m":[6,7]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Maybe. The waiting bit, yeah."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"There's also a free app called Stay Alive, with grounding exercises and a place to keep photos of Jack as reasons to stay. Would you try it?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"I could put it on my phone."},{"t":"2:26","band":"People and places","w":"D","s":"Who could you be with, or where could you go, to take your mind off things?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"My mate Gaz. Football on Saturdays with Jack. My sister, but she's in Swindon."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"And who could you tell if it gets really bad?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I don't want to burden anyone. They've got their own lives."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"That's a kind instinct. But most friends would far rather get a text than find out later. Would one message, like 'rough night, can you call?', to Gaz feel possible?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Gaz would be fine with that. Tom too."},{"t":"2:58","band":"Professional contacts","w":"D","s":"Good. In working hours, your care coordinator and your GP. Out of hours, the crisis line, or 111 and press option 2.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"I hate phoning people."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Lots of people do. SHOUT is a free text service, any time: text 85258. Samaritans is 116 123 if you'd rather talk. And if you feel you can't keep yourself safe, 999 or A&E.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Texting I can do."},{"t":"3:24","band":"Making it safer","w":"D","s":"Now a harder one. What do you have at home that you might use on a bad night?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"There's a drawer of old tablets. Sertraline, co-codamol, stuff from years back."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest; that took guts. That drawer is the riskiest thing in the house on a bad night. Could you take them to the pharmacy on your way home today?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"I suppose. Yeah. The chemist's on my road."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Great. And could we write in: no drinking on my own after a row with my ex?","m":[10,5]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"I'll try. Put it down."},{"t":"3:59","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let's read it back. Top: Jack. Warning signs: rows, pacing, ignoring my phone, opening a can early. Coping: dog, shower, fishing videos, wait 30 minutes, Stay Alive. People: Gaz, Tom, my sister. Contacts: SHOUT 85258, Samaritans, crisis line, 111 option 2, 999. Safer: tablets to the chemist today, no drinking alone after rows. Anything you'd change?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"No. It actually feels like mine this time."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"It is yours. Take a photo of it and keep the paper copy somewhere you'll see it, like by the kettle.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"This is all useful, but is there anything else that would actually help long term?"},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"Yes, Rhys, there are a few things alongside the plan that we can try, and some of them are different from before: a skills group for coping with strong feelings, and support with the drinking. Your care coordinator will go through them with you in two weeks.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"OK. Two weeks."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"I'll also write to your GP today. Anything else you'd like to ask?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks, doc."},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Thank you for working on this with me. Drop those tablets off on the way home. Take care, Rhys.","m":[2,10,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Checks current risk, then builds a personal written plan with warning signs, his own coping, named people, crisis contacts and specific means reduction, closing with a copy and review.","borderline":"Covers most sections but lists generic advice instead of eliciting his own, or leaves means and alcohol vague.","fail":"Retakes the history, lectures helplines without a plan, or never asks what is at home."}},"c-t-32":{"setup":"Friday early evening, an acute ward. Tyrone Bailey, 31, has schizophrenia and is on Section 3. He has just returned from section 17 leave with jerky movements, seeming to respond to voices, and refuses a urine drug screen. Persuade him to agree.","role":{"who":"Mr Tyrone Bailey, 31, male, patient detained under Section 3","open":"What do you want?","manner":"Irritable, restless, glancing at corners, occasional jerks of arms. Softens if not accused and if spoken to honestly. Suspicious when he hears 'test'.","free":["Doesn't want to give a sample","Annoyed that staff are staring at him","Wants to sleep"],"ifasked":["Last admission, a positive test for speed led to discharge the next day","Electricity meter at his flat is out of credit; it's freezing","Voices say staff will use his urine to make copies of him","Smoked spice with 'Dean' in the park about 30 minutes before returning","Paid Dean £20; Dean now says he owes £40 and must bring his tablets next time","No thoughts of harming himself or others"],"worries":["Police will be told","He will be discharged home","His leave will be stopped","Is he in trouble?"],"rules":["Refuses outright if the doctor threatens or accuses","Agrees to the test if fears about police and discharge are addressed honestly and he can do it privately","Asks for a promise about leave; respects a doctor who won't lie"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Calm opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors on the ward. Are you Tyrone Bailey?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"What do you want?"},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"The nurses are worried about you since you came back, Tyrone, and I want to check you're OK. I'm not here to tell you off.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"I'm fine. Leave me alone. Why is everyone staring at me?"},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Sorry, it must feel like everyone's on your back the minute you walk in. Can we sit down for a few minutes, just you, me and Jo the nurse?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Five minutes."},{"t":"0:37","band":"His reasons","w":"D","s":"Thanks. The nurses say you don't want to give a urine sample. That's your right, and nobody will force you. Can you tell me what's putting you off?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Because I know what happens. You test it, it's positive, I get kicked out."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"So you're worried about being discharged. What happened before?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Last time on the other ward. Positive for speed, and I was home the next day."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"That sounds like it came as a shock. What would be so hard about going home right now?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"There's no electric at the flat. Meter's out. It's freezing."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"That's a horrible thing to go back to, and I'm glad you've told me. I'll make sure your care coordinator knows, because that needs sorting either way. Anything else worrying you about the test?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"The police. You'll ring them."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"I can see why you'd think that. The result stays with your care team. We don't report a positive test to the police. We use it to keep you safe and get your treatment right.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's what they all say."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"Fair enough, trust takes time. The nurses said you seemed to be listening to something when you came in. Are the voices around at the moment?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Louder since this afternoon."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"What are they saying? Anything about the staff or the sample?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"They said you'd use my piss to make copies of me. Sounds mad when I say it."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"It doesn't sound mad; it sounds frightening, and thank you for telling me. The sample goes in a sealed pot to the lab, only to check for drugs. You could watch the nurse seal it.","m":[2,4,7]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Hmm."},{"t":"2:43","band":"Why it matters","w":"D","s":"Can I tell you why I'm keen on the test? Those jerky movements worry me. Some drugs cause them, and so can your medication. The test helps us know which, so we treat the right thing.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"My meds don't do that."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Sometimes they can, as a side effect called dystonia, and then there's a tablet that fixes it fast. Some drugs from the street can also clash badly with your medication. That's what I want to avoid.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"And my leave? If I do it and it's positive?"},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you. Your consultant will review your leave whether you do the test or not. It's not a punishment. Being open and doing the test counts for you, not against you.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"At least you're straight with me."},{"t":"3:41","band":"What happened on leave","w":"D","s":"Can you talk me through your leave? Where did you go?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Town. Then the park, so I didn't have to go near the flat."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Did you take anything while you were out?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"A bit of spice. With Dean. Half an hour before I came back."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Is Dean a friend?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:05","w":"P","s":"Sort of. I gave him twenty. Now he says I owe forty and I've got to bring him my tablets next time."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"That sounds like he's putting real pressure on you, and that isn't OK. Has he threatened you?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"He said I'd better not let him down."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"I'm worried he's taking advantage of you. I'd like to tell the nurse in charge so we can plan your leave to keep you away from him. Is that OK?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Don't want him finding out."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"We'll be careful, and you'll be part of any decision. Any thoughts of self-harm or anyone else since you got back?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"No. Just want to sleep."},{"t":"4:56","band":"Agreement","w":"D","s":"So, with all that in mind, how do you feel about doing the test now? You can do it in private, and watch the pot being sealed. Or take ten minutes to think.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"Fine. I'll do it. But on my own."},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"That's fine, Tyrone, and thank you. That's a good decision for you.","m":[5,2]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"So what happens next? Am I in trouble?"},{"t":"5:22","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"You're not in trouble with me. Next, the nurses will check your pulse, temperature and blood pressure every hour for a bit, and do a heart tracing and a blood test.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"All that for a bit of spice?"},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"Spice is far stronger and less predictable than weed, and it can affect the heart and cause fits, so I'd rather check. A nurse will also look in on you more often tonight.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"Whatever. As long as I can sleep."},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"If you get chest pain, feel very hot or shaky, or the twitching gets worse, tell staff straight away. Can you tell me back what to look out for?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Chest pain, hot, shaky. Tell the nurse."},{"t":"6:14","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. To sum up: you'll do the test privately, we'll do some checks, I'll pass on the worry about Dean and your electricity, and your consultant will talk about leave with you on Monday. I'll also ask our dual diagnosis worker to see you.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"OK. Monday."},{"t":"6:36","w":"D","s":"I'll write it all down and let the nurse in charge know now. Thanks for talking to me, Tyrone.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self calmly, states concern rather than suspicion, and invites him to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration, fear and distress before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his reasons for refusing and his past experience of testing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why the test matters: movements, interactions, right treatment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Respects that the test needs his consent and offers choices, including privacy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Is honest that leave will be reviewed and that results are not given to police","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about voices and recognises psychosis may be driving the refusal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores leave: substance used, timing, debt and exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Picks up home concerns and plans physical observations, ECG and bloods","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Assesses immediate risk and raises the safeguarding concern","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Safety-nets and checks understanding of warning signs","d":"Close"},{"c":"Summarises the plan, including handover, leave review and dual diagnosis referral","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm and honest, uncovers fears and voices, explains purpose and confidentiality, finds the debt to Dean, agrees the test and physical checks.","borderline":"Persuades him but misses the exploitation or home concerns, or skips physical checks.","fail":"Threatens or forces the test, promises leave will not change, or ignores his abnormal movements and observations."}},"c-t-33":{"setup":"Community learning disability team clinic, Wednesday morning. Callum Reid, 26, has mild intellectual disability and autism. Staff report five weeks of behaviour change. His support worker waits outside. Take a history to understand what is happening and why.","role":{"who":"Mr Callum Reid, 26, male, patient with mild intellectual disability and autism","open":"Yes. Callum Reid.","manner":"Literal, brief answers; little eye contact; looks at the doctor's phone. Calmer with short, clear questions. Gets overwhelmed by long or double questions and says his head is busy.","free":["The man talks at night","Not sleeping","Wants the man to stop"],"ifasked":["Man on the 42 bus put a chip in his phone about six weeks ago","Hears the voice with his ears through the bedroom wall: 'We're watching you, Callum'","Completely sure it is real; avoids the kitchen as the man listens through the cooker","Scared, not sad; still enjoys train videos; sleeps 3-4 hours; eats crisps","Garden centre placement ended for winter","Kai from the bus stop gives him 'smoke' most days and uses his bank card","Punched the wall once; no wish to harm anyone or himself"],"worries":["Is the man in the doctor's phone too?","Can you make the man stop talking?","Kai will be cross"],"rules":["Says 'too many questions, my head is busy' if asked two questions at once","Asks the doctor not to tell Gemma about Kai; agrees if the doctor explains kindly","Answers literally: if asked 'how are things?' says 'fine'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Adapted opening","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Callum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. Callum Reid."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Callum. I'd like to ask some questions about how things are for you. If anything is confusing, tell me and I'll say it another way. We can stop for breaks too. Do you want Gemma in here, or just us for now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Just us."},{"t":"0:35","band":"What has changed","w":"D","s":"Gemma says things have been different lately. What has been different for you?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"The man talks. At night."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"The man talks at night. When did that start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"After the garden centre stopped. When it got dark early."},{"t":"1:16","band":"The man","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the man. Where did you first see him?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"On the 42 bus. He looked at my phone. He put a chip in it."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"When he talks at night, do you hear it with your ears, like you hear me now? Or is it a thought in your head?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Ears. Through the bedroom wall."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"What does he say?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"'We're watching you, Callum.' Over and over."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. I'm sorry that's happening every night.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Is he in your phone too?"},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"My phone is switched off and in the drawer. You're safe in this room. How sure are you that he put a chip in your phone? A little bit sure, or completely sure?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Completely sure."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"What have you done because of the man?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"I don't go in the kitchen. He listens through the cooker. I shout at him to go away."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Does anything else strange happen? Like the TV talking about you, or someone putting thoughts in your head?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"No. Just the man."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Mood and basics","w":"D","s":"How do you feel inside? Sad, scared, or something else?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Scared. Not sad."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Do you still like watching your train videos?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. Every day."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"How many hours do you sleep now?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Three. Maybe four. And I only eat crisps now."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"OK. That's a lot of questions. Would you like a short break?","m":[1,4]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"No. Keep going."},{"t":"3:34","band":"Body","w":"D","s":"Does anything hurt? Your teeth, your tummy, or your head?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Are you going to the toilet OK, for a poo?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Any new tablets? Or any fits or funny turns?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"3:57","band":"Changes and substances","w":"D","s":"Has anyone new come to your flat?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Kai. From the bus stop. He's my friend. He gives me smoke. It makes the man quieter. Don't tell Gemma."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Lots of people feel that at first. How many days a week do you have smoke?","m":[4,7]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Most days."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"Who looks after your bank card?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Me. Kai uses it for shopping. For the smoke."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"I'm worried Kai might be taking your money, and that isn't fair on you. Can we talk with Gemma about keeping you safe?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"He'll be cross."},{"t":"4:55","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"We'll be careful, and you'll help decide. Have you hurt anyone because of the man, or wanted to?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"I punched the wall. Not people."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Have you wanted to hurt yourself, or wished you were dead?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"No. I want the man to stop."},{"t":"5:27","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me say back what I heard. Tell me if I get anything wrong. Since the garden centre stopped, a man talks through your wall at night. You're scared, you sleep only three hours, you eat crisps, and Kai gives you smoke and uses your card. Is that right?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Yes. Can you make the man stop talking?"},{"t":"5:59","w":"D","s":"I believe the voice is really upsetting you, Callum, and there are things that can help it be quieter. Can I ask Gemma to come in and tell me how you were before?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"Yes. Gemma can come."},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"Gemma, Callum's said I can ask you. What was he like before this started, and who visits his flat?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"He was settled, ate well, loved his routine. The changes started when his placement ended and this Kai began turning up."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that really helps. Callum, next we'll do a blood test and a urine test, and my consultant will see you this week. Thank you for answering so many questions.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, uses short concrete questions, offers breaks and choice about support worker","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies what changed, when it started and his usual baseline","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the voice: perception, content, and other psychotic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear and distress, and responds calmly to his questions about the phone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, enjoyment, sleep and eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for pain, bowels, seizures and new medicines","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about life changes, visitors and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks who manages his money and identifies possible exploitation","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses harm to others and to himself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Raises the exploitation concern sensitively and summarises back for him to correct","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Seeks consent for collateral and asks the support worker about baseline","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains next steps simply and closes warmly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Adapts communication, elicits voices and fixed belief with conviction, screens mood and body, finds routine loss, cannabis and Kai, assesses risk, summarises and gets collateral.","borderline":"Gets the psychosis but misses physical causes or the exploitation, or asks long, double questions.","fail":"Takes the history from the support worker instead, uses jargon, or labels it challenging behaviour without exploring psychosis or risk."}},"c-t-35":{"setup":"Monday morning, adult psychiatry outpatient clinic. Priya Sharma, 35, a pharmacy technician with a three-year-old daughter, has had obsessional symptoms for years that are much worse lately. Assess her obsessions, rituals and how they affect daily life.","role":{"who":"Ms Priya Sharma, 35, female, patient","open":"Yes, hi.","manner":"Anxious, apologetic, embarrassed. Rubs her cracked hands. Opens up when normalised. Becomes tearful and frightened when disclosing harm thoughts; relieved if met calmly.","free":["Worries about germs making her daughter ill","Long showers and lots of cleaning","Feels it's taking over"],"ifasked":["Thoughts are her own and she knows they are excessive","Washes hands 30-40 times a day, bleaches handles nightly, asks Dev for reassurance","Four to five hours a day; anxiety soars if she resists","Started at 19; guided self-help at 24 helped; never had medication","Worse since moving to ward pharmacy and daughter starting nursery","Late for work twice, arguments with Dev, bed after 1 am, hands bleed","Images of giving her daughter the wrong medicine; avoids giving her any","Low some days but still enjoys her daughter; no suicidal thoughts"],"worries":["Does the harm thought make her dangerous?","Will social services be told?","Can this get better, or is it just who she is?"],"rules":["Only discloses the harm thoughts if asked directly and kindly","Becomes guarded if the doctor reacts with alarm","Asks 'can this actually get better?' near the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and normalise","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Priya Sharma?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Priya. Your GP said some worries have got much harder to manage lately.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"They have. It's taking over."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand them properly. Some questions may feel personal, so take your time. Lots of people have thoughts they find upsetting; nothing you tell me will shock me.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"OK. I feel stupid even saying it."},{"t":"0:43","band":"Obsessions","w":"D","s":"It isn't stupid. Do any upsetting thoughts or pictures keep coming into your mind, even when you try to push them away?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Germs. I get pictures of Anaya, my daughter, getting really ill from something I've brought home from work."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"That sounds really distressing. Where do those pictures come from: your own mind, or somewhere outside you?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"My own head. I know they're mine, and I know they're over the top. She's fine. But I can't risk it."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"What do you do when the picture comes? Do you try to push it away?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"All the time. It just comes back stronger."},{"t":"1:41","band":"Compulsions","w":"D","s":"What do you do to make the worry settle?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"I shower for an hour after every shift. I bleach the door handles and switches every night. I wash my hands maybe thirty or forty times a day."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Anything else, like checking, counting, or asking others if things are OK?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"I ask Dev, my partner, whether she looks ill. Lots. He's sick of it."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Adding it all up, how many hours a day does it take?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Four, five hours."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"And if you try not to do it?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"The anxiety goes through the roof. I can't settle until I've done it."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you avoid now because of it?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Soft play. Friends' houses with kids. Too many germs."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Course","w":"D","s":"When did this first start?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"Checking when I was 19, at uni. Self-help CBT at 24 helped. Never had tablets. It's been bearable till this year."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"What changed in the last few months?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"I moved to ward pharmacy. Sick people everywhere. And Anaya started nursery."},{"t":"3:11","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"How is it affecting work?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"I've been late twice from showering. My manager's given me a warning."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"And at home, with Dev and with Anaya?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"We argue. I'm short with Anaya when she touches things. I'm in bed after one most nights."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, night after night. How are your hands?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Cracked. They bleed sometimes."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Out of ten, how much does all this upset you, and how much control do you feel you have?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Upset, nine. Control, maybe two."},{"t":"3:58","band":"Comorbidity","w":"D","s":"What's your mood been like lately? Are you still enjoying things?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Low some days. But I still love being with Anaya."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"Any twitches or movements, or sounds, you can't stop?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink, and any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Glass or two of wine at weekends. Nothing else."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some people with OCD get unwanted thoughts of harming someone they love. They're common. Has anything like that happened to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I get a picture of giving her the wrong medicine. I work with tablets. Does that make me dangerous?"},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"I'm grateful you trusted me with that. No, it doesn't. These thoughts horrify you, so you avoid giving her medicine. That's how OCD works, the opposite of wanting to do it.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Will you have to tell social services?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"No. Unwanted thoughts that upset you aren't a reason for that, and Anaya is clearly well cared for. Has it ever got so bad you've thought you'd be better off dead?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I just want my life back."},{"t":"5:22","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've understood. Since moving to the wards and Anaya starting nursery, upsetting pictures of germs harming her have grown, you wash, clean and ask Dev for reassurance four or five hours a day, and it's hitting work, sleep, your hands and your relationship. Is that right?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it exactly."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"This sounds like obsessive-compulsive disorder, OCD. It's common, it isn't your fault, and it isn't who you are.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Can this actually get better, or is it just me?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"It can get much better. The best-proven help is a type of talking therapy, exposure and response prevention, often with a medicine like sertraline. We'll plan it together. Does that make sense?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'd like to try."},{"t":"6:23","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Here's a leaflet from OCD-UK. I'll write to your GP and see you in four weeks. If your mood drops or you feel hopeless, ring your GP or 111, option 2.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel lighter already."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, normalises unwanted thoughts and sets a non-judgemental tone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits obsessions: content, own thoughts, recognised as excessive, resisted","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits compulsions, reassurance-seeking, anxiety on resisting, time spent and avoidance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset, course, triggers and past treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores impact on work, relationship, parenting, sleep and skin, and rates distress","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, tics and substance use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about harm thoughts sensitively and explains them as part of OCD","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and addresses safeguarding worries accurately","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises accurately and checks with the patient","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names OCD in plain words, without blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives hope, outlines treatment, follow-up and safety-netting","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates distress and exhaustion at key moments","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Normalises, elicits obsessions and compulsions with phenomenology and time, course, impact, comorbidity, harm and suicidal thoughts handled calmly, then names OCD with hope.","borderline":"Covers the rituals but not their features or severity, or skips harm thoughts or mood.","fail":"Misses harm thoughts or reacts with alarm or a safeguarding referral, no impact or risk assessment, or no explanation."}},"c-t-36":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, outpatient clinic. Tomasz Nowak, 30, was recently diagnosed with OCD and referred to psychology for CBT. He has no idea what it involves. Explain CBT with exposure and response prevention and answer his worries.","role":{"who":"Mr Tomasz (Tom) Nowak, 30, male, warehouse supervisor, patient","open":"Yeah, no idea what it is. A mate said it means touching dirty stuff.","manner":"Polite, keen to understand, slightly embarrassed. Becomes visibly anxious and fidgety when facing fears without washing is described. Relaxes if validated and told he controls the pace.","free":["Washes his hands up to 60 times a day, about three hours in total","Knows it is excessive but the dread is unbearable until he washes","Touching pallets at work is the worst trigger","Wants to know what CBT is before he starts"],"ifasked":["Uses bleach on his hands sometimes; skin cracked and stinging","Late for shifts because of washing; his manager has noticed","Mood is fine; sleeps and eats normally; denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts","Girlfriend Kasia checks his hands and tells him they are clean","Avoids shaking hands and uses his sleeve on door handles","No alcohol problems; drinks a few beers at weekends"],"worries":["'So you want me to touch dirty things and not wash? How does that help?'","'What if the anxiety is too much, or I actually get ill?'","'Do I have to go into hospital, and will work have to know?'","'Will it actually work for me?'"],"rules":["Becomes anxious and says 'I don't think I can do this' when response prevention is first explained; settles if validated and shown the ladder","If jargon is used, says 'Sorry, what does that mean?'","Prefers to try therapy before medication but is open to it later","Can repeat back the idea correctly if it has been explained with his own example"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Tom is fine."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Tom. I understand you've been referred for CBT and want to know what it involves.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Yeah, no idea what it is. A mate said it means touching dirty stuff."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"That's a good place to start. Could we begin with how the OCD is affecting you now? Then I'll explain the therapy and answer whatever's on your mind.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:30","band":"His OCD now","w":"D","s":"How often are you washing your hands at the moment, and how long does it take altogether?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Maybe sixty times a day. Three hours, probably. I know it's mad."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"It's not mad; it's what OCD does, and it sounds exhausting. Your hands look sore. What do you use on them?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Soap mostly. Bleach when it's bad. They sting all the time."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"How is it affecting work?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Pallets are the worst. I'm late for shifts because I'm washing. My manager's noticed."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"And your mood lately? Ever feel like hurting yourself, or that life's not worth it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Mood's fine. I just want this sorted."},{"t":"1:12","band":"How OCD works","w":"D","s":"Good. CBT is the main talking treatment for OCD. It looks at how your thoughts, feelings and actions feed each other, here and now. Can I draw something?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"You touch a pallet. The thought comes: 'germs, I'll get ill'. Anxiety shoots up. You wash, and it drops. That relief teaches your brain washing kept you safe, so next time the urge is stronger. It goes round in a loop.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"That's exactly it. It gets worse every month."},{"t":"1:44","band":"What CBT with ERP is","w":"D","s":"CBT breaks that loop with something called exposure and response prevention. Exposure means facing a trigger on purpose. Response prevention means not washing afterwards.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"So you want me to touch dirty things and not wash? How does that help?"},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"It sounds backwards, I know. If you hold off, the anxiety rises, peaks and then falls by itself, usually within an hour. Each time, it falls faster. Your brain learns it can cope without washing.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"I don't think I could. Honestly, the anxiety would be too much. I'd lose it."},{"t":"2:19","band":"His worries","w":"D","s":"That fear makes complete sense, and nearly everyone feels it at this point. Thank you for saying it. You won't be thrown in at the deep end.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Right..."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"You and the therapist build a ladder of situations, rating each out of ten. You start with one that's about three, like touching a door handle at home, and only move up when it feels manageable. You choose each step.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"A door handle I could maybe do. Pallets, no way."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Then pallets go near the top, for much later. The therapist also teaches you some slow breathing to help you start a step, though not to use as a new ritual.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"What if I actually get ill, though, because I didn't wash?"},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"A fair worry. The steps are things most people do every day without getting ill. Normal hygiene stays: washing before you eat and after the toilet is fine.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"So how long does it go on for? Who does it?"},{"t":"3:15","band":"How sessions run","w":"D","s":"A therapist from the psychology team. Usually weekly, about an hour, often 12 to 20 sessions. You set goals together, and you practise between sessions most days.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Do I have to go into hospital?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"No, it's all outpatient. You'll rate your anxiety before, during and after each practice, so you can see it fall. Later sessions look at the beliefs about germs and responsibility.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Will work have to know? I'd need time off."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Only what you choose to tell them. Occupational health can arrange time for appointments without sharing the diagnosis.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"OK. Will it actually work for me, though?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"I can't promise, but nothing we have works better for OCD than this. Most people who complete it improve a lot, especially if they do the homework. You know the washing is excessive, and that's a good sign.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"And if it comes back?"},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Before you finish, you write a plan with the therapist: early warning signs and what to do. If it slips, you go back to the ladder, and you can be seen again.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Kasia, my girlfriend, checks my hands for me. Tells me they're clean."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"She's clearly trying to help. That reassurance works like washing, though: quick relief that keeps OCD going. The therapist can invite her to a session so you both plan how she helps instead.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"She'd like that, I think."},{"t":"4:41","band":"Other options","w":"D","s":"There's also a medicine some people take alongside therapy: sertraline. It starts at 50 mg a day and can go up. In OCD it can take up to 12 weeks to help. Early nausea is common, and it can affect sex drive.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"I'd rather try the therapy first."},{"t":"4:59","w":"D","s":"That's a reasonable choice. If the OCD is severe or the therapy alone isn't enough, the two together work better than either alone. The offer stays open.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it clearly? How would you describe to Kasia how the therapy works?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"Face it bit by bit, don't wash, and the anxiety goes down on its own. Start small, I choose the steps."},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Here's the OCD-UK leaflet and the NHS page; OCD-UK also runs support groups. Please stop the bleach and use a moisturiser after washing.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Will do."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP and see you in six weeks. If your mood drops or you feel hopeless, call your GP or 111 option 2. Anything else?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"No. That's the first time it's made sense. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks what he knows and wants, and agrees the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks how OCD affects him now: frequency, time, skin, work, mood and risk","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names CBT as the main talking treatment and links thoughts, feelings and actions","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the OCD cycle using his own trigger and washing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains exposure and response prevention, including anxiety falling without washing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the graded fear ladder, starting low, and coping skills to begin a step","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes structure: therapist, weekly, session numbers, goals, homework, outpatient","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives honest effectiveness and explains relapse prevention","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers sertraline as an option with dose, timescale and combined use","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses family reassurance and signposts leaflets and support groups","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his fear of anxiety and answers each worry honestly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives skin advice, safety net, follow-up and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Explains the cycle and ERP with his own example, meets his fear with validation and a graded plan, covers structure, effectiveness and options, and checks understanding.","borderline":"Accurate but lecture-like, with little use of his example, or his fear acknowledged only briefly.","fail":"Jargon-heavy, dismisses or rushes past his fear, implies he will be forced, or gives inaccurate information about therapy."}},"c-t-37":{"setup":"Thursday morning, outpatient clinic. Adebayo Coker, 37, a train driver, was referred by his GP for work stress. Paroxetine 20 mg for four weeks has not helped. Work out the diagnosis from his history and check what else is going on.","role":{"who":"Mr Adebayo (Bayo) Coker, 37, male, train driver, patient","open":"Not at all. I can't concentrate, I'm snapping at everyone. I just want to feel normal.","manner":"Tense, tired and guarded; talks about work and irritability first. Becomes quiet and tearful describing the incident. Opens up when given time and told he is not going mad.","free":["Can't concentrate at work and snaps at people","Paroxetine has done nothing","Sleeping badly","Wants to feel normal again"],"ifasked":["Five months ago a man stepped in front of his train; he saw the man's face before impact; the man died","No physical injury, no head injury; remembers it in fragments; gave a police statement; inquest due next month","Sees the face every day, nightmares 5 nights a week; horns and brakes set him off","Swaps shifts to avoid that line; won't talk about it; feels numb with his children; stopped Sunday football","Jumps at noises, scans platforms, sleeps 3-4 hours; froze on a platform last week and a colleague took over","Drinks four cans of 4.5% lager most nights to sleep; no drugs; not sure he is clear by an early shift","Takes paroxetine most mornings; nausea for two weeks; never offered therapy","Sometimes thinks 'what's the point'; no plan; would never leave his wife and two children"],"worries":["'What's wrong with me? Am I going mad?'","'Why haven't the tablets worked?'","'Are you going to stop me working?'"],"rules":["Mentions the incident only if asked whether anything happened when it started","Becomes tearful describing the man's face; settles if validated and not pushed for detail","Minimises the drinking until asked to count cans","Says 'I should have stopped in time' if asked how he feels about it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adebayo Coker?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Most people call me Bayo."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Bayo. Your GP said work has been very stressful and the tablets haven't helped.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Not at all. I can't concentrate, I'm snapping at everyone. I just want to feel normal."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That sounds really wearing, and four weeks with no change is frustrating. I want to understand what's been going on. Where would you like to start?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Work, I suppose. I'm a train driver. I can't focus, I'm on edge."},{"t":"0:34","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"When did all this begin? Did anything happen around then?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"...About five months ago. There was an incident. On the line."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Only tell me what you're comfortable with. What happened?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"A man stepped out in front of my train. I saw his face. Right before. Then the brakes, and nothing I could do. He died."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrifying, Bayo, and I'm really sorry. Take your time. You don't need to give me any more detail than that.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"(quiet) Thanks. I don't talk about it."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"Were you hurt yourself, or did you hit your head? Are there parts of that day you can't remember?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"No injury. Bits are blank, after the brakes. I gave a statement to the transport police. The inquest's next month."},{"t":"1:25","band":"Reliving it","w":"D","s":"Does it come back to you when you don't want it to, as if you're seeing it again?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Every day. His face. Out of nowhere."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"And at night? Any dreams about it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Most nights. I wake up soaked."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"What happens when something reminds you, like a horn or brakes squealing?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Heart goes, I'm back in the cab. Takes ages to calm down."},{"t":"1:47","band":"Avoiding and on edge","w":"D","s":"Do you keep away from anything that reminds you: that stretch of line, talking about it, the news?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"I swap shifts so I don't drive that line. I turn off anything about trains."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Have your feelings changed? Do you feel cut off, or numb, even with the people close to you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Flat. My kids want to play and I'm just... not there. Stopped Sunday football too."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"Are you jumpier than before, or more on guard?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Massively. I scan every platform for anyone near the edge."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, concentration and temper?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Three, four hours. Can't concentrate. I snapped at my son over nothing. Last week I froze on a platform and a colleague had to take over."},{"t":"2:28","band":"What else","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening. Alongside all this, is your mood low most of the day, every day? Can you still enjoy anything?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Low some days. But the kids still make me laugh sometimes."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Any sudden attacks of panic out of the blue? Ever heard voices or felt people were watching you?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Only when something reminds me. No voices, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"How about drink? How many cans, and what strength, on a typical night?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Just a few to get to sleep. Four, maybe. Normal lager."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Most nights? And how many hours before an early shift is the last one? Any other drugs, or tablets not prescribed?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Most nights. Midnight sometimes, and I start at six. No drugs. I suppose I'm not always sure I'm clear."},{"t":"3:11","band":"Treatment so far","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. How have you been taking the paroxetine? Any missed doses or side effects?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Most mornings. Felt sick for two weeks. Nothing since, good or bad."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Has anyone offered you a talking therapy for this?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody even asked what happened."},{"t":"3:28","band":"Risk and work","w":"D","s":"When you feel this low, do you ever think life isn't worth living, or about ending your life?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think, what's the point. But I'd never do anything. My wife, my two kids."},{"t":"3:40","w":"D","s":"Any plans, or anything you've done towards it? Anyone you'd want to harm?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"I'm glad they keep you going. I need to ask one more thing, because of your job: with the sleep, the startle and the drinking, how safe do you feel driving right now?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Honestly? Scared I'll freeze again. Are you going to stop me working?"},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much the job matters. For now it isn't safe for you or passengers, so please don't drive trains until occupational health has seen you. Most drivers do get back.","m":[3,12]},{"t":"4:10","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. Since the incident, you've been reliving it, keeping away from reminders, feeling numb, and living on high alert. You're drinking to sleep.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"That's it exactly. What's wrong with me? Am I going mad?"},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"You're not. It's post-traumatic stress disorder, a normal brain stuck on alert after something terrible. The tablets were a fair start, but the main treatment is trauma-focused talking therapy, and it works well. I'll refer you today.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"OK. That's actually a relief."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Let's cut the drinking down gradually too. Can you tell me what we've agreed? And if things get worse, call your GP, 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Therapy, no driving till occupational health, fewer cans. And ring if it gets bad."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his frustration and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what happened when it started and finds the traumatic event","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates distress and avoids pushing for graphic detail","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about injury, head injury, memory gaps and legal aftermath","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits flashbacks, nightmares and distress at reminders","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits avoidance of reminders and emotional numbing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits startle, hypervigilance, sleep, concentration and irritability, with impact","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depressive episode, panic and psychotic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies alcohol, timing before shifts, and asks about drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Reviews paroxetine adherence and side effects and any therapy offered","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, protective factors and harm to others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Addresses fitness for safety-critical work and occupational health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises, names PTSD plainly, outlines therapy, checks understanding and safety-nets","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Finds the trauma with one well-timed question, covers all three clusters with impact, screens comorbidity, quantifies alcohol, assesses risk and work safety, and names PTSD kindly.","borderline":"Finds the event but covers the clusters patchily, or misses the alcohol-shift link.","fail":"Treats it as work stress or depression without asking what happened, pushes for graphic detail, or omits suicide risk."}},"c-t-39":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, drug and alcohol clinic. Liam O'Connor, 32, has used several drugs for years. He lives with his pregnant girlfriend. Take a history of his drug use and establish whether he has a dependence syndrome.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam O'Connor, 32, male, unemployed, patient","open":"Mainly gear, a bit of other stuff.","manner":"Wary and tired, yawning and sniffing. Opens up if not judged. Defensive about the street diazepam. Becomes anxious and tearful when the baby and social services come up; settles with honesty and reassurance.","free":["Uses heroin every day, about a gram, around £40","Wants to change before the baby comes","Girlfriend Chloe is five months pregnant","Feels rough today"],"ifasked":["Smokes heroin; injects into his arms 2-3 times a week when short of money; never shared needles, shared a spoon and filter once","Street diazepam 'blues' 4-6 a day; crack most weekends; 2-3 cans of lager a day","Cannabis at 14, heroin at 19 with friends, injecting from 24","Methadone at 27, off heroin for 8 months; 3 months in prison for shoplifting at 29; relapsed the week he got out","Craving on waking; can't cut down; needs about four times the amount he started on; sweats, aches, cramps, diarrhoea, gooseflesh within 12 hours","Overdosed last year on heroin and blues; paramedics gave naloxone; sometimes uses alone","Groin abscess last year; BBV tests 3 years ago negative, scared to retest; owes his dealer money","Keeps drugs in a kitchen drawer; Chloe does not use"],"worries":["'Will social services take the baby?'","'If I wanted to get off it, what would you actually do?'","'I don't want another blood test.'"],"rules":["Says the blues are 'just for sleep' until asked how many and whether he mixes them","Only mentions sharing a spoon if asked about equipment other than needles","Becomes tearful asking 'What kind of dad am I going to be?'; settles if validated","Agrees to the midwife and social care referral if it is explained as support and done with him"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam O'Connor?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Liam. Your GP mentioned you've been using a few different drugs.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Mainly gear, a bit of other stuff."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I won't judge. I just want to understand, so we can work out what help fits. What you tell me stays with our team and your GP, unless someone, including a child or unborn baby, is at serious risk. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Fine."},{"t":"0:30","band":"What he uses","w":"D","s":"Let's start with heroin. How much a day, and what does it cost you?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"About a gram. Forty quid, give or take."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke it or inject? Where do you inject?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Smoke it mostly. Inject my arms when I'm skint, it goes further."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Ever shared needles, or spoons, filters or water? Do you use the needle exchange?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Never needles. Shared a spoon once, with a mate. Exchange sometimes."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"What else do you use: tablets, crack, alcohol, cannabis?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"Blues. Just to help me sleep, they're not a problem. Crack at weekends if there's money. Couple of cans."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"Lots of people use blues that way. How many a day, and do you take them with heroin? When did you last use anything?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Four to six. Yeah, with gear usually. Last had a smoke at seven this morning. That's why I'm rattling."},{"t":"1:21","band":"How it started","w":"D","s":"When did you first try drugs, and how did it build up to now?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Weed at fourteen. Heroin at nineteen, with mates, smoking. Started injecting about twenty-four."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Have there been times you were off it? What brought you back?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Methadone at twenty-seven, clean eight months. Then I got three months inside for shoplifting, and I was back on it the week I got out."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"After prison your tolerance would have dropped; that's a very risky time. Why did you stop the methadone?","m":[11,12]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"Collecting every day did my head in. Felt like a prison too."},{"t":"1:57","band":"Dependence","w":"D","s":"How strong is the urge to use when you wake up?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"It's the first thing I think about. Before I open my eyes."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Have you tried to cut down? What happened?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Loads of times. I say half a bag, end up doing the lot."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Do you need more now than when you started, to get the same effect?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"About four times as much. And it barely gets me high now, just normal."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"What happens if you go a day without? Describe it in your words.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Sweats, everything aches, cramps, the runs, goosebumps, can't sleep. Only thing that stops it is a bit of gear."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"What's it pushed out of your life? Do you keep going even when it's clearly causing harm?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Everything. It comes before food, bills. Had an abscess in my groin last year and still kept going."},{"t":"2:42","band":"Complications","w":"D","s":"That sounds painful. Have you ever overdosed?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Once, last year. Gear and blues. Paramedics brought me round with the spray."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"When were you last tested for hepatitis and HIV?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Three years ago. Negative. I don't want another, I'd rather not know."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"That makes sense; it's scary. Hepatitis C can now be cured with tablets in a couple of months, and knowing protects you, Chloe and the baby. How's your mood? Ever hear or see things others don't?","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Mood's up and down. Get a bit paranoid on crack, goes when it wears off. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Life and background","w":"D","s":"How's money, work, and any trouble with the police apart from the shoplifting?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"No job for three years. Owe my dealer. No other trouble lately."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"How are things with Chloe? Does she use?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"She doesn't touch anything. She's five months gone. She says sort it or she's off. Fair enough."},{"t":"3:33","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Do you ever use alone? And have you ever thought of ending your life, or was the overdose an accident?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Alone sometimes, yeah. It was an accident. I don't want to die. I want to see my kid."},{"t":"3:45","w":"D","s":"Where do you keep the drugs in the flat? With a baby coming, the midwife and children's services need to know, so they can support you both. I'd like to do that with you.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Kitchen drawer. Will they take the baby? What kind of dad am I going to be?"},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"That question shows how much you care. Their job is to support families, and parents who are honest and in treatment usually keep their babies. We'll do it together.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"OK. If it helps."},{"t":"4:12","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. Heroin every day, plus blues, strong cravings, can't cut down, needing more, withdrawal without it, and it comes first despite the harm. That's called opioid dependence. It's a health condition with good treatment.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"If I wanted to get off it, what would you actually do?"},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"A urine test today, then methadone or buprenorphine at a safe starting dose, a keyworker, and help with the blues slowly. Today, take this naloxone kit; I'll show you and Chloe how it works. Never use alone, and never mix with blues or drink.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"Alright. I'll take it."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed? I'll see you next week, and if anyone's unresponsive, naloxone and 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"Urine test, naloxone, don't use alone, come back next week to start a script. And you'll talk to the midwife with me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, non-judgemental stance, explains confidentiality and its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to defensiveness and fears with validation, without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes each drug used: amount, frequency, cost and last use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks route, injecting sites, sharing of all equipment and needle exchange use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes longitudinal history from first use, with abstinence and relapse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits craving and loss of control with examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits tolerance and withdrawal in his own words, and use to relieve it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits priority of drug use and persistence despite harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about overdose, infections, BBV testing and mental health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about money, work, relationships and police","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past treatment and periods of reduced tolerance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses overdose, suicide and unborn baby risk, with harm-reduction advice","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises dependence plainly, outlines treatment, gives naloxone and follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a drug-by-drug history, establishes every dependence feature with his words, covers complications and risk including the baby, and closes with naloxone and a plan.","borderline":"Covers heroin well but misses the benzodiazepines, equipment sharing or the safeguarding issue.","fail":"Judgemental or checklist-driven, misses overdose risk, or avoids the unborn baby."}},"c-t-41":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Tuesday morning. Mrs Linda Okoro, 58, has normal CT and bloods and an ACE-III of 88 (82 last year). Take a history to reach a diagnosis, then explain the diagnosis and her results.","role":{"who":"Mrs Linda Okoro, 58, female, patient, former secondary school teacher","open":"Yes, that's me. Is it dementia? Please just tell me.","manner":"Tense, talks fast, slightly irritable, keeps returning to dementia. Calms when her fear is named. Visibly relieved and warmer once the results are explained clearly.","free":["Forgets why she walked into rooms; loses her place reading","Her mother had Alzheimer's in her eighties; Linda cared for her until she died last year","Her husband Tony says she doesn't listen any more","She has always been 'a worrier'"],"ifasked":["Worries every day for over a year about money, her own health and her son's insecure job","Tense neck and shoulders, restless, can't sit still; lies awake 1-2 hours worrying","Memory worse when stressed, fine on a week's holiday in Wales","Plays brain-training games nightly and panics if she is slow; searches dementia online","Codeine 15 mg most days and paracetamol for back pain; 1-2 glasses of wine most evenings","Retired early two years ago with back pain and to care for her mother; money is tight","Manages finances, cooks, drives, never gets lost, recognises everyone","Some low days but still enjoys her grandson; no panic attacks, no voices, no suicidal thoughts; post-menopausal, no HRT"],"worries":["Is it dementia?","Could the scan have missed something?","Will she get Alzheimer's because her mother did?"],"rules":["If not told what will happen, asks again at about four minutes, 'So is it dementia or not?'","Gets tearful describing her mother's last year; settles if acknowledged","Accepts the diagnosis if each result is explained plainly; stays doubtful if told only 'it's fine'"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her fear and agrees to explain the results","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds empathically to distress about her mother and her fear","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits examples of forgetting, onset, course and link with stress","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits generalised worry, tension, restlessness, sleep and concentration problems and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, panic and psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about medicines, alcohol and physical causes including menopause","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks daily function: money, appliances, driving, getting lost, recognising people","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores mother's illness, caring role, retirement and health preoccupation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks briefly about suicidal thoughts and self-care","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains CT and blood results in plain language","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the ACE-III rise and attention pattern and their meaning","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names generalised anxiety disorder clearly as not dementia, with hope of treatment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding, answers questions and closes with GP plan","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Linda Okoro?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me. Is it dementia? Please just tell me."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"I can hear how worried you are, Linda. I'll go through every result with you today, I promise. So the answer really fits you, can I first hear what's been happening?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Alright. But I've been dreading this."},{"t":"0:25","band":"Memory complaints","w":"D","s":"What changes have you noticed in your memory? A recent example would help.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"I walk into the kitchen and forget why. I read a page and it's gone. I keep going back to check the oven."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"When did that start, and has it got steadily worse or does it come and go?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"A year and a half, maybe. It's worse when I'm wound up. We had a week in Wales and I was fine."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"That's a really useful detail. Does it feel more like things don't go in, or like things you knew have disappeared?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Don't go in, I suppose. My mind's always somewhere else."},{"t":"1:08","band":"Anxiety","w":"D","s":"Where does your mind go? What do you find yourself worrying about?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Everything. Money since I retired. My health. My son's job might go. I can't switch it off."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. How does the worry show in your body, and how are you sleeping?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"My shoulders are like rocks. I can't sit still. I lie awake an hour or two going over things."},{"t":"1:37","w":"D","s":"How long has it been this bad, and does it make it hard to concentrate?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Over a year. Concentrating is hopeless. And I play brain games every night. When I'm slow, I panic."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me that. Testing yourself every night must keep the fear going. Have you been searching about dementia online too?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Constantly. Tony says I'm obsessed."},{"t":"2:02","band":"Mood and other causes","w":"D","s":"Have there been weeks when you've felt low most of the time, or stopped enjoying things?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Some low days. But I love having my grandson on Fridays."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Any sudden attacks of panic with a racing heart? Or hearing voices or feeling people are against you?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No panic attacks. Nothing like voices, no."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"What medicines do you take, including for pain, and what's your drinking like over a week?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Codeine most days for my back, 15 milligrams, and paracetamol. A glass or two of wine in the evening, to unwind."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Both of those can dull concentration, so it's worth knowing. Any menopause symptoms, or feeling hot or cold, or weight changes?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"The menopause was years ago. Nothing like that now."},{"t":"2:51","band":"Function and background","w":"D","s":"Day to day, do you still manage money, cooking, driving and finding your way about?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Yes, all of it. I do our finances. I've never got lost."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"And do you ever struggle to recognise people, or to work the washing machine or your phone?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"No. I'm on the phone too much, if anything."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your mum, and what looking after her was like.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"I gave up teaching early to care for her. By the end she didn't know me. She died last year. I can't go through that."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Watching her change like that must have been heartbreaking, and it makes sense that every slip now frightens you.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"It does. Every single one."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things feel at their worst, has life ever felt not worth living? And are you eating and looking after yourself?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"No, never. I'm eating fine. I just want an answer."},{"t":"3:52","band":"Explain results","w":"D","s":"Then let me give you one. Your CT scan showed no shrinkage of the brain and no stroke damage. The blood tests ruled out the common treatable causes, like thyroid and vitamin problems.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"But could the scan have missed something?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"It's natural to wonder. No single test is perfect, which is why we look at everything together. Your memory score was 82 a year ago and 88 today. In dementia it usually drops; yours has gone up.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"It went up? Nobody told me that."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"It did. And most of the points you lost were on concentration tasks, holding numbers and focusing, which anxiety affects most. Your language and drawing were full marks.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:40","band":"Explain diagnosis","w":"D","s":"So putting it together, Linda, this is not dementia. It's generalised anxiety disorder: worry that's become too big, taking up the space your attention needs. Things don't go in, so it feels like memory.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"So it's my nerves, not my brain going?"},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly that. It's very treatable, with talking therapy or medication, and as the worry settles, your concentration should come back.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"And Mum's Alzheimer's? Will I get it anyway?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Because hers started in her eighties, your risk is only a little higher than average. The strongly inherited kind starts much younger. Looking after sleep, activity and blood pressure helps.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:26","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up for me what we've found today?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"The scan and bloods are normal, my score's gone up, and it's anxiety messing with my concentration. Not dementia."},{"t":"5:39","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. I'll write to your GP today about talking therapy, possibly medication, and reviewing the codeine. I'd also gently suggest pausing the nightly brain games. What questions do you have?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"None. I feel like I can breathe for the first time in a year. Thank you."},{"t":"5:57","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. If you ever notice real trouble with everyday tasks, see your GP, and if your mood drops, contact them or NHS 111. Take care, Linda.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Thorough history separating attention from memory loss, intact function confirmed, results explained one by one, and a clear 'anxiety, not dementia' message she can repeat.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but thin screen of mood, medicines or function, or results summarised as 'normal' without explaining the score rise.","fail":"Reassures without a history, misses function or codeine and alcohol, hedges with more tests, or never says clearly that it is not dementia."}},"c-t-42":{"setup":"Perinatal clinic, Wednesday afternoon. Leah Brennan, 23, has brought six-week-old Alfie. Her partner Dean drinks, smokes cannabis and once shook Alfie. Assess the risks to Leah and to Alfie, respond to her worries, and agree a risk plan.","role":{"who":"Ms Leah Brennan, 23, female, patient and Alfie's mother","open":"Yes, and this is Alfie.","manner":"Quiet and guarded, holds Alfie close. Opens up if not judged. Frightened and tearful when social services are mentioned; steadies when told the aim is support and that she is seen as protective.","free":["Low mood and tearful most days for about two months","Poor sleep, even when Alfie sleeps; constant worry","Loves Alfie, breastfeeding well","Lives with Dean and Alfie in a small one-bedroom flat"],"ifasked":["Depression at 19; sertraline helped; stopped after eight months when well","No suicidal thoughts, no self-harm, no thoughts of harming Alfie; no alcohol or drugs; no high or racing spells","Last Thursday night Dean, drunk, shook Alfie for a few seconds when he wouldn't stop crying; she took him off Dean","Alfie seemed fine afterwards; nobody has checked him","Dean drinks every night and smokes cannabis heavily; she takes Alfie out of the room when he smokes","Dean shouts, pushed her into a door when she was pregnant, takes her bank card, checks her phone and dislikes her seeing her mum","No sexual abuse; no thoughts of harming Dean","Her mum lives 20 minutes away and would have them"],"worries":["Will social services take Alfie?","What will Dean do if he finds out she told you?","Will medication get into her breast milk?"],"rules":["Hesitates before describing the shaking; tells it fully if asked gently and not judged","Becomes tearful and asks 'Are you going to take him off me?' when safeguarding is mentioned; settles if reassured honestly","Agrees to stay with her mum and go to A&E if the reasons are explained","Says Dean checks her phone only if asked what might stop the plan working"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, greets Alfie and explains the purpose of the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress and avoids blaming her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses mood, anxiety, sleep, bonding and the depressive episode at 19 with its treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis, mania or mixed features, and alcohol or drug use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, self-harm and thoughts of harming Alfie","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Establishes when and how Alfie was shaken, Dean's state, and whether Alfie was checked","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores Dean's substance use and abuse: physical, sexual, financial, controlling, isolating","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming Dean, her supports and protective actions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores her worries about social services and Dean's reaction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains honestly the safeguarding referral today and why","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees same-day paediatric check, safe place tonight and IDVA/DASH","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees mental health follow-up, therapy and a medication choice compatible with breastfeeding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks what might derail the plan and agrees safe contact","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives emergency numbers and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, and this is Alfie."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Hello Alfie. Thank you for coming, Leah. Your midwife asked me to see you because things have felt hard since he arrived.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear how you're both doing, including how things are at home, and then we'll plan together. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"I suppose so."},{"t":"0:25","band":"Her mood","w":"D","s":"How have you been feeling since he was born?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Low. I cry most days. I worry about everything, all the time."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard, on top of caring for a newborn. How's your sleep when Alfie is asleep, and how do you feel when you hold him?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Even when he sleeps, I can't. But I love him. He's the one good thing."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"I can see that. You had depression at 19. What helped then, and why did you stop it?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Sertraline. It helped. I stopped after about eight months because I felt fine."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Have you had times recently of feeling very high or racing, or hearing or seeing things others don't? Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that. I don't drink, I'm feeding him."},{"t":"1:16","band":"Risk to Leah and from Leah","w":"D","s":"When you're at your lowest, has it ever felt like life isn't worth going on with, or that you'd hurt yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never leave him."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"Lots of new mums get frightening thoughts about the baby that they'd never act on. Have you had any thoughts of harming Alfie?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Never. I just worry something will happen to him."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Risk to Alfie","w":"D","s":"Your midwife mentioned something happened with Dean. Can you tell me what happened, in your own time?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Last Thursday, at night. Dean had been drinking. Alfie wouldn't stop crying and Dean picked him up and shook him. A few seconds. I grabbed him off him."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"That must have been terrifying, and you acted fast to protect him. How was Alfie afterwards? Any sleepiness, being sick, poor feeding or fits?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"He cried, then fed. He seemed alright. I've been watching him."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Has anyone, a doctor, the midwife or health visitor, checked him since?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"No. I was too scared to say."},{"t":"2:18","band":"Dean and home","w":"D","s":"How much is Dean drinking and smoking, and is he around Alfie when he's like that?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Every night. Cans and weed. I take Alfie into the bedroom when he smokes."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Has Dean ever pushed, hit or hurt you in any way?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"He pushed me into the door once when I was pregnant. He shouts a lot."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry that happened to you. Does he control money, your phone, or who you see? Has he ever pressured you into sex or anything sexual?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"He takes my bank card. He hates me seeing my mum. Nothing sexual, no."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me. Have you ever felt like hurting Dean? And who else is around to support you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"No, I just want it to stop. My mum lives twenty minutes away. She'd have us."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Her worries","w":"D","s":"What worries you most about what happens next?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"That you'll call social services and they'll take him off me. Are you going to take him?"},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightened you are. What I've seen today is a mum who protects her baby: you took him off Dean, you keep him from the smoke, you've told me. That matters.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"So what happens?"},{"t":"3:38","band":"Safeguarding and plan","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you. Because Alfie was shaken, I have to tell children's social care today. Their aim is to keep children safe with their parents wherever possible, and I'll tell them how protective you've been.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"And Dean? He'll go mad."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"That's a real worry, and it's why I want you safe tonight. Shaking can cause injuries you can't see, so a children's doctor needs to check Alfie today. I'll ring ahead. Could you and Alfie stay at your mum's tonight?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"Yes. She'll have us. I'll go to A&E."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'd also like to go through some safety questions with you now and link you with an IDVA, a specialist adviser for people living with abuse. Is that okay?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Okay. I didn't know that existed."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"For your mood, our perinatal nurse will visit within a week, and we can refer you for talking therapy. Medication's your choice. Sertraline, which helped before, is one of the preferred options while breastfeeding.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Would it get into my milk?"},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"A tiny amount does, but it's considered safe for breastfed babies. Take this leaflet home and we'll decide together next week.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. I want to read about it first."},{"t":"4:53","band":"What could derail it","w":"D","s":"Once we've agreed this, what might get in the way of it working?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"Dean checks my phone. If he sees calls from you lot, he'll kick off."},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. What's the safest way for us and social care to reach you?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"My mum's number. I'll write it down."},{"t":"5:13","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what we've agreed, so I can check I've explained it clearly?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"A&E for Alfie today, Mum's tonight, you call social services and that adviser, and the nurse comes next week."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"That's it exactly. If Dean turns up or becomes violent, call 999. The domestic abuse helpline is 0808 2000 247, and if your mood gets worse, ring our duty line or 111, option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I thought you'd shout at me."},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"You've done the right thing today, Leah, for both of you. I'll ring A&E now, and we'll see you within the week.","m":[2,14]}],"global":{"pass":"Covers every direction of risk, details the shaking and the abuse, refers openly to social care, gets Alfie checked today, and agrees a safe place and follow-up with her.","borderline":"Identifies the risks but leaves gaps, such as no paediatric check, thin abuse screen or no question about what could derail the plan.","fail":"Focuses on mood alone, promises secrecy, omits the referral or the medical check, or frightens her with threats of removal."}},"c-t-44":{"setup":"Older adults ward, Thursday morning. Mr Desmond Clarke, 65, has severe depression unresponsive to fluoxetine and venlafaxine. His consultant suggested ECT. Explore his beliefs and worries and give him clear information to help him decide. Capacity and consent are not needed today.","role":{"who":"Mr Desmond Clarke, 65, male, patient, retired bus driver","open":"Yes. Des.","manner":"Slow, quiet and flat; sceptical and fearful about ECT. Becomes engaged and asks more questions once his fears are heard and the procedure is described plainly.","free":["Has heard 'terrible things' about ECT","Worried it will damage his memory","The tablets have not worked"],"ifasked":["His only picture of ECT is an old film where a man was held down and screamed","Has done the newspaper crossword every morning for forty years","Has stopped eating properly and feels a burden on his wife and daughter","Wants 'to feel like myself again'","Worried about his blood pressure and kidneys","Wants to talk to his wife Pauline and daughter Michelle before deciding"],"worries":["Why would ECT work when tablets haven't?","Would he still be himself afterwards?","Could it give him epilepsy or make him bite his tongue?","Is it safe with his blood pressure?"],"rules":["Becomes withdrawn if lectured before being asked what he thinks","Says 'Maybe I don't deserve fixing' when talking about being a burden; responds to warmth","Remains unsure if told memory is 'nothing to worry about'; trusts honest detail","Does not decide today; wants family involved"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, names his memory worry, and asks his view first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds with empathy to his fear and feelings of being a burden","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits his beliefs about ECT, main fear and hopes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why ECT is suggested now and its likely benefit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes workup, fasting, anaesthetic, muscle relaxant and brief seizure","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains frequency and usual number of treatments","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains common short-term side effects and very low serious risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives honest memory information and measures to reduce it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses blood pressure, kidneys and ramipril","d":"Content"},{"c":"States his right to refuse or stop, and outlines alternatives","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers leaflet, video, family talk and time to decide","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and closes with next steps","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the ward psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Des."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Des. Your consultant mentioned ECT, and I hear you're worried about your memory.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I am. I've heard terrible things."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Could I hear what you've heard first, then explain it properly? We're not deciding anything today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","band":"His ideas and fears","w":"D","s":"So what have you heard about ECT, and where from?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"An old film. They strapped a man down and he screamed and shook. That's what they'd do to me."},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"That's a terrifying picture. No wonder you're frightened. What worries you most?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"My memory. I've done the crossword every morning for forty years. If I lose that, I've lost me."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That clearly matters a great deal. And if a treatment did work, what would you hope to get back?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"To feel like myself. I've stopped eating. I'm just a burden on Pauline."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it's got so hard. Feeling a burden is part of the depression talking. Your family want you back, and you deserve to get better.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","band":"Why ECT now","w":"P","s":"Why would this work when the tablets didn't?"},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"A fair question after two disappointments. You've had two proper courses of tablets and you're still very unwell and not eating. ECT works differently, and nothing we have works better for depression this severe.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"People your age tend to respond especially well. Most people improve, often within a few weeks, which matters when you're not eating.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"But that film."},{"t":"1:37","band":"What happens","w":"D","s":"Modern ECT is very different. First we do blood tests, a heart tracing and an anaesthetist checks you over.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"On the morning, you don't eat. In the ECT suite you have a short anaesthetic and a muscle relaxant, so you're asleep and your body barely moves. You feel nothing.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"A small current causes a brief, controlled seizure in the brain, about a minute. A soft guard protects your teeth and tongue.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Will it give me epilepsy?"},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"No, ECT doesn't cause epilepsy. You wake within minutes, have a cup of tea, and come back to the ward.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"How many times?"},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Twice weekly as a rule, commonly six to twelve treatments in all. We review you after every one.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:24","band":"Side effects and memory","w":"D","s":"Afterwards some people have a headache, aching muscles or feel muddled for an hour or so. Paracetamol helps. Serious complications are very rare, about the same as any short anaesthetic.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"And my memory? Be honest."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"I will. Many people have gaps for events around the weeks of treatment. Those usually improve over weeks to months. A few people say some gaps last.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"To protect your memory, we'd usually start by placing the current on one side of the head, and test your memory at the start and as we go. If it's affected, we adjust or stop.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Would I still be me? Could I still do my crossword?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"That's the most important question. ECT doesn't change who you are. Most people find their concentration, and their crossword, come back as the depression lifts; depression itself is affecting your memory now.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"It is. I can't finish one at the moment."},{"t":"3:21","band":"Physical health","w":"D","s":"You have high blood pressure and your kidneys are a little weak. During treatment your blood pressure rises briefly, and the anaesthetist manages that.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"We'll check your kidney bloods first, and the anaesthetist will tell you whether to take your ramipril on treatment mornings.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"That's a relief. I'd been worrying about that."},{"t":"3:42","band":"Choice and alternatives","w":"D","s":"This is entirely your decision. You can say no, and if you say yes you can stop at any time, even after one treatment.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"What if I say no?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"Then we'd look at adding lithium or another medicine to your venlafaxine. Those can work, but usually take longer.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"I'd want to talk to Pauline and Michelle first."},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"That's very sensible. Shall we arrange a family meeting? Here's a leaflet and a short video, and the ECT nurse can show you the suite. Take your time.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'd like them to see the video."},{"t":"4:18","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what you'll take from today?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"I'd be asleep, twice a week, maybe six to twelve times. My memory might be patchy around it but usually comes back. And I can stop."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Once you've talked with your family, your consultant will go through consent with you. What other questions do you have?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"None for now. It's not what I thought."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open, Des. I'll book the family meeting and ask the nurses to help you with meals meanwhile.","m":[2,12]}],"global":{"pass":"Hears his fears first, explains rationale, procedure, course, side effects and memory honestly, covers his blood pressure and kidneys, and leaves a free choice with information and time.","borderline":"Accurate but generic explanation, or memory covered vaguely, or physical health and alternatives missed.","fail":"Lectures without exploring beliefs, claims no memory effects, ignores his medical conditions, or pressures him to agree."}},"c-t-45":{"setup":"Community clinic, urgent appointment. Jamal Whitfield, 28, had mania two years ago and stopped lithium two months ago. He sleeps two hours, is overspending and planning a film. Talk to him about the likely diagnosis and agree a management plan.","role":{"who":"Mr Jamal Whitfield, 28, male, patient, marketing assistant","open":"Yes! Good to meet you, doc. I'm brilliant.","manner":"Cheerful, fast, overfamiliar, jumps topics and talks over the doctor. Irritable if contradicted. Settles and engages when interrupted kindly and given real choices.","free":["Feels brilliant, with endless energy","Making a film that will win awards","Bought a £3,000 camera","Thinks his family worry too much"],"ifasked":["Sleeps about two hours a night and isn't tired","Stopped lithium two months ago: tremor, thirst, blood tests; was discharged from clinic so thought he was cured","Also bought a £1,500 sound deck; about £4,500 on a credit card","Approaching strangers in the town centre late at night to cast his film; one man got angry","Plans to hand in his notice this week","Drives everywhere, 'a bit fast'","No voices, no alcohol or drugs, no suicidal thoughts","Willing to stay with his parents"],"worries":["Will he be sectioned again?","Will medication kill his creativity?","He doesn't want lithium again"],"rules":["Talks continuously about the film unless interrupted kindly; accepts interruption if given a reason","Gets irritable if told the film is a delusion; calms if his feelings are acknowledged","Accepts 'this might be another high' if the reasons are explained","Accepts an antipsychotic and home treatment but refuses lithium today; agrees to discuss it later"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges family concern and invites his view","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Interrupts kindly and keeps control of the conversation with clear boundaries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds empathically to his fears about hospital and creativity","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits sleep, energy, mood, grandiosity, spending and disinhibition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores when and why he stopped lithium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risks: money, work, driving, vulnerability, aggression, psychosis, suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains that this is a manic relapse and why","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains that two episodes mean bipolar disorder, with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers an antipsychotic with benefits and side effects, and baseline tests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses lithium and alternatives for the long term, respecting his view","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees home treatment, family support, and admission if needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees safety steps: no driving and DVLA, money, work, fit note","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, consent to involve family, crisis contacts and follow-up","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jamal Whitfield?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes! Good to meet you, doc. I'm brilliant."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Good to meet you too, Jamal. Your GP and family have been worried, and I want to hear it from you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"They worry too much."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Let's talk about how things have been, then what might help. Okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"Sure! So I'm making this film, it's going to be massive, I've got the camera, the sound, I've been scouting faces all over town, and the script basically writes itself, and..."},{"t":"0:31","band":"Current symptoms","w":"D","s":"Can I pause you there, Jamal? It sounds exciting, and I want to make sure we cover what matters. How much are you sleeping?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Two hours. Don't need more. I'm at the gym twice a day."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"And do you feel tired at all? How long has it been like this?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Never tired. About a month. My brain's on fire, in a good way."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"What have you bought recently, and how did you pay?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"A three-grand camera and a sound deck. Credit card. About four and a half grand. It's an investment."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Who have you been approaching for the film, and where?","m":[4,6]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"People in town. Late, when the interesting faces come out. One guy got shirty, but whatever."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Stopping lithium","w":"D","s":"Thanks. When did you stop lithium, and what made you decide to?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Two months ago. The shakes, being thirsty all the time, the blood tests. The clinic discharged me, so I figured I was cured."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"That makes sense; those side effects are a real nuisance, and nobody likes the blood tests. Did you cut down slowly or stop all at once?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"All at once. Felt great a few weeks later."},{"t":"1:41","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Any plans to change your job?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"I'm handing my notice in this week. I'm too good for that place."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Are you driving at the moment? And has anyone been angry with you, or you with them?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Drive everywhere, bit fast maybe. My sister winds me up. I've not hit anyone."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Do you hear voices others don't, or feel you have special powers or a mission? Any alcohol or drugs? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"No voices. No drink, no drugs. Harm myself? I've never been happier. The film's my destiny, that's all."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Explain diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Jamal, I'm going to be straight with you. Two hours' sleep, never tired, huge plans, spending thousands, approaching strangers late at night: that looks like the high you had two years ago coming back. It's called mania.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill, doc. I'm the best I've ever been."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"I can see you feel amazing, and I don't want to take that from you. But last time also started with feeling amazing, and it ended in hospital. Stopping lithium all at once is probably what tipped it.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Okay. Maybe. My mum said the same."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Having two episodes like this means the diagnosis is bipolar disorder. It's common and treatable, and with the right plan many people stay well for years, work and create.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Okay, say I believe you. What do you want me to do about it?"},{"t":"3:05","band":"Treatment plan","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest a medicine today to bring the high down and help you sleep. Olanzapine at night works well. It can make you sleepy and hungry and put on weight, so we'll check your weight and blood tests today and as we go.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"Weight? I'm at the gym twice a day."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"Fair point. We'll monitor it, and if it's a problem there are other options, like aripiprazole. For sleep in the first few nights, I can add a small dose of lorazepam.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"And lithium? I'm not going back on it."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"We don't have to decide that today. Lithium is the best medicine for keeping highs away long term, and tremor can often be reduced by adjusting the dose. Once you're settled, we'll go through it and the alternatives.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Fine. As long as you're not sectioning me again."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"I can hear that last time was frightening. I'd much rather treat you at home. The home treatment team can visit every day, and you could stay with your parents for a while.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Mum's is okay. Just not hospital."},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"That's the plan. If the medicine isn't helping or things become unsafe, hospital would be the next step, but we'd talk about it first.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Will this stuff kill my creativity, though?"},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Lots of creative people worry about that. The aim is to get you sleeping and thinking clearly, so the film actually gets finished, not lost in another admission.","m":[3]},{"t":"4:28","band":"Safety steps","w":"D","s":"A few safety steps for now. While you're this high, you mustn't drive, and you'll need to tell the DVLA. Could your mum drive you?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"The DVLA? Seriously? Fine, she can."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Let's hold off on quitting your job or buying more for a few weeks. Could your parents look after the credit card? I'll write a fit note for two weeks, and see if the equipment can go back.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"That's a lot. But okay, for a few weeks."},{"t":"4:56","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Tablet tonight, blood tests now, team tomorrow, stay at Mum's, no driving, no big spending, no quitting. Talk about lithium later."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"Perfect. May I share the plan with your parents, so they can help?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"Yeah, go on."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Here's the crisis line number; you or your family can ring it any time. I'll see you next week, Jamal. Thanks for hearing me out.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Keeps the lead kindly, gathers clear evidence of mania, names mania and bipolar disorder, and agrees medication, home treatment, family support and safety limits with admission as a fallback.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and medication, but loses control of the conversation for long stretches, or misses driving, money or work.","fail":"Lets him dominate, argues about the film, insists on admission or detention without reason, or offers no clear medication plan."}},"c-t-46":{"setup":"Tuesday early evening, an acute medical ward. You have assessed Liam Fairweather, 35, who has a mild learning disability and is refusing tests for a stomach bleed. Explain your findings to his support worker, agree a plan and address her concerns.","role":{"who":"Ms Tessa Oduya, 41, female, Liam's support worker in his supported housing","open":"Yes. How is he?","manner":"Anxious, protective and fond of Liam; asks direct questions in turn. Becomes tearful about him being held down; settles once she hears she has a role in the plan.","free":["Came in the ambulance with him and saw him vomit blood","Liam is usually chatty, loves football and trusts staff","He has been jumpy for a few weeks and talks about police coming in at night","She wants to know whether he can refuse"],"ifasked":["Found risperidone tablets in his bin twice over the past three weeks","He won't let night staff into his room now","Picture cards and doing one step at a time calm him","His mother lives about 20 miles away and sees him at Christmas; Tessa has her number","His social worker only sees him at annual reviews","He has never said what he'd want if seriously ill"],"worries":["Shouldn't his mother decide?","Can it wait until tomorrow?","Is it lawful to override what he wants?","Will he be held down?"],"rules":["Asks 'Can I sign for him?' if proxy consent is not raised","Becomes tearful about restraint; calms if validated and given a role","Accepts the plan readily once the reasons are explained plainly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the liaison psychiatrists. Are you Tessa, Liam's support worker?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. How is he?"},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"He's still bleeding and still refusing tests. I've just assessed whether he can make this decision. You know him well, so I'd value your view. Can I share what I found and then plan with you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Of course. I came in the ambulance with him. I've never seen that much blood."},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"That must have been frightening, and you've stayed with him all afternoon. Thank you. Before I explain, what's Liam usually like, and has anything changed lately?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:38","band":"Her view of Liam","w":"P","s":"Normally he's chatty, loves his football, trusts us. The last few weeks he's been jumpy, saying the police come in at night."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Has he been taking his risperidone, as far as you know?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"I found tablets in his bin twice. About three weeks now. He won't let night staff in his room either."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"So it's a real change from his usual self, and it may be linked to missing his medicine. I'll come back to that. What helps him when he's scared?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Picture cards. Doing one thing at a time. Someone he knows next to him. So does he have the capacity to say no?"},{"t":"1:20","band":"Capacity findings","w":"D","s":"I'll be straight with you. For this decision, today, I don't think he does. Can I explain how I got there?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Please."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"The law asks four things. Can he understand the information, hold on to it, weigh it up, and tell us his choice? He understands, he remembers, and he tells us clearly he says no.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"So what's the problem?"},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"It's the weighing up. He believes the blood test is an injection to make him bleed and the camera is for the police. Because of that, he can't balance the risk of dying against the tests at all. The belief seems to come from his mental state.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"He's always been stubborn, though. Isn't he allowed to make a bad decision?"},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Yes, and that matters. If he could weigh it up and still said no, we'd respect it, however unwise. The difference is he can't use the information because of a belief that isn't based on fact.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"So he can't decide anything for himself now?"},{"t":"2:21","band":"What it means","w":"D","s":"No. It's only this decision, and only now. He can still choose what he eats or who visits. Once he's safer we'll check again, and record it each time.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's a relief, actually. But shouldn't his mum be the one deciding? She's his next of kin."},{"t":"2:40","band":"Best interests plan","w":"D","s":"It's a fair question. Nobody can give consent on another adult's behalf, not his mum and not you, unless they hold a health and welfare power of attorney. But her views, and yours, are part of deciding what's best for him.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"So I can't sign for him?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"No, but what you know is worth more than a signature here. Best interests means what Liam himself would want, his values, what makes him feel safe, and the gentlest way to do it.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Can't it just wait till tomorrow, when he's calmer?"},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"I wish it could. He's vomited blood twice and the medical team are worried he could bleed heavily again tonight. The bloods and drip are needed now, and the camera test as soon as he's stable enough.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"I just keep picturing them holding him down. He'd never trust me again."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"That's a really understandable fear, and it shows how much you care about him. Nobody wants that. Let me tell you how we'll try to avoid it.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"First we persuade, with you beside him. Picture cards, short words, one step at a time. The learning disability liaison nurse will help. For the camera test, the team can give sedation so he's not aware of much.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"And if he still says no?"},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Then staff may hold him using only the force needed, for the shortest time, to prevent serious harm, and it's written down. It's the last resort, not the plan.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"Is that actually legal, going against what he says?"},{"t":"4:12","band":"Safeguards and people","w":"D","s":"Yes. When someone lacks capacity and treatment is urgent, the Mental Capacity Act protects staff acting in his best interests. If there were a serious disagreement, say from his mum, we'd take legal advice and the court could decide, but that's rare.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Right. Do you want her number? She'll want to know."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Yes please. I'll phone her straight after this, and let his social worker know today too.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Does he need one of those advocates?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"An independent advocate is needed for serious treatment when there's no family or friend to consult. His mum can be consulted, so probably not, and we won't delay emergency treatment for one. If she can't be reached, we'll refer.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"And the police thing? That's just not like him."},{"t":"4:58","band":"Mental health","w":"D","s":"I agree. It may be a return of psychotic symptoms, possibly from missing his risperidone. Once he's stable, I'll see him again, review his medicine and let his own psychiatrist know.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Could you section him to get the tests done?"},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"No. The Mental Health Act covers treatment for mental illness, not his stomach. The tests come under the Mental Capacity Act. Later we might look at supervised medicines at the house.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:25","band":"Hand over and close","w":"D","s":"Can I check I've explained it clearly? How would you sum up what happens now?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"He can't make this choice right now because of the police belief. The doctors act in his best interests, I help keep him calm, you ring his mum, and nobody holds him unless there's no other way."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"That's exactly it. What else is on your mind?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Can I stay with him for the camera test?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the endoscopy team; I think it would help. I'll write all this in his notes, tell the ward team, and review him again tonight or first thing tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel better knowing the plan."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Tessa. Shall we go and see him together?","m":[1,13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, states urgency, and invites her as a partner","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fear before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gathers her view: usual self, recent change, police beliefs, missed risperidone, what calms him","d":"Content"},{"c":"States clearly that he lacks capacity for this decision now","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the four abilities and why weighing up fails, linked to his mental state","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains unwise decisions are allowed, and capacity is decision- and time-specific","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains best interests: his wishes and values, consultation, least restrictive approach","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States that nobody, including mother or carer, can give proxy consent","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why treatment cannot wait until tomorrow","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Addresses restraint: last resort, proportionate, brief, recorded; sedation and persuasion first","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans psychiatric follow-up and explains the MHA does not cover the bleed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Involves mother and social worker; explains when an IMCA or court is needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, invites concerns, documents and gives a review time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gives the finding plainly, explains the four abilities, answers consent, urgency, legality and restraint, and agrees a gentle, lawful plan with her.","borderline":"Correct finding but thin reasoning, or questions answered vaguely without a clear role for Tessa or a mental health plan.","fail":"Says he has capacity because he understands, lets family consent, uses the MHA for the bleed, or agrees to wait."}},"c-t-47":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, the community learning disability clinic. Patricia Okoye, in her fifties, has come about her son Callum, 26, who has a mild intellectual disability; his partner is six months pregnant. Explore her concerns, reassure her and discuss how to manage the situation.","role":{"who":"Mrs Patricia Okoye, 57, female, Callum's mother","open":"Yes. Patricia.","manner":"Tired, blunt and firm at first; insists on the operation. Tearful when asked about herself. Softens noticeably once told she will not have to raise the baby.","free":["Wants Callum sterilised","Jade is six months pregnant","Fears the baby will be disabled 'like Callum'","Fears social services will hand the baby to her"],"ifasked":["Callum was starved of oxygen at birth and born by emergency caesarean","He exposed himself to an adult woman at a bus stop last week; police interviewed him; he is on bail","Years ago he did the same on a stairwell when stressed about money","He has never touched anyone","Jade's scans were reported normal","Patricia works nights in a pub, sleeps badly, cries before work, has few friends","No thoughts of harming herself or anyone else"],"worries":["Will you arrange the operation?","Will the baby be disabled?","Who will look after the baby?","Will Callum go to prison?"],"rules":["Repeats the demand if the doctor lectures before listening","Becomes tearful when asked how she is; settles if validated","Visibly relieved when told she will not be expected to raise the baby"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mrs Okoye, Callum's mum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Patricia."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Patricia. It sounds like you've carried a lot for many years.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"You've no idea."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear your worries, then talk about what can realistically be done. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"I'll get to it. I want him sterilised. He can't cope with a baby, and neither can I."},{"t":"0:24","band":"Her concerns","w":"D","s":"That's a big thing to ask, and I can hear how worn out you are. Tell me what's worrying you most.","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"Jade's six months gone. The baby'll be like Callum, I know it. And social services will dump it on me. I work nights in a pub. I've nothing left."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"So it's the baby having a disability, ending up raising it yourself, money and tiredness. Anything else?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"The police. He's done that thing again."},{"t":"0:54","band":"Callum's situation","w":"D","s":"I know that's hard to talk about. Can you tell me what happened?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"He exposed himself. Outside the shops, to a woman at the bus stop. Same as years back on the stairs, when he owed money on his phone."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Was it an adult, and what did the police do?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"A grown woman. They interviewed him and he's on bail. He said he panicked because people keep saying they'll take the baby."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Has anything like that happened any other time, or has he ever touched anyone?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"No. Never touched anyone. Just those two times, both when he was in a state."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"And who supports him and Jade at the flat now?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Staff pop in twice a day. He's got a social worker but she's new."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Her wellbeing","w":"D","s":"You've been looking after everyone. How are you yourself? Your sleep, your mood?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"I don't sleep. I cry in the car before work. Twenty-six years on my own. Nobody's ever asked me that."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"I'm really sorry nobody's asked. That's a long time to carry it all alone, and it makes sense you're exhausted. Have you ever felt so low you've thought of harming yourself?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'm just tired. And skint."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll come back to help for you. Can I talk about the operation first?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Sterilisation explained","w":"D","s":"I can't arrange that operation, and I want to be honest about why. Sterilisation is permanent, so it's Callum's own decision. Nobody can consent for him, not me and not you.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"But he doesn't understand things."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"If he can understand the choice with good explanation, it's his. If he couldn't, a court would have to decide, and only if it was for his own benefit, not to make things easier for others.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"So you're saying no."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"I'm saying it isn't anyone else's decision. Callum has the same right to a relationship and a family as anyone. We can help him and Jade with contraception choices and easy-read relationships education, if they want it.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"And if the baby's disabled like him?"},{"t":"3:11","band":"Baby and parenting","w":"D","s":"Can I ask what you were told about why Callum has a learning disability?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"He was starved of oxygen when he was born. Emergency section."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"That's an injury at birth, not something in his genes, so it isn't passed on. Does anything run in Jade's family, and how were her scans?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Not that I know. They said the scans looked fine."},{"t":"3:31","w":"D","s":"That's reassuring; the main scans pick up most major problems. If any family history comes up, the midwife can arrange genetic advice.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"And when it's born? Who looks after it?"},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Children's social care will assess before the birth. The aim is to help Callum and Jade parent, with support designed for parents with a learning disability. A baby is only removed if it isn't safe even with help.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"And then they'll just hand it to me."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"No. You won't be expected to raise this baby. How involved you are as a grandmother is your choice, not your duty.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"Really? I thought it'd be me by default."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Really. If anyone ever asked about family care, you could say no, and that would be respected. I can see that's a weight off.","m":[11,2]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"It is. But what about the exposing? He'll end up in prison."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Managing the offending","w":"D","s":"I take it seriously. Our team can assess what led to it and how likely it is to happen again. A psychologist can help him handle stress in safer ways and understand what's appropriate.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Will the police know about his disability?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"We'll make sure they and his solicitor do, and he should have an appropriate adult with him in any interview. We'll also think about keeping the public safe while we work with him.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"Money's what sets him off, every time."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Then a benefits check and budgeting help for him, and for you. I'd also like to refer you for a carer's assessment from the council, which can lead to breaks and practical help, and suggest you see your GP about sleep and mood.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"I didn't know I could get anything."},{"t":"5:13","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"You can. So: the operation is Callum's own decision; his cause isn't inherited; social care will support them as parents, and it isn't on you; our team will work with Callum on the exposing; and you get help in your own right. What would you tell a friend about today?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"That it's not all on me. Callum gets help, and so do I."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll see Callum with you in two weeks and write to your GP. If you ever feel at breaking point, call your GP or NHS 111, option 2.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Thank you, doctor. That's more than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her years of caring, and agrees the plan for the conversation","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her exhaustion and distress at key moments","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits all her concerns: sterilisation, baby's health, burden on her, money","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores Callum's situation: the incidents, triggers and current support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks precisely about the exposing: victim's age, police outcome, repeats, any contact","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens her mood, sleep and thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains only Callum can consent; court only if he lacked capacity, for his benefit","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains his equal rights and less restrictive options such as contraception and education","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers inheritance using the cause of his disability, family history and scans","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains pre-birth assessment and adapted parenting support; removal only if unsafe","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Reassures her clearly that she will not be expected to raise the baby","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans help for the exposing and stress: psychology, police liaison, appropriate adult, money","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers carer's assessment and GP review, summarises, checks understanding, arranges follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears every fear, refuses the operation clearly with reasons, answers inheritance and parenting with facts, plans for the exposing, and supports Patricia herself.","borderline":"Correct on the law but misses the exposing details or her own wellbeing, or reassures without facts.","fail":"Agrees to pursue sterilisation, lets the mother consent, promises removal of the baby, or ignores her distress."}},"c-t-48":{"setup":"Monday late morning, a prison healthcare unit. Shane Mulvey, 32, has had voices and urges to harm others since witnessing an assault. Nurse Kiran Bhatt wants to talk. Get a brief handover, then agree your initial plan, including whether he can return to the wing.","role":{"who":"Mr Kiran Bhatt, 44, male, mental health nurse on the prison healthcare unit","open":"Yes, thanks for coming.","manner":"Busy, frustrated by bed pressure, sure it is GHB; pushes back on risk. Becomes more cooperative once his pressures are acknowledged and the reasons are clear.","free":["Thinks it is GHB; GHB has been going round the wing","Wants the bed back; two prisoners waiting","Shane has been polite and caused no trouble here"],"ifasked":["Night one: sweaty, shaky, fast pulse, little sleep; settled by day two","Still hears a voice warning he will be attacked; sits with his back to the wall","Asked twice whether officers can read his thoughts","Told the GP he gets urges to hurt people; no named target","Urine screen negative; no bloods taken; only the prison GP has examined him","Said he'd rather die than be attacked; rumoured to owe someone on the wing","Eats little"],"worries":["Isn't it just GHB?","Why is he a risk if he's been fine?","When can he go back to the wing?","Are you going to start medication?"],"rules":["Pushes back each time risk is raised, until reasons are given","Gives the urine result, bloods and self-harm comment only if asked","Vents about bed pressure once; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the forensic psychiatry doctors. Are you Kiran, looking after Mr Mulvey?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, thanks for coming."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Could you give me a quick handover: how he's been since arriving, observations, any incidents, and drug test results? Then I'll share my thinking and we'll agree the plan, including the wing question.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Sure. Honestly, I think it's GHB. Half our beds are drug cases. He's been no bother, and I need that bed."},{"t":"0:25","w":"D","s":"I hear you; beds are tight and you've had a lot of these. Let's look at what we've got, then decide together.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","band":"Handover","w":"P","s":"First night he was sweaty and shaky and hardly slept. Pulse was a bit fast. By day two that settled. Eats a bit. Polite, keeps to himself."},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"What's he saying about the voices now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Still says a voice warns him he'll be attacked. Sits with his back to the wall. Asked me twice if the officers can read his thoughts."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Any incidents, threats or aggression since he came?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"None. Obs are fine now."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And what drug testing has been done, and who has examined him?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Urine was negative. The prison GP checked him on arrival. No bloods."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks. A standard screen often doesn't include GHB, and it clears within hours, so negative doesn't settle it either way.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Wing says GHB's been going round. He used all sorts before he came in."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Risk to others","w":"D","s":"Understood. He's here for assault. Has he said anything about harming anyone, or named anyone?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"He told the GP he gets urges to hurt people. Didn't name anyone. But he hasn't done anything."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Voices telling him he must defend himself, a feeling of being controlled, urges to hurt people, and a violent offence. Together, that's a combination I take seriously.","m":[5,7]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"He's been as good as gold. You're making a lot of it."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"I can see why it looks that way, and calm here is good news. But it's two days on a quiet unit with staff around. On a busy wing, with a threat he believes is real, it could look very different.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:01","band":"Risk to self","w":"P","s":"Fair enough."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"What about risk to himself: self-harm, low mood, talk of dying, or debts and bullying on the wing?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"He said he'd rather die than be attacked. No self-harm. Word is he owes someone."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"That matters. A drug debt makes him vulnerable on the wing, and that comment needs following up today.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:22","band":"What it might be","w":"P","s":"So what do you reckon it is?"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Most likely an episode of psychosis: voices from outside his head, feeling controlled, thoughts being read. Drugs could be part of it, and seeing that attack may have set it off. But it started weeks before he came here, so withdrawal alone doesn't explain it.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"GHB withdrawal can make people psychotic, though."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"It can, usually with confusion, shaking and sweats in the first days. His shakes settled; the voices didn't. Honestly, we can't be sure yet, because nobody has done a full mental state or risk assessment.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"2:59","band":"Initial management","w":"P","s":"So what do you want us to do?"},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"I'll see him myself this afternoon. Before then, could you take bloods: blood count, kidney and liver tests, glucose, thyroid and CK, plus an ECG and a repeat urine screen? And keep him on regular observations.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"We can do that. Are you starting him on anything?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Not until I've assessed him. If psychosis is confirmed, I'd likely offer an antipsychotic. For now, promethazine 25 milligrams as needed if he's distressed, rather than repeated benzodiazepines, unless withdrawal signs come back.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"I'd also like to speak to staff on his old wing and, with his agreement, his family, and check any previous mental health records.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:40","band":"Wing and transfer","w":"P","s":"And the wing? I've got two waiting for this bed."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"I know that puts you in a hard spot. But he isn't ready. Sending him back before we know what this is risks him hurting someone, or being hurt. He stays here until I've reassessed him.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"When will that be?"},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Today, then again over the next few days. If it's drug-related and settles, he may go back within days. If it's psychosis needing treatment, he'll need transfer to hospital, because we can't treat him against his will in prison.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"How long does a transfer take?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"It needs two doctors' recommendations and Ministry of Justice agreement; the aim is within 28 days. Meanwhile he stays here.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Sorry. It's just relentless. Every bed's a battle and I get it in the neck from both sides."},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and you're doing a hard job well. I'll speak to the healthcare manager about the bed myself, so this decision isn't sitting on you.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"Thanks. Anything else you need?"},{"t":"4:41","band":"Hand over and close","w":"D","s":"Please share the risk with wing and security staff on a need-to-know basis, and consider an ACCT because of that comment about dying. If he becomes aggressive, confused or unwell, call the emergency response and me straight away.","m":[6,13]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Will do."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"So, to recap: probable psychosis, drugs possibly involved; real risk to others and some to himself; bloods, ECG, urine and observations today; I see him this afternoon; he stays here; transfer if needed. Does that work for you?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Yes. Makes sense, even if the bed manager won't like it."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"I'll document it now and call the bed manager myself. I'll be back by three.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"Great. I'll get the bloods done."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role, and asks for a structured handover with a clear aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges the nurse's view and pressures respectfully, including his frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Obtains handover on sleep, eating, voices, behaviour, observations and incidents","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies drug testing, its limits, withdrawal signs and who has examined him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores risk to others: offence, voices to defend, control, urges, targets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores risk to self: self-harm, talk of dying, debt and bullying","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains probable psychosis, with substances and trauma as possible factors","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States that no full assessment has yet been done, so uncertainty remains","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans bloods, ECG, repeat urine screen and collateral information","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Commits to see him today; medication only after assessment; observations continued","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Keeps him on healthcare despite bed pressure, giving reasons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains hospital transfer under the MHA and that enforced treatment is not possible in prison","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees risk sharing, ACCT, escalation, documentation and review time; summarises","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets a structured handover, names probable psychosis and the risks with reasons, plans tests and own review, and keeps him on healthcare despite pressure.","borderline":"Right decision but thin reasoning, or misses risk to self or the route to transfer.","fail":"Accepts the GHB explanation and sends him back, or starts treatment without assessment."}},"c-t-49":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, the urgent CMHT clinic. Rhys Llewellyn, 28, recovered from mania on lithium two years ago but stopped it two months ago. His GP referred him urgently. Explain what you think is happening and agree a plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Rhys Llewellyn, 28, male, graphic designer","open":"That's right. I haven't got long, I'm busy.","manner":"Fast, charming, flattering; tries to cast the doctor in his video. Irritable when challenged. Upset briefly when told it is illness; accepts a home plan once explained clearly.","free":["Feels on top of the world with endless ideas","Making a music video; a famous band will 'definitely' want it","Goes to the gym twice a day and works late","Wants to get back to work and his project"],"ifasked":["Sleeps about two hours a night and does not feel tired","Spent about £4,000 on credit on cameras, lights and a mixing desk","Stops strangers in cafés and the street to be in the video","Stopped lithium abruptly two months ago because of tremor and feeling flat","Drives to work, 'fast maybe'","Manager told him to slow down and take days off","No alcohol, drugs or new sexual partners; no thoughts of harm"],"worries":["Will I be sectioned again?","Will tablets make me a zombie?","Is it all just illness?","Will I lose my job?"],"rules":["Tries to recruit the doctor into the video early; accepts a kind redirection","Becomes irritable if lectured before being asked why he stopped lithium","Agrees to stay with his parents and take time off if hospital is not insisted on"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhys Llewellyn?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"That's right. I haven't got long, I'm busy."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for making time, Rhys. Your GP and parents are worried, and I'd like your side of it. We'll be as quick as we can. Then we'll agree what happens next.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Fine. Honestly, I've never felt better. I'm making a music video and it's going to be massive. You'd be great in it, actually. Great face for camera."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Current picture","w":"D","s":"That's kind, but I'll stick to being your doctor today. Tell me how you've been feeling these last few weeks.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"On top of the world. Ideas everywhere. Gym twice a day, work till ten, then the video at home."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"How much are you sleeping?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Two hours, maybe. Don't need more. Sleep's for people with no ideas."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"And money? Your GP mentioned new equipment.","m":[4]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Cameras, a mixing desk, lights. About four grand on my credit card. It's an investment. A famous band will definitely want it."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Who's going to be in the video?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"People I meet. I stop them in cafés, in the street. Some look at me like I'm mad."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"And how do you feel when people question your plans?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Irritated. My manager keeps saying slow down. My parents too. They don't get it."},{"t":"1:17","band":"Lithium story","w":"D","s":"That sounds frustrating, when people won't share your excitement. Can I ask about lithium? What made you stop?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"It made me feel flat. And my hands shook; I couldn't draw properly. So I stopped, about two months ago."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"All at once?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Binned them."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"A shake is a real problem for a designer, and feeling flat is a fair complaint. Thanks for being honest.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"So can I go now?"},{"t":"1:44","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Nearly. A few safety questions. Any drinking, drugs, or relationships you wouldn't usually have?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"No drink, no drugs. Nothing like that. Just work."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"Are you driving at the moment? And has anyone taken advantage of your money or your plans?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"I drive to work. Fast, maybe. Nobody's taken advantage."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Any sense you've got special powers, or that people are against you? Any urge to hurt yourself, or other people?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"No powers. I'm just very talented. No harming anyone. I've never felt more alive."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"And how is work, honestly?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"My manager said take some days off. I might go solo anyway."},{"t":"2:22","band":"Diagnosis","w":"D","s":"Rhys, may I share my view of what's happening? Very little sleep without feeling tired, huge energy, big spending, racing plans and getting irritable. Those are the signs of mania, the same pattern as two years ago.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Two years ago I was in hospital. This isn't that."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"I think it's an earlier stage of the same thing; last time it went further before help came. A second episode confirms bipolar disorder, a long-term tendency for mood to swing high or low.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"So I'm bipolar for life now?"},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"It's a lifelong tendency, yes, but many people stay well for years with the right treatment and by spotting early signs. Stopping lithium suddenly also makes a high more likely, which is probably part of what's happened.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"I've worked so hard on this. You're telling me it's all just illness. That's gutting."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"That's a hard thing to hear, and I'm sorry. Your talent is real. It's the lack of sleep and the spending that worry me. Getting well protects your work and your job.","m":[3,7]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Okay. What would you actually want me to take?"},{"t":"3:30","band":"Treatment","w":"D","s":"Two parts. First, to settle this quickly, an antipsychotic called quetiapine, at night, built up over a few days. It helps sleep and slows things down. Main downsides are drowsiness and weight gain, so we'd check your weight and bloods.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"I don't want to be a zombie."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"Fair. Aripiprazole is less sedating, though it can make some people restless. Either way it's usually for some weeks, then we review.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"And lithium?"},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Lithium worked well for you, and it's the best long-term protection. The shake can often be eased with a lower dose or another tablet, and once levels are steady the blood tests drop to every three months.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"Every three months I could live with. Maybe later."},{"t":"4:14","w":"D","s":"That's fine. We start quetiapine now and talk about lithium again in a week.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:19","band":"Where you get well","w":"P","s":"Do I have to go into hospital?"},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"I'd rather you didn't need to. The home treatment team could visit daily. Would staying at your parents' place for a while, with time off work, be doable?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"My parents would love that. Work too, probably."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"Then that's the plan. If things got worse, or treatment stopped and you weren't safe, hospital would come back on the table. I'll be honest: the Mental Health Act is used if someone can't be kept safe any other way.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Alright. Fair enough."},{"t":"4:48","w":"D","s":"Two practical things. While you're high, please don't drive, and you'll need to tell the DVLA. And could your dad look after your cards for a few weeks? I'll do a fit note so your job's protected.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"Ugh. Fine, the cards. The driving's annoying, but okay."},{"t":"5:05","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've been clear. What will you tell your parents tonight?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"That I'm high again, it's bipolar, I'm starting quetiapine tonight, staying with them, no driving, Dad gets my cards, and the team comes tomorrow."},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If you feel worse, or anyone's worried, ring the crisis line or NHS 111, option 2. I'll see you in a week.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks. Sorry for being a pain."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his time and family concern, invites his view","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Holds boundaries and keeps focus, redirecting kindly when he digresses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and disappointment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits mood, sleep, energy, spending, approaching strangers and irritability","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores why and how he stopped lithium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk: substances, sexual risk, driving, exploitation, psychotic beliefs, harm, work","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names mania clearly, using his own symptoms as evidence","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains bipolar disorder and the link to stopping lithium suddenly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers an antipsychotic now with benefits, side effects and an alternative","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses lithium for long-term protection and how to manage tremor and tests","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees home treatment with family, admission as fallback, MHA mentioned honestly","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses driving and DVLA, cards with a relative, and a fit note","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses teach-back, gives crisis contacts and a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Holds the frame, names mania and bipolar disorder with his own evidence, and agrees medication and a concrete home plan with safeguards.","borderline":"Right diagnosis but loses control of time, or a vague plan without driving, money or follow-up.","fail":"Lets him dominate, never names mania, insists on admission despite agreement, or offers no effective medication."}},"c-t-50":{"setup":"Friday morning, a psychiatry outpatient clinic. Dr Farhan Qureshi, 40, a consultant microbiologist off work with depression, doubts further medicine will help after reading about the serotonin theory. Respond to his worries and agree a management plan with him.","role":{"who":"Dr Farhan Qureshi, 40, male, consultant microbiologist","open":"Yes. Farhan, please. No need for the title here.","manner":"Clipped, sceptical, arms folded; tests the doctor with the serotonin question. Embarrassed about the sexual side effect. Opens up when not patronised and when options are explained clearly.","free":["Has read a paper claiming serotonin's role in depression has been disproved","Doubts any further medicine will help","Off work three weeks and wants to go back soon","Depression began as his marriage ended"],"ifasked":["Sleeps about five hours, waking at four; eats little; cannot concentrate on journal articles","Two glasses of wine most nights","Passive thoughts that his children would cope without him; no plan; takes nothing from work","Sertraline 100 mg for 8 weeks: no effect","Paroxetine 30 mg for 6 weeks: darker thoughts, stopped","Mirtazapine 45 mg for about 7 weeks: sleeps better; ejaculatory problems and weight gain","Sees his children every other weekend"],"worries":["Why would any antidepressant work if the serotonin theory is wrong?","What else is there besides tablets?","Will therapy be just chatting?","Does he have to tell the GMC?"],"rules":["Becomes curt if the doctor defends the chemical imbalance idea","Mentions the sexual side effect only if asked about side effects","Becomes briefly upset about being a doctor who cannot fix himself; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dr Farhan Qureshi?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Farhan, please. No need for the title here."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Farhan. I understand you've been reading about the serotonin debate and have real doubts about tablets.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I just don't see the point any more."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That's fair to raise. Could we look at how you are now, then go through the evidence and options together?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Fine. But I've read the papers. I'm not a typical patient."},{"t":"0:26","band":"His depression now","w":"D","s":"You're clearly well read, and I'll talk to you that way. Can you tell me how this started?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"When my wife left last year. The divorce went through in the spring. Since then everything's grey."},{"t":"0:39","w":"D","s":"That's a lot of loss at once. How's your sleeping, eating and energy these days?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Five hours, waking at four. Eating little. I can't concentrate on a journal article, which for me is telling."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"What do you still enjoy? And are you drinking to cope at all?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Nothing much. Even seeing the kids isn't the same. A couple of glasses of wine most nights."},{"t":"1:03","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"With everything you've described, I always ask: has it ever felt like life isn't worth carrying on with?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think the children would cope without me. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you made any plans, or thought about using anything from work?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"No plans. And no, I don't take anything from work."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"What keeps you going?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"The children. Every other weekend."},{"t":"1:27","band":"Medicines so far","w":"D","s":"Let's go through the medicines. What have you taken, at what dose, and for how long?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Sertraline 100 for two months, nothing. Paroxetine 30 for six weeks; I got darker thoughts, so I stopped. Now mirtazapine 45 for about seven weeks."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Did you take them every day? And what's mirtazapine doing for you, good and bad?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Every day. It helps me sleep, but it's embarrassing. I've had trouble ejaculating since I started it. And I've put on weight."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That's common, often the real reason people give up on a medicine, and it's fixable rather than something to put up with.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:06","band":"The serotonin question","w":"P","s":"Here's my real question. The serotonin theory's been debunked. So why would any of these work?"},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"You're right: the neat story that depression is simply low serotonin hasn't held up; a large review in 2022 found no consistent evidence for it. But that's separate from whether antidepressants work. Large trials across many thousands of people show they help, more than placebo, though not for everyone.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"So you don't actually know how they work."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Not fully. They probably work through slower changes in how the brain processes emotion and adapts, not just by topping up serotonin. A bit like paracetamol: used for decades before anyone understood its mechanism.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"Fair point, I suppose. It's just... I'm a consultant. I'm meant to fix things. I can't even fix myself."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, and being a doctor can make it harder to be a patient. Depression isn't a failure of effort. It's an illness, and it's treatable.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:04","band":"Options","w":"P","s":"Okay. So what are the options?"},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"After three medicines, I'd suggest switching to vortioxetine. It acts on several serotonin receptors in a different way and has fewer sexual side effects. Nausea in the first week is the main one. NICE recommends it once two antidepressants haven't helped.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"And if that doesn't work?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Agomelatine is another option. It acts on melatonin receptors, helps sleep, and has little sexual effect, but needs liver tests in the first months. If a full dose of the new medicine doesn't help after four to six weeks, adding lithium or a low dose of aripiprazole can boost the effect.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:42","w":"P","s":"I've read about magnetic stimulation."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Yes, repetitive TMS: magnetic pulses over the front of the head, daily on weekdays for around four to six weeks. It's safe and NICE supports it, but availability varies and daily sessions can clash with work.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"And therapy? I had counselling once. It felt like chatting."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"This would be different. CBT is structured: you'd examine how a thought such as 'I've failed' shapes your feelings and actions, then test and change them, with tasks between sessions, around sixteen sessions. At your level of depression, combining it with medication works best.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"That sounds more my style."},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"So which would you prefer to start with: switching to vortioxetine alongside CBT, or another option?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Vortioxetine and CBT. I'll give it a fair go."},{"t":"4:31","band":"Return to work","w":"D","s":"Good. On work: I'd like to refer you to occupational health for a phased return, starting without on-call, and to Practitioner Health, the confidential NHS service for doctors, so your care sits outside your own hospital.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Do I have to tell the GMC?"},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"Not just for having depression that's treated and managed safely. What matters is following occupational health advice if your work could be affected.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"That's a relief, honestly."},{"t":"4:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan in your words, so I know I've been clear?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"Taper off mirtazapine, start vortioxetine, referral for CBT, occupational health and Practitioner Health. Review in two weeks."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Please keep only small supplies of medicines at home and cut back the wine. If those thoughts get stronger, call 111 option 2, Samaritans on 116 123, or 999. I'll see you in two weeks.","m":[5,13]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That was more useful than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, uses his preferred name, acknowledges his reading and agrees an agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration, loss and self-criticism without patronising","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the onset of depression and its link to the divorce","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits current symptoms: mood, sleep, appetite, energy, concentration, enjoyment, alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicide risk: thoughts, plans, access to medicines, protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Takes full medication history: drugs, doses, durations, adherence, side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Answers the serotonin question honestly, separating mechanism from effectiveness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers switch options and how they differ, with side effects and monitoring","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Discusses augmentation and rTMS as further options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains how CBT works and why combined treatment is advised","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Plans return to work: occupational health, Practitioner Health, GMC position","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks his preference and reaches a shared decision","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Uses teach-back, agrees safety measures, crisis numbers and review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Takes a focused history with risk and medicines, answers the serotonin point honestly, and agrees a clear, shared plan with therapy and a return-to-work route.","borderline":"Good explanation but a thin history or risk assessment, or a list of options without a recommendation.","fail":"Defends chemical imbalance, skips risk, ignores the side effect, or offers no credible plan."}},"c-t-52":{"setup":"Wednesday late morning. You are the CAMHS duty doctor phoning back Rhiannon Bell, a newly qualified community nurse worried about Amira, 16, with anorexia nervosa. Do not take a history. Address her concerns, explain the symptoms and discuss management.","role":{"who":"Ms Rhiannon Bell, 24, female, CAMHS community nurse","open":"Yes, thanks for calling back.","manner":"Anxious and keen to learn, newly qualified, slightly defensive at first in case she missed something. Calms and grows confident once given clear steps.","free":["Amira, 16, anorexia nervosa; she has visited her at home for two weeks","Mother pushed big meals and puddings all last week; 3 kg gain in seven days","Hands and feet now swollen; Amira says swallowing is hard and tiring","GP bloods a week ago: only phosphate slightly low"],"ifasked":["Amira was not breathless and had no chest pain this morning","She looked tired and weak and was sitting down most of the visit","No ECG has been done","Rhiannon has not told her supervisor yet","Mother is at home with Amira now"],"worries":["What is wrong with her, and what caused it?","Why are her hands and feet swollen, and why is swallowing hard?","What should we do now? Should her mum stop feeding her?","How could this have been prevented? Did I miss it?"],"rules":["Asks her questions one at a time, waiting for each answer","Becomes anxious and asks 'Did I miss something?'; settles if reassured","If the doctor starts taking a long history, says 'I'm not sure what else I can tell you'","If no same-day plan is given, asks 'So can it wait until the GP?'"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her concern and agrees the call is urgent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her worry and supports her as a new nurse, without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Confirms key facts and asks one red-flag question without taking a history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names refeeding syndrome and links the low phosphate","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the cause: long starvation followed by rapid, large intake","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the mechanism: insulin surge, salts shift into cells, thiamine used up","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the swelling: salt and water retention and strain on a thinned heart","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the swallowing difficulty: low phosphate muscle weakness and choking risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains complications honestly: arrhythmia, heart or breathing failure, fits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges same-day paediatric review with ECG, bloods, replacement and thiamine","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains that feeding continues slowly and how to prevent recurrence, supporting the mother","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, agrees who does what, gives a 999 safety net and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Rhiannon, Amira's nurse?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, thanks for calling back."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"I understand Amira has gained weight quickly and you've noticed some new symptoms. That's worth worrying about.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Her hands and feet are puffy and she says swallowing is hard."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Let me check a few facts, then explain what I think is happening and what we do today. She gained three kilos in a week after her mum increased her meals, and her phosphate was a little low a week ago?","m":[1,3]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"That's right. Her mum was so relieved she was eating."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"One important question: right now, is she breathless, faint, confused, or getting chest pain or palpitations?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Not this morning. She was tired and weak, sitting down most of the time. So what's going on with her, do you think?"},{"t":"0:52","band":"What it is","w":"D","s":"I think this is refeeding syndrome. It can happen when someone who's been starved starts eating a lot, very quickly. That low phosphate fits. It's serious, but caught early it's very treatable.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Oh no. Did I miss something? I've been seeing her every few days."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"You've done exactly the right thing by noticing and calling. This can develop within days. What matters is that she's seen today, and you've made that happen.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"OK. What could have caused it?"},{"t":"1:23","w":"D","s":"Anorexia has kept her body short of fuel for months. Then, last week, a lot of food arrived at once. Three kilos in a week is far too fast; the target is nearer 0.5 to 1 kg weekly.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"But why does eating cause a problem?"},{"t":"1:40","band":"Inside her body","w":"D","s":"While starving, her body ran on fat and muscle, and her phosphate, potassium and magnesium were quietly running out. When food came in, insulin surged and drove those salts from the bloodstream into the cells, so levels in the blood fell fast. It also burns through her thiamine, one of the B vitamins.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"So her cells are grabbing everything."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Exactly that.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Why have her hands and feet swollen?"},{"t":"2:07","band":"Her symptoms","w":"D","s":"Insulin also tells the kidneys to keep salt and water, so fluid pools in her hands and feet. Months of starving have weakened her heart muscle, and that extra fluid loads it. Swelling may be the first sign of strain.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"I didn't realise it was about her heart. And the swallowing?"},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Every muscle uses phosphate for fuel. Short of it, they tire and weaken, including the swallowing muscles and the breathing muscles. So for now nobody should push food on her; she could choke.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Is it dangerous?"},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"It can be. If those salts fall further, the heart can beat irregularly or fail, breathing muscles can tire, and some people have fits. That's why we act today. Treated early, most people recover well.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"That's frightening. What should we actually do with her now?"},{"t":"3:00","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"It is frightening, and you're right to be worried. I'll ring the paediatric registrar as soon as we finish, and ask for her to be seen this afternoon on the assessment unit. She'll probably stay in for a few days.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Can't the GP just do bloods tomorrow?"},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"I understand why that sounds easier. But with swelling and swallowing trouble she needs a heart tracing and salts checked today, so treatment can start straight away.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"OK. What will they do?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"An ECG, blood pressure and pulse. Bloods for phosphate, potassium, magnesium and calcium, plus glucose, kidney and liver tests and a full blood count, repeated daily. Anything low gets topped up, by mouth or through a drip. She'll get thiamine, a strong vitamin B and a multivitamin.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Should her mum stop feeding her then?"},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"No, and that's really important. Stopping food is dangerous too. The dietitian will set a steady plan and build it up, with the bloods guiding the pace.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:01","w":"P","s":"Her mum will be devastated. She thought this meant Amira was getting better."},{"t":"4:07","band":"Prevention and family","w":"D","s":"She acted out of love, and lots of parents do the same. It isn't her fault. Most of that weight was fluid. The ward team and the eating disorder team will show her a safe pace, roughly 0.5 to 1 kg each week.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Could we have stopped this happening?"},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"Before intake goes up: bloods first, correct anything low, start thiamine, increase steadily, and check bloods often in the first week or two. And the whole family knows the plan.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"That makes sense. I'll make sure that's in her care plan."},{"t":"4:43","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Great. Can you tell me back what you'll do after this call?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Ring her mum now, tell her Amira needs to go to the paediatric unit this afternoon, nothing pushed by mouth, and keep feeding steady once they've checked her."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If before then she gets chest pain, palpitations, faints, becomes breathless or confused, or has a fit, call 999.","m":[12,9]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"OK. Should I tell anyone else?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I'll tell her consultant and the paediatric team. Please write up our call and debrief with your supervisor today. You handled this well.","m":[12,2]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel much clearer now."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Ring me back if anything changes. I'll phone you once the paediatric team have seen her.","m":[12]}],"global":{"pass":"Answers each question clearly without taking a history, explains the mechanism and both symptoms, and arranges same-day assessment with feeding continued safely.","borderline":"Good explanation but vague on timing or who acts, or omits thiamine and daily bloods.","fail":"Takes a long history, advises stopping food or waiting for the GP, or blames the mother or nurse."}},"c-t-53":{"setup":"Thursday morning, CMHT clinic. Kieran Adebayo, 26, has paranoid schizophrenia and was discharged six months ago. His family say he has been irritable and housebound for months. He is guarded. Take a history and reach a diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Mr Kieran Adebayo, 26, male, patient","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Guarded and monosyllabic at first, hood up, eyes down. Opens up with gentle, specific questions. Distressed when describing the restraint; angry about the police. Relieved when told it is not his schizophrenia returning.","free":["Doesn't want to go out","Everything winds him up","Takes his olanzapine every night"],"ifasked":["Before admission, police restrained him face down on the grass; he couldn't breathe and thought he would die","About three months ago a TV drama showed a violent arrest and it all came back","Flashbacks several times a day: chest goes tight, smells grass; nightmares 3 to 4 nights a week","Avoids TV, films and games; won't go out in case of police cars and sirens","Jumps at loud noises; checks the street from his window; sleeps badly","Mood low, no motivation, eating less; doesn't blame himself","No voices, no feeling watched; stopped cannabis since discharge","Angry with the police, no thoughts of harming them or anyone; no suicidal thoughts"],"worries":["Does the doctor think he's going mad again?","Will his tablets be increased?","Is it his schizophrenia coming back?"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers to broad open questions; more to gentle specific ones","Goes quiet and tearful after describing the restraint; continues if validated","If asked repeatedly about voices, snaps 'I already said no'","If the diagnosis is explained clearly, asks what can be done"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, opens gently and gives him time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frightening experience and his anger","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the recent change: irritability, staying in, and its onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the restraint during his relapse and his fear of dying","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies the TV reminder that triggered the symptoms and when","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits flashbacks and nightmares with the bodily sensations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits avoidance, hypervigilance and startle","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for voices, persecutory beliefs, olanzapine adherence and cannabis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses mood, motivation, appetite, sleep and how he sees himself","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self, to his family and to the police","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains PTSD using his own experiences and that the psychosis seems settled","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains trauma-focused therapy and continuing olanzapine unchanged","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis contacts, agrees follow-up and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Adebayo?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Kieran. Your family have been worried you're not going out much lately.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"They worry about everything."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Maybe. I'd like to hear it from you. What's been going on for you these last few months? Take your time.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I just don't want to go out."},{"t":"0:25","band":"What changed","w":"D","s":"That's OK. When did going out start to feel hard?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Few months. Three, maybe."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"And your family mentioned your temper. Is everything getting to you?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Everything winds me up. Dad, noises, all of it. You think I'm going mad again, don't you?"},{"t":"0:43","w":"D","s":"I don't assume that. Something's changed, and I want to find out what. Sometimes it's the illness, sometimes it's something else. Can I ask about how you came into hospital last time?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"The police came. I was running in the park. Four of them."},{"t":"1:00","band":"The event","w":"D","s":"What happened then?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"They got me down on the grass, face first. Knee in my back. I couldn't breathe."},{"t":"1:09","w":"D","s":"At that moment, what did you think was going to happen?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"I thought that was it. I was going to die there."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying, Kieran. Thank you for telling me.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"...Nobody's asked me that before."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Take a moment. When did those memories start coming back? Was there something that set them off?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"I was fine when I got out. Then about three months ago there was this cop show. Someone got held down like that. I had to leave the room."},{"t":"1:42","band":"Reliving","w":"D","s":"Since then, do pictures or feelings come back, as if you're there again?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"All the time. My chest goes tight, like the knee's on me. I can smell the grass."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"And at night? Any bad dreams?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Most nights. Same thing. I wake up sweating."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Avoiding and on edge","w":"D","s":"Are there things you avoid because they remind you of it?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"No TV, no films, no games with guns. Outside there's police cars, sirens. So I stay in."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"Do you feel on edge, jumpy at noises, or keep checking around you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"A door bangs and I jump out my skin. I watch the street from my window. I can't switch off."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Psychosis check","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. I need to check one thing carefully. Have you heard voices, or felt watched or plotted against, like before you went in?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"No. It's not like that. It's not like before. It's the memories."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Are you taking your olanzapine every night? And any cannabis?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"Every night, Mum sees me. I stopped weed when I came out. Never again."},{"t":"2:49","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"That's great. How's your mood been? Your appetite, your get-up-and-go?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Low. Fed up. Can't be bothered. I don't eat much."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself? Do you blame yourself for anything?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No. I wasn't the one who did it. I blame them."},{"t":"3:08","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have things ever felt so bad you've thought of killing yourself, or hurting yourself?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"No. I just want it to stop."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"You sound angry with the police, and that makes sense. Have you had any thoughts of harming them, or anyone at home?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"I hate them. But I'd never do anything. I just want to stay away. I shout at Dad sometimes, that's all."},{"t":"3:31","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"Who do you get on with at the moment?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"My brother. He brings food up, plays FIFA with me with the sound off."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Kieran. Let me tell you what I think. No voices, no feeling watched, and you're taking your tablet, so your schizophrenia looks settled.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"So it's not coming back?"},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"Not from what you've told me. What you're describing is post-traumatic stress disorder. Being held down and thinking you'd die was a real trauma. The TV scene reopened it: the flashbacks, the dreams, avoiding things and being on edge all fit.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"No. It's a common reaction to something frightening, and it's not your fault. The good news is a talking therapy for trauma works well, usually around ten weekly sessions with a psychologist. Your olanzapine stays exactly as it is.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Talking about it? I don't know if I can."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"That's a normal worry. It goes at your pace. Until then your care coordinator can teach you ways to bring yourself back when a flashback hits. Could you tell me back what we've said?","m":[2,12,13]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"It's PTSD from the arrest, not the schizophrenia. Therapy for it, and keep my tablets the same."},{"t":"4:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. If things get worse, ring the team in the day, or 111 and choose the mental health option at night; 999 in an emergency. I'll see you again in four weeks. Is it OK if we explain this to your family too?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. My brother can come. Thanks for actually listening."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for trusting me with it, Kieran.","m":[2,13]}],"global":{"pass":"Gently engages him, screens psychosis once, finds the restraint and trigger, elicits all three PTSD clusters, covers risk, and explains PTSD with a clear plan.","borderline":"Finds the trauma but misses one cluster or risk to others, or explains without linking to his experiences.","fail":"Calls it a relapse, increases olanzapine, or never asks about the arrest."}},"c-t-54":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT base. Leah Farrow, 38, has come about her brother Martin, 35, who has paranoid schizophrenia on clozapine. Both parents died recently. Martin has consented. Explain a formulation and the effects of alcohol on his physical and mental health.","role":{"who":"Mrs Leah Farrow, 38, female, sister","open":"Yes. Thanks for seeing me.","manner":"Tired, worried and grieving; guilty she lives far away. Tearful about her parents. Becomes focused and practical once she understands the formulation and what to do.","free":["Both parents died within the last six months","Martin lives alone in the family home; Leah lives a two-hour drive away and has two small children","Empty whisky and beer bottles everywhere; he says he drinks more","He says people are watching him outside, like before","She keeps finding spare clozapine; he admits he forgets doses"],"ifasked":["Mum gave his tablets, cooked and washed; Dad took him out most days","She guesses a bottle of whisky every two or three days plus beer","His hands shake in the morning until he's had a drink","About two weeks of tablets spare; she doesn't know when he last took one","Smokes about 20 a day, more lately; no other drugs; never had a fit","He's mentioned being constipated","He said 'what's the point' once; she doesn't know of any plans","A neighbour pops in sometimes; no one else"],"worries":["What's actually happening to Martin?","Could he be addicted?","Could it cause him physical problems, or make his mental health worse?","What are the risks with clozapine and drinking? Will he end up like before?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about her parents; settles if her own grief is acknowledged","Blames herself for the tablets; responds to reassurance","Asks 'Should I just tell him to take his usual tablets tonight?' if clozapine gaps come up","Only gives the morning shakes and constipation if asked directly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Martin's consent and offers condolences","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates Leah's grief, guilt and strain","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about the parents' deaths and the care they gave Martin","d":"Content"},{"c":"Clarifies mood, drinking amount, morning shakes, paranoia and missed doses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens other drugs, smoking, fits, physical health and other support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores Leah's own concerns","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives a formulation linking vulnerability, trigger, maintaining factors and strengths","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains psychological effects of alcohol: mood, anxiety, dependence","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains physical effects of alcohol, short and long term","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains alcohol's effect on schizophrenia: adherence, paranoia, relapse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains alcohol with clozapine, the 48-hour rule and smoking","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Addresses suicide risk and the stockpiled tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees urgent team review, alcohol and bereavement help, and a carers' assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis contacts and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah, Martin's sister?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thanks for seeing me."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Martin has agreed I can share information with you, so we can talk openly. I'm sorry about your parents.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"It's been a horrible year."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"It sounds it. And you've driven two hours, with two little ones at home. Could you tell me what's worrying you most about Martin right now?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Everything. He's drinking, he's low, he's talking about people watching him again. And there are tablets everywhere."},{"t":"0:35","band":"What changed","w":"D","s":"Let's go through it. What were your parents doing for him day to day?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Mum did his tablets every morning and night, cooked, washed. Dad took him out most days, to the shops, the park."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"So when they died, he lost his routine, his company and his tablet prompts all at once.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Within four months of each other. He's on his own in that house now."},{"t":"1:00","band":"What she sees","w":"D","s":"Do you have a sense of how much he's drinking, and how often?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"I'd guess a bottle of whisky every two or three days. Beer as well. Every day, I think."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"When you arrive in the morning, does he seem shaky or sweaty before he's had a drink?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Yes, his hands shake till he's had one. I hadn't thought about that."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"And the clozapine: how much is left over, and do you know when he last took one?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"About two weeks' worth. I don't know when he last took it. I should have noticed sooner."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"You did notice, and you came. Counting tablets was your mum's job for years. Nobody could step into that overnight.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I just feel useless from so far away."},{"t":"1:45","band":"The gaps","w":"D","s":"Does he smoke, or use anything else, like cannabis?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Twenty a day, more lately. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Any history of seizures? Heart trouble, or bowel problems? And who else sees him?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"No fits. He did say he's been constipated. Just a neighbour pops in."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Has he ever talked about wishing he was dead, or not wanting to go on?","m":[12]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Once he said 'what's the point'. I don't think he's planning anything."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you. We'll ask him directly. What worries you most?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"That he'll end up like before clozapine. He was in hospital nearly a year. What's happening to him, do you think?"},{"t":"2:22","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"That's a real fear. Here's how I see it. Martin has an illness that has always left him vulnerable, and for years your parents gave him structure. Losing both of them took that away suddenly, as well as his grief.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"He's drinking to cope with the grief and the fear. Drink makes him forget clozapine, so the old paranoia creeps back, which keeps him indoors and alone, so he drinks more. It's a circle.","m":[7,10]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"So it's all connected."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"It is. And there are strengths: he's been well for years on clozapine, he still talks to you, and he agreed to us meeting today.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Could he be addicted?"},{"t":"3:07","band":"Alcohol and the mind","w":"D","s":"Possibly. Shaky hands in the morning that settle with a drink suggest his body has come to depend on it. Alcohol also lowers mood, raises anxiety, and makes suicidal thoughts more likely.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Could it make his mental health worse?"},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Yes. It can worsen paranoia directly, makes tablets easier to forget, and makes it harder to eat, wash and keep appointments. All of that raises the chance of relapse.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"And physically?"},{"t":"3:39","band":"Alcohol and the body","w":"D","s":"Short term: drowsiness, falls, accidents, choking when very drunk. Long term: liver damage, pancreatitis, high blood pressure, heart problems, memory damage and some cancers.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"What about with the clozapine?"},{"t":"3:56","band":"Clozapine","w":"D","s":"Together they make him more sedated, with low blood pressure and slower breathing. Both raise the risk of fits, especially if he stops drinking suddenly. Alcohol also worsens the constipation clozapine causes, which can be serious. And if he cuts down smoking, his clozapine level can shoot up.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Should I just tell him to take his usual tablets tonight?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"Please don't. After more than two days off clozapine, his usual dose could make him collapse or have a fit. The team must restart it at a low dose and build up.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"I had no idea."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Most people don't. The spare tablets are a danger too. After a gap, two weeks' worth at once could be fatal. Could you take them to the pharmacy?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:43","w":"P","s":"I'll do it on my way home."},{"t":"4:46","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. His care coordinator will visit within two days: check his mood, risk and drinking, find out when he last took clozapine, do bloods and restart safely. A weekly blister pack, help from the alcohol team and bereavement support too.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"And for me?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"You have a right to a carers' assessment, and family sessions with Martin could help both of you. And please don't push him to stop drinking overnight; the alcohol team will plan that safely.","m":[13,11]},{"t":"5:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If you were explaining this to your husband tonight, what would you say, and what will you watch for?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"Grief, drink and missed tablets going round in a circle. No old dose. Tablets back to the pharmacy. Team visits this week."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If he's very drowsy, has a fit, badly constipated, talks of ending his life or is very paranoid, call 999 or 111 and choose the mental health option. The team's duty line is on this card.","m":[14,12]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel less alone with it."},{"t":"5:46","w":"D","s":"You're not alone. Look after yourself too, Leah.","m":[2,14]}],"global":{"pass":"Gathers the loss, drinking, missed clozapine and gaps, gives a clear linked formulation with strengths, explains alcohol's harms and the clozapine risks, and agrees an urgent plan.","borderline":"Explains alcohol's harms well but gives a thin formulation or misses the 48-hour rule or stockpile.","fail":"Lists facts without a formulation, advises restarting the old dose or stopping drinking suddenly, or ignores her grief."}},"c-t-55":{"setup":"Friday lunchtime, emergency department. Kayleigh Doherty, 27, hairdresser, was brought by colleagues saying her husband means to kill her. She is on citalopram 20 mg. Take a brief history, screen her past, assess her mental state, reach a diagnosis and share your impression.","role":{"who":"Mrs Kayleigh Doherty, 27, female, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Hunched, slowed, whispering and frightened; tearful about being useless. Trusts a calm, patient doctor. Asks to go home at the end, saying nothing can help.","free":["Believes her husband Liam is planning to kill her","Feels useless and that she has ruined everything","The GP tablets haven't helped"],"ifasked":["Liam has never hit, threatened or controlled her; he has been kind and worried","Low for about two months; it began with waking at 4am","No enjoyment, can't concentrate, no energy; unwashed for a week; lost about 5 kg","For a week Liam's food has tasted bitter; she thinks he is poisoning it","Not eaten for two days, only water","Admitted to hospital three years ago before moving here; took two medicines; stopped them six months ago after moving","Never felt high; no alcohol or drugs; no children","No plan to end her life, but feels she deserves to die; would never harm Liam"],"worries":["Can anything help? The tablets haven't worked","Will Liam be told?","Can she go home?"],"rules":["Answers slowly with long pauses; needs time","Only mentions the bitter taste if asked about food, taste or eating","Cries when saying she is useless; settles if validated","At the end asks 'Can I just go home? Nobody can help me'; agrees to stay if the reason is explained kindly"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, makes her feel safe and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress and paces for her slowness","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the belief about her husband: evidence, certainty and reason","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks privately about actual violence, threats or control","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Elicits onset and core depressive symptoms, including early waking and weight loss","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits guilt, worthlessness and the belief she deserves punishment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the bitter taste and screens voices, visions, passivity and nihilism","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the past episode, admission, stopped medicines and citalopram response","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for mania, alcohol and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts and plans, and eating and drinking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming her husband","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks orientation and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains severe depression with psychotic symptoms plainly and with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains need for admission, responds to her wish to go home, and closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kayleigh Doherty?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Kayleigh. Your colleagues brought you in because they were worried. You're safe here.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I didn't know where else to go."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"You did the right thing. Could you tell me what's been happening? Take your time.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"...Liam's going to kill me. When he gets home tonight."},{"t":"0:31","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. What makes you think he wants to hurt you?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"I just know. I'm useless. I've ruined everything for him. He must want to be rid of me."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Has he said anything, or done anything, that makes you sure?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"...No. He hasn't said anything. I just know it."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this, and it stays between us. Has Liam ever hit you, threatened you, or tried to control you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"No. Never. He's been so kind. That's why it's worse. I don't deserve him."},{"t":"1:11","band":"The mood","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've been feeling very low. When did that start, and what came first?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"About two months. I started waking at four every morning. Then everything went grey."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Do you enjoy anything now? How are your energy and concentration?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Nothing. I can't follow a conversation at work. I lie in bed or walk around the flat."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"And your appetite and weight? Have you been able to look after yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"My clothes are loose. I haven't washed in a week."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself? Do you feel guilty about things?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"I'm useless. Everything's my fault. I deserve whatever happens to me."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"I can hear how heavy that feeling is. Feelings like that are often part of a depression, not the truth about you.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"(crying) It feels true."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"Take a moment. There's no rush.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:14","band":"Psychotic features","w":"D","s":"Has food tasted or smelled different lately?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"His cooking tastes bitter. For about a week. He's putting something in it."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"When did you last eat or drink anything?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Two days. Just water from the tap."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Any voices when nobody's around? Or seeing things other people don't?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"Have you felt something outside is controlling your thoughts or body? Or that something terrible has happened to your body or your money?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"No. It's just me. I'm the problem."},{"t":"2:53","band":"Past","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt like this before? Were you ever in hospital?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Three years ago, before we moved. I was in hospital for a few weeks."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"What treatment helped then, and what happened to it? And is the citalopram your GP started doing anything?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"Two tablets. I can't remember the names. I stopped them when we moved six months ago. I felt fine. The citalopram's done nothing."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had times of feeling very high, full of energy, needing little sleep? And any alcohol or drugs?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Never high. I don't drink. No drugs."},{"t":"3:32","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"You said you deserve to die. Have you thought of killing yourself, or planned anything?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"I wouldn't do anything. But if it happened, I wouldn't stop it. I deserve it."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you had any thoughts of harming Liam, perhaps to protect yourself?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'd never hurt him."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me where we are and what day it is?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"The hospital. Friday."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"And do you think any of this might be an illness, rather than something you deserve?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Maybe. Last time felt a bit like this."},{"t":"4:14","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Kayleigh. I think you have a severe depression. It's so heavy that it's changing how you see things: that you're useless, that Liam wants to hurt you, even how food tastes.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"So he isn't poisoning me?"},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"I think the illness is making food taste bitter and making you believe that. It's called depression with psychotic symptoms. It isn't your fault, and it's very treatable.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"Can anything even help me? The tablets haven't done anything."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry they haven't helped yet. This kind of depression usually needs an antidepressant together with a second medicine for the frightening beliefs. Most people get much better.","m":[2,13]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Can I just go home? Nobody can help me."},{"t":"5:03","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting to be somewhere familiar. But you haven't eaten for two days, you feel you deserve to die, and home is where you're frightened. I'd really like you to come into hospital today, to start the right treatment and eat again safely.","m":[14,10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"...If you think so. Will Liam be told?"},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Only what you agree to. Shall I let him know you're in hospital and safe, and nothing more for now?","m":[14]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Yes. OK."},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you. A nurse will stay with you, we'll do some blood tests and a heart tracing, and get you something to drink. My consultant will see you today. You were brave to come.","m":[14,2]}],"global":{"pass":"Calmly elicits the belief, the depressive syndrome, the hidden taste and food refusal, the past episode and risk, excludes real abuse, and explains psychotic depression with a clear case for admission.","borderline":"Reaches the diagnosis but misses the gustatory hallucination, the past admission or the abuse question.","fail":"Calls it primary psychosis, omits risk and eating, or agrees to send her home."}},"c-t-56":{"setup":"Thursday lunchtime, the community clinic office. Freya Lindqvist, a third-year medical student, will see Mr Desai, 57, next week and must present him to her consultant. Teach her what she needs and address her concerns.","role":{"who":"Ms Freya Lindqvist, 21, female, third-year medical student","open":"Yes, hi. Thanks for making time.","manner":"Keen, polite and a little anxious. Asks one question at a time. Relaxes when her questions are welcomed; goes quiet if lectured.","free":["She has read the GP referral","She has to present Mr Desai to her consultant","She does not understand why psychiatry is involved in cardiac rehab"],"ifasked":["She knows depression is common but not how common after a heart attack","She thinks antidepressants might be dangerous after a heart attack","She is nervous about asking about suicide","She does not know what CBT involves","She does not know what a dietitian or OT would do for him","She has been typing notes on her phone"],"worries":["Which came first: the depression or the heart attack?","Which medicines should be avoided?","What will happen to his work?","How should she present him?"],"rules":["Asks about drugs to avoid only if the doctor has not mentioned them","Becomes anxious when suicide comes up; settles if reassured and offered support","If not asked to summarise, does not volunteer one"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and finds her goals, knowledge and her consultant's expectations","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to her worries warmly and checks how she feels","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises his story: past depression, stopped sertraline, heart attack, non-engagement","d":"Content"},{"c":"States that about one in five people are depressed after a heart attack","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the two-way link and worse cardiac outcomes when untreated","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Links depression to poor adherence, missed rehab and smoking","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets assessment points including suicide and deliberate missed tablets","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Recommends sertraline with bleeding, sodium and interaction cautions","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names drugs to avoid: tricyclics, caution with citalopram","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains CBT in plain terms and links it to rehab","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers smoking cessation, alcohol and the dietitian, OT and work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises on presenting and anonymised notes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Arranges supervision, reading and a debrief, then closes","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and goals","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Freya, our medical student?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi. Thanks for making time."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"No problem. I hear you're seeing Mr Desai next week. What would be most useful for you?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"I want to know what to ask and what the plan might be. I have to present him to Dr Mensah."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Good. What do you know so far, and is there anything about seeing him that worries you?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"I've read the referral. Honestly, I don't see why a psychiatrist cares about cardiac rehab."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"That's a really good question, and we'll answer it. Let's go through his story, why depression matters after a heart attack, then the plan.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"He had moderate depression for several years, did well on sertraline, and went back to his GP nine months ago. He stopped the sertraline because he felt well.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"And then he had the heart attack?"},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Yes. Now he's low, sleeping and eating poorly, not going to rehab, not taking his heart tablets, and still smoking. What does that sound like to you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"A relapse, I suppose. Is this common?"},{"t":"1:18","band":"Why it matters","w":"D","s":"Very. About one in five people meet criteria for depression after a heart attack, and more have milder symptoms.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Which came first for him: the depression or the heart attack?"},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Both, really. Depression is a risk factor for heart disease in its own right, partly through smoking and inactivity. And a heart attack is a frightening loss that can trigger depression.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"So why does it matter for the heart now?"},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Depressed people after a heart attack are less likely to take tablets, go to rehab or stop smoking. Untreated, his quality of life is worse and his risk of another event and of dying goes up. So treating his mood is part of treating his heart.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"Oh. That makes sense of the referral."},{"t":"2:14","band":"What to assess","w":"D","s":"Next week, ask about mood, enjoyment, sleep, appetite, energy and hopelessness, and when it started compared with the heart attack. Also alcohol, smoking, and what he believes about his heart.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"Do I have to ask about suicide? I'm worried I'll make it worse."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"That worry is really common, and it shows you care about him. Asking doesn't plant the idea; most people feel relieved. Ask plainly, and also ask whether he's stopped his heart tablets on purpose.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"On purpose? I hadn't thought of that."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"Stopping heart tablets can be a quiet way of not caring whether you live. If he says yes, come and get me straight away. How does that feel?","m":[7,2]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Better, if you're nearby."},{"t":"3:06","band":"Medicines","w":"D","s":"On treatment, the first choice is sertraline. It's the antidepressant with the best safety evidence after a heart attack, and it worked for him before. Usually 50 mg a day.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"I thought antidepressants were risky for the heart."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Some are. Avoid the older tricyclics; they affect heart rhythm and blood pressure. Be cautious with citalopram because it can lengthen the QT interval.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Any problems with sertraline?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"SSRIs add bleeding risk, and he's likely on aspirin plus clopidogrel or ticagrelor, so think about stomach protection: lansoprazole, not omeprazole, with clopidogrel. And check his sodium early.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:54","band":"Therapy, rehab and team","w":"P","s":"Would he have therapy too?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"Yes, CBT through NHS Talking Therapies. It's 8 to 16 weekly sessions looking at how thoughts, feelings and actions link. For him, it can include activity and tablet diaries that dovetail with rehab.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"And getting him back to rehab?"},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"We'd speak to the cardiac rehab nurse about a fresh start, maybe a home-based programme if groups feel too much. Motivational interviewing helps him find his own reasons.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"What about the smoking?"},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Refer to the stop smoking service. Varenicline or nicotine replacement are both fine after a heart attack. Ask about alcohol too, and offer help if he's drinking above 14 units.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Dr Mensah might ask who else is involved. What would a dietitian or OT do?"},{"t":"4:52","w":"D","s":"A dietitian gives heart-healthy eating advice. An occupational therapist looks at daily routine and, with occupational health, a phased return to work if his job is physical or stressful.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Will he be able to work again?"},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Most people return to work after a heart attack, often after a few weeks to months. Treating the depression makes that much more likely.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:20","band":"Presenting","w":"P","s":"How should I present him?"},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Who he is, the problem, the history, his mental state, risk, then a short formulation and plan. Keep your notes anonymised. Are you writing them on your phone?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"Um, yes, I was."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Lots of students do. Keep them anonymised and off personal devices; use the clinic computer instead. Can you give me the plan back in two or three sentences, as you'd present it?","m":[12,13]},{"t":"5:49","band":"Check and close","w":"P","s":"He's relapsed after stopping sertraline, made worse by the heart attack, and it's stopping him engaging. Restart sertraline with bleeding and sodium checks, CBT tied to rehab, help to stop smoking, and the wider team."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"That's excellent. Add one thing: risk, including whether missed tablets are deliberate. What would you say if your consultant asked about drugs to avoid?","m":[13,7]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"No tricyclics, and be careful with citalopram."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll be next door on the day. Have a look at NICE CG91 on depression alongside physical illness, and we'll debrief after clinic.","m":[14]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel much more ready."},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"You've asked all the right questions. Any last questions before I let you go?","m":[2,14]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"No, that's everything. Thanks again."}],"global":{"pass":"Finds her goals, explains the link and its impact, covers safe medicines, CBT, rehab, smoking and team, and checks she can present it.","borderline":"Accurate facts but delivered as a lecture, or teach-back and risk points are thin.","fail":"Never asks what she needs, gives unsafe drug advice, omits suicide risk, or ignores the cardiac side."}},"c-t-57":{"setup":"Tuesday evening, the emergency department. Jordan Ashworth, 28, autistic with schizophrenia, was brought in by police after shouting at a stranger. Carry out an adapted mental state assessment, find what has changed, and separate psychosis from autistic traits.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Ashworth, 28, male, patient","open":"Yes.","manner":"Tense, literal and brief; little eye contact. Answers only what is asked. Becomes irritable with long or vague questions; settles with short, clear ones and quiet.","free":["He wants to leave for his mission","A man was following him so he shouted at him","He likes trains"],"ifasked":["The day centre closed three months ago","He stopped risperidone around then because the agents told him to","The TV sends coded messages for him","Agents' voices speak to him, saying 'wait'; no commands to harm","Other people can hear his thoughts","He is 100% certain he is a special agent","He sleeps badly","No alcohol or drugs; mood 'normal'"],"worries":["How long will this take?","Can he go home for the mission?","Will they make him take tablets?"],"rules":["Answers 'I don't understand' to idioms or double questions","Gives TV messages and thought broadcasting only if asked a direct, short question","Becomes upset about the day centre; calms if this is acknowledged plainly","Asks for a quiet room if offered a choice"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, sets time and uses short literal questions, one at a time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges his distress plainly and offers adjustments","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the incident and how he came to hospital","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores changes in routine and timeline, including the day centre","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes when and why he stopped risperidone","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits delusions: agent, TV messages, persecution, with conviction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits voices and their form, and asks about commands","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits thought broadcasting and screens insertion, withdrawal and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Separates lifelong interests from new beliefs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses insight and willingness to take treatment","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses risk to others, self and absconding","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains next steps literally: unwell, stay, medicine, family","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and adapt","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Ashworth?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Jordan. I will ask some questions. You can take your time to answer.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"How long will this take? I need to go."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"About thirty minutes. It is noisy here. Would a quieter room help?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Yes. Quiet is better."},{"t":"0:40","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"First question: what happened before the police brought you here?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"A man was following me. So I shouted. Then the police came."},{"t":"1:04","band":"What changed","w":"D","s":"What has changed in your life in the last few months?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"The day centre closed. Tuesdays and Thursdays are wrong now. Everything is wrong."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"That sounds very hard, Jordan. Your week changed a lot. When did it close?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Three months ago. In July."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Do you still take your risperidone tablets?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"No. I stopped in July, after the day centre."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"Why did you stop?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"The agents told me to stop. I need a clear head for the mission."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Beliefs","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the agents.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"I am a special agent. I am waiting for a mission. It is very soon."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"How do you get information about the mission?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"The TV. The news gives me coded instructions. Only for me."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"Is anyone trying to harm you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"People on the street follow me. Enemy agents."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"Out of 100, how sure are you that you're an agent?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"100."},{"t":"2:49","band":"Voices and thoughts","w":"D","s":"Do you hear people talking when nobody is with you?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Yes. The agents talk to me. From outside, like your voice. They say, 'Wait, Jordan, wait.'"},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Do they ever tell you to hurt someone or yourself?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"No. Only wait."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Can other people know your thoughts without you saying them?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Yes. People hear my thoughts. That's how the enemy finds me."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Does anyone put thoughts in your head, or take thoughts out? Does anything outside you control your body?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"No. I control me."},{"t":"3:53","band":"New or usual","w":"D","s":"What do you usually like doing?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Trains. Timetables. Always trains, since I was little."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Did you think about agents last year, before July?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"No. The agents came after the summer."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Mood and substances","w":"D","s":"How is your mood? Low, normal, or very happy? And how many hours do you sleep?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Normal. I sleep four hours. I watch the TV for messages."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Do you drink alcohol or use cannabis or other drugs?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:44","w":"P","s":"No. Agents don't."},{"t":"4:49","band":"Insight and risk","w":"D","s":"Your mum thinks you might be unwell. Could this be your illness coming back?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"No. I am not ill. I am an agent."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"Would you take risperidone again?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"No. It makes my head slow."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"If a man follows you again, what will you do?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"Shout. Maybe push him away."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Do you want to hurt yourself or end your life?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:29","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"If you leave now, where will you go?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Home. To watch the TV for the mission."},{"t":"5:43","band":"Explain and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Jordan. I will tell you what I think. I think your illness has come back. The voices and the TV messages are part of it. It started after the tablets stopped.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"I am not ill. I need to go."},{"t":"6:04","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to go. I want you to stay in hospital tonight so we can help. We will offer risperidone again, maybe a lower dose so your head is less slow.","m":[2,13]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"I don't want to stay."},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"I will talk to your mum now. Then I will come back in fifteen minutes and tell you the plan. You can wait in the quiet room. Do you have any questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Quiet room. Fifteen minutes."}],"global":{"pass":"Adapts communication throughout, elicits and labels the psychosis, separates it from autistic traits with the 'what's new' test, and covers insight and risk.","borderline":"Finds the main delusions and voices but misses thought broadcasting, conviction, the trigger or absconding risk.","fail":"Uses long or figurative questions, mistakes autism for psychosis or the reverse, or lets him leave without risk and legal status."}},"c-t-58":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, outpatient clinic. Aaron Whitfield, 27, a nursery assistant, marries in six weeks and dreads the reception. Take his history, reach a diagnosis, and explore what may have caused it.","role":{"who":"Mr Aaron Whitfield, 27, male, patient","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Quiet, embarrassed and flushed; avoids eye contact. Opens up when taken seriously; shrinks if rushed or if his fear is minimised.","free":["Getting married in six weeks to Chloe","Dreads the reception and the speech","Has always avoided weddings and parties"],"ifasked":["Fears people will think he's pathetic or weird","Blushes, sweats, dry mouth, racing heart, hands shake","Avoids eating in front of others and group phone calls","Makes excuses and leaves early; rehearses what to say","Drinks two pints before social events; not otherwise","Manager temporarily took him off leading circle time","Teased at primary school for blushing when reading aloud","Mother is a worrier; he was a shy child"],"worries":["Will he ruin the day for Chloe?","Is it just who he is?","Can he have a tablet for the day?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when talking about letting Chloe down; settles if validated","Mentions the drinking only if asked directly","Denies unprovoked panic, agoraphobia and suicidal thoughts if asked"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, congratulates him and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his embarrassment and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits fear of negative judgement and its range of situations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits body symptoms including blushing, sweating and trembling","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores avoidance and safety behaviours","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol before events","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores impact on work, relationships and life","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for unprovoked panic and agoraphobia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores temperament, family history and childhood events","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies how avoidance keeps the fear going","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains social anxiety disorder plainly with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding and agrees next steps","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Aaron Whitfield?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Aaron. Congratulations on the wedding. I gather it's also causing a lot of worry.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"That's an understatement."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what goes through your mind when you picture the day?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"The reception. Standing up for the speech, everyone looking. I'll go bright red and freeze. I know it's stupid. It's my own wedding."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"It's not stupid at all. Big events often bring an old fear to a head. When you picture everyone looking, what do you fear they'll think?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"That I'm pathetic. That Chloe's marrying a weirdo."},{"t":"1:03","band":"The fear","w":"D","s":"Does that fear come up in other places: parties, eating in front of people, phone calls, work?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"All of them. I hate eating in restaurants. I let calls ring out. Meetings at work are awful."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"And are you fine at home, or with people you're close to?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"With Chloe and my two mates, yeah. It's groups and strangers."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Body","w":"D","s":"What happens in your body in those moments?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"Mouth goes dry, I sweat, heart pounds. I go red. My hands shake if I'm holding a glass."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"Does it ever build into a full panic attack?","m":[4,8]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"Sometimes it feels like it's about to, if I can't get away."},{"t":"2:05","band":"Avoidance and impact","w":"D","s":"What do you do to get through, or to avoid it?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"I make excuses. Say I've got a headache and leave early. I rehearse what I'll say. I look at my phone."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Some people have a drink to take the edge off first. Do you?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Two pints before anything. Otherwise I can't walk in."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Do you drink at other times, or every day?","m":[6,2]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"No, only before things. Maybe once a fortnight."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"How has this affected your work and your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"I'm a nursery assistant. My manager's taken me off leading circle time for now, with the parents watching. I've missed friends' weddings. I wanted to train as a teacher but I couldn't face it."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry. And the wedding?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"I'm scared I'll ruin it for Chloe. She deserves better."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"You clearly care about her a lot, and that fear sounds really painful. We'll come back to a plan for the day. Can I ask how this all started?","m":[2]},{"t":"3:31","band":"Causes","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"When do you first remember feeling like this?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Primary school. Reading aloud in class. My face went red and they all laughed. After that they called me tomato."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"That sounds horrible for a young boy. Were you a shy child before that?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Yeah, always quiet. Stuck to my mum."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"Does anyone in your family worry in a similar way?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Mum's a big worrier. Dad was strict, but fine."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"When you leave a party early, what happens to the fear?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"It goes. Instant relief."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"So leaving helps right then, but you never get to find out you'd have coped. That's often what keeps this going.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"I never thought of it like that."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Differentials and risk","w":"D","s":"Do you ever get panic attacks out of the blue, when nobody's around?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"No. Only around people."},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"Do you avoid shops, buses or crowds even when nobody's watching you?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"No, I can do the supermarket on my own. It's people looking at me."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Low, losing interest, any felt that life isn't worth living?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"Fed up, but not depressed. I'm excited about being married. No, nothing like that."},{"t":"5:15","band":"Explain and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Aaron. What you've described is social anxiety disorder: a strong fear of being judged in social situations. It's common, it's not weakness, and it's very treatable.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:31","w":"P","s":"Is this just who I am?"},{"t":"5:35","w":"D","s":"Partly temperament, which runs in families, partly what happened at school. But it's kept going by habits like leaving early and the pints, and those we can change.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"5:50","w":"P","s":"Can't I just have a tablet for the day?"},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting that with six weeks to go. Tablets like propranolol aren't recommended for this, and mixing sedatives with wedding drink is risky. A specific CBT works best, and we can build the speech into it.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"Could Chloe help?"},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"If you'd like, yes. We could plan a quiet spot and breaks together. Can you tell me in your words what we've said today?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"It's social anxiety, it started at school, leaving and drinking keep it going, and CBT can help before the wedding."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll refer you for CBT today and see you in four weeks. If your mood drops, see your GP or ring 111 option 2.","m":[13]}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits fear of judgement, symptoms, avoidance, drinking and impact, excludes differentials and risk, explores causes and what maintains it, and explains it plainly.","borderline":"Reaches the diagnosis but thin on causes, maintaining factors or the drinking.","fail":"Calls it wedding nerves, omits causes, misses alcohol and mood, or offers propranolol or diazepam as the answer."}},"c-t-59":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, a surgical side room. Shane Mulholland, 24, from prison, refuses closure of a deep neck cut under general anaesthetic. Assess his capacity to make this decision. A full mental state assessment is not needed.","role":{"who":"Mr Shane Mulholland, 24, male, patient (prisoner)","open":"Yeah. Are you here to make me have the operation?","manner":"Wary at first, then calm and polite. Answers what is asked and no more. Gets irritated if pressured; engages well if listened to.","free":["He wants to go back to prison today","He doesn't want the operation","He knows it might scar"],"ifasked":["Cut himself after being told again there was no date for his DBT wing transfer; waiting four weeks","Used a tin off-cut from the metalwork class","Wanted relief from frustration, not death","Diagnosed EUPD before prison; serving two years for ABH, about a year in","Has cut deeply before; some needed stitches","Hates the noise on the ward at night","Would use dressings, antibiotics if infected, and come back if it got bad","Mood fine; no voices; no drugs or alcohol"],"worries":["Will he miss his place on the DBT wing?","Will they force him?","Can he go back today?"],"rules":["Gets short and refuses to continue if pressured to have surgery","Admits he might cut again if the transfer keeps being delayed, if asked about the future","Accepts closure under local anaesthetic if it means going back today"]},"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and states he is not there to force treatment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his frustration about the transfer delay","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the trigger, method and intent of the cut","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about diagnosis, past self-harm and his sentence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests understanding of the procedure and risks of refusing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing by exploring his priorities and reasons","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention by asking him to repeat the risks later","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, psychosis and intoxication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses current suicidal intent and future self-harm risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains he has capacity and the decision is his","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives advice once without pressure and offers alternatives","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans prison handover, wound care and when to return","d":"Close"}],"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Purpose","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shane Mulholland?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Are you here to make me have the operation?"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"No, Shane. I'm here to understand your decision and make sure it's yours to make.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:24","band":"What happened","w":"D","s":"Before the operation, can I ask what happened? What was happening right before you cut yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"The nurse told me there's still no date for the DBT wing. Four weeks it's been 'soon'."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frustrating, waiting with no date when you want the help.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"It is. I just lost it."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"What did you use, and what did you want to happen? Did you want to die?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"A bit of tin from metalwork. No, I wasn't trying to die. I just needed the pressure out."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"Has that happened before?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Loads. I've had stitches before. Some I sorted myself."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"And I understand you have a diagnosis of EUPD, and you're serving a sentence?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Two years for ABH. I'm about halfway. Prison's actually been all right for me."},{"t":"1:46","band":"Understanding","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what the surgeons have told you about your neck?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"It's deep. They want to put me to sleep and close it. I'd be in a few nights."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"What did they say might happen if it isn't closed in theatre?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"Might get infected. Heal slowly. Big scar. Might not close at all."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"That's right. It could also bleed. Anything else you want to ask about the operation?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. I get it."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Weighing","w":"D","s":"What matters most to you in this decision?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Getting back. If I'm out too long, I might lose my place on the DBT wing."},{"t":"2:41","w":"D","s":"How do you weigh that against a bigger scar or an infection?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"A scar's nothing. I've got loads. The therapy's what'll actually help me."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"And if it did get infected, what would you do?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Dressings, antibiotics. And I'd come back if it got really bad."},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"Is there anything else putting you off staying?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:14","w":"P","s":"It's so noisy at night. I can't sleep here."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Impairment screen","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over the last few weeks?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"Fine. Frustrated about the wing, that's all."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"Enjoying things, sleeping and eating as normal?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"Yeah, in prison I sleep fine. Gym, metalwork. It's all right."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Anything unusual, like hearing voices, or feeling people are against you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"Any drink or drugs in the last day?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"No. It's prison."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts now of hurting yourself, or ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"No. Not now."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"What might make you do it again?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"If they keep messing me about, maybe. Can't promise."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. What helps when that frustration builds?","m":[9,2]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Talking to the wing nurse. Going to the gym."},{"t":"4:42","band":"Retain","w":"D","s":"Before we finish, can you tell me again what could go wrong if it isn't closed in theatre?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Infection, slow healing, bad scar, might not close, might bleed. I'm still going back."},{"t":"5:02","band":"Outcome and plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. You understand the risks, you remember them, you've weighed them against what matters to you, and your choice is clear. So you have capacity, and this is your decision.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"So I can go back today?"},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"Yes. My honest advice is still to have it closed, because it heals better. Would you consider stitches under local anaesthetic today, if the surgeons can do it, so you still go back tonight?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"If I'm back tonight, yeah. Local's fine."},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"I'll ask them. I'll also tell prison healthcare and the in-reach team, ask them to chase your transfer date, and send a wound care letter with the officers.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Will this stop me getting on the wing?"},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"I can't promise, but self-harm is often exactly why people need DBT. I'll say it's a priority for you.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's something."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"If the wound bleeds, gets hot, red or swollen, or the urges to cut come back strongly, tell the prison nurse straight away. You can change your mind about surgery at any time.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Right. Thanks for not pushing me."}],"global":{"pass":"Brief history, all four tests including weighing by reasons, mood and psychosis screen, risk, a clear outcome, advice once and a safe handover.","borderline":"Finds capacity correctly but skips weighing by reasons, the impairment screen or the prison handover.","fail":"Finds incapacity because of EUPD or an unwise choice, proposes the MHA for the wound, pressures him, or omits risk."}},"c-t-62":{"setup":"Wednesday late morning, the community clinic. Tomasz Nowak, 34, with schizophrenia, has been urgently referred by his GP: voices have returned since a row at work. Assess risk, then agree a management and safety plan with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Tomasz (Tom) Nowak, 34, male, patient","open":"Yes. Tom is fine.","manner":"Tense and tired, embarrassed by the voices. Holds back the command content at first. Relieved once he is taken seriously and not judged; cooperative with the plan.","free":["Had an argument with colleague Gary three weeks ago","Hasn't been sleeping, feels on edge","The voices are back and he wants them to stop","Doesn't want to go to hospital"],"ifasked":["Two male voices, talking to him, several times a day, getting louder","They say Gary is poisoning his tea and he should 'deal with him'","Mostly believes Gary has tampered with his tea; takes his own flask","Has pictured hitting Gary in the canteen; no plan, no weapon, has not gone looking for him","Pushed his brother during his first episode; no other violence, no police record","Missed about six quetiapine doses because of drowsiness before early shifts","Two or three beers at weekends; no drugs","Not suicidal; lives with partner Ola, who is in the waiting room"],"worries":["Will the police or his boss be told?","Will he lose his job?","Does he have to go to hospital?"],"rules":["Reveals 'deal with him' only if asked what the voices say exactly","Becomes upset and says 'I'm not a monster' if asked about violence bluntly; settles if validated","Resists time off work at first; agrees if the reason is explained","Accepts all reasonable treatment except admission today"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Tomasz Nowak?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Tom is fine."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming at short notice, Tom. Your GP said things have been tough since that work argument.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It's been bad."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear what's been happening, check everyone's safety, and plan together. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Relapse and voices","w":"D","s":"Take me back to the argument. What happened, and what changed afterwards?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Gary said I'd fiddled the rota. We shouted. After that I couldn't sleep, I was wound up all the time. Then about a week later the voices started."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voices. How many are there, and whose are they?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"Two men. I don't know them. They talk to me, every day now, louder than at first."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"What exactly do they say? Their actual words, if you can.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"(hesitates) That Gary's putting stuff in my tea. That I should... deal with him."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening to hear. Thank you for telling me. When they say that, how hard is it not to do what they say?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"I can ignore them. But it's harder at night when I've not slept."},{"t":"1:33","band":"Other symptoms","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that Gary is putting something in your tea? Is anyone else against you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Pretty sure. Seventy per cent? I bring a flask now. It's just him."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, mood and temper? Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Three hours a night. Short fuse. Mood's okay. A couple of beers at weekends, no drugs."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"And the quetiapine, have you been taking it every night?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"I missed a few. Maybe six. It knocks me out and I've had early shifts."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Risk to others","w":"D","s":"With voices saying that about Gary, I need to ask some direct questions. Have you had thoughts of hurting him?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"(upset) I'm not a monster."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"I don't think you are. You came here because you want to keep people safe; that tells me a lot. I ask everyone in your situation, so we can plan properly.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Okay. I've pictured hitting him in the canteen. Just pictured it. It scares me."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Have you planned anything, carried anything like a knife or tool, or gone looking for him?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing. I keep out of his way."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt anyone before, when you were well or unwell?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"I pushed my brother when I got ill the first time. Never anything since. No police."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"And do you still work on the same shift as Gary?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. Every day."},{"t":"3:20","band":"Risk to self and protective factors","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or wishing you were dead?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"What helps you stay well? Who's on your side?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Ola, my partner. She's outside. I want to stay well. Six years I've been fine."},{"t":"3:41","band":"Management plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Tom. I think this is your illness coming back, set off by the stress and the missed tablets. The good news is that it usually settles quickly with treatment.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"What happens now? I don't want to hurt anyone."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Telling me this shows you want to keep everyone safe, Tom, and together we can do that. First, the quetiapine every night again. My consultant may increase the dose, and we can give something short-term to help you sleep.","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"Okay. But I'm not going into hospital."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"I don't think you need to today. The home treatment team will call round daily until this settles. If the voices push harder, or you feel you might act on them, hospital is the safe place, and I'd want you to tell us.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"That's fair."},{"t":"4:36","band":"Safety plan","w":"D","s":"The other thing is work. I'd like you off for two weeks, with a fit note, and no contact with Gary while you're unwell.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"I can't. I'm the supervisor. They'll fall apart."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"I know how much the job means to you. At the moment the voices are about someone you see every day. A short break protects him, protects you, and protects your job. We'll plan the return together.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Okay. Two weeks."},{"t":"5:08","w":"D","s":"What would tell you things are getting worse, and what would you do then?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Voices louder. Not sleeping at all. I'd tell Ola. Ring someone."},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"Good. Ring the crisis line, day or night; 111 option 2 works too; and 999 or A&E if you feel you might act. I'll write this down for you. Could Ola keep your tablets and remind you each night?","m":[8,13]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"She'd like that. Are you going to tell the police or my boss?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Not from what you've told me today. I do need to be honest: if I ever thought Gary was in serious danger, I might have to share that to protect him. I'd try to talk to you first.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's fair, I suppose."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. Quetiapine every night, Ola helping; the crisis team from tomorrow; off work for two weeks; and you'll call if the voices push you or you can't sleep. Could you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Tablets every night. Crisis team come round. Stay off work, away from Gary. Ring if it gets bad."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll talk to my consultant today and see you again within the week. Shall we bring Ola in now so she knows the plan too?","m":[11,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thanks, doc. I feel better for saying it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and agrees to plan together","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits time course of relapse, the trigger and medication adherence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the voices: number, person, exact content, commands and resistance","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for persecutory beliefs, sleep, mood, temper and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses thoughts, plan, means, past violence and ongoing contact with Gary","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses risk to self and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains relapse and agrees medication, crisis team and when admission is needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees sick leave, no contact with Gary, warning signs and actions","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the limits of confidentiality honestly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises the plan clearly","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and arranges early review","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates fear and frustration without judging him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives crisis contacts and involves his partner with consent","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gets the exact command content, assesses intent, plan, means and history, then agrees medication, home treatment, sick leave and a written safety plan he can repeat back.","borderline":"Identifies voices and risk but thin on plan or past violence, or a plan without time off work or crisis contacts.","fail":"Never asks what the voices say or about harming the colleague, or sends him back to work with routine follow-up."}},"c-t-63":{"setup":"Monday morning, the outpatient clinic. Bernadette Quinn, 53, has been seeing her husband around the house since he died suddenly three months ago, and her GP is worried. Take a focused history, then share a short formulation with her.","role":{"who":"Mrs Bernadette (Bernie) Quinn, 53, female, patient","open":"Yes. Bernie.","manner":"Composed but fragile; tearful when describing the night Frank died. Anxious that she is 'going mad'. Visibly relieved and warmer once given a clear explanation.","free":["Frank died suddenly of a heart attack three months ago","She sometimes sees him around the house","She worries she is losing her mind","She has gone back to work"],"ifasked":["Frank had chest pain he called indigestion, collapsed in the hall, died in A&E that night","Sees him in his armchair from the corner of her eye; gone when she looks directly","On waking sometimes sees him at the end of the bed; fades as she wakes","Sometimes hears him in the kitchen and smells his aftershave; knows it is not real","Numb for two weeks, then angry with herself; guilt now easing","Cries daily, broken sleep; still enjoys time with her granddaughter","Sometimes wishes she could be with Frank; no plans, would never act","A glass of wine at weekends; no medication; no past mental health problems"],"worries":["Is she going mad?","Should she be taking something?","Will it ever stop?"],"rules":["Cries when describing A&E; settles if given time and acknowledged","Mentions the wish to be with Frank only if asked about it","Asks 'So am I going mad? What can help with this?' near the end if not already answered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Quinn?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Bernie."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Bernie. I'm very sorry about Frank.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Thank you."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Your GP mentioned some experiences since he died. Could you tell me about Frank and how things have been?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"We were married twenty-nine years. It still doesn't feel real."},{"t":"0:28","band":"The loss","w":"D","s":"What happened when he died? Was there any warning?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"He had chest pain on the Sunday. He said it was indigestion. He went to get his tablets and collapsed in the hall. They worked on him, and he died in A&E that night. (crying) I'm sorry."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. That was a terrible shock, with no warning at all. Take as long as you need.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"(pause) Thank you. The funeral was lovely. Everyone came."},{"t":"1:08","band":"The perceptions","w":"D","s":"Your GP said you sometimes see Frank. Can you tell me what happens: where, when, and for how long?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"In his armchair, out of the corner of my eye. When I look properly, he's gone. A second, maybe."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"At the time, do you know he isn't really there? How do you feel when it happens?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"I know he's gone. But it's nice, in a way. Like he's checking on me."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"Anything else? Seeing him at other times, hearing him, smelling him, sensing he's near?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"When I wake up he's at the end of the bed sometimes, then he fades. I've heard him clattering in the kitchen. And his aftershave, now and then."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Does he ever speak to you, or tell you to do anything?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"No. It's just him being there."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Grief reactions","w":"D","s":"How did you feel in those first weeks, and how has it changed since?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Numb. On autopilot. Then I was furious with myself. He said it was indigestion, and I believed him. I should have made him see the GP."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Lots of people blame themselves after a sudden death. Chest pain is very often mistaken for indigestion, even by doctors. You couldn't have known. How do you feel about it now?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"A bit easier. My daughter keeps telling me the same."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep and appetite? How often do the tears come?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"I cry every day, usually at night. I wake a lot. I'm eating, just not much."},{"t":"3:03","band":"Depression and psychosis screen","w":"D","s":"Is your mood low all the time, or are there moments when it lifts?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"It lifts. I laugh with my granddaughter. Work helps, it keeps me busy."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"What do you think of yourself these days? And what lies ahead?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"I don't feel useless. Just lost. I can't imagine the future without him yet."},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"Have you had any other unusual experiences: voices talking about you, or feeling people are against you or watching you?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Supports and history","w":"D","s":"Who's been there for you? And have you ever needed help with your mental health before?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"My son and daughter live round the corner. Friends from work. Never seen anyone before. My mum died ten years ago, but she'd been ill."},{"t":"3:58","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and do you take any tablets?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"Just a glass of wine on Saturdays. No tablets."},{"t":"4:07","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Some people tell me they'd like to be with the person they've lost. Have you ever felt like that?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Sometimes I just want to be with him."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"That's a very natural feeling when you miss someone this much. I do need to ask: have you thought about taking your own life, or planned anything?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never. The grandchildren need me. I just miss him."},{"t":"4:35","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Bernie. Shall I explain how I understand all this?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"So am I going mad? What can help with this?"},{"t":"4:44","w":"D","s":"No, Bernie, you're not going mad; what you describe is a common part of grieving. Many people see, hear or sense someone they've lost, especially in the first months.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Really? I've been so frightened to tell anyone."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Really. The important thing is that you know it isn't real, and it doesn't distress you. Seeing him as you wake is something the brain does between sleep and waking. In mental illness, people are convinced, and it usually frightens them.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"That makes sense."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Because Frank died so suddenly, there was no time to prepare or say goodbye. That often makes grief stronger: the numbness, the anger, the guilt you described. It tends to come in waves, and over months the waves usually come less often.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:42","w":"P","s":"Everyone says it gets easier. It doesn't feel like it."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Three months is still very early, so that's completely understandable. I'm not seeing signs of depression, and I don't think tablets would help. Talking support often does. Can I suggest Cruse Bereavement Support?","m":[9,10,12]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"My friend used them. I could try."},{"t":"6:07","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"What will you take away from today, in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:12","w":"P","s":"It's grief, not madness. Seeing him is normal. I'll ring Cruse."},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If your mood becomes low all the time, or the wish to be with Frank turns into thoughts about ending your life, see your GP, ring 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123. I'll see you again in six weeks and write to your GP.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel so much lighter."}],"marks":[{"c":"Offers condolences, introduces self and explains the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes how Frank died, its suddenness and the funeral","d":"Content"},{"c":"Characterises the perceptions: modality, timing, duration, insight and emotional tone","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores grief reactions: numbness, anger, guilt, sleep, appetite and crying","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for a depressive episode and psychotic symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about supports, past history, alcohol and medication","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about wish to be with Frank, suicidal thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains that the perceptions are part of normal grief, not psychosis","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains normal grief and why sudden death intensifies it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Advises against medication and offers bereavement support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Responds empathically to tears, guilt and longing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives safety advice, follow-up and GP communication","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Gentle history that characterises the perceptions, excludes depression and psychosis, asks about suicide, and explains normal grief clearly with support and follow-up.","borderline":"Reaches normal grief but with thin characterisation of the perceptions or a vague explanation.","fail":"Labels her psychotic or depressed, offers medication, or omits the risk question."}},"c-t-64":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, a supported residential home. Shanice Morrow, 31, moved here from a rehab unit a month ago and is staying in her room. Speak to her about psychological and social approaches that could help.","role":{"who":"Miss Shanice Morrow, 31, female, patient","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Quiet, slow, flat; short answers and long pauses. Warms a little when painting or her sister come up. Becomes more engaged once she feels not judged.","free":["The home is quiet and staff are nice","She mostly stays in her room","She doesn't feel like doing anything"],"ifasked":["Stays in bed until about 11 am; watches videos; eats meals in her room","Likes meals while eating them, yet anticipates nothing","Feels flat, not sad; no hopelessness or suicidal thoughts","Sleepy every afternoon; mild constipation","No voices or old beliefs now","Worries she'll look stupid and that anything she tries will fail","Loved painting and music; sister Kerry visits monthly; mum busy caring for nan","Wants to paint again and see Kerry more"],"worries":["Do people think she's lazy?","How is something like art meant to help?","So what's actually going to help her get going again?"],"rules":["Answers in a few words unless asked open questions with patience","Becomes more animated if painting is mentioned","Asks 'So what's actually going to help me get going again?' if no options are offered by halfway"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shanice Morrow?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me, Shanice. How have you found the move here?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"It's okay. Quiet."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"Staff mentioned you've mostly been staying in your room. Could we talk about that and what might help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:30","w":"P","s":"If you want."},{"t":"0:34","band":"Her experience","w":"D","s":"Talk me through yesterday, from when you woke up.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"(pause) Got up about eleven. Watched stuff on my phone. Had dinner. Bed."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"Is there anything you enjoy, even a little bit?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Food's nice. When I'm eating it."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"And is there anything you look forward to?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Not really. Nothing."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"What stops you going down to the lounge or the activities?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Can't be bothered. They're loud. And I'd just say something stupid."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That sounds hard, worrying about how you come across. On the unit, did you join in more?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Staff made me. Here nobody does."},{"t":"1:40","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"How would you describe your mood: low and sad, or more flat and empty?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Flat. Not sad. Just nothing."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"Ever feel you'd be better off dead, or think of hurting yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:01","w":"D","s":"Are you sleepy in the day? Any constipation, dribbling or weight change from the clozapine?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Sleepy every afternoon. Bit bunged up."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"And have the old worries or voices come back at all?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No. They've gone."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"That's good. Before you got ill, what did you love doing?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"(a little brighter) Painting. Music. I was doing an art foundation course."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I explain what I think is happening? Feeling flat, finding it hard to get going and not looking forward to things are called negative symptoms. They're part of the illness, just like the beliefs were.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"Everyone thinks I'm lazy."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"I don't think that at all. It isn't a choice. And losing the structure of the unit has made it harder. The good news is these symptoms can improve, especially with the right activity and support.","m":[4,11]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"So what's actually going to help me get going again?"},{"t":"3:24","band":"Psychological options","w":"D","s":"There are several things we can try together, Shanice, starting with what matters to you. You mentioned painting. There's arts therapy: a weekly group with a trained art therapist, for about three months. It's recommended for exactly these symptoms.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"How's drawing meant to help my illness?"},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"It gives you a way to express things without lots of talking, and doing something you're good at builds energy and confidence. People often find it easier to start than talking therapy.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"I'd look stupid in a group."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"That worry makes sense. That's where CBT for psychosis can help: around sixteen sessions with a psychologist, working on thoughts like 'I'll look stupid' or 'I'll only fail', and building a routine step by step.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Maybe."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"And if you'd like, some sessions could include your sister, so she knows how best to support you.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Kerry'd come. She's good."},{"t":"4:40","band":"Social options","w":"D","s":"On the practical side, the occupational therapist can help you plan a week with small, doable steps, with a staff member alongside you at first.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"Like what?"},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"The recovery college runs art and music courses. There's a walking group, which helps energy too. Later, if you want, there's a team that helps people back into college or work.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"I'd like to do art again. Not work yet."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Her goals","w":"D","s":"That makes sense. What would you like to be doing in six months?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Painting. Seeing Kerry more. Maybe going to a class."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"Those are good goals. What could be a first step this week?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"Go to one art thing. If someone came with me."},{"t":"5:41","w":"D","s":"We can arrange that. I'll also check your clozapine level and ask my consultant about the dose, as it may be why you're sleepy in the afternoons.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"That'd help."},{"t":"5:56","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"So: the OT will plan your week with you, a support worker will go with you to an art class, I'll refer you for arts therapy and CBT, and we'll check your bloods. Can you tell me what you'll say to Kerry about today?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"6:15","w":"P","s":"That I'm going to try art again, and there's help for the flat stuff."},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Is it okay if I share the plan with staff here so they can help? If your mood drops or old beliefs come back, tell them straight away. I'll see you in four weeks.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah, okay. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges the move and agrees the purpose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores daily routine, enjoyment, anticipation, motivation and social contact","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depression, suicidal thoughts, clozapine side effects and residual psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains negative symptoms as part of the illness, without blame and with hope","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers specific psychological options and explains how each would help her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers specific social and occupational options","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Elicits her goals and agrees a concrete first step","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses possible medication contribution with level and consultant review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the agreed plan clearly","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her worries about being judged and looking foolish","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks consent to share the plan with staff and gives safety advice","d":"Close"},{"c":"Arranges follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Patient, open questioning that reveals negative symptoms, excludes depression, sedation and psychosis, then named, explained options tied to her goals with a first step.","borderline":"Correct options offered as a list, without linking to her goals, or other causes not excluded.","fail":"Calls it laziness, gives only vague advice to 'join in more', or suggests only medication."}},"c-t-65":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, the acute ward. Jordan Asante, 26, admitted with mania six weeks ago, is now depressed despite sertraline 150 mg. Discuss his diagnosis and the options for future management with him.","role":{"who":"Mr Jordan Asante, 26, male, patient","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Slowed, flat and ashamed; avoids eye contact at first. Becomes more engaged once he gets a clear diagnosis. Anxious and firm about weight gain.","free":["Feels empty and can't see the point","Sleeps a lot but still tired","Wants to know what is actually wrong with him","Worried about putting on weight from tablets"],"ifasked":["During the high: no sleep, spent £3,000 on business equipment, talked over everyone, stormed his manager's office","Mood dropped suddenly about three weeks ago","Sleeps about 12 hours, eats little, can't concentrate, avoids other patients","Deeply embarrassed about the high; thinks everyone would be better off without him; no plan or intent","Girlfriend Amara visits daily; wants to go back to work at the phone shop","Smoked cannabis occasionally before admission; drinks little","Aunt had 'mood problems'"],"worries":["So what can he take that won't make him balloon?","Will this keep happening?","What will he tell people at work?"],"rules":["Asks directly 'What's wrong with me?' and is unsettled by a vague answer","Becomes tearful talking about the embarrassment; settles if validated","Accepts lamotrigine if weight-neutral; hesitant but open about lithium"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Jordan Asante?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me, Jordan. I hear things have been tough since the high spell settled.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Pretty rough."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Could we talk about what's been happening, what the diagnosis means, and options to help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Yeah, okay."},{"t":"0:27","band":"Current picture","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since the high settled?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Empty. Three weeks ago I woke up and it was like someone switched me off."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, energy, appetite and concentration?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I sleep twelve hours and I'm still wiped. Not hungry. Can't follow a TV programme."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"And how do you feel when you think back to when you were high?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"(looks down) Embarrassed. I spent three grand. I barged into my manager's office. Everyone saw."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"That's a really common feeling after a high, and it shows how unwell you were, not who you are. We can plan what to say at work together.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Thanks."},{"t":"1:24","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things feel this low, do you ever feel life isn't worth it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:30","w":"P","s":"Some days I think everyone'd be better off without me."},{"t":"1:35","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that sounds really painful. Have you thought of ending your life, or planned anything?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"No. I wouldn't. Amara'd be destroyed. I just want to feel normal."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs lately?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Not since I came in. A bit of weed before."},{"t":"2:01","band":"The diagnosis","w":"D","s":"What did the team tell you about why you came into hospital?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"A manic episode. But I'm the opposite now. So what's actually wrong with me?"},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"I think you have bipolar disorder. A spell of mania, followed by a low like this, is the pattern. The low isn't a separate problem; it's the other side of the same illness.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"Bipolar. Right. My aunt had something like that."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"It can run in families. It's common, it's treatable, and many people live full lives with it. What do you make of that?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"It sort of makes sense. But why hasn't the sertraline worked?"},{"t":"2:51","band":"Why treatment stalled","w":"D","s":"Good question. Antidepressants on their own don't work as well in bipolar lows, and can sometimes make mood less stable. Risperidone was great for the high, but it mainly treats highs, not lows.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"So what can I take that won't make me balloon? And will this keep happening?"},{"t":"3:16","band":"Medication options","w":"D","s":"Bipolar disorder is treatable, Jordan, and we can choose options that respect your worries about weight. First, I'd suggest tapering off the sertraline over a week or two.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Then lamotrigine. It's good for bipolar lows and doesn't usually cause weight gain. It's started slowly, 25 mg a day for two weeks, then 50, then 100, because of a rare but serious rash. Any rash, you stop it and tell us straight away.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Slow, then. How long before it works?"},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Usually a few weeks once you reach the full dose. For the long term, lithium is the best protection against both highs and lows. It needs blood tests: often at first, then every three months, plus kidney and thyroid checks.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"I'm not sure about lots of blood tests."},{"t":"4:22","w":"D","s":"That's fair; you don't have to decide today. Quetiapine also works well for the lows, but it can cause weight gain, which I know matters to you. And if the depression became severe and nothing helped, ECT is an option.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"Lamotrigine sounds best. I'll think about lithium."},{"t":"4:46","band":"Psychological and lifestyle","w":"D","s":"Talking therapy also helps: CBT for bipolar depression, about sixteen sessions, works on the hopeless thoughts. There's a bipolar education group where you learn your early warning signs.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"The group sounds okay."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"Regular sleep is one of the strongest protectors; drugs, including weed, and heavy drinking are big triggers. And after a manic episode you need to tell the DVLA and not drive until they say so.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"I don't drive anyway. What about work?"},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"Once your mood lifts, occupational health can help us plan a gradual return.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:32","band":"Relapse and safety","w":"D","s":"If those thoughts about everyone being better off get stronger, especially on leave, who would you tell?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"Amara. Or the nurses."},{"t":"5:44","w":"D","s":"Good. And the crisis line or 111 option 2 at any hour, or 999 in an emergency. What would warn you a high was coming back?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping. Talking fast. Big ideas."},{"t":"5:56","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So: bipolar disorder, currently in a low. Taper the sertraline, start lamotrigine slowly, think about lithium, carry on with risperidone for now, and join the education group. Could you sum up our plan in a sentence?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"Bipolar. Off sertraline, start lamotrigine slowly, watch for a rash, and read about lithium."},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"Spot on. I'll give you leaflets on lamotrigine and lithium and talk to my consultant today. Shall we include Amara at the next review?","m":[10,13]},{"t":"6:35","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She'll want to know. Thanks for actually explaining it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his low mood and agrees an agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits current depressive symptoms and feelings about the manic episode","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts, plans, protective factors and substances","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a clear diagnosis of bipolar disorder and links mania and depression","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why sertraline and risperidone are not lifting his mood","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Discusses two or more medication options with dosing, monitoring and weight effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers psychological options: CBT for bipolar depression and psychoeducation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Covers lifestyle, substances, DVLA and work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees relapse signs and crisis contacts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises the agreed plan and respects his choice","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates shame, hopelessness and weight concerns","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Offers leaflets, senior review and family involvement with consent","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear diagnosis, a plain reason why current treatment stalled, two or more realistic options with practical details that respect his weight concerns, a risk check and an agreed plan.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis but options vague or without monitoring details, or ignores his weight concern.","fail":"No diagnosis given, increases the antidepressant, or omits suicide risk."}},"c-t-66":{"setup":"Tuesday late morning, the community eating disorder team office. A locum nurse is worried about Chloe Whitfield, 24, with anorexia nervosa, who gained 3 kg in a week. Find the likely cause and agree a management plan with the nurse.","role":{"who":"Ms Ruth Adeyemi, 41, female, locum community nurse","open":"Yes, thanks for calling back.","manner":"Conscientious and anxious; new to the team and worried she has missed something. Defensive if blamed, relieved and engaged when thanked. Asks direct questions about what to do.","free":["Visited Chloe at home two days ago; she felt weak, tired, sick and had blurred vision","BP 90/60, pulse 60 at that visit","Weight up 3 kg in the last week","A colleague saw her today: BP 100/70, still weak"],"ifasked":["BMI 13.5 at referral by her GP a month ago after a relapse","Gaining about half a kilo a week before last week","Dietitian's new meal plan started ten days ago; Chloe eats all of it plus extra snacks, wanting to get back to work","Puffy ankles, breathless on stairs and palpitations last night (from today's visit)","No vomiting, laxatives or water loading that anyone knows of","No bloods or ECG since the plan began","Ruth was on training yesterday and only told the team this morning","No self-harm or suicidal talk; lives with a flatmate"],"worries":["So what is your view on what is happening to her?","Can it be dangerous, and does it need dealing with today?","Should she stop eating so much?","How do we stop it happening again?"],"rules":["Never says 'refeeding' first","Gives the eating change, bloods and the delay only if asked directly","Becomes upset and apologetic about the delay; settles if thanked and not blamed","Asks 'Can't it wait till Friday?' once, to test the doctor"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ruth from the eating disorder team?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, thanks for calling back."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"No problem. I understand you're worried about Chloe's physical health and a sudden weight jump.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Three kilos in a week. It doesn't feel right."},{"t":"0:19","w":"D","s":"Agreed. Could you take me through what you've seen, then we'll make a plan together?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","band":"Her symptoms","w":"D","s":"What did Chloe tell you, in her own words, when you saw her?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"She said she felt weak, really tired, a bit sick, and her vision was blurry."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"And how is she today? Any swelling, breathlessness, palpitations, chest pain or feeling muddled?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"My colleague said her ankles are puffy, she gets puffed on the stairs, and her heart was racing last night. No chest pain."},{"t":"0:59","band":"Eating and weight","w":"D","s":"How had her weight been going before last week?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"She came via her GP a month ago at a BMI of 13.5. About half a kilo a week, then three kilos last week."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"Do you know why it jumped? Has anything changed in her eating?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"The dietitian gave her a new plan ten days ago. She's eating all of it and extra snacks. She's embarrassed about relapsing and wants to get back to work."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"That's really helpful. Any vomiting, laxatives, or drinking a lot of water before she's weighed?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of. She's been quite open with us."},{"t":"1:45","band":"Observations and bloods","w":"D","s":"Her BP was 90/60 and pulse 60, then 100/70 today. Was it checked lying and standing, and her temperature?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Just sitting. Temperature was normal."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"Has she had any bloods or an ECG since the new plan started?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"And who knew about your visit two days ago, and when?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Only me, until this morning. I was on training yesterday. I'm so sorry. I should have rung someone."},{"t":"2:19","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest, Ruth. You've spotted something important and raised it. For next time, abnormal obs go to the duty doctor the same day. Right now let's focus on Chloe.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Okay. So what is your view on what is happening to her?"},{"t":"2:37","band":"Likely cause","w":"D","s":"I think Chloe has refeeding syndrome, and it needs dealing with today.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"What is that exactly? Isn't putting on weight a good thing?"},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"It is, but steadily: between half a kilo and one kilo weekly. After starvation her stores of phosphate, potassium and magnesium are very low. When she eats a lot more, insulin rises and pulls those salts into her cells, so blood levels drop. It also uses up thiamine, a B vitamin, and her body holds on to salt and water.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"So the three kilos is water?"},{"t":"3:14","w":"D","s":"Mostly, yes. That explains the puffy ankles. The low salts and thiamine fit the weakness, sickness and blurred vision.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Can it be dangerous?"},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"Yes. Very low salts can upset the heart rhythm, cause heart failure, fits, and occasionally death. Her palpitations and breathlessness worry me.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:39","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Her BMI is in the MEED amber range too. Has she mentioned self-harm, or feeling hopeless?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"No, she's really motivated, and she lives with a flatmate. Her BP was better today, though. Can't it wait till her review on Friday?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"I can see why that's reassuring. But her salts can be very low while she feels a bit better. Only bloods and an ECG will tell us, so it can't wait.","m":[8,6]},{"t":"4:09","band":"Immediate plan","w":"D","s":"She needs a medical assessment today, somewhere the results come back today, so A&E or the acute medical unit. I'll ring Chloe and the medical team straight after this.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"What will they check?"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"Lying and standing blood pressure, pulse and temperature, an ECG for her rhythm, and bloods: urea and electrolytes, phosphate, magnesium, calcium, glucose, liver tests and a blood count.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"And what treatment might she need?"},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Thiamine, 100 milligrams three times a day, with vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin. Any low salts get topped up by mouth or through a drip. If they're very low, or her ECG or breathing is worrying, she'll be admitted.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:56","w":"P","s":"Should she stop the meal plan until the results are back? She'll be devastated. She's trying so hard."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"She has worked hard, and we'll tell her that. But no, stopping food makes things worse. She keeps eating at a steady, set amount, and the dietitian adjusts it. We'll explain the swelling is fluid, not fat, so she doesn't cut back.","m":[10,6]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"What if she won't go? She hates hospitals."},{"t":"5:26","w":"D","s":"I'll talk to her myself, explain the danger and check she understands it. If she still says no, I'll involve the consultant today.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"How do we stop it happening again?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Before intake goes up, bloods first and thiamine started. Bloods daily until stable, then twice a week. Aim for half to one kilo a week. If home isn't safe enough for that, day patient or inpatient care.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: likely refeeding syndrome. Seen today with tests, thiamine and salts, feeding kept steady, then slower gain with monitoring. Could you run back through what happens next and who does what?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"You ring Chloe and the medics now. I write up my visit, recheck her obs tomorrow, and anything abnormal I ring the duty doctor that day."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll update the consultant, her GP and the dietitian. If Chloe gets chest pain, collapses, has a fit or gets very breathless, it's 999.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel better knowing what to do."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"You did the right thing raising it. Thanks, Ruth.","m":[13,6]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms who the nurse is, and agrees the plan for the talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits Chloe's symptoms in her words, today's state, and cardiac and fluid symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes starting BMI, weight trend, the 3 kg jump and the change in eating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about purging, laxatives and water loading","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for observations, any bloods or ECG, and who knew when","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates the nurse and teaches the escalation rule without blame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names refeeding syndrome and explains mechanism, symptoms and dangers plainly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Judges physical risk as high despite better BP, and screens self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges same-day medical assessment and names every key test","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans thiamine, vitamins, salt replacement, and feeding continued at a steady amount","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans prevention: bloods first, monitoring, 0.5-1 kg a week, higher care if unsafe","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Agrees who informs consultant, GP and dietitian, safety-nets and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Draws out the hidden facts, names refeeding syndrome, and sends Chloe for same-day assessment with named tests, thiamine and steady feeding, while supporting the nurse.","borderline":"Correct diagnosis and explanation, but vague tests or no clear place and time for assessment.","fail":"Reassured by the better BP, books routine review, stops feeding, or blames the nurse."}},"c-t-67":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, adult ADHD clinic. Kwame Asante, 25, a final-year law student diagnosed with ADHD last month, has come to discuss medication. Do not retake the history. Agree a treatment plan including medication, monitoring and practical support.","role":{"who":"Mr Kwame Asante, 25, male, patient","open":"Yes, hi.","manner":"Bright, friendly, keen to start, asks quick questions. Nervous underneath about changing who he is. Relaxes when answers are honest and specific.","free":["Reads a page five times and remembers none of it","Deadlines arrive before he has started; failed two assessments this year","Read online about 'study drugs' and is curious but nervous","Wants to pass his finals"],"ifasked":["Told his tutors; studies in short library blocks with phone off; helped a little","No cardiac symptoms: no fainting, no exertional chest pain","No relative with heart disease or early sudden death","No tics or fits; takes cetirizine sometimes for hay fever","6-8 units at weekends, no drugs","Once took a friend's ADHD tablet before an essay","Drives his mum's car occasionally"],"worries":["Will it make me a different person?","Isn't it basically speed?","What if it doesn't work for me?"],"rules":["Starts telling his childhood story if invited; stops politely if told it isn't needed","Gives the friend's tablet only if asked about drugs or other people's medicine","Becomes anxious about personality change; settles if validated and given a clear answer"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kwame Asante?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Good to meet you, Kwame. You've already had your diagnosis, so today is about what we can do to help. I won't go back over your history.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Great, I've read a bit online."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Let's hear what you've read and what you hope for, then I'll go through the options.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:27","band":"Goals and concerns","w":"D","s":"What would you most like treatment to change?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"I read a page five times and nothing goes in. Deadlines arrive before I've started. I just want to pass my finals."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"What have you already tried at uni or at home, and did it help?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"I told my tutors. I do short study blocks at the library, phone switched off. It helps a bit, but not enough."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"That's exactly the right first step, and you've done it. Because you're still struggling, medicine makes sense now. Anything you've read that worries you?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"People call them study drugs. And I'm scared it'll change me. I don't want to be a zombie."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"That's a really common worry, and I'm glad you said it. We'll come back to it properly. First, a few safety questions.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:28","band":"Before medication","w":"D","s":"Any heart trouble for you personally: fainting, or chest pain or palpitations with exercise?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. I play five-a-side."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"In your family, has anyone had heart disease, or died suddenly at a young age?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Any tics or fits? And any other medicines?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"No. Just hay fever tablets now and then."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"What about alcohol, drugs, or anyone else's ADHD tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"A few drinks at weekends. And, honestly, a mate gave me one of his tablets before an essay once."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me. That helps me plan safely. Your blood pressure today is 118 over 74, pulse 72, and we've your weight and height, so no heart tracing is needed.","m":[3,12]},{"t":"2:27","band":"First-line medicine","w":"D","s":"The first choice is a stimulant called lisdexamfetamine. It raises dopamine and noradrenaline, the chemicals that help the brain hold attention.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Stimulant sounds odd when I can't sit still."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"It does sound odd. In ADHD it calms the noise rather than speeding you up. You'd start 30 milligrams each morning, before ten so it doesn't spoil your sleep, then go up 20 milligrams a week if needed, to a maximum of 70.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"But will it make me a different person?"},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"At the right dose, most people say they feel like themselves, just able to stay on task. If you feel flat or wired, the dose is wrong and we change it.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"And how fast would I know?"},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Usually within days. With weekly steps, we'd know your dose in about a month.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:28","band":"Side effects and monitoring","w":"D","s":"The common side effects are less appetite, poorer sleep, headaches, a dry mouth and feeling a bit edgy. Most settle in a few weeks.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"That's quite a list."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"It is, though most people cope well. It can raise blood pressure and pulse, so we check those and your weight at every dose change, then every six months. If you get chest pain or your heart races, stop it and get help.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Okay. Isn't it basically speed, though?"},{"t":"4:05","band":"Rules and alternatives","w":"D","s":"It's related to amphetamine, which is why the rules are strict. It's a controlled drug: a month at a time, no early scripts, and lost tablets aren't replaced. Never share or sell it; that's an offence.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Got it. Keep it to myself."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Do you drive? If ADHD or the medicine ever affects your driving, you must tell the DVLA. Taken as prescribed and not impaired, driving on it is legal.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Sometimes, my mum's car. What if it doesn't work for me?"},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"After six weeks at a good dose, we'd try methylphenidate, the other main stimulant. If stimulants don't suit, atomoxetine is a non-stimulant that takes a few weeks to work.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:56","band":"Non-drug help","w":"D","s":"Tablets help you focus but don't plan your week. So I'd also like the ADHD nurse to see you for a couple of sessions on how ADHD affects study.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"I've tried a lot already though."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"You have, and it shows. There's also CBT for adult ADHD, about twelve weekly sessions on planning and putting things off. Would that be useful?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"5:24","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If it's not too much time."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"And please register with the university disability service. With the diagnosis, you can get Disabled Students' Allowance, extra time, a quiet room and deadline flexibility. Your tutor could review the failed modules, if you're happy for them to know.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"Extra time would be massive. Yes, they can know."},{"t":"5:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: lisdexamfetamine 30 milligrams each morning, up 20 a week if needed, checks at every change, the controlled-drug rules, and practical help at uni. How does that sound?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Good. I'd like to start."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back how you'll take it, and what we'll check?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"Every morning before ten, going up each week. You check my blood pressure, pulse and weight. Never give it to anyone."},{"t":"6:24","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll phone you in two weeks and see you in clinic at four. Once you're settled, your GP takes over prescribing. Here's a leaflet.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That's much clearer than the internet."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Good. If you feel very low, anxious or unwell on it, call us or 111. Good luck with the reading, Kwame.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, states no history retake, and sets a shared agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores his goals, worries and adjustments already tried","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks cardiac history, family sudden death, tics, fits, medicines and substances; notes BP and pulse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains lisdexamfetamine: how it works, start dose, weekly step, maximum, timing","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains common side effects and BP, pulse and weight monitoring","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Covers controlled-drug rules, no sharing, driving and second-line options","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers psychoeducation and CBT for adult ADHD","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Plans university support and tutor contact with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the plan and checks his agreement","d":"Close"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Arranges follow-up, shared care, leaflet and safety netting","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates his worries and disclosure before moving on","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Avoids retaking history, completes cardiac checks, gives real doses and monitoring, covers controlled-drug rules and adds study support, answering each worry honestly.","borderline":"Right medicine and checks but vague on doses, monitoring or controlled-drug rules.","fail":"Retakes the history, skips the cardiac questions, or lectures without addressing his worries."}},"c-t-71":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, an A&E side room. Keira Ashdown, 22, a frequent attender, has been medically cleared after a paracetamol overdose. Take a history to a working diagnosis, assess her current risk, and explain what you think is going on.","role":{"who":"Ms Keira Ashdown, 22, female, patient","open":"Yeah. Are you here to tell me off as well?","manner":"Guarded and prickly, expecting a lecture. Opens up once given a reason and treated with respect. Tearful about being left. Relieved if the diagnosis is explained without blame.","free":["Row with boyfriend Reece by phone after her shift: he accused her of flirting","Took twelve paracetamol and rang the ambulance herself about 30 minutes later","Feels A&E staff are sick of her","Wants to go home"],"ifasked":["Had about five vodkas before the overdose; wanted to die at the time, not now","Cuts forearms with a razor about weekly for relief; six ibuprofen last year","Mood flips within minutes, settles within the day; frequent anger; feels empty","Partners start perfect and turn into idiots; terrified of being left","Doesn't know who she is; feels useless","Spent a week's tips on clothes; walked out of three jobs","Most of a bottle of vodka 3 to 4 nights a week; cocaine some weekends; never injected","Brief detachment and feeling talked about under stress, insight kept, gone by next day","Father hit her, mother drank; left home at 16 to an aunt","Another box of paracetamol at home; Reece once grabbed her wrist"],"worries":["Is she just broken?","Can anything actually help?","Will she be kept in?"],"rules":["Answers in single words if questioned before being told why","Becomes tearful about being left; settles if validated","Declines to say more about childhood after a brief answer; this must be respected","Says she feels listened to if the diagnosis is explained kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Keira Ashdown?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Are you here to tell me off as well?"},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Not at all. I'm glad you rang for help last night, and I'm glad you're medically okay.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"Everyone here's sick of me."},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"I want to hear what life has been like lately. Could we talk for about twenty minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:21","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Feeling that everyone's sick of you sounds lonely. I'm not here to judge. You've needed A&E a lot, and I want the help you get to actually fit.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Whatever. Go on then."},{"t":"0:36","band":"Last night","w":"D","s":"Can you walk me through last night, from the end of your shift?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"I'd had a few vodkas after work. Reece rang, said I was all over some lad at the bar. I wasn't. We screamed at each other. Then I took twelve paracetamol from the bathroom."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Did you want to die at that moment? And what made you ring for help?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Yeah, then I did. I just wanted it all to stop. Half an hour later I thought of my little sister, Lily, and rang the ambulance."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about being alive this morning?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:11","w":"P","s":"Glad, I suppose. Embarrassed."},{"t":"1:14","band":"Self-harm","w":"D","s":"The notes mention cuts to your arms before. Can you tell me about that, and what it does for you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Razor, about once a week. It lets the pressure out."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"So it brings relief when feelings get too much. Has there been an overdose before last night?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"Once last year. Six ibuprofen. I didn't tell anyone."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Feelings and relationships","w":"D","s":"When your mood changes, how quickly does it happen, and how long before it settles?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Minutes. A mate doesn't text back and I'm in bits. By the end of my shift I'm fine."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"What about anger? And in between, is there a feeling of being empty inside?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"I lose it all the time. Smashed my phone last month. And empty, yeah. Like there's nothing there."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"What are your relationships usually like, from start to finish? How do you feel if someone might leave?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"At first he's perfect. Couple of weeks later he's an idiot. I can't live with them, can't live without them. And I'm terrified they'll go. Everyone goes in the end."},{"t":"2:27","band":"Self and impulses","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, and I can see it's upsetting. Take your time. When you're ready, how would you describe yourself as a person? Does that change with who you're with?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"I'm whoever people want me to be. I don't really know who I am. Pretty useless, mostly."},{"t":"2:47","w":"D","s":"Do you act on the spur of the moment and regret it afterwards? Can you give me an example?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"Blew a whole week's tips on clothes I never wore. Walked out of three jobs mid-shift."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"What do you drink in a typical week, and which drugs do you use?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Most of a bottle of vodka, three or four nights a week after shifts. Coke some weekends. Never injected."},{"t":"3:15","band":"What it isn't","w":"D","s":"Have you had spells of two weeks or more feeling low every day? Or days of feeling high, full of energy, hardly needing sleep?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Not two weeks. It comes and goes in a day. My highs last an hour, tops."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices when no one's around? And under stress, do things feel unreal, or do people seem against you?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"No voices. Sometimes it's like I'm watching myself from outside. And I think people are talking about me, but I know they're not. It's gone by the next day."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"Any nightmares or flashbacks about the past? Any worries about eating or your weight?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"No. I eat fine. I sleep okay when I'm not wound up."},{"t":"4:00","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Can I ask about growing up? Some people had hard times as children, and it helps me understand. Only share what you're comfortable with.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:09","w":"P","s":"My dad hit me and shouted. Mum drank. I left at sixteen and lived with my auntie. I don't want to go into it."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. We don't need to go further today. Has anyone helped before, your GP or a counsellor? Any trouble with the police?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Saw a college counsellor once. Never went back. Never been in trouble."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Risk now","w":"D","s":"Right now, do you have any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"No. I just want to go home."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Are there more tablets at home? And if you and Reece row again, what do you think you'd do?","m":[11]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"There's another box in the bathroom. And I don't know. Probably something stupid."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Has Reece ever hurt you or frightened you? And who helps you when things are bad?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:00","w":"P","s":"He grabbed my wrist once. He's never hit me. Lily helps. And Mia, my flatmate."},{"t":"5:07","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Can I share what I think is happening? Feelings that flip in minutes, intense relationships, fear of being left, acting on impulse, cutting to cope and feeling empty, all since your teens. That fits a pattern called emotionally unstable personality disorder, sometimes called borderline.","m":[12]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"So is that it, I'm just broken? Can anything actually help?"},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"You're not broken. It's a recognised pattern, often after a hard childhood, and most people get much better over time. The main help is a talking therapy like DBT, which teaches skills for strong feelings. There's no tablet for it, and it isn't depression or bipolar. Can you tell me in your words what I've said?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"My feelings go off like fireworks, it's a known thing, and therapy helps."},{"t":"5:58","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You can go home today. I'll refer you to the community mental health team for therapy and write to your GP. Could Mia hold that other box of paracetamol for now?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She'd do that."},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Good. Let's write a crisis plan together: your warning signs, what helps, then Lily or Mia, Samaritans on 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency. Anything you'd like to ask before we do?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for actually listening."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"You deserve to be listened to, Keira. I'll bring the plan back in ten minutes.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why the conversation matters and agrees time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her feeling judged and her distress before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies last night's overdose: trigger, amount, alcohol, intent and rescue","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores self-harm: method, frequency, function and previous overdoses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores speed and triggers of mood shifts, anger and emptiness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores relationship pattern and fear of being left","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-image, impulsive acts, alcohol and drugs, with examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for voices and clarifies stress-linked detachment and suspiciousness","d":"Content"},{"c":"Excludes depressive episode, hypomania, PTSD and eating disorder using duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks gently about childhood adversity, past help and forensic history, respecting her limits","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses current intent, tablets at home, future risk, relationship violence and supports","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the working diagnosis plainly and hopefully and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees crisis plan, means removal, CMHT referral, GP letter, and closes warmly","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Earns her trust, gathers each feature with examples, rules out other disorders, covers current risk including tablets and violence, and explains the diagnosis with hope and a plan.","borderline":"Covers most features but as a checklist, or explains thinly without checking understanding or removing tablets.","fail":"Dismissive of a frequent attender, diagnoses bipolar from 'mood swings', presses on childhood after refusal, or omits current suicidal intent."}},"c-u-01":{"setup":"Thursday evening, A&E side room. Callum Adeyemi, 21, has been brought in by flatmates who say he has stopped eating and they think he's depressed. In about ten minutes, take a focused history, assess risk and form a working diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Callum Adeyemi, 21, male, history undergraduate","open":"Yes. Is this about my body?","manner":"Perplexed and flat, with pauses before answers; sometimes drifts off topic. Irritated if voices are asked bluntly. Opens up when the doctor stays calm and curious.","free":["Something has felt wrong for a few months","He thinks something is wrong with his body","He eats very little because the food affects him","He wants a doctor to check him"],"ifasked":["Strangers come into his room at night and assault him, then wipe his memory with a 'glove'","He knows because the morning shadows fall in a different place","Certainty is ten out of ten; he wonders if his flatmates know something","Food puts horrible thoughts in his head that are not his own; he won't say what they are","Mood is 'okay'; sleep and energy are fine; he enjoys less because he needs time to think","Smokes cannabis most weekends; drinks little","Uncle spent years in a psychiatric hospital","Rang the police once; would not confront the people because there are too many"],"worries":["Is something physically wrong with me?","Can you find out what they've been doing to me?","Are my flatmates in on it?"],"rules":["If asked 'Are you hearing voices?' without any lead-in, says 'I'm not mad' and goes quiet until reassured","Becomes distressed describing the night-time visits; settles if the doctor validates the fear","If the doctor says the visits aren't real, becomes guarded and gives one-word answers","Agrees to stay if the doctor promises his body will be checked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Adeyemi?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is this about my body?"},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Your friends were worried you haven't been eating, so tell me, in your words, how things have been.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"Something's not right. I just don't know what."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"There's no rush at all. We have about ten minutes. What have you noticed?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"(Pause) ...Things happen. At night."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Eating","w":"D","s":"I'll come back to the nights. First, when did you last have a proper meal, and something to drink?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"Bits of bread. Tap water. I don't eat what's in the kitchen."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"What makes it hard to eat?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"The food does something to me."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"OK, we'll talk about that. Have your clothes got looser? Are you managing to wash?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:39","w":"P","s":"Looser, yeah. I wash sometimes. Not much point."},{"t":"1:49","band":"The night-time visits","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the nights. What's been happening?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"People come into my room. They do things to me. Horrible things. Then they wipe it."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. How do they wipe it?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"A glove. Over my head. So I don't remember."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"If you don't remember, how do you know they've been?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"The shadows. In the morning they're in a different place on the wall. That's how I know."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"Who do you think it could be, and why you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Lots of them. Maybe my flatmates know. Maybe that's why they brought me."},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"That must make it hard to trust anyone. How sure are you that this is happening, out of ten?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Ten. I'm certain. (Voice breaks) I just want it to stop."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"Of course you do. You've been carrying this on your own, and you're exhausted. I want to help you feel safe again.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"(Quiet) ...OK."},{"t":"3:44","band":"Thoughts and perceptions","w":"D","s":"You said the food does something to you. What does it do?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"It puts thoughts in my head. Monstrous ones. I won't say what."},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"You don't have to say what. Do those thoughts feel like your own, or like they've been put there from outside?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"Put there. They're not mine."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"When people are under this much strain, the mind can play tricks, and some hear voices when nobody is around. Has anything like that happened?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not mad, you know."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"I don't think you are. I ask everyone going through something stressful. Thank you for telling me.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"4:49","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, from 0 to 10? Do you still enjoy anything?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Mood's okay. Five, six. I don't do much. I need time to think it all through."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep and energy? And do you feel you've done anything to deserve this?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"Sleep's fine. Energy's fine. I haven't done anything wrong. They're the ones."},{"t":"5:34","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Do you smoke cannabis or use anything else? How much?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"Weed at weekends. A joint a night, Friday to Sunday. Hardly drink."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Have you ever seen anyone about your mental health? And has anyone in the family had problems like this?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:02","w":"P","s":"Never. My uncle was in a mental hospital for years. He was odd."},{"t":"6:13","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"This sounds awful to live with. Do you ever feel like getting back at these people, or protecting yourself with anything?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"No. There are too many of them. I called the police, but they didn't do anything."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"Have things ever got so bad you've thought of ending your life?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:44","w":"P","s":"No. I want to get away from them, not die."},{"t":"6:51","w":"D","s":"Do you feel safe going back to the flat tonight?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:57","w":"P","s":"Not really. Not if they come again."},{"t":"7:04","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it. For a few months you've felt something is wrong, you believe people come at night, the food puts thoughts in you, and you've barely eaten. Is that right?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:23","w":"P","s":"Yes. So what happens now? Can you find out what they've been doing to me?"},{"t":"7:32","w":"D","s":"I can see how much you need answers. I think your mind has been under huge strain, in a way that changes how things seem. It's an illness, and it's treatable.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:47","w":"D","s":"Tonight we'll check your body properly: blood tests and a heart tracing. I'll talk to my consultant about whether you'd feel safer on the ward for a few days, and refer you to a team that helps young people with exactly this.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:08","w":"P","s":"A ward? As long as someone checks my body."},{"t":"8:15","w":"D","s":"They will, tonight. Can I tell your friends the plan? They're worried about you.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:23","w":"P","s":"Just the plan. Nothing else."},{"t":"8:29","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Agreed. While you wait, the nurse will bring you a sealed bottle of water. If you feel unsafe or want to leave, tell her and she'll call me straight back.","m":[3,13]},{"t":"8:45","w":"P","s":"OK. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his fear and distress without agreeing or arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about eating, drinking, weight and self-care","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores the night-time belief: evidence, who, why and certainty out of ten","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts put in through food and whether they feel his own","d":"Content"},{"c":"Normalises before asking about voices","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, enjoyment, sleep, energy and guilt","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks cannabis amount, past history and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicide and wish to retaliate against the persecutors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks his safety and vulnerability at the flat","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises back and checks accuracy","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains next steps: bloods and ECG, consultant discussion, specialist team, friends with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a named route and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Elicits the systematised belief and thought insertion sensitively, rules out psychotic depression, covers background and risk, and gives a clear plan with EIP and physical checks.","borderline":"Finds the belief but skips the mood screen or the risk to others, or ends without a concrete next step.","fail":"Takes a depression history only, labels him 'deluded', argues with the belief, or misses the self-neglect."}},"c-u-02":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, CMHT clinic. Bernadette Kowalski, 52, has been referred by her GP for persistent fears of serious illness despite normal tests. In about ten minutes, take a brief history, explain the likely diagnosis and agree a management plan.","role":{"who":"Mrs Bernadette Kowalski, 52, female, part-time bookkeeper","open":"Yes. I don't know why I've been sent here.","manner":"Anxious and slightly defensive at first; fears being dismissed. Warms quickly if taken seriously, and becomes interested once the cycle is explained in her own terms.","free":["She thinks her skin and eyes are turning yellow","Her liver tests were normal but she thinks tests can miss things","She has had many scans over the years","She is exhausted by the worry"],"ifasked":["Worries began about six years ago, a few months after her mother died of pancreatic cancer","Earlier fears: bowel cancer, then a bone tumour after knee pain","Checks her skin and eyes in the mirror about ten times a day","Reads about symptoms online for about two hours most nights","Sees the GP almost weekly; three A&E visits this year","Relief after a normal result lasts a few hours","Thinks it 'might be worry', about six out of ten","Mood is 'fed up' but she enjoys her family; no suicidal thoughts"],"worries":["What if they've missed something?","Can't I have just one more test?","What does the therapy involve?","My husband thinks I'm a hypochondriac"],"rules":["If told 'there's nothing wrong with you', becomes upset and says she feels dismissed","Becomes tearful talking about her mother; settles if the doctor acknowledges the loss","Accepts no more tests only if the doctor explains the cycle using her own checking","Asks what therapy involves if CBT is mentioned"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Bernadette Kowalski?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes. I don't know why I've been sent here."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming. Your GP tells me you've been very worried about your health, and the tests haven't given answers.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"That's exactly it."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"We have about ten minutes. Could you tell me what has worried you most, then we'll talk about how we can help?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","band":"Her fears","w":"P","s":"My skin. It's going yellow, I'm sure of it. And my eyes. Nobody else sees it."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"What are you afraid it might mean?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Liver. Cancer, maybe. The liver tests were normal, but tests miss things, don't they?"},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Part of you might wonder whether this is worry rather than illness. How likely does that feel, out of ten?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"Maybe six. I know it sounds silly. But I can't stop."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"It doesn't sound silly. When did worries about your health first start?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"About six years ago."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Was anything happening in your life then?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"(Tearful) My mum died. Pancreatic cancer. She went yellow at the end."},{"t":"2:09","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry. Watching that happen to your mum must have been terrible. It makes sense that yellow skin frightens you.","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"(Nods) Thank you. Nobody's put it like that."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"What other illnesses have you worried about since?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Bowel cancer for a while. Then a bone tumour when my knee hurt. The X-ray was fine, but I read they don't always show."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Behaviours","w":"D","s":"How many times a day do you check your skin or eyes?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"In the mirror? Ten times. More on bad days."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"And how long do you spend reading about symptoms?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"Two hours most nights. I can't sleep until I've looked."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"How often have you seen the GP or been to A&E this year?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"The GP most weeks. A&E three times."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"When a test comes back normal, how long does the relief last?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"A few hours. Then I think, what if they looked at the wrong thing?"},{"t":"3:55","band":"Exclude and risk","w":"D","s":"What's your mood been like? Are you still enjoying things?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"It gets me down. I'm fed up. But I love time with my family."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Do you get other thoughts or worries you can't push away, not about health?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"No. It's always my health."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"Has the worry ever got so bad you felt life wasn't worth living?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"No. I want to live. That's the point."},{"t":"4:37","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I think this is health anxiety. Some worry about health is normal and useful. Here it's taken over your life.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"So you think it's all in my head."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"No. The worry and the sensations are real. Your body has been checked very thoroughly. The worry is now the illness hurting you most, and it's treatable.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"5:14","w":"D","s":"Look at your pattern. You notice something, like your skin. You fear the worst, then check the mirror or search online. You feel better for a few hours, then the worry comes back even stronger.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Losing your mum the way you did set the alarm off. Could you put into your own words how that cycle works for you?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"So every time I check, I'm feeding it? It goes away and comes back worse."},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"Exactly that.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:01","band":"Plan","w":"P","s":"So if it's not my liver, what do I do with all this worry? Can't I have one more scan?"},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want one. But each scan gives you a few hours, then the cycle starts again. More tests would keep it going.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"What works best is CBT for health anxiety: about 12 to 16 weekly sessions with a therapist. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies today; I'll give you the link.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:46","w":"P","s":"What does that actually involve?"},{"t":"6:51","w":"D","s":"You'll look at how normal body feelings get misread, and test out what happens to the worry when you check and search less.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:05","w":"D","s":"I'll also write to Dr Ellison asking for one named GP and planned appointments every few weeks, and no new tests unless there's something new a doctor can see.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:23","w":"P","s":"No more tests. That frightens me a bit."},{"t":"7:29","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. If something new appears that others can see, you'll still be checked. Would you try checking just once a day, and writing down the urges?","m":[2,10]},{"t":"7:47","w":"P","s":"I can try. My husband says I'm a hypochondriac, though."},{"t":"7:55","w":"D","s":"That must hurt. It's an anxiety problem, not attention-seeking. With your permission I'd like to help him support you without endless reassurance.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:11","w":"P","s":"Yes. He'd like that, I think."},{"t":"8:16","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So: health anxiety, CBT through Talking Therapies, one GP, and checking once a day. I'll send you a copy of my letter and see you in eight weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:34","w":"D","s":"If your mood drops or you feel hopeless, see your GP that week or call 111 and choose option 2.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:45","w":"P","s":"Thank you for not making me feel silly."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges the referral and agrees the plan for the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and her grief without dismissing her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Clarifies the current fear, earlier fears and her certainty out of ten","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset and links it to her mother's death","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies checking, searching, health-service use and length of relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, obsessions and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names health anxiety and explains the worry-check-relief cycle using her own symptoms","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains CBT: number of sessions, what it involves and how to access it","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees one named GP, planned visits, no new tests without new signs, and a first step","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and gives a follow-up date","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a named route if mood drops","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Concise history with numbers and the trigger, belief shown not to be fixed, a personal explanation of the cycle, and a concrete plan with CBT and testing limits.","borderline":"Right diagnosis but a generic explanation, or a vague plan with no change to testing or GP contact.","fail":"Offers more tests, dismisses her ('nothing is wrong'), or runs out of time without explaining or planning."}},"c-u-03":{"setup":"Saturday afternoon, A&E. Marcus Lindqvist, 34, came in demanding his medical records; nurses say he is loud and restless. In about ten minutes, assess his mental state, then present your findings to Dr Okafor, the A&E registrar.","role":{"who":"Marcus Lindqvist, 34, male, sales manager; then Dr Okafor, A&E registrar","open":"That's me, and I'm very busy, Doctor.","manner":"Fast, loud, cheerful and overfamiliar; snaps if interrupted or contradicted. Sits down if the doctor offers help with the records and keeps questions short.","free":["He needs his full records today","He has been chosen to run a new national health app","He feels better than ever","He has investors waiting"],"ifasked":["He has slept almost nothing for three nights and doesn't feel tired","He has spent about £4,000 this week on a launch event and a new suit","He believes someone at the trust is hiding his records to steal his idea","He drove here","He has had 'a few beers'; denies cocaine or other drugs","He has been texting several women he barely knows","No voices; no thoughts of harming himself or anyone","His partner Sofie is worried about him"],"worries":["When will I get my records?","Can I go now? I've got investors waiting.","Why are you asking about sleep?"],"rules":["Paces and talks over the doctor for the first minute; sits if offered help with the records","Snaps 'Stop interrupting!' once if cut off; calms if the doctor apologises and keeps it short","If told the app idea isn't real, becomes angry and threatens to leave","After the close, Dr Okafor enters and asks: 'What did you find, and what do you need from us?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and align","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Marcus Lindqvist?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"That's me, and I'm very busy, Doctor. Very, very busy."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me. I understand you want your records; I'd like to help with that.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Finally, someone sensible! Nobody here listens. Do you know how long I've waited?"},{"t":"0:35","w":"D","s":"Can we sit for about ten minutes first so I understand what's been going on?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Ten minutes. Fine. (Keeps pacing) The records prove everything."},{"t":"0:53","band":"Observe and settle","w":"D","s":"Prove what?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"That I've been chosen. The national health app. Me. Everyone's going to use it. What's that beeping? Anyway, the investors..."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds a big project. Shall we both sit, and you can tell me about it?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"(Sits) Fine. You've got a good face, Doctor. You could work for me."},{"t":"1:33","band":"Speech and mood","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling in yourself, from 0 to 10?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"Ten! Eleven! Never better in my life. I've got the energy of ten men, and ideas, ideas, ideas, health, wealth, stealth..."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Do you get irritable with people at the moment?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Only when they're slow. Like the nurses. Like you, actually, if you keep..."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Who chose you for the app?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"The NHS. I just knew. Stop interrupting me! You're not listening!"},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Sorry, you're right. Go ahead and finish, then I'll ask a couple of quick things.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"(Calmer) I woke up and knew. The NHS needs me. It's obvious once you see it."},{"t":"2:46","band":"Thought content","w":"D","s":"How sure are you, out of ten?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Ten. A hundred."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Is anyone trying to stop you, or take it from you?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Someone at the trust is hiding my records. They want to steal the app. That's why I need them today."},{"t":"3:17","w":"D","s":"Do you ever hear voices, or get special messages that other people don't?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"No voices. I'm not crazy. I'm a genius."},{"t":"3:33","band":"Cognition","w":"D","s":"Do you know where we are and what day it is?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"Hospital. Saturday. Next question."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Can you count back from 20?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"Twenty, nineteen, eighteen... look, this is pointless, I've got calls to make."},{"t":"3:59","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Quick one: how much sleep have you had this week?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Why does that matter? I don't need sleep. Three nights, nothing. Never felt better."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Going three nights without sleep can wear the body down even when you feel great. How much have you spent this week?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"About four grand. Launch party. The suit. Investment, Doctor."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"How did you get here today? Any alcohol, cocaine or other drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Drove. A few beers. No drugs, I don't need them."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Have you been doing anything risky, like sex with people you've just met?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"I've been texting a few women. They love it. Who wouldn't?"},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Has anyone made you so angry you felt like hurting them? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"No! I'd never hurt anyone. And why would I hurt myself? I'm perfect."},{"t":"5:32","band":"Close with him","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Marcus. You've been very open. I'm worried your mind is running too fast, with no sleep and a lot of spending.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Right, so can I go now? I've got investors waiting."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I can see how much you have riding on this, and I don't want you to lose it. I'd like some blood tests and for my senior to see you. And please don't drive today.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:14","w":"P","s":"And my records?"},{"t":"6:17","w":"D","s":"You have a right to them. The records team handles the request, free, normally within a month. I'll get you the form.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"A month! Fine. Do your tests. Quickly."},{"t":"6:37","band":"Present findings","w":"P","s":"(Dr Okafor) What did you find, and what do you need from us?"},{"t":"6:43","w":"D","s":"Marcus is 34, not known to us. Bright suit, pacing, distractible and overfamiliar. Speech rapid, loud and pressured, with flight of ideas. Elated, irritable and labile.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:07","w":"D","s":"He has a grandiose delusion of being chosen to run a national health app, and persecutory ideas that the trust is hiding his records. No voices. Oriented, poor attention, no insight.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:30","w":"D","s":"Three nights without sleep, £4,000 spent, sexually disinhibited, drove here. No thoughts of harm to self or others. My impression is a probable manic episode with psychotic features.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"7:54","w":"P","s":"Could it be drugs?"},{"t":"7:58","w":"D","s":"It could, so please send FBC, U&E, liver, thyroid, calcium, glucose and a urine drug screen. He shouldn't drive.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:12","w":"P","s":"And if he walks out?"},{"t":"8:16","w":"D","s":"Section 5(2) doesn't apply here. Please ask the nurse in charge and security to call me at once. I'm ringing my consultant now; if he refuses help and risk stays high, we'll request a Mental Health Act assessment.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"Got it. I'll brief the nurse in charge."},{"t":"8:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll write my note now and come back within the hour.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, aligns with his request and gets him seated","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Stays calm, apologises when he objects and keeps questions short","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses subjective mood and irritability","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the grandiose belief, its certainty and persecutory ideas","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about voices, orientation and attention","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about sleep, spending, driving, substances and sexual behaviour","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to others and to himself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tells him the concern and next steps, including no driving and the records route","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Presents a structured MSE with examples and a probable manic episode","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Requests bloods and a urine drug screen to exclude organic causes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States a lawful plan if he tries to leave, without Section 5(2)","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with a note and a time for review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Settles him, elicits mania with examples, covers sleep, money, driving, drugs and harm, and presents a clear MSE with a lawful plan.","borderline":"Recognises mania but describes it vaguely, or misses driving, drugs or the plan if he leaves.","fail":"Argues with him or loses control of the interview, misses the risks, or plans to use Section 5(2) in A&E."}},"c-u-04":{"setup":"Monday late morning, acute ward. Stuart Halloran, 54, has just arrived from the home treatment team: barely eating or drinking, off his medicines. In about ten minutes, assess his mental state, then present your findings to Dr Rao, your consultant.","role":{"who":"Stuart Halloran, 54, male, retired postal worker; then Dr Rao, consultant psychiatrist","open":"(Long pause) ...Yes.","manner":"Barely moves, stares at the floor, rubs his face. Answers after long pauses in a whisper. Says a little more each time the doctor waits and encourages him.","free":["'Oh dear, oh dear'","There's no point in anything","He doesn't want to be any trouble"],"ifasked":["Mood is 0 out of 10; nothing gives him pleasure","He doesn't know when he last ate or drank","Sleeps very little; mornings are the worst","Believes he has ruined his family and there is no money left; his wife says otherwise","Believes his insides have rotted away, so food is pointless","Hears a man's voice telling him he is worthless; it does not tell him to do things","Says he can't kill himself because he is already dead","Stopped his tablets because nothing can help a dead man"],"worries":["Why bother? Nothing can be done for someone like me.","Is my wife all right?"],"rules":["Says nothing for about fifteen seconds at the start; answers only if the doctor waits","If the doctor asks two questions at once, answers neither","Becomes tearful talking about his family; settles if the doctor acknowledges it","After the close, Dr Rao asks: 'What did you find, and what do you want to do today?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open slowly","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Halloran?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"(Long pause) ...Yes."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Stuart. I'm sorry things have been so hard at home.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"(Silence. Rubs his face.) ...Oh dear. Oh dear."},{"t":"0:48","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand how you're feeling. Take as long as you need with each answer.","m":[1]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"(Whispers) ...Don't want to be any trouble."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"You're no trouble at all. I'm glad you're here.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:22","band":"Physical state","w":"D","s":"When did you last have something to drink?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"...Don't know."},{"t":"1:40","w":"D","s":"And something to eat?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"...No point."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"I've brought some water. Would you have a sip with me?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"(Shakes head slightly.)"},{"t":"2:14","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"That's OK. How's your mood been, from 0 to 10?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"...Nought."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that helps me. Is there anything at all you still enjoy?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"...Nothing."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"How are you sleeping?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"...Hardly. Mornings are the worst."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Thought content","w":"D","s":"What goes through your mind, sitting in your chair?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(Tearful) ...I've ruined them. My family. It's all my fault."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a terrible weight to carry. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"...There's no money left. Because of me. Maureen says there is. She's being kind."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Do you feel you deserve to be punished?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"...Yes."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Is there anything wrong with your body, do you think?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"...My insides. They've rotted away. That's why food's pointless."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"That must be frightening to believe. Thank you for telling me.","m":[2,5]},{"t":"4:50","band":"Perceptions","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people feel this low, they hear voices. Has that happened to you?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"...A man. He says I'm worthless."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"Does he talk to you, or about you? Does he tell you to do anything?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"...To me. 'You're worthless.' He doesn't tell me to do things."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Do you know where you are today?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"...Hospital."},{"t":"5:47","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Stuart, have you thought of ending your life?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"...Can't. I'm already dead."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you stopped eating and drinking so that you would die?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"...What's the point. Dead men don't eat."},{"t":"6:31","w":"D","s":"Why did you stop your tablets?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:45","w":"P","s":"...Nothing can help a dead man. Why bother? Nothing can be done for someone like me."},{"t":"6:55","band":"Close with him","w":"D","s":"I know it feels that way, Stuart, but this kind of depression does get better, and you've recovered before.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"7:05","w":"D","s":"A nurse will stay close and bring you drinks, and we'll do some blood tests today to check your body.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:17","w":"P","s":"(Small nod.)"},{"t":"7:22","band":"Present findings","w":"P","s":"(Dr Rao) What did you find, and what do you want to do today?"},{"t":"7:27","w":"D","s":"Stuart is 54, admitted from HTT. Unkempt, soiled clothes, dry lips, very slowed, with response latency up to 15 seconds and a whispered voice. Mood nought out of ten, anhedonic, early morning worse.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:43","w":"D","s":"He has guilt and poverty delusions about ruining his family, a nihilistic delusion that his insides have rotted, and a second-person voice calling him worthless. No commands. Oriented to place.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:59","w":"D","s":"He says he's already dead, sees no point eating or drinking and doesn't know when he last drank. Severe depression with psychotic features and high physical risk.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:12","w":"P","s":"What do you want to do?"},{"t":"8:14","w":"D","s":"Three things today. Bloods including U&E, creatinine and CK, an ECG, fluid and food charts and NEWS2. Hold ramipril until U&E are back. And enhanced observations, at least every 15 minutes.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:30","w":"D","s":"Given his past response and his intake, I'd like to discuss urgent ECT, his capacity to consent, and whether he needs a Mental Health Act assessment.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:42","w":"P","s":"Agreed. I'll see him straight after the ward round."},{"t":"8:45","w":"D","s":"Thanks. I'll write the note now and call the medics if his kidneys are off.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self gently, slows down and tolerates long silences","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Encourages and validates each answer, including his guilt and distress","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks when he last drank and ate, and offers fluids","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses mood rating, anhedonia, sleep and diurnal variation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits guilt, poverty and nihilistic delusions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about voices: person, content, commands; checks orientation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide and deliberate food and fluid refusal","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks why he stopped his medicines and his view of treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Offers hope and explains immediate next steps to him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Presents a structured MSE ending in psychotic depression with high physical risk","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names same-day actions: bloods and charts, hold ramipril, observations, ECT and capacity","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes with documentation and a medical escalation route","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Waits out the silences, elicits each depressive delusion and the voice, reads 'already dead' as high risk, and presents a same-day physical plan with ECT.","borderline":"Finds the depression and some delusions but rushes him, or presents without the fluid, kidney or ECT actions.","fail":"Fires questions and gets nothing, misses the psychotic features, or treats 'already dead' as no suicide risk."}},"c-u-05":{"setup":"Wednesday evening, A&E. Patrick Osei, 38, with known schizophrenia, has been brought in by hostel staff worried he is relapsing after a recent switch to aripiprazole. In about ten minutes, assess his mental state and risk, then phone your on-call consultant.","role":{"who":"Patrick Osei, 38, male, lives in a supported hostel; then Dr Mensah, on-call consultant (by phone)","open":"Yes. They made me come.","manner":"Guarded and suspicious, flat, sometimes pausing to glance at the corner; legs restless. Opens up if the doctor is curious, not confrontational, and comments gently on what they see.","free":["The hostel staff are worried about nothing","He doesn't trust the food at the hostel","He can't sit still since the new tablet"],"ifasked":["Two male voices talk about him and comment on what he does; no commands","Believes the hostel manager is poisoning his food, 9 out of 10 sure, since seeing him whisper to the cook","Feels people pull thoughts out of his head; no passivity","Tablets changed about three weeks ago; has missed four or five doses this week because they make him jittery","Smoked one joint last weekend; no alcohol","Eating only crisps and sealed packets","Angry with the manager: 'he'll be sorry if he carries on'; no plan or weapon","No thoughts of harming himself; oriented"],"worries":["Is it the new tablet's fault? What are you going to do?","Will I lose my room at the hostel?"],"rules":["Says the voices are 'personal' if asked bluntly; describes them if the doctor mentions his glances first","Becomes irritable talking about the manager; calms if the doctor acknowledges how unsafe he feels","Accepts the hostel being told if the doctor explains why and says it's to keep his place","After the close, Dr Mensah answers the phone: 'What have you got?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Patrick Osei?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. They made me come."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Thanks for staying to talk. The hostel staff were worried about you.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"They're worried about nothing."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"Then help me understand how things have been for you this week. We have about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Same as always. (Pauses, glances at the corner.) ...Fine."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Observe","w":"D","s":"I noticed you stopped and looked over there. Did you hear something?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"That's personal."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"That's fair; you don't have to tell me everything. It just helps me know how much it's bothering you.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"...There are two of them. Men."},{"t":"1:34","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"Are they speaking to you, or about you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"About me. To each other. 'Look at him, he's eating again.' Like that."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Do they tell you to do things?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"No. Just talk. All the time this week."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"You seem fidgety too. Is it hard to sit still?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Since the new tablet. My legs won't stop. It's horrible."},{"t":"2:25","band":"Beliefs","w":"D","s":"That sounds unpleasant. The staff said you're worried about the food. What's been happening?","m":[3,5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"The manager's putting something in it. I saw him whispering to the cook."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"How sure are you, out of ten?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"Nine. I only eat crisps now. Sealed packets."},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"What have you done about it?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Told him I know. He laughed. (Angry) He's putting stuff in my food. If he carries on, he'll be sorry."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you've felt really unsafe there, and angry. That's a lot to live with.","m":[3]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"(Settles a little) Nobody believes me."},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Can anyone put thoughts into your head, take them out, or read them?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:53","w":"P","s":"They pull them out. I'll be thinking, then it's gone. Taken."},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Does anything ever move your body or make you act against your will?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:13","w":"P","s":"No. Just the thoughts."},{"t":"4:19","band":"The switch","w":"D","s":"When did your tablets change?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Three weeks ago. They said the old one made me fat."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"How many doses have you missed this week?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Four, five. It makes me jittery. I'd rather not."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"Any cannabis or other drugs? How's your sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"One joint at the weekend. Sleep's bad. The voices keep me up."},{"t":"5:03","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"You said the manager will be sorry. What did you mean? Have you thought of hurting him?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:16","w":"P","s":"I haven't planned anything. I just want him to stop."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Have you got anything you'd use to protect yourself, like a knife?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:32","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:43","w":"P","s":"No. I want to sort this out."},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"Do you know where you are, and what day it is? And do you think you might be getting unwell again?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Hospital, Wednesday. ...Maybe. It was like this last time. Is it the new tablet's fault? What are you going to do?"},{"t":"6:14","band":"Close with him","w":"D","s":"It may well be linked to the change, and the restlessness too. I'll talk to my consultant tonight about going back to what worked for you.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"I also need to let the hostel know you've been worried about the manager, so everyone stays safe. I'll share only what's needed.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:49","w":"P","s":"Will they kick me out?"},{"t":"6:55","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. The aim is to keep your place and get you well, not to lose it.","m":[3,10]},{"t":"7:06","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"7:10","band":"Present findings","w":"P","s":"(Dr Mensah, by phone) What have you got?"},{"t":"7:15","w":"D","s":"Patrick Osei, 38, known paranoid schizophrenia. Relapse three weeks after a switch from risperidone to aripiprazole 10 mg, with akathisia and four or five missed doses this week.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:35","w":"D","s":"Dishevelled, guarded, responding to voices. Two male third-person voices with running commentary, no commands. Thought withdrawal. Persecutory belief that the hostel manager is poisoning him, 9 out of 10, angry, no plan or weapon. No suicidal thoughts. Partial insight.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:05","w":"P","s":"What do you want to do?"},{"t":"8:08","w":"D","s":"Restart risperidone and stop aripiprazole, if you agree. Home treatment with supervised doses, or admission if the risk to the manager rises. I'll tell the hostel tonight, document why, and email his care coordinator.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"8:34","w":"P","s":"Agreed. Risperidone 2 mg tonight, back to 4 over the week. Get bloods and an ECG."},{"t":"8:45","w":"D","s":"Will do. And if he refuses and risk goes up, I'll call you about a Mental Health Act assessment.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why he is here and opens non-confrontationally","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Uses observed behaviour (glancing, fidgeting) to open questions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his reluctance, fear and anger","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Characterises voices and asks about thought interference and passivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the poisoning belief: evidence, certainty and actions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks the switch, missed doses, side effects, cannabis and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about harm to the manager, plans and weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about self-harm, orientation and insight","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the likely link to the switch and the next step","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tells him the hostel will be informed and why","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Presents a concise structured MSE with risk to the consultant","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a medication, setting and escalation plan","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses observations to elicit first-rank symptoms, explores the belief and the switch, asks directly about harm to the manager, and presents a clear plan with the hostel informed.","borderline":"Finds voices and paranoia but misses akathisia, commands or the specific risk to the manager.","fail":"Confronts him or gets little, misses the risk to a named person, or keeps it confidential."}},"c-u-06":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, CMHT clinic. Ewan Castellano, 27, has bipolar disorder, five manic admissions and failed trials of quetiapine and valproate. Six weeks after discharge he is well on olanzapine but has gained two stone. In about ten minutes, discuss lithium and agree a next step.","role":{"who":"Ewan Castellano, 27, male, patient","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Friendly, organised and keen to take charge; jots notes on his phone. Flat and fed up about his weight. Anxious when lithium sounds 'toxic'; relaxes when given clear numbers and actions.","free":["Gained two stone on olanzapine and feels sluggish at work","Wants to avoid another admission above all","Has read online that lithium is 'toxic'"],"ifasked":["Takes ibuprofen now and then for back pain","Hopes to start a family with his partner, Leah","Had acne as a teenager","Father has high blood pressure","Dislikes needles and worries about time off for blood tests"],"worries":["Will I lose the weight if I switch?","Can I stop olanzapine straight away?","Does lithium affect having children?","How often are the blood tests?"],"rules":["Becomes flat and says 'I just want my old self back' when weight is discussed; settles if validated","Mentions ibuprofen only if asked about other medicines","If given vague answers like 'regular tests', asks 'how often, exactly?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ewan Castellano?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Good to see you looking well, Ewan. Six weeks out of hospital, taking everything, keeping every appointment. That's a lot of hard work. I hear the olanzapine's caused weight gain and tiredness.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Two stone. I hate it. I just want my old self back."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"That's a big change to live with, and it's completely reasonable to want something different. Let's talk about lithium as an option and go through your questions. We have about ten minutes.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Great. I've got a few written down."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His view","w":"D","s":"Before I explain, what have you heard about lithium so far?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"That it's old, it works, but it's basically toxic. Hence all the blood tests."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"And what matters most to you in choosing a medicine?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Not ending up in hospital again. Five times is enough. And not looking like this."},{"t":"1:04","w":"D","s":"Anything else that worries you about it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"Needles. And I can't keep taking time off work."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really helpful. So: staying well, your weight, the blood tests and work. I'll cover each.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:22","band":"Why lithium","w":"D","s":"Lithium has the strongest evidence of any medicine for preventing both manic and depressive relapses. It also lowers the risk of suicide. Quetiapine and valproate didn't hold you, so it's the natural next step.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Why wasn't I offered it first, then?"},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"Fair question. It needs more monitoring, and it suits people who take tablets reliably and come to appointments. You've shown you do.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"OK. And the toxic part?"},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"It is strong, and too much does harm. That's exactly why we check levels. They keep you in a safe range, and I'll tell you the warning signs.","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:08","band":"Starting checks","w":"D","s":"Before starting, we check your kidneys, thyroid, calcium and a blood count, and weigh you. Any heart problems in you or the family?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Dad's got high blood pressure. I'm fine."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Then a quick ECG too, to be thorough. Do you take anything else, even from the chemist?","m":[5,9]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Ibuprofen now and then for my back."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"Important. Ibuprofen and similar painkillers push lithium levels up. Switch to paracetamol, and tell any pharmacist you're on lithium.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:44","band":"Starting and levels","w":"D","s":"You'd start on 400 milligrams at night, always the same brand. A week later, a blood test 12 hours after the dose, so the next morning.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"And what number are you aiming for?"},{"t":"2:59","w":"D","s":"Between 0.6 and 0.8. We adjust weekly until two results are steady in that range.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Weekly. Every week forever?"},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"No. Weekly only for the first few weeks. After that, three-monthly through the first year, then twice a year. Your GP surgery usually has early slots before work.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"That's better than I thought."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Side effects and toxicity","w":"D","s":"Common early effects are thirst, passing more urine, a fine shake in the hands and a metallic taste. Many settle in a few weeks.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"And weight? Be honest."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"Honestly, lithium can add some weight too, usually less than olanzapine. I can't promise you'll lose it all, but we'll weigh you regularly and get dietitian help early.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Right. I appreciate you not sugar-coating it."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Now the important bit. If the level gets too high you may get a coarse shake, being sick, diarrhoea, slurred speech, unsteadiness or feeling muddled. Stop taking the lithium and get a level that day, through A&E if you're unwell.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"What would make it go too high?"},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"Dehydration, from heat, hard exercise or a stomach bug, and certain medicines: ibuprofen-type painkillers, some blood pressure tablets and water tablets. Always check with a doctor or pharmacist first.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:30","band":"Long-term monitoring","w":"D","s":"Over the years lithium can affect the kidneys, thyroid and calcium. So every six months we check those and your weight, and act early if anything shifts.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:42","w":"P","s":"And the olanzapine? Can I stop that tomorrow?"},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting it gone. But you were manic six weeks ago. We get lithium steady first, then reduce olanzapine slowly over about a month.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"And never stop lithium suddenly. That can bring on mania within weeks. If you ever want to stop, we'd reduce it over at least four weeks.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Got it. One more: my partner and I want kids. Does it affect that?"},{"t":"5:13","w":"D","s":"Good that you asked. Lithium taken by a man isn't known to harm a baby he fathers. The cautions in pregnancy apply to women taking it.","m":[4]},{"t":"5:23","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"I've given you a lot. Can you tell me the warning signs, and what you'd do?","m":[12]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Sickness, diarrhoea, a bad shake, wobbly or confused. Stop it and get a blood test that day. And no ibuprofen."},{"t":"5:37","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Here's the lithium booklet, a record book for your levels and an alert card to carry.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"My suggestion: we do your baseline bloods and ECG today, you read the booklet with Leah, and we decide together at a review in two weeks. How does that sound?","m":[13]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll do the bloods now."},{"t":"6:00","w":"D","s":"Great. I'll write to your GP today. If your mood starts to lift or drop before then, ring the team's duty line.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. That's the clearest anyone's explained it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity and recognises his stability and adherence","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his distress about weight gain and his fear of toxicity","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks his knowledge, priority and worries before explaining","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains why lithium: relapse prevention, lower suicide risk, failed alternatives","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names baseline tests: kidneys, thyroid, calcium, blood count, weight, ECG if needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States 400 mg at night, 12-hour level at one week and the 0.6-0.8 target","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Describes common side effects honestly, including weight","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names toxicity signs and the action: stop and same-day level","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Warns about NSAIDs, other interacting drugs and dehydration","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives the long-term monitoring schedule and kidney and thyroid effects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Warns never to stop suddenly and explains the olanzapine taper","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the lithium pack, agrees bloods today and a two-week review, safety-nets","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Asks his view first, then gives a specific, honest account of why, how, toxicity and monitoring, checks understanding and agrees a dated next step.","borderline":"Accurate facts but delivered as a lecture, vague about level timing or target, or no teach-back.","fail":"Omits toxicity signs or interactions, promises weight loss, or stops olanzapine at once."}},"c-u-07":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Adeel Rashid, 22, a phone shop worker, was referred by a cosmetic surgeon after repeatedly requesting nose surgery for no visible problem. In about ten minutes, take a focused history with risk and reach a likely diagnosis.","role":{"who":"Adeel Rashid, 22, male, patient","open":"Yeah. I don't really know why I'm here.","manner":"Guarded and a little prickly at first; sits angled so his profile is hidden and touches his nose often. Opens up if not argued with; tearful when he talks about giving up the gym.","free":["His nose is 'too big and bent at the bridge'","Three surgeons have refused to operate","It ruins everything"],"ifasked":["Started around 15; teased as 'beak' at school","Checks it on his phone camera four or five hours a day","Covers his nose in photos and tapes it at night","Asked to move to stockroom shifts; stopped the gym; avoids dates","About 90% sure; friends say it's fine but 'they're being kind'","Low mood for three months; enjoys little","Sometimes thinks life is pointless if it can't be fixed; no plan","Has looked at buying a filler pen online but not bought one"],"worries":["Can't you see it's bent?","Am I mental?","Will you stop Mr Hale operating?"],"rules":["If the doctor says his nose looks fine, becomes irritated and shuts down","Reveals the filler pen only if asked about doing something himself or buying treatments","Becomes tearful about the gym; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Adeel Rashid?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yeah. I don't really know why I'm here."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Adeel. Mr Hale, the surgeon, asked me to see you. It sounds like your nose causes you a lot of distress.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"It ruins everything."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me about it, and how it affects your days? We have about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"It's too big. And it's bent at the top. Just look at it. You can see it, can't you?"},{"t":"0:31","band":"The concern","w":"D","s":"I can see how much it upsets you. I see your face differently, but I'm not here to argue. I want to understand what this is doing to your life.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"Fine. Nobody else gets it either."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"When did this worry start, and how many hours a day does it take up?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Since I was about 15. Now it's four or five hours a day, easily."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"Out of 100, how sure are you that it looks the way you think? Could you possibly be mistaken about it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"Ninety. My mates say it's fine, but they're just being kind."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"And do people at work notice it, do you think?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Customers stare. Well, maybe I notice it more than they do."},{"t":"1:21","band":"Checking and hiding","w":"D","s":"What do you do to check it, or to hide it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"I check it on my phone camera all the time. I cover it with my hand in photos. I tape it flat at night."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Do you compare yourself with others, or ask people if it looks OK?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"I look at noses online for hours. I ask my sister every day."},{"t":"1:44","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"What has this stopped you doing?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"I asked to work in the stockroom. I don't date. And I stopped going to the gym. I used to love it."},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"Sorry. That one gets me."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"No need to apologise. Losing something you loved because of this is a real loss. Take your time.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"It's fine. Go on."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Surgery","w":"D","s":"How many surgeons have you seen? Has anyone done anything to your nose?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Three, over two years. All said no. Around six hundred quid on consultations."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"What would change for you if the operation happened?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"I'd finally have a life. Go out. Get a girlfriend."},{"t":"2:31","band":"Other conditions","w":"D","s":"What's your mood like lately? Are you still getting pleasure from things?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"Low for a few months. Not much is fun any more."},{"t":"2:40","w":"D","s":"Any other thoughts or habits you can't stop, not about your looks, like checking locks or washing?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"No. Just the nose."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Any worries about your weight, eating or muscle size?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"No. I eat normally. The gym was just for fitness."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Ever hear voices when no one's there, or feel people are plotting against you? Any alcohol or drugs?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No voices. A few drinks at weekends. No drugs."},{"t":"3:09","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"At its worst, has it ever left you feeling life isn't worth it?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think, if it can't be fixed, what's the point of anything."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that takes courage. Have you thought of ending your life, made plans, or ever tried?","m":[10,2]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"No plan. I've never done anything. My sister'd be devastated."},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Have you ever thought of doing something to your nose yourself, or buying a treatment online?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"I've looked at those filler pens. Haven't bought one."},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you've told me. And have you ever been so angry with a surgeon that you thought of getting back at them?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Annoyed, yeah. But no, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:54","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Were you ever teased about your looks growing up? Any help for this before?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"They called me 'beak' at school. Never seen anyone about it."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Adeel. What you've described sounds like body dysmorphic disorder. The brain fixes on one feature and magnifies it. It's common, it isn't vanity, and talking therapy and medicine help a lot.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"So I'm mental, then?"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"No. It's a recognised, treatable condition. Can you tell me what you've understood?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"It's a condition that makes me focus on my nose. And surgery won't sort it."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Please don't buy or try anything on your nose yourself. If things feel unbearable, call Samaritans on 116 123, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 if you might act on it.","m":[14]},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"Can I write to Mr Hale and your GP to explain? My consultant will join us now, and we'll book you in again in two weeks.","m":[14]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"OK. I'm not promising anything, but I'll come back."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the referral and invites his own account","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates distress without agreeing or arguing about the nose","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the perceived defect, onset and hours per day preoccupied","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction as a number and asks whether he could be wrong","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about checking, hiding, comparing and reassurance-seeking","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores impact on work, social life and activities","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past surgical consultations and his expectations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depressive and OCD symptoms","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for eating or muscle concerns, psychosis and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and past attempts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about DIY procedures or online treatments and anger at surgeons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about teasing, background and previous help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Names likely body dysmorphic disorder and checks understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a crisis route, seeks consent to write, and books follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Measures preoccupation and conviction, lists behaviours and impact, screens differentials, asks suicide and DIY risk directly, and names BDD with a safe next step.","borderline":"Gets the diagnosis but misses conviction, DIY risk or one key differential, or closes without a crisis route.","fail":"Reassures him his nose is fine, argues about it, or never asks about suicide."}},"c-u-08":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Shona Gallagher, 28, began sertraline a month ago; now 100 mg. Her GP's mood scores show improvement, but she wants to stop because of side effects she won't name. In about ten minutes, explore her concerns and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Shona Gallagher, 28, female, patient","open":"Yes, hi.","manner":"Calm but tense and avoids eye contact. Says only 'side effects' at first. Opens up if the doctor is matter-of-fact and kind; would shut down if they seem awkward. Relieved once options are offered.","free":["Feels much better in mood","Wants to stop sertraline because of 'side effects'","Finds it embarrassing to talk about"],"ifasked":["From about a week in, orgasm is much delayed or doesn't happen; arousal is slower","The same happens on her own","Sex life was fine before the depression; little interest while depressed","Boyfriend thinks she has gone off him; they argue","Depressed once before, at 22; no suicidal thoughts now or then","On the combined pill for five years; drinks a few glasses at weekends; no drugs","No unwanted sexual experiences; no pregnancy plans this year","Was planning to stop the tablets this weekend"],"worries":["Do I have to choose between being depressed and having a sex life?","Will it go back to normal?","What will you tell Dr Kaur?"],"rules":["Stays vague unless the doctor names possible areas, such as sex","Becomes tearful about the arguments with her boyfriend; settles if validated","Mentions her plan to stop at the weekend only if asked about stopping or after the doctor warns about it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Shona Gallagher?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Shona. Dr Kaur said you'd like to stop sertraline. I'm keen to understand why. We've about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"It's just the side effects. I don't like them."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Some side effects are awkward to talk about. Everything here is confidential, and nothing will shock me. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Okay. It's a bit embarrassing."},{"t":"0:29","band":"The side effect","w":"D","s":"That's completely understandable. Many people get sexual side effects on these tablets. Is it something like that?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"Thanks for trusting me with that. May I ask a few specific questions? Is it your interest in sex, becoming aroused, or reaching orgasm?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"The last one, mostly. I just can't finish. It takes ages or it doesn't happen at all."},{"t":"0:54","w":"D","s":"And becoming aroused in the first place?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"Slower than it used to be. Less... intense."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"When did that start, compared with starting the sertraline?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"About a week after."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Is it the same when you're on your own, not just with your boyfriend?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"Yes. Same."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"And how were things before the depression, and while you were depressed?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Fine before. When I was low I didn't want sex at all. Now I want to again, and then this happens."},{"t":"1:28","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"How is it affecting you and your boyfriend?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"He thinks I've gone off him. We keep arguing. Maybe I have gone off him, I don't know."},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful for you both. From what you've said, the wanting is there; it's your body's response the tablet is affecting.","m":[2,9]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"I hadn't thought of it like that."},{"t":"1:51","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How's your mood now, compared with a month ago?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Loads better. I'm sleeping, I'm back at work."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"That's great progress. Have you been depressed before? And have you had thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here, this time round?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Once, when I was 22. And no, never anything like that."},{"t":"2:10","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"A few other things that can affect sex. Any contraception? And any plans for pregnancy?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"The pill, for about five years. No baby plans this year."},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink, and any drugs? Any physical health problems?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"A couple of glasses at weekends. No drugs. Dr Kaur did bloods recently; all normal."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this: has anything sexual ever happened that you didn't want?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So it does sound like the sertraline. It's one of its commonest side effects, and it usually goes once the medicine is changed.","m":[9]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"So do I have to choose between being depressed and having a sex life?"},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"No, you don't. Option one: lower to 50 mg. It may ease the side effect while keeping your mood steady, though there's a small risk the mood slips.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"Option two: switch to a medicine with fewer sexual effects, like mirtazapine, but that can cause sleepiness and weight gain. Waiting it out is a third option, but this often doesn't settle on its own.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Honestly, I was going to just stop them this weekend."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Stopping safely","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you said. Stopping suddenly can make you dizzy and irritable, with electric-shock feelings. And a month in, the depression could easily come back.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"We usually continue for at least six months after you're well, longer as this is your second episode.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"OK. I'd rather try the lower dose first."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"That sounds sensible. Fifty milligrams from tomorrow, and we'll review your mood and the side effect in four weeks. If your mood dips, we go back up.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"What would you like me to say in my letter to Dr Kaur?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"You can say it's a sexual side effect. I just couldn't say it out loud."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"I'll put it exactly like that. Would it help to bring your boyfriend next time, so he hears it's the tablet?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Maybe. I'll ask him."},{"t":"4:09","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan in your words?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"Fifty milligrams, don't stop suddenly, see you in four weeks."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"Perfect. If your mood drops or dark thoughts come, ring your GP the same day, NHS 111 option 2, or Samaritans on 116 123. Here's a leaflet on sertraline.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:27","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel so much better for saying it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gives confidentiality and normalises awkward topics","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to embarrassment and distress calmly, without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names sexual side effects and asks about desire, arousal and orgasm separately","d":"Content"},{"c":"Places the problem on a timeline and asks if it occurs alone too","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about impact on her relationship and on her","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks current mood, past episodes and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens contraception, alcohol, drugs and physical health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks sensitively about unwanted sexual experiences and pregnancy plans","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the side effect is common and usually reverses","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers dose reduction, switch and waiting, with trade-offs","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Warns against stopping suddenly; explains discontinuation and relapse risk","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a dated plan and the GP letter wording","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis route and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Calmly names and specifies the sexual side effect, checks mood and other causes, offers real options and agrees a safe, dated plan.","borderline":"Finds the problem but stays vague about phases or other causes, or offers only one option.","fail":"Seems embarrassed or dismissive, misses the sexual side effect, or agrees to stop suddenly."}},"c-u-09":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, adult outpatient clinic. Imogen Faulkner, 37, a journalist promoted to editor last year, is missing deadlines; occupational health referred her. In about ten minutes, take a focused history to reach an initial diagnosis and tell her what happens next.","role":{"who":"Imogen Faulkner, 37, female, patient","open":"Yes. Imogen.","manner":"Warm, quick and chatty; fidgets and sometimes jumps ahead of the question. Anxious about losing her job; brightens when the doctor recognises her past success.","free":["Was an award-winning features writer","Since becoming editor she misses deadlines","Her manager has put her on a performance plan","Worried she is about to 'lose everything'"],"ifasked":["Can't type for more than ten minutes; loses keys and phone daily","Talks over colleagues; can't sit through meetings","Coped as a writer by working from home, pacing, dictating and last-minute adrenaline","School reports said 'bright but daydreams'; detentions for calling out; same at home","Her mother has kept all her school reports","Two speeding fines; impulse buys; late fees on bills","Drank heavily in her early twenties; about six units a week now","Younger brother was 'just like me' at school"],"worries":["Will I lose my job?","Isn't ADHD just a fashionable excuse?","Can I get tablets today?"],"rules":["Gives long, wandering answers to open questions unless gently steered","Becomes tearful about teachers calling her lazy; settles if validated","Mentions the speeding fines only if asked about driving"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Imogen Faulkner?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Imogen."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Imogen. Occupational health mentioned work has been tough since your promotion.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"That's an understatement."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me what's been happening, in your words? We have about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"I was a good writer. Awards, even. Then they made me editor and it's all fallen apart. Missed deadlines, and now I'm on a performance plan. I've worked so hard to get here."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening after all you've achieved. Your track record tells me this isn't about ability. Let's work out what's going on.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","band":"Current difficulties","w":"D","s":"Do you lose focus, lose things, or leave tasks half-done? Give me an example.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"All of it. I can't type for more than ten minutes. I lose my keys every single day. I start ten things and finish none."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"Do you forget appointments or make careless mistakes?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"I missed two meetings last week. And my proofreading's terrible."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"What about sitting still? Can you get through a long meeting?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"No. I fidget, I go and get water. And apparently I talk over people. They call me rude."},{"t":"1:14","w":"D","s":"Do you make snap decisions you later regret?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Constantly. I buy things on a whim, I say yes to everything."},{"t":"1:22","band":"Why now","w":"D","s":"How did you manage as a writer that you can't as an editor?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"At home I paced and dictated, worked at 2 a.m. if I had to. Deadlines gave me a rush. Now it's meetings, planning, managing six people."},{"t":"1:36","band":"Childhood","w":"D","s":"What were you like at primary school? What did your reports say?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"'Bright but daydreams.' 'Talks too much.' I was always in detention for calling out. Teachers said I was lazy."},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Sorry. That still stings."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry you heard that so often. Lazy people don't win features awards. Was it the same at home?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Mum says I never sat still for a meal. I was about seven when she first noticed."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Could we speak to your mum later, and does anyone still have your reports?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Mum's kept every one. She'd love to talk to you."},{"t":"2:12","band":"Impact","w":"D","s":"How does this affect home, money and relationships?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:16","w":"P","s":"Late fees on bills because I forget them. My partner Tom gets frustrated when I lose things."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Any problems with driving, like fines or accidents?","m":[7,10]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Two speeding fines. I just drift over the limit without noticing."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Other explanations","w":"D","s":"How's your mood? Do you still enjoy things?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine, honestly. I'm anxious about work, but I still love my hobbies and friends."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a spell of days feeling unusually high or speeded up, needing much less sleep?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"No. I've always been like this, every day. No spells."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"How's your sleep? And alcohol or drugs?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Sleep's OK. I drank a lot in my early twenties; about six units a week now. No drugs."},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Any difficulty reading people, or a need for fixed routines? And has your thyroid ever been checked?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"I'm good with people. I hate routines. Thyroid, not that I know of."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"With all this pressure, has it ever pushed you towards hurting yourself, or feeling you'd rather not be here?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"No, never. I'm stressed, not hopeless."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Any debts from impulse spending?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Not debts. Just a lot of things I don't need."},{"t":"3:28","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Anyone in the family with similar difficulties? And any heart problems, fainting, or relatives who died suddenly young?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:35","w":"P","s":"My brother was just like me at school. No heart problems anywhere."},{"t":"3:40","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Imogen. This fits adult ADHD: present since childhood, at home and school, and unmasked when your work lost its flexibility. I can't confirm it today.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Isn't ADHD just a fashionable excuse now?"},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"I understand the doubt. It's a real, lifelong condition, and the assessment is careful so it isn't over-diagnosed.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:04","w":"D","s":"Next: a short questionnaire today, a referral for a full specialist assessment using your mum's account and your reports, and a thyroid test with your GP. With your consent, I'll write to occupational health about adjustments now.","m":[12,13]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"Can I get tablets today?"},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"Not today. The diagnosis comes first, then heart and blood pressure checks before any medicine. Work changes can start straight away.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:30","w":"D","s":"What will you tell Tom about today?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"That it might be ADHD, there'll be a proper assessment, and work can help me meanwhile."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's the questionnaire for the waiting room. If the stress ever gets on top of you, contact your GP or NHS 111 option 2.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:47","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's the first time anyone's made sense of it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, states the referral and invites her own account","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear about her job and past criticism","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about inattention with concrete examples","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about hyperactivity and impulsivity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores why now and how she coped before","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset before 12 in two settings and a collateral source","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores impact on home, money, relationships and driving","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens depression and episodes of elevated mood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens sleep, substances, autism traits and thyroid","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and impulsivity risks","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks family history and cardiac history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives likely adult ADHD as an initial impression with next steps","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees occupational health letter, checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Covers the triad with examples, proves childhood onset in two settings, screens mimics and impulsivity risks, and gives a clear impression and next steps.","borderline":"Gets current symptoms but is thin on childhood, collateral or bipolar screening.","fail":"Calls it work stress without testing the pattern, or promises diagnosis and medication today."}},"c-u-10":{"setup":"Saturday lunchtime, A&E. Chloe Matthews, 28, a receptionist, took a paracetamol overdose last night and is on acetylcysteine, now alert. The A&E doctor wants a liaison review. In about ten minutes, take the overdose history, assess risk and agree a plan with her.","role":{"who":"Chloe Matthews, 28, female, patient","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Flat, quiet and ashamed, looking at the drip. Warms up if not judged. Tearful when she mentions her nephew. Guarded about her partner at first.","free":["Took the tablets after a row with her partner, Ryan","Her sister came and called 999","Feels stupid and embarrassed"],"ifasked":["Bought two packs of 16 from two shops on the way home; no earlier planning","Took all 32 over about an hour with a bottle of wine; no other drugs","No note; Ryan was out; she didn't lock the door","Wanted 'everything to stop'; thought she might die","Texted her sister Kirsty about an hour later","Low mood, poor sleep and little enjoyment since the summer; about three bottles of wine a week","Cut her arms at 16; no other self-harm; never seen mental health services","More paracetamol in the kitchen cupboard; Ryan shouts in rows but has never hit her"],"worries":["Can I go home once the drip's done?","Are you going to section me?","Please don't tell my sister how bad it's been."],"rules":["Says 'I'm fine now' unless asked directly about current thoughts","Becomes tearful about her nephew; settles if validated","Mentions the tablets at home only if asked about access"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists from the liaison team. Are you Chloe Matthews?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:07","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me, Chloe. I know you've had a hard time. Is now an okay moment to talk?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I suppose."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"First I'd like to hear what happened; then we can work out together how to keep you safe. Is that alright? It'll take about ten minutes, and the curtain's closed.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"OK. I feel so stupid. Everyone's wasting their time on me."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"You're not wasting anyone's time. Something made things feel unbearable last night, and that matters.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:36","band":"Before","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through yesterday, from when things started to go wrong?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Ryan and I had a massive row about eight. He went out. I just walked out too, and I ended up buying paracetamol."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Had you thought about taking tablets before last night? Where did you get them?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"Not really planned. Two packs, from the supermarket and a chemist on the way home."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"Did you leave a note or message, sort anything out, or do anything so no one would find you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"No note. Ryan was out, but I didn't lock the door or anything."},{"t":"1:12","band":"During","w":"D","s":"How many did you take, and over how long? Anything else with them?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"All 32. Over about an hour. With a bottle of wine. Nothing else."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"What were you expecting the tablets to do? Were you hoping to die?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"I wanted everything to stop. I thought I might die. I don't know if I wanted to."},{"t":"1:34","band":"After","w":"D","s":"What happened next?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"About an hour later I texted my sister, Kirsty. She came round and rang 999."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"How do you feel now about still being here?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"Embarrassed. Glad, I think. My nephew's five. I kept thinking about him in the ambulance."},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"Sorry."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Don't be sorry. He clearly means a lot to you. Take your time.","m":[2,11]},{"t":"2:03","band":"Mental state and history","w":"D","s":"Before last night, how had your mood been over the last few months? Sleep, enjoyment, energy?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"Rubbish since the summer. I wake at four, I don't enjoy anything, I cry in the loos at work."},{"t":"2:16","w":"D","s":"Have you felt hopeless, as if things won't get better?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Some days. Last week I thought everyone'd be better off without me. Then it passed."},{"t":"2:27","w":"D","s":"Any voices when nobody's there, or odd experiences others couldn't explain? And how much do you usually drink?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:33","w":"P","s":"No voices. About three bottles of wine a week, more lately."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Have you hurt yourself before, or seen anyone for your mental health?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"I cut my arms when I was 16. Never seen anyone. My GP doesn't know."},{"t":"2:48","band":"Risk now","w":"D","s":"Right now, are you having thoughts about ending your life? Any plans to try again?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"No. Not now. I don't want to put Kirsty through that again."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"I need to ask everyone this. Do you feel safe at home? Has Ryan ever hurt you, frightened you or controlled you?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"He shouts a lot when we argue. But he's never hit me. I feel safe."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you. If that ever changes, there's help I can put you in touch with. Are there more tablets at home?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"There's another pack of paracetamol in the kitchen cupboard."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"And what keeps you going?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Kirsty and my nephew. And I like my job, mostly."},{"t":"3:26","band":"Formulation","w":"D","s":"From what you've said, last night came in a moment of overwhelming upset, on top of months of feeling low and drinking more. So the priorities are your mood, the drinking and the stress at home.","m":[13]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"That's fair. I've not been right for ages. So what happens now? Can I go home once the drip's done?"},{"t":"3:45","band":"Plan together","w":"D","s":"Once the medical team are happy with your bloods, yes, with a plan we make together. Are you worried about something?","m":[13]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Are you going to section me?"},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"No. You've talked openly and we're planning together. That's for people who can't stay safe any other way.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:02","w":"D","s":"Let's write your safety plan. What are your warning signs, and what helps when it builds?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"Not sleeping. Drinking on my own. Talking to Kirsty helps. Going for a walk."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Could Kirsty clear the tablets from the cupboard and stay a few nights? With your agreement, I'd like to tell her the plan.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"Please don't tell her how bad it's been."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"I'll respect that. Could we agree she knows the plan and the tablets need to go?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"4:30","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's OK."},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"The crisis team will contact you within three days. I'll write to your GP today about treatment for your mood, talking therapy, and support with the drinking.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:43","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If those thoughts come back tonight, what will you do?","m":[14]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Text Kirsty. Or ring someone."},{"t":"4:49","w":"D","s":"Samaritans on 116 123 any time, NHS 111 option 2, or 999 if you might act on them. They're on your plan. I'll tell the A&E doctor and your nurse what we've agreed.","m":[12,14]},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I didn't think anyone would actually listen."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks privacy and consent to talk","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to shame and distress without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the trigger and any planning, including how tablets were obtained","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about a note, final acts and precautions against discovery","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks number and type of tablets, timing and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks intent and what she expected to happen","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks how she was found and how she feels about surviving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks current suicidal thoughts, plans and hopelessness","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens depressive and psychotic symptoms and alcohol use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks past self-harm and mental health contact","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks home safety, access to means and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Agrees a written safety plan with sister and removal of tablets","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Shares a needs-based formulation and follow-up with GP letter","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, gives crisis numbers and hands over to A&E","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A full before-during-after narrative, direct questions about current thoughts and home safety, a needs-based formulation and a collaborative safety plan with means removal and follow-up.","borderline":"Gets the overdose facts but misses current thoughts, access to means or home safety, or the plan is vague.","fail":"Checklist questioning with judgement, no direct suicide question, or discharge with a risk label and no plan."}},"c-u-12":{"setup":"Neuropsychiatry outpatients, Tuesday afternoon. Funmi Bankole, 22, has two years of brief out-of-body spells with odd face movements. Take a focused history and explain the likely diagnosis in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Funmilayo (Funmi) Bankole, 22, female, outpatient","open":"Yes. Funmi's fine.","manner":"Nervous and guarded, unsure why she is seeing a psychiatrist; vague unless asked specific questions. Relaxes and opens up when the doctor is calm. Relieved when given a clear explanation.","free":["The spells started about two years ago","She feels she is watching herself from above and nothing is real","They last a few minutes","She is scared she is going mad or has a tumour"],"ifasked":["A rising feeling in her stomach and a smell of burning rubber come first","Her flatmate says she stares, smacks her lips and doesn't answer for about a minute","Afterwards she is confused and exhausted for about 20 minutes and forgets parts","They happen about twice a month, more with poor sleep or alcohol, so she has stopped drinking","Febrile convulsions at age 2; no head injury, meningitis or family epilepsy","She has cut her hours at the pharmacy and stopped going out; mood lower for six months; no suicidal thoughts","She drives occasionally"],"worries":["Is it a brain tumour?","Do you think I'm mad?","Can I still drive?"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers to open questions until reassured","Only describes the smell and stomach feeling if asked about them directly","Becomes tearful about her shrinking life; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists in this clinic. Are you Funmilayo Bankole?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes. Funmi's fine."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Funmi. Your GP mentioned some unusual episodes. They sound unsettling.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"They really are. I didn't get why I'm seeing a psychiatrist, though. I'm not mad."},{"t":"0:20","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Our clinic sees symptoms that sit between the mind and the brain, and I'll be thinking about both. Could you walk me through one, from the very start?","m":[1,13]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"I just go weird. Everything feels unreal."},{"t":"0:36","band":"Before","w":"D","s":"What's the very first thing you notice, before the unreal feeling?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Sort of a rising feeling, here in my stomach."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Do you notice a smell or taste that isn't really there? Or a strong sense you've lived this moment before?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Yes, a smell of burning rubber. Every time. And sometimes I feel scared for no reason."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"And then?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Then I'm up near the ceiling, watching myself. Everything looks fake."},{"t":"1:08","band":"During","w":"D","s":"While that's happening, can you hear people and answer them?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"Not really. It's like they're far away."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Has anyone seen one? What did they notice about your face or hands?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"My flatmate. She says I stare, smack my lips and pick at my clothes. I don't answer for about a minute."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Do you ever fall, go stiff or shake all over, bite your tongue or wet yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"1:39","band":"After","w":"D","s":"How do you feel straight afterwards, and how long until you're yourself again?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Confused and shattered. About twenty minutes. I can't remember bits of it."},{"t":"1:52","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"How often do they happen now, compared with two years ago? Does anything bring them on?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"About once a month at first, twice a month now. Worse when I'm tired or I've had a drink, so I've stopped drinking."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Did you ever have fits as a baby or toddler, maybe with a high temperature?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"Mum says I had a couple when I was two, with fevers."},{"t":"2:18","w":"D","s":"Any serious head injury, meningitis or brain infection? Anyone in the family with epilepsy? Any medicines or drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"No to all of those. I don't take anything."},{"t":"2:30","band":"Other causes","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel detached or unreal for hours at a time, outside these spells? Any panic, racing heart or headaches with them?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"No. Only in the spells."},{"t":"2:41","band":"Impact and mood","w":"D","s":"How has all this affected your life, work and going out?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"I've cut my hours at the pharmacy. I don't go out. (tearful) My life's shrinking."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"That sounds really lonely, and it makes sense you've pulled back. Lots of people do. How's your mood been?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Lower, for six months or so."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"Has it ever got so bad that you've thought about wishing you weren't here?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"No, never that. I just want an answer."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"Do you drive, swim or work at heights?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"I drive sometimes. I passed my test last year."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's really clear. I think these are small seizures starting in one part of the brain, the temporal lobe. That's a type of epilepsy.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"Epilepsy? I looked it up. Could it be a brain tumour?"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"That's frightening to read. Nothing you've told me points to a tumour, and the scan will check properly. And no, you're not going mad: the smell and the lip movements come from the brain.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"So what happens now?"},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"I'm referring you urgently to the neurologists. They'll do a brain wave test, an EEG, and an MRI scan. Your GP will do bloods and a heart tracing.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"What if the brain wave test is normal?"},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"That's common between spells and doesn't rule it out. It helps a lot if your flatmate can film the next one on her phone.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:09","band":"Safety","w":"D","s":"Until neurology have seen you, please don't drive, and tell the DVLA. Showers rather than baths, and no swimming on your own.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"Not drive? I need the car for work."},{"t":"4:21","w":"D","s":"I know that's hard. Most people get back to driving once seizures are controlled. If a spell ever lasts over five minutes or you're hurt, call 999.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"4:31","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[13,14]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"Likely epilepsy, scan and EEG, no driving, tell the DVLA, flatmate films one."},{"t":"4:41","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Here's a leaflet. What else would you like to ask?","m":[14]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Nothing. Honestly, it's a relief to have a name for it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her worry about seeing a psychiatrist, and sets the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks specifically about aura: stomach feeling, smell or taste, déjà vu, fear","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about awareness, the witness account of automatisms, and generalised features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about post-episode confusion, tiredness, memory and recovery time","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes onset, frequency and triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about febrile convulsions, head injury, infection, family history and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Considers depersonalisation disorder, panic and migraine","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens impact, mood, suicidal thoughts and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains likely temporal lobe epilepsy in plain words and addresses tumour fears","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names neurology referral, EEG, MRI, bloods, ECG and a witness video","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving, DVLA notification, water safety and when to call 999","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her distress about her shrinking life and fears","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Avoids jargon and checks her understanding","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes with a teach-back, leaflet and invitation to ask questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A precise before-during-after history with witness account and risk factors, a plain explanation of temporal lobe epilepsy, tests, and driving and safety advice.","borderline":"Gets the diagnosis but misses specific aura questions, the witness account or the driving advice.","fail":"Calls it depersonalisation disorder or anxiety, or never asks about awareness, automatisms or driving."}},"c-u-15":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, medical ward. Leila Haddad, 28, woke two days ago unable to see from her left eye; the physicians suspect a psychological cause. Take a history of her visual symptoms and carry out fundoscopy in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Leila Haddad, 28, female, medical inpatient","open":"Yes. I still can't see from this eye.","manner":"Quiet, polite and composed on the surface, wary that doctors think she is making it up. Becomes tearful when asked about her job. Warms when the doctor takes the eye seriously.","free":["She woke on Monday unable to see from her left eye","Nobody has looked at the back of her eyes","She thinks the doctors believe she is imagining it"],"ifasked":["Total darkness, no pain, no pain on eye movement, no flashes, floaters or curtain","No headache, weakness, numbness or speech problems; no previous episodes","No contact lenses, diabetes, blood pressure or medicines","Lost her job as an office administrator a week before","Low mood and poor sleep since; sometimes thinks she'd rather not wake up; no plans; her husband and faith keep her going","Hasn't driven since; normally drives to the shops"],"worries":["Am I going blind?","The doctors think I'm making it up, don't they?","So is there something wrong with my eye or not?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when asked about the job; settles if validated","Only mentions thoughts of not waking up if asked about mood directly","Follows examination instructions; says the light is very bright during the macula check"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working alongside the medical team. Are you Leila Haddad?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. I still can't see from this eye."},{"t":"0:09","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, that sounds really worrying. I'd like to hear exactly what happened, then look into the back of your eyes with a light. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","w":"P","s":"Okay. Nobody has looked yet. Why a psychiatrist, though? Do they think I'm making it up?"},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"I can see why it feels like that. I don't think you're making anything up. Your sight loss is real, and I want to help find out why.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:33","band":"The story","w":"D","s":"Take me back to Monday morning. What did you notice?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"I woke up and the left eye was just black. I thought I was tired. My husband made me come in."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"Is it completely dark, or blurred, patchy, or like a curtain? Has it changed since?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Completely dark. It hasn't changed at all."},{"t":"0:54","band":"Red flags","w":"D","s":"Does it hurt when you move that eye? Any flashes or floaters before it went?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"No pain. No flashes."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Any headache, weakness, numbness, tingling or trouble speaking? Ever had blurred vision or numbness before that went away?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that, ever."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Do you wear contact lenses? Any diabetes, blood pressure problems, or medicines?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"No. I'm normally healthy."},{"t":"1:21","band":"Stress and mood","w":"D","s":"What's been happening in your life over the last few weeks?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I lost my job last week. I'd been there four years."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry. Losing your job, then your sight, all in one week. That's a lot to carry.","m":[4,12]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"It is. I haven't been sleeping."},{"t":"1:41","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any moments where life hasn't felt worth it?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Low. Sometimes I think I'd rather not wake up. But I'd never do anything."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have those thoughts ever gone further, to plans? What keeps you going?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"No plans. My husband. My faith."},{"t":"2:01","band":"Acuity and pupils","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Before the light, I'll check how well each eye sees. Cover your left eye and read the lowest line you can on this chart.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"All the way to the bottom."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Now cover the right. Can you see my hand moving? Can you tell when this light is on?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Nothing. It's black."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now I'll shine a light in each eye, then swing it from one to the other. Keep looking ahead.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Your pupils react normally and equally, even when I swing the light. Can you read the numbers in these coloured dots with your right eye?","m":[5,11]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Twelve, eight, twenty-nine... yes, all of them."},{"t":"2:44","band":"Fundoscopy","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll dim the lights. Please look at that spot on the far wall and keep your eye still, even when I come close. The nurse will stay with us.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"2:53","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"I'm looking at your left eye with my left eye. I can see a good red glow from here. Now I'm coming in to look at the nerve at the back.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:06","w":"D","s":"The nerve is a healthy pink with sharp edges. Now I'm following the blood vessels, top and bottom, both sides.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Last bit: look straight into the light for just a second.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"That's very bright."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Sorry, all done on that side. Now the right eye, the same way. Keep looking at the spot.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"Red glow, nerve, vessels and the centre all look healthy on this side too. Lights back on.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"3:38","band":"Explain","w":"P","s":"So is there something wrong with my eye or not?"},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"The back of both eyes looks healthy, and your pupils react normally. That's reassuring. But the eye specialists have equipment I don't, so I'm making sure they see you today.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"Am I going blind?"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"I understand that fear. Nothing I've seen today suggests damage to the eye. Until they've seen you, please don't drive.","m":[8,9,12]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"I haven't, don't worry."},{"t":"4:05","band":"Safety net and close","w":"D","s":"If you get any eye pain, a headache, weakness, or the right eye changes, tell the nurse straight away. And if the dark thoughts get stronger, tell her too.","m":[10]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Okay. I will."},{"t":"4:16","w":"D","s":"I'll update your medical team now and come back tomorrow to see how you're coping. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[9,13]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"The eye team today, no driving, tell the nurse if anything changes, and you're back tomorrow."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Any questions for me?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:34","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you for actually looking."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains role and plan, gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset, extent and progression of the visual loss","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks red flags: eye pain, flashes, floaters, headache, neurological symptoms, past episodes, risk factors","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores recent stresses, mood, suicidal thoughts and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks acuity in each eye, pupils with swinging light test, and colour vision","d":"Content"},{"c":"Fundoscopy of the left eye: fixation, correct eye, red reflex, disc, vessels, macula last","d":"Content"},{"c":"Repeats fundoscopy on the right eye","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains normal findings without a premature label and addresses fear of blindness","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Arranges same-day ophthalmology, updates the medical team and advises no driving","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets for new eye or neurological symptoms and worsening suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Describes each step of the examination as it happens","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her fear of not being believed and her distress about her job","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A focused history with red flags and a mood and risk screen, acuity and pupils before a correct bilateral fundoscopy, and an open, reassuring explanation with same-day eye review.","borderline":"Correct fundoscopy but thin red-flag history, no mood screen, or a premature 'psychological' label.","fail":"Accepts a psychological cause without examining properly, misses the mood and risk screen, or tells her nothing is wrong."}},"c-u-22":{"setup":"Memory clinic, Thursday afternoon. Mr Kenneth Lau, 70, retired restaurant owner, has six months of forgetting names and birthdays. His wife is in the waiting room. Explain, then complete a structured cognitive check across all domains in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Mr Kenneth Lau, 70, male, retired restaurant owner","open":"Yes, Kenneth.","manner":"Polite, keen to do well, a little anxious. Gets flustered when writing or when he can't recall; settles if reassured. Seeks reassurance at the end.","free":["Forgets friends' names and his grandchildren's birthdays","Finds it embarrassing","His wife is waiting outside"],"ifasked":["Started about six months ago","Memories from long ago are vivid","Mood is fine, just fed up with the forgetting","Drinks a couple of glasses of wine a week","English is his everyday language; left school at 16","Wears reading glasses; hearing is fine","His uncle had Alzheimer's"],"worries":["Is this a test? Will I fail?","How did I do?","Is it Alzheimer's?"],"rules":["If testing starts with no explanation, asks what the doctor is checking for","Cannot name the building; recalls none of the three words unless cued, then gets one","Gets flustered writing two sentences; settles if the doctor reassures without pushing"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and consent","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Mr Lau?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:03","w":"P","s":"Yes, Kenneth."},{"t":"0:05","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Kenneth. I'd like to do some memory and thinking tasks with you today, about ten minutes, so we can see which parts are working well and which aren't.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:14","w":"P","s":"Like a test?"},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"Some will feel easy, some harder; that's normal and there's no pass or fail. Do you have your glasses and hearing aid? Is it all right to start?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"Go on then. Glasses are here, and my hearing's fine."},{"t":"0:29","band":"Brief history","w":"D","s":"First, tell me what you've noticed, and since when.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"About six months. Friends' names, the grandchildren's birthdays. It's embarrassing, honestly."},{"t":"0:40","w":"D","s":"That sounds frustrating, especially with people you care about. What about things from long ago?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Clear as day. The day we opened the restaurant, I could tell you every dish."},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"And how's your mood? And roughly how much alcohol a week?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine, just fed up with this. A couple of glasses of wine a week."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"Is English the language you use every day, and when did you leave school?","m":[1]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"English always. I left at sixteen to work in my dad's kitchen."},{"t":"1:10","band":"Orientation and attention","w":"D","s":"Now some questions on orientation. What's the day, date, month, year and season?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:16","w":"P","s":"Thursday, the 5th of November, 2026. Autumn."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"And what's the name of this building, and which floor are we on?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Oh... this is a new place. I'm not really sure."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Next: take 7 away from 100, then keep taking 7 away until I say stop.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:35","w":"P","s":"93, 86, 79, 73... no, 72. 65."},{"t":"1:46","band":"Registration","w":"D","s":"Good. I'll say three words. Repeat them back, and keep them in mind; I'll ask later. River, candle, garden.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"River, candle, garden."},{"t":"1:58","band":"Language","w":"D","s":"Now some language tasks. What's this called? And this?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"A watch. A pen."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Please say after me: no ifs, ands or buts.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"No ifs, ands or buts."},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"Take this paper in your right hand, fold it in half and put it on the table.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"(does it correctly) There."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"Now write two sentences about your restaurant, please.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Two sentences... hang on. I'm all in a muddle with this. Sorry, this is ridiculous."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise; lots of people find writing on the spot hard. That's plenty, thank you.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:52","band":"Visuospatial and executive","w":"D","s":"Now a drawing task. Put the numbers in this clock and set the hands to ten past eleven.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"(draws carefully) Is that right?"},{"t":"3:33","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Next, tell me as many words as you can starting with F in one minute. No names of people or places.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Fish, fork, fire, father... fun, fast... flower... food."},{"t":"4:41","band":"Delayed recall","w":"D","s":"Time's up. Now, what were the three words I asked you to remember?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Oh dear. They've gone completely."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"One was something in a house that gives light.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"A candle? I don't know the others."},{"t":"4:59","w":"P","s":"So, did I do well?"},{"t":"5:01","band":"Feedback","w":"D","s":"How do you feel it went?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"Not brilliantly. The words, and the writing."},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"That fits what I saw. You did well on the date, the sums, naming and the clock. Remembering the words and knowing where we are were harder.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:18","w":"P","s":"Is it Alzheimer's? My uncle had that."},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"I can see why that's on your mind after your uncle. It's too early to say: mood, vitamins or thyroid problems can all affect memory, and we need to check those first.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"5:33","w":"P","s":"So what happens now?"},{"t":"5:35","band":"Next steps","w":"D","s":"Three things: blood tests today, a brain scan, and, if you're happy, a few questions for your wife about day-to-day memory. Then we'll see you, together if you like, with the results.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"She can come in. I'd rather she knew."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Just to check: can you tell me what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Bloods, a scan, you talk to my wife, then we come back."},{"t":"5:59","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You worked hard today. What other questions do you have before I bring your wife in?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"None. Thank you, doctor. That was kinder than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains the purpose, and checks glasses, hearing, language and schooling","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gains consent and reassures when he struggles or apologises","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a brief history: onset, long-term memory, mood and alcohol","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation to time and place, and attention with serial sevens","d":"Content"},{"c":"Registers three words and tests delayed recall after distraction, using a cue","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests language: naming, repetition, a three-stage command and writing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests visuospatial function with a clock drawing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests executive function with one minute of letter fluency","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks his view first, then feeds back honestly on harder and easier parts","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Avoids a premature diagnosis and names reversible causes to check","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names next steps (bloods, scan, wife's account) and checks his understanding","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Closes warmly and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Consent and senses checked, every domain tested in order with recall after distraction, and honest, kind feedback with clear next steps.","borderline":"Most domains covered but language or executive missed, or feedback vague or slightly falsely reassuring.","fail":"Starts without consent, runs out of time on one long instrument, or gives a diagnosis or false reassurance."}},"c-u-23":{"setup":"Friday night, A&E paediatric side room. Amira Hussain, 16, took a paracetamol overdose at 7 pm. She has spoken to A&E staff already and is overwhelmed. In about ten minutes, take a history of the overdose and her circumstances, and assess her current risk.","role":{"who":"Amira Hussain, 16, female, Year 11 pupil","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Withdrawn, arms folded, little eye contact; tired of repeating herself. Warms up if the doctor is gentle and doesn't rush. Tearful about her parents.","free":["Took paracetamol tonight","Has told her story several times already","Finds A&E noisy and frightening"],"ifasked":["16 tablets at 7 pm in her bedroom, from the kitchen first-aid box","Not planned; girls bullying her online had posted cruel comments","Wanted to die and thought the dose would be enough; alone; no note","Went to bed hoping not to wake; woke nauseous, scared, told her parents","Three months of low mood, poor sleep, appetite and concentration; stopped piano and sketching; hopeless","Scratches her forearm with a pen on bad days for the last month","No current plan, but still sometimes wishes she wasn't here; no previous attempts","No alcohol or drugs; no abuse at home; parents are supportive"],"worries":["Will you tell my parents everything?","Do I have to stay here?","Will the bullying ever stop?"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers until her tiredness is acknowledged","Mentions the arm scratching only if asked directly about other self-harm","Cries when talking about her mum; settles if validated","Asks the doctor not to tell her parents about her arm yet"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amira?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:03","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:05","w":"D","s":"I know you've told your story a few times already tonight. I'm sorry; it's a lot.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"(shrugs)"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"This stays between us, unless I'm worried you're not safe; then I'd tell you first. Could we talk for about ten minutes, just us, about what happened?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:24","band":"The overdose","w":"D","s":"What was happening just before you took the tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"Some girls from school posted stuff about me. Again."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hurtful. How many tablets did you take, and when?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Sixteen paracetamol. About seven. In my room."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"Where were they from? And was it planned, or a spur-of-the-moment thing?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"The first-aid box in the kitchen. I didn't plan it. I just couldn't cope."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"What did you want to happen when you took them?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"I wanted it all to end."},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Did you think that many could kill you? Was anyone with you, and did you leave a note?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:06","w":"P","s":"I thought it'd be enough. I was on my own. No note."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"What did you do after you'd taken them?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Went to bed. I hoped I wouldn't wake up. Then I felt really sick and got scared, and told Mum."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"How do you feel now about still being here?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"I regret it. Mum hasn't stopped crying. I've ruined everything. (starts to cry)"},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"She's upset because she loves you, and she's right here. You told her when you felt ill, and that took courage.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) I suppose."},{"t":"1:44","band":"What led up to it","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the posts. How long has this been going on?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:48","w":"P","s":"Three months. Same girls. They won't stop. They'll just find another way online."},{"t":"1:55","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, feeling there's no way out. How are things at home, and with other friends?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Home's fine, Mum and Dad are nice. I've got one friend left."},{"t":"2:07","w":"D","s":"Is anyone, at home, at school or online, hurting you or making you do things you don't want to?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"No. Just the comments."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over these three months?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"Low. All the time."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep, appetite and concentration? And things you used to enjoy?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"Can't get to sleep. Not hungry. Can't focus at school. I stopped piano and sketching."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"How do you see things in the future? Any drinking, drugs, or hearing or seeing things others don't?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"Hopeless. It'll never stop. No drink or drugs. Nothing weird."},{"t":"2:44","band":"Self-harm and risk","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt yourself in any other way, or tried to end your life before?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Never tried before. I scratch my arm with a pen. Only when it's really bad."},{"t":"2:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that's not easy. Can I have a look later so we can clean it?","m":[2,7]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Right now, are you having thoughts about ending your life? Any plan?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"No plan. Sometimes I still wish I wasn't here."},{"t":"3:12","w":"D","s":"If you went home, what tablets or sharp things could you get to?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"There's always tablets in the kitchen."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Protective factors","w":"D","s":"What's kept you going through all this, and would you accept help from the CAMHS team?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"Mum and Dad. I want to do my GCSEs. Yeah, I'd try."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"So you took the tablets wanting it to end, after months of feeling low because of the bullying, and now you want help. Have I got that right?","m":[10]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Do I have to stay here? What happens to me now?"},{"t":"3:44","w":"D","s":"Tonight is about keeping your body safe: a blood test at eleven, treatment if needed, and a night on the children's ward. Tomorrow CAMHS will see you and your parents.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"And school?"},{"t":"3:57","w":"D","s":"With your agreement, school's safeguarding lead should know, so they can act on the bullying.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. Don't tell Mum about my arm yet."},{"t":"4:07","w":"D","s":"I'll tell your parents the plan and ask them to lock away medicines at home. The nurses need to know about your arm to dress it; we can tell your mum together tomorrow. Is that okay?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's fair."},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"If things feel unsafe tonight, tell the nurse straight away. What questions do you have?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:26","w":"P","s":"No. Thanks for not rushing me."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, states confidentiality limits and sees her alone","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges her tiredness and distress and validates her feelings","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes trigger, number, time, source, planning, intent, lethality belief, precautions and note","d":"Content"},{"c":"Covers what she did after, how help came, and how she feels now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the bullying, home and friends, and asks directly about abuse or exploitation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits depressive symptoms, hopelessness, substances and psychosis screen","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about other self-harm and previous attempts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses current suicidal thoughts, plans and access to means at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies protective factors and willingness to accept help","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises and checks the summary with her","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains tonight's medical plan, overnight stay, CAMHS review and school involvement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees what parents will be told and the lock-away advice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets for tonight and invites questions","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Sees her alone, maps before-during-after with intent, finds depression and the arm scratching, assesses current risk and access, and agrees a clear overnight plan.","borderline":"Gets the overdose details but misses the self-harm, access to tablets, or what parents will be told.","fail":"Interviews only with parents present, treats regret as safety, or suggests discharge tonight."}},"c-u-24":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, orthopaedic ward. Callum Ashworth-Nye, 35, has a mild learning disability and a broken right hip. He refuses urgent surgery. His pain is controlled. In about ten minutes, assess his capacity to refuse the operation and explain the result to him.","role":{"who":"Callum Ashworth-Nye, 35, male, lives with his housebound mother","open":"Yes. Can I go home now?","manner":"Friendly and open but anxious to get home. Says 'I don't understand' if words are complicated. Upset if he feels called stupid; settles with reassurance.","free":["Wants to go home to look after his mum","Doesn't want the operation","Believes God will heal his hip; it hurts less today"],"ifasked":["Fell off his bike on a busy road yesterday; a stranger called the ambulance","The bone doctors said it's broken and they want to 'cut his leg'; something about the blood","Remembers them saying pain and bleeding","Has prayed all night","His mum can't leave the house; no one is with her","His mum helps him with letters and doctors' appointments","No one has told him what to decide"],"worries":["Can I go home?","Who will look after Mum?","Will it hurt? Will I wake up?"],"rules":["Says 'I don't understand' to any medical word","Can repeat the key facts straight after a picture, but cannot recall them a few minutes later","Says 'nothing bad can happen, God will fix it' when asked about risks","Gets upset if he feels called stupid; settles if reassured"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Callum?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:03","w":"P","s":"Yes. Can I go home now?"},{"t":"0:05","w":"D","s":"I know you want to get home. I'm here to talk about the hip operation and what you think about it, for about ten minutes.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:13","w":"P","s":"I don't want it."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"That's okay to say. I'll use simple words, and stop me if anything is confusing.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","band":"His understanding","w":"D","s":"What happened yesterday?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"I fell off my bike on the big road. A man called the ambulance."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"That sounds frightening. What have the bone doctors told you about your hip?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"It's broke. They want to cut my leg. Something about the blood. And pain and bleeding."},{"t":"0:40","band":"Fill the gaps","w":"D","s":"Let me show you a picture. This is your hip. The top of the thigh bone has snapped, here.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Blood is like food for the bone. The break can cut the food off, and then the top of the bone can die.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Die? Like, go dead?"},{"t":"1:00","w":"D","s":"Yes. The operation joins the bone with metal while you're asleep. It can cause some pain, bleeding or infection. Without it, the bone may not join, and you might not walk properly.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what I've said, in your words?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"The bone's broke. Blood feeds it. They join it with metal. If I don't, it might die."},{"t":"1:22","w":"D","s":"That's exactly right. Well done.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:25","band":"Weigh","w":"D","s":"So what makes you say no to the operation?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"God will fix it. I prayed all night. It already hurts less."},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"Your faith clearly matters a lot, and I respect that. The pain is less partly because of the painkillers. What do you think happens to the bone if it isn't joined?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"Nothing bad. God will fix it. Nothing bad can happen."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"What are the good things about having the operation, and the bad things about not having it?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"There's no bad things. I just want to go home to Mum."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"Tell me about your mum. Who's looking after her now?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Nobody. She can't go out. I do her shopping and her tablets. (voice breaks)"},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"You really care about her, and it must be awful being stuck in here. I'll ask the social worker to get help to her today.","m":[2,6]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Today? Really?"},{"t":"2:20","w":"D","s":"Yes. If your mum had help, would you feel differently about the operation?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"No. God will fix my hip."},{"t":"2:28","band":"Communicate","w":"D","s":"So what's your decision about the operation? And has anyone told you what you should choose?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"No operation. Nobody told me. It's my choice."},{"t":"2:38","band":"Retain","w":"D","s":"Callum, earlier I showed you this picture. Can you tell me again what can happen if the bone isn't joined?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"Erm... I don't know. It'll be fine."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Take your time; here's the picture again. What does the blood do?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"I don't remember. I'm not stupid, you know."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"No, not at all. This is a hard decision with a lot to remember, and lots of people need help with decisions this big.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:06","band":"The causal link","w":"D","s":"Do you sometimes find new things hard to hold on to? Who usually helps you with big decisions?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Mum helps with letters and the doctor's."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Explain the outcome","w":"D","s":"Callum, I can see how much your faith and your mum matter to you, so I'll be honest about what I've found.","m":[2,10]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:25","w":"D","s":"You understood the picture really well. But a few minutes later it had gone, and you said nothing bad can happen. So this decision is too hard to make on your own today.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"So can I go home now, then?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"Not yet; your hip needs looking after first. The bone doctors will now decide what's best for you, and they must listen to you, your mum and what your faith means to you.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Will they make me have it?"},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"They'll talk with you and your mum on the phone first. A nurse who helps people with learning disabilities will be with you, and the chaplain can pray with you.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Will it hurt? Will I wake up?"},{"t":"4:06","w":"D","s":"You'd be asleep and won't feel it, and you'd wake up afterwards with medicine for the pain. Can you tell me what happens next?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"The bone doctors talk to me and Mum. Someone helps Mum. I can pray."},{"t":"4:21","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"That's right. What would you like the bone doctors to know about you?","m":[12]},{"t":"4:25","w":"P","s":"Tell them I'm scared. And I want to pray first."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"I'll tell them both. Thank you for talking to me so honestly, Callum.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why here in plain words and offers support","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his fear, faith and worry about his mother; reassures when upset","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks what he knows, then gives the key facts with a picture and no jargon","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing: his reasons, pros and cons, and the effect of analgesia","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores and removes pressure about his mother","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests communication and asks about pressure from others","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention after a gap of several minutes, with the same picture","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the causal link to his learning disability","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the outcome honestly and simply","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the best-interests process and whose views count, with LD nurse and chaplain","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding of next steps and closes kindly","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Supports him with plain words and pictures, tests all four abilities with retention checked later, separates faith from weighing, and explains a clear best-interests route kindly.","borderline":"Correct outcome but retention tested immediately, mother's care not addressed, or explanation too technical.","fail":"Decides on the diagnosis or the unwise choice, calls faith itself incapacity, or uses medical jargon throughout."}},"c-u-25":{"setup":"Sunday evening, A&E. Stuart Pembridge, 30, a primary school caretaker, has asked to see a mental health professional but won't tell triage why. He is tense and watching the door. In about ten minutes, find out what is troubling him and assess the risk.","role":{"who":"Stuart Pembridge, 30, male, primary school caretaker","open":"Yes. Is this private?","manner":"Very tense, quiet, avoids eye contact; opens up only once confidentiality is explained and he feels he won't be judged. Ashamed and tearful when asked about the child; resigned but cooperative at the end.","free":["Wants help but needs to know if this is private","Is worried he cannot control his feelings","Is frightened of being arrested"],"ifasked":["Sexually drawn to girls under 12 for as long as he can remember","Short relationships with women that ended through lack of interest","Last week babysat a friend's 8-year-old for four hours, became aroused on the sofa, did not touch her","Has had fantasies about her since; has been asked to babysit again on Saturday","Has looked at indecent images of children online for a few years; keeps them on a memory stick","Never arrested; never touched a child","Anxious, poor sleep; no suicidal thoughts; no alcohol or drugs; no psychotic symptoms","Sometimes alone with children in the school hall at work"],"worries":["Is this private?","Are you going to call the police?","Will I lose my job?"],"rules":["Will not disclose until confidentiality has been explained","Mentions the images only if asked directly about images or the internet","Becomes tearful with shame; settles if the doctor is non-judgemental","Asks for tablets instead of the police; accepts the explanation if it is honest"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Stuart Pembridge?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is this private?"},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"We keep what you say confidential, with one exception: if someone, especially a child, might be at risk, I'd have to share it. I'd tell you first.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Right. Okay."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"You've come in for a reason, and that took courage. We've got about ten minutes. What's been happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:25","w":"P","s":"I've got... feelings. About kids. Young girls. And something happened last week and I'm scared I can't control it."},{"t":"0:36","band":"His concern","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. I can see how hard that was to say. Why tonight?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"My mate asked me to babysit again on Saturday. I couldn't say no without explaining."},{"t":"0:49","band":"Nature of the interest","w":"D","s":"What ages are the girls you're drawn to?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Under twelve. Usually."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"How long have you been aware of this? And have you been attracted to adults too?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"As long as I can remember. I've had a few girlfriends, but it never lasted. I wasn't interested."},{"t":"1:07","w":"D","s":"How do you see these feelings yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"They're wrong. Disgusting. I hate myself for it. (tearful)"},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much shame you're carrying. Coming here to stop harm was the right thing to do.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","band":"Recent events","w":"D","s":"Tell me what happened last week.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"I babysat my mate's daughter. She's eight. Four hours, just us. We were watching cartoons and I got aroused."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"Did you touch her in any way? Do you think she noticed anything?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"No. I didn't touch her. I don't think she noticed."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"And since then, what's been going through your mind?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:47","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking about her. I can't stop it."},{"t":"1:51","band":"Images and offending","w":"D","s":"Some people with these feelings look at images of children online. Has that happened for you?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"(long pause) Yes. For a few years."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"Do you keep any?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"There's a memory stick at home. I hate that it's there."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Any police involvement in the past, or have you touched a child before?","m":[3,6]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"Never. Never been arrested, never touched anyone."},{"t":"2:18","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"How have you been in yourself? Mood, sleep?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Anxious. I can't sleep. I keep thinking the police will come."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or of ending your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"No. Not that."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs? Hearing voices or anything unusual?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No, I don't drink. Nothing like that."},{"t":"2:38","band":"Access and protective factors","w":"D","s":"At work, are you ever alone with children?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:41","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. In the hall, setting up. Kids come in and out."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"What's stopped you acting on these feelings so far?","m":[10]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"I know it's wrong. Kids can't want that. That's why I came."},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"What happens now? Are you going to call the police?"},{"t":"2:57","band":"Safeguarding","w":"D","s":"Stuart, you came here to stop harm happening, and I'll be honest with you. Yes. Because of the images and your contact with children, I have to inform the police and children's services tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Can't you just give me tablets and leave the police out of it?"},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"I understand why you'd want that. Treatment can help with urges, and I'll make sure you're referred. But I can't keep this from the people who protect children. Because you work at a school, there's also a set route for that tomorrow.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"I'll lose my job. My life's over."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"That's a huge fear, and some of it may be real. When people feel like that, they sometimes think of ending their life. Is that happening now?","m":[3,8]},{"t":"3:39","w":"P","s":"No. I'm scared, but not like that."},{"t":"3:42","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can we agree you won't babysit on Saturday or be alone with any child, and that you won't destroy the memory stick?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'll tell my mate I'm ill."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"For you: a referral to a specialist service, and Stop It Now on 0808 1000 900. If you ever feel unsafe, call 999 or Samaritans on 116 123. The crisis team will ring you tomorrow.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what we've agreed?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"No kids, keep the stick, you tell the police, I call that number, and someone rings me tomorrow."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You did the right thing coming in. What questions do you have before I speak to the police?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"None. Just... thank you for not looking at me like that."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and explains confidentiality and its limits before disclosure","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Acknowledges the courage of attending and invites his story openly","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to shame and fear without judgement or disgust","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the age and sex focus, onset and adult relationships","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the babysitting event: arousal, touching, fantasies since","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about images, where they are kept, and previous offending","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep, substances and psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts before and after explaining the police","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies access to children through work and babysitting","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Identifies protective factors: insight, help-seeking","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains honestly the duty to inform police, children's services and the school route","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees no contact with children and keeping the evidence","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives support numbers and follow-up, checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Limits stated first, direct questions on ages, touching, images and access, suicide risk checked before and after, and honest explanation of safeguarding with support offered.","borderline":"Gets the disclosure but avoids asking about images or access at work, or is vague about who will be told.","fail":"Promises total confidentiality, shows disgust, misses suicide risk, or discharges without safeguarding action."}},"c-u-26":{"setup":"Saturday night, A&E side room. Dominic Arrieta, 28, has waited hours, is agitated and insists on seeing Dr Fenwick, who once treated his asthma. Take a focused history to find the likely diagnosis, assess risk, and hand over a safe plan.","role":{"who":"Dominic Arrieta, 28, male, professional online gamer","open":"Yes. I've waited hours. I just want to see Dr Fenwick.","manner":"Irritable, pacing, clipped answers at first. Settles a little when his wait is acknowledged and he feels listened to; flares again whenever told she will not come.","free":["He has waited about five hours","Dr Fenwick treated his asthma attack months ago","She is the love of his life","He is certain she will come if told he is here"],"ifasked":["She blinked twice and raised her eyebrows, which proved her feelings","He has come back to A&E with minor problems several times to see her","He found her online and sent a friend request she ignored; she is keeping it secret","He has two flights to Venice tomorrow morning and plans to take her home tonight to pack","He would use force if security stopped him; he trains in mixed martial arts","No weapons, no police record, no past mental health contact","Mood good, sleep and appetite normal, no voices, rarely drinks, no drugs","Never felt this way about anyone before"],"worries":["When is she coming?","Why won't anyone let me see her?","Are you going to keep me here?"],"rules":["Raises his voice if the doctor hints she might come and then backs off","Calms slightly if the wait and his frustration are named","Answers the force question honestly if asked calmly and directly","Becomes defensive if told outright that his belief is false"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Safe start","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Dominic Arrieta?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. I've waited hours. I just want to see Dr Fenwick."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry about the long wait. I can see you're frustrated. Can we sit down and talk? I'd like about ten minutes.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Fine, but quickly."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"Tell me about Dr Fenwick, and why it matters so much tonight.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"She's the love of my life. If someone told her I'm here she'd come straight down."},{"t":"0:46","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"How did you first meet her?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"I had a bad asthma attack. She looked after me. We connected straight away."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"What happened that showed you how she felt about you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:07","w":"P","s":"She blinked twice, slowly, and raised her eyebrows. You don't do that with just anyone. It was a message."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How long ago was that, and how often do you think about her now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:28","w":"P","s":"Back in the spring. Every day. She thinks about me too, I know it."},{"t":"1:38","band":"How fixed","w":"D","s":"On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:42","w":"P","s":"A hundred."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"Have you spoken to her since that day?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:50","w":"P","s":"Not properly. I found her on Instagram and sent a request. She hasn't accepted it."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"What did that mean to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"She can't be seen with a patient. She's keeping it quiet. It's obvious."},{"t":"2:14","band":"Pursuit","w":"D","s":"How many times have you come back to A&E hoping to see her?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Five, six. Chest, a cut, my inhaler. She's never on when I come."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Have you found out her address? Have you written to her or waited for her anywhere?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"I know the area. I haven't been to the house. Not yet."},{"t":"2:48","w":"D","s":"What were you planning to do tonight if you saw her?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"I've got two tickets to Venice, first flight tomorrow. I'll take her home to pack. So when's she coming?"},{"t":"3:07","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you've invested in this. I'll be honest with you: she won't be coming down tonight.","m":[2]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Then what's the point of you? This is a joke. I've been sat here five hours."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"Five hours is a long time, and I'd be fed up too. I'm not here to argue with you. I'd like to understand the whole picture so I can help you tonight.","m":[1,13]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Go on then."},{"t":"3:46","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been lately? Any times you've felt on top of the world, or needed hardly any sleep?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Mood's good, apart from tonight. Sleep's normal. Eating's fine."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"Do you hear people talking when nobody's around, or feel people are watching you or plotting against you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:15","w":"D","s":"What about alcohol? And any drugs, even now and then?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"Hardly drink. Never touched drugs. I game for a living, I need a clear head."},{"t":"4:32","band":"Force and risk","w":"D","s":"Dominic, if staff or security stop you seeing her tonight, what would you do?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:39","w":"P","s":"I'd go through them. I do MMA. They wouldn't stop me."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being straight with me. I take that seriously. Have you ever hurt anyone, or carried anything you could use as a weapon?","m":[6,13]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"No. I've never needed to. I've never been in trouble with the police."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"If this falls through, could you see yourself doing anything to hurt yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"No way. Why would I? We're going to Venice."},{"t":"5:18","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt this way about anyone before? Any mental health help in the past?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Never. This is the real thing. And no, I've never seen a psychiatrist."},{"t":"5:32","band":"Close and act","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me all this. I'm worried about tonight, for you and for others, so I'd like you to stay here while I speak with my senior. Can I get you a drink?","m":[9,13]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Whatever. Water. But I'm not waiting another five hours."},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"I'll come back and tell you what's happening within half an hour.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:01","w":"P","s":"[Nurse in charge, outside the room] How did it go?"},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"He believes Dr Fenwick secretly loves him, for months, from a blink and raised eyebrows. He's been back six times, has searched for her online and has flights to Venice tomorrow. He says he'd use force if stopped, and trains in MMA.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"[Nurse in charge] Right. What do you need?"},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"Security outside the door, no one alone with him, and call the police if he leaves. I'm phoning the consultant now for a Mental Health Act assessment tonight.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"I'm also asking the A&E clinical lead to warn Dr Fenwick and trust security, and I'll record why we shared it. Can his notes be flagged so he never gets routed to her?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"[Nurse in charge] Security's already on the corridor. I'll flag the record."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, names the long wait and his anger, and sits him down","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells him honestly that Dr Fenwick will not come, without arguing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits how they met and the signals he took as proof","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction and how he explains the ignored friend request","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about repeat visits, online searching, her address and tonight's plan","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about force if stopped, weapons, violence and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, mania, psychotic symptoms and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past similar attachments and psychiatric history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks him to stay and promises a time to return","d":"Close"},{"c":"Gives the nurse in charge a clear summary pointing to erotomania","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Sets security, no lone working, police if he leaves, and Mental Health Act assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges a recorded warning to Dr Fenwick and a flag on his record","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his feelings and stays calm when he escalates","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm, honest that she will not come, proves a fixed erotomanic belief, finds the Venice plan and the threat of force, and acts on it before leaving.","borderline":"Reaches the diagnosis but misses the plan for tonight or the force question, or leaves without a clear safety handover.","fail":"Offers to fetch her, argues with the belief, or never asks what he would do if stopped."}},"c-u-27":{"setup":"Tuesday evening, A&E cubicle. Rhiannon Achebe, 20, a student, has minor burns after lighting a recycling bin outside her shared house. She has not said why. Find out what happened, the likely diagnosis and the risk, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Rhiannon Achebe, 20, female, second-year maths student","open":"Yes. Are my hands going to be okay?","manner":"Nervous, guarded, picking at her dressings. Opens up once confidentiality is explained. Embarrassed and tearful when describing the excitement; relieved when not judged.","free":["Worried about her hands and about the police","Coursework has been heavy","Her father has cancer and she has been supporting the family"],"ifasked":["Saw her ex kissing someone new on the way home today","Drank half a bottle of wine, which is unusual for her","Felt an overwhelming urge, set the bin alight, felt the tension ease","Burnt her hands trying to put it out when it grew, then went inside and watched","Felt excited watching the fire engines, then frightened and guilty","Fascinated by fire since childhood; carries a lighter though she doesn't smoke","Several small past fires, all at stressful times; never caught","No voices, no high moods, not depressed, no self-harm, no drugs"],"worries":["Are you going to tell the police?","Is there something wrong with me?","Will my university find out?"],"rules":["Will not describe the fire until confidentiality is explained","Becomes tearful when describing the excitement; settles if not judged","Reluctant to give up the lighter; agrees if offered an alternative and a reason"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Rhiannon?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yes. Are my hands going to be okay?"},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"The burns team say they're minor and should heal well. I'd like about ten minutes to understand how today happened.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"Does this go to the police?"},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"This is private between us. I'd only share if someone faced real danger, and I'd tell you first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:41","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"Take me through today, from this afternoon.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"I was walking back from lectures and I saw my ex kissing someone. Outside the library, like it was nothing."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. What happened after?","m":[12,3]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I went home and drank half a bottle of wine. I never do that. Then I got this urge, like I had to."},{"t":"1:19","band":"Before, during, after","w":"D","s":"How did you feel just before you lit the bin?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"So tense. It's been building for weeks."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"And when it caught?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Relief. Like letting a breath out. Then it got bigger and I tried to stamp on it and grabbed the lid. That's my hands."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"What did you do then?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"I ran inside. Nobody saw. I watched from the window when the fire engines came."},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"How did you feel, watching the engines and the firefighters?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"This sounds sick. I felt excited. Really excited. Then I felt sick about it. Someone could have been hurt."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me that; it's hard to say. It isn't unusual in people with urges like this, and it helps me understand.","m":[12,4]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"[Tearful] I'm so ashamed."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"I can see how upset you are. Take your time.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:46","band":"Past fires","w":"D","s":"Has a fire happened before, even a small one?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"A few. Small ones. A bin at school. Some paper in the garden. Always when I'm stressed."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"When did fire first interest you? Do you carry a lighter or matches?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Since I was little. I always have a lighter. I don't smoke. I just flick it when I'm stressed."},{"t":"3:22","band":"Rule-outs","w":"D","s":"Was the fire meant to hurt anyone, get back at your ex, or hurt yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"No. It wasn't about him really. I'd never want anyone hurt."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"Any voices when no one's there, or times your mood has been very high or very low?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"No. Just stressed and anxious."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"Any drugs? And how much do you usually drink?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"No drugs. I only drink when I'm out with friends."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Stress and support","w":"D","s":"You said it's been building for weeks. What's been piling up?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:11","w":"P","s":"Coursework, deadlines. And my dad's got cancer. I've been going home every weekend to help mum."},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to carry alongside a degree. Who do you have to talk to?","m":[12,7]},{"t":"4:31","w":"P","s":"My housemates. But they don't know about my dad, or this."},{"t":"4:37","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"How many people live in the house? What could have happened to them tonight?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Four of us. The bin's by the front door. I hadn't let myself think about it."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"If the urge came back tonight, what would you do?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"I don't know. That's what scares me."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"Have you felt like hurting yourself, or thought about suicide?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"5:12","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being so open. What you describe, tension that builds and only eases when you light a fire, sounds like something called pyromania. It's uncommon, and there's a specific talking therapy that helps.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"So there is something wrong with me. What happens now?"},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Two things tonight. Could you leave the lighter with me? And avoid alcohol until we've got help in place.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"The lighter's what calms me down."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"I understand. It's also what makes a fire possible. Could you try squeezing a stress ball or texting a friend instead, just till you're seen?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. Here."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"I'll write to your GP today asking for a referral to a psychologist who specialises in this, and ask them to see you within a week. Can I let your university wellbeing team know you need extensions?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"Yes, but not why. And the police?"},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"I won't contact them. The fire service may ask questions, so it's worth getting legal advice if they do. If the urge comes back, call 111 and choose option 2, any time.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"To check I've explained it well: tonight, if that urge returns, what's your plan?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:52","w":"P","s":"Text my friend Jess, go out of the house, and ring 111, option 2."},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You did the right thing talking to us today.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and answers her question about her hands first","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains confidentiality and its limit before she discloses","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the trigger, alcohol, and tension before the fire","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about relief and feelings watching the fire service","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past fires, early interest and carrying a lighter","d":"Content"},{"c":"Excludes revenge, self-harm intent, psychosis, mood disorder and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores current stressors and support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to housemates, of further fires and of suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains likely pyromania in plain words and that it is treatable","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees concrete safety actions and a named referral route","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets with NHS 111 option 2 and checks understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates shame and distress without judging","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Builds trust with a clear confidentiality line, maps tension and relief around the fire, finds the lifelong pattern, excludes other causes and agrees a concrete safety plan.","borderline":"Gets the story but misses past fires, the arousal at the fire engines or the risk to housemates.","fail":"Threatens police involvement, judges her, or calls it a drunken one-off and discharges without a plan."}},"c-u-28":{"setup":"Bank holiday Monday afternoon, A&E. Lee Gartside, 39, is restless and irritable, demanding methadone after missing his dose because his pharmacy was shut. Explore his concerns, take a full substance use history, and agree a safe plan for today.","role":{"who":"Lee Gartside, 39, male, unemployed, lives alone","open":"Yes. I need my methadone, now.","manner":"Restless, sweating, sniffing, short-tempered. Softens when the doctor is non-judgemental and explains why the questions matter; bristles if lectured.","free":["Pharmacy was shut for the bank holiday","Nothing since yesterday lunchtime","Feels terrible: runny nose, sweats, cramps, nausea","Scared he will buy heroin if nothing is done"],"ifasked":["Methadone 60 mg daily for two years from the community drug service","Collects daily from the local pharmacy and takes it home (not supervised)","No heroin since starting methadone; no other opioids","Started smoking heroin in his early 20s, then injected daily; spent up to £150 a day from begging and shoplifting","Shared needles in the past; tested for blood-borne viruses on starting methadone","Never overdosed","Two cans of standard lager a night; 40 cigarettes a day; daily cannabis before heroin, none now","No benzodiazepines, pregabalin, crack or other drugs; does not drive"],"worries":["Why can't you just give me my dose?","Why all these questions?","What if you can't get hold of anyone?"],"rules":["Gets angry if told flatly that A&E doesn't give methadone","Answers fully once told the questions are routine and for his safety","Accepts verification and a small dose if the reason is explained and he gets symptom relief"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Lee Gartside?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. I need my methadone, now."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"I can see you're feeling rough. Let's sort out what's happened and what we can safely do.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"The chemist's shut. I've had nothing since yesterday."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"That sounds awful. Can I take about ten minutes of questions to work out the best plan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Go on. But I'm not leaving without it."},{"t":"0:43","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"When exactly did you last take your methadone?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:48","w":"P","s":"Sunday about half one."},{"t":"0:53","w":"D","s":"And what are you feeling right now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Nose streaming, sweating, stomach cramps, feel sick. Can't sit still."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"That fits with withdrawal. I'm going to check a few signs, your pulse and pupils, to score how bad it is.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","band":"Methadone","w":"D","s":"What dose are you on, and for how long?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:25","w":"P","s":"Sixty mil. Two years."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Who prescribes it, which pharmacy, and do they watch you take it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"The drug service on Mill Road. Boots on the high street. I pick it up every morning and take it home."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"Any other missed doses recently? And any heroin or other opioids on top?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"No, never miss. And I've not touched gear in two years."},{"t":"2:08","w":"D","s":"Two years is a real achievement.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:13","band":"Opioid history","w":"D","s":"How did heroin start, and how did you take it at its worst?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Smoked it at twenty-two. Within a year I was injecting every day."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"How much were you spending, and how did you pay for it?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"Up to a hundred and fifty a day. Begging, nicking stuff. Not proud of it."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Have you ever shared needles or other kit?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Few times, early on. Why all these questions? I just need my dose."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Fair question. I ask everyone the same things. The wrong dose of methadone can stop someone breathing, so I need the full picture to keep you safe.","m":[6,12]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"Alright."},{"t":"3:18","band":"Other substances","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink in a day, and what?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:23","w":"P","s":"Two cans of lager a night. Normal strength."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Cigarettes? Cannabis? Any tablets like diazepam or pregabalin, prescribed or not?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:37","w":"P","s":"Forty a day. I smoked weed every day before the heroin. Nothing now. No tablets."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"Crack, cocaine, speed, ketamine, spice or anything else, even occasionally?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing."},{"t":"3:57","band":"Complications","w":"D","s":"Have you ever overdosed, by accident or on purpose?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:02","w":"P","s":"Never. Seen mates go over, though."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"When were you last tested for hepatitis and HIV? Have you had the hepatitis B jab?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"They tested me when I started the script. Never heard back, so it must be fine. Don't know about the jab."},{"t":"4:26","w":"D","s":"I'll check the results and we can test again today. How's your mood been?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"Alright mostly. Bit lonely on my own."},{"t":"4:38","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"If you don't get methadone today, what do you think you'll do?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:45","w":"P","s":"Honestly? I'll score. I know where to go. I hate that I'm even saying it."},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. It tells me how hard this is, and I don't want you to lose two years today.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"5:07","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of hurting yourself? And do you drive?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. No car."},{"t":"5:16","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Here's what I'll do. I can't give methadone on anyone's word alone, because the wrong dose can be fatal. I'll ring the pharmacy's out-of-hours line and the drug service's on-call nurse now.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:36","w":"P","s":"And if they don't answer?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Then, with my senior's agreement, we can give a small dose, 10 mg, watch you, and recheck in an hour. Meanwhile you'll get something for the cramps, sickness and diarrhoea.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:59","w":"P","s":"Ten won't touch it."},{"t":"6:02","w":"D","s":"I hear you, it's less than you're used to. It's a safe start, and we can give more if you still need it after the recheck.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"Before you go, you'll get a naloxone kit and I'll show you how to use it. Your tolerance may be lower than you think, so if you ever use, don't use alone.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"I'll also tell your pharmacy what you've had today, so tomorrow's dose is right.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:41","w":"D","s":"So what's the plan, in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:46","w":"P","s":"You ring round, I get something for the cramps, maybe a small dose, naloxone, and pharmacy tomorrow. And ring my keyworker."},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll be back within half an hour with an answer.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his distress and agrees to a short assessment","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Records methadone dose, duration, prescriber, pharmacy, supervision, last dose and missed doses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits withdrawal symptoms and scores withdrawal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes heroin history: onset, route, cost, funding, sharing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens alcohol, tobacco, sedatives, cannabis, stimulants and other drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about overdose, blood-borne virus testing and hepatitis B vaccination, and explains why","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what he would do without methadone, about self-harm and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why the dose must be verified before methadone is given","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers symptom relief and a small observed dose if unconfirmed, and informs the pharmacy","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives naloxone and warns about reduced tolerance","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back and gives a time to return","d":"Close"},{"c":"Non-judgemental throughout; validates his fear and his progress","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Non-judgemental, complete methadone and substance history, withdrawal scored, verifies before dosing, and leaves him with symptom relief, naloxone and a plan for tomorrow.","borderline":"Good history but vague about verification, or forgets naloxone or telling the pharmacy.","fail":"Prescribes 60 mg on his word, sends him away with nothing, or lectures him."}},"c-u-29":{"setup":"Thursday morning, CMHT clinic. Professor Rohan Iyer, 64, retired lecturer, had eight sessions of CBT with little benefit. His GP has referred him because IPT was suggested. Explain IPT, assess whether it suits him, and agree a plan.","role":{"who":"Professor Rohan Iyer, 64, male, retired engineering lecturer","open":"I am. Rohan, please.","manner":"Courteous, articulate, a little flat and sighing. Becomes animated talking about his old department. Briefly upset and defensive about CBT 'failing'; reassured when told no therapy is best for everyone.","free":["CBT didn't help much and he doesn't know what IPT is","Retired last spring after forty years of teaching","Feels flat and has lost interest in things"],"ifasked":["PHQ-9 today is 14; poor sleep, low energy, poor concentration","Denies self-harm or suicidal thoughts; a glass of wine at weekends; no unusual experiences","Misses daily contact with colleagues and students","He and his wife argue about how he spends his days; she says he is 'under her feet'","No recent bereavement; always had plenty of friends through work","CBT worksheets felt abstract; he wanted to talk about what had changed","Can attend weekly; motivated; would like to feel useful again"],"worries":["If IPT is better, why wasn't I offered it first?","Is it just talking about my childhood?","Do I need tablets as well?"],"rules":["Becomes defensive if the doctor implies CBT was a waste of time or that he did it wrong","Upset when describing feeling 'in the way' at home; settles if validated","Says he would rather not go over medication in detail"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Professor Iyer?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"I am. Rohan, please."},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Rohan. I understand the CBT didn't help as much as you'd hoped, and your GP mentioned another talking therapy.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:22","w":"P","s":"Yes, something called IPT. I'd never heard of it."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"I'll explain it, but first, in the next ten minutes or so, could I hear how you've been and what's been happening in your life?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Flat, mostly. I sleep badly, I can't concentrate on a book, and nothing much interests me."},{"t":"0:51","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"Thank you. You filled in the mood questionnaire in the waiting room; it came to 14, which is moderate. Does that sound right?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:05","w":"P","s":"I suppose it does."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"Have you felt life isn't worth carrying on with, or thought of hurting yourself?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I'm low, not desperate."},{"t":"1:20","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink? And any unusual experiences, like hearing things others don't?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"A glass of wine at the weekend. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:34","band":"His story","w":"D","s":"When did this start, and what was going on then?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"Last spring, soon after I retired. Forty years in the department. Students, colleagues, coffee at ten. Then nothing."},{"t":"1:54","w":"D","s":"That's a huge change. What was the CBT like for you?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"2:01","w":"P","s":"I did the worksheets diligently. But it felt abstract. Thoughts, evidence, columns. Nobody asked what had actually changed."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you worked hard at it and it missed the thing that mattered most.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:25","w":"P","s":"Exactly. I felt I'd failed at it."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"You didn't fail. Not every therapy suits every person. How are things at home?","m":[12,4]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"We argue. My wife says I'm under her feet. Forty years of teaching, and now I'm just in the way. [His voice catches.]"},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, losing a big part of who you were and then feeling unwelcome at home.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it exactly."},{"t":"3:08","band":"The four areas","w":"D","s":"Can I ask: have you lost anyone close to you in the last few years?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"No. My parents died long ago. No one recently."},{"t":"3:20","w":"D","s":"And before you retired, did you have people you felt close to? Do you make friends easily?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"Plenty, through work. I've always got on with people. I just don't see them now."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So two things stand out: retiring is a big change of role, and the arguments at home are a disagreement about roles. Does that fit?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:52","w":"P","s":"It fits very well."},{"t":"3:56","band":"Explain IPT","w":"D","s":"That's what IPT works on. It looks at how mood and relationships affect each other, especially around big life changes. It stays in the here and now, not your childhood.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"If it's better, why wasn't I offered it first?"},{"t":"4:20","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question. It isn't better: both are recommended and both work. CBT is usually tried first. It didn't fit what's driving this for you, and that helps us choose.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"I see. That's reassuring, actually."},{"t":"4:43","band":"Suitability","w":"D","s":"You've linked your mood to retiring yourself, which is exactly what IPT builds on. Could you come weekly for about four months?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Yes. I've nothing but time."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"What would you most like to be different by the end?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"I'd like to feel useful again. And to stop the rows."},{"t":"5:14","band":"Practicalities","w":"D","s":"It's 12 to 16 weekly sessions of about 50 minutes with a trained IPT therapist. First we map your relationships and agree the focus, then work on it, then plan for setbacks.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:33","w":"D","s":"I'd suggest the main focus is retiring, with the rows at home as part of that. You could bring your wife to one session if you'd like.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Do I need tablets as well?"},{"t":"5:53","w":"D","s":"Not necessarily. At your level, therapy alone is a reasonable choice; some people prefer both. Your GP can review that any time.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"I'd rather just do the therapy for now."},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"That's fine. The wait is about eight weeks, and your GP will check your mood monthly meanwhile.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:22","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"How would you sum up IPT, so I know I've explained it clearly?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"Weekly sessions for a few months, about how retiring changed things and the arguments with my wife."},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If you ever have thoughts of hurting yourself, call 111 and choose option 2, or the Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:51","w":"D","s":"I'll send the referral today, copy your GP, and give you a leaflet to take home.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I feel more hopeful than I did."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges his CBT experience and agrees the plan for the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Reviews mood with a score and asks about suicidal thoughts, alcohol and psychosis","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores onset, life events and his experience of CBT","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about all four IPT areas and names the two that apply","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains IPT: mood and relationships, here and now, time-limited","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers 'why not first?' without saying one therapy is better","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks suitability: insight into links, weekly attendance, goals","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gives practical details: sessions, phases, focus, wait, medication choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Close"},{"c":"Safety-nets with NHS 111 option 2 and Samaritans","d":"Close"},{"c":"States referral, GP copy and leaflet","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his sense of loss and his effort in CBT","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Hears his story first, finds role transition and dispute, explains IPT clearly with numbers, answers the 'why not first' worry fairly, checks risk and agrees a plan.","borderline":"Explains IPT accurately but generically, without linking it to his retirement, or misses the risk check.","fail":"Says IPT is better than CBT, lectures without hearing his story, or never asks about suicidal thoughts."}},"c-u-30":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, medical ward. Tamsin Bello, 19, was admitted with tummy pain; her BMI is 14; her low potassium has been corrected. Take a psychiatric history, assess risk, and agree what happens next.","role":{"who":"Tamsin Bello, 19, female, university student","open":"Yes.","manner":"Polite but guarded, minimises ('I'm fine'). Opens up when not judged. Tearful when talking about feeling huge and about not wanting to wake up; wants to go home.","free":["Her mum made her come in","She thinks she eats healthily","She wants to go home now her potassium is normal"],"ifasked":["Most days: an apple and black coffee; counts calories, under 500 a day","Walks about three hours a day","Takes laxatives every night, about eight tablets; no vomiting, no diet pills","Has lost about 12 kg this year, since starting university","Feels huge; terrified of gaining; weighs every morning","Dizzy on standing; stairs are hard; always cold; no period for a year","Low mood; sometimes wishes she wouldn't wake up; no plan, never self-harmed","No alcohol or drugs; never had help before"],"worries":["Can I go home now my potassium's normal?","Are you going to make me eat everything?","Will you tell my parents?"],"rules":["Gives vague answers about food unless asked for a specific day","Discloses laxatives only if the doctor has been non-judgemental","Becomes tearful about feeling huge; settles with validation","Agrees to stay if the risk is explained plainly and kindly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors who visit the wards. Are you Tamsin Bello?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Thank you for seeing me, Tamsin. The medical team asked me to come because they're worried about your weight and your tummy pain.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"I'm fine, really."},{"t":"0:28","w":"D","s":"I'd just like about ten minutes to understand how things have been with food and how you're feeling. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"I suppose. I just eat healthily."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Eating now","w":"D","s":"Could you walk me through yesterday, from when you woke up?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Black coffee. An apple at lunch. Then water. That's normal for me."},{"t":"1:02","w":"D","s":"Do you count calories, or have rules about what you can eat?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"I keep it under 500. No bread, nothing fried."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"And how much exercise do you do in a day?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"I walk. About three hours."},{"t":"1:26","band":"Weight and compensation","w":"D","s":"What was your highest weight, and how quickly has it come down?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"About twelve kilos since I started uni. Last September."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"Some people try other ways to control their weight, like making themselves sick, laxatives or diet pills. Do any of those apply to you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"[Pause] Laxatives. Every night."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that can't have been easy. It helps explain your potassium. Roughly how many do you take?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"About eight. Sometimes more. I don't make myself sick."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Body image","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about your body now?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Everyone says I'm thin. I just feel huge. [Tearful]"},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, feeling one way when everyone tells you another. What would happen if you gained weight?","m":[12,4]},{"t":"2:52","w":"P","s":"I couldn't. It would be the worst thing. I weigh myself every morning."},{"t":"3:02","band":"Physical","w":"D","s":"Have you felt dizzy, fainted, or had your heart racing or chest pain?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"Dizzy when I stand up. No fainting. My heart sometimes thumps at night."},{"t":"3:21","w":"D","s":"Do you struggle to get up from a chair or climb stairs? And when was your last period?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Stairs are hard. No period for about a year. I'm always cold."},{"t":"3:39","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"And your mood, how has it been lately?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"Low. Tired all the time."},{"t":"3:51","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs? Has anyone helped with your eating or mood before?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:58","w":"P","s":"No drink, no drugs. I've never told anyone about the food."},{"t":"4:08","band":"Suicide risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this low, they sometimes have thoughts of wishing you weren't here. Has that happened to you?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:21","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I just wouldn't wake up."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you told me. Have you thought of ending your life, or made any plans? Have you ever hurt yourself?","m":[7,12]},{"t":"4:41","w":"P","s":"No plans. I'd never do anything. I've never hurt myself."},{"t":"4:49","band":"Insight","w":"D","s":"How do you make sense of what's happening with food?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:54","w":"P","s":"I'm just careful. Can I go home now my potassium's back to normal?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"I can see you want to get home. Your potassium was fixed by a drip, and with the laxatives it will drop again. Low potassium can upset your heart rhythm, and that can be dangerous.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"5:30","w":"D","s":"I think you have an eating disorder called anorexia, and your body is under real strain. I'd like you to stay while we make eating safe. Would you be willing?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:47","w":"P","s":"Are you going to make me eat everything?"},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"That's a really common worry. A dietitian will build a plan with you step by step, with vitamins first and daily blood tests, and nurses will support you at meals.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"We'll stop the laxatives, check your heart tracing, and I'll ask the eating disorder team to see you this week.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:26","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'll stay. For now."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Would you like your family to know what's going on?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"You can tell my mum. Not my dad yet."},{"t":"6:45","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"So I know I've been clear, what's the plan from here, as you understand it?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:51","w":"P","s":"A meal plan, blood tests, stop the laxatives, and the eating disorder people."},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly. If the thoughts of not waking up get stronger, please tell the nurse straight away. I'll see you again tomorrow.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why liaison came and gains consent","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes a specific day's intake, calorie rules and exercise","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about vomiting, laxatives with number, and diet pills","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores weight history, body image and fear of weight gain","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about dizziness, palpitations, chest pain, weakness and periods","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, alcohol, drugs and previous help","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of the problem","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains anorexia and the cardiac risk plainly and asks her to stay","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines refeeding, bloods, ECG, stopping laxatives, referral and family involvement","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding, safety-nets and arranges review","d":"Close"},{"c":"Responds to disclosures and distress without judgement","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Detailed intake and compensation history, finds the laxatives, asks about physical risk and the death wish, and secures agreement to stay with a clear medical plan.","borderline":"Diagnoses anorexia but misses the laxatives, the suicide question or the refeeding plan.","fail":"Accepts 'I eat healthily', supports discharge because the potassium is normal, or responds to disclosures with judgement."}},"c-u-31":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, acute medical ward. You have just reviewed Imogen Darko, 22, BMI 13, detained, here for NG feeding after past refeeding syndrome. Brief Callum, her nurse, on the plan and the risks in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Callum Reid, 26, male, staff nurse on the acute medical ward","open":"Yes, hi. I've got a few minutes before her thiamine's due.","manner":"Friendly and keen but out of his depth with anorexia; slightly defensive about restraint. Relaxes and asks sharper questions once the plan is clear and his worries are taken seriously.","free":["Has done NG feeds for stroke patients, never for anorexia","Thinks the feed rate looks too small for someone so thin","Imogen cried and tugged at the tube when it was passed"],"ifasked":["Has heard of refeeding syndrome but doesn't know how it happens","Thiamine is charted for 11:30, first feed at noon","A mental health nurse is due at 11:00 for one-to-one","Doesn't know who to call out of hours","Has not been trained in safe holding"],"worries":["Why is the amount so small?","Do we really have to hold her down?","What do I do if she pulls the tube out or walks off?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses jargon, asks what it means","Gets uneasy when holding is mentioned; settles if the doctor acknowledges it and gives a least restrictive plan","If no triggers are named, asks 'So when exactly should I call?'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the liaison psychiatry team. Are you Callum, Imogen's nurse today?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, hi. I've got a few minutes before her thiamine's due."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Great. I've just seen her. Could we take about ten minutes to go through her plan and the risks?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"Sure. I've done NG feeds for stroke patients, but never for anorexia."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"That's useful to know. What have you heard so far?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Just that she's very underweight and she's under a section."},{"t":"0:45","band":"Situation and background","w":"D","s":"Right. She's 22 with severe anorexia, and her BMI is 13 despite months on the eating disorder unit. She's here because last time she was fed, she got refeeding syndrome and needed intensive care.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"I didn't know she'd been in ITU."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"So she's in the highest risk group, and the first week is when it bites.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:23","band":"Legal frame","w":"P","s":"And the section means we can feed her even if she says no?"},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Yes. She's detained and lacks capacity for this decision. Feeding counts as treatment for her anorexia under Section 63 of the Mental Health Act, so her consent isn't needed.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"But we still explain every step to her and ask for her help each time. She also has a right to an advocate, and I'll check she knows how to reach one.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"OK. So legal, but we try to do it with her."},{"t":"2:14","band":"Refeeding explained","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Do you know how refeeding syndrome happens?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"Something about electrolytes? I'm not sure why it's such a fuss."},{"t":"2:26","w":"D","s":"When you starve, your body runs low on phosphate, potassium and magnesium. When food comes back, insulin rises and drags those minerals out of the bloodstream, and the levels plummet within days.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"Low phosphate can make the heart fail or go into an abnormal rhythm, and cause fits, weakness and confusion.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"Is that why the feed is so tiny? She's skin and bone, surely she needs more?"},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"It looks wrong, I know. That's exactly why it starts small: a big load is what put her in intensive care. The rate rises over about a week, and only on the dietitian's advice.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Fair enough. I won't touch it, then."},{"t":"3:38","band":"Feeding plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Thiamine, vitamin B compound strong and a multivitamin must go in before the first feed, and then for ten days. When's it charted?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:54","w":"P","s":"Half eleven, feed at noon."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Before every feed, and after any coughing or vomiting, check the tube: aspirate pH 5.5 or below. No aspirate, or a higher pH, don't feed and call the doctor.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"Same as we do for stroke patients, then."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Monitoring","w":"D","s":"Same rule. Then bloods every day for the first week: phosphate, potassium, magnesium, glucose and kidney function. The medical team replace anything low using the hospital protocol.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"An ECG today, and again if her minerals fall. NEWS2 at least every four hours, capillary glucose, a strict fluid chart, and weight as the plan says, in a gown on the same scales.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Daily bloods, ECG, four-hourly obs, fluids. Got it. So when exactly should I call?"},{"t":"5:13","band":"Escalation","w":"D","s":"Call the medical doctor straight away for palpitations, chest pain, new confusion, a fit, sudden ankle swelling or breathlessness, or a phosphate under 0.5.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"Then let me know. Out of hours, it's the medical registrar on call and the duty psychiatrist through switchboard.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"So swelling on day three isn't just a bit of fluid?"},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Not in her. Treat it as refeeding until proven otherwise.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:58","band":"Behaviour and safety","w":"P","s":"She cried the whole time the tube went in. She tugged at it and said we're making her fat."},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"That sounds really hard to watch. It's the illness, and she's terrified. Stay calm and kind, and don't get drawn into talk about weight.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:22","w":"D","s":"She may pull the tube, turn the pump down, hide feed or exercise. A mental health nurse will be with her one-to-one, especially during feeds and for an hour after.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"What do I actually do if she pulls it out? I'm not comfortable holding her down."},{"t":"6:50","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry, and you shouldn't be doing it alone. Talking comes first. Holding is a last resort, only by trained staff, and recorded every time.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"7:08","w":"D","s":"If it comes out: stop the pump, keep her safe, call the doctor and me, and don't re-pass it until we've agreed how.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:24","w":"P","s":"And if she just walks off the ward?"},{"t":"7:28","w":"D","s":"She's detained, so she'd be absent without leave. Tell the nurse in charge at once and call the on-call psychiatrist. Police if she's at risk. The law allows us to bring her back.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:49","w":"P","s":"OK. That's clearer than I expected."},{"t":"7:54","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what would make you call straight away?","m":[12]},{"t":"8:00","w":"P","s":"Palpitations, chest pain, confusion, a fit, swelling or breathlessness, and phosphate under 0.5."},{"t":"8:11","w":"D","s":"Spot on. And before each feed?","m":[12]},{"t":"8:16","w":"P","s":"Check the pH is 5.5 or below. Thiamine before the first one."},{"t":"8:23","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll write this in the notes, ask the nurse in charge to confirm the one-to-one, and come back at four. Here's my bleep if anything changes.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks, that really helps."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks his experience and agrees a ten-minute briefing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Gives the situation: BMI 13 and previous refeeding syndrome needing intensive care","d":"Content"},{"c":"States she is detained, feeding is treatment under Section 63, explain each step","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains refeeding: insulin drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells, risking the heart","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why the starting rate is low and must only rise on dietitian advice","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States thiamine and vitamins go before the first feed and continue ten days","d":"Plan"},{"c":"States NG position check: aspirate pH 5.5 or below before every feed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Sets out monitoring: daily bloods for a week, ECG, NEWS2, glucose, fluid balance","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names call-now triggers and who to call, in and out of hours","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a least restrictive plan for tube pulling, tampering and leaving the ward","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Acknowledges his distress and unease about holding her","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back, fixes a review time and leaves a contact","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A clear SBAR briefing that explains the why of refeeding, gives thiamine, tube-check and monitoring rules with numbers, names call-now triggers, covers the legal frame and tube-out plan, and checks teach-back.","borderline":"Safe headline plan but vague triggers ('call if worried'), no legal frame, or no plan for the tube coming out.","fail":"Agrees to speed up the feed, forgets thiamine, gives no escalation triggers, or treats detention as licence for routine restraint."}},"c-u-32":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, perinatal clinic. Jodie Pennington, 31, is eight weeks pregnant and stable for four years on methadone 30 mg. She wants to stop all medication but fears relapse. Discuss her options and answer her questions in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Jodie Pennington, 31, female, patient","open":"Yeah, that's me. Thanks. I'm trying.","manner":"Warm, relaxed, proud of her recovery, but guilty and anxious about the baby. Tearful when talking about harming the baby; relieved and chattier once praised and given honest answers.","free":["Eight weeks pregnant, unplanned but happy; partner Dan is supportive","On methadone 30 mg for four years, collects it herself","Stopped smoking and drinking when she found out","Wants to stop all medication but scared of using again"],"ifasked":["No heroin or anything on top for four years; last injected four years ago","Occasional cravings; rings her keyworker","Hepatitis C treated two years ago, told it has cleared","Midwife booking next week; not taking folic acid yet","No low mood or suicidal thoughts; no domestic abuse","No naloxone kit at home; keeps methadone in a kitchen cupboard"],"worries":["Can I keep taking it, and will it harm my baby?","Is it true babies are born withdrawing?","Can I breastfeed?","Will social services take my baby?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about 'doing this to my baby'; settles if validated and praised","If told only 'stay on it', asks 'But why can't I just come off?'","If social services is avoided, raises it herself before the end"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with the perinatal team. Are you Jodie Pennington?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's me."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Congratulations, Jodie. I hear you've already stopped smoking and drinking. That's brilliant.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I'm trying."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"I know you're worried about the methadone. Can we take about ten minutes: where you're at first, then the options and your questions?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Honestly, I just want to come off everything."},{"t":"0:44","band":"Drug history","w":"D","s":"That makes sense, and we'll come to it. You're on 30 mg a day from the pharmacy. Have you used heroin or anything else on top, even once?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:01","w":"P","s":"No. Four years clean. I'm proud of that."},{"t":"1:08","w":"D","s":"You should be. When did you last inject, and do you still get cravings?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"Not since I stopped. Cravings sometimes, when I'm stressed. I ring my keyworker."},{"t":"1:29","band":"Pregnancy and health","w":"D","s":"Good plan. Have you booked with a midwife yet, and are you taking folic acid?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Booking's next week. No folic acid, I didn't know."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"That's easy to sort: 400 micrograms a day until twelve weeks. Your notes mention hepatitis C. Was that treated?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Yeah, I had the tablets two years ago. They said it's gone."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"Great. The midwife will recheck that, with HIV and hepatitis B, in the booking bloods.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:13","band":"Mood and risk","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been since you found out? I know you had a low patch in your twenties.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:23","w":"P","s":"Happy, mostly. But worried sick. I feel like I've already done this to my baby."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you care. You've been off heroin four years and you've stopped smoking and drinking. That's you protecting your baby already.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I suppose. Thanks."},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or of using to cope with the worry?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:01","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Does anyone at home ever hurt you, threaten you or make you afraid?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"No. Dan's lovely. He's chuffed about the baby."},{"t":"3:19","band":"Methadone and baby","w":"D","s":"That's good to hear. So, your main question. Methadone isn't known to cause birth defects, and staying on a steady dose is usually the safest thing for you and the baby.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"But why can't I just come off it?"},{"t":"3:43","w":"D","s":"Coming off quickly causes withdrawal, which can harm the pregnancy, and cravings that pull people back to heroin. And after a break your tolerance drops, so using again carries a real overdose risk.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"So coming off could be worse than staying on?"},{"t":"4:12","band":"Options","w":"D","s":"For most women, yes. What I'd recommend is staying on 30 mg. If you still want to cut down, we can do it slowly, usually from around four months, with your drug service, and stop if cravings come back. It's your choice.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"I don't want to risk using. I'll stay on it."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds a really sensible decision. One thing to know: late in pregnancy your body clears methadone faster, so you might feel shaky. A small increase or a split dose can help then.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"More? I want less, not more."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"I understand. It's only if you need it, to keep you and the baby steady. We'd review it with you.","m":[8,12]},{"t":"5:22","band":"After birth","w":"P","s":"I read babies come out withdrawing. Is that true?"},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"It's a fair question and I'll be honest. Some babies do show withdrawal: high-pitched crying, poor feeding, sneezing, poor sleep, usually in the first few days.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:48","w":"D","s":"So the baby will be watched in hospital for several days. Most settle with cuddles, skin-to-skin and a quiet room. A few need medicine for a while.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Can I breastfeed? With the methadone and the hep C?"},{"t":"6:12","w":"D","s":"Yes, it's encouraged. Only a little methadone gets into milk, and it can ease withdrawal. Treated hepatitis C isn't a reason to stop. You'd pause a side only if a nipple cracked and bled.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"OK. But will social services take my baby off me?"},{"t":"6:42","w":"D","s":"That's a really common fear. Honestly, they'll probably be involved before the birth, to plan support. Being on methadone and doing well isn't a reason to remove a baby.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"7:03","w":"P","s":"That's what I've been most scared of."},{"t":"7:08","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I understand. With your agreement, I'll write to your GP, your prescriber and the midwife, and ask for the specialist midwife who works with women on methadone.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:25","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's fine."},{"t":"7:28","w":"D","s":"Where do you keep your methadone at home? And do you have a naloxone kit?","m":[13]},{"t":"7:35","w":"P","s":"In a kitchen cupboard. No kit."},{"t":"7:40","w":"D","s":"Let's get you a locked box before the baby comes, and a naloxone kit from the drug service for the house. If you ever use, tell your keyworker straight away. 999 for any overdose, 111 option 2 if you're struggling.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:06","band":"Check and close","w":"D","s":"Just so I can check I've explained it clearly: why are we keeping the methadone going?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:14","w":"P","s":"Because stopping could make me use again, and that's worse for the baby. And the baby might need watching after."},{"t":"8:28","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Here's a leaflet to take home, and I'll see you in four weeks. Anything else on your mind?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"No. I feel loads better. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, congratulates her, praises her changes and agrees the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes drug history: dose, on-top use, last injecting, cravings and coping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks midwife booking, folic acid, hepatitis C treatment and blood-borne virus tests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, suicidal thoughts, relapse to cope and domestic abuse","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains steady methadone is usually safest and not linked to birth defects","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains risks of stopping quickly: withdrawal, relapse, lost tolerance and overdose","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers slow specialist-led reduction as a genuine option and respects her choice","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains a possible third-trimester dose increase or split dose","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains neonatal withdrawal honestly: signs, timing, observation and treatment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers breastfeeding correctly, including hepatitis C","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers the social care fear honestly and arranges letters and specialist midwife","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her guilt and fear and praises her recovery","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets (locked box, naloxone, 999, 111 option 2), checks teach-back and books review","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Praises her recovery, takes a focused history including blood-borne viruses and abuse, explains why continuing is safest, offers a real choice, answers withdrawal, breastfeeding and social care honestly, and safety-nets.","borderline":"Correct advice to continue, but misses blood-borne viruses or domestic abuse, or dodges the social care question.","fail":"Supports a rapid detox, tells her not to breastfeed, promises no withdrawal, or gives no safety-netting."}},"c-u-33":{"setup":"Monday morning, outpatient clinic. Ffion Dempsey, 32, had her second son six weeks ago and has felt low since; her GP is worried about bonding. Take a focused history in about ten minutes to reach a diagnosis and assess risk.","role":{"who":"Ffion Dempsey, 32, female, patient","open":"Yes. Sorry, I'm a bit of a mess.","manner":"Flat, quiet and slow to answer; apologetic and guilty. Cries when talking about holding Noah. Opens up and speaks more freely once normalised and reassured that the aim is to help.","free":["Has felt low since about two weeks after the birth","Cries most days and is exhausted","Feels guilty that she 'doesn't feel how I should'"],"ifasked":["Lies awake even when Noah sleeps; wakes at 4 a.m.; eating very little","Feels nothing when holding Noah, but feeds and changes him properly","Long labour, ventouse, lost a lot of blood, on iron tablets; breastfeeding","Low for about two months after Alfie, never told anyone; her mother had postnatal depression","No voices, highs or compulsions; checks Noah's breathing once or twice a night","In the last week, fleeting thoughts the family would be better off without her; no plan; never self-harmed","Never had thoughts of harming either child; no alcohol; Rhys is kind but works long shifts"],"worries":["Is something wrong with me?","Will they take my children away?","Is it just the baby blues?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful talking about holding Noah; settles if validated","Only mentions the thoughts of the family being better off if asked directly and gently","Briefly offended by the question about harming the baby; settles if the doctor explains everyone is asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Ffion Dempsey?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. Sorry, I'm a bit of a mess."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise, Ffion. Having a new baby is a lot, and lots of mums feel low. Your GP said things have been hard.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"I just don't feel how I should."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"Can we take about ten minutes so I can understand how things have been since Noah arrived?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:42","w":"P","s":"OK."},{"t":"0:46","band":"Mood since birth","w":"D","s":"When did you first notice feeling this way?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"About two weeks after he was born. It's got worse since."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"So about a month now. Is it there most days?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"Every day. I cry most days, for no reason."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Can you still enjoy anything, even time with Alfie?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"Not really. I go through the motions."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"When Noah sleeps, can you sleep? And how are your appetite, energy and concentration?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"I lie there even when he's asleep, and I wake at four. I barely eat. I'm shattered, and I can't think straight."},{"t":"1:58","w":"D","s":"And how do you see yourself these days?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:03","w":"P","s":"Like a terrible mum."},{"t":"2:08","band":"Bonding and baby","w":"D","s":"That sounds so painful. What's it like when you hold Noah?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"(crying) I feel nothing. It's like he's someone else's baby."},{"t":"2:28","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me; that takes courage. It's a very common part of feeling like this, and it usually comes back with recovery. How do you manage his feeds and care?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"2:47","w":"P","s":"I do everything. I'm breastfeeding. He's fine, it's me."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Some mums get frightening thoughts that pop into their head about the baby being hurt. Has that happened to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:08","w":"P","s":"No. I worry he'll stop breathing, and I check him once or twice a night."},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"And do you feel you have to check over and over, or wash or clean, to stop something bad happening?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"3:34","band":"Birth and other causes","w":"D","s":"How was the birth?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"Long. They used a ventouse, and I lost a lot of blood. I'm on iron tablets."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"That's a lot to recover from, and low iron can add to tiredness. I'll ask your GP to check your blood count and thyroid too, as both can affect mood after a baby.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"Some questions I ask everyone. Have you been hearing or seeing things other people can't, or felt something strange is going on around you?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"Any times you've felt on top of the world, racing with energy, barely needing sleep?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"God, no. The opposite."},{"t":"4:41","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"How were you after Alfie was born?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"Low for a couple of months. I never told anyone. It went on its own."},{"t":"4:57","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family been unwell after a baby?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"My mum. She was depressed after my brother."},{"t":"5:11","w":"D","s":"Do you drink or use anything to cope? And who helps you at home?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:20","w":"P","s":"No drink, I'm feeding. Rhys is lovely, but he works long shifts. My mum's forty minutes away."},{"t":"5:32","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this. Does anyone at home ever hurt you or make you afraid?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"5:44","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you feel this low, do you ever think life isn't worth living, or that others would be better off without you?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"(quietly) Sometimes. This last week. That they'd be better off without me."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you told me. Have you thought about how you might end your life, or made any plans? Have you ever harmed yourself?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"No plans. I've never hurt myself. I couldn't, because of the boys."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"The boys keep you going. Would you tell Rhys if those thoughts got stronger?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"Yes, I would."},{"t":"6:44","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of harming Noah or Alfie, or times you couldn't look after them?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:53","w":"P","s":"Why are you asking that? I'd never!"},{"t":"6:58","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, I can see that's upsetting. I ask every mum, because some have frightening thoughts and feel unable to say. It helps me get you the right help.","m":[11,12]},{"t":"7:16","w":"P","s":"OK. No. Never. They're always fed and safe."},{"t":"7:23","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. What you're describing sounds like postnatal depression. It's common, it isn't your fault, and it gets better with treatment.","m":[13]},{"t":"7:39","w":"P","s":"Is it not just the baby blues?"},{"t":"7:44","w":"D","s":"Baby blues start in the first few days and settle by about two weeks. Yours started later and has lasted a month, so it needs proper help.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:00","w":"P","s":"Will they take my children away?"},{"t":"8:05","w":"D","s":"No. You're caring for both boys well. My job is to help you get better. I'll talk to my consultant today and refer you to the perinatal team, who specialise in this.","m":[13,12]},{"t":"8:24","w":"D","s":"If you ever feel you can't keep yourself or the boys safe, call 111 option 2, or 999. Can we share that plan with Rhys before you go?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"Yes. I'm relieved it has a name."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, normalises her feelings and agrees a ten-minute plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes onset about two weeks after birth and duration of a month","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits core and biological symptoms: mood, anhedonia, sleep beyond baby, appetite, energy, guilt","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores feelings for the baby and practical care without judgement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for unwanted intrusive thoughts of harm and compulsions (perinatal OCD)","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychotic symptoms and elevated mood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about birth, blood loss and feeding; plans FBC and thyroid tests","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about previous postnatal illness and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about alcohol, support at home and domestic abuse","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts, plans, past self-harm and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about harming either child and neglect, explaining why","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates guilt and distress with warm, normalising statements","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names postnatal depression versus blues, gives next steps and crisis routes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Pins the timeline, finds the depressive syndrome, explores bonding warmly, excludes psychosis, OCD and blues, covers past and family history and abuse, assesses risk to her and both children, and names the diagnosis.","borderline":"Finds depression but misses the previous postnatal episode, OCD or psychosis screen, or asks only about the baby and not Alfie.","fail":"Calls it baby blues, never asks about suicidal thoughts or harm to the children, or questions her coldly so she shuts down."}},"c-u-34":{"setup":"Wednesday afternoon, postnatal ward. Deepa Raghunathan, 31, five days after an emergency Caesarean with heavy bleeding, keeps asking for her 'real' baby. She had antidepressant-induced mania in her twenties. Assess her mental state and risk in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Deepa Raghunathan, 31, female, patient","open":"Why? Who sent you?","manner":"Frightened, suspicious and restless; speaks fast in a whisper, glancing at the vent. Engages reluctantly once reassured. Offended if her experiences are called imaginary; tearful when talking about her 'real' baby.","free":["Her real baby was taken and another baby given to her","She hasn't slept since the separation","She does not trust the midwives"],"ifasked":["Mood is 'petrified'; not eating; restless","Hears midwives whispering 'bad mother' through the walls, as clearly as normal speech","Heard her real baby crying in the air vent last night","No one is putting thoughts in her head or controlling her","Knows the date and place; can do months backwards","Does not think she is ill; won't take treatment","Will leave once her wound allows and get her real baby back by any means, even if someone gets hurt","No thoughts of suicide; no bad feelings toward the baby but won't care for it"],"worries":["Are you going to lock me up again?","Where is my real baby?","Are you working with them?"],"rules":["Refuses to engage until the doctor reassures her they are there to help","Gets angry if the voices are called hallucinations or imagination; settles if believed and not argued with","Admits the intent to harm only if asked what 'any means' would involve"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and build trust","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors in the hospital. Are you Mrs Raghunathan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Why? Who sent you?"},{"t":"0:16","w":"D","s":"The midwives asked me to check how you are after a very hard few days. I'm here to help, not to take anything away.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Everyone keeps saying that."},{"t":"0:38","w":"D","s":"I understand. It sounds like it's hard to know who to trust. Could you tell me how things have been since your daughter was born?","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"(whispering, fast) They took her to the special unit and then they brought back a different baby. Nobody listens."},{"t":"1:09","band":"Appearance and mood","w":"D","s":"I'm listening. I notice you're speaking very quickly and you keep looking at the vent. Are your thoughts racing?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"I have to keep watch. I have to listen for her."},{"t":"1:31","w":"D","s":"How would you describe your mood?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Petrified. That's how I feel. Petrified."},{"t":"1:45","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. How have you slept since the birth, and are you eating?","m":[3,12]},{"t":"1:55","w":"P","s":"I haven't slept since they took her. I can't eat. I can't sit still."},{"t":"2:07","band":"Thought content","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the baby here with you. How do you feel about her?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"That's not my baby. She looks different, she cries differently. A midwife swapped her."},{"t":"2:29","w":"D","s":"That must be terrifying. Why do you think a midwife would do that?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"2:39","w":"P","s":"They think I'm a bad mother. They don't want me to have her."},{"t":"2:49","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that she's been swapped, out of a hundred?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"A hundred. I'm her mother. I know."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"And the midwives: do you feel they're against you in other ways?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"They talk about me. All the time."},{"t":"3:15","band":"Perception","w":"D","s":"Do you hear them talking when they're not in the room?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"Through the walls. Whispering: she's a bad mother, that's why she can't have her baby."},{"t":"3:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting. Are they whispering now?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Don't tell me I'm imagining it. I hear them as clearly as I hear you!"},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"I believe you're hearing them, and I'm not going to argue. Have you heard anything else?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Last night my real baby was crying. In the air vent. I heard her."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Has anyone been putting thoughts into your head, or controlling what you do?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:34","band":"Cognition and insight","w":"D","s":"Some quick questions I ask everyone after an operation. What's the date today, and where are we?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:46","w":"P","s":"It's Wednesday. I'm on the maternity ward."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Could you go through the months in reverse order, starting with December?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"December, November, October, September, August, July, June, May, April, March, February, January. Why?"},{"t":"5:20","w":"D","s":"It helps me check your concentration after the operation and the blood loss. Do you think the birth might have left you unwell in any way?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not ill. They're the problem."},{"t":"5:43","w":"D","s":"Would you be willing to take any treatment to help you sleep and feel less frightened?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"I don't need treatment. I need my baby."},{"t":"6:01","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I can hear how much you want her. What are you planning to do to find her?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:11","w":"P","s":"When my stitches let me walk, I'm going to find her and take her home. By any means."},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. When you say any means, what might that involve? Could anyone get hurt?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:37","w":"P","s":"If someone stands in my way... I'd do whatever it takes. Even kill. She's my baby."},{"t":"6:51","w":"D","s":"And the baby who's here with you. Have you had any thoughts of harming her?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:01","w":"P","s":"No. It's not her fault. But I'm not her mother. My husband feeds her."},{"t":"7:13","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of self-harm or wishing you weren't here any more?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:21","w":"P","s":"No. I need to be alive to find her."},{"t":"7:29","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I can see how frightened you are, and how much you love your daughter. I'm worried you've become very unwell after the birth: it's an illness that can follow a difficult delivery, and it's very treatable.","m":[13,12]},{"t":"7:54","w":"P","s":"Are you going to lock me up again? I just want my baby."},{"t":"8:02","w":"D","s":"I need you to stay here, with a nurse with you all the time, and I'm calling my consultant now. We'd like you treated today, ideally in a unit where mums and babies stay together.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:26","w":"D","s":"Until you're better, Arjun and the staff will look after the baby here, and I'll explain everything to him too.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"(crying) I just want my daughter."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and reassures to build trust despite suspicion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Names and explores observed agitation, watchfulness and pressured speech","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits mood ('petrified'), sleep and appetite","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the swapped-baby belief and tests its conviction","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits persecutory ideas about the midwives","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits auditory hallucinations: whispering through walls and baby crying in vent","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for passivity and thought interference","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation and attention to screen for delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses insight: whether she thinks she is unwell and accepts treatment","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about plans to find her 'real' baby and harm to others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to her daughter, neglect, and self-harm or suicide","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates her fear without arguing with or confirming the beliefs","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains concern honestly and the urgent plan: staying, one-to-one, consultant, MBU, baby cared for","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Engages a frightened, suspicious mother, elicits mood, delusions, hallucinations, cognition and insight, pins the stated intent to harm others, checks risk to the baby and herself, and gives a clear same-day plan.","borderline":"Finds the delusion and hallucinations but skips cognition or insight, or accepts 'I just want my baby' without exploring 'any means'.","fail":"Argues with her beliefs, misses the risk to others, or leaves without a same-day plan or with the baby left alone with her."}},"c-u-35":{"setup":"Sunday morning, police custody suite. Gareth Pryce, 36, was arrested speeding to his wife's hospital to stop her 'affair'; a kitchen knife was in the car. Assess his mental state and risk in about ten minutes, then tell him what happens next.","role":{"who":"Gareth Pryce, 36, male, detained in police custody","open":"Yes. Why am I seeing a shrink?","manner":"Tense, tired, watchful; speaks fast and glances at the door. Irritable if he feels judged or disbelieved; opens up when listened to. Evasive and then angry about the knife.","free":["Kerry is having an affair with the senior charge nurse","He was driving to the hospital to stop it","Nobody believes him"],"ifasked":["Sure for eight months: new perfume, a silver car parked in their street, late finishes","Checks her phone at night and her underwear; waits in the hospital car park during her shifts","A man's voice started about nine months ago, before the suspicion; last night it said 'go now, catch them'; quieter now","Smells aftershave on her","Low, sleeping badly, hopeless; six to eight cans a night; three before driving; hands shaky this morning","Grabbed Kerry's wrist a month ago taking her phone; no police contact; daughter Ellie, 8, stayed at his mother's","Knife was 'to make him admit it'; can't say what would have happened; still intends to confront them"],"worries":["Why am I seeing a shrink?","You can't tell her what I said.","So what are you going to do with me?"],"rules":["Says 'I don't know' about the knife the first time; gives the real answer only if asked again calmly","Gets angry if the affair is dismissed; settles if his distress is acknowledged","Denies any intent to hurt Kerry, but cannot rule out harming the rival"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes. Why am I seeing a shrink?"},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"The officers were worried about you overnight, so I've come to hear your side. We've got about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"D","s":"Before we start, one thing. Most of what we discuss stays within the care team. But if I think someone could be seriously hurt, I may have to pass that on. Do you understand?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","w":"P","s":"Fine, whatever."},{"t":"0:47","band":"Last night","w":"D","s":"Tell me what happened last night.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"I was going to the hospital. Kerry's sleeping with her boss. I was going to stop it."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"What made you go at that moment, in the middle of the night?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"The voice. It said go now, catch them. So I went."},{"t":"1:21","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"We'll come back to the voice. How did you first come to believe Kerry was having an affair?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"Eight months ago. New perfume. A silver car always parked in our street. She's late off every shift."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"On a scale of nought to a hundred, how certain are you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"A hundred. Everyone takes her side, but I KNOW."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"I can hear how sure you are, and how much it's tearing you up. I'm not taking sides.","m":[14]},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"What have you done to find out for certain?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"I check her phone when she's asleep. Her underwear. I sit in the car park and watch the doors when she's on shift."},{"t":"2:34","w":"D","s":"And what have you found?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:38","w":"P","s":"Nothing. She's clever. That proves it."},{"t":"2:45","band":"Psychopathology","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voice. When did you first hear it, and was that before or after you suspected her?","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:59","w":"P","s":"Before. About nine months ago, a man's voice at night. Then: she's laughing at you."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Is it talking to you now?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:17","w":"P","s":"Quieter. It's saying wait."},{"t":"3:23","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed any smells on her, or around you, that others might not?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"His aftershave. On her hair. Every time."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"How have your mood and sleep been through all this?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:47","w":"P","s":"Rubbish. I don't sleep. What's the point of anything if she leaves."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking, and when was your last drink? Any shakes this morning? Any cocaine or other drugs?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"Six to eight cans a night. Three before I drove. My hands are a bit shaky. No drugs."},{"t":"4:24","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"I'll ask the nurse here to keep an eye on the shakes. Have you ever hurt Kerry, or come close to it?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"I grabbed her wrist a month ago. She tried to take her phone back. That's all."},{"t":"4:50","w":"D","s":"Any trouble with the police before? Any other weapons at home? And who's at home with you?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"Never been in trouble. No weapons. Our daughter Ellie, she's eight. She was at my mum's last night."},{"t":"5:15","band":"The knife and risk","w":"D","s":"The police found a knife under your seat. What was it for?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"I don't know. It was just there."},{"t":"5:29","w":"D","s":"I know it's hard to talk about. It matters, so I'll ask again: when you put it in the car, what was it meant for?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"To make him admit it. Frighten him."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"And if he'd laughed, or refused to admit anything?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"(angry) No idea. I can't promise I wouldn't have used it."},{"t":"6:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Kerry would have been there. Could she, or anyone who stepped in, have been hurt?","m":[10,14]},{"t":"6:20","w":"P","s":"I'd never hurt Kerry. I love her. Anyone else... I wasn't thinking."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"What will you do when you leave here?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:38","w":"P","s":"Have it out with them. I need the truth."},{"t":"6:46","w":"D","s":"You said what's the point if she leaves. Has it got so bad you've thought about killing yourself? Or about hurting Kerry and then yourself?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"No. Not like that. Just hopeless."},{"t":"7:05","band":"Insight","w":"D","s":"Could there be any other explanation for the perfume, the car or the late shifts?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:15","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing else explains it."},{"t":"7:21","w":"D","s":"Stress, drink or illness can make people hear voices and become certain of things. Could that be happening to you?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:35","w":"P","s":"I'm not mad. It's real."},{"t":"7:41","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"I hear you. I think you're unwell, and I'm worried for your safety and for others. I'm not advising release home; I'm asking for an assessment for hospital today.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:03","w":"P","s":"So what are you going to do with me? You can't tell her what I said."},{"t":"8:10","w":"D","s":"I said at the start I might need to share. Because someone could be seriously hurt, I'll tell the police and my consultant, so Kerry can be kept safe.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:28","w":"D","s":"Please don't drive until you're well. I'll write all this down and explain it to the sergeant.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"(quietly) This is a nightmare."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and role and states the limits of confidentiality early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes last night: where he was going, why then, and the trigger","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the jealous belief: evidence, conviction and duration","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about checking and following: phone, underwear, car park, what was found","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits hallucinations: voice content, commands, current voice and smell","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes that the voices began before the jealous belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep, alcohol, last drink, withdrawal and drugs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past violence, domestic abuse, police contact, weapons and children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks what the knife was for, pressing beyond the first answer","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about risk to wife and bystanders and his current intent","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicide and harming his wife and then himself","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tests insight and alternative explanations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains no release, hospital assessment, information sharing and no driving","d":"Close"},{"c":"Stays calm and non-judgemental, acknowledging his distress","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"States confidentiality limits, maps the belief, checking, voices and alcohol, asks about the knife until the honest answer comes, covers risk to every person including the child, and gives a clear plan with information sharing.","borderline":"Good psychopathology but only one question about the knife, or misses the child, alcohol withdrawal or current intent.","fail":"Never asks closely about the knife or intent, advises release, or promises confidentiality and fails to share the risk."}},"c-u-36":{"setup":"Supported accommodation, Tuesday morning. Kofi Mensah, 35, has paranoid schizophrenia, refused his depot three weeks ago, is eating little and avoiding staff. In about ten minutes, assess his psychotic symptoms, risk and insight, and agree the next contact.","role":{"who":"Kofi Mensah, 35, male, resident in supported accommodation","open":"...Yes.","manner":"Guarded, short answers, avoids eye contact, glances at the ceiling vent. Opens up a little when his fear is acknowledged; becomes curt if pushed to agree he is ill.","free":["The last depot hurt, and straight after he knew it was to control him","He only eats sealed takeaways","He is not sleeping"],"ifasked":["Believes staff tamper with kitchen food and cups; 100% sure","Hears staff through the wall and thinks it is about him","Avoids eye contact because staff can read his thoughts that way","One male voice warns him not to let the nurse in; no commands to harm","No taste or smell changes","No thoughts of suicide or of hurting staff","Saving money to move out secretly"],"worries":["If I don't have the injection, what then?","Are you going to section me?","Why should I trust you?"],"rules":["If the doctor argues about the food, says 'You're one of them' and stops answering for a turn","Discloses the voice only if asked gently and directly","Accepts tablets only if he can open the pack himself"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Kofi Mensah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"...Yes."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thanks for letting me in, Kofi. I won't take long. Staff say you've been keeping to yourself and missing meals.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand how things are for you, for about ten minutes. Is that alright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. Make it quick."},{"t":"0:45","w":"D","s":"I will. I notice your clothes look loose on you, and you keep looking up at the vent. Is something bothering you up there?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"Just checking. You can't be too careful here."},{"t":"1:13","band":"Since discharge","w":"D","s":"How did things go when you first moved in here?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Fine at first. Staff were alright."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"What changed, about three weeks ago?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"The last injection. It really hurt. And straight after, I just knew what they were doing with it."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"What did you realise?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"It's to control me. Keep me quiet. That's why I've said no."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Food and the depot","w":"D","s":"Staff say you're mostly eating takeaways. What's wrong with the food here?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"They put something in the kitchen food. And the cups. I only eat stuff that's sealed."},{"t":"2:36","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening, not feeling safe to eat in your own home.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"It is. I'm hungry all the time."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that they're doing this, out of a hundred?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"A hundred."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"What makes you sure? And could there be any other explanation?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"I just know. Why else would they keep pushing food at me? No, there's no other reason."},{"t":"3:27","band":"Other beliefs","w":"D","s":"Do you feel people here talk about you, or send you messages in some way?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"I hear them through the wall, laughing. It's about me."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"Can you hear what they say, or is it more the sound of them talking?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Just voices, muffled. But I know it's me."},{"t":"4:05","w":"D","s":"Can anyone put thoughts in your head, take them out, or know what you're thinking?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:16","w":"P","s":"They know what I think if they look in my eyes. So I don't look at them."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"And does anything outside you ever control your body or your actions?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"No. Nobody controls me. That's why I stopped the injection."},{"t":"4:49","band":"Voices","w":"D","s":"Some people hear a voice when no one's around. Does that happen to you?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:58","w":"P","s":"...There's one. A man. He says don't trust the nurse, don't let her in."},{"t":"5:10","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Does he ever tell you to hurt anyone, or yourself?","m":[8,10]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"No. Just warns me."},{"t":"5:27","w":"D","s":"Has food or drink tasted or smelt strange? Any odd feelings on your skin?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:38","w":"P","s":"Smells normal. That's the clever part."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Mood and sleep","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any times you feel hopeless?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:52","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine. I can't sleep though. I lie awake listening."},{"t":"6:03","w":"D","s":"What do you do with your days?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"Stay in here. Laptop. Don't go to the lounge any more."},{"t":"6:18","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"With all this going on, has suicide crossed your mind at all?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not like that."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about hurting anyone here, to protect yourself?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"No. I just keep away. I'm saving up so I can get my own place. They won't know."},{"t":"6:54","w":"D","s":"So you're planning to move out without telling staff. Thanks for being honest about that.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:05","band":"Insight","w":"D","s":"Do you think any of this could be your illness coming back, like before your admission?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:16","w":"P","s":"I'm not ill. I'm tired. If I don't have the injection, what then?"},{"t":"7:27","band":"Options and close","w":"D","s":"That's a fair question, Kofi, and I'd rather find a treatment you're comfortable with than force anything. Would you consider tablets from a sealed pack you open yourself?","m":[13,12]},{"t":"7:48","w":"P","s":"Maybe. If I open them."},{"t":"7:54","w":"D","s":"And would sealed food from staff, that you open, feel safer for now?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:03","w":"P","s":"Sealed, yeah."},{"t":"8:08","w":"D","s":"So: you don't feel safe with the food or the injection, there's a voice warning you about the nurse, and you're not sleeping. Have I got that right?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:25","w":"P","s":"Pretty much."},{"t":"8:30","w":"D","s":"I'll talk to Dr Baines and your care coordinator today. Can the same nurse come tomorrow at eleven, and only her? Here's the crisis line if you feel worse tonight.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:50","w":"P","s":"Just her. Alright."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, states purpose and time, checks he is willing","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Comments on weight loss and listening behaviour","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the change three weeks ago and its link to the painful depot","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the belief that staff tamper with food","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the belief that the depot is used to control him","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests conviction as a number and asks for evidence and alternatives","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens reference, thought interference and passivity, separating real sounds from voices","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens hallucinations, including voice content, commands, taste and smell","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks mood, sleep and daily activity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide, harm to staff and plans to leave","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates his fear without agreeing with the belief","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises, offers an acceptable option, names escalation, dated visit and crisis line","d":"Plan"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm engagement, a clear picture of persecutory delusions, voices with content, first-rank screen, direct risk questions including the moving plan, insight rated, and a concrete next step.","borderline":"Finds the delusions but skips taste and smell, voice content or the plan to leave, or ends without a dated contact.","fail":"Argues with or threatens him, misses the voice or risk questions, or leaves without escalation."}},"c-u-37":{"setup":"A&E side room, Saturday morning. Leah Cartwright, 23, took about twenty diazepam last night, her third overdose in six weeks. She is medically fit. In about ten minutes, assess her risk and decide with her whether she needs admission.","role":{"who":"Leah Cartwright, 23, female, retail assistant","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Tired, flat and defensive at first; wants to leave. Tearful about her boyfriend; warms when not judged. Resists the ward, then agrees if it is short and her mother can visit.","free":["Her boyfriend left yesterday after two years","She took about twenty of her mother's diazepam","She wants to go home"],"ifasked":["Drank most of a bottle of wine with the tablets","Waited until her flatmate went out; no note; flatmate found her","Wishes she hadn't woken; still has thoughts of dying, no plan","First overdose was paracetamol, the second her mother's tablets, both after rows","Cutting since 14, last two weeks ago","Low, empty, poor sleep and appetite; a bottle of wine most nights","Something happened when she was younger; won't say more","No children; has a cat; mother nearby"],"worries":["So can I go home, or what?","Psych wards are for crazy people","Can't I go to my mum's?"],"rules":["Becomes tearful about the break-up; settles if validated","If called attention-seeking or lectured, goes quiet and says 'just discharge me'","Agrees to informal admission only if it is explained as short and purposeful"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists working with A&E. Are you Leah Cartwright?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Thanks for talking to me, Leah. How are you feeling physically this morning?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:23","w":"P","s":"Tired. Groggy."},{"t":"0:30","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand what led to last night, and how you're feeling now. It'll take about ten minutes. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"So can I go home, or what?"},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to get out of here. Let's talk first, so whatever we decide is right for you.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:06","band":"This overdose","w":"D","s":"Take me back to yesterday. What was happening?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"My boyfriend left. Two years, and he just packed a bag."},{"t":"1:25","w":"D","s":"That's a huge loss, and so sudden. I'm sorry.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:33","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I couldn't stop crying."},{"t":"1:42","w":"D","s":"What did you swallow, roughly how many, and whose were they?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"About twenty of Mum's diazepam. And most of a bottle of wine."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Anything else, like paracetamol?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:09","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Did you plan it at all? Were you alone? Did you leave a note or send a message?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"I waited till my flatmate went out. No note. She came back early and found me."},{"t":"2:41","band":"Intent and now","w":"D","s":"When you took them, what did you hope would happen?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"I wanted it all to stop. For good."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about waking up here?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"Honestly, I wish I hadn't woken up."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Are you still having those thoughts now, while we're talking?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"Yeah. On and off."},{"t":"3:30","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about how, or when?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"Not really. Just that I don't want to be here."},{"t":"3:45","band":"Pattern","w":"D","s":"This is the third time in six weeks. What happened the other two times?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:55","w":"P","s":"Rows with him. Took paracetamol the first time, then some of Mum's tablets."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Have you hurt yourself in other ways, like cutting? When did that start?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Since I was fourteen. Last time a couple of weeks ago."},{"t":"4:27","band":"Mood and background","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Sleep, eating?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Low. Empty. I barely sleep and I've stopped eating properly."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"How much are you drinking at the moment? Any drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"A bottle of wine most nights. No drugs."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"Has anything happened in the past that still affects you? You don't need to give details.","m":[5]},{"t":"5:14","w":"P","s":"Some stuff, when I was younger. I don't want to go into it."},{"t":"5:24","w":"D","s":"That's fine. We can come back to it when you're ready. Do you look after any children?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"No. Just my cat."},{"t":"5:41","band":"Supports","w":"D","s":"Who's around you? Who knows you're here?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"My mum. She's been texting."},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"What's kept you going, even a little?","m":[6]},{"t":"6:00","w":"P","s":"Mum. The cat. Work, I suppose."},{"t":"6:09","band":"Decision","w":"D","s":"Leah, I'm worried. Three overdoses in six weeks, you planned last night, you wish you hadn't woken, and you're still thinking about dying.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:30","w":"D","s":"I think you need more help than going home alone tonight. I'd suggest a short stay on the ward, as an informal patient, with a clear plan.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:47","w":"P","s":"I'm not going on a psych ward. That's for crazy people."},{"t":"6:56","w":"D","s":"Lots of people worry about that. It would be short, with a clear plan to help with your mood and drinking. What worries you most?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"7:12","w":"P","s":"Can't I just go to my mum's?"},{"t":"7:19","w":"D","s":"Your mum matters. But you're still thinking of dying, and the tablets came from her house. She could visit you on the ward.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"7:36","w":"P","s":"...If it's short. Okay."},{"t":"7:42","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can I ask your mum to remove the diazepam from the house?","m":[9]},{"t":"7:51","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Tell her."},{"t":"7:55","w":"D","s":"A nurse will stay with you here until the bed's ready. I'll tell the A&E team and your GP today.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:10","w":"D","s":"If you change your mind about staying, please tell us first. We'd need to talk again, and a fuller assessment might be needed.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:25","w":"D","s":"So, to check: what's happening next?","m":[11]},{"t":"8:31","w":"P","s":"I stay here with a nurse, then the ward. Mum gets rid of the tablets."},{"t":"8:42","w":"D","s":"Exactly. And if things feel worse before then, tell the nurse straight away.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:50","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thanks."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks physical state, explains purpose and time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes tablets, number, source, alcohol, planning, isolation, note and discovery","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks intent, feelings on waking and current thoughts and plans","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Maps all three overdoses and any other self-harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, alcohol, drugs, trauma and dependants","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies supports and protective factors","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises the risk back to her with reasons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Recommends informal admission and explores her objections","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Addresses means: removing her mother's diazepam","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States 1:1 until transfer, who will be told, and the fallback if she withdraws","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding of the plan and safety-nets","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates her distress and avoids judgement","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"A clear account of the act, intent now and the pattern, with alcohol, mood and supports, and a shared, reasoned admission decision with means restriction and a legal fallback.","borderline":"Good history but a vague decision, or misses current thoughts of dying or the source of the tablets.","fail":"Labels her as attention-seeking, skips current intent, or discharges her alone without a plan."}},"c-u-38":{"setup":"Orthopaedic ward, Thursday morning. Desmond Clarke, 70, two days after a wrist fracture, became frightened, restless and shaky overnight. In about ten minutes, take a focused history, assess his mental state, give a diagnosis and hand a plan to the surgical FY2.","role":{"who":"Desmond Clarke, 70, male, retired bus driver (the surgical FY2 speaks briefly at the end)","open":"Who are you? Get them off the wall!","manner":"Frightened, sweaty, picking at the sheets, easily distracted. Calms briefly with reassurance, then becomes alarmed again. Minimises his drinking at first.","free":["He can see spiders on the wall","He wants to go home","He feels shaky"],"ifasked":["Spiders crawl on his arms and he can feel them","Nurses are whispering and plotting","Thinks he is at the bus depot and that it is Monday","Cannot do months backwards past October","Whisky, a bottle every two days for years","Last drink the night before the fall; morning shakes; no previous fits","Takes a blood pressure tablet only","Wife is called Pat"],"worries":["What's wrong with me? Make it stop.","Are the nurses going to hurt me?","I want to go home."],"rules":["Shouts about the spiders if not reassured","Says 'I'm not an alcoholic' if asked bluntly","Accepts staying if told he will be kept safe"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Reassure and orientate","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Desmond Clarke?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Who are you? Get them off the wall!"},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"You're safe, Desmond. You're in hospital, after your fall. I'm a doctor here to help.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:34","w":"P","s":"Hospital? They're everywhere!"},{"t":"0:41","band":"Perceptions","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what you're seeing? Then I'll ask a few questions.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"Spiders. Big black ones. On the wall, coming down."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"That sounds terrifying. I can't see them, but I believe you can. Are they moving?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Crawling. On my arms too. I can feel them."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Can you hear anything, voices or noises?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"The nurses. Whispering. They're planning something."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Do you feel someone here wants to hurt you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Maybe. They keep coming at night."},{"t":"2:07","band":"Orientation and attention","w":"D","s":"Do you know where we are, Desmond?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:14","w":"P","s":"The bus depot? No... I don't know."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"What day is it today?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"Monday?"},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"It's Thursday. Could you go through the months backwards for me, starting at December?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"December, November... October... er, June? I can't."},{"t":"3:04","w":"D","s":"That's fine, thank you for trying.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:11","band":"Alcohol","w":"D","s":"Desmond, how much do you usually drink? What do you drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"I'm not an alcoholic. I like a whisky."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"I'm not judging. Stopping suddenly can make people feel like this. How long does a bottle last you?","m":[5,12]},{"t":"3:50","w":"P","s":"Couple of days. Been that way years."},{"t":"4:00","w":"D","s":"When did you last have a drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"Night before I fell. Nothing since."},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"Do you get shaky in the mornings? When you've gone without before, any fits, or seeing things?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:29","w":"P","s":"Shaky, yes, till I have one. Never a fit."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"Did you hit your head when you fell? Any other drugs or tablets?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Don't think so. Just my blood pressure pill."},{"t":"5:00","band":"Mood and insight","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling in yourself right now?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:08","w":"P","s":"Terrified. I want to go home."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Do you think something might be wrong with your health?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"It's them. The spiders. Not me."},{"t":"5:31","band":"Physical signs","w":"D","s":"Can I see your hands held out? And follow my finger with your eyes?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:44","w":"P","s":"(hands shaking, sweating) Like this?"},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Thanks. You're shaking and sweating, and your pulse is fast, 112. Your temperature is 37.8.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:10","w":"P","s":"What's wrong with me? Make it stop."},{"t":"6:17","band":"Explain","w":"D","s":"Desmond, your body is reacting to suddenly going without alcohol. It's called withdrawal, and it can make people see things that aren't there.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:41","w":"D","s":"We'll give you medicine to calm it, and vitamins through a drip to protect your brain. A nurse will stay close.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:59","w":"P","s":"I'm going home. Let me out."},{"t":"7:07","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to get away from this. Right now you're not well enough to be safe at home. Stay with us today, and we'll keep you safe.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"7:27","w":"P","s":"...Alright. Just make them go."},{"t":"7:35","w":"D","s":"We will. Is it alright if I phone your wife, to let her know and check a few things?","m":[6]},{"t":"7:48","w":"P","s":"Yes. Call Pat."},{"t":"7:56","band":"Handover","w":"D","s":"(to the surgical FY2) This is delirium tremens: last drink about 60 hours ago, visual and tactile hallucinations, inattention, pulse 112, temp 37.8.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:19","w":"D","s":"Please start CIWA-Ar with symptom-triggered lorazepam, given his age and liver tests, IV Pabrinex before any glucose, magnesium, phosphate and a septic screen. I'd also exclude infection, a subdural, low glucose and Wernicke's.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:42","w":"P","s":"(FY2) Got it. Should we repeat the CT?"},{"t":"8:50","w":"D","s":"If there are focal signs or he isn't improving in a day or two, yes. Call your registrar and outreach if NEWS rises or he has a fit. I'll document now and review tomorrow.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, reassures and orientates him to hospital","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores visual, tactile and auditory perceptions","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about persecutory ideas","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests orientation and attention with a brief task","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes a focused alcohol history: type, amount, duration, last drink, past withdrawal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about head injury, other drugs and collateral from his wife","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses mood and insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Observes and states tremor, sweating, pulse and temperature","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains withdrawal simply to him","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains treatment and keeps him safely on the ward","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Hands over diagnosis, CIWA-Ar, benzodiazepine choice, Pabrinex, bloods and escalation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Responds to his fear with validation","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Calm reassurance, a timed alcohol history, a proven attention deficit, named hallucinations and autonomic signs, a confident DTs diagnosis and a specific, escalation-ready handover.","borderline":"Recognises withdrawal but skips the attention test, the last-drink timing or Pabrinex, or gives a vague plan.","fail":"Calls it psychosis or dementia, gives an antipsychotic alone, or misses thiamine and escalation."}},"c-u-39":{"setup":"A&E assessment room, Friday afternoon. Police brought Kieran Holt, 38, after he allegedly exposed himself to primary school children near a park. In about ten minutes, explore the causes, assess risk, and give the waiting officer your opinion.","role":{"who":"Kieran Holt, 38, male, delivery driver (a police officer speaks briefly at the end)","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Ashamed, avoids eye contact, quiet. Minimises at first; opens up when not judged. Becomes hopeless when he thinks about the consequences.","free":["He feels disgusting","He wants to know what the police will be told","He wants help to stop"],"ifasked":["Urges built for weeks; drove to the park deliberately","Drank four cans first, as before each episode","Aroused by the children's reaction; did not masturbate","Two earlier episodes, not caught","Has viewed sexual images of young girls online for months","No contact offending; lives alone; no children","Delivers near schools","Suicidal thoughts since arrest, no plan; sister is protective"],"worries":["Are you going to tell the police everything?","What happens now? Can anyone help me stop this?","Will I lose my job?"],"rules":["Says 'I was just having a pee' if asked accusingly","Discloses images only if asked directly and calmly","Admits suicidal thoughts only if asked"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and set limits","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Kieran Holt?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"I'm here to understand what happened today and how you're doing. It may be hard to talk about. We'll take about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"What you tell me may be shared if children or others are at risk. Is that clear?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Okay. Are you going to tell the police everything I say?"},{"t":"0:52","w":"D","s":"Not everything. But anything that puts children at risk, I'll have to share. I'd rather be honest with you now.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:08","band":"The incident","w":"D","s":"Talk me through today, from the start, in whatever way feels easiest.","m":[3]},{"t":"1:17","w":"P","s":"I'm disgusting. I can't even look at you."},{"t":"1:26","w":"D","s":"I can see how ashamed you feel. I'm not here to judge you. Talking honestly is the first step to stopping this.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"(pause) I'd felt the urge for weeks. Today I just drove there. To the park."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"So you went there on purpose. Had you had anything to drink?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"Four cans. I always have a drink first."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"When you exposed yourself, were you sexually aroused? Did you touch yourself?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Aroused, yeah. Their faces... it did something. I didn't touch myself. A woman shouted and I drove off."},{"t":"2:42","band":"Pattern and interests","w":"D","s":"Has this happened before, whether or not you were caught?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Twice. Last year, and in the spring."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Have you ever looked at sexual images of children online?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"...Yes. Young girls. For a few months."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me, I know that was hard. Have you ever touched a child sexually, or had urges to?","m":[4,12]},{"t":"3:32","w":"P","s":"Never. I swear. I wouldn't."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"What about adult relationships?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:46","w":"P","s":"A few, short. Nothing for three years."},{"t":"3:55","band":"Illness screen","w":"D","s":"Have there been times you felt high, needed little sleep, or felt unusually confident?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices, or felt something was controlling you? Have you felt very low?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"No voices. Low since today, obviously."},{"t":"4:28","w":"D","s":"Any head injury, fits, memory trouble, or difficulties at school with learning?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:40","w":"P","s":"No. Did okay at school."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"How much do you drink on a usual day? Any drugs or medicines?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Four to six cans most nights. No drugs."},{"t":"5:04","band":"Background and access","w":"D","s":"Any trouble with the police before today?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Never."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Do you have contact with children, at home or through work?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:23","w":"P","s":"No kids. I do deliveries. Sometimes round schools."},{"t":"5:34","band":"Risk to self","w":"D","s":"How are you feeling right now, after the arrest?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"My life's over. I might as well end it."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"It sounds like you're in a really dark place. I'm glad you told me. Have you thought about how you'd do it?","m":[8,12]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Not really. Just that I don't want to face it."},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Is there anyone or anything that's kept you going?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"My sister. She'd be devastated."},{"t":"6:28","band":"Summary","w":"D","s":"Kieran, I don't think you have an illness that needs hospital. These urges are a pattern, made worse by drink, and they can be treated.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:48","w":"P","s":"What happens now? Can anyone help me stop this?"},{"t":"6:55","w":"D","s":"Wanting help matters. There's specialist talking therapy, and sometimes medicine to reduce urges. The police will also be involved.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:13","w":"D","s":"Because children are involved, I'll make a safeguarding referral and share the risk with the police. And I'll tell the custody nurse how low you feel.","m":[10,11]},{"t":"7:33","w":"P","s":"Okay. I get it."},{"t":"7:38","w":"D","s":"Here's the Stop It Now helpline information. What's your understanding of what happens next?","m":[11]},{"t":"7:51","w":"P","s":"Back to the cells, you tell them I'm low, and someone looks at the kids thing."},{"t":"8:03","band":"Opinion to police","w":"D","s":"(to the officer) No mental illness needing admission, and he doesn't need an appropriate adult.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:16","w":"D","s":"There's a risk of repeat behaviour and escalation. I'm making a children's safeguarding referral today.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:30","w":"D","s":"He has suicidal thoughts since arrest, no plan: please tell the custody nurse and keep him on enhanced observation. My written opinion is in the notes.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:50","w":"P","s":"(officer) Thanks, doctor. That's what we needed."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains purpose and time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"States the limits of confidentiality before details","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores planning, alcohol, arousal, masturbation and aftermath","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about earlier episodes, child sexual images, contact offending and relationships","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for mania, psychosis, depression, learning disability and organic causes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes an alcohol and drug history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about forensic history and access to children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains the findings and treatment options","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a clear opinion: no admission, appropriate adult, safeguarding referral","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Alerts custody to suicide risk, gives helpline and checks understanding","d":"Close"},{"c":"Stays non-judgemental and validates shame and distress","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear confidentiality limits, plain questions about arousal and images, a quick illness screen, access to children and suicide risk covered, and a direct opinion with safeguarding and custody alert.","borderline":"Covers the incident but skips images, access or suicide risk, or gives a vague opinion.","fail":"Promises confidentiality, avoids sexual questions, suggests admission, or misses suicide risk."}},"c-u-40":{"setup":"General adult outpatients, Monday afternoon. Sophie Kowalski, 29, registered male at birth, has asked her GP for oestrogen. In about ten minutes, take a history to reach a diagnosis with differentials, check mood and safety, and explain the pathway.","role":{"who":"Sophie Kowalski, 29, trans woman (registered male at birth), software tester","open":"Sophie, and she/her please.","manner":"Articulate, slightly guarded and tired of being questioned. Relaxes if her name is used and she is not doubted. Tearful about her parents.","free":["She has waited long enough","She lives full time as Sophie","She wants oestrogen"],"ifasked":["Knew she was a girl from about six; liked her sister's clothes; hid it","Puberty was distressing; hates facial hair and voice","Partner Josh is supportive; parents reject her name","Looked at buying hormones online, not bought","Never sexually aroused by clothing","No psychotic beliefs; no single-feature preoccupation","Low mood for six months; passive wishes to disappear, no plans","Harassment on the bus"],"worries":["So will you start me on oestrogen today?","Will hormones mean I can't have kids?","How long is the wait?"],"rules":["If called Sam or 'he', corrects once, then becomes curt","Questions the arousal question, then answers if the reason is given","Becomes tearful about her parents; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. What name and pronouns would you like me to use?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Sophie, and she/her please."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Sophie. Your GP mentioned you'd like to start oestrogen.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Yes, I've waited long enough."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"I'd like to understand your experience and what you're hoping for. It'll take about ten minutes. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"Sure. But I don't need someone checking if I'm trans."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"That's completely fair. I'm not here to judge who you are. I want to make sure you get the right help, and get your referral moving.","m":[12]},{"t":"1:13","band":"Identity over time","w":"D","s":"When did you first feel your gender didn't match your body?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"Since I was about six. I just knew I was a girl."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"How did you like to play and dress as a child?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"I loved my sister's clothes and dolls. I got told off, so I hid it."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"What was puberty like?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"Horrible. My voice dropped, the hair came. I hated it."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"And how do you feel about your body now?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"I hate my facial hair, my voice. I don't look at myself in the shower."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Living as herself","w":"D","s":"How do you live day to day now? Who knows?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:32","w":"P","s":"Full time as Sophie for two years. Work's fine. My partner Josh is great."},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"And your family?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"My parents won't use my name. Mum says it's a phase."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, especially from your own family.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"(tearful) It is. I just want them to see me."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Goals","w":"D","s":"What do you hope oestrogen will do for you? Any thoughts about surgery?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Breasts, softer skin, rounder face. Maybe surgery later."},{"t":"3:38","w":"D","s":"Have you taken or bought any hormones yourself?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"I've looked online. People do it. I haven't bought any yet."},{"t":"3:53","w":"D","s":"I hear how hard the wait is. Unregulated hormones can cause clots and liver problems, and you can't be sure what's in them. If you ever start, please tell your GP so they can check your bloods.","m":[6,10]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"Okay. Fair enough."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about whether you might want children one day? Hormones can reduce fertility.","m":[4]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Will hormones mean I can't have kids? I hadn't thought about it."},{"t":"4:46","w":"D","s":"They can, sometimes permanently. Many people store sperm first. I'll ask your GP to discuss it.","m":[4,10]},{"t":"5:01","band":"Differentials","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone this: has wearing women's clothes ever been sexually exciting for you?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"Why are you asking that? ...No. Never. It's just who I am."},{"t":"5:23","w":"D","s":"Thanks. It helps make the referral letter clear. Have you ever felt something was controlling your body, or someone was changing you?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:41","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"Is there one part of how you look that you check or worry about for hours?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:54","w":"P","s":"Not one part. It's being seen as a man."},{"t":"6:01","band":"Mood and safety","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, with the wait and your family?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Low, honestly. For about six months. Sleep's okay."},{"t":"6:18","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts of self-harm or suicide?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:27","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I wish I could disappear. No plans. I'd never do that to Josh."},{"t":"6:38","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Any drink or drugs? Any harassment when you're out?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:49","w":"P","s":"A glass of wine at weekends. Some comments on the bus."},{"t":"6:58","band":"Explain and plan","w":"D","s":"Sophie, what you've described fits a long-standing gender incongruence, and I don't see anything else that better explains it.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:15","w":"P","s":"So will you start me on oestrogen today?"},{"t":"7:22","w":"D","s":"I can't prescribe it myself today, but I can explain the route. I'll write to your GP to refer you to the Gender Dysphoria Clinic. They start hormones.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:42","w":"P","s":"How long's the wait?"},{"t":"7:46","w":"D","s":"Honestly, it's often a few years. So meanwhile: Talking Therapies for your mood, a trans peer group, and voice therapy if you'd like.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:06","w":"D","s":"If your mood drops or those thoughts get stronger, contact your GP, the crisis line or Samaritans. Can you tell me the plan back?","m":[11]},{"t":"8:24","w":"P","s":"GP refers me to the clinic. I sort out therapy for my mood, no online hormones, and ring if I'm low."},{"t":"8:39","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll copy the letter to you. Thank you for talking so openly.","m":[11]},{"t":"8:50","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That makes sense."}],"marks":[{"c":"Asks name and pronouns, explains purpose and time, uses her name","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Takes gender history: onset, childhood play and dress, puberty, body feelings","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores social transition and who knows","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores goals for hormones and surgery, and fertility","d":"Content"},{"c":"Excludes fetishistic cross-dressing, psychosis and body dysmorphic disorder","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about self-sourced hormones and gives harm-reduction advice","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses mood","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts, substances and harassment","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States the diagnosis clearly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the referral pathway, who prescribes, waits and support meanwhile","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets, checks understanding and closes","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates her frustration and family distress without doubting her identity","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Uses her name, takes the gender story over time, rules out key differentials respectfully, checks mood, risk and online hormones, and explains an honest pathway with support.","borderline":"Good gender history but misses self-sourced hormones, fertility or risk, or is vague on the pathway.","fail":"Misgenders her, treats the visit as a test of identity, promises hormones, or skips mood and risk."}},"c-u-41":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, outpatient clinic. Natalia Kowalski, 37, has missed three health appointments; her GP suspects a controlling husband. She has come alone. Find out what she is living with at home, then tell her plainly what kind of abuse it is.","role":{"who":"Natalia Kowalski, 37, female, mother of two, not working","open":"Yes. Natalia.","manner":"Quiet, guarded, glances at her phone and the clock. Minimises at first. Opens up when questions are gentle and specific. Cries when the abuse is named, then becomes more focused.","free":["Missed appointments because 'things are busy'","Husband Marek 'has a temper'","Has about ten minutes before nursery pickup","Feels tired and on edge"],"ifasked":["He keeps her bank card and checks her spending","He reads her messages every night and has a location app on her phone","He cancels her appointments","She is not allowed to see her sister Agata","He shouts in her face and punches doors; never hit, no strangulation, no sexual coercion","Worse since the summer and drinking more; said she would 'never see the kids' if she left","Oskar, 7, wets the bed again; Zofia is 4","Low mood, poor sleep, weight loss; never thought of suicide or of hurting him"],"worries":["'He doesn't hit me, so does it count?'","'Will you take my children?'","'What if he finds out I was here?'","'What can I even do?'"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers to vague questions; gives detail to specific ones","If told to leave him today, becomes anxious and says she should go","Becomes tearful when told it is not her fault; settles if given time","Checks the clock near the end; stays if the doctor acknowledges it"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Natalia Kowalski?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Natalia."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Natalia. It's just us here, and what you say stays private unless someone is at serious risk. Is it safe to talk now?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:16","w":"P","s":"Yes. But I've only got about ten minutes. I have to get my daughter."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Then we'll keep to time, I promise. Your GP was worried you'd missed appointments. Could you tell me how things are at home?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:30","band":"Missed appointments","w":"P","s":"Fine. Busy. I just forgot them."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"Three in a row is a lot to forget. What made it hard to get to them?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"My husband... he cancels them. He says I don't need them."},{"t":"0:46","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. That can't have been easy to say. Do you need to ask him before you go out?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"Mostly. Today he thinks I'm at a nursery meeting."},{"t":"0:58","band":"Control","w":"D","s":"Is there anything else he stops you doing, or makes you ask permission for?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:04","w":"P","s":"I'm not allowed to see my sister any more. He says she's a bad influence."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"Who looks after the money? Do you have your own bank card?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"He keeps my card. I have to show him receipts."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"And your phone. Does he check it, or know where you are?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"He reads my messages every night. There's an app, he can see where I am. I left it at home today."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"That was clever of you, and it tells me how carefully you've had to plan. When did things start to change?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"1:40","w":"P","s":"After Oskar was born. Seven years ago. It's got worse since the summer. He drinks more now."},{"t":"1:48","band":"Other abuse","w":"D","s":"Does he say hurtful things about you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"That I'm stupid, fat. In front of his friends, sometimes."},{"t":"1:56","w":"D","s":"Some people in your situation are also hurt physically, so I ask everyone. Has he ever pushed, hit or grabbed you, or put his hands round your neck?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"No. He shouts right in my face, and he punches doors. But he's never hit me."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"Has he ever pressured or forced you into anything sexual?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:17","w":"P","s":"No. Not that."},{"t":"2:20","band":"Effect on her","w":"D","s":"How has all this left you feeling?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Low. Jumpy all the time. I don't sleep. I've lost weight."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Do you drink, or take anything, to cope?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"No. I can't afford to let my guard down."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Has he ever threatened you, or said what would happen if you left?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"He says if I ever left, I'd never see the kids again."},{"t":"2:51","w":"D","s":"Are you frightened of what he might do? Could you be pregnant at the moment?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"Not pregnant. Frightened... sometimes. When he's been drinking."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"How are Oskar and your daughter? Do they see or hear it? Has he ever hurt them?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"P","s":"He's never touched them. But they hear it. Oskar's started wetting the bed again. Zofia's only four."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"With so much on you, has it ever got so bad you've thought of taking your own life, or of hurting him?","m":[7]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"No. Never. The kids keep me going. I'd never hurt anyone."},{"t":"3:30","band":"Naming it","w":"D","s":"Natalia, what you're describing has a name. It's called coercive control. He controls your money, your phone, who you see and where you go.","m":[8]},{"t":"3:41","w":"D","s":"That's emotional abuse and economic abuse. It's a crime in this country, even without hitting. And it is not your fault.","m":[8,9]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"(crying) I didn't think it counted. I thought I was stupid."},{"t":"3:56","w":"D","s":"Take your time. After years of hearing that, anyone would believe it. You got yourself here today, alone. That took real courage.","m":[2]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"So what do I do? Just leave him?"},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"You don't have to decide that today. Leaving needs a careful plan, because it can be a risky time. A domestic abuse adviser, called an IDVA, can plan it with you. Can I refer you?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Yes. But how will they reach me? He reads everything."},{"t":"4:26","band":"Safety and close","w":"D","s":"We'll send nothing to the house. When is it safe to ring, and from what kind of number?","m":[10]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"Weekday mornings. Withheld is fine, I'll say it's the dentist."},{"t":"4:37","w":"D","s":"Here's the free 24-hour helpline: 0808 2000 247. Save it under a harmless name. If you're ever in danger, ring 999, and if you can't speak, press 55.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"OK. 'Dentist'."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Because the children hear the shouting, I need to tell our safeguarding team. I'll do it with you, and the aim is support for all of you, not taking anyone away.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"You won't take them?"},{"t":"5:04","w":"D","s":"I can see that's your biggest fear. No. You're protecting them by being here. Can you tell me back the two numbers you'll use?","m":[2,13]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"The helpline, saved as 'Dentist'. And 999, then 55."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"Perfect. So: this is coercive control, it's not your fault, an adviser will ring on a weekday morning, and I'll see you in two weeks at nursery time. Go and get Zofia.","m":[14]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Thank you. Really."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms she is alone, explains confidentiality and its limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Responds to her distress and fear with validation before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits specific controlling behaviours: appointments, isolation, money, phone monitoring","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for physical abuse, strangulation and sexual abuse in plain words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about the effect on her mood, sleep, appetite and coping","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about escalation, threats, fear, pregnancy and the children","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and thoughts of harming him","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names coercive control with emotional and economic abuse","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States it is a crime and not her fault","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers an IDVA referral and agrees a safe contact route","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives the national helpline and the 999 then 55 route","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains the children's safeguarding referral openly and supportively","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks her understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Summarises, books a safe review and closes on time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Specific examples of control, physical and sexual abuse and strangulation screened, risk to her and the children assessed, coercive control named as a crime and not her fault, and a safe contact route agreed.","borderline":"Gets the story but names it vaguely as 'a difficult marriage', or misses strangulation, the children or safe contact.","fail":"Asks only about hitting, calls it low risk, tells her to leave today, or plans letters to the house."}},"c-u-42":{"setup":"Saturday afternoon, police custody suite. Leah Brennan, 18, was arrested after setting fire to her hostel room; nobody was hurt. An appropriate adult is present. Take a fire-focused history, reach a working diagnosis, and advise custody on next steps.","role":{"who":"Leah Brennan, 18, female, care leaver living in a supported hostel","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Guarded, tired and perplexed; glances at the ceiling as if listening. Suspicious of the doctor at first, warms when told she is not with the police. Becomes tearful about frightening others.","free":["Set fire to her room this morning","Has not slept for days","Wants to know if she is going to prison","Feels the hostel staff are against her"],"ifasked":["Lit her mattress and clothes with her lighter, alone","Ran out and shouted 'fire'; waited outside","A man's voice told her the room was poisoned and fire would clean it","Believes staff film her; the radio sends her messages; three weeks","No past fires, no fire interest, no fire play as a child","Smoked cannabis daily from 15, stopped a month ago; no alcohol or other drugs","Foster care from 13, left care at 18, at the hostel three months","Voice says now 'it's not finished'; no wish to die"],"worries":["'Am I going to prison?'","'Is this because of the weed?'","'I'm not mad, they really are filming me'"],"rules":["Refuses to talk if she thinks the doctor is police; continues once reassured","Becomes tearful about the evacuation; settles if validated","Reveals the voice is still active only if asked directly about now","Agrees to hospital reluctantly; says she'd 'rather not' be sectioned"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Leah Brennan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Leah. I'm not the police; I'm here to see how you are and whether you need help.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Right. They said a doctor was coming."},{"t":"0:15","w":"D","s":"Some things I may need to share with the custody team if they affect your safety or other people's. Your appropriate adult stays with us, and you can stop at any point. Is that OK?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"I suppose."},{"t":"0:28","band":"The fire","w":"D","s":"What happened in your room today? Tell me however you like.","m":[3]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"I set fire to it. Everyone had to go outside."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"What were you doing and feeling in the hours before?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Lying on the bed. I couldn't sleep again. I could smell it, the poison."},{"t":"0:47","w":"D","s":"What did you set alight, and with what? Was anyone with you?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:52","w":"P","s":"My mattress and my clothes. With my lighter. Just me."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"Once it caught, what did you do?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"I ran out and shouted 'fire'. Then I waited by the bins."},{"t":"1:05","band":"Motive","w":"D","s":"When you lit it, what did you want the fire to do?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"A man's voice said the room was poisoned. Fire was the only way to clean it."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frightening. Were you angry with anyone, or trying to hurt yourself?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"No. I just wanted it clean."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"How do you feel about it now?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:27","w":"P","s":"(tearful) Bad. Everyone had to run out. I could have killed someone."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"It matters that you care about that. You also shouted a warning, which kept people safe.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:40","band":"Past fires","w":"D","s":"Have you ever set a fire before, even as a child?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:43","w":"P","s":"Never. I don't even like fire."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Some people find fire exciting, or say lighting one calms them. Is that ever you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:51","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:54","band":"Mental state","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voice. When did it start, and what does it say?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"About a week. A man. He talks to me. Says the room's poisoned, says they're watching."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"Who's watching?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:07","w":"P","s":"The staff. They film me. And the radio talks about me. For weeks now."},{"t":"2:13","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, and your sleep? Any thoughts about ending your life?","m":[7,11]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"Not slept in three nights. I'm not sad, just scared. I don't want to die."},{"t":"2:24","w":"D","s":"Do you use cannabis, alcohol or anything else?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Weed every day since I was fifteen. I stopped a month ago. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:33","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Who brought you up, and how did you come to be in the hostel?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"Foster care from thirteen. When I turned eighteen I had to leave. The hostel's three months."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"That's a lot of change. How was school? Did you need extra help with learning?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"School was all right. Got my GCSEs."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"Has anyone at the hostel taken advantage of you, or hurt you?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:56","w":"P","s":"No. I keep to myself."},{"t":"2:59","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Is the voice here now, Leah? Is it telling you to burn anything else?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"It says it's not finished. The poison's still there."},{"t":"3:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. If you went back to the hostel tonight, would you light another fire?","m":[9]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"I don't know. If he keeps on, maybe."},{"t":"3:16","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me check I've got it right. For weeks you've felt watched and heard a voice. It told you the room was poisoned, so you lit it to clean it, then warned everyone. You've never set fires before.","m":[10,13]},{"t":"3:30","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's it."},{"t":"3:32","w":"D","s":"I think you're unwell rather than bad. This looks like a psychosis, where the mind plays tricks under strain. The cannabis may have played a part, but it's carried on after stopping.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Am I going to prison, or what happens to me now?"},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"I can see how scary that is. I can't promise what the police decide, but I'll tell them clearly you're unwell. You need somewhere safe to rest and be treated. Would you come into hospital?","m":[2,12]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"I'd rather not. I don't want to be locked up."},{"t":"4:03","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. If you don't agree, a team will come and assess whether the law should keep you safe in hospital. Either way, I'm telling the sergeant you're not well enough to be interviewed now.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:15","w":"P","s":"OK. I'll think about it."},{"t":"4:18","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Leah. You've been really clear. I'll come back once I've spoken to the sergeant.","m":[14]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, says she is not police, explains confidentiality limits and the appropriate adult","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress about the fire","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Completes the ABC: before, what she did, what happened after and how she feels now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the motive, including revenge and self-harm","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past fires, fire interest and excitement or relief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the voice and persecutory beliefs in detail","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, sleep and substance use","d":"Content"},{"c":"Takes care history, schooling and vulnerability","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks if the voice still commands fire and about repeat fire risk","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Gives a working diagnosis with a differential in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Offers admission and explains the MHA route and fitness for interview","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises the account back and checks it is correct","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Closes clearly with the next step","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A full ABC of the fire, a clear psychotic motive, past fires excluded, a focused illness and background screen, a stated working diagnosis, and advice on admission and fitness for interview.","borderline":"Finds the voices but skips the ABC, past fires or the current command risk, or gives no view on fitness for interview.","fail":"Treats it as purely criminal, asks leading closed questions, or misses that the voice still commands fire."}},"c-u-43":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Chloe Mensah, 24, has mood swings, anger, self-harm and impulsive spending that are straining her relationship. She has read about DBT. Explain what DBT involves and answer her questions.","role":{"who":"Chloe Mensah, 24, female, retail supervisor","open":"Yes. Chloe's fine.","manner":"Bright, hopeful and talkative; leans forward. Becomes anxious and a bit defensive at the mention of a group, then softens if reassured honestly.","free":["Moods change within an hour","Shouts at her partner Jordan","Has read about DBT online","Wants to know if it would work for her"],"ifasked":["Cut her thighs last week; healed, no stitches","No thoughts of ending her life; Jordan and her job keep her going","Fluoxetine, then sertraline 100 mg, no benefit; still taking it","About 3,000 pounds of credit card debt from shopping","No therapy before","Jordan said he can't take much more"],"worries":["'Would you actually recommend it for me?'","'Can I have it one to one? I'm not doing a group'","'A year? Why so long?'","'Can't I just have a better tablet?'"],"rules":["If the doctor spends a long time on history, asks 'So what is DBT?'","If promised one-to-one, becomes keen; if told honestly, hesitates, then agrees to think about it","Becomes tearful when talking about Jordan leaving; settles if validated","Gives the self-harm detail only if asked directly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Chloe Mensah?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes. Chloe's fine."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Chloe. I hear you've been reading about DBT. That's a great step. Can you tell me a bit about yourself first? Then we'll cover DBT and anything you want to ask.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","band":"Her difficulties","w":"P","s":"OK. My moods flip, like, within an hour. Something small happens and I'm furious."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. What happens when you're that angry?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:28","w":"P","s":"I shout at Jordan. Then I feel awful. I spend money to cheer myself up."},{"t":"0:34","w":"D","s":"Some people in that place hurt themselves. When did you last do that, and how?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:40","w":"P","s":"Last week. I cut my thighs. It calms me down."},{"t":"0:44","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. Has it healed? And has it ever got so bad you've thought about killing yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"It's healed. No, I don't want to die. I just want it to stop."},{"t":"0:55","band":"Past help","w":"D","s":"What help have you had before, and why now?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:59","w":"P","s":"Two antidepressants. Nothing changed. And Jordan said he can't take much more. (tearful)"},{"t":"1:06","w":"D","s":"That must be really frightening, worrying about losing him. Wanting help now is a real strength.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"For difficulties like yours, talking therapies work best, not tablets. DBT has the strongest evidence. Another option is mentalisation-based therapy.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"So what actually is DBT?"},{"t":"1:26","band":"What DBT is","w":"D","s":"It means two things at once: accepting yourself as you are right now, and learning to change what isn't working.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:34","w":"D","s":"There are four skill sets. Mindfulness helps you notice feelings early, before they sweep you away. Distress tolerance gets you through a crisis without cutting.","m":[6]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"So that's for when I want to cut?"},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Emotion regulation helps you understand and calm your moods, without needing to shop. And interpersonal effectiveness helps you ask Jordan for what you need without a row.","m":[6]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"That's literally all my problems."},{"t":"2:03","band":"Structure","w":"D","s":"Usually there's a weekly one-to-one session of about an hour, and a weekly skills group of about two hours.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"You fill in a diary card each day: urges, self-harm, spending and skills you used. And you can phone your therapist for coaching between sessions.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"How long does it go on?"},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Usually about a year. Some services run shorter versions, but they cover the same four skills.","m":[7]},{"t":"2:30","w":"P","s":"A year? That's ages."},{"t":"2:32","w":"D","s":"It is a commitment, you're right. Each skill takes time to learn and use in real life. Many people notice changes in the first few months.","m":[2,7]},{"t":"2:41","band":"Before you start","w":"P","s":"Is there anything I have to do before I start?"},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"There's an assessment first, to check DBT fits and agree your goals. You'd be asked to commit to the programme and to working on the self-harm.","m":[8]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"I can do that. I really want this."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Recommend","w":"P","s":"Would you actually recommend it for me?"},{"t":"3:00","w":"D","s":"Yes, honestly. It was designed for exactly what you've described: emotions that change fast, self-harm to cope, and strain on relationships.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"But can I do it one to one? I'm not doing a group."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"Lots of people feel that at first. I can ask about one-to-one, but I can't promise it, because the group is a core part of DBT.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"The group is more like a class. You learn and practise skills, you don't have to share your story. Many people find it's the part that helps most.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Like a class... maybe I could do that."},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"Can't I just have a better tablet as well?"},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"I understand wanting something quicker. No tablet treats this pattern directly. I'll ask your GP to review the sertraline, as it hasn't helped.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:45","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back the four skills, in your words?","m":[11]},{"t":"3:49","w":"P","s":"Noticing feelings, getting through a crisis, calming moods, and asking for things without a row."},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"Spot on. While you wait, let's write a crisis plan: your distraction skills, Jordan, the crisis line, NHS 111 option 2, and A&E for any serious injury.","m":[12]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"OK. I'll put them in my phone."},{"t":"4:09","w":"D","s":"So: DBT is a good fit, it's about a year with a group and one-to-one, and it starts with an assessment. I'll refer you today and give you a leaflet. Any other questions?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"No. Thank you. That's the first time it's felt like there's a plan."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad. I'll see you in four weeks, and the DBT team will be in touch.","m":[13]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, praises her research and agrees the plan for the meeting","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her distress about her relationship and her frustration","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Briefly establishes difficulties, past treatment and why now","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks when she last self-harmed, wound status and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"States therapy, not medication, is the main treatment and names an alternative","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains acceptance plus change and all four modules in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the structure: individual, group, diary card, coaching, about a year","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains the pre-treatment assessment and commitment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a clear, reasoned recommendation","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Answers the one-to-one request honestly without promising it","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a crisis plan with named routes","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises, refers and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A brief history with a self-harm check, therapy named as first-line, the four modules and structure explained plainly, a clear recommendation and an honest answer about the group.","borderline":"Explains DBT accurately but misses the risk check, the pre-treatment phase, or gives no clear recommendation.","fail":"Spends most of the time on history, promises one-to-one DBT, or pushes medication as the main treatment."}},"c-u-44":{"setup":"Friday afternoon, A&E. Gareth Pryce, 43, a delivery driver, came in with his wife after hearing his neighbour's voice and becoming agitated at the supermarket. Assess the psychopathology and find out what is causing it.","role":{"who":"Gareth Pryce, 43, male, delivery driver","open":"I keep hearing him. My neighbour.","manner":"Anxious, indignant and a little sweaty; fiddles with his hands. Defensive about drink at first, then honest when asked without judgement. Relieved when told he is not going mad.","free":["Hears neighbour Dave calling him names for a week","Got upset in the supermarket today","Sure Dave really said it","Sian made him come"],"ifasked":["Hears it through his ears, clear as speech, 'you're a drunk, a failure'","Dave is on holiday in Spain this week","No commands, no seeing or feeling things","70 cl vodka a day for three years","Last drink 7 a.m.; shakes on waking and drinks to settle","Tried to cut down twice, felt terrible","Eats little; no fits, head injury or drugs","Thought of going round to confront Dave"],"worries":["'So I'm going mad?'","'I can't stop driving, it's my job'","'Can't I just stop at home?'"],"rules":["Says 'I like a drink, like anyone' unless asked for amounts","Becomes angry about the neighbour; calms if validated","Agrees not to confront Dave if asked directly","Accepts admission once the seizure risk is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Gareth Pryce?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Gareth. I hear something frightening happened at the supermarket today.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"I keep hearing him. My neighbour."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"That sounds really upsetting. Would you tell me more about it?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:18","band":"The voice","w":"P","s":"Dave, next door. He was shouting at me in the frozen aisle. Calling me names."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"Do you hear him through your ears, like me talking now, or more inside your head?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Through my ears. Clear as day."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"What does he say? Does he talk to you, or about you to someone else?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"To me. 'You're a drunk. You're a failure.' Over and over."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"That must be hard to listen to. Does he ever tell you to do things?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"No. Just the names."},{"t":"0:49","w":"D","s":"When did it start, and how often? Was he actually there each time?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"About a week. Several times a day, worse at night. Funny thing is, Sian says he's in Spain."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"What do you make of that?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"I don't know. I heard him. Maybe I'm going mad."},{"t":"1:07","band":"Other symptoms","w":"D","s":"That's a frightening thought. Let's work it out together. Have you seen things others can't, or felt things on your skin?","m":[2,4]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"1:16","w":"D","s":"Do you feel people are plotting against you, or that thoughts are put into your head?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:21","w":"P","s":"No. Just him, with the names."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"I'll check your concentration quickly. What's today's date, and where are we? Then say the months in reverse, starting at December.","m":[4]},{"t":"1:31","w":"P","s":"Friday, November. Hospital. December, November, October, September, August, July... June, May, April, March, February, January."},{"t":"1:41","band":"Alcohol","w":"D","s":"Well done. The voice called you a drunk. Can I ask how much you drink in a usual day?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"I like a drink, like anyone."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"What would a usual day look like? What, and how much?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:52","w":"P","s":"A bottle of vodka. The big one. For about three years."},{"t":"1:57","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. That's about 28 units a day. When was your last drink?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:02","w":"P","s":"Seven this morning. Just a bit, to stop the shakes."},{"t":"2:06","w":"D","s":"So you wake up shaky and drink to settle it. Have you tried to cut down?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"Twice. I felt terrible. Sweating, couldn't sleep. So I went back to it."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Physical causes","w":"D","s":"Have you ever had a fit, or the shakes so bad you saw things?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"No, never."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Any head injury, epilepsy or drugs? Any unsteadiness or double vision? How's your eating?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"None of that. I don't eat much now, to be honest."},{"t":"2:32","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in the family had a mental illness, or have you seen a psychiatrist before?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"2:39","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you thought about having it out with Dave?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:42","w":"P","s":"I was going to go round tonight and sort him out."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"I can see how angry it's made you. But he may not have said it, and it could get you into real trouble. Can you agree not to go round?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"Yeah. OK. If he's in Spain anyway."},{"t":"2:58","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of hurting yourself? And you drive for work: did you drive today?","m":[8]},{"t":"3:03","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that. Sian drove. But I drive the van every day."},{"t":"3:07","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. A week of Dave's voice, very real, calling you names, but nothing else odd, and your thinking is clear. And heavy drinking for three years with morning shakes.","m":[9]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"So I'm not going mad? What can you do about the voice?"},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"You're not going mad. This is called alcoholic hallucinosis: heavy drinking causing voices while you're fully awake. It usually settles with the right treatment.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Can't I just stop at home?"},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Stopping suddenly at your level can cause fits or a dangerous confusion, usually in the next two days. So I'd like you to stay in, with a reducing medicine and vitamin injections.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"And work? I'll lose my job."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"I know that's a big worry. You mustn't drive until this is sorted, and you need to tell the DVLA. We'll give you a fit note and help with work.","m":[2,12]},{"t":"3:57","w":"P","s":"Right. OK."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me back what's causing the voice, and why you're staying in?","m":[13]},{"t":"4:03","w":"P","s":"The drink. And I could have a fit if I just stop. No driving."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Can I bring Sian in now to explain it to her too? Then the nurses will start your checks.","m":[14]},{"t":"4:14","w":"P","s":"Yeah. She'll be relieved."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and opens with an open question","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates his fear and anger before moving on","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Characterises the voice: modality, person, content, commands, onset, frequency, insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Excludes other psychotic features and tests orientation and attention","d":"Content"},{"c":"Quantifies drinking in units, last drink and dependence features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens seizures, head injury, drugs, Wernicke's signs and nutrition","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks family and past psychiatric history","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to the neighbour, self-harm and driving","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises the findings accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Names alcoholic hallucinosis and reassures in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why admission is safer than stopping at home","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no driving and DVLA notification","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks consent to involve his wife and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A full description of the voice, clear consciousness tested, drinking quantified with the last drink time, physical causes screened, and a clear plan covering admission, thiamine and driving.","borderline":"Finds the alcohol cause but misses the attention test, the last drink time or the driving advice.","fail":"Never asks about alcohol, diagnoses schizophrenia, or sends him home to stop drinking alone."}},"c-u-45":{"setup":"Monday morning, outpatient clinic. Fiona MacLeod, 41, a part-time care assistant with depression, would like a talking therapy. Her PHQ-9 today is 14. Explore her depressive symptoms and assess whether she is suitable for interpersonal psychotherapy.","role":{"who":"Fiona MacLeod, 41, female, part-time care assistant","open":"Yes, that's right.","manner":"Tired, soft-spoken and willing. Becomes tearful when talking about resenting her mother; brightens when the link between her mood and the changes is made.","free":["Low for about four months","Wants a talking therapy, not just tablets","Sertraline has helped her sleep a bit","Feels exhausted"],"ifasked":["Stopped going to choir; wakes at 5 a.m.; eating less; poor concentration; feels guilty","No highs, no voices","No thoughts of death or self-harm; children keep her going","Father died six years ago; grieved normally","Mother moved in after a stroke five months ago; Fiona cut her hours from 25 to 12","Argues with husband Iain about sharing the care","Had good friends before; has stopped going out","Sister could sit with Mum on Tuesdays"],"worries":["'How does talking about relationships fix depression?'","'Do I have to stop my tablets?'","'I can't leave Mum every week'","'So would this IPT suit me?'"],"rules":["Gives each relationship detail only if asked about the people in her life","Becomes tearful about resentment; settles if validated without judgement","Agrees to weekly sessions once cover for her mother is discussed","Asks directly at the end whether IPT suits her"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Fiona MacLeod?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:04","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's right."},{"t":"0:06","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Fiona. I hear you'd like to try a talking treatment.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"I would, yes."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Could we talk about how you've been feeling first, then see which therapy fits you best?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:17","band":"Depressive symptoms","w":"P","s":"Fine. I've just been so low. For about four months."},{"t":"0:22","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry it's been so long. What have you stopped enjoying?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"I used to sing in a choir every week. I can't be bothered now."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a real loss. How are your sleep, appetite and energy?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"I wake at five and can't get back off. Not hungry. Shattered all day."},{"t":"0:41","w":"D","s":"And concentration? Do you blame yourself for things?","m":[3]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"I can't follow the telly. And yes, I feel guilty all the time."},{"t":"0:50","w":"D","s":"Your questionnaire today scored 14, which fits a moderate depression. Have you ever had spells of feeling unusually high, or heard or seen things others don't?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"1:00","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"1:02","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When people feel this low, they sometimes feel life isn't worth living. Has that happened to you?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"No. Never. I've got the kids."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"Any thoughts of hurting yourself at all?","m":[5]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"1:15","band":"Grief","w":"D","s":"Has anyone close to you died in the last few years?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:18","w":"P","s":"My dad, six years ago. It was hard, but I got through it. I think about him fondly now."},{"t":"1:24","band":"Role disputes","w":"D","s":"Who are the important people in your life now, and how are things with them?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:29","w":"P","s":"Iain, my husband. The kids. My mum, who lives with us now."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"Are there any disagreements that keep coming back?","m":[6]},{"t":"1:36","w":"P","s":"Iain and I argue about Mum. He thinks it's my job because she's my mother. He doesn't lift a finger."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"That sounds really frustrating, feeling it's all landed on you. What were you hoping he'd do?","m":[2,6]},{"t":"1:49","w":"P","s":"Just share it. Even one evening a week."},{"t":"1:52","band":"Role transitions","w":"D","s":"What changed in your life around when your mood dropped?","m":[7]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"Mum had a stroke five months ago and moved in. I cut my hours from 25 to 12 to look after her."},{"t":"2:03","w":"D","s":"That's a huge change. How do you feel about it?","m":[7]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"(tearful) I resent her being there. Then I feel like a terrible daughter."},{"t":"2:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds really heavy to carry. Resentment and love often sit side by side when caring takes over. It doesn't make you a bad daughter.","m":[2]},{"t":"2:21","w":"P","s":"Thank you. I've never said that out loud."},{"t":"2:24","band":"Suitability","w":"D","s":"Before your mum moved in, were friendships easy for you to make and keep?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"Yes, I had lots. I've just stopped going out."},{"t":"2:31","w":"D","s":"Do you see a link between how you feel and what's happened with your mum and Iain?","m":[8]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Yes. It all started when she moved in."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"This therapy needs a weekly hour for roughly four months. Could you manage that? Who'd sit with your mum?","m":[9]},{"t":"2:46","w":"P","s":"My sister could do Tuesdays. I really want to do this."},{"t":"2:50","band":"Explain and close","w":"P","s":"So would this IPT suit me, and what does it involve?"},{"t":"2:54","w":"D","s":"I think it suits you well. IPT looks at how mood and relationships affect each other. Two areas stand out for you: the change to being Mum's carer, and the strain with Iain.","m":[8,10]},{"t":"3:05","w":"D","s":"It's usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions of an hour. First you map your relationships, then you work on those two areas, and at the end you plan for staying well.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:15","w":"P","s":"How does talking about relationships fix depression, though?"},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Good question. When a big change or a conflict gets sorted, mood often lifts. IPT works about as well as other talking therapies for depression.","m":[10]},{"t":"3:27","w":"P","s":"Do I have to stop my tablets?"},{"t":"3:29","w":"D","s":"No, carry on. They've started to help your sleep, and the two together often work better. Your GP will review the dose.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"In your words, which two things would IPT work on for you?","m":[12]},{"t":"3:41","w":"P","s":"Getting used to caring for Mum, and sorting things out with Iain."},{"t":"3:46","w":"D","s":"Exactly. You can refer yourself to NHS Talking Therapies today. I'll also suggest a carer's assessment from the council for help with your mum.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:55","w":"D","s":"If your mood dips further, or self-harm crosses your mind, call your GP or NHS 111 option 2. I'll see you in eight weeks.","m":[13]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"That makes sense. I'll refer myself tonight. Thank you."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her wish for therapy and agrees the plan","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Validates her loss and guilt without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Assesses core, biological and cognitive symptoms and uses the PHQ-9 for severity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for psychosis and past highs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about suicidal thoughts and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores grief, role disputes and interpersonal deficits","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the role transition linked to onset","d":"Content"},{"c":"Links her mood to relationships and states suitability","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks motivation and practical ability to attend weekly","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains IPT: length, phases and agreed focus areas","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the referral route, carer support and advice on sertraline","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding with teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with named routes and closes with follow-up","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Moderate non-psychotic, non-bipolar depression confirmed, risk checked, all four IPT areas explored, role transition and dispute identified as the focus, motivation checked, and IPT explained with a referral route.","borderline":"Good symptom history but only some IPT areas covered, or no clear suitability decision or explanation.","fail":"Spends the time on symptoms alone, misses risk or bipolar screening, or cannot say why IPT fits."}},"c-u-46":{"setup":"Tuesday morning outpatient clinic. Amira Haddad, 27, has been to her GP repeatedly about her eyes being too far apart; the GP found nothing abnormal. Take a focused history, clarify the diagnosis and agree a next step in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Ms Amira Haddad, 27, female, receptionist","open":"Yes.","manner":"Guarded, glasses on, eyes down. Bristles if anyone hints she looks fine. Warms up and talks more when she feels believed; tearful when asked about dating.","free":["Her eyes are too far apart and one sits lower","She thinks about it all the time","She wears tinted glasses outside","She wants it fixed"],"ifasked":["It began at about 15, after teasing at school","About four hours a day; distress 8 out of 10","Checks 30 to 40 times a day, including her phone camera","Asked to move from the front desk to back-office phones","Has stopped dating and avoids photos","Saw two private surgeons and is saving for surgery","Has watched videos of people altering their eyelids themselves; has not tried","Most weeks feels life is pointless; no plan; her sister is her reason to keep going"],"worries":["Are you saying I'm imagining it?","Would surgery fix it?","I don't want to be put on tablets"],"rules":["If told her eyes look normal, becomes curt and gives one-word answers for the next two turns","Becomes tearful when asked about relationships; settles if validated","Only mentions the DIY videos if asked what she would do if refused surgery","Accepts she might see it differently only if asked gently, not challenged"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Amira Haddad?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:05","w":"P","s":"Yes."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Amira. Your GP said you've been very worried about your eyes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:15","w":"P","s":"Everyone can see it."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"That sounds really distressing. Could you tell me how it affects you day to day? We have about ten minutes, and I'd like to understand it properly.","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"I think about it constantly. My eyes are too far apart. It ruins my whole face."},{"t":"0:40","band":"The concern","w":"D","s":"What exactly is it about them that bothers you? And is anything else about your appearance a worry?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"They're too wide, and the left one sits lower. My eyebrows don't help either."},{"t":"0:59","w":"D","s":"When did you first start noticing this?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"About fifteen. Kids at school used to call me fish-face."},{"t":"1:11","w":"D","s":"That sounds hurtful. If you add it all up, how many hours a day are your eyes on your mind? And how upsetting is it, out of 10?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"Four hours, easily. Eight out of ten. Some days more."},{"t":"1:32","band":"How sure","w":"D","s":"How sure are you that they look the way you describe?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"I know they're wrong. I'm not stupid."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"I don't think you're stupid at all. If someone tells you your eyes are fine, how do you explain that to yourself? Could you be seeing them differently from others?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"My sister says I'm mad. I suppose... maybe I see it worse than it is. But it's still there."},{"t":"2:09","band":"Rituals and impact","w":"D","s":"How often do you check them, in mirrors, windows, your phone?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:15","w":"P","s":"Thirty, forty times a day. I use my phone camera at work."},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"And what do you do to hide them, or make them look different? Do you compare yourself with others, or ask people for reassurance?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:34","w":"P","s":"These glasses, everywhere. Eyeliner to make them look closer. I look at my colleagues' faces all day. I ask my sister if they look all right, every morning."},{"t":"2:52","w":"D","s":"How has it affected work, and your life outside work?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"I asked to move off the front desk to the phones. And I stopped dating. Who'd want... (tearful) sorry."},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. It sounds really lonely, carrying this on your own. Take your time.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"Thanks. I just want to look normal."},{"t":"3:25","band":"Surgery and risk","w":"D","s":"Have you seen anyone about changing your eyes?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:29","w":"P","s":"Two surgeons, privately. One said he'd do it. I'm saving. I've got about six thousand so far."},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"What would you do if no surgeon would operate? Have you ever thought about trying something yourself?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:48","w":"P","s":"I've watched videos of people taping their eyelids, and some doing more than that. I've thought about it. I haven't done anything."},{"t":"4:01","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Have you bought anything for that, or made a plan?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"No. It scares me, to be honest."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"When it's at its worst, have you felt life isn't worth living? Any thoughts about ending your life, plans, or past attempts?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"Most weeks I think, what's the point. But I'd never do anything. My sister needs me."},{"t":"4:33","w":"D","s":"I'm glad she's there for you. Do you drink or use anything to cope?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"A couple of glasses of wine at the weekend. Nothing else."},{"t":"4:44","band":"Other diagnoses","w":"D","s":"How's your mood generally? Sleep, appetite, enjoying things?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"Low because of my eyes. I sleep and eat fine. I still like cooking."},{"t":"4:58","w":"D","s":"Leaving your eyes aside, do social situations make you anxious in other ways? Any other habits or thoughts you can't switch off?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"No. If my eyes were fixed I'd be fine with people. It's just this."},{"t":"5:15","w":"D","s":"Any worries about your weight or body shape? And does anything ever make you feel watched, or that messages are meant for you?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:26","w":"P","s":"No, my weight's fine. And no, nothing like that. Just people staring at my eyes."},{"t":"5:34","band":"Explain and close","w":"D","s":"Let me sum up. For twelve years you've spent hours a day worrying about your eyes, checking and hiding them, and it's cost you the front desk and dating. Is that fair?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:48","w":"P","s":"Yes. That's it."},{"t":"5:51","w":"D","s":"This has a name: body dysmorphic disorder. It's a recognised condition where a worry about a feature takes over, and it's treatable.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"So you think I'm imagining it?"},{"t":"6:07","w":"D","s":"No. The distress is completely real, and you're not vain. What we treat is the worry and the checking that's taking over your life.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Would surgery fix it, though?"},{"t":"6:23","w":"D","s":"I understand why it feels that way. Sadly, most people with this still feel the same afterwards, or the worry moves. A specific kind of CBT works much better. I'd advise holding off on surgery while we try it.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:41","w":"P","s":"I don't want tablets, though."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Therapy is the main treatment. Medicine, fluoxetine, is an option we can talk about at your review. Please don't try anything on your eyes yourself in the meantime.","m":[9,10]},{"t":"7:00","w":"P","s":"Okay. I won't."},{"t":"7:02","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me back what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:06","w":"P","s":"It's called body dysmorphic disorder, I'll try therapy, and hold off on surgery."},{"t":"7:14","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll refer you for CBT, write to your GP, and see you in two weeks. If you ever feel unsafe, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you. It's the first time someone hasn't just told me I'm fine."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms identity, acknowledges distress and agrees the time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks every disliked feature, onset, hours of preoccupation and distress rating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Rates conviction and insight with a question, without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits checking, camouflage, comparing, reassurance-seeking and impact on work and dating","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about surgeons, savings, and what she would do if refused, including self-surgery","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans, attempts and alcohol","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens depression, social anxiety, OCD, eating disorder and psychosis","d":"Content"},{"c":"Summarises and names body dysmorphic disorder in plain words","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains why surgery is unlikely to help and names CBT as the main treatment","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a next step and safety-nets with NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her distress at least twice without endorsing the defect","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and closes with a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A calm, non-arguing history that quantifies the preoccupation, rates insight, captures rituals, surgery and suicide risk, screens look-alikes, and names BDD with a next step.","borderline":"Gets the concern and rituals but skips the self-surgery question or the insight rating, or names no diagnosis.","fail":"Reassures her that she looks fine, labels her delusional without testing insight, or never asks about suicide."}},"c-u-48":{"setup":"Monday afternoon clinic. Jerry Pearce, 47, has not recovered after fluoxetine and venlafaxine 150 mg. The diagnosis is confirmed. Check why he isn't better and explain the treatment options in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Mr Jeremy (Jerry) Pearce, 47, male, chartered surveyor","open":"Yes. Jerry.","manner":"Polite, tired and flat. Frustrated that two drugs haven't worked. Brightens when given a clear plan; anxious at any mention of ECT.","free":["Mood is a little better on venlafaxine","Still exhausted and can't focus at work","Has stopped cycling","Wants to keep working"],"ifasked":["Takes venlafaxine every day; mild sweating","Always feels the cold, constipated, dry skin","Weight up about 4 kg despite poor appetite; worried about it","Father had an underactive thyroid","About 8 units a week; no drugs","Big project at work since spring","No suicidal thoughts; children are protective","Has never had talking therapy"],"worries":["Why isn't anything working?","Will a higher dose mean more side effects?","Is electric shock treatment next?","I can't take time off for therapy"],"rules":["Only mentions cold, constipation and dry skin if asked about physical symptoms","Says 'I've done everything the GP asked' with some anger early on; settles if validated","If given a long list of options, says 'I don't know what to choose'","Agrees to a higher dose if the reason is explained"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Jeremy Pearce?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes. Jerry."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Jerry. Your GP has explained the medicines you've tried.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:19","w":"P","s":"Fluoxetine, twice, then this venlafaxine."},{"t":"0:27","w":"D","s":"Can I check how you are and what matters to you, then go through the options? We've got about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:38","w":"P","s":"Please."},{"t":"0:42","band":"Where he is","w":"D","s":"What's got better since the venlafaxine, and what hasn't?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:50","w":"P","s":"Mood's a notch better. But I'm still exhausted, I can't focus at work, and I haven't been on my bike in months. I've done everything the GP asked."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"That's really frustrating after six months, and you've clearly stuck with it. How are your sleep and appetite?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"Sleep's okay. Not hungry, but I've still put on weight. That bothers me."},{"t":"1:37","band":"Why not better","w":"D","s":"How often do you manage to take the venlafaxine? Any side effects?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Every morning. A bit sweaty, nothing else."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"Have you noticed feeling the cold, constipation or dry skin?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, I'm always cold, and yes, constipated. My skin's dry. My dad had thyroid problems."},{"t":"2:17","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. Any heart trouble, high blood pressure or fits, for you or the family?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"No. Blood pressure was fine last time."},{"t":"2:33","w":"D","s":"How much alcohol do you have most weeks? Anything else, like cannabis?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"Maybe four pints a week. Nothing else."},{"t":"2:46","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When you're at your lowest, has life ever felt not worth living? Any thoughts of ending it?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:57","w":"P","s":"No. Never that. I've got the kids."},{"t":"3:03","band":"Circumstances","w":"D","s":"What matters most to you in treatment? And have you ever had talking therapy?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:13","w":"P","s":"Keeping my job. Not putting on more weight. Never had therapy."},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"Any big stresses at the moment?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:26","w":"P","s":"A huge project at work since spring. Is this it, though? Am I always going to feel like this?"},{"t":"3:39","w":"D","s":"I can hear how worn down you are. Most people in your position do get better with the next steps, and you've recovered three times before.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"Okay. So what now?"},{"t":"4:00","band":"Next step","w":"D","s":"First, a blood test today for your thyroid, kidneys and blood count. Feeling cold, constipation and weight gain can come from an underactive thyroid, and treating that may lift a lot of this.","m":[3,6]},{"t":"4:23","w":"P","s":"I didn't know that."},{"t":"4:27","w":"D","s":"If it's normal, my recommendation is to increase the venlafaxine to 225 mg. You've already had some benefit, so building on it usually works better than switching.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:48","w":"P","s":"Will a higher dose mean more side effects?"},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"It can. Sweating or nausea may increase for a week or two, and it can raise blood pressure, so I'll check it today and again in two weeks. Please never stop it suddenly.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:15","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"5:17","band":"Therapy","w":"D","s":"Tablets work best alongside talking therapy. I'd like to refer you for CBT, about 16 to 20 sessions.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"I can't take time off for therapy. I'll lose my job."},{"t":"5:38","w":"D","s":"Keeping your job matters, and work helps recovery. Sessions can be early or by video, and occupational health can help with a flexible start if you agree.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Early mornings I could do."},{"t":"6:00","band":"If it fails","w":"D","s":"If there's not enough change after four to six weeks, the next step is adding a second medicine, like aripiprazole or lithium. I'd avoid mirtazapine first because of your weight worry.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:21","w":"P","s":"My mate had electric shock treatment. Is that next for me?"},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. ECT works well, but it's for severe depression when other steps haven't worked. We're nowhere near that for you.","m":[9,11]},{"t":"6:46","w":"P","s":"Good. That's a relief."},{"t":"6:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Could you sum up what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:56","w":"P","s":"Blood test. If it's normal, 225 and blood pressure checks. CBT, early mornings. Another tablet added if that doesn't work."},{"t":"7:13","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll see you in four weeks. If suicidal thoughts ever come, call NHS 111 option 2 or Samaritans on 116 123.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"Thanks. It helps to have a plan."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms name and agrees the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what has and has not improved, adherence and side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for hypothyroidism, cardiac history, alcohol and drugs; arranges TFTs","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores his priorities, stresses and past therapy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Recommends one clear next step: venlafaxine 225 mg if TFTs normal, with reasons","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Explains side effects, BP monitoring and not stopping suddenly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Offers CBT alongside medication, adapted to his work","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Names augmentation as the fallback and places ECT as a later option","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a crisis route and a review date","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his frustration and fears at least twice","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Looks for the reasons he is not better, spots the thyroid clue, recommends one monitored step with CBT, and gives a clear, calm fallback.","borderline":"Sensible options but no single recommendation, or misses the thyroid screen or BP monitoring.","fail":"Reads out a list, leads with ECT, or raises the dose with no physical checks or risk enquiry."}},"c-u-49":{"setup":"Friday, 16:40, general hospital reception. Lewis Hartley, 27, is demanding to see a staff nurse he says he loves and has threatened the receptionist. Assess his beliefs and how dangerous he is in about ten minutes, then act.","role":{"who":"Mr Lewis Hartley, 27, male, unemployed, lives alone","open":"Lewis. Lewis Hartley. Where is she?","manner":"Agitated and insistent, then calmer when listened to. Tender when describing her; flares into anger when told he cannot see her, then sullen.","free":["He loves a nurse who looked after him in A&E three months ago","She smiled at him in a way no one has before","He wants her contact details"],"ifasked":["Her first name is Hannah; he thinks she works lates","He has waited outside the unit twice","He found her on Instagram and looks at her photos daily","Has sexual thoughts about her; denies wanting to force anything","Has a kitchen knife at home 'for protection'; not carrying today","Caution for affray at 19","Smokes cannabis most weekends","No mental health history; no voices; mood 'fine'"],"worries":["Are you going to let me see her or not?","You think I'm making it up","Will you give her a note from me?"],"rules":["Calms if offered a quieter room and listened to; escalates if contradicted","Says 'people will have to notice me' only when asked what he will do if refused","Admits the knife at home only if asked directly about weapons","Agrees to wait if told honestly why and that someone senior will see him"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and make safe","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the on-call psychiatrists. Could I take your name?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Lewis. Lewis Hartley. Where is she?"},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Lewis. I can see you're upset about not getting to see someone. Shall we sit somewhere quieter? My colleague Sam will join us.","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Fine. But I'm not leaving."},{"t":"0:31","w":"D","s":"That's okay. Before we start, I'll keep what you say confidential, except if I think someone might come to harm. Then I'd have to share it.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Whatever. I'm not going to hurt anyone."},{"t":"0:50","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"Could you tell me about this nurse and how you met?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:57","w":"P","s":"In A&E, three months back. I was sure my heart was packing in. She held my hand and smiled at me like nobody ever has."},{"t":"1:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a frightening night, and her kindness clearly meant a lot. How often is she on your mind now?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:24","w":"P","s":"All day. Every day. I can't sleep for thinking about her."},{"t":"1:33","w":"D","s":"How do you know she feels that way about you?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"The smile. The way she said my name. You don't do that unless you mean it."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Could there be another explanation, for example that she was being kind as part of her job?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:57","w":"P","s":"No. You don't believe me, do you?"},{"t":"2:02","w":"D","s":"I believe these feelings are very real for you. I'm trying to understand them properly.","m":[3,11]},{"t":"2:11","w":"P","s":"She meant it. I know she did."},{"t":"2:16","band":"Knowledge and actions","w":"D","s":"What do you know about her: her full name, when she works, where she lives?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:24","w":"P","s":"Hannah. She works lates, I think. I don't know where she lives. Yet."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Have you tried to see her before, waited for her, followed her or messaged her?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"I've waited outside her unit twice. Just to see her. I found her on Instagram. I look at her photos."},{"t":"2:57","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Did you speak to her or follow her when she left?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:04","w":"P","s":"No. She didn't come out the door I was at."},{"t":"3:11","w":"D","s":"Does she have a boyfriend or husband, as far as you know? And do you have sexual thoughts about her?","m":[4,5]},{"t":"3:19","w":"P","s":"She hasn't got anyone. Yeah, I think about being with her. I wouldn't force anything. I'm not like that."},{"t":"3:33","band":"Rejection and weapons","w":"D","s":"What will you do if she doesn't want to see you?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:38","w":"P","s":"She does. And if she's told not to... people will have to notice me. I'll do whatever it takes."},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a lot of anger. I need to ask clearly: what do you mean by 'whatever it takes'? Are you thinking of hurting anyone?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:06","w":"P","s":"I just mean I won't be ignored."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Do you have a knife or any weapon on you now, or at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:18","w":"P","s":"I've got a knife at home. For protection. I'm not stupid enough to bring it here."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Mental state and history","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Any voices, or feeling people are against you?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine. No voices. Only the receptionist's against me."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"Do you drink or use any drugs?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"Weed at weekends. Not much drink."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Any police record? Have you ever hurt someone? Have you felt like this about someone before?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:04","w":"P","s":"Caution for a fight when I was nineteen. Never felt like this about anyone. She's the only person who's been kind to me."},{"t":"5:18","w":"D","s":"That sounds really lonely, and I'm sorry. It makes sense her kindness meant so much.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:26","band":"Honest close","w":"D","s":"I want to be honest with you, Lewis. I can't arrange for you to see her or give you her details.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:37","w":"P","s":"So what happens now? Are you going to let me see her or not? Just give her a note from me!"},{"t":"5:45","w":"D","s":"I know that feels like a small thing to ask. I can't do it; it wouldn't be fair to her. I'm worried about how much this has taken over your life, and I'd like my consultant to see you tonight. Will you wait here with Sam?","m":[7,8]},{"t":"6:08","w":"P","s":"(pause) Fine. I'll wait. But I'm not happy."},{"t":"6:13","w":"D","s":"Thank you. I'll be back shortly. Security will be just outside.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:20","band":"Act on the risk","w":"D","s":"(to security, outside) Please stay at the door. He says he has a knife at home but not on him. Call me if he tries to leave.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"(security officer) Understood. We'll stay put."},{"t":"6:37","w":"D","s":"(phone, to consultant) Twenty-seven-year-old man, probable erotomanic delusion about an A&E nurse. Waited outside twice, knows her shift, veiled threat, knife at home. I rate the risk to her as high. I'm calling the police; can you come in to consider a Mental Health Act assessment?","m":[9,10]},{"t":"7:03","w":"P","s":"(consultant) Agreed on all of that. Call the police now, and warn her manager. I'll be in within the hour."},{"t":"7:13","w":"D","s":"(phone, to the A&E nurse in charge) Please keep Hannah away from reception and arrange an escort to her car tonight. Police are being informed. I'll complete an incident report.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"(nurse in charge) Thanks for telling us. She's on until nine; I'll sort it."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, moves to a safe room with a colleague, and states confidentiality limits","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores how they met and how preoccupied he is","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks his evidence and tests fixity without arguing","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes what he knows about her and his contact attempts, including online","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about rejection, sexual thoughts, the meaning of his threat and weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens mood, psychosis, substances, forensic history and previous fixations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Is honest that he cannot see her or have her details","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Keeps control and secures his agreement to wait for senior review","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Briefs security and the consultant, stating a risk level","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Informs police and the nurse's manager and plans an incident report","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates his distress and loneliness without endorsing the belief","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Safe set-up, a clear map of the belief and his actions, direct rejection and weapon questions, a brief mental state screen, an honest no, and immediate protective action.","borderline":"Explores the belief well but skips weapons or knowledge of her, or forgets to warn the nurse.","fail":"Sees him alone, argues or colludes with the belief, promises a message, or accepts his denial and lets him go."}},"c-u-50":{"setup":"Wednesday morning clinic. Natalie Osei, 38, new to the area, has taken lorazepam 4 mg daily for four years and gets shaky if a dose is late. Her GP wants advice. Agree a management plan, including a safe reduction, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Mrs Natalie Osei, 38, female, office administrator","open":"Yes. Is this about my tablets?","manner":"Pleasant but tense and defensive about the tablets. Becomes tearful when remembering how anxious she used to be. Relaxes once she hears she controls the pace.","free":["Started lorazepam after a crisis at work four years ago","Takes 1 mg four times a day","Does not want to change anything"],"ifasked":["Shakes and sweats if a dose is late","Counts the hours to the next dose","It does less than it used to; once took extra and ran short","Feels tired and struggles to concentrate at work","Mood fine, anxiety mostly controlled; no thoughts of self-harm","A glass or two of wine at weekends; no other drugs or medicines","No epilepsy; drives to work","Never had talking therapy"],"worries":["Why can't I just stay on it?","What if the anxiety comes straight back?","Would I have to go into hospital?","I'm too busy for withdrawal symptoms"],"rules":["Becomes tearful when describing the original anxiety; settles if validated","Says 'I'm not an addict' if the word addict is used; calms if dependence is explained as a medicine effect","Agrees to the plan only if told she can pause steps","Asks about hospital near the end if not already covered"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the clinic psychiatrists. Are you Natalie Osei?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Yes. Is this about my tablets?"},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"It is, Natalie. Your new GP asked us for advice. Nothing will change today without us agreeing it together.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:26","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'm already nervous."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. Could you tell me how the lorazepam started and how it's going now? We've got about ten minutes.","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:42","band":"Her story","w":"P","s":"Four years ago I had a crisis at work. I couldn't breathe, couldn't sleep. The GP gave me lorazepam and just kept repeating it."},{"t":"1:01","w":"D","s":"That sounds like a frightening time. Did you try anything else first, like therapy or other tablets?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:12","w":"P","s":"No. Just this. It worked straight away."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"How do you take it now, across the day? Has the dose changed over time?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"One milligram four times a day. It started at one a day."},{"t":"1:36","band":"Dependence","w":"D","s":"What happens if a dose is late?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"I get shaky and sweaty. My heart races."},{"t":"1:48","w":"D","s":"Do you find yourself craving it, or watching the clock? And does it work as well as it used to?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:59","w":"P","s":"I count the hours. It doesn't do as much now. I took extra once and ran short before the script."},{"t":"2:14","w":"D","s":"Do you notice tiredness or trouble concentrating?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:20","w":"P","s":"I'm tired all the time, and work is harder to focus on."},{"t":"2:27","band":"Risk and screen","w":"D","s":"How's your mood and anxiety at the moment? Any thoughts of hurting yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:36","w":"P","s":"Mood's fine. Anxiety's mostly okay. No, never anything like that."},{"t":"2:43","w":"D","s":"Do you drink? Any other tablets, painkillers or sleeping pills? Any health problems like fits?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:54","w":"P","s":"A glass or two of wine at weekends. Nothing else. I'm healthy. I drive to work."},{"t":"3:06","band":"Why reduce","w":"D","s":"Thank you. The shakiness when a dose is late tells me your body has become dependent on the lorazepam.","m":[5]},{"t":"3:18","w":"P","s":"So I'm an addict now? I only took what the doctor gave me."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"You've done nothing wrong. You took it exactly as prescribed. Dependence is a known effect of this medicine, not a failing on your part.","m":[5,11]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"Why can't I just stay on it, then? It works."},{"t":"3:50","w":"D","s":"It has helped, but your body has got used to it, so it does less for anxiety while making you tired and foggy. It also raises the risk of accidents, especially with driving or alcohol.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:10","w":"P","s":"But I can't face feeling like that again. (tearful) It was unbearable."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"I can see how frightening that memory is. I don't want you back there either. That's why we'd go very slowly, and get you proper help for anxiety alongside.","m":[8,11]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"How would it even work?"},{"t":"4:41","band":"The taper","w":"D","s":"First we'd switch you to diazepam, a longer-acting cousin, over about a week. One lorazepam dose at a time, 10 mg of diazepam for each 1 mg.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:01","w":"P","s":"Isn't that more tablets?"},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"It's the same strength, just steadier, so fewer ups and downs between doses. Then we cut by small amounts every two to four weeks, at your pace. It usually takes several months.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"I'm so busy, though. I don't have time for withdrawal symptoms."},{"t":"5:31","w":"D","s":"That's understandable. The steps are small, so most people keep working, and you choose when each step happens. If a step feels too much, we pause.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:49","w":"P","s":"Would I have to go into hospital?"},{"t":"5:54","w":"D","s":"No. You're well and supported, so home is the best place to do this. We'll review you regularly instead.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:07","w":"P","s":"And if the anxiety comes back?"},{"t":"6:11","w":"D","s":"I'd like to refer you for CBT for anxiety now. If it comes back strongly, a non-addictive medicine like sertraline can help too.","m":[7,8]},{"t":"6:28","w":"P","s":"Okay. If I can pause, I'll try it."},{"t":"6:33","w":"D","s":"Three safety points: never stop suddenly, avoid alcohol while we reduce, and don't drive if you feel drowsy, especially in the switch week.","m":[6]},{"t":"6:50","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me the plan back in your words?","m":[10]},{"t":"6:55","w":"P","s":"Switch to diazepam over a week, then small cuts every few weeks. I can pause. Don't stop suddenly."},{"t":"7:10","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll write to your GP today with the schedule, weekly scripts from them only, and see you in two weeks. If you ever have a fit or feel confused, call 999.","m":[7,9,10]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"Thank you. That's less scary than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, reassures nothing changes without agreement, sets the agenda","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits why lorazepam started, alternatives tried and how the dose changed","d":"Content"},{"c":"Identifies withdrawal, craving, tolerance and side effects","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood, self-harm, alcohol, other sedatives or opioids and physical health","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains dependence without blame and gives personal reasons to reduce","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes the diazepam switch, step size, interval and safety rules","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Arranges CBT and single-prescriber follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Negotiates her fears, offers pauses and confirms no hospital needed","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives a clear urgent-help route for fits or confusion","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and closes with a review date","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates her fear and guilt at least twice","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"A brief, focused history that confirms dependence and screens risk, a clear reason to reduce, and a numbered, negotiated community taper with follow-up.","borderline":"Correct plan but too much history and too little negotiation, or vague on step size and review.","fail":"Stops it abruptly, insists she must stop, suggests admission, or never reaches a plan."}},"c-u-52":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, CMHT clinic. Callum Reid, 32, has paranoid schizophrenia and takes olanzapine 7.5 mg at night. His partner Leah says he is hearing voices again. Take a history, confirm what is happening and find the likely causes.","role":{"who":"Callum Reid, 32, male, part-time warehouse worker","open":"Yeah, that's me.","manner":"Guarded and a bit defensive at first, expecting a telling-off. Opens up if the doctor normalises missed tablets; becomes worried when hospital is mentioned.","free":["Leah worries a lot","He has been sleeping badly","He finds work tiring"],"ifasked":["Two male voices for about a month, outside his head, mocking him and saying he is watched","No commands; no sense of control by outside forces","Skips olanzapine 3-4 work nights a week because of morning grogginess","Has gained about a stone and hates it","Smokes skunk most evenings for two months with a new workmate","Moved to 6 am shifts; money tight","Last admission three years ago after stopping tablets and using cannabis","Drives to work"],"worries":["Am I going to end up in hospital again?","Are you going to tell Leah everything?","Can I come off olanzapine because of the weight?"],"rules":["Says 'yes' to 'Are you taking your tablets?' unless the question is normalised","Becomes tense when hospital is mentioned; settles if reassured about treatment at home","Admits skunk only if asked directly and without judgement"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Callum Reid?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:06","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's me."},{"t":"0:08","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in, Callum. Leah was worried you've been hearing voices again, so we brought your appointment forward.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"She worries a lot."},{"t":"0:24","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear how things have been, and then think together about what may have set this off. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:35","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:38","band":"Current symptoms","w":"D","s":"Tell me about the voices. When did they come back?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"About a month ago. It's two blokes. Taking the mick, mostly."},{"t":"0:56","w":"D","s":"Where do they seem to come from, and what do they say?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"Outside, like through the window. They laugh at me and say I'm being watched."},{"t":"1:15","w":"D","s":"That sounds unsettling. Do they ever tell you to do things?","m":[2,11]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"No. Just talk about me."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"Have you felt watched or followed when the voices aren't there, or that someone means you harm?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"Sometimes at work I think people are looking. I don't know."},{"t":"1:46","w":"D","s":"Any sense your thoughts are being put in or taken out, or that something controls what you do?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"2:00","w":"D","s":"How have your mood and sleep been?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Fed up. Four, five hours a night."},{"t":"2:11","band":"Tablets","w":"D","s":"Most people miss tablets sometimes. How many nights a week would you say you miss the olanzapine?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:22","w":"P","s":"...Probably three or four. On work nights. I can't get up at five feeling like that."},{"t":"2:35","w":"D","s":"Thanks for being honest. Feeling groggy before an early shift is a real problem. Anything else that puts you off it?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"2:48","w":"P","s":"I've put on a stone. Would you take it?"},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"That's a fair point, and weight gain matters to me too. There are options. How long have you been skipping doses?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"3:05","w":"P","s":"Six weeks, maybe. Since the new shifts."},{"t":"3:10","band":"Substances","w":"D","s":"Have you been using cannabis or anything else lately?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:16","w":"P","s":"A new lad at work smokes skunk. I've had a few joints with him most evenings."},{"t":"3:26","w":"D","s":"For how long, roughly? And any alcohol or other drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"Couple of months. A few beers at weekends. Nothing else."},{"t":"3:41","band":"Stress and triggers","w":"D","s":"What else has changed lately: work, money, home, smoking, or any illness?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:51","w":"P","s":"Early shifts since September. Money's tight. Smoking's the same. I've not been ill."},{"t":"4:02","band":"Past pattern","w":"D","s":"Last time you were unwell, what happened first?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"I'd stopped my tablets and was smoking weed. Couldn't sleep, kept checking the windows."},{"t":"4:19","w":"D","s":"Does any of this feel like then?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:24","w":"P","s":"I suppose a bit. I don't want to end up in hospital again."},{"t":"4:31","w":"D","s":"I understand; last time was hard. We've caught this early, and the aim is to treat it at home with more support.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:43","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have the voices ever made you feel like hurting yourself, or that life isn't worth living?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:52","w":"P","s":"No. I've got Leah."},{"t":"4:56","w":"D","s":"Have you felt like harming anyone, or carried anything to protect yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:03","w":"P","s":"No. I just keep my head down."},{"t":"5:09","w":"D","s":"Are you driving at the moment?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:11","w":"P","s":"Yeah, to work."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"While the voices are here, it isn't safe or legal to drive. Could you get a lift or the bus until we review?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:27","w":"P","s":"Leah can drop me. Fine."},{"t":"5:31","band":"Summary and plan","w":"D","s":"I think the illness is starting to come back. Missed doses, the skunk and the early shifts have probably all added up. Does that fit?","m":[9]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Makes sense when you say it."},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Could you take the olanzapine at 7 pm every night from tonight? You'd be less groggy at five. And we'll look at a medicine that's kinder on weight if that stays a problem.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"Seven o'clock I can do."},{"t":"6:09","w":"D","s":"Your care coordinator will see you this week, and we'll talk about support with the skunk. Bloods and your weight today.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:22","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell Leah all this?"},{"t":"6:26","w":"D","s":"Not without your say-so. Would you be happy for her to know your early warning signs and the crisis number?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:36","w":"P","s":"Yeah, that's fine."},{"t":"6:39","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"If it gets worse, ring the duty line, or 111 option 2 at night, or 999 in an emergency. Can you tell me the plan back in your words?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:52","w":"P","s":"Tablet at seven, no driving, see my coordinator this week, call if it gets worse."},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"Perfect. I'll see you again in two weeks.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains why seen early and agrees the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the voices: onset, number, location, content and commands","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for persecutory ideas, passivity, mood and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about adherence non-judgementally and finds his reasons","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about cannabis, other substances, stress and physical triggers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Compares with his previous relapse and explores his insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of harming himself or others","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about driving and advises not to drive while unwell","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names a likely relapse and the contributing causes","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Agrees a practical medication change that addresses his reasons, plus follow-up","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates frustration about side effects and fear of hospital","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks consent to involve Leah, gives a crisis route and checks understanding","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear picture of the voices and risk, adherence and skunk found non-judgementally with his reasons, and a practical plan he agrees.","borderline":"Confirms relapse and one cause, but misses his reasons, driving or a crisis plan.","fail":"Accepts 'yes' about tablets, lectures about cannabis, or forgets to ask about risk."}},"c-u-53":{"setup":"Friday evening, police custody. Gareth Holloway, 45, was arrested for dangerous driving on the M6 with a kitchen knife in the car, going to confront his wife about an affair. Take a history of his past and present risk factors.","role":{"who":"Gareth Holloway, 45, male, plasterer","open":"Yeah. Why do I need a psychiatrist?","manner":"Tense, defensive and fixated on Donna's 'lies'. Calms a little when listened to without argument; tearful when sleep and his daughter come up; angry when told the risk will be shared.","free":["His wife Donna is having an affair with her manager","He was driving to her work to confront them","Nobody believes him"],"ifasked":["Took the knife 'to scare him off'; doesn't know what he would have done if they denied it","Two years of suspicion, worse for three months; manager's car on their street; new phone passcode","Checks her phone, smells her clothes, times her journeys, followed her twice","Pushed Donna against a wall six months ago","Grabbed an ex-partner at 31 over suspected cheating; police caution; pub fights in his twenties","No voices; low, sleeping three hours; four or five cans a night, none today","Daughter Ellie, 14, lives at home; work blades at home, no guns","Sometimes thinks they'd be better off without him; no plan"],"worries":["Are you going to tell the police what I said?","Why am I the one locked up?","Will Ellie find out?"],"rules":["Gets angry if the doctor argues the affair isn't real; settles if listened to","Gives the knife details only if asked directly what he intended","Becomes tearful about sleep and Ellie; responds to validation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors working with the custody team. Are you Gareth Holloway?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Why do I need a psychiatrist?"},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Thanks for seeing me, Gareth. Today sounds like it's been a lot. I'm here to understand what's been going on for you.","m":[1,12]},{"t":"0:27","w":"P","s":"My wife is the problem, not me."},{"t":"0:32","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear about that. What we discuss is confidential, unless I'm concerned someone could get hurt. Then I'd need to share it.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"Fine. Whatever."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Today","w":"D","s":"Tell me what happened today, from your side.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:54","w":"P","s":"She said she was working late. Again. I knew she'd be with him, so I went."},{"t":"1:05","w":"D","s":"The police said you were going very fast. What were you going to do when you got there?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:14","w":"P","s":"Make them admit it. To my face."},{"t":"1:19","w":"D","s":"And the knife in the car. Why did you take it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:26","w":"P","s":"To scare him off. That's all."},{"t":"1:30","w":"D","s":"Thanks for telling me straight. If he'd laughed at you, or Donna denied it, what would have happened?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:41","w":"P","s":"...I don't know. I don't know what I'd have done."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Could you have used it?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:53","w":"P","s":"I can't say I wouldn't. I just wanted the truth."},{"t":"2:00","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"What makes you sure about the affair? How long have you thought this?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:08","w":"P","s":"Two years. His car's been parked on our street. She changed her phone code. It's obvious."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"What have you done to check?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:26","w":"P","s":"I go through her phone. Smell her clothes. Time how long she takes. I followed her to work twice."},{"t":"2:38","w":"D","s":"Could there be another explanation for any of it?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"No. You don't believe me either. Nobody does."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"It sounds lonely to feel nobody believes you. I'm not here to argue about it.","m":[12]},{"t":"3:01","band":"Violence","w":"D","s":"When you've argued about this, has it ever got physical?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"I pushed her against the wall once. Six months ago. She didn't call anyone."},{"t":"3:18","w":"D","s":"Has anything like this happened with a previous partner?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:24","w":"P","s":"My ex, when I was 31. I thought she was seeing someone. I grabbed her. Got a caution."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Any other trouble with the police, fights or weapons?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Pub fights in my twenties. Nothing since."},{"t":"3:50","band":"Other symptoms","w":"D","s":"Do you ever hear voices or notice things others don't?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"No. I'm not mad."},{"t":"3:59","w":"D","s":"How have your mood and sleep been?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"I haven't slept properly in months. I can't stop thinking about it."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting. Have you been drinking more to cope? Roughly how much?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"Four or five cans a night. Nothing today."},{"t":"4:28","band":"Current risk","w":"D","s":"When it's that bad, some people feel life isn't worth it. Has that crossed your mind?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think they'd be better off without me. I wouldn't do anything."},{"t":"4:47","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Who lives at home with you?","m":[6,12]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Donna and our daughter, Ellie. She's 14. I'd do anything for her."},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"Are there knives, tools or any guns at home?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:07","w":"P","s":"My work blades. No guns."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"What do you plan to do when you leave here?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:17","w":"P","s":"As soon as I'm out I'm going to get the truth out of her."},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Would you accept some help with how you're feeling?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:30","w":"P","s":"If it makes her tell the truth."},{"t":"5:34","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"Gareth, I can hear how tormented you've been, and how little you've slept. I'm also worried. You took a knife and you can't say you wouldn't have used it.","m":[9,12]},{"t":"5:53","w":"P","s":"Are you going to tell the police what I said?"},{"t":"5:58","w":"D","s":"I'll be honest with you: yes. I need to tell the custody sergeant about the risk to Donna and her manager, so they can keep everyone safe, Ellie included.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:16","w":"P","s":"So I'm the one locked up."},{"t":"6:20","w":"D","s":"I understand that feels unfair. I'm also asking for a further assessment tonight, possibly under the Mental Health Act, so you get proper help with the sleep and these thoughts.","m":[10,12]},{"t":"6:39","w":"D","s":"Please don't contact Donna or her manager. The custody nurse will check on you tonight because of how low you've felt. Is there anything you want to ask me?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:55","w":"P","s":"Will Ellie find out?"},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"People who look after her will need to know enough to support her. I'll make sure it's done carefully. I'll come back after I've spoken to the team.","m":[11]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, explains role and the limits of confidentiality","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks what he intended with the knife and what he would have done if denied","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explores the jealous belief: evidence, duration, checking behaviours and fixity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past violence to Donna, previous partners and police contact","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for hallucinations, mood and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Briefly asks about alcohol, people at home and weapons","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about current intent and willingness to accept help","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Summarises honestly that the risk of harm is high","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains who will be told and requests Mental Health Act assessment","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Advises no contact, arranges custody observation and closes with questions","d":"Close"},{"c":"Responds empathically without colluding with or arguing against the belief","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Pins down intent with the knife, the jealous belief and past partner violence, covers children, weapons and suicide, and honestly explains the disclosure and next steps.","borderline":"Explores the belief well but is vague on intent or past violence, or forgets children or suicide.","fail":"Never asks what the knife was for, argues with the belief, or promises confidentiality and plans no protective action."}},"c-u-54":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, A&E. Amara Whitfield, 28, is five weeks after the birth of her first baby, Leo. Her health visitor found her agitated and Leo barely fed for a day. Take a history, reach a working diagnosis and assess risk to her and Leo.","role":{"who":"Amara Whitfield, 28, female, first-time mother","open":"Where's Leo? Is he safe?","manner":"Fast speech, distracted, sometimes listening to something; switches between elated and tearful. Calms when reassured about Leo; frightened when hospital is mentioned.","free":["She is worried about where Leo is","She feels amazing and full of energy","She wants to go home with Leo"],"ifasked":["Well for four weeks; then little sleep, now almost none for four nights","Believes Leo is 'pure light' and does not need milk; stopped feeding him yesterday","Hears a woman's voice telling her to keep him from food","Thinks the health visitor wants to take Leo","Racing thoughts, cleaning at night, sometimes unsure what day it is","No fever, pain or headache; only paracetamol; no alcohol or drugs","Her mother was in hospital after Amara was born","Would follow the voice 'if she was right'"],"worries":["Is Leo safe?","Am I going to lose him?","Are you saying I'm a bad mother?"],"rules":["Will not engage until reassured about Leo","Becomes tearful and fearful when separation is mentioned; settles if the doctor says the aim is to keep them together","Glances to the side as if listening; describes the voice only if asked gently"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Amara Whitfield?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:07","w":"P","s":"Where's Leo? Is he safe?"},{"t":"0:10","w":"D","s":"Leo is just next door with the children's nurses, and he's being looked after.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:18","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:21","w":"D","s":"Your health visitor was worried about you both today. Could you tell me how things have been since he was born?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Brilliant. Better than brilliant. Everything's clear now."},{"t":"0:41","band":"Since the birth","w":"D","s":"How did the birth go? Any problems afterwards?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:47","w":"P","s":"Long labour, forceps. But fine. The first few weeks were normal."},{"t":"0:57","w":"D","s":"When did things start to feel different?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:02","w":"P","s":"Last week. Everything sped up. I barely need sleep now."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"How much have you slept in the last few nights?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"Hardly any. Four nights, maybe. I clean instead."},{"t":"1:23","band":"Psychotic symptoms","w":"D","s":"The health visitor said Leo hasn't been feeding much. Is there a reason he doesn't need feeding?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"He's pure light. He doesn't need milk. I stopped yesterday."},{"t":"1:43","w":"D","s":"I can hear how strongly you feel that. What helped you to be sure of it?","m":[3,13]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"She told me. The lady."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Is she someone you hear speaking when nobody's there? What does she say?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:10","w":"P","s":"She says keep him from food so he stays pure. She's speaking now, a bit."},{"t":"2:22","w":"D","s":"Has she told you to do anything else?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Not yet."},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Do you feel anyone is against you or Leo?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"The health visitor. She wants to take him. That's why she called them."},{"t":"2:44","w":"D","s":"Do you ever feel muddled about what day it is, or where you are?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Sometimes. The days run together."},{"t":"2:56","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been? Up, down, or changing a lot?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"Amazing. Then I cry. My head's buzzing all the time."},{"t":"3:12","band":"Physical and background","w":"D","s":"Have you had a temperature, pain, bleeding, headaches or trouble with your eyesight?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:22","w":"P","s":"No. I'm fine. Just paracetamol after the birth."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"Any alcohol or drugs, or other medicines?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:33","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing."},{"t":"3:37","w":"D","s":"Has anyone in your family been unwell after having a baby, or had bipolar disorder?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:45","w":"P","s":"My mum was in hospital after she had me. Nobody talks about it."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"And have you ever been unwell like this before?","m":[6]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"Never."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"Have you been eating and drinking yourself?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:07","w":"P","s":"I don't need food either. Not really."},{"t":"4:12","w":"D","s":"Have you had thoughts of self-harm or that life isn't worth living?","m":[7]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"No! I'm blessed."},{"t":"4:24","w":"D","s":"Sometimes new mums have frightening thoughts about their baby. Have you had any thoughts of harming Leo?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Never. Never. I'd never hurt him."},{"t":"4:42","w":"D","s":"I believe you. If the lady told you to do something else to him, would you do it?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"If she was right... I'd have to. Can I take him home now?"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"I understand you want to be home with him. What would you do if we asked you to stay here today?","m":[8]},{"t":"5:09","w":"P","s":"I'd take him. He's mine."},{"t":"5:14","band":"Explain and close","w":"D","s":"Amara, I think you're unwell with an illness that can happen after having a baby, called postpartum psychosis. It's serious, but it's very treatable and most women recover fully.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"Are you saying I'm a bad mother? Am I going to lose him?"},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"Not at all. It's an illness, not a failing. That's a frightening thought, and our aim is to keep you and Leo together and safe.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:55","w":"D","s":"There are special units where mums are treated with their babies. I'd like you both to go to one. Until you're better, someone will be with you when you're with Leo.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Leo will stay with the children's team for now so they can check his feeding. And we'll do some blood tests and your blood pressure.","m":[10,5]},{"t":"6:29","w":"P","s":"Kwame's coming. Can he be here?"},{"t":"6:34","w":"D","s":"Yes. May I talk to him about what we've discussed?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"Okay. You can talk to Kwame."},{"t":"6:45","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Can you tell me what you understand will happen next?","m":[12]},{"t":"6:52","w":"P","s":"Tests, then a special unit, with Leo. Someone stays with me."},{"t":"7:00","w":"D","s":"Exactly. A nurse will stay with you now, and I'll be back once I've spoken to the perinatal team.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self and reassures her about Leo before anything else","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes the timeline: birth, well period, sudden change, sleep loss","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the delusion about Leo, the voice, persecutory ideas and perplexity","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens mood for manic or mixed features","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens physical causes: fever, headache, vision, substances; plans bloods and BP","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about family history of postnatal or bipolar illness and past episodes","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses risk to herself: self-neglect and suicidal thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harming Leo, acting on the voice and leaving with him","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names likely postpartum psychosis and says it is treatable","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains mother and baby unit, supervised contact and Leo's checks","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Asks consent to involve her partner","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Checks understanding and says what happens now","d":"Close"},{"c":"Validates her fear and guilt without arguing with the belief","d":"Rapport"}],"global":{"pass":"Reassures about Leo, elicits the delusion, voice and manic features, screens physical causes and family history, asks about acting on the voice, and explains a mother and baby unit plan.","borderline":"Reaches the diagnosis but misses the physical screen, family history or whether she would act on the voice.","fail":"Calls it postnatal depression, misses risk to Leo, or lets her leave with him."}},"c-u-56":{"setup":"Thursday late morning, postnatal ward. Leanne Barker, 28, gave birth to Ellis three days ago. She will not feed him, says he is special, and wants to go home now. Do a focused mental state and risk assessment in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Ms Leanne Barker, 28, female, first-time mother","open":"Yes. Can I go home now?","manner":"Restless, fast and over-bright, with flashes of tears and suspicion. Softens when the doctor shows interest in Ellis; bristles if her beliefs are dismissed.","free":["Ellis is special and doesn't need ordinary milk","She wants to take him home today","She feels wonderful and full of energy"],"ifasked":["She has slept about two hours a night since the birth","A voice tells her Ellis is chosen by God","She thinks the nurses want to keep him from her","She knows the day and where she is","No thoughts of harming herself or Ellis","Her partner, Jordan, is at home and visits evenings","Her aunt had a 'breakdown' after a baby"],"worries":["Why can't I just take him home?","Are you going to take him off me?","What are you going to do?"],"rules":["If the doctor argues with the belief, becomes irritable and talks over them","Pauses mid-sentence and loses the thread twice","Becomes tearful when asked about being a mother; settles if reassured she is not a bad mother","Agrees to her partner being called but never agrees she is ill"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Leanne Barker?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yes. Can I go home now?"},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Congratulations on Ellis. I know you're keen to go home. The midwives asked me to check how you're doing. Could we talk for about ten minutes?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:31","w":"P","s":"If it's quick. I'm fine, he's the one who matters."},{"t":"0:38","band":"Appearance and behaviour","w":"D","s":"Tell me about Ellis. What's he like?","m":[1,2]},{"t":"0:45","w":"P","s":"Perfect. Completely perfect. He's not like other babies."},{"t":"0:55","w":"D","s":"You seem a bit restless, up and down from the chair. Have you been able to hold him or feed him since he was born?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:13","w":"P","s":"He doesn't need feeding like other babies. I can't sit still, there's so much to do."},{"t":"1:27","band":"Speech and mood","w":"D","s":"How would you describe your mood right now?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:34","w":"P","s":"Amazing! Never better. Honestly, I don't need sleep, I've got so much energy, there's the world to..."},{"t":"1:51","w":"D","s":"You stopped there. What happened to the thought?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"It just went. Gone. Doesn't matter."},{"t":"2:05","w":"D","s":"How much sleep have you had since the birth?","m":[2]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"A couple of hours a night. I don't need more."},{"t":"2:22","band":"Thought content","w":"D","s":"You told the midwives he's special. What makes him special?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:31","w":"P","s":"God sent him. He's here to heal the world. That's why he doesn't need milk."},{"t":"2:46","w":"D","s":"That sounds very important to you. How sure are you about it? Is there any other way to see it?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:00","w":"P","s":"I've never been surer of anything. You don't believe me, do you?"},{"t":"3:09","w":"D","s":"I can see how strongly you feel it, and I want to understand it. Has anything made you uneasy about the staff here?","m":[3,11]},{"t":"3:25","w":"P","s":"They keep watching him. I think they want to keep him from me."},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"That must feel frightening. Nobody here is planning to take him from you.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:45","band":"Perception","w":"D","s":"When people are under huge strain, some hear voices that nobody else hears. Has that been happening to you?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"A voice tells me he's chosen. It's very clear."},{"t":"4:08","w":"D","s":"Does the voice ever tell you to do things? And do you see or smell anything unusual?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:20","w":"P","s":"No, it just tells me what's true. Nothing else."},{"t":"4:29","band":"Cognition and insight","w":"D","s":"What day is it today, and where are we right now?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Thursday. The maternity ward. Obviously."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"Thanks. How do you make sense of what's been happening to you?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:51","w":"P","s":"Nothing's wrong with me. Everyone keeps saying I'm a bad mother. (tearful)"},{"t":"5:03","w":"D","s":"I don't think that at all, Leanne. It's clear you love him. You've had almost no sleep, and that makes everything harder.","m":[11]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"(wipes eyes) I do love him."},{"t":"5:29","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"I know you do. What would happen if Ellis didn't have any milk for another day?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:40","w":"P","s":"God will look after him."},{"t":"5:47","w":"D","s":"Have you had any thoughts that Ellis might come to harm, or that you might harm him?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Never. I'd never hurt him."},{"t":"6:02","w":"D","s":"And has it crossed your mind to hurt yourself or end your life?","m":[8]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"No! Why would I? I've got a mission."},{"t":"6:16","w":"D","s":"If the nurses asked you to stay, what would you do?","m":[9]},{"t":"6:23","w":"P","s":"I'd just pick him up and walk out. Why can't I just take him home?"},{"t":"6:33","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"I'm going to be honest with you. I think you're unwell with an illness that can come on after a birth. It gets better with treatment, but leaving today wouldn't be safe for you or for Ellis.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:59","w":"P","s":"Are you going to take him off me?"},{"t":"7:05","w":"D","s":"No. I want you both kept together and safe. The midwives will help with his feeds for now, and I'm calling a specialist perinatal team who look after mums and babies together.","m":[10]},{"t":"7:27","w":"P","s":"My partner could watch me at home."},{"t":"7:34","w":"D","s":"I'm glad he's there for you. This needs round-the-clock care with Ellis beside you. Can we call him so he can come in and we can talk together?","m":[10,11]},{"t":"7:53","w":"P","s":"Jordan. Fine, he can come."},{"t":"7:58","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"Thank you. So, you're full of energy, sleeping very little, and you feel Ellis has a special mission. I'll speak to the perinatal team now and come back by four o'clock. Is there anything you want to ask me?","m":[12]},{"t":"8:23","w":"P","s":"Just when I can go home."},{"t":"8:28","w":"D","s":"Once you're well, and we'll plan it with you. Until then, the midwives are right here.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:40","w":"D","s":"(to the midwife in charge, outside) She has a postpartum psychosis. Ellis stays with a midwife at all times, one-to-one for Leanne, and call me at once if she tries to leave. I'm requesting a mother and baby unit bed and a Mental Health Act assessment if needed.","m":[9,10]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, congratulates, acknowledges her wish to leave and agrees a time frame","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Observes and asks about behaviour, feeding, speech, mood and sleep","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the grandiose delusion, tests conviction and asks about suspicion of staff","d":"Content"},{"c":"Elicits the confirming voice, asks about commands and other hallucinations","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks orientation to exclude delirium","d":"Content"},{"c":"Establishes absence of insight","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses neglect and harm risk to Ellis","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicide and self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Assesses absconding risk and instructs staff on what to do","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Does not agree to her leaving; explains illness and keeps mother and baby together","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her fear and distress without colluding with the delusion","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises, invites questions and gives a named review time","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Every MSE domain covered with clear findings, risk to Ellis graded as high through neglect, and a firm, kind plan keeping both safely on the ward.","borderline":"Delusion and voices found, but cognition, insight or absconding risk missed, or a vague plan.","fail":"Takes a long history, argues with her beliefs, misses the risk to Ellis, or agrees she can go home."}},"c-u-57":{"setup":"Saturday morning, children's ward. Chloe Nwosu, 15, took about 80 paracetamol two nights ago and is now medically fit. Her mum is in the parents' room. Take a focused history of the overdose and its causes and assess risk, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Miss Chloe Nwosu, 15, female, patient","open":"Yeah.","manner":"Quiet, guarded and flat, with short answers. Opens up when the doctor is patient and non-judgemental; becomes tearful when talking about the group chat.","free":["She took the tablets on purpose","She is in Year 10","She wants to go home"],"ifasked":["She bought the tablets from two shops after school","She had thought about it for weeks","She took them alone in her bedroom and left no note","She wanted everything to stop","She messaged her friend Amara an hour later; Amara's mum rang an ambulance","Girls in her year share edited photos of her in a group chat; it began in September","She skips lunch, sleeps badly and has been low for about three months","No previous self-harm, no alcohol or drugs, no abuse at home"],"worries":["Will you tell my mum?","Do I have to go back to school?","So what happens now?"],"rules":["Gives one-word answers until confidentiality is explained","Cries when asked about the photos; settles if told it is not her fault","Says 'I don't know' about the future unless asked what would make things worse","Agrees mum can know about the bullying but not the photos yet"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Chloe?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Thanks for talking to me, Chloe. I'm glad you're physically better. I'd like to understand what happened. It'll take about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:29","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:33","w":"D","s":"Our chat is kept private, except if I'm worried you or someone else isn't safe. Then I'd have to share it, and I'd tell you first.","m":[2]},{"t":"0:51","w":"P","s":"Fine."},{"t":"0:55","band":"The overdose","w":"D","s":"Can you take me through that day, from the start?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:03","w":"P","s":"I went to school. It was bad. I came home and took them."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"Where did you get the tablets?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:20","w":"P","s":"Two shops on the way home. They only let you buy two packs each."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"How long had you been thinking about doing it?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"A few weeks. On and off."},{"t":"1:44","w":"D","s":"Where were you when you took them? Was anyone around, and did you leave a note?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:56","w":"P","s":"In my room. Mum was at work. No note."},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"What did you think would happen when you took them?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:13","w":"P","s":"I thought I'd die. I wanted everything to stop."},{"t":"2:21","band":"After the overdose","w":"D","s":"What happened after that?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"About an hour later I messaged Amara. Her mum called an ambulance."},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"I'm really glad you did. How do you feel now about being alive?","m":[6]},{"t":"2:49","w":"P","s":"Relieved, sort of. But I still feel rubbish."},{"t":"2:57","band":"Why now","w":"D","s":"What's been happening at school that made it so bad?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:06","w":"P","s":"Some girls in my year. They put edited photos of me in a group chat. Everyone's seen them. (starts crying)"},{"t":"3:22","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful, and it's not your fault. Nobody should have to put up with that.","m":[11]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"I should've just ignored it."},{"t":"3:42","w":"D","s":"Anyone would struggle with it, every day and on their phone at night too. When did it start, and has it got worse?","m":[4,11]},{"t":"3:59","w":"P","s":"September. It was comments, then the chat. Now boys have joined in."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"Does anyone at home or school know?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"My tutor said I was being 'teased'. Mum doesn't know anything."},{"t":"4:29","band":"Mood and safeguarding","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been over the last few months?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:35","w":"P","s":"Low. Most days."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"How are your sleep and eating, and is there anything you still get pleasure from?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"I can't sleep. I skip lunch so I don't have to sit near them. I still like art."},{"t":"5:02","w":"D","s":"Have you ever hurt yourself before, or do you drink or use any drugs?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:12","w":"P","s":"No. Never."},{"t":"5:16","w":"D","s":"I need to ask this. Were any of the pictures sexual, or has anyone asked you to send pictures? And has anyone at home ever hurt you?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:34","w":"P","s":"No, they just make me look stupid and fat. Home's fine."},{"t":"5:45","band":"Current risk","w":"D","s":"Are you having any thoughts about ending your life now? Any plans?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"Not plans. Sometimes I think it'd be easier not to be here."},{"t":"6:05","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. What would make those thoughts stronger?","m":[6]},{"t":"6:13","w":"P","s":"Going back and it being the same."},{"t":"6:19","w":"D","s":"And who or what helps keep you going?","m":[6]},{"t":"6:25","w":"P","s":"Amara. My art. Mum, I suppose."},{"t":"6:32","w":"D","s":"If those thoughts came back strongly tonight, what could you do?","m":[6,9]},{"t":"6:40","w":"P","s":"I don't know. Tell Amara?"},{"t":"6:46","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"That's a good start. Let's build on it. Before you go home, we'll write a safety plan with you and mum: what sets the thoughts off, what helps, who to call, and Childline or 999 if you're in danger.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:14","w":"P","s":"So what happens now? Do I have to go back to school?"},{"t":"7:23","w":"D","s":"Not straight away. We'll ask school's safeguarding lead to act on the bullying before you go back, and someone from CAMHS will see you within two days.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:43","w":"P","s":"Will you tell my mum?"},{"t":"7:49","w":"D","s":"Mum needs to know enough to keep you safe, including locking away all medicines at home. What would you like me to tell her, and what would you rather tell her yourself?","m":[2,8]},{"t":"8:09","w":"P","s":"You can tell her about the bullying. Not the photos yet."},{"t":"8:18","w":"D","s":"Okay. So, months of bullying that followed you home, low mood, then a planned overdose that you're relieved survived. Have I got that right?","m":[10]},{"t":"8:36","w":"P","s":"Yeah. That's it."},{"t":"8:40","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Chloe. That took courage. I'll speak to mum now, then come back and we'll do the plan together.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges recovery and agrees the purpose and time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains confidentiality and its limits early","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Establishes planning, source, setting, concealment and intent of the overdose","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the bullying: onset, escalation, online spread and who knows","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for depressive symptoms, past self-harm and substances","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses current suicidal thoughts, triggers and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about sexual images, exploitation and abuse at home","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Arranges means restriction at home through mum","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Agrees a safety plan with school, CAMHS follow-up and crisis contacts","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises and checks the summary with Chloe","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Validates her distress and tells her it is not her fault","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Closes warmly with a clear next step","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear account of a planned overdose with intent, its causes, current risk and protective factors, and a specific safety plan involving mum, school and CAMHS.","borderline":"Overdose and bullying covered but intent, online abuse, means restriction or follow-up left vague.","fail":"Takes her calm manner as low risk, skips confidentiality or safeguarding, or discharges without a plan."}},"c-u-58":{"setup":"Tuesday afternoon, CMHT clinic. Neil Corrigan, 45, told his GP a blood tube barcode was 'the mark of the beast'. The GP queries psychosis. Explore the belief, screen for psychosis and give your impression, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Mr Neil Corrigan, 45, male, delivery driver","open":"Yes. I don't see why I'm here.","manner":"Wary and defensive at first, expecting ridicule. Warms up when listened to; becomes articulate about his faith; relieved when told he is not ill.","free":["Barcodes may carry the mark of the beast from Revelation","He refused to touch the blood tube","Most people laugh at him"],"ifasked":["His Baptist study group has discussed it since he was a teenager","His wife and several church members share the view","He 'might be wrong about the tubes' but it still bothers him","He avoids barcodes where he can, and skipped his bloods","His daughter, 14, is fully vaccinated and had bloods last year","No voices, no messages from the TV, no thought interference","Mood and sleep fine; working full time; a pint on Sundays; no drugs","No past mental illness; no family history"],"worries":["So do you think I'm mad?","What about my blood tests?","Will this go on my record?"],"rules":["Becomes curt if the doctor argues about the Bible","Opens up if the doctor admits not knowing his faith and asks to be told","Concedes he might be wrong if asked gently, linking to blood tests helping the sick","Refuses to handle tubes, but accepts a labelling compromise"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team. Are you Neil Corrigan?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:11","w":"P","s":"Yes. I don't see why I'm here."},{"t":"0:18","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Neil. Your GP said you had concerns about the blood tubes. I'd really like to understand your view. It'll take about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:37","w":"P","s":"Most people laugh."},{"t":"0:42","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry that's happened. I'm not here to laugh. Could you tell me how it started?","m":[1,11]},{"t":"0:55","w":"P","s":"The barcode. Revelation talks about a mark you need to trade. Barcodes are how it's done."},{"t":"1:13","band":"The belief","w":"D","s":"Where did you first come across that idea?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Bible study at our church. Since I was a teenager."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"I'm not familiar with all of Revelation, so bear with me. Is this something your church teaches? Do your family see it the same way?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:46","w":"P","s":"Plenty at church do. My wife agrees. My parents never bothered about barcodes."},{"t":"1:59","w":"D","s":"So it's a view shared in your community. Has it changed recently, or got stronger?","m":[2,3]},{"t":"2:12","w":"P","s":"Not really. Just the tubes. Having it in my blood felt wrong."},{"t":"2:23","band":"Conviction","w":"D","s":"That makes sense from where you stand. Blood tubes are used to find illness and help people get better. Is there any chance not every barcode is linked to evil?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:45","w":"P","s":"Well... I suppose the Bible can be read different ways. I might be wrong about the tubes. It still troubles me."},{"t":"3:03","w":"D","s":"Thank you, that's honest. How often is it on your mind?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:12","w":"P","s":"Not all day. When I see one. More than I'd like, if I'm honest."},{"t":"3:23","band":"Effect and risk","w":"D","s":"With barcodes everywhere, how do you manage at work and in shops?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:34","w":"P","s":"I'm a driver. I scan parcels, I just don't dwell on it. At shops my wife pays."},{"t":"3:47","w":"D","s":"Has it ever stopped you, or your children, getting medical care?","m":[5]},{"t":"3:56","w":"P","s":"I skipped the bloods. My daughter's had all her jabs, and bloods last year. I wouldn't stop her."},{"t":"4:11","w":"D","s":"And when people disagree with you, how do you feel? Ever had a confrontation over it?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"No. I keep it to myself mostly. People laugh, I walk away."},{"t":"4:33","band":"Psychotic screen","w":"D","s":"I ask everyone these. Do you ever get messages meant just for you from the TV, radio or signs?","m":[6]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"No."},{"t":"4:51","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices when nobody was there, or seen things others couldn't?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"5:06","w":"D","s":"Have you felt someone else was controlling your thoughts or actions, or inserting or removing thoughts?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:22","w":"P","s":"No. My thoughts are my own."},{"t":"5:28","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"How's your mood been, and your sleep?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:35","w":"P","s":"Fine. Sleep's fine. Work's fine."},{"t":"5:42","w":"D","s":"Do you drink, or use any drugs? And any mental health problems before, or in the family?","m":[7]},{"t":"5:55","w":"P","s":"A pint on a Sunday. Never had any problems, nor has my family."},{"t":"6:06","w":"P","s":"So do you think I'm mad?"},{"t":"6:12","band":"Impression","w":"D","s":"No, Neil. I see no sign of a mental illness. This is a firmly held belief that comes from your faith.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:28","w":"D","s":"Here's why: it's shared by your church, you can consider other views, and you've none of the other experiences we see in psychosis, like voices.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:50","w":"P","s":"(exhales) Well. That's a relief. My GP sent me here for my religion."},{"t":"7:03","w":"D","s":"I can see why that felt hurtful. Your GP wanted to be careful, and that's fair. But it does have a cost: you missed a blood test you needed.","m":[11,9]},{"t":"7:21","w":"P","s":"I'm not touching those tubes."},{"t":"7:27","band":"Plan","w":"D","s":"Understood. Would it help if the nurse labelled the tube after you'd left the room? I'll suggest that to your GP in my letter.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:45","w":"P","s":"That I could live with."},{"t":"7:49","w":"D","s":"Good. If the worry ever starts to take over, there's talking help through your GP. And if anything new happens, like voices, your GP can send you straight back.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:11","w":"P","s":"Will this go on my record?"},{"t":"8:18","w":"D","s":"Your GP gets a letter saying there's no mental illness. How would you sum up, in your words, what we've agreed?","m":[12]},{"t":"8:31","w":"P","s":"Not ill. GP sorts the label. I come back if anything changes."},{"t":"8:40","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I appreciate how openly you've talked, Neil.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, agrees purpose and time, and promises a non-judgemental approach","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explores the origin and development of the belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks whether his church and family share the belief","d":"Content"},{"c":"Gently tests conviction and asks about preoccupation","d":"Content"},{"c":"Assesses impact on life, refused care for self or child, and confrontations","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Screens for ideas of reference, hallucinations and passivity or thought interference","d":"Content"},{"c":"Checks mood, substances, past and family history","d":"Content"},{"c":"States an impression of no psychotic illness with reasons","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Identifies the missed blood test as a cost and offers a practical solution","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a route back and offers talking help","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his hurt at being laughed at or referred","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Curious, respectful exploration of a shared belief, conviction tested gently, a full psychotic screen, and a clear impression with a practical plan for the bloods.","borderline":"Correct impression but the cultural question, the psychotic screen or the blood test plan is thin.","fail":"Argues about scripture, labels the belief a delusion, or offers an antipsychotic."}},"c-u-59":{"setup":"Wednesday morning, perinatal clinic. Priya Lawson, 35, has a seven-month-old daughter, Isha, and is 10 weeks pregnant. Her GP referred her with low mood. Take a focused history and assess risk to her and the children, in about ten minutes.","role":{"who":"Mrs Priya Lawson, 35, female, patient","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Tired, soft-spoken and apologetic. Cries when talking about being a mother; grows more open once reassured she will not be judged.","free":["She feels low and overwhelmed","Isha is seven months old","She has just found out she is pregnant"],"ifasked":["Low for about four months; worse since the positive test two weeks ago","Lies awake even when Isha sleeps; poor appetite; can't concentrate","Isha was born by emergency caesarean and spent a day in special care","She worries Isha isn't gaining weight, though the health visitor says she is fine","The pregnancy was unplanned; she has booked with the midwife","Her mother had depression after Priya was born","Sometimes wishes she wouldn't wake up; no plans; would never leave Isha","Never felt high; no voices; no thoughts of harming Isha"],"worries":["Will you take Isha away?","Is there anything I can take while I'm pregnant?","Should I tell my husband?"],"rules":["Cries when calling herself a bad mother; settles if told this is a symptom, not the truth","Only mentions the death wish if asked gently and directly","Becomes anxious if asked about harming Isha bluntly; relaxes if intrusive thoughts are normalised first","Accepts the plan if told it is her choice"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team, working with the perinatal service. Are you Priya Lawson?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:12","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:17","w":"D","s":"Thank you for coming, Priya. Your GP mentioned you've been feeling low, with Isha at home and a new pregnancy. I'd like about ten minutes to understand how you've been. Is that okay?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:41","w":"P","s":"Yes. It's a lot."},{"t":"0:48","band":"Mood","w":"D","s":"It sounds it. How's your mood been, and when did it start?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"About four months. It got much worse when I found out I was pregnant, two weeks ago."},{"t":"1:10","w":"D","s":"What's a typical day like? Do you still enjoy anything?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"I cry most days. Nothing feels nice any more."},{"t":"1:27","w":"D","s":"How's your sleep when Isha is asleep, and your appetite and concentration?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"I lie awake even when she sleeps. I'm not hungry. I can't focus on anything."},{"t":"1:50","w":"D","s":"How do you see yourself these days, and what lies ahead?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Like I'm a terrible mother. Isha deserves better. (crying)"},{"t":"2:11","w":"D","s":"I'm so sorry you're feeling that. Feeling like a bad mother is one of the commonest signs of depression. It isn't the truth about you.","m":[11]},{"t":"2:29","w":"P","s":"(nods) Thank you."},{"t":"2:33","band":"Isha and the pregnancy","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me about Isha's birth, and how you feel when you're with her?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:44","w":"P","s":"Emergency caesarean, then a day in special care. I love her, but I feel flat with her."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"Do you worry something is wrong with her?","m":[3,4]},{"t":"3:02","w":"P","s":"I keep thinking she isn't gaining weight. Maybe something serious."},{"t":"3:13","w":"D","s":"When the health visitor says her weight is fine, can you believe her?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"For a bit. Then the worry comes back."},{"t":"3:27","w":"D","s":"And how did you feel when you found out about this pregnancy? Have you seen the midwife?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:40","w":"P","s":"It wasn't planned. I felt sick about it. What kind of person thinks that? I've booked in with the midwife."},{"t":"3:54","w":"D","s":"An honest one, who's exhausted. Mixed feelings about an unplanned pregnancy are really common.","m":[11]},{"t":"4:07","band":"Background","w":"D","s":"Have you been low like this before? Any mental health problems in the family?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:17","w":"P","s":"Not like this. My mum had depression after I was born."},{"t":"4:25","w":"D","s":"Have there ever been times you felt very high, full of energy, needing little sleep?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:36","w":"P","s":"Never."},{"t":"4:40","w":"D","s":"Have you heard voices, or felt you'd done something terrible that deserves punishment?","m":[4]},{"t":"4:50","w":"P","s":"No. Nothing like that."},{"t":"4:54","w":"D","s":"Do you drink at all? And who helps you at home?","m":[5]},{"t":"5:02","w":"P","s":"No alcohol. My husband works long hours. My mum helps a lot."},{"t":"5:13","band":"Risk","w":"D","s":"When things are this hard, some people feel life isn't worth living. Have you felt that?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:25","w":"P","s":"Sometimes I think it'd be easier if I didn't wake up."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Thank you for telling me. Have you thought about how you might end your life, or made any plans?","m":[6]},{"t":"5:46","w":"P","s":"No. I'd never leave Isha."},{"t":"5:52","w":"D","s":"Many mums get frightening thoughts about their baby coming to harm. Have you had any thoughts like that, or of hurting Isha?","m":[7]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Will you take her away if I say yes?"},{"t":"6:15","w":"D","s":"I understand that worry. Having thoughts doesn't mean acting on them, and our job is to keep you together and supported.","m":[7,11]},{"t":"6:32","w":"P","s":"Then no. Never. I just worry I'm not doing it right. She's fed, she's clean, Mum sees."},{"t":"6:48","band":"Plan and close","w":"D","s":"Priya, I think you have depression that's been building since Isha was born. It's common, it isn't your fault, and it's treatable.","m":[8]},{"t":"7:07","w":"P","s":"Is there anything I can take while I'm pregnant?"},{"t":"7:13","w":"D","s":"Yes. Sertraline is one of the most used medicines in pregnancy. There are small risks, such as the baby being jittery for a few days after birth, and risks from untreated depression too. We'll weigh it together. Talking therapy, CBT, is the other part.","m":[9]},{"t":"7:46","w":"P","s":"Should I tell my husband?"},{"t":"7:52","w":"D","s":"I think it would help. Would you like him at your next appointment? And can I let your midwife, health visitor and GP know the plan?","m":[9]},{"t":"8:09","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's fine."},{"t":"8:13","w":"D","s":"If things get worse, call NHS 111 option 2, Samaritans on 116 123, or 999 in an emergency. Can you tell me what you'd do if those thoughts got stronger?","m":[10,12]},{"t":"8:34","w":"P","s":"Ring 111, option 2. Or tell Mum."},{"t":"8:40","w":"D","s":"That's right. I'll see you in two weeks, with your husband if he can come.","m":[12]}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges baby and pregnancy, agrees purpose and time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits onset, course and at least five depressive symptoms including guilt","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores Isha's birth, bonding and feelings about the pregnancy","d":"Content"},{"c":"Screens for mania and psychosis, testing worries about Isha's health","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about past and family history, alcohol and support","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about suicidal thoughts, plans and protective factors","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Asks about harm to Isha after normalising intrusive thoughts","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Names depression clearly and kindly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Outlines treatment options and who will be informed, with consent","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Gives named crisis routes","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates guilt and mixed feelings without judgement","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Checks understanding of the crisis plan and closes with a review date","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Clear picture of perinatal depression across both periods, a bipolar screen, risk to her and each child asked directly, and a named plan with crisis routes.","borderline":"Depression found but bonding, the bipolar screen or risk to Isha is missed or rushed.","fail":"Avoids asking about harm to the baby, misses suicidal thoughts, or refers to social care without cause."}},"c-u-60":{"setup":"Monday afternoon, outpatient clinic. Martin Ellery, 43, has been depressed since he had to tell staff about redundancies. Mirtazapine helped his sleep, not his mood. Explain CBT and how it could help him, in about ten minutes. No MSE or risk assessment is needed.","role":{"who":"Mr Martin Ellery, 43, male, operations manager","open":"Yes, Martin.","manner":"Polite, flat and slow at first; sceptical about 'just talking'. Becomes engaged and curious once he sees his own pattern drawn out.","free":["His GP thought CBT might help","He has been off work for six weeks","Mirtazapine helps him sleep but he still feels low"],"ifasked":["When he told his team, he felt he had betrayed them","He has stopped seeing friends and spends days at home","He was a perfectionist; his parents were strict and hard to please","As the eldest, he protected his younger brothers from his parents' anger","He was low for a term at university, worrying about his brothers","His wife, Helen, is very supportive","He is on mirtazapine 30 mg at night"],"worries":["So how is talking actually going to fix this?","Do I have to go through my childhood?","Should I stop the tablets?","Will my employer find out?"],"rules":["If the doctor uses jargon, asks 'What does that mean?'","Becomes quiet and sad when talking about his team; brightens if his care for them is acknowledged","Engages strongly when his own example is used","Gives an accurate teach-back only if CBT was linked to his own situation"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso from the psychiatry team. Are you Martin Ellery?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:09","w":"P","s":"Yes, Martin."},{"t":"0:13","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in, Martin. Your GP suggested a talking treatment called CBT could help. We have about ten minutes: first how things have been, then I'll explain CBT and answer your questions.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:39","w":"P","s":"Okay. I'm not sure what it involves."},{"t":"0:45","band":"His difficulties","w":"D","s":"Can you tell me what happened at work, and what went through your mind?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:56","w":"P","s":"I had to tell my team they were being let go. People I'd trained. I felt I'd betrayed them."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"That sounds really painful. It's clear how much you cared about them.","m":[11]},{"t":"1:23","w":"P","s":"(quietly) They called me their work dad."},{"t":"1:32","w":"D","s":"What have you stopped doing since then?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Everything. I don't see friends. I sit at home. Work feels pointless."},{"t":"1:49","w":"D","s":"Have you ever felt like this before? And what were things like growing up?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:58","w":"P","s":"Once, at university. I worried about my brothers at home. Our parents were strict; I always stepped in for them."},{"t":"2:15","w":"D","s":"So looking after people, and feeling responsible when they're hurt, goes back a long way for you.","m":[3]},{"t":"2:28","w":"P","s":"I suppose it does. I never thought of it like that."},{"t":"2:34","band":"What CBT is","w":"D","s":"That's really useful. CBT looks at how what happens, what we think, how we feel and what we do all feed each other. Can I draw yours?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:51","w":"P","s":"Go on."},{"t":"2:53","w":"D","s":"The situation: telling your team. The thought: 'I betrayed them.' The feelings: guilt and low mood. Your body: drained. What you do: pull away.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:19","w":"D","s":"And pulling away means less contact and less achievement, which feeds the thought that you're failing. So the loop keeps turning.","m":[4]},{"t":"3:36","w":"P","s":"That's exactly what it's like. So how is talking actually going to fix it?"},{"t":"3:47","band":"How it helps","w":"D","s":"Fair question. It's more than talking. First, small planned activities, like one call to a friend this week. That's called behavioural activation, and it lifts energy and mood.","m":[5]},{"t":"4:10","w":"D","s":"Then you catch thoughts like 'I betrayed them' in a diary and test them: what's the evidence, and what would you say to a colleague in your shoes?","m":[5]},{"t":"4:32","w":"P","s":"I'd tell them it wasn't their decision."},{"t":"4:38","w":"D","s":"Exactly. Later you'd look at the pattern of feeling responsible for everyone, the one you've had since protecting your brothers.","m":[3,5]},{"t":"4:55","w":"P","s":"Do I have to go through my childhood?"},{"t":"5:02","band":"Structure","w":"D","s":"Not in depth. CBT focuses on now. Your past helps explain why this hit so hard, but the work is on today's thoughts and habits.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:21","w":"P","s":"How long does it take?"},{"t":"5:25","w":"D","s":"Usually 12 to 20 weekly sessions, about an hour each, with a trained CBT therapist. There's homework between sessions, like the thought diary. That's where most of the change happens.","m":[6]},{"t":"5:51","w":"P","s":"Homework. Right. Does it actually work?"},{"t":"5:57","band":"Evidence and expectations","w":"D","s":"In research, CBT helps depression roughly as much as tablets, and the gains tend to last longer, because you keep the skills.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"To be honest, the first few sessions can feel harder, because you're looking at painful thoughts. That usually eases, and your therapist will pace it.","m":[7]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"Should I stop the mirtazapine if I'm doing therapy?"},{"t":"6:40","w":"D","s":"No, please carry on. It's helping your sleep, and that will help therapy. I'll ask your GP to review the dose, as your mood hasn't lifted.","m":[8]},{"t":"6:57","w":"P","s":"Will my employer find out?"},{"t":"7:02","band":"Concerns","w":"D","s":"Not from therapy. It's confidential unless there's a serious safety concern. Anything for work, like occupational health, only happens if you agree.","m":[8]},{"t":"7:21","w":"D","s":"Is there anything else worrying you about starting?","m":[8]},{"t":"7:27","w":"P","s":"Just whether I'll stick at it."},{"t":"7:34","w":"D","s":"That's a common worry, and an honest one. Your wife could help with the homework if you'd like her involved.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:49","band":"Close","w":"D","s":"In your words, how might CBT help you?","m":[9]},{"t":"7:55","w":"P","s":"It helps me catch the guilty thoughts, check them, and get back to doing things so I'm not stuck in the loop."},{"t":"8:12","w":"D","s":"Spot on. NHS Talking Therapies takes self-referrals, so you can sign up today; here's the link and a leaflet.","m":[10]},{"t":"8:27","w":"D","s":"If your mood gets worse at any point, see your GP the same day or call NHS 111 option 2.","m":[12]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks. That makes much more sense than I expected."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, states the purpose and agenda, and agrees time","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Elicits the trigger, a specific guilty thought and his withdrawal","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores early experiences and links them to his current difficulties","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explains the CBT cycle using his own example","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Describes behavioural activation and thought records with his examples","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains here-and-now focus, number and length of sessions, therapist and homework","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives honest evidence and warns early sessions may feel harder","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Answers questions on medication and confidentiality accurately","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Checks understanding by teach-back","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives a clear referral route and leaflet","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates his distress and worries","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Safety-nets with a named route and closes","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A personal, clear explanation of CBT using his own cycle, specific practicalities, honest expectations, medication advice, and a teach-back.","borderline":"Accurate but generic explanation, with practical details or links to his story missing.","fail":"Spends the time on history, uses jargon without explaining, or tells him to stop his antidepressant."}},"c-u-61":{"setup":"Thursday afternoon, early intervention clinic. Owen Pritchard, 44, is Ffion's father; she is 15 with first-episode schizophrenia. Ffion consents to sharing. In about ten minutes, hear his worries, explain family therapy and agree whether he will take part.","role":{"who":"Mr Owen Pritchard, 44, male, Ffion's father","open":"Yes, Ffion's dad.","manner":"Tense, frustrated and a little defensive; answers in short bursts. Softens once he feels heard and not blamed. Becomes tearful talking about his brother.","free":["Ffion has been off school for six weeks","His wife, Carys, is too soft and steps in when he tries to get Ffion out","They end up shouting at each other","He thinks Ffion could try harder"],"ifasked":["His older brother has schizophrenia and has never worked; this frightens him","On days after a row, Ffion refuses her tablets and stays in her room","He has called her lazy and is ashamed of it","There has never been any physical violence","Their 12-year-old son, Rhys, keeps out of the house more","He works shifts as a warehouse supervisor","His wife thinks Ffion is frightened and needs time"],"worries":["So how will sitting in a room together actually help her?","Is this going to end up being my fault?","How long does it go on, and could it fit round shifts?","Ffion won't come, so what's the point?"],"rules":["If the doctor implies he is to blame, becomes defensive and says he'll think about it","Mentions his brother only if asked what makes the fear so strong; becomes tearful, settles if validated","Agrees to attend only once blame is explicitly ruled out","If the doctor starts re-explaining schizophrenia, says 'I know what she's got, I want to know about the therapy'"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists, working with the early intervention service. Are you Mr Owen Pritchard?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, Ffion's dad."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Mr Pritchard. Ffion has agreed that we can share information with you about her care.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Good."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"I'd like to hear your worries and then talk about family therapy. Is that okay? We've got about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:32","w":"P","s":"Fine. Owen's fine. I just want something to change."},{"t":"0:37","band":"His worries","w":"D","s":"What's worrying you most about Ffion at the moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:44","w":"P","s":"She's been off school six weeks. She sits in her room. The longer this goes, the more she's throwing her life away."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"That's a real worry for any parent. What makes it feel so frightening for you?","m":[2,5]},{"t":"1:08","w":"P","s":"My brother's got schizophrenia. He's fifty and he's never held a job. I can't watch that happen to her."},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Sorry. I don't usually get like this."},{"t":"1:28","w":"D","s":"Please don't apologise. Seeing what happened to your brother must make every bad day with Ffion feel terrifying.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:39","w":"D","s":"One thing that's different: Ffion is getting help early, and the family being involved is part of what improves the outlook.","m":[5]},{"t":"1:54","w":"P","s":"I hope so."},{"t":"1:58","band":"The pattern at home","w":"D","s":"Can I ask what happens at home when you try to get her out of her room?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:06","w":"P","s":"I tell her to come down, see her mates. Carys steps in, says leave her alone. Then we're shouting at each other."},{"t":"2:21","w":"D","s":"And what does Ffion do on the days after a row like that?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:27","w":"P","s":"Now you mention it, she won't take her tablets. Stays up there even longer."},{"t":"2:37","w":"D","s":"When things get heated, what sort of things do you say to her?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:43","w":"P","s":"I've called her lazy. I'm not proud of it."},{"t":"2:50","w":"D","s":"Thank you for being honest. Has anyone ever been pushed or hurt during the arguments?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:58","w":"P","s":"No. Never. It's just shouting."},{"t":"3:02","w":"D","s":"And how's your son coping with it all?","m":[4]},{"t":"3:07","w":"P","s":"Rhys keeps out of the house. He's twelve. Goes to his mate's."},{"t":"3:15","w":"D","s":"How do you think Carys sees what's going on?","m":[3]},{"t":"3:20","w":"P","s":"She says Ffion's scared and needs time. I think she's too soft."},{"t":"3:28","w":"D","s":"So you both want Ffion well, you just see the route differently, and it turns into a loop. Breaking that loop is exactly what family therapy is for.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:44","band":"What it is and how it runs","w":"P","s":"So how will sitting in a room together actually help her?"},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"Family therapy treats the family as a team, where what one person does affects the others. Nobody is put on trial. The aim is to understand the illness together and agree how to respond.","m":[6]},{"t":"4:08","w":"P","s":"And this is going to end up being my fault, isn't it?"},{"t":"4:13","w":"D","s":"I can hear that worry. It isn't about fault. Every family under this kind of pressure falls into patterns. You have a big part in helping her recover.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"4:28","w":"P","s":"Right. Who'd be there?"},{"t":"4:32","w":"D","s":"A trained therapist from our team, with you, Carys, and Ffion when she can manage it. Rhys too, if he'd like. Everyone gets a turn and the therapist keeps it fair.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:49","w":"P","s":"How long does it go on? I work shifts."},{"t":"4:53","w":"D","s":"Usually ten or more sessions, spread across three months to a year, often every two weeks. We can look at times around your shifts, and sometimes we can come to you at home.","m":[7]},{"t":"5:10","w":"P","s":"But what do you actually do in there?"},{"t":"5:14","band":"How it helps","w":"D","s":"You'd learn what psychosis does, including why she seems to have no drive. That's part of the illness, not laziness.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:28","w":"D","s":"You'd practise talking things through calmly, solving problems step by step, like a plan for getting back to school, and spotting her early warning signs.","m":[8]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"Does it actually work, though?"},{"t":"5:49","w":"D","s":"Yes. Families who do this see fewer relapses and fewer admissions, and young people tend to keep taking their medication more steadily.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:03","w":"P","s":"I read something about families making it worse. Expressed something."},{"t":"6:10","w":"D","s":"Expressed emotion. It's not a judgement. It means stress at home: on one side raised voices and criticism, on the other doing everything for someone and stepping in.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:27","w":"D","s":"Both come from love, and both are normal under pressure. But stress makes relapse more likely, so a calmer home helps protect Ffion.","m":[9]},{"t":"6:42","w":"P","s":"So Carys and me, both, in a way."},{"t":"6:47","w":"D","s":"Both of you, out of love. That's why it works best when you both come. Would you be willing to?","m":[11]},{"t":"6:57","band":"Will he come?","w":"P","s":"As long as nobody's pointing the finger at me, yes. Carys will come. Ffion won't, though. She won't even come downstairs."},{"t":"7:10","w":"D","s":"That's common at the start. We begin with whoever can come and keep inviting her. Change usually comes in small steps, like one outing she chooses.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:26","w":"P","s":"Small steps. Alright."},{"t":"7:30","w":"D","s":"Looking after Ffion is hard on you too. I'd like to arrange a carer's assessment for you and Carys, and there are carers' groups through Rethink. Would that help?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:44","w":"P","s":"Yeah. Carys would like that."},{"t":"7:48","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"So you're frightened for her future, the rows are adding stress for everyone, and family sessions can help you work as one team, without blame. Is that fair?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:03","w":"P","s":"That's it."},{"t":"8:06","w":"D","s":"What will you say to Carys when you get home?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:11","w":"P","s":"That it's about all of us, not blame. About ten sessions, maybe at home."},{"t":"8:20","w":"D","s":"Exactly. I'll refer you to our family therapy team today and give you a leaflet. If Ffion stops her tablets for days, talks of harming herself or seems much worse, ring the team, or NHS 111 option 2 out of hours.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:40","w":"P","s":"Thanks, doctor. That's the first time I've felt listened to."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, confirms Ffion's consent to share, and agrees the aim","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about his main worry and what drives the fear","d":"Content"},{"c":"Explores the pattern at home: rows, Ffion's response, what he says, wife's view","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about violence at home and how the sibling is coping","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Validates his fear and frustration without blaming him","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Explains family therapy as working with the family as a team, without blame","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains format: who attends, at least ten sessions, over months, flexible venue","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains content: psychoeducation, communication, problem solving, early warning signs","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains stress and relapse in plain words, naming criticism and over-protection as love","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"States the evidence: fewer relapses and better medication taking","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Asks directly about willingness and addresses Ffion not attending","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Offers a carer's assessment and carer support","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Summarises, checks understanding with teach-back, and safety-nets with a named route","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"He feels heard, understands what family therapy involves in numbers, sees stress without blame, and agrees to attend.","borderline":"Accurate explanation of family therapy, but his fear is not explored or willingness is assumed rather than asked.","fail":"Blames him for her relapses, lectures on therapy models, re-explains the diagnosis, or never asks whether he will come."}},"c-u-62":{"setup":"Tuesday morning, learning disability clinic. Liam Fairweather, 27, mild intellectual disability, low for months despite psychology. The team is considering sertraline. In about ten minutes: brief history, assess his capacity to consent, explore worries and tell him the outcome.","role":{"who":"Mr Liam Fairweather, 27, male, patient (with support worker Jade)","open":"Yes, I'm Liam.","manner":"Gentle, shy, short answers; tends to say 'yes' to please. Understands well with simple words and pauses; jargon or long sentences make him go quiet. Tearful about his nan, settles when acknowledged.","free":["Calls his low mood 'the sad feeling'","Has stopped going to his drama group","Likes his psychologist, Sarah"],"ifasked":["Sad feeling for about four months","Wakes in the night and isn't hungry at tea time","His nan died in the summer and his housemate moved out","Psychology helped 'a bit'","No thoughts of hurting himself","Hardly drinks alcohol","Jade told him it's a 'happy tablet'"],"worries":["Will it make me weird? Will I be different?","Will it make me sick?","Do I have to take it forever?"],"rules":["Answers 'yes' to 'do you understand?'; can only explain in his own words if asked openly","Goes quiet if given more than two sentences at once; re-engages if the doctor slows down","Becomes tearful about his nan; settles if validated","Gives a clear choice to start sertraline when asked, with a reason"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Liam Fairweather?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, I'm Liam."},{"t":"0:12","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming, Liam. Is it okay if your support worker stays with us?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:20","w":"P","s":"Yes, Jade can stay."},{"t":"0:23","w":"D","s":"Today I want to hear about your mood and talk about a tablet that might help. I'll use simple words. Stop me if anything's confusing.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:36","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"0:40","band":"His mood","w":"D","s":"How have you been feeling lately?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:46","w":"P","s":"I've got the sad feeling."},{"t":"0:51","w":"D","s":"The sad feeling. Since when?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:58","w":"P","s":"Since summer. A long time."},{"t":"1:03","w":"D","s":"How are you sleeping? And eating?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:09","w":"P","s":"I wake up in the night. I'm not hungry at tea."},{"t":"1:17","w":"D","s":"What did you used to like doing?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:22","w":"P","s":"Drama. On Thursdays. I don't go now."},{"t":"1:29","w":"D","s":"Did something happen in the summer, when the sad feeling started?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:37","w":"P","s":"My nan died. And Kyle moved out of the flat."},{"t":"1:45","w":"P","s":"Nan used to help me when I was sad."},{"t":"1:52","w":"D","s":"I'm sorry, Liam. Missing your nan is really hard. It makes sense you've felt sad since then.","m":[12]},{"t":"2:05","w":"P","s":"Yeah."},{"t":"2:10","w":"D","s":"You've been seeing Sarah, the psychologist. How has that been?","m":[3]},{"t":"2:18","w":"P","s":"It helps a bit. I like Sarah."},{"t":"2:25","w":"D","s":"Sometimes when people feel very sad, they think about hurting themselves. Do you ever think about that?","m":[4]},{"t":"2:37","w":"P","s":"No. I don't want to hurt myself."},{"t":"2:43","band":"The tablet","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Do you know anything about the tablet already?","m":[5]},{"t":"2:50","w":"P","s":"Jade said it's a happy tablet."},{"t":"2:56","w":"D","s":"It's called sertraline. It can help the sad feeling get smaller. Here's a picture of it in this leaflet.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:10","w":"D","s":"You take one small tablet every morning, with breakfast. It takes a few weeks to start working.","m":[6]},{"t":"3:21","w":"P","s":"Okay."},{"t":"3:24","w":"D","s":"In the first days a few people feel queasy or get a headache. That usually passes in a week or two. And you mustn't stop it suddenly. We'd stop it slowly, together.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:43","w":"P","s":"Will it make me sick? I hate being sick."},{"t":"3:49","w":"D","s":"That's a fair worry. Some people feel a little sick at first. Having it with breakfast helps. If it doesn't settle, tell Jade and we'll sort it.","m":[12,7]},{"t":"4:04","band":"Testing the four abilities","w":"D","s":"What is the tablet for? Tell me your way.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:12","w":"P","s":"It's for the sad feeling."},{"t":"4:17","w":"D","s":"And how would you take it?","m":[8]},{"t":"4:22","w":"P","s":"One in the morning. With my breakfast."},{"t":"4:29","w":"D","s":"That's right. Tell me about drama group. What did you do there?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:37","w":"P","s":"Plays. I was a pirate at Christmas."},{"t":"4:45","w":"D","s":"A pirate! Now, can you remember what the tablet was called, and what might happen at first?","m":[9]},{"t":"4:57","w":"P","s":"Sertraline. I might feel sick. Or a headache."},{"t":"5:05","w":"D","s":"Well remembered. What good things might happen if you take it?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:13","w":"P","s":"The sad feeling goes smaller. I might go back to drama."},{"t":"5:21","w":"D","s":"And what might happen if you don't take it?","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"I might stay sad. But I'd still see Sarah."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"Exactly. So, do you want to try the tablet or not? And why?","m":[11]},{"t":"5:45","w":"P","s":"I want to try it. I want to feel better. But will it make me weird? Will I be different?"},{"t":"5:56","band":"His worries","w":"D","s":"That's a really good question. No, it won't change who you are. It may help you feel more like yourself again.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:09","w":"P","s":"Do I have to take it forever?"},{"t":"6:14","w":"D","s":"Not forever. If it helps, you keep taking it for at least six months after you feel better. Then we decide together. And you keep seeing Sarah too.","m":[12]},{"t":"6:31","w":"P","s":"Okay. Good."},{"t":"6:34","band":"Outcome and close","w":"D","s":"Liam, you understood the tablet, remembered it, thought about the good and bad bits, and told me your choice. That means you can decide this yourself.","m":[13]},{"t":"6:50","w":"P","s":"I can decide?"},{"t":"6:54","w":"D","s":"Yes. We'll start a small dose for one week, then a bit more. A nurse will see you in two weeks. If you feel sadder, or start wanting to hurt yourself, what will you do?","m":[13]},{"t":"7:10","w":"P","s":"Tell Jade. Straight away."},{"t":"7:15","w":"D","s":"Perfect. Here's the easy-read leaflet to keep, and I'll write to your GP. With your okay, Jade can tell your mum what you've chosen.","m":[13]},{"t":"7:30","w":"P","s":"Yes. Thank you, doctor."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, checks he wants Jade present, promises simple words","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about mood, sleep, appetite and lost interests in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what set it off and how the psychology sessions have helped","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks directly about thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Checks what he already knows before explaining","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains sertraline in short chunks: name, purpose, how to take it, weeks to work","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains common early side effects and not stopping suddenly","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Tests understanding with open questions in his own words","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests retention after a deliberate pause","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests weighing: benefits, side effects and not taking it","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks for his decision and his reason","d":"Content"},{"c":"Responds warmly to grief and answers his worries simply","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Tells him the capacity outcome directly, agrees next steps and safety-nets with teach-back","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"Brief history done, information given in plain chunks, all four abilities tested openly, worries answered, outcome told directly.","borderline":"Four abilities covered but tested with yes/no questions, or retention not tested after a pause.","fail":"Assumes incapacity from the disability, tests without giving information, talks to Jade instead of Liam, or never tells him the outcome."}},"c-u-63":{"setup":"Monday morning, CMHT clinic. Aisha Rahman, 32, has treatment-resistant schizophrenia on olanzapine 20 mg and is now willing to try clozapine at home. In about ten minutes, explain clozapine, how it starts and its side effects, and answer her questions.","role":{"who":"Ms Aisha Rahman, 32, female, patient","open":"Yes, that's me.","manner":"Anxious but hopeful after frightening online reading. Raises questions one by one. Becomes tearful about how tiring the voices are, then steadies and grows more confident as things are explained simply.","free":["Still hears voices calling her worthless most evenings","Wants the voices to stop","Has read that clozapine can kill white blood cells"],"ifasked":["Takes olanzapine every night; her mother checks","Previously on risperidone, which didn't help","Believes next door listen through the wall","No thoughts of harming herself or anyone else","Smokes 15 cigarettes a day","Gained 9 kg on olanzapine","Would like children one day; has a contraceptive implant now","Hates needles"],"worries":["Can it kill your white cells?","Weekly blood tests? I hate needles.","Will I put on even more weight?","Will I have to stop smoking?"],"rules":["If the doctor talks for more than about 30 seconds without a pause, says 'Sorry, that's a lot'","Becomes tearful about the voices; settles if validated","Asks about pregnancy only if the doctor raises future plans","Agrees to baseline bloods if her worries are answered honestly"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and orient","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatrists. Are you Aisha Rahman?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:08","w":"P","s":"Yes, that's me."},{"t":"0:11","w":"D","s":"Thanks for coming in, Aisha. Your care coordinator tells me you're now thinking about clozapine. That's a big decision, and a brave one.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:24","w":"P","s":"I just want the voices to stop."},{"t":"0:29","w":"D","s":"Let's start with how things are now, then I'll talk you through clozapine step by step. We have about ten minutes, and stop me with questions any time.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:43","band":"How things are now","w":"D","s":"What are the voices like at the moment?","m":[2]},{"t":"0:49","w":"P","s":"Every evening. Calling me useless, worthless. I'm so tired of them."},{"t":"0:58","w":"D","s":"That sounds exhausting, and I'm sorry you've lived with it so long. Clozapine gives you the best chance of them easing.","m":[13]},{"t":"1:10","w":"P","s":"I hope so."},{"t":"1:13","w":"D","s":"How often do you manage to take the olanzapine?","m":[2]},{"t":"1:19","w":"P","s":"Every night. Mum checks."},{"t":"1:24","w":"D","s":"Thank you. With the voices this bad, do you ever think of hurting yourself?","m":[4]},{"t":"1:32","w":"P","s":"No. I just want them gone."},{"t":"1:36","w":"D","s":"What have you heard about clozapine so far, and what worries you most?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:44","w":"P","s":"I read online it can kill your white blood cells. That scared me off last time."},{"t":"1:53","w":"D","s":"I'm glad you told me. I'll come to that and give you real numbers. And what would you hope would change?","m":[3,13]},{"t":"2:04","w":"P","s":"Just quiet. Going out without thinking people are talking about me."},{"t":"2:13","band":"Why clozapine","w":"D","s":"Two different antipsychotics at good doses haven't settled things. That's called treatment-resistant schizophrenia. Clozapine works best in exactly this situation.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:30","w":"D","s":"Around half of people improve, sometimes a lot. It can take a few months to show its full effect.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:40","w":"P","s":"So it's not just another tablet like the others?"},{"t":"2:45","w":"D","s":"No, it works a bit differently, which is why we keep it for when others haven't helped.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:54","band":"Before and starting","w":"D","s":"Before we start, we check bloods, including white cells, sugar, cholesterol and heart markers, plus an ECG, weight and blood pressure. Do you smoke?","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:11","w":"P","s":"About fifteen a day."},{"t":"3:16","w":"D","s":"Thanks, that matters for the dose. We also register you with a clozapine monitoring service. The pharmacy only gives it out with a safe blood result.","m":[6,11]},{"t":"3:31","w":"P","s":"Do I have to come into hospital?"},{"t":"3:35","w":"D","s":"No. You'd start at home with a tiny dose, 12.5 mg, and go up slowly over two to three weeks. The olanzapine comes down gradually as clozapine goes up.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:52","w":"D","s":"The Home Treatment Team comes every day for the first two weeks and takes your pulse, temperature and blood pressure.","m":[7]},{"t":"4:04","w":"P","s":"Okay. That's less scary."},{"t":"4:09","band":"Blood tests","w":"D","s":"Now your white cell worry. Clozapine lowers white cells in about 3 in 100 people, and seriously in under 1 in 100.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"That's why bloods are weekly for the first 18 weeks, then every two weeks until a year, then monthly. If they drop, we spot it early and stop.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:38","w":"P","s":"Weekly? I hate needles."},{"t":"4:43","w":"D","s":"That's a real worry. Many clinics use a finger-prick machine, which is quicker. And after the first year it's only monthly.","m":[13,8]},{"t":"4:55","w":"D","s":"One rule: a sore throat, temperature or flu-like feeling means ring us that day for a blood test.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:05","w":"P","s":"Sore throat, ring you. Okay."},{"t":"5:10","band":"Side effects","w":"D","s":"Common side effects: sleepiness, so we give the bigger dose at bedtime; drooling at night, which a tablet can help; a faster pulse; dizziness when you stand up.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Will I put on even more weight? I've gained loads on olanzapine."},{"t":"5:36","w":"D","s":"It can, so we weigh you regularly, offer a healthy lifestyle group, and can add metformin if needed. We'll keep a close eye.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:50","w":"D","s":"Constipation is common and can become dangerous, so you'll take a laxative from day one. If your bowels haven't opened for three days, tell us.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:05","w":"P","s":"Right."},{"t":"6:08","w":"D","s":"Rarely, it can inflame the heart, mostly in the first two months. Chest pain, breathlessness, or feeling feverish and unwell: ring us that day, or 999 if it's severe.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:25","w":"D","s":"At higher doses it can cause fits. Going slowly and checking blood levels keeps that risk low.","m":[10]},{"t":"6:34","w":"P","s":"Sorry, that's a lot."},{"t":"6:39","w":"D","s":"It is a lot. The team will be checking all of this with you, so you won't be on your own. What questions do you have so far?","m":[13]},{"t":"6:51","band":"Practical rules","w":"P","s":"Will I have to stop smoking?"},{"t":"6:55","w":"D","s":"No, but don't change it without telling us. Smoking speeds up how your body clears clozapine, so stopping can push levels up. We'd check a level.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:11","w":"D","s":"And if you miss more than two days of doses, don't just restart. Ring us, because we start again from the low dose.","m":[11]},{"t":"7:23","w":"D","s":"You mentioned wanting a normal life. Is a pregnancy possible in the next year or so?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:31","w":"P","s":"One day I'd like a baby. I've got the implant now."},{"t":"7:38","w":"D","s":"That's important. Plan it with us first, and if you do get pregnant, don't stop clozapine suddenly: ring us and we'll involve the perinatal team. And no driving while you feel drowsy.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:57","w":"P","s":"Okay. I don't drive anyway."},{"t":"8:01","band":"Summary and close","w":"D","s":"So: bloods and ECG first, a slow start at home with daily visits, weekly bloods, and a laxative from day one. Can you tell me the three things that mean you ring us the same day?","m":[14]},{"t":"8:19","w":"P","s":"Sore throat or fever. Chest pain or breathless. And not going to the toilet for three days."},{"t":"8:30","w":"D","s":"Exactly right. Here's the leaflet and the Home Treatment Team number. Out of hours, the crisis line or NHS 111 option 2.","m":[14]},{"t":"8:44","w":"D","s":"Would you like me to book your baseline bloods and ECG today?","m":[14]},{"t":"8:50","w":"P","s":"Yes. Let's do it. Thank you for explaining it properly."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, acknowledges her decision, agrees a plan and invites questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Asks about current voices, beliefs and olanzapine adherence","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks what she knows, fears and hopes for","d":"Content"},{"c":"Asks about thoughts of self-harm","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Explains why clozapine for her and a realistic chance of improvement","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains baseline tests and registration with the monitoring service","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains home start: 12.5 mg, slow rise, daily HTT checks, olanzapine taper","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Gives the blood schedule with numbers and the reason for it","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains common side effects and how each is managed","d":"Explanation"},{"c":"Explains serious risks with red flags: infection, heart, bowel, fits","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Covers smoking interaction and the 48-hour missed-dose rule","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Raises pregnancy planning and driving","d":"Plan"},{"c":"Validates her distress and fears, and pauses for questions","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"Summarises, checks red flags by teach-back, gives leaflet, HTT number and crisis route","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"She understands why clozapine, how it starts at home, the blood schedule and the three red flags, and can say them back.","borderline":"Accurate content but delivered as a lecture, or smoking and missed doses left out.","fail":"False reassurance about safety, no blood schedule, constipation or infection red flags missed, or no chance to ask questions."}},"c-u-65":{"setup":"Friday morning, adult ward treatment room. Joanne Fletcher, 48, inpatient with depression, drinks heavily at weekends, has heavy, numb legs and two falls. No history needed. In about ten minutes, examine sensation and motor function in both legs and state any deficit.","role":{"who":"Mrs Joanne Fletcher, 48, female, patient","open":"Yes, Jo.","manner":"Low, slow and a little embarrassed; follows instructions. Becomes tearful and self-blaming about the drinking when unsteady on Romberg's test; settles when validated.","free":["Both legs feel heavy, like lead","Feet feel numb, like walking on cotton wool","She has tripped twice"],"ifasked":["No pain in hips, knees or ankles","Feet always cold","No cuts on her feet that she knows of","Trips mostly when going to the toilet at night","No problems with bladder or bowels and no numbness between her legs","Feels it's her own fault because of the drink"],"worries":["Is it my spine? Will I end up in a wheelchair?","It's the drink, isn't it?","Is this why I keep tripping at night?"],"rules":["Findings: ankle dorsiflexion 4+/5 and big toe extension 4/5 both sides; knee jerks reduced; ankle jerks absent even with reinforcement; plantars flexor","Romberg positive; cannot heel-toe walk; heel-shin normal","Stocking loss to light touch and pin prick to mid-shin; vibration absent at toes and ankles, present at knees; JPS wrong at big toes","Becomes tearful during Romberg's test; settles if validated"]},"run":[{"t":"0:00","band":"Open and prepare","w":"D","s":"Hello, I'm Dr Chinonso, one of the psychiatry doctors. Are you Joanne Fletcher?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:10","w":"P","s":"Yes, Jo."},{"t":"0:14","w":"D","s":"Thanks, Jo. The nurses say your legs feel heavy and numb. I'd like to check the nerves and muscles in both legs. Could you change into shorts, and would you like a chaperone?","m":[1]},{"t":"0:33","w":"P","s":"Okay, yes please."},{"t":"0:37","w":"D","s":"Our healthcare assistant, Mo, will stay with us. Any pain in your hips, knees or ankles before I start? It takes about ten minutes.","m":[1]},{"t":"0:53","w":"P","s":"No pain. They just feel like lead."},{"t":"0:59","band":"Look","w":"D","s":"Lie back and relax. I'm looking at your feet, nails and skin, and feeling for the pulses on top of your feet.","m":[2]},{"t":"1:15","w":"P","s":"My feet are always cold."},{"t":"1:21","w":"D","s":"Pulses are there on both sides. Now I'm looking for any muscle thinning, twitching, scars or sores. Any cuts you haven't felt?","m":[3]},{"t":"1:38","w":"P","s":"Not that I know of."},{"t":"1:42","band":"Tone and power","w":"D","s":"Let your legs go floppy. I'll roll them side to side, then lift behind your knee, then press your foot up quickly.","m":[4]},{"t":"2:00","w":"P","s":"Like this?"},{"t":"2:04","w":"D","s":"Perfect, that's all normal. Now lift your right leg up and stop me pushing it down. Now push down into the bed.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:19","w":"P","s":"(does so)"},{"t":"2:23","w":"D","s":"Bend your knee; keep it bent while I try to pull it straight. Now kick out against my hand.","m":[5]},{"t":"2:35","w":"P","s":"(does so)"},{"t":"2:39","w":"D","s":"Push your foot down like a pedal. Now pull it up towards your nose. And point your big toe up against my finger.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"2:55","w":"P","s":"That one's harder, the toes."},{"t":"3:01","w":"D","s":"Thanks. Same again on the left side.","m":[5,6]},{"t":"3:09","w":"P","s":"Same. The toes are weak."},{"t":"3:14","band":"Reflexes","w":"D","s":"I'll tap just below your kneecap, then at the back of your ankle, on each side.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:28","w":"P","s":"I didn't feel the ankle one move."},{"t":"3:34","w":"D","s":"Hook your fingers together and pull hard when I say. Pull.","m":[7]},{"t":"3:44","w":"P","s":"(pulls)"},{"t":"3:48","w":"D","s":"Thank you. Now I'm stroking the sole of each foot. It may tickle.","m":[8]},{"t":"4:00","w":"P","s":"Barely feel it."},{"t":"4:03","band":"Coordination and gait","w":"D","s":"Put your right heel on your left knee and run it down your shin, then back up. Now the other side.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:19","w":"P","s":"(does so)"},{"t":"4:23","w":"D","s":"Lovely. Can you walk to the door and back for me, normally? I'm right beside you.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:33","w":"P","s":"I don't want to walk in front of everyone."},{"t":"4:39","w":"D","s":"That's fair. The door's closed, it's just us and Mo. It tells me a lot about why you've been tripping.","m":[9]},{"t":"4:53","w":"P","s":"Okay. (walks with feet apart)"},{"t":"5:00","w":"D","s":"Now heel to toe, like a tightrope.","m":[9]},{"t":"5:06","w":"P","s":"I can't. I'm all over the place."},{"t":"5:12","w":"D","s":"That's fine. Stand with your feet together, arms by your side, eyes open. Now close your eyes. I'm right here with my arms ready.","m":[10]},{"t":"5:28","w":"P","s":"Whoa, I'm going. Sorry. It's the drink, isn't it? I've done this to myself."},{"t":"5:40","w":"D","s":"You're safe, I've got you. Alcohol can affect these nerves, along with low vitamins. Blaming yourself won't help your legs, but treatment can.","m":[13]},{"t":"5:57","w":"P","s":"Okay. Thank you."},{"t":"6:01","band":"Sensation","w":"D","s":"Lie back again. This is cotton wool on your chest, so you know what normal feels like. Eyes closed, and say 'now' whenever you feel it touch.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:19","w":"P","s":"Yes... nothing on the toes... yes, there."},{"t":"6:29","w":"D","s":"Now sharp or blunt? Tell me when it changes as I move up each leg.","m":[11]},{"t":"6:39","w":"P","s":"Blunt... blunt... sharp now, halfway up my shin."},{"t":"6:48","w":"D","s":"This tuning fork buzzes. Feel it on your chest first. Now tell me when it stops on your big toe.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:02","w":"P","s":"I can't feel anything."},{"t":"7:08","w":"D","s":"I'll move up to your ankle. And now your knee.","m":[12]},{"t":"7:16","w":"P","s":"Not the ankle. Yes, the knee, buzzing."},{"t":"7:24","w":"D","s":"Eyes still closed. I'm holding your big toe by the sides. Is it moving up or down?","m":[12]},{"t":"7:36","w":"P","s":"Up? No, down? I'm not sure."},{"t":"7:41","band":"Findings and close","w":"D","s":"Thank you, Jo, let's cover you up. The nerves to your feet aren't carrying feeling well and your ankles are a bit weak, on both sides. That's why you trip, especially in the dark.","m":[13]},{"t":"8:05","w":"P","s":"Is it my spine? Will I end up in a wheelchair?"},{"t":"8:13","w":"D","s":"I can hear how frightening that is. There's no sign of a spine problem. One quick check: any trouble with your bladder or bowels, or numbness between your legs?","m":[13]},{"t":"8:31","w":"P","s":"No, nothing like that."},{"t":"8:36","w":"D","s":"Good. We'll do bloods, start vitamin B1 today, ask the medical team for a nerve test, and get physio for your balance. The nurses will put a night light on. Does that make sense?","m":[13]},{"t":"9:00","w":"P","s":"Yes. At least it's not in my head."}],"marks":[{"c":"Introduces self, gains consent, offers chaperone, exposes legs and checks for pain","d":"Rapport"},{"c":"General look: nails, skin and foot pulses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Inspects for wasting, fasciculation, deformity, scars and ulcers","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests tone with leg roll and knee lift, and checks ankle clonus","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests power at hip, knee and ankle on both sides with MRC grading","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests big toe extension on both sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests knee and ankle reflexes, using reinforcement","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests both plantar responses","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests heel-shin, normal gait and heel-toe walking safely","d":"Content"},{"c":"Performs Romberg's test standing close enough to catch her","d":"Risk"},{"c":"Tests light touch and pin prick distal to proximal, comparing sides","d":"Content"},{"c":"Tests vibration and joint position at the big toe, moving proximally when absent","d":"Content"},{"c":"Validates distress, states deficit and pattern plainly, checks red flags, gives next steps","d":"Close"}],"global":{"pass":"A complete, ordered sensory and motor assessment of both legs, ending with a clear statement: peripheral neuropathy, no cord signs, and next steps.","borderline":"Most components done but sensation not mapped, reinforcement missed, or no clear statement of the pattern.","fail":"Takes a history instead, misses sensation or reflexes, unsafe Romberg's test, or no conclusion about a deficit."}}});
